fa7ac124-64c9-4044-80e4-9cee6eceae17	a4060b92-a35c-4592-ae90-415be6266d0b	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	The paper introduces Asymmetric Correspondence Learning (ACL) to separate lexical and style information in self-supervised speech embeddings by distilling HuBERT features into Acoustic Word Embeddings (AWEs) and Acoustic Style Embeddings (ASEs). Evaluations reveal that despite attempts to isolate features, substantial variance from extrinsic sources remains encoded in each latent space, challenging the effectiveness of standard diagnostic benchmarks for feature isolation.	- ACL distills HuBERT features into separate AWEs and ASEs via a split-latent correspondence bottleneck.\n- AWEs show strong lexical clustering, while ASEs achieve high speaker accuracy but retain unrecoverable lexical variance.\n- Standard diagnostic benchmarks may fail to verify true feature isolation as latent spaces retain hidden speech structure.\n- Group-level permutation feature importance links AWEs to vocal tract filters and ASEs to fundamental frequency (F0) dynamics.	This research provides relevant insights into disentangling speech features in self-supervised embeddings, which is important for improving speech representation learning and downstream tasks.	\N	2026-11-10 15:37:29.395247+00	2026-11-10 15:37:29.395249+00
