a1262ceb-9e5b-4c97-9332-d91334f25ee4	a2299ea5-2bd6-4ac8-88d8-aa9743f5cb3b	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research paper investigates representational drift in the OpenCoder-1.5B model during pretraining, focusing on how the neurons carrying specific Python constructs change over time while the model's behavior remains stable. The study finds that the set of neurons responsible for carrying a construct never fully settles, contradicting the initial hypothesis that these carriers would stabilize.	- Representational drift refers to changes in which neurons carry a representation while behavior remains constant.\n- In OpenCoder-1.5B, the neurons carrying Python constructs continue to change throughout training, even after behavior stabilizes.\n- Removing neurons identified at earlier checkpoints does not significantly impact function, indicating that the function is carried by whichever neurons are current.\n- The hypothesis that neuron carriers settle over time is refuted by the experiment.\n- The study discusses potential causes of this drift and proposes further experiments to distinguish them.	This research provides relevant insights into neural network training dynamics and interpretability, particularly regarding how internal representations evolve, which is important for understanding and improving AI models.	\N	2026-11-10 15:37:13.262412+00	2026-11-10 15:37:13.262416+00
b28dece2-a161-4304-8da0-6101b30d6fa5	31d571a8-328e-495f-bda5-46e52428d602	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	Khmer PFGen-Bench is introduced as the first judge-free benchmark for evaluating open-ended generation in Khmer, a low-resource language. The benchmark adapts scoring methods from a Japanese benchmark and uses references generated by post-trained models from multiple families to ensure validity and variety, achieving stable model rankings consistent with native speaker evaluations.	- Khmer PFGen-Bench is the first judge-free benchmark for multi-sentence open-ended generation in Khmer.\n- It adapts scoring methods from PFGen-Bench for Japanese, using keyword rules and n-gram statistics.\n- Post-trained models produce more valid but less varied references; mixing model families increases variety.\n- The benchmark yields stable and consistent model rankings aligned with native speaker ratings.\n- Provides guidance for building judge-free benchmarks in other low-resource languages.	This research provides a novel, judge-free evaluation method for open-ended generation in a low-resource language, addressing challenges in reliable benchmarking without human judges, which is relevant for advancing NLP in underrepresented languages.	\N	2026-11-10 15:37:16.577418+00	2026-11-10 15:37:16.57742+00
