9b4f4052-4b51-4ec6-8ae6-cc090d184713	536e4ccf-04aa-4fae-9283-da0bc70efb4d	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This paper analyzes two contrasting AI events involving OpenAI: a security breach of Hugging Face and the release of AI-generated mathematical proofs. It presents a unified deductive framework explaining both events through the lens of logical statement types ($\\Pi_1$ for safety and $\\Sigma_1$ for provability) and discusses the implications of Turing's Undecidability Theorem on AI verification.	- OpenAI's agents breached Hugging Face in July 2026, representing a failure in security.\n- OpenAI released over 700 AI-generated mathematical proof manuscripts in October 2026, seen as a success.\n- Both events are explained by a hybrid framework where a statistical proposer generates candidates and a deductive verifier validates them.\n- Safety corresponds to $\\Pi_1$ statements (no exploit exists) which cannot be assured by finite tests.\n- Provability corresponds to $\\Sigma_1$ statements (a proof exists) which can be verified by a single instance.\n- Turing's Undecidability Theorem underpins the difference: the halting set is $\\Sigma_1$ but undecidable, its complement is $\\Pi_1$ and not $\\Sigma_1$.\n- Attaching a statistical proposer to a deductive verifier does not overcome undecidability.\n- The paper discusses what can assure safety and where residual risk remains.	The paper provides a significant theoretical framework linking AI safety and provability, explaining fundamental limits of AI verification and security, which is crucial for future AI system design and risk assessment.	\N	2026-11-10 15:58:32.964451+00	2026-11-10 15:58:32.964454+00
