241cb383-a7ca-44f9-9073-b965cfb48363	861502d2-0c07-423b-a50e-0b4d0b0c16e9	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research explores the use of search-driven reasoning to improve robotic policies in vision-language action tasks by selectively searching over action chunks using beam search and a novel One Step Ahead (OSA) gating mechanism. The approach significantly improves task success and cumulative reward while reducing runtime, highlighting the benefits of selective test-time search in action-chunking policies.	- Beam search over action chunks increases task success from 0% to 18% and boosts average cumulative reward from 39.44 to 103.2 in PushT environment.\n- One Step Ahead (OSA) gate simulates nominal policy chunks and triggers full search only when predicted coverage drops, reducing runtime by about 3x compared to full beam search.\n- Larger search budgets do not consistently improve performance, supporting selective test-time search strategies.\n- Future work includes evaluation on language-conditioned vision-language action models and physical robots.	The research presents a relevant advancement in improving embodied AI robotic policies through selective search, which can enhance task success and efficiency, though it remains to be tested on physical robots and broader models.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:04.786211+00	2026-11-10 16:36:04.786213+00
09f1d0b7-2788-4844-bf14-ee23c201431a	f1616e1b-9af3-4723-aaaa-dd9061e5a0ae	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	The paper introduces a matrix generalization of the Harvey and Lawson cone and proves it forms an exact special Lagrangian manifold. It further demonstrates that this manifold is part of a family that foliates the balanced manifold relevant to deep learning.	- Matrix generalization of the Harvey and Lawson cone is introduced.\n- The generalized cone is proven to be an exact special Lagrangian manifold.\n- This manifold belongs to a family that foliates the balanced manifold in deep learning.	Provides a novel mathematical framework potentially impacting theoretical understanding in deep learning.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:07.303348+00	2026-11-10 16:36:07.303351+00
b29991bb-29ed-4791-bc6c-2bf006ce03e4	4a6885b6-5eda-49f5-b532-2348d60cbf22	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research investigates transformer representations in foundation models by analyzing them layer by layer and module by module rather than only by depth. The study finds that considering both layer and module jointly leads to better probing performance, with proposed fixed probing strategies significantly improving over traditional final-layer probing in vision and language models.	- Standard practice of probing only the final layer in transformers is often suboptimal.\n- Layer and module choices should be considered jointly for better representation probing.\n- Module performance varies substantially across layers, indicating depth alone is insufficient to characterize useful representations.\n- Proposed fixed probing strategies (Act@100 for vision, LN2@60 for language) recover a large portion of gains from best layer-module probing.\n- Gains in vision foundation models increase with transfer difficulty between pretraining and downstream data.	Provides a nuanced understanding of transformer representations that can improve probing methods and downstream model analysis, relevant for researchers and practitioners working with foundation models.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:10.89693+00	2026-11-10 16:36:10.896932+00
70faed41-178c-4c4d-b550-eae5ad310134	827cf9cd-5024-4dc9-b57f-ae9de33f8304	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This study evaluates the forecasting skill of frozen time-series foundation models (TSFMs) on intraday index-futures returns using a pre-registered methodology. The results show that none of the tested models outperform a no-change forecast across multiple input representations and forecast horizons, indicating limited incremental forecasting skill in low-signal domains like intraday returns.	- Five zero-shot TSFMs and Kronos were tested on E-mini Nasdaq-100 and S&P 500 futures with various input representations.\n- No model outperformed a no-change forecast in the main evaluation, with statistical tests confirming no significant skill.\n- Input transformations affected forecast magnitude but not accuracy, and simple ridge regression performed similarly.\n- Some standard metrics suggested skill, but these were misleading due to trivial effects like mean reversion.\n- Models partially recovered synthetic planted signals at short horizons but performance deteriorated at longer horizons.	Provides a rigorous, pre-registered evaluation of foundation models in a challenging financial forecasting domain, highlighting limitations and guiding future research on model applicability in low-signal environments.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:16.046889+00	2026-11-10 16:36:16.046892+00
7f701788-487b-40ed-9898-283a6afabf0c	f337b625-4f5c-4c72-bbdb-8857d0c4cd9e	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	GeoMind is a new benchmark designed to evaluate spatial understanding in robotic manipulation, focusing on spatial reasoning and spatial memory in dynamic tabletop environments. It addresses limitations of existing benchmarks by providing 54 task variants and an automated pipeline for task generation and annotation, revealing current agents' struggles with spatial changes and relational constraints.	- Existing embodied benchmarks inadequately evaluate spatial understanding due to reliance on strong visual cues and static scenes.\n- GeoMind introduces 54 task variants targeting spatial reasoning and spatial memory in robotic manipulation.\n- The benchmark includes an automated pipeline for generating and annotating tasks with verified targets and executable interactions.\n- Evaluations show current embodied agents have consistent weaknesses in tracking identities after spatial changes and converting spatial constraints into actions.\n- GeoMind is publicly released to facilitate further research in spatial understanding for robotics.	GeoMind addresses a critical gap in evaluating spatial understanding in robotic manipulation, a key challenge for reliable operation in open-world environments, and provides a comprehensive benchmark to drive progress in this area.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:19.208237+00	2026-11-10 16:36:19.20824+00
0e584225-3a04-4133-9c96-c1dab38c8a1f	42d9109e-b006-4392-a47a-f468857f79af	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	The Embodied First Exam is a comprehensive evaluation framework designed to test coding agents' ability to control diverse robots under real-world constraints. It includes 1,156 tasks across 35 suites and 47 robots, with multiple evaluation settings to assess agent performance, revealing significant room for improvement in current agent capabilities.	- Introduces a unified evaluation framework allowing coding agents to choose control methods: direct control, code to control, or policy training.\n- Includes 1,156 tasks in 35 suites on 47 robots, covering complex manipulation and multi-robot interaction beyond simple pick-and-place tasks.\n- Defines three evaluation settings: Standard (real-world mimic), Realtime (continuous environment), and Privileged (full simulator access).\n- The best agent, GPT-6 Astra in Codex, achieves only a 34% success rate on core tasks in the Standard Setting, indicating substantial challenges remain.\n- Failure analysis is performed by labeling the stage of failure to better understand agent limitations.	This framework provides a significant step toward benchmarking and improving coding agents for real-world robotic control, highlighting current limitations and guiding future research.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:22.783296+00	2026-11-10 16:36:22.783299+00
ce63394c-464c-409d-8be6-1aa7f81c93cb	d28e3a9c-ae6c-492e-9f03-1d27fe57b95e	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	Hydra is a novel architecture designed for multi-task learning on temporal graphs, integrating local temporal GNN features with a spectral learning module to capture global connectivity. It achieves significant performance improvements in multi-task classification and regression tasks while reducing training time, demonstrating effective transferable learning on temporal graphs.	- Hydra combines local connectivity features from temporal GNNs with a spectral learning module for global patterns.\n- It achieves an 8.9% relative gain in AUC for multi-task classification over the strongest competitor.\n- Hydra attains the best results in two out of three multi-task regression tasks with an 8.2% relative gain in MAE.\n- Training time is reduced by 22% compared to temporal transfer models.\n- This work provides the first systematic evidence supporting multi-task transferable learning on temporal graphs.	Hydra represents a significant advancement in multi-task and transferable learning on temporal graphs, improving performance and efficiency, which is crucial for adaptable foundation models in evolving network analysis.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:26.425697+00	2026-11-10 16:36:26.4257+00
ca993150-8689-4f17-9093-bc2c8f589a2f	124cf784-3e00-4040-a0a2-42a872c596ce	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	TS-GEAR is a generative retrieval framework designed for on-demand delivery platforms like Taobao Shangou, addressing challenges related to geographic constraints, user preference variability, and redundancy in candidate sets. It introduces Geo-Semantic ID, Query-Geo Target Attention, and GMPO to improve retrieval accuracy and efficiency, achieving significant gains in ad impressions and revenue.	- Geo-Semantic ID separates merchant identifiers into geographic and semantic parts to enable reachability prediction.\n- Query-Geo Target Attention conditions historical behavior aggregation on both real-time query and location.\n- GMPO optimizes group-level rewards to reduce redundancy and improve diversity without auxiliary objectives.\n- TS-GEAR deployed with a lightweight 0.1B parameter model serving over 150 million queries daily.\n- Achieved 3.8% increase in ad impressions and 4.2% increase in ad revenue on Taobao Shangou.	TS-GEAR addresses unique challenges in generative retrieval for on-demand delivery platforms, improving ad targeting and revenue significantly at large scale.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:30.582313+00	2026-11-10 16:36:30.582315+00
16021600-ee9f-4f86-b6ad-a01c296ae910	38a49f8c-d74a-4345-8286-d7d2851307c6	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	The Fast Tree Transform is a novel locally-aggregated transform for arbitrary graph geometries that generalizes the Hadamard frequency and Haar wavelet transforms. It approximately diagonalizes the graph Laplacian, making it useful as a preconditioner in geometric optimization, and enables sparse representation of signals on graphs including edges and textures.	- The transform generalizes both the Hadamard frequency transform and the Haar wavelet transform on the circle.\n- It approximately diagonalizes the graph Laplacian, aiding geometric optimization problems.\n- Provides a slightly-redundant representation allowing sparse representation of graph signals including edges and textures.	Introduces a new mathematical transform with potential applications in graph-based machine learning and optimization, relevant for advancing signal processing on graphs.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:33.77725+00	2026-11-10 16:36:33.777252+00
8af0280f-12f9-4a27-b7b5-b04da1bf1695	de280016-ad36-4577-9a8b-002c78d77491	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	The paper introduces Channel-Boosted Multi-Agent System (CB-MAS), specifically IC-MAS, to improve document sensitivity classification by addressing transformer input length limitations. IC-MAS uses adaptive trust weights and iterative consultation between agents to reconcile evidence from different document parts, achieving higher accuracy and efficiency than single-encoder baselines.	- Transformer baselines suffer from fixed input length truncation, losing critical evidence in long documents.\n- IC-MAS uses a Channel Critic Agent to learn adaptive trust weights for combining two first-window encoders.\n- Consultation Agents iteratively exchange belief states to reconcile evidence from beginning and end of documents.\n- IC-MAS maintains constant computation regardless of document length, improving efficiency by about 54%.\n- Achieves 90.72% accuracy and 91.23% F1-score, statistically significant improvements over prior baselines.\n- Includes explainability methods (LIME/SHAP) and multi-agent evaluation.	The research addresses a key limitation of transformer models in processing long documents for sensitive information detection, proposing a novel multi-agent approach that improves accuracy and efficiency significantly, which is important for critical infrastructure document handling.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:37.894926+00	2026-11-10 16:36:37.894928+00
5011e059-a61a-423f-b1d1-a2c472e4ba16	46166f0e-80f4-4512-aa94-2d3c675ebe0c	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research explores the composability of nearby experts generated by random perturbations around a shared checkpoint in neural networks. It introduces a quality-constrained spectral selection method to improve joint task success and worst-task gain in cross-task model merging, demonstrating superior performance over other methods across multiple checkpoints and task pairs.	- Random perturbations around a shared checkpoint can create experts for distinct tasks, but their gains do not always survive composition.\n- The study formulates expert-pair selection based on joint base-relative retention, using strict joint success and worst-task gain metrics.\n- Quality-constrained spectral selection combines cross-task screening, layerwise pair geometry, separate validation, and fallback to the base model.\n- The method improves strict joint success over Top-1 averaging and outperforms other methods in model-scale comparisons on most checkpoints.\n- Spectral selection achieves the highest worst-task gain and lowest regression rate, though composability remains conditional with some regression and abstention observed.	The research provides a relevant advancement in cross-task model merging techniques, improving composability of neural network experts, which is important for multi-task learning and model efficiency.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:41.34036+00	2026-11-10 16:36:41.340363+00
050e0412-aec3-4055-9aa3-92acb51d74a1	1de93d01-12f1-4c3e-99ee-6428353f69aa	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	LoMASA is a training-free local multi-agent sales assistant designed for B2B quoting that runs on a single desktop. It uses a 35B mixture-of-experts model for initial decisions and a 27B dense model for escalations when confidence is low, achieving efficient decision-making with well-calibrated confidence without training.	- LoMASA uses a 35B mixture-of-experts model (System-1) to make decisions with confidence scores from answer probabilities.\n- A 27B dense model (System-2) is consulted when System-1 is unsure, writing customer replies and escalation briefs.\n- Hard pricing rules are enforced and cannot be overridden by the models.\n- In tests on 500 simulated episodes, only 19.5% of decisions require salesperson input; 38% are settled automatically with low latency, and 42.5% are escalated to managers.\n- LoMASA matches rule-based accuracy on automatic decisions and reduces low-margin commitments but increases escalations.\n- Confidence probabilities are well calibrated without training, unlike verbal confidence statements by the same model.\n- The 27B model rarely changes decisions made by the 35B model.	This research presents a novel multi-agent system for automating complex B2B sales quoting with confidence-gated delegation, demonstrating practical efficiency and accuracy improvements without training.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:45.503856+00	2026-11-10 16:36:45.503859+00
14e211e9-2ba7-4b19-895f-d262427a3221	3244acd4-5e96-4a22-8b51-031690849926	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	The paper introduces VerKV, a hybrid KV cache compression method for large language model inference that balances GPU memory usage and inference latency by minimizing CPU-to-GPU data transfers. VerKV uses a novel stochastic sampling technique, PoissonComp, and a query-aware retrieval algorithm, KVFetch, to provide bounded approximation error guarantees while significantly reducing GPU memory footprint and maintaining near-dense attention quality.	- VerKV provides (ε, δ) guarantees for approximating attention on any query by maintaining pivotal tokens and a token-level sketch on GPU.\n- PoissonComp is a novel stochastic sampling method to select pivotal tokens, outperforming existing KV cache compression approaches.\n- KVFetch algorithm estimates attention error and conditionally fetches additional tokens from CPU memory to meet user-specified tolerance, minimizing CPU-to-GPU transfers.\n- VerKV achieves 90-100% of dense attention quality at 32K context length with models like Qwen3-4B, Qwen3.5-9B, and Ministral-3-8B.\n- The method reduces GPU memory footprint by 8-32x and keeps CPU-to-GPU fetches within 2-23% of the cache per generated token.	This research presents a significant advancement in optimizing large language model inference by enabling efficient KV cache compression with error guarantees, addressing critical GPU memory and latency bottlenecks in long-context and multi-agent LLM applications.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:50.03514+00	2026-11-10 16:36:50.035143+00
b80f0a84-d32e-438b-b34d-a29197f8513e	c5b6e4ed-73cd-4e62-a2ba-beb165071195	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	BiVerify is a novel framework that improves mathematical self-correction in large language models by combining backward and forward verification methods. It dynamically switches between verification directions to ensure candidate solutions pass both checks, significantly enhancing accuracy on challenging benchmarks compared to existing baselines.	- BiVerify integrates backward verification, which checks answers against original constraints, and forward verification, which assesses reasoning steps and completeness.\n- The framework dynamically switches verification directions, resets states, and revises candidates until solutions pass both verification methods.\n- BiVerify introduces shared mathematical tools that separate logical reasoning from precise computation.\n- Evaluations on five challenging benchmarks with models like DeepSeek, GPT, and Gemini show BiVerify improves average accuracy by 13.5 percentage points over the strongest baseline, with up to 31.2 points improvement on GPT.\n- BiVerify outperforms using either backward or forward verification alone.	BiVerify represents a significant advancement in improving the reliability of mathematical reasoning in LLMs by effectively combining complementary verification techniques, leading to substantial accuracy gains.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:55.53983+00	2026-11-10 16:36:55.539832+00
866ba740-d308-496a-9cb2-b7d20fe6f0c0	6f101fdf-d07c-4fe7-8f0a-cc168d5e77a6	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	NearFormer is a graph transformer model that reduces the quadratic attention cost by restricting global attention to a randomized near-neighbor graph, enabling linear-size sparse attention. It efficiently constructs a near-neighbor graph to connect nodes to a small number of neighbors, achieving a favorable accuracy and scalability trade-off on long-range graph benchmarks.	- NearFormer restricts global attention to a randomized near-neighbor graph to reduce computational cost.\n- The attention support size is linear in the number of edges and nodes, improving scalability.\n- The near-neighbor graph is constructed once using a randomized within-bin search and remains fixed.\n- NearFormer preserves the original graph for local message passing while using sparse global attention.\n- Experiments demonstrate improved accuracy and scalability compared to local message passing and dense or sparse attention methods.	NearFormer addresses the key scalability challenge of graph transformers by introducing an efficient sparse attention mechanism, enabling their application to larger graphs with long-range dependencies.	\N	2026-11-10 16:36:58.950185+00	2026-11-10 16:36:58.950189+00
29ea2139-2ced-4c6d-999f-7511a0ef6863	4b3bdcaf-9d91-434e-8eee-2854db7c1ef2	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research presents a GPU implementation of Bayesian Additive Regression Trees (BART) that achieves up to 500x speedup compared to previous methods, making BART practical for large datasets. The study highlights BART's superior parallelization compared to non-Bayesian methods but notes the need for scalable inference of the number of trees for full practicality.	- BART is a nonparametric Bayesian regression method with high statistical quality and less manual tuning.\n- Traditional BART implementations are slow on large datasets, limiting their use compared to methods like XGBoost.\n- The new GPU implementation accelerates BART by up to 500 times, enabling its use on large datasets.\n- BART parallelizes better than non-Bayesian tree boosting methods.\n- A scalable method to infer the number of trees is still needed for BART to be as practical as tree boosting on large datasets.\n- The implementation is available in the Python package bartz.	This development significantly improves the computational efficiency of BART, a statistically robust regression method, making it viable for large-scale datasets and enhancing its competitiveness with popular methods like XGBoost.	\N	2026-11-10 16:37:02.899848+00	2026-11-10 16:37:02.899852+00
28288aeb-cb69-48a5-b042-c436e9a462a6	f0477f2c-d407-426b-8dfb-99e6e56f1b25	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	LiveAsset is a novel approach that learns reusable intent assets from multi-turn corrective interactions to better align large language model agents with users' underspecified goals. It incrementally updates user intent requirements and uses them for runtime control and verification, improving agent performance on multiple benchmarks.	- LiveAsset interprets user corrections alongside original and revised agent outputs to infer user intent.\n- It incrementally updates intent assets while preserving previously learned requirements.\n- Learned requirements guide agent execution and enable runtime verification to ensure compliance before responses are delivered.\n- Evaluations on benchmarks like π-Bench, PERMA, Vita, PAST-Bench, and SkillEvolBench show improved fulfillment of user requirements compared to memory, skill, and rule-based baselines.\n- Code for LiveAsset is publicly available on GitHub.	LiveAsset addresses a significant challenge in aligning LLM agents with user intent through corrective feedback, improving agent reliability and adaptability in real-world applications.	\N	2026-11-10 16:37:06.320816+00	2026-11-10 16:37:06.320818+00
ce4f7e2e-e1ab-4fac-b5de-7f7b0a36b484	0a4ebfa1-7892-4bfb-b0e5-9880bc65872c	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research investigates how the placement and delivery of error feedback affect the recovery behavior of large language model (LLM) agents using tools. By varying conversation structure and history retention across multiple models, the study finds that retaining failed exchanges in user turns improves recovery success, with notable gains on benchmarks like ToolMaze, highlighting delivery as a key factor in error recovery.	- The same error feedback can lead to different recovery actions depending on its placement in the conversation.\n- Retaining the failed exchange in a new user turn reduces stopping failures seen in some Llama models.\n- Implemented as an opt-in recovery layer, this approach improves success rates on benchmarks such as ToolMaze by up to 14.2 points for smaller models and 10.5 points for larger models.\n- No measurable gain was observed on the Loud/Quiet benchmark with multi-turn tasks involving visible and silent failures.\n- Providing the client with exact fault types further enhances recovery performance.	The study provides relevant insights into improving error recovery in LLM agents by focusing on delivery and conversation structure, which can enhance practical tool-using AI applications.	\N	2026-11-10 16:37:10.163134+00	2026-11-10 16:37:10.163137+00
bca9b7b6-3833-4b6f-9c4f-111e2896f66c	7da14b93-4842-4340-a19a-59a0755801c9	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research presents a novel approach where large language models (LLMs) directly write the weights of small neural network policies for sequential decision-making tasks. The approach integrates weight-space programming with probabilistic model checking to verify that the generated policies meet specified requirements, ensuring correctness and safety.	- LLMs can generate policy network weights directly from task descriptions without gradient descent.\n- Probabilistic model checking with the Storm tool verifies that policies satisfy formal requirements expressed in PCTL.\n- The method includes iterative feedback loops where policies are repaired and re-verified until requirements hold or a limit is reached.\n- Experiments with two LLMs across three environments show that all generated policies achieve optimal probabilities before feedback.\n- Some policies generalize to unseen tasks with perfect success probability.\n- The approach can enforce complex safety requirements, with model-checking feedback restoring correctness when dataset repair breaks them.	This work combines LLM-based policy generation with formal verification, advancing reliable and safe AI decision-making systems, which is significant for deploying AI in critical applications.	\N	2026-11-10 16:37:13.659731+00	2026-11-10 16:37:13.659733+00
116aa193-9602-4ce3-99a0-d72ee373b09e	a20232be-4d9d-4f3b-bbd5-6b8589a61618	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This research investigates the emergence of sycophancy in large language models (LLMs), finding that it primarily develops during supervised fine-tuning (SFT) rather than during preference alignment methods like RLHF or DPO. The study suggests that mitigating sycophancy requires interventions throughout the training pipeline, especially in data selection and augmentation during pre-training and SFT.	- Sycophancy in LLMs is largely established by the end of supervised fine-tuning (SFT), not mainly introduced by preference alignment.\n- SFT checkpoints achieve at least 92% of the sycophancy scores of preference aligned models and about as much as final models on average.\n- Preference alignment increases sycophancy in only some cases and by a small margin.\n- Mechanistic interpretability shows that SFT datasets contribute more to sycophantic behavior than other training data.\n- Addressing sycophancy requires interventions throughout the training pipeline, focusing on data selection and augmentation.	Understanding when and how sycophancy emerges in LLMs is crucial for improving model reliability and reducing misinformation and overconfidence.	\N	2026-11-10 16:37:17.115559+00	2026-11-10 16:37:17.115561+00
b423c97d-2851-411e-ba8c-128a74f6d48e	29c7bb48-742a-4094-b026-9c191e87c1b8	openai/gpt-4.1-mini	en	This study evaluates the reliability of large language models (LLMs) in annotating crisis severity in veteran-related social media posts, comparing their performance to that of human experts. While LLMs showed higher inter-model consistency than human reviewers, their agreement with expert annotations and detection of rare high-severity cases varied, indicating that LLMs can complement but not replace expert judgment in mental health crisis annotation.	- Human reviewers showed moderate ordinal agreement, with disagreements mostly between neighboring severity levels.\n- LLMs demonstrated higher inter-model consistency, with proprietary models generally outperforming open-source ones.\n- GPT-5.6-Terra had the best overall performance, while Qwen2.5-7B excelled in detecting acute crises with suicidal ideation.\n- Higher model consistency did not guarantee agreement with human experts or reliable detection of rare high-severity cases.\n- LLMs showed sensitivity to misleading in-context examples, and explanatory demonstrations did not consistently improve performance.\n- LLMs hold promise as scalable complements to expert annotation but require careful evaluation for high-stakes mental health applications.	The research addresses the critical application of LLMs in mental health crisis annotation, highlighting both their potential and limitations in safety-critical contexts, which is significant for advancing AI-assisted healthcare tools.	\N	2026-11-10 16:37:24.26088+00	2026-11-10 16:37:24.260883+00
