60bd6561-cf4b-4865-91b2-65d17a92126a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09400v1	Causal graph fusion across incomplete cohorts and causal steering of tabular foundation models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09400	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09400	Yanke Li, Dhiyaa Al Jorf, Ayoube Ighissou, Anke Scheel-Sailer, Robert Riener, Diego Paez-Granados	arXiv:2611.09400v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Clinical data are split across incomplete cohorts with partially overlapping variables. Causal discovery under missing data and multi-source integration have developed separately, and tabular foundation models cannot take a causal graph without retraining. We make two contributions. First, causal graph fusion learns a partial ancestral graph on the shared variables, fuses it into each cohort's graph under temporal background knowledge and lets Markov blankets guide re-imputation, so missing data are handled inside discovery. Second, causal attention steering compiles the learned graph into the attention of a frozen tabular foundation model at inference. Against known ground truth, fusion dominates pooled and per-cohort baselines in every missingness and heterogeneity condition tested (p<0.001), and steering improves discrimination under distribution shift (p<10^{-5}), a gain that a random graph does not produce. In two spinal cord injury cohorts, fusion orients age, albumin and hemoglobin as ancestors of length of stay, a set no single cohort orients in full, and yields compact, temporally ordered predictor sets that match or exceed automatic feature selection on four of five outcomes.	arXiv:2611.09400v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Clinical data are split across incomplete cohorts with partially overlapping variables. Causal discovery under missing data and multi-source integration have developed separately, and tabular foundation models cannot take a causal graph without retraining. We make two contributions. First, causal graph fusion learns a partial ancestral graph on the shared variables, fuses it into each cohort's graph under temporal background knowledge and lets Markov blankets guide re-imputation, so missing data are handled inside discovery. Second, causal attention steering compiles the learned graph into the attention of a frozen tabular foundation model at inference. Against known ground truth, fusion dominates pooled and per-cohort baselines in every missingness and heterogeneity condition tested (p<0.001), and steering improves discrimination under distribution shift (p<10^{-5}), a gain that a random graph does not produce. In two spinal cord injury cohorts, fusion orients age, albumin and hemoglobin as ancestors of length of stay, a set no single cohort orients in full, and yields compact, temporally ordered predictor sets that match or exceed automatic feature selection on four of five outcomes.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.53031+00	4	\N	0c7075c3f270db76ad575f10039117d9fc9942cd92d09b242311ae49ab90a4f1	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.530699+00	2026-11-10 14:51:29.266282+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
75bdf65f-604f-4c9b-befa-363582e278b1	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/measuring-how-ai-agents-handle-wet-lab-science	Measuring how AI agents handle everyday wet-lab science	https://openai.com/index/measuring-how-ai-agents-handle-wet-lab-science	https://openai.com/index/measuring-how-ai-agents-handle-wet-lab-science	\N	OpenAI introduces BioBasicsBench, a 100-task benchmark measuring how well AI agents interpret raw experimental data and handle everyday wet-lab science.	OpenAI introduces BioBasicsBench, a 100-task benchmark measuring how well AI agents interpret raw experimental data and handle everyday wet-lab science.	research	en	2026-11-06 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.873334+00	4	\N	4942d4a238583d581c4760e347d23de46d833f48c4ed6b38c63fd528679f4da0	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.873728+00	2026-11-10 14:44:17.784814+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5797ae13-3e96-495b-941a-8081ad7535f0	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/partnering-with-hacktron	Partnering with Hacktron to find vulnerabilities in OpenAI’s systems	https://openai.com/index/partnering-with-hacktron	https://openai.com/index/partnering-with-hacktron	\N	OpenAI is partnering with Hacktron to find vulnerabilities, test connections with third-party services, and strengthen defenses with frontier AI.	OpenAI is partnering with Hacktron to find vulnerabilities, test connections with third-party services, and strengthen defenses with frontier AI.	news	en	2026-11-06 08:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.87083+00	4	\N	9630f48f5b7e179705dd3ce4f255345919d971d17a07a211448e68b10c3e91d4	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.871381+00	2026-11-10 14:44:22.322196+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8dd77d9e-7f26-442f-bd04-e529c89dc3b1	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09665v1	Online Causal Configuration for Networked Systems via Doubly Robust Steady-State Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09665	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09665	Yuli Liu, Zhiheng Zhang	arXiv:2611.09665v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Measurement exploration reveals how interacting local controllers behave but can alter the steady-state regime a network operator seeks to optimize. Exploratory rewards may therefore fail to identify a configuration's no-exploration value: under full exploration, identical logged distributions can conceal different target values. Identification requires accounting for both action-law mismatch and shifts in the steady-state context distribution. We introduce CAUSAL-UCB, which selects configurations using optimistic bounds on target causal values. Its interference-aware clipped doubly robust evaluator produces predecision certificates separating statistical variation from calibrated identification, transport, and temporal-dependence errors. For any finite horizon and predetermined schedule of measurement-exploration rates $\\{\\eta_t\\}$, we prove lower bounds of $\\Omega((1+\\sum_t\\eta_t)^{-1/2})$ on minimax inference error and $\\Omega(\\sum_t\\eta_t)$ on measurement-induced deployment loss in a stationary, potentially slow-mixing family. We bound regret under valid certificates and selected-information growth, and establish an anytime finite-sample certificate with a shared radius for estimation, selection, and reporting in a restricted known-local-interference model. In a high-mismatch benchmark with 24 paired seeds and 200 rounds, CAUSAL-UCB attains target regret of $1.732$ versus $6.326$ for certified IPS-UCB, with deployment gaps of $30.607$ and $23.217$, respectively. Independent-audit coverage tests and trace-driven semi-synthetic studies further assess certificate coverage and deployment trade-offs.	arXiv:2611.09665v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Measurement exploration reveals how interacting local controllers behave but can alter the steady-state regime a network operator seeks to optimize. Exploratory rewards may therefore fail to identify a configuration's no-exploration value: under full exploration, identical logged distributions can conceal different target values. Identification requires accounting for both action-law mismatch and shifts in the steady-state context distribution. We introduce CAUSAL-UCB, which selects configurations using optimistic bounds on target causal values. Its interference-aware clipped doubly robust evaluator produces predecision certificates separating statistical variation from calibrated identification, transport, and temporal-dependence errors. For any finite horizon and predetermined schedule of measurement-exploration rates $\\{\\eta_t\\}$, we prove lower bounds of $\\Omega((1+\\sum_t\\eta_t)^{-1/2})$ on minimax inference error and $\\Omega(\\sum_t\\eta_t)$ on measurement-induced deployment loss in a stationary, potentially slow-mixing family. We bound regret under valid certificates and selected-information growth, and establish an anytime finite-sample certificate with a shared radius for estimation, selection, and reporting in a restricted known-local-interference model. In a high-mismatch benchmark with 24 paired seeds and 200 rounds, CAUSAL-UCB attains target regret of $1.732$ versus $6.326$ for certified IPS-UCB, with deployment gaps of $30.607$ and $23.217$, respectively. Independent-audit coverage tests and trace-driven semi-synthetic studies further assess certificate coverage and deployment trade-offs.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.58063+00	3	\N	70a1197d3c5012ab6230d89f71a5104cd1c3311e50e9e5c93bd525ef05b433d9	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.581094+00	2026-11-10 14:52:34.935142+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
