1b0bc80a-ec30-4d10-9b2d-22b44c166fc3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09843v1	Fifty-Two Shades of Pig: What the UCR Archive Really Asks Classifiers to Do	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09843	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09843	Omar Swelam, Jaris K\\"uken, Shi Bin Hoo, Frank Hutter, Lennart Purucker	arXiv:2611.09843v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The pigs were bleeding. That was the point of the experiment: could airway pressure reveal hemorrhage before it became critical? In the UCR Time Series Classification Archive, the de facto benchmark for Time Series Classification (TSC), the same recordings ask a different question: "which of 52 pigs produced this trace?". The dataset PigAirwayPressure is not an outlier. Although the archive is the standard benchmark, whether its tasks resemble the problems TSC is meant to solve has rarely been examined. We find that several datasets are repurposed from their original task, built from toy setups, or have flawed test splits. We further question how representative the archive's ordered non-temporal sequences (e.g. spectra, shape outlines) are of real TSC, and the potential downsides from the decomposition of multivariate problems. Our findings call for more careful interpretation of archive-wide scores and greater attention to deployment-relevant evaluation. The audit is at https://github.com/automl/ucr-provenance-audit.	arXiv:2611.09843v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The pigs were bleeding. That was the point of the experiment: could airway pressure reveal hemorrhage before it became critical? In the UCR Time Series Classification Archive, the de facto benchmark for Time Series Classification (TSC), the same recordings ask a different question: "which of 52 pigs produced this trace?". The dataset PigAirwayPressure is not an outlier. Although the archive is the standard benchmark, whether its tasks resemble the problems TSC is meant to solve has rarely been examined. We find that several datasets are repurposed from their original task, built from toy setups, or have flawed test splits. We further question how representative the archive's ordered non-temporal sequences (e.g. spectra, shape outlines) are of real TSC, and the potential downsides from the decomposition of multivariate problems. Our findings call for more careful interpretation of archive-wide scores and greater attention to deployment-relevant evaluation. The audit is at https://github.com/automl/ucr-provenance-audit.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.623034+00	3	\N	04ebe64b21f33ad8b37110c213fe4f272a2342ea5967d66f421a26d11c38a080	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.623433+00	2026-11-10 14:53:25.722499+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
