e46b56f5-9d1b-416d-9cbd-7ad62b6fb31c	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.08956v3	Agentic AI Scientists Are Not Built For Autonomous Scientific Discovery	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08956	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08956	Harshit Bisht, Vinay Kumar, Kevin Maik Jablonka,  Mausam, N. M. Anoop Krishnan	arXiv:2605.08956v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: A growing body of work pursues AI scientists capable of end-to-end autonomous scientific discovery. This position paper argues that although they already function as co-scientists, agentic AI scientists are not built for autonomous scientific discovery. We identify the following challenges in building and deploying autonomous AI scientists: (1) Problem selection is influenced by the McNamara fallacy; (2) Agents are built on large language models (LLMs) whose training corpora omit tacit procedural and failure knowledge of laboratory practice; (3) Preference optimisation during post-training compresses output diversity toward consensus; and (4) Most scientific benchmarks measure single-turn prediction accuracy and lack feedback from physical experiments back to the computational model. These challenges are not just questions of scale and scaffolding; they require revisiting fundamental design choices. To build truly autonomous AI scientists, we recommend scientific simulations as verifiers for training, a persistent, mutable epistemic state that carries beliefs and shifting objectives across an investigation, the establishment of a centralized preregistration repository for all AI-generated hypotheses, and application driven by scientific need rather than tool affordance.	arXiv:2605.08956v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: A growing body of work pursues AI scientists capable of end-to-end autonomous scientific discovery. This position paper argues that although they already function as co-scientists, agentic AI scientists are not built for autonomous scientific discovery. We identify the following challenges in building and deploying autonomous AI scientists: (1) Problem selection is influenced by the McNamara fallacy; (2) Agents are built on large language models (LLMs) whose training corpora omit tacit procedural and failure knowledge of laboratory practice; (3) Preference optimisation during post-training compresses output diversity toward consensus; and (4) Most scientific benchmarks measure single-turn prediction accuracy and lack feedback from physical experiments back to the computational model. These challenges are not just questions of scale and scaffolding; they require revisiting fundamental design choices. To build truly autonomous AI scientists, we recommend scientific simulations as verifiers for training, a persistent, mutable epistemic state that carries beliefs and shifting objectives across an investigation, the establishment of a centralized preregistration repository for all AI-generated hypotheses, and application driven by scientific need rather than tool affordance.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.357818+00	4	\N	7edb1425637463dd779dca85943c904f0eef7376608a9376ee7a388f67678ac6	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.358206+00	2026-11-10 16:00:01.124476+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
404f424c-8015-4bba-a45f-772ece8d6a75	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.12151v2	Distinct mechanisms underlying in-context learning in transformers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12151	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12151	Cole Gibson, Wenping Cui, Gautam Reddy	arXiv:2604.12151v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern distributed networks, notably transformers, acquire a remarkable ability (termed `in-context learning') to adapt their computation to input statistics, such that a fixed network can be applied to data from a broad range of systems. Here, we provide a complete mechanistic characterization of this behavior in transformers trained on a finite set $S$ of discrete Markov chains. The transformer displays four algorithmic phases, characterized by whether the network memorizes and generalizes, and whether it uses 1-point or 2-point statistics. We show that the four phases are implemented by multi-layer subcircuits that exemplify two qualitatively distinct mechanisms for implementing context-adaptive computations. Minimal models isolate the key features of both motifs. Memorization and generalization phases are delineated by two boundaries that depend on data diversity, $K = |S|$. The first ($K_1^\\ast$) is set by a kinetic competition between subcircuits and the second ($K_2^\\ast$) is set by a representational bottleneck. A symmetry-constrained theory of a transformer's training dynamics explains the sharp transition from 1-point to 2-point generalization and identifies key features of the loss landscape that allow the network to generalize. Put together, we show that transformers develop distinct subcircuits to implement in-context learning and identify conditions that favor certain mechanisms over others.	arXiv:2604.12151v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern distributed networks, notably transformers, acquire a remarkable ability (termed `in-context learning') to adapt their computation to input statistics, such that a fixed network can be applied to data from a broad range of systems. Here, we provide a complete mechanistic characterization of this behavior in transformers trained on a finite set $S$ of discrete Markov chains. The transformer displays four algorithmic phases, characterized by whether the network memorizes and generalizes, and whether it uses 1-point or 2-point statistics. We show that the four phases are implemented by multi-layer subcircuits that exemplify two qualitatively distinct mechanisms for implementing context-adaptive computations. Minimal models isolate the key features of both motifs. Memorization and generalization phases are delineated by two boundaries that depend on data diversity, $K = |S|$. The first ($K_1^\\ast$) is set by a kinetic competition between subcircuits and the second ($K_2^\\ast$) is set by a representational bottleneck. A symmetry-constrained theory of a transformer's training dynamics explains the sharp transition from 1-point to 2-point generalization and identifies key features of the loss landscape that allow the network to generalize. Put together, we show that transformers develop distinct subcircuits to implement in-context learning and identify conditions that favor certain mechanisms over others.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.078891+00	4	\N	401d31244c88374adfbd6a4f934d5586a47700f377ee709a7c2b8720b116a576	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.079794+00	2026-11-10 15:25:22.888776+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c6687c4a-45e3-4b4c-9d6f-9eb7b462e58c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.04908v3	Temporal Pair Consistency for Flow Matching	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04908	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04908	Chika Maduabuchi, Jindong Wang	arXiv:2602.04908v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Flow-matching-style generative models learn time-dependent vector fields, but standard objectives supervise each timestep independently, leaving shared-path temporal structure unused. We introduce Temporal Pair Consistency (TPC), a simple training-time objective that couples velocity predictions at paired timesteps along the same probability path. TPC leaves the architecture, probability path, sampler, and inference-time computation unchanged. Under matched backbones, training budgets, solvers, and numbers of function evaluations, TPC improves sample quality across flow matching, OT-CFM, rectified flow, DiT-style backbones, and MeanFlow. On CIFAR-10, TPC improves FM from 6.35 to 3.19 FID and OT-CFM from 3.58 to 2.90 FID at the same NFE. Mechanism diagnostics show lower measured gradient variance, higher paired-gradient correlation, and reduced temporal roughness, while random and local pairing controls are substantially weaker. Higher-resolution ImageNet experiments further show consistent gains for DiT-XL/2 at 256 and 512 resolution and for MeanFlow at 256 resolution, all at matched inference cost.	arXiv:2602.04908v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Flow-matching-style generative models learn time-dependent vector fields, but standard objectives supervise each timestep independently, leaving shared-path temporal structure unused. We introduce Temporal Pair Consistency (TPC), a simple training-time objective that couples velocity predictions at paired timesteps along the same probability path. TPC leaves the architecture, probability path, sampler, and inference-time computation unchanged. Under matched backbones, training budgets, solvers, and numbers of function evaluations, TPC improves sample quality across flow matching, OT-CFM, rectified flow, DiT-style backbones, and MeanFlow. On CIFAR-10, TPC improves FM from 6.35 to 3.19 FID and OT-CFM from 3.58 to 2.90 FID at the same NFE. Mechanism diagnostics show lower measured gradient variance, higher paired-gradient correlation, and reduced temporal roughness, while random and local pairing controls are substantially weaker. Higher-resolution ImageNet experiments further show consistent gains for DiT-XL/2 at 256 and 512 resolution and for MeanFlow at 256 resolution, all at matched inference cost.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.03743+00	3	\N	92e6d18537f6d87804f66104d1c908406f1d8f7958474dc2d66c723f7d0a0f52	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.038132+00	2026-11-10 15:25:19.836922+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2d8fd04b-65df-4b21-800a-120c8ae0b805	5d593eb7-b3c4-4600-aefa-56c14415c194	\N	https://huggingface.co/blog/train-to-paint-with-code	Training a coding model to paint watercolours with TRL and OpenEnv	https://huggingface.co/blog/train-to-paint-with-code	https://huggingface.co/blog/train-to-paint-with-code	\N	\N	\N	tutorial	en	2026-09-03 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:36.157731+00	3	\N	1a10eec3345dd344872306e043682dcb143a0db5371a3219e0377d523611d9ad	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:36.158139+00	2026-11-10 13:30:30.956003+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
983f572e-43cc-4663-877d-9be56b8e98b7	5d593eb7-b3c4-4600-aefa-56c14415c194	\N	https://huggingface.co/blog/allenai/benchmirt	BenchMIRT: What are LLM benchmarks actually measuring?	https://huggingface.co/blog/allenai/benchmirt	https://huggingface.co/blog/allenai/benchmirt	\N	\N	\N	research	en	2026-09-01 21:39:07+00	2026-11-09 17:12:36.159606+00	3	\N	b9622c1b1625e9d4de24e25cd23508472dcc3fc64ddb50f2646ebd1d899c044c	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:36.159966+00	2026-11-10 13:30:33.518263+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0f5af895-0d07-46fc-9669-31fd62f127f0	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.08802v3	Improving Generalization Robustness of Multimodal RLVR	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08802	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08802	Pengfei Zhou, Zhiwei Tang, Chenrui Zhou, Lama Moukheiber, Yixing Ma, Bin Xu, Jiajun Song, Zhenglin Wan, Wangbo Zhao, Jiasheng Tang, Bohan Zhuang, Fan Wang, Yang You	arXiv:2608.08802v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) makes Multimodal Large Language Models more accurate, but the gains are brittle: simply paraphrasing a question or changing the prompt template can degrade them, which challenges reliable deployment in high-stakes scenarios like medical VQA. We trace this to two issues of the standard RL objective. First, the binary verifier conflates format with content, so the reward signal cannot tell a wrong answer apart from a misformatted one. Second, the training distribution covers only a thin slice of the real-world prompts that the model might meet at deployment, so policies that perform well on the training distribution can behave differently under unseen prompts during test. Both failures call for a robust post-training method that helps the policy cover a broader distribution of semantically equivalent prompts, and we identify two measures that help achieve this objective: separating format from semantics in the reward, and applying policy invariance across perturbed prompts with equivalent semantics. We therefore propose Prompt-Invariant RLVR (PIRL), consisting of a dynamic trinary reward and a consistency regularizer based on an embedding-space adversary. Under stress testing, PIRL's average accuracy on benchmarks drops by only $\\le 1\\%$, where GRPO drops ~3%. On dynamic evaluation, PIRL also achieves the smallest performance drop.	arXiv:2608.08802v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) makes Multimodal Large Language Models more accurate, but the gains are brittle: simply paraphrasing a question or changing the prompt template can degrade them, which challenges reliable deployment in high-stakes scenarios like medical VQA. We trace this to two issues of the standard RL objective. First, the binary verifier conflates format with content, so the reward signal cannot tell a wrong answer apart from a misformatted one. Second, the training distribution covers only a thin slice of the real-world prompts that the model might meet at deployment, so policies that perform well on the training distribution can behave differently under unseen prompts during test. Both failures call for a robust post-training method that helps the policy cover a broader distribution of semantically equivalent prompts, and we identify two measures that help achieve this objective: separating format from semantics in the reward, and applying policy invariance across perturbed prompts with equivalent semantics. We therefore propose Prompt-Invariant RLVR (PIRL), consisting of a dynamic trinary reward and a consistency regularizer based on an embedding-space adversary. Under stress testing, PIRL's average accuracy on benchmarks drops by only $\\le 1\\%$, where GRPO drops ~3%. On dynamic evaluation, PIRL also achieves the smallest performance drop.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.401665+00	4	\N	eb5259f51db52891029eece717b98d3ad06245df7040ce78f39c755eb105de4c	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.402112+00	2026-11-10 16:00:51.441232+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
76b8e732-251e-401d-b023-92ddecce9853	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.19429v2	A Comprehensive Comparison of Reinforcement Learning Algorithms in Autonomous Driving	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19429	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19429	Dechen Xie, Mushuang Liu	arXiv:2610.19429v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive comparison of four representative Reinforcement Learning (RL) methods in autonomous vehicle (AV) decision-making, including Fitted Value Iteration (FVI), Rollout Classification Policy Iteration (RCPI), Deep Q-Network (DQN), and a discrete-action adaptation of Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (D-DDPG). The four methods are applied to the same AV decision-making problem at an unsignalized intersection and are compared in terms of optimality, safety, training stability, and training and decision cost. Observations suggest that RCPI provides the closest estimation to the exact solution and has the most stable training curve; however, it is data-intensive and requires the largest volume of data. D-DDPG is the most data-efficient one, but at the price of the training stability. Based on the observations, we provide suggestions for the algorithm selection for AV decision making.	arXiv:2610.19429v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive comparison of four representative Reinforcement Learning (RL) methods in autonomous vehicle (AV) decision-making, including Fitted Value Iteration (FVI), Rollout Classification Policy Iteration (RCPI), Deep Q-Network (DQN), and a discrete-action adaptation of Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (D-DDPG). The four methods are applied to the same AV decision-making problem at an unsignalized intersection and are compared in terms of optimality, safety, training stability, and training and decision cost. Observations suggest that RCPI provides the closest estimation to the exact solution and has the most stable training curve; however, it is data-intensive and requires the largest volume of data. D-DDPG is the most data-efficient one, but at the price of the training stability. Based on the observations, we provide suggestions for the algorithm selection for AV decision making.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.27477+00	3	\N	353366b6a5ae4d6663387db16ba76b7944e60d39a2bfd43173d5def8f476426e	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.275193+00	2026-11-10 15:29:30.975957+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cff24868-ab0a-4ca5-a881-0737df48d643	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.20585v4	Learnable Hodge-Laplacian Driven Mixture of Experts for Steady-PDE Surrogates on Coarse Unstructured Meshes	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20585	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20585	Stavros Nousias, Akis Nousias, Konstantinos Gkrispanis	arXiv:2610.20585v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Surrogate models approximate the solutions of partial differential equations given a certain geometry and boundary conditions. Traditional transformer-based approaches employ a single mixing mechanism to the whole solution. although the solution fields exhibit variations across multiple spatial scales. We propose a mixture of transformer experts that combines a routing mechanism with multiscale supervision. Specifically, the individual experts are routed through a controller that utilizes learnable Laplacian matrices to compute weights that are subsequently used to define a weighted sum of the experts for the output feature vectors at each point of the domain. Subsequently, a decoder receives the weighted sum of the experts output and predicts the solution fields. During training, the reference fields are decomposed into components at different spatial scales by repeated smoothing with a mesh Laplacian. We evaluate our approach on the official AirFRANS dataset using remeshed solution domains leading to unstructured triangular meshes of $4.9$k vertices. The experimental outcomes reveal lower mean relative $L_2$ error and interior mean squared errors among the compared models of this study.	arXiv:2610.20585v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Surrogate models approximate the solutions of partial differential equations given a certain geometry and boundary conditions. Traditional transformer-based approaches employ a single mixing mechanism to the whole solution. although the solution fields exhibit variations across multiple spatial scales. We propose a mixture of transformer experts that combines a routing mechanism with multiscale supervision. Specifically, the individual experts are routed through a controller that utilizes learnable Laplacian matrices to compute weights that are subsequently used to define a weighted sum of the experts for the output feature vectors at each point of the domain. Subsequently, a decoder receives the weighted sum of the experts output and predicts the solution fields. During training, the reference fields are decomposed into components at different spatial scales by repeated smoothing with a mesh Laplacian. We evaluate our approach on the official AirFRANS dataset using remeshed solution domains leading to unstructured triangular meshes of $4.9$k vertices. The experimental outcomes reveal lower mean relative $L_2$ error and interior mean squared errors among the compared models of this study.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.280796+00	3	\N	792b226e15d7c41db34b00f8cb650e269d0b5edab362f67178cec1efb9556a33	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.281248+00	2026-11-10 15:29:38.179802+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a930430c-f99a-433d-85f5-d3517cdb4c01	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.34590v2	Sparse Sum-of-Squares Layers for Certified Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34590	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34590	Ege Gursoy, Nicolas Mansard	arXiv:2610.34590v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: A central limitation of modern machine learning systems is the lack of formal certificates guaranteeing their behavior beyond empirical, sample-based evaluation. Sum-of-squares (SOS) methods provide such guarantees by certifying polynomial inequalities over continuous domains via semidefinite programming (SDP). While prior work has introduced SDP layers as differentiable components in neural networks, repeated learning calls can lose the computational benefit of the forward program's sparse representation. We propose Sparse SOS Layers, differentiable value layers whose backward passes reuse the local coordinates of the forward solve. Under a fixed template and learned objective coefficients, each layer avoids building dense certificates and reuses its sparse structure across training steps. Compared with existing methods, our approach achieves up to 2.7 times speedup in total warm-call time, with nearly 100 times lower memory usage (1.7 vs. 160 GiB) for relatively large problem sizes for the tested dense parameterization. Experiments cover sparse polynomial certification, pendulum control, combinatorial relaxations, robotics projection, oscillator chains, and pose graph synchronization. A design study achieves zero certified violations across unseen problem instances and sizes, while requiring roughly 7 times fewer optimization solves than estimating gradients by perturbing the objective coefficients and re-solving the problem (finite differences). These results show that sparse certificate values can be effectively integrated into learning pipelines when the backward pass respects the verifier's structure.	arXiv:2610.34590v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: A central limitation of modern machine learning systems is the lack of formal certificates guaranteeing their behavior beyond empirical, sample-based evaluation. Sum-of-squares (SOS) methods provide such guarantees by certifying polynomial inequalities over continuous domains via semidefinite programming (SDP). While prior work has introduced SDP layers as differentiable components in neural networks, repeated learning calls can lose the computational benefit of the forward program's sparse representation. We propose Sparse SOS Layers, differentiable value layers whose backward passes reuse the local coordinates of the forward solve. Under a fixed template and learned objective coefficients, each layer avoids building dense certificates and reuses its sparse structure across training steps. Compared with existing methods, our approach achieves up to 2.7 times speedup in total warm-call time, with nearly 100 times lower memory usage (1.7 vs. 160 GiB) for relatively large problem sizes for the tested dense parameterization. Experiments cover sparse polynomial certification, pendulum control, combinatorial relaxations, robotics projection, oscillator chains, and pose graph synchronization. A design study achieves zero certified violations across unseen problem instances and sizes, while requiring roughly 7 times fewer optimization solves than estimating gradients by perturbing the objective coefficients and re-solving the problem (finite differences). These results show that sparse certificate values can be effectively integrated into learning pipelines when the backward pass respects the verifier's structure.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.284149+00	4	\N	43bfd63d1a5676a893dbc2f39609135c0ed0c0360c3e25a0b654e329eae12f7c	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.285104+00	2026-11-10 15:29:41.354493+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b3534d50-34c2-430c-a16f-f66b3aee97bc	d58a9c86-f9be-4f8b-bd34-60f063fd344d	\N	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/10/prompting-claude-fable-52/	Quoting Prompting Claude Fable 5.2	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/10/prompting-claude-fable-52/	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/10/prompting-claude-fable-52/	\N	Claude Fable 5.2 responds closely to what the prompt says about who owns the code it works on. In Anthropic’s testing on small coding tasks, when a prompt said nothing about ownership or presented the code base as someone else’s, the model tended to add to the existing structure, even when that repeated existing code. [...] If you want the model to improve the structure as it works, say that the code is its own: This codebase is yours: what it's for and how it's built are your call, and you'll keep building on it. In Anthropic’s testing on small coding tasks, the model merged repeated code in about half of the tries with this sentence, and in under a tenth of the tries without it. — Prompting Claude Fable 5.2 , Anthropic documentation Tags: prompt-engineering , anthropic , generative-ai , ai , llms , claude-mythos-fable	Claude Fable 5.2 responds closely to what the prompt says about who owns the code it works on. In Anthropic’s testing on small coding tasks, when a prompt said nothing about ownership or presented the code base as someone else’s, the model tended to add to the existing structure, even when that repeated existing code. [...] If you want the model to improve the structure as it works, say that the code is its own: This codebase is yours: what it's for and how it's built are your call, and you'll keep building on it. In Anthropic’s testing on small coding tasks, the model merged repeated code in about half of the tries with this sentence, and in under a tenth of the tries without it. — Prompting Claude Fable 5.2 , Anthropic documentation Tags: prompt-engineering , anthropic , generative-ai , ai , llms , claude-mythos-fable	article	en	2026-11-10 05:3