dc2c3c1a-0889-4bea-b3b8-cb6b0c1b012e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10429v1	Applied bifidelity material response model construction and uncertainty quantification with a sampling hypernetwork	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10429	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10429	Reese E. Jones, Cosmin Safta, Dan Bolintineau, Craig M. Hamel, Gianluca Geraci, Sharlotte LB Kramer	arXiv:2611.10429v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Multifidelity modeling offers a practical route to accurate prediction and uncertainty quantification when high-fidelity data are scarce but lower-fidelity data are comparatively abundant. This work develops a bifidelity Langevin sampling hypernetwork framework that constructs probabilistic neu- ral response models by learning a posterior distribution over model parameters rather than a single deterministic fit. The method combines a flexible shared-latent data representation for low- and high-fidelity responses, enabling parameter sharing and transfer learning between the fidelities. We introduce several methodological enhancements to improve the robustness and efficiency of this ap- proach, including stability-promoting drift models, staged and conditional training strategies, adap- tive initialization of Langevin trajectories, and training procedures designed to better exploit corre- lations between fidelity levels. These additions improve convergence behavior, reduce the sampling effort required to obtain representative posterior ensembles, and provide more reliable predictive uncertainty in data-limited regimes. The resulting framework makes more effective use of available multifidelity data while preserving interpretability through the learned posterior structure, yielding improved predictive performance, better uncertainty characterization, and a more stable workflow for constructing uncertainty-aware surrogate models. The method is demonstrated on experimental, high-fidelity simulation and illustrative synthetic data.	arXiv:2611.10429v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Multifidelity modeling offers a practical route to accurate prediction and uncertainty quantification when high-fidelity data are scarce but lower-fidelity data are comparatively abundant. This work develops a bifidelity Langevin sampling hypernetwork framework that constructs probabilistic neu- ral response models by learning a posterior distribution over model parameters rather than a single deterministic fit. The method combines a flexible shared-latent data representation for low- and high-fidelity responses, enabling parameter sharing and transfer learning between the fidelities. We introduce several methodological enhancements to improve the robustness and efficiency of this ap- proach, including stability-promoting drift models, staged and conditional training strategies, adap- tive initialization of Langevin trajectories, and training procedures designed to better exploit corre- lations between fidelity levels. These additions improve convergence behavior, reduce the sampling effort required to obtain representative posterior ensembles, and provide more reliable predictive uncertainty in data-limited regimes. The resulting framework makes more effective use of available multifidelity data while preserving interpretability through the learned posterior structure, yielding improved predictive performance, better uncertainty characterization, and a more stable workflow for constructing uncertainty-aware surrogate models. The method is demonstrated on experimental, high-fidelity simulation and illustrative synthetic data.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.688957+00	\N	\N	6dd12862e192cdb1dfa417f7a4901ddf50ac56e8edf494f2419bc7d73ab1ee25	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:01.689324+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.689325+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d6e8980f-d7fd-4508-9989-eec53e601bde	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/scaling-ai-for-everyone	Scaling AI for everyone	https://openai.com/index/scaling-ai-for-everyone	https://openai.com/index/scaling-ai-for-everyone	\N	Today we’re announcing $110B in new investment at a $730B pre money valuation. This includes $30B from SoftBank, $30B from NVIDIA, and $50B from Amazon.	Today we’re announcing $110B in new investment at a $730B pre money valuation. This includes $30B from SoftBank, $30B from NVIDIA, and $50B from Amazon.	news	en	2026-02-27 05:30:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:43.07451+00	5	\N	4fcd62bba68730a0693331f662414f3368a49187689ed3886bcd2c17debafb3a	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:43.074879+00	2026-11-10 14:16:40.79265+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
abf2db88-282c-4382-94fe-30c496986061	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10468v1	Dynamic State Estimation from Sparse PMUs via Physics-Informed Graph Neural Operators	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10468	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10468	Huy Hoang Le, Haoguang Wang, Christian Moya, Marcos Netto, Guang Lin	arXiv:2611.10468v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: This paper proposes a physics-informed graph neural operator for power system dynamic state estimation. From a sparse, intermittent measurement window at a few generator buses, the operator estimates the rotor-angle and speed trajectories of all machines, unmeasured ones included, in one forward pass. A graph-attention encoder processes the measurements, and the swing equations are imposed as a hard constraint or as a soft one with a learned correction that absorbs model errors. It is trained on simulated measurements, without state labels. With several phasor measurement units, masked training hides one from the input but keeps it in the loss. On four transmission systems, simulations show that the operator is the most accurate estimator that neither optimizes per scenario nor uses state labels, except on the smallest system at large model errors, where tuned Kalman smoothers are better. Networks trained on simulated states are more accurate on the two smallest systems but not on the 118-bus system, where the operator is faster and more accurate than hard-constrained moving-horizon estimation. Under model errors, it matches or outperforms an optimization-based benchmark at a fraction of its cost. The results hold for a detailed machine model with exact parameters seen only through voltage phasors.	arXiv:2611.10468v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: This paper proposes a physics-informed graph neural operator for power system dynamic state estimation. From a sparse, intermittent measurement window at a few generator buses, the operator estimates the rotor-angle and speed trajectories of all machines,