ebbbb94b-00e8-4b30-a121-450da68e5e5e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11279v1	How Efficient Is Your Agent? Online Predictive Bounds under Budget Constraints	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11279	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11279	Roy Maor Lotan, Shai Feldman, Yaniv Romano	arXiv:2611.11279v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We evaluate AI agents in an online setting where tasks arrive sequentially and, before running each task, we construct an upper predictive bound on its \\emph{time-to-success}---the number of interactions until the task is completed successfully. Under a limited budget, some runs are stopped early, so their time-to-success is unobserved (right-censored), and the correctness of the bound may be unknown. We introduce \\emph{\\textbf{PR}edictive Bounds for \\textbf{O}nline \\textbf{B}udget \\textbf{E}fficiency} (\\texttt{PROBE}), a method for constructing calibrated upper predictive bounds while adaptively allocating a limited budget across agent tasks to reduce the anticipated coverage deficit. Our method calibrates the bounds online using inverse-probability-weighted feedback together with a correction designed to minimize coverage deficit while remaining unbiased. We prove that \\texttt{PROBE} provides a coverage guarantee for any finite time horizon while satisfying the budget constraint. Experiments on software engineering, retrieval-augmented generation, and instruction following show that \\texttt{PROBE} attains a coverage rate closer to the target level with tighter predictive bounds than a calibrated baseline.	arXiv:2611.11279v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We evaluate AI agents in an online setting where tasks arrive sequentially and, before running each task, we construct an upper predictive bound on its \\emph{time-to-success}---the number of interactions until the task is completed successfully. Under a limited budget, some runs are stopped early, so their time-to-success is unobserved (right-censored), and the correctness of the bound may be unknown. We introduce \\emph{\\textbf{PR}edictive Bounds for \\textbf{O}nline \\textbf{B}udget \\textbf{E}fficiency} (\\texttt{PROBE}), a method for constructing calibrated upper predictive bounds while adaptively allocating a limited budget across agent tasks to reduce the anticipated coverage deficit. Our method calibrates the bounds online using inverse-probability-weighted feedback together with a correction designed to minimize coverage deficit while remaining unbiased. We prove that \\texttt{PROBE} provides a coverage guarantee for any finite time horizon while satisfying the budget constraint. Experiments on software engineering, retrieval-augmented generation, and instruction following show that \\texttt{PROBE} attains a coverage rate closer to the target level with tighter predictive bounds than a calibrated baseline.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.9123+00	\N	\N	2e3cb88456c66cdd978f5fb25da992048c029d62cd525218ce198bcccb91151b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:01.912628+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.91263+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
92ea04d9-7f0c-49f3-87a1-08e3b693886a	c9402267-ab70-45fa-b843-f47b6189bd14	\N	https://research.google/blog/where-wild-things-roam-identifying-wildlife-with-speciesnet/	Where wild things roam: Identifying wildlife with SpeciesNet	https://research.google/blog/where-wild-things-roam-identifying-wildlife-with-speciesnet/	https://research.google/blog/where-wild-things-roam-identifying-wildlife-with-speciesnet/	\N	Climate & Sustainability	Climate & Sustainability	research	en	2026-03-06 17:59:38+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.17097+00	4	\N	5cee2b83e280afa70fe89957b5b142c0eee290553b72ae6320a7e84b8dd4f1c0	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.171456+00	2026-11-10 14:17:41.887383+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d8c04032-55fa-4a55-8ec6-dff7e1200456	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11313v1	Shape-Adaptive Subspace-Constrained Quadratic Matrix Factorization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11313	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11313	Zheng Zhai, Zhuan Liang, Sujun Guo	arXiv:2611.11313v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Quadratic manifold fitting provides a flexible framework for modeling nonlinear low-dimensional structures embedded in high-dimensional spaces. However, existing methods typically employ prescribed noise models or fixed loss functions, limiting their adaptability to varying noise characteristics. To address this limitation, we propose an alternating maximum likelihood estimation method for subspace-constrained quadratic matrix factorization, termed AMLE-SQMF, which jointly estimates the quadratic manifold and the underlying noise distribution. Specifically, we model the residuals using a generalized Gaussian distribution (GGD), whose shape parameter controls the tail behavior and determines the associated fitting loss. By exploiting the closed-form maximum likelihood estimator of the scale parameter, we eliminate the scale variable and derive a profile negative log-likelihood. Based on this formulation, we develop an alternating optimization algorithm that iteratively updates the manifold parameters and estimates the noise shape parameter. This enables adaptive loss selection within a unified likelihood-based framework, without requiring prior specification of the noise characteristics. Experiments on synthetic and real-world datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of AMLE-SQMF in quadratic manifold fitting and its robustness to diverse noise distributions.	arXiv:2611.11313v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Quadratic manifold fitting provides a flexible framework for modeling nonlinear low-dimensional structures embedded in high-dimensional spaces. However, existing methods typically employ prescribed noise models or fixed loss functions, limiting their adaptability to varying noise characteristics. To address this limitation, we propose an alternating maximum likelihood estimation method for subspace-constrained quadratic matrix factorization, termed AMLE-SQMF, which jointly estimates the quadratic manifold and the underlying noise distribution. Specifically, we model the residuals using a generalized Gaussian distribution (GGD), whose shape parameter controls the tail behavior and determines the associated fitting loss. By exploiting the closed-form maximum likelihood estimator of the scale parameter, we eliminate the scale variable and derive a profile negative log-likelihood. Based on this formulation, we develop an alternating optimization algorithm that iteratively updates the manifold parameters and estimates the noise shape parameter. This enables adaptive loss selection within a unified likelihood-based framework, without requiring prior specification of the noise characteristics. Experiments on synthetic and real-world datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of AMLE-SQMF in quadratic manifold fitting and its robustness to diverse noise distributions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.916645+00	\N	\N	e5543a593007e25dcc1b8dfe1c74a16946c055652d818f51ad1683b9c29cb9eb	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:01.916957+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.916958+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2a019b64-38ba-4a09-a468-bd06312b2adf	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11324v1	When Sensors Fail: Coverage Auditing with Incomplete Sensor Records	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11324	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11324	Chung-I Huang, Wen-Yi Chang	arXiv:2611.11324v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Sensor outages can conceal prediction-interval coverage deficits behind acceptable marginal performance, while unavailable future readings leave some issued forecasts unscorable. We present a measurement-record audit that links issue-time outage duration, original target availability, and time-uniform statistical evidence. The audit retains complete forecast keys, batches all sensors and overlapping horizons at each measurement tick, and projects simultaneous covered-count and scored-count bounds onto a cumulative conditional expected coverage ratio. This target is distinguished from empirical coverage and outage-conditional population coverage. Selected traffic and air-quality results illustrate the gap between pooled and long-outage coverage; in METR-LA, only 7.6% of issued long-gap forecasts have originally observed targets. Of 30 primary sequential outage-state audits, 26 are inconclusive and four lack scored support. This concise presentation gives the core construction and selected aggregate evidence without the full experimental supplement.	arXiv:2611.11324v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Sensor outages can conceal prediction-interval coverage deficits behind acceptable marginal performance, while unavailable future readings leave some issued forecasts unscorable. We present a measurement-record audit that links issue-time outage duration, original target availability, and time-uniform statistical evidence. The audit retains complete forecast keys, batches all sensors and overlapping horizons at each measurement tick, and projects simultaneous covered-count and scored-count bounds onto a cumulative conditional expected coverage ratio. This target is distinguished from empirical coverage and outage-conditional population coverage. Selected traffic and air-quality results illustrate the gap between pooled and long-outage coverage; in METR-LA, only 7.6% of issued long-gap forecasts have originally observed targets. Of 30 primary sequential outage-state audits, 26 are inconclusive and four lack scored support. This concise presentation gives the core construction and selected aggregate evidence without the full experimental supplement.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.920905+00	\N	\N	a03c364e73e2a874d72609855aa832e71f7275670ec3d49fbeea678eaaa8846e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:01.92122+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.921222+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bcd8ccdc-b90c-48fa-9e4a-644c7e3ac40f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11330v1	When Credit Turns Against Tools: Transition-Aware Credit Assignment in Agentic Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11330	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11330	Xiangyu Lu, Zhanke Zhou, Jiazhe Ning, Chentao Cao, Jiangchao Yao, Bo Han	arXiv:2611.11330v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Agentic reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards trains language models through multi-turn tool interactions, yet their reasoning performance and tool-calling ability can decline during training. We trace this degradation to three coupled mechanisms: (1) tool-token imbalance produces negative overall tool credit when incorrect trajectories contain more tool tokens per turn than correct ones; (2) aggregation amplification can further strengthen this negative signal as token-mean aggregation gives longer incorrect trajectories greater weight in policy updates; and (3) failure-driven retries prolong trajectories as models tend to call tools again after execution errors, extending potential negative credit to recovery attempts. To counter this suppression, we propose Transition-Aware Credit Assignment (TACA), which targets the transition from reasoning to tool use through supervision of both call initiation and generation. Specifically, TACA uses Advantage Projection to enforce a nonnegative mean advantage on tool-call tokens within each prompt group through a minimal uniform shift. Additionally, TACA guides tool-use decisions with Decision Contrast, comparing rewards for tool-assisted and direct answers to the same prompt. Through Supervised Replay, it reinforces tool-call generation using calls from previously sampled correct trajectories. Across models and tasks, TACA improves performance and sustains more reliable tool interaction during training, raising mean accuracy over MT-GRPO on six reasoning benchmarks by 4.61 percentage points for Qwen2.5-1.5B and 3.61 for Qwen2.5-3B. Our code is available at https://github.com/tmlr-group/TACA.	arXiv:2611.11330v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Agentic reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards trains language models through multi-turn tool interactions, yet their reasoning performance and tool-calling ability can decline during training. We trace this degradation to three coupled mechanisms: (1) tool-token imbalance produces negative overall tool credit when incorrect trajectories contain more tool tokens per turn than correct ones; (2) aggregation amplification can further strengthen this negative signal as token-mean aggregation gives longer incorrect trajectories greater weight in policy updates; and (3) failure-driven retries prolong trajectories as models tend to call tools again after execution errors, extending potential negative credit to recovery attempts. To counter this suppression, we propose Transition-Aware Credit Assignment (TACA), which targets the transition from reasoning to tool use through supervision of both call initiation and generation. Specifically, TACA uses Advantage Projection to enforce a nonnegative mean advantage on tool-call tokens within each prompt group through a minimal uniform shift. Additionally, TACA guides tool-use decisions with Decision Contrast, comparing rewards for tool-assisted and direct answers to the same prompt. Through Supervised Replay, it reinforces tool-call generation using calls from previously sampled correct trajectories. Across models and tasks, TACA improves performance and sustains more reliable tool interaction during training, raising mean accuracy over MT-GRPO on six reasoning benchmarks by 4.61 percentage points for Qwen2.5-1.5B and 3.61 for Qwen2.5-3B. Our code is available at https://github.com/tmlr-group/TACA.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.92289+00	\N	\N	396461c6eabb299d4f2b7ffeaf523b3c936adb9fb1408053dc8afbde09306beb	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:01.923229+00	2026-11-10 16:30:01.923231+00	pending	\N	\N