17d03ec2-ab47-4266-bf97-1137a69f0193	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10345v1	The Power of Two Choices in Dynamic Pricing: As Good As Full Feedback, Instance-Wise	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10345	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10345	Yan Dai, Tao Lin, Aleksandrs Slivkins	arXiv:2611.10345v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study the dynamic pricing problem, where a firm sells a product to $T$ myopic customers and their values are independent samples from an unknown value distribution $F$. With posted prices, the firm only sees whether each customer buys, and the minimax regret is $\\widetilde \\Theta(T^{2/3})$ [KL03]. A menu instead offers several options, each selling the product with some probability at some expected payment. Infinitely many menu options allow the seller to fully reveal customer values, reducing the regret to $\\Theta(\\sqrt T)$. How many options are needed?\n  Perhaps surprisingly, a second menu option suffices, achieving the same regret as infinite menus (up to $\\log$ factors). This holds broadly: without knowing any property of $F$, our algorithm with two menu options attains $\\tilde O(\\sqrt T)$ regret in general, $\\tilde O(T^{1/3})$ regret for monotone-hazard-rate distributions, and $\\tilde O(T^{d/(d+1)})$ instance-dependent regret when $d\\in(0,1)$ is a suitable notion of "dimension". All are optimal even with infinite menus. Moreover, we argue, via a novel lower-bound construction, that our algorithm is near-optimal for each problem instance.\n  Along the way, we study the query complexity of revenue maximization, where an algorithm issues a query to each customer to learn the property of interest (either the best price or the CDF) up to some precision. We show that interval queries suffice to achieve the same query complexity as value queries (up to $\\log$ factors) for both properties and across several classes of value distributions. We provide a new interval-query algorithm for each property. The best-price-estimation algorithm is the key ingredient in our main dynamic pricing algorithm, and the CDF-estimation algorithm underpins a simpler algorithm attaining $\\tilde O(\\sqrt T)$ regret with three menu options.	arXiv:2611.10345v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study the dynamic pricing problem, where a firm sells a product to $T$ myopic customers and their values are independent samples from an unknown value distribution $F$. With posted prices, the firm only sees whether each customer buys, and the minimax regret is $\\widetilde \\Theta(T^{2/3})$ [KL03]. A menu instead offers several options, each selling the product with some probability at some expected payment. Infinitely many menu options allow the seller to fully reveal customer values, reducing the regret to $\\Theta(\\sqrt T)$. How many options are needed?\n  Perhaps surprisingly, a second menu option suffices, achieving the same regret as infinite menus (up to $\\log$ factors). This holds broadly: without knowing any property of $F$, our algorithm with two menu options attains $\\tilde O(\\sqrt T)$ regret in general, $\\tilde O(T^{1/3})$ regret for monotone-hazard-rate distributions, and $\\tilde O(T^{d/(d+1)})$ instance-dependent regret when $d\\in(0,1)$ is a suitable notion of "dimension". All are optimal even with infinite menus. Moreover, we argue, via a novel lower-bound construction, that our algorithm is near-optimal for each problem instance.\n  Along the way, we study the query complexity of revenue maximization, where an algorithm issues a query to each customer to learn the property of interest (either the best price or the CDF) up to some precision. We show that interval queries suffice to achieve the same query complexity as value queries (up to $\\log$ factors) for both properties and across several classes of value distributions. We provide a new interval-query algorithm for each property. The best-price-estimation algorithm is the key ingredient in our main dynamic pricing algorithm, and the CDF-estimation algorithm underpins a simpler algorithm attaining $\\tilde O(\\sqrt T)$ regret with three menu options.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.353815+00	\N	\N	fab08dd7483b2ba45e19796251cf9949a67060011db2ec84a1e530a819139305	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.354125+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.354127+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a9307764-e986-4c38-b402-7c6902397cbc	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10359v1	Spiking Sensory Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10359	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10359	Lleyton Ariton	arXiv:2611.10359v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Effective sensory reception is critical for survival, yet the computational mechanisms behind how peripheral sensory neurons (SNs) transduce external signals into spiking representations for downstream brain circuits remain to be fully understood. Numerous theoretical frameworks have been proposed to model sensory processing, with Bayesian approaches among the most prominent. However, existing machine learning attempts to apply Bayesian theory to spiking neurons remain largely phenomenological, disconnected from empirical biology, and poorly validated on non-stationary temporal data. Conversely, neuroscientific models often rely on strong inductive experimental biases or explicit kinetic dynamics fit post hoc to match spiking data. Here, we introduce a class of recurrent Poisson VAE models, termed Spiking Sensory Networks (SSNs), that bridge these paradigms by reconciling variational inference with biologically inspired spike transduction for time-varying stimuli. Evaluated on real-world odor plume recordings from locust olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs), SSNs match or exceed the reconstruction fidelity of Spiking Neural Network (SNN) and GLM baselines while exhibiting superior representational characteristics. Furthermore, SSNs are well-suited for neuroscientific investigation, learning empirical in vivo ORN response motifs and exhibiting emergent spike-frequency adaptation under unsupervised reconstruction without manual tuning. Moreover, we theoretically link the recurrent decoding objective to mutual information maximization, and derive a formal thermodynamic lower bound connecting the Poisson Kullback-Leibler regularizer to the excess non-equilibrium metabolic power of biophysical homeostasis. Ultimately, our work synthesizes principles from machine learning theory and empirical neuroscience, providing a viable bridge that concretizes Bayesian models with biological mechanisms.	arXiv:2611.10359v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Effective sensory reception is critical for survival, yet the computational mechanisms behind how peripheral sensory neurons (SNs) transduce external signals into spiking representations for downstream brain circuits remain to be fully understood. Numerous theoretical frameworks have been proposed to model sensory processing, with Bayesian approaches among the most prominent. However, existing machine learning attempts to apply Bayesian theory to spiking neurons remain largely phenomenological, disconnected from empirical biology, and poorly validated on non-stationary temporal data. Conversely, neuroscientific models often rely on strong inductive experimental biases or explicit kinetic dynamics fit post hoc to match spiking data. Here, we introduce a class of recurrent Poisson VAE models, termed Spiking Sensory Networks (SSNs), that bridge these paradigms by reconciling variational inference with biologically inspired spike transduction for time-varying stimuli. Evaluated on real-world odor plume recordings from locust olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs), SSNs match or exceed the reconstruction fidelity of Spiking Neural Network (SNN) and GLM baselines while exhibiting superior representational characteristics. Furthermore, SSNs are well-suited for neuroscientific investigation, learning empirical in vivo ORN response motifs and exhibiting emergent spike-frequency adaptation under unsupervised reconstruction without manual tuning. Moreover, we theoretically link the recurrent decoding objective to mutual information maximization, and derive a formal thermodynamic lower bound connecting the Poisson Kullback-Leibler regularizer to the excess non-equilibrium metabolic power of biophysical homeostasis. Ultimately, our work synthesizes principles from machine learning theory and empirical neuroscience, providing a viable bridge that concretizes Bayesian models with biological mechanisms.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.363193+00	\N	\N	7b16d5e9d752f890d2d907ec6a05365105cbcc9437653dce660f2d0be39147fb	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.363508+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.363509+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
