f48fa933-bf1f-4d45-ad74-4e3cd2f89580	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/ai-agent-link-safety	Keeping your data safe when an AI agent clicks a link	https://openai.com/index/ai-agent-link-safety	https://openai.com/index/ai-agent-link-safety	\N	Learn how OpenAI protects user data when AI agents open links, preventing URL-based data exfiltration and prompt injection with built-in safeguards.	Learn how OpenAI protects user data when AI agents open links, preventing URL-based data exfiltration and prompt injection with built-in safeguards.	product_update	en	2026-01-28 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:43.191146+00	4	\N	a9440ce347c4c840365b7a85b05f6bac5254dac58e790fc4915390025744ca3a	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:43.191591+00	2026-11-10 14:14:27.501793+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
94f27d77-e2d6-4b04-b887-8976dfe56b9e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11291v1	Multi-Node Transformers for Object Detection and Captioning Across Distributed Edge Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11291	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11291	Saurabh Kumar, Dewant Katare, Prakriti Jain	arXiv:2611.11291v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: This paper presents a unified transformer-based framework for object detection and dense region captioning across distributed edge networks. The architecture uses a shared visual encoder followed by detection, region extraction, and autoregressive language-decoding stages, allowing localization and region description to reuse the same image features. This design removes the second image-encoding path required by decoupled detection and captioning pipelines. To support execution on resource-limited devices, a workload-allocation formulation assigns model stages and input streams across connected edge nodes while accounting for execution time, memory capacity, processor utilization, power limits, and intermediate tensor transfer cost. The framework is evaluated on a six-device edge testbed comprising four NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano and Orin NX inference nodes and two Raspberry Pi 5 auxiliary CPU nodes, with TensorRT FP16 and INT8 execution on the NVIDIA devices. On a Jetson Orin Nano, the unified INT8 model achieves 38.1 FPS with 3.5 GB peak memory use and 11.8 W measured power. Under four concurrent streams, the proposed allocation achieves 35.2 FPS and 28.4 ms mean end-to-end latency with an internode transfer rate of 6.8 MB/s.	arXiv:2611.11291v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: This paper presents a unified transformer-based framework for object detection and dense region captioning across distributed edge networks. The architecture uses a shared visual encoder followed by detection, region extraction, and autoregressive language-decoding stages, allowing localization and region description to reuse the same image features. This design removes the second image-encoding path required by decoupled detection and captioning pipelines. To support execution on resource-limited devices, a workload-allocation formulation assigns model stages and input streams across connected edge nodes while accounting for execution time, memory capacity, processor utilization, power limits, and intermediate tensor transfer cost. The framework is evaluated on a six-device edge testbed comprising four NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano and Orin NX inference nodes and two Raspberry Pi 5 auxiliary CPU nodes, with TensorRT FP16 and INT8 execution on the NVIDIA devices. On a Jetson Orin Nano, the unified INT8 model achieves 38.1 FPS with 3.5 GB peak memory use and 11.8 W measured power. Under four concurrent streams, the proposed allocation achieves 35.2 FPS and 28.4 ms mean end-to-end latency with an internode transfer rate of 6.8 MB/s.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.521928+00	\N	\N	14436e91d7455d88e054f097dd8a7dffa1c673fc2b8c7d010c040e52937ae179	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.522242+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.522243+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
