f498ffb8-61ed-4d0e-90d3-93d889720439	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11427v1	Flex-DDCM: Zero-Shot Task-Oriented Image Compression via Diffusion Trajectory Guidance	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11427	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11427	Yixin Gao, Runsen Feng, Xin Li, Zhibo Chen	arXiv:2611.11427v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Recent zero-shot image compression methods exploit the powerful priors of pretrained diffusion models to produce perceptually pleasing reconstructions without compression-specific training. However, image compression serves not only human viewing but also machine analysis, while existing zero-shot codecs have limited flexibility in meeting the diverse reconstruction requirements of these applications. In this paper, we propose Flex-DDCM, a framework for zero-shot task-oriented image compression via diffusion trajectory guidance. Building on Turbo-DDCM, we adapt the coded diffusion trajectory through task-guided noise selection, embedding the effects of task guidance into the compressed representation. Guidance terms can be applied individually or combined, with timestep-dependent strengths controlling their influence throughout denoising. This design enables task adaptation without introducing additional decoding complexity. Experiments demonstrate flexible trade-offs among pixel fidelity, perceptual quality, and downstream task performance, with competitive performance against trained task-oriented codecs and faster decoding than Diff-ICMH.	arXiv:2611.11427v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Recent zero-shot image compression methods exploit the powerful priors of pretrained diffusion models to produce perceptually pleasing reconstructions without compression-specific training. However, image compression serves not only human viewing but also machine analysis, while existing zero-shot codecs have limited flexibility in meeting the diverse reconstruction requirements of these applications. In this paper, we propose Flex-DDCM, a framework for zero-shot task-oriented image compression via diffusion trajectory guidance. Building on Turbo-DDCM, we adapt the coded diffusion trajectory through task-guided noise selection, embedding the effects of task guidance into the compressed representation. Guidance terms can be applied individually or combined, with timestep-dependent strengths controlling their influence throughout denoising. This design enables task adaptation without introducing additional decoding complexity. Experiments demonstrate flexible trade-offs among pixel fidelity, perceptual quality, and downstream task performance, with competitive performance against trained task-oriented codecs and faster decoding than Diff-ICMH.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.535633+00	\N	\N	e2cece3ffd07d31cb7266e8066320aae9b350f538f81bec51c99e4b25e041b1f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.535956+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.535957+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
