dd11ba20-a96a-4af9-849c-6f4e5e85f54c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11512v1	Amortized Bayesian Experimental Design with In-Context Knowledge Conditioning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11512	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11512	Zhanghu Zhao, Daolang Huang, Xinyi Wen, Samuel Kaski	arXiv:2611.11512v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Amortized Bayesian experimental design (BED) enables real-time design strategies by shifting acquisition cost offline, but existing methods remain tied to the prior and task distribution they were trained on and cannot exploit additional information available at deployment. We introduce in-context amortized BED with unified knowledge encoding (IMBUE), a BED framework that admits external knowledge at deployment through a unified in-context interface. IMBUE accepts two forms of such knowledge without retraining, namely user-specified prior knowledge and observations from previous instances of the experiment. Both are encoded as additional input tokens and processed in-context with the accumulated observations by a shared Transformer, and a learned reliability filter scores each external token against the observations and excludes inconsistent ones. Across four benchmarks, IMBUE accelerates early-stage information acquisition when the external knowledge is reliable, and remains close to the no-external baseline when it is not.	arXiv:2611.11512v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Amortized Bayesian experimental design (BED) enables real-time design strategies by shifting acquisition cost offline, but existing methods remain tied to the prior and task distribution they were trained on and cannot exploit additional information available at deployment. We introduce in-context amortized BED with unified knowledge encoding (IMBUE), a BED framework that admits external knowledge at deployment through a unified in-context interface. IMBUE accepts two forms of such knowledge without retraining, namely user-specified prior knowledge and observations from previous instances of the experiment. Both are encoded as additional input tokens and processed in-context with the accumulated observations by a shared Transformer, and a learned reliability filter scores each external token against the observations and excludes inconsistent ones. Across four benchmarks, IMBUE accelerates early-stage information acquisition when the external knowledge is reliable, and remains close to the no-external baseline when it is not.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.559197+00	\N	\N	b5fa6443d10fa47ae0be2bb7ced11e308091b3c3a2a4b9521bf26fa93c4b456d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.559512+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.559513+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6f501dd4-5d50-4c2e-ae83-ad7ab2cf1c88	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11667v1	Nonasymptotic causal structure learning in generalized linear DAGs with continuous optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11667	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11667	Chang Deng, Inbeom Lee, Bryon Aragam	arXiv:2611.11667v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study the problem of learning directed acyclic graphs from observational data in nonlinear models and provide finite-sample, nonasymptotic statistical guarantees in these models. Although widely studied, statistical guarantees for gradient-based methods in nonlinear models are missing from the literature. Under a generalized linear model for each node, we analyze a global nonsmooth objective combining an exact acyclicity constraint with a quasi-minimax concave penalty, which can be optimized via standard gradient-based solvers. We establish finite-sample guarantees under this model, including the most widely used logistic and Poisson models. Experiments corroborate structure recovery accuracy and computational performance, including both standardized data and negative controls as baselines.	arXiv:2611.11667v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study the problem of learning directed acyclic graphs from observational data in nonlinear models and provide finite-sample, nonasymptotic statistical guarantees in these models. Although widely studied, statistical guarantees for gradient-based methods in nonlinear models are missing from the literature. Under a generalized linear model for each node, we analyze a global nonsmooth objective combining an exact acyclicity constraint with a quasi-minimax concave penalty, which can be optimized via standard gradient-based solvers. We establish finite-sample guarantees under this model, including the most widely used logistic and Poisson models. Experiments corroborate structure recovery accuracy and computational performance, including both standardized data and negative controls as baselines.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.595423+00	\N	\N	985699b95ac121452091ecfebe79b324aa9f9875672f11a9acfeb4700f9a7534	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.595834+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.595836+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
087c8412-e9bf-4b11-bcaf-8492d102456f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11517v1	InfCoiL: Coordinated Planner-Controller Learning for Closed-Loop Physics-Based Human-Object Interaction	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11517	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11517	Yude Zou, Yifei Yao, Yiwei Hao, Hanqing Wang, Xing Gao	arXiv:2611.11517v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Text- and goal-conditioned human-object interaction (HOI) provides an intuitive interface for specifying high-level intents in physics-based character animation. Mapping such intents to physically plausible, contact-rich behaviors naturally calls for a generative planner and a physics-based controller, yet existing planner-controller systems either execute plans open-loop or close the loop while keeping the planner fixed, so execution feedback never reshapes what is planned. We present InfCoiL, a closed-loop framework for physics-based HOI from coordinated planner-controller learning. InfCoiL couples a morphology-aware flow-matching planner with a unified multi-morphology controller through a shared morphology interface in an autoregressive plan-and-execute loop, and alternately fine-tunes both components with reinforcement learning from the same physical rollouts. To optimize the flow-matching planner online, we introduce Semantic Flow Policy Optimization (SFPO), which stochasticizes selected denoising transitions with channel-wise semantic covariance over heterogeneous HOI features and assigns group-relative, sequence-level rewards from complete closed-loop executions. On held-out OMOMO sequences, InfCoiL outperforms open-loop hierarchical baselines in goal-reaching task success and physical tracking accuracy. Project page: https://infcoil.github.io/	arXiv:2611.11517v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Text- and goal-conditioned human-object interaction (HOI) provides an intuitive interface for specifying high-level intents in physics-based character animation. Mapping such intents to physically plausible, contact-rich behaviors naturally calls for a generative planner and a physics-based controller, yet existing planner-controller systems either execute plans open-loop or close the loop while keeping the planner fixed, so execution feedback never reshapes what is planned. We present InfCoiL, a closed-loop framework for physics-based HOI from coordinated planner-controller learning. InfCoiL couples a morphology-aware flow-matching planner with a unified multi-morphology controller through a shared morphology interface in an autoregressive plan-and-execute loop, and alternately fine-tunes both components with reinforcement learning from the same physical rollouts. To optimize the flow-matching planner online, we introduce Semantic Flow Policy Optimization (SFPO), which stochasticizes selected denoising transitions with channel-wise semantic covariance over heterogeneous HOI features and assigns group-relative, sequence-level rewards from complete closed-loop executions. On held-out OMOMO sequences, InfCoiL outperforms open-loop hierarchical baselines in goal-reaching task success and physical tracking accuracy. Project page: https://infcoil.github.io/	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.561476+00	\N	\N	236450fbb462a2c0130506195b10f606b086506e03faabfa59bd0a1b0029354d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.561838+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.561839+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4a48384a-9a56-4078-afdb-8bc858bde55f	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/openai-campus-network-student-club-interest-form	OpenAI Campus Network: Student club interest form	https://openai.com/index/openai-campus-network-student-club-interest-form	https://openai.com/index/openai-campus-network-student-club-interest-form	\N	Join the OpenAI Campus Network—connect student clubs worldwide, access AI tools, host events, and build an AI-powered campus community.	Join the OpenAI Campus Network—connect student clubs worldwide, access AI tools, host events, and build an AI-powered campus community.	product_update	en	2026-05-11 10:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.781145+00	3	\N	9ed26ef37eb9593c7cd109de5dec435d2e7635e3c198418c0b4d272ea458cfe7	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.781509+00	2026-11-10 14:23:04.13582+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9eb294d5-7926-43a1-8a83-239e1447b31f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11522v1	Music Inpainting With Similarity-Guided Latent Diffusion	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11522	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11522	Sean Turland, Eloi Moliner, Vesa V\\"alim\\"aki	arXiv:2611.11522v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We propose a music inpainting technique combining similarity matching and a diffusion-based generative model to replace large segments of audio recordings with suitable alternatives. The proposed hybrid method, Similarity-Guided Diffusion Posterior Sampling (SimDPS), retrieves candidate replacement segments from the wider signal based on contextual similarity and uses them to guide the diffusion process toward coherent reconstructions. Being model-agnostic, the framework is compatible with any pre-trained diffusion model operating in either the waveform or latent domain. Objective evaluations demonstrate that SimDPS outperforms widely adopted RePaint and DPS methods across acoustic features and neural-embedding distance metrics. Subjective evaluations on multi-genre music datasets confirm that SimDPS enhances perceptual plausibility compared to previous diffusion-based methods and demonstrates robustness to poorly matched similarity candidates. These findings pave the way for improved music restoration and context-aware audio editing.	arXiv:2611.11522v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We propose a music inpainting technique combining similarity matching and a diffusion-based generative model to replace large segments of audio recordings with suitable alternatives. The proposed hybrid method, Similarity-Guided Diffusion Posterior Sampling (SimDPS), retrieves candidate replacement segments from the wider signal based on contextual similarity and uses them to guide the diffusion process toward coherent reconstructions. Being model-agnostic, the framework is compatible with any pre-trained diffusion model operating in either the waveform or latent domain. Objective evaluations demonstrate that SimDPS outperforms widely adopted RePaint and DPS methods across acoustic features and neural-embedding distance metrics. Subjective evaluations on multi-genre music datasets confirm that SimDPS enhances perceptual plausibility compared to previous diffusion-based methods and demonstrates robustness to poorly matched similarity candidates. These findings pave the way for improved music restoration and context-aware audio editing.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.566306+00	\N	\N	045cc8e5440bb790d8ae16e04c3035922b581bf8d4327f1d585aab09ee47bb1c	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.566618+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.566619+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e2161f5b-c0d8-4e05-a283-0ebb641b12df	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11578v1	Recursive Pricing and Incumbent Subtree Column Generation for Solving Optimal Classification Trees	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11578	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11578	Tengmu Hu, Chen Chen, Theodore T. Allen	arXiv:2611.11578v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Solving optimal classification tree (OCT) problems is computationally challenging because branching, routing, and leaf-assignment decisions must be optimized jointly. We develop an incumbent subtree column generation algorithm (ISCG-OCT) that exploits the recursive structure of classification trees. We first introduce a mixed set-packing and set-partitioning formulation (MSSF). We then construct a restricted master problem whose columns represent feasible subtree patterns. Unlike conventional dual-based column generation, our incumbent-centered pricing evaluates objective improvements relative to the current solution. We establish a recursive decomposition in which fixing a branching decision separates the remaining pricing problem into independent subtree subproblems, enabling exact dynamic programming. We also develop threshold-based variants that trade optimality guarantees for computational efficiency. Computational experiments on 27 benchmark datasets demonstrate competitive training and testing accuracy and substantial reductions in solution time on many instances. When initialized with a CART solution, ISCG-OCT can certify global optimality faster than warm-started mixed-integer programming solvers on selected instances. These results demonstrate how recursive pricing and structured subtree optimization can improve the computational efficiency of exact classification tree methods.	arXiv:2611.11578v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Solving optimal classification tree (OCT) problems is computationally challenging because branching, routing, and leaf-assignment decisions must be optimized jointly. We develop an incumbent subtree column generation algorithm (ISCG-OCT) that exploits the recursive structure of classification trees. We first introduce a mixed set-packing and set-partitioning formulation (MSSF). We then construct a restricted master problem whose columns represent feasible subtree patterns. Unlike conventional dual-based column generation, our incumbent-centered pricing evaluates objective improvements relative to the current solution. We establish a recursive decomposition in which fixing a branching decision separates the remaining pricing problem into independent subtree subproblems, enabling exact dynamic programming. We also develop threshold-based variants that trade optimality guarantees for computational efficiency. Computational experiments on 27 benchmark datasets demonstrate competitive training and testing accuracy and substantial reductions in solution time on many instances. When initialized with a CART solution, ISCG-OCT can certify global optimality faster than warm-started mixed-integer programming solvers on selected instances. These results demonstrate how recursive pricing and structured subtree optimization can improve the computational efficiency of exact classification tree methods.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.583115+00	\N	\N	e91ee380ef4ca6fd994a8dc47d0b105e9ea3e307ad1397570b35d41aaf09ecc8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.583447+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.583448+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bcb2edb2-247a-44cf-bbe2-44e247727d59	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11586v1	FastGRT: Accelerating Training of 3D Gaussian Ray Tracing	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11586	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11586	Meet Banthia, Xiaoteng Liu, Hexu Zhao, Aurojit Panda, Jinyang Li	arXiv:2611.11586v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Ray Tracing enables rendering capabilities that are difficult to achieve with rasterization-based approaches such as 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS). However, the training cost of Gaussian-based ray tracing (GRT) is much higher than that of 3DGS. In this paper, we study how ray tracing using Gaussians executes on GPUs and show that a considerable fraction of the training cost arises from how the computation is organized. We develop FastGRT, which decouples ray traversal from blending and reuses work done in the forward pass for the backward pass. FastGRT further incorporates optimizations such as fast gradient accumulation and a hybrid BVH update strategy to tackle other major bottlenecks in the training pipeline. We implement FastGRT in Vulkan. To the best of our knowledge, it is the first Vulkan-based GRT implementation that supports both training and rendering. Compared to 3DGRT, FastGRT improves training time by 1.9$\\times$ to 4.97$\\times$, while preserving the original rendering formulation. Our code is open source.	arXiv:2611.11586v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Ray Tracing enables rendering capabilities that are difficult to achieve with rasterization-based approaches such as 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS). However, the training cost of Gaussian-based ray tracing (GRT) is much higher than that of 3DGS. In this paper, we study how ray tracing using Gaussians executes on GPUs and show that a considerable fraction of the training cost arises from how the computation is organized. We develop FastGRT, which decouples ray traversal from blending and reuses work done in the forward pass for the backward pass. FastGRT further incorporates optimizations such as fast gradient accumulation and a hybrid BVH update strategy to tackle other major bottlenecks in the training pipeline. We implement FastGRT in Vulkan. To the best of our knowledge, it is the first Vulkan-based GRT implementation that supports both training and rendering. Compared to 3DGRT, FastGRT improves training time by 1.9$\\times$ to 4.97$\\times$, while preserving the original rendering formulation. Our code is open source.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.585347+00	\N	\N	f93aed80e4a6ddf23924d4e3dfac81d906ab61061be12654bb3f6f7cafcda15e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.585675+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.585677+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
38c9f6ad-60ae-4033-86e6-be015aecf40d	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-3-codex-system-card	GPT-5.3-Codex System Card	https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-3-codex-system-card	https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-3-codex-system-card	\N	GPT‑5.3-Codex is the most capable agentic coding model to date, combining the frontier coding performance of GPT‑5.2-Codex with the reasoning and professional knowledge capabilities of GPT‑5.2.	GPT‑5.3-Codex is the most capable agentic coding model to date, combining the frontier coding performance of GPT‑5.2-Codex with the reasoning and professional knowledge capabilities of GPT‑5.2.	product_update	en	2026-02-05 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:43.139972+00	4	\N	f106e8c1cfc480fa531bb949927c0eb6283d3eb61a0644974357260b5eabbd7e	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:43.140351+00	2026-11-10 14:15:21.89548+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4351fac9-16c5-4e62-ae42-0b53480fe0ca	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11681v1	A Sequential Monte Carlo Method for Data Assimilation in Subsurface Flow Systems with Hierarchical Uncertainty	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11681	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11681	Wenchao Teng, Guido Di Federico, Louis J. Durlofsky	arXiv:2611.11681v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Subsurface flow systems often involve hierarchical geological uncertainty, meaning both higher-level metaparameters and cell-by-cell properties are uncertain. This leads to challenges for data assimilation because both sets of parameters must be estimated. In this work we develop an adaptive-tempered sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) procedure for joint Bayesian estimation of these quantities. The method uses noncentered geological parameterizations, with geomodel realizations represented by metaparameters and Gaussian latent variables that are independent under the prior. Adaptive likelihood tempering, importance weighting, resampling, and blockwise Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) mutation are applied at each iteration. Flow surrogates are used for all function evaluations. The hierarchical SMC procedure is further extended to treat successive data assimilation. In this case, existing posterior particles are updated in a manner that accounts for correlations in surrogate model errors across observation batches. We consider two distinct 3D systems with hierarchical uncertainty. The first is a faulted carbon storage system containing three stacked aquifers and two extensive faults. Principal component analysis is used to represent target-aquifer heterogeneity, while geological metaparameters characterize uncertain aquifer and fault properties. The second is a channelized oil-water system containing three facies with uncertain properties. A latent diffusion model represents variability in channel geometry and facies realizations. For the four synthetic true models considered, SMC posterior metaparameter distributions and flow predictions agree closely with reference MCMC results. SMC, however, requires fewer function evaluations and provides speedups of 2.5--7.0 for single-stage data assimilation. Even greater speedups are achieved with multiple GPUs.	arXiv:2611.11681v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Subsurface flow systems often involve hierarchical geological uncertainty, meaning both higher-level metaparameters and cell-by-cell properties are uncertain. This leads to challenges for data assimilation because both sets of parameters must be estimated. In this work we develop an adaptive-tempered sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) procedure for joint Bayesian estimation of these quantities. The method uses noncentered geological parameterizations, with geomodel realizations represented by metaparameters and Gaussian latent variables that are independent under the prior. Adaptive likelihood tempering, importance weighting, resampling, and blockwise Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) mutation are applied at each iteration. Flow surrogates are used for all function evaluations. The hierarchical SMC procedure is further extended to treat successive data assimilation. In this case, existing posterior particles are updated in a manner that accounts for correlations in surrogate model errors across observation batches. We consider two distinct 3D systems with hierarchical uncertainty. The first is a faulted carbon storage system containing three stacked aquifers and two extensive faults. Principal component analysis is used to represent target-aquifer heterogeneity, while geological metaparameters characterize uncertain aquifer and fault properties. The second is a channelized oil-water system containing three facies with uncertain properties. A latent diffusion model represents variability in channel geometry and facies realizations. For the four synthetic true models considered, SMC posterior metaparameter distributions and flow predictions agree closely with reference MCMC results. SMC, however, requires fewer function evaluations and provides speedups of 2.5--7.0 for single-stage data assimilation. Even greater speedups are achieved with multiple GPUs.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.602979+00	\N	\N	9b313588573c80f2d99501d9ee33ee12473ba0634bc7bb017d3d2c2243b9136f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.603311+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.603313+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
486c3c84-0cb6-4a66-a172-ba7a0d9aa13d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11709v1	Data-Driven Precision Control of Soft Helicoid Robots	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11709	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11709	Roshan S. Kaundinya, Mark Leone, Roberto Sabella, Hugo Buurmeijer, Luis Pabon, John Irvin Alora, Marco Pavone, George Haller	arXiv:2611.11709v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: A purely data-driven method grounded in the theory of adiabatic spectral submanifolds (aSSMs) was recently used to model and control nonlinear soft robotic simulators. Here, we extend the methodology to learn aSSM-reduced models from data collected from hardware soft robotic platforms. Specifically, we learn a five dimensional (5D) aSSM-reduced nonlinear ordinary differential equation (ODE) that accurately models the dynamics of a soft-helicoid trunk robot. We then use this ODE in a model predictive control scheme to demonstrate real-time precision control across various challenging closed-loop trajectory-tracking tasks, ranging from 2D to 3D trajectories with static and dynamic constraints. We find that our methodology enhances accuracy and precision across all control tasks compared to available data-driven linear methods and model-free reinforcement learning. While we focus here on a soft-helicoid trunk robot, our methodology relies solely on hardware data and is therefore applicable to a broader class of soft robotic systems.	arXiv:2611.11709v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: A purely data-driven method grounded in the theory of adiabatic spectral submanifolds (aSSMs) was recently used to model and control nonlinear soft robotic simulators. Here, we extend the methodology to learn aSSM-reduced models from data collected from hardware soft robotic platforms. Specifically, we learn a five dimensional (5D) aSSM-reduced nonlinear ordinary differential equation (ODE) that accurately models the dynamics of a soft-helicoid trunk robot. We then use this ODE in a model predictive control scheme to demonstrate real-time precision control across various challenging closed-loop trajectory-tracking tasks, ranging from 2D to 3D trajectories with static and dynamic constraints. We find that our methodology enhances accuracy and precision across all control tasks compared to available data-driven linear methods and model-free reinforcement learning. While we focus here on a soft-helicoid trunk robot, our methodology relies solely on hardware data and is therefore applicable to a broader class of soft robotic systems.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.612432+00	\N	\N	c4db1818af703c985ea709e13a3320aa39502b8b387f4f4bedd94b2cd49b7065	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.612754+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.612755+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
dc3e2139-7a28-44d4-a18f-24601bad2a05	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11711v1	CRUSTPairs: A Cross-Language Dataset of Paired C and Rust Solutions	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11711	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11711	Vojtech Hejlek, Leonardo Jesus Almeida, Alneu de Andrade Lopes	arXiv:2611.11711v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: C-to-Rust migration is an active agenda, but no dataset pairs the two languages at the level of whole solutions to a shared problem. We present CRUSTPairs, 1857 C/Rust implementation pairs drawn from competitive programming archives, normalised, deduplicated, and stratified into difficulty tiers by zero-shot cross-language embedding similarity, with perrecord provenance and licensing. Cross-language retrieval is hard on this data, and harder than a single baseline would suggest: TF-IDF over shared identifiers reaches MRR 0.21 and so beats a pretrained code model at 0.19, which on the hardest quarter of the data scores 1.7x chance. Finetuning reaches 0.79, and lifts that hardest quarter 10x. Renaming every user identifier settles what is being learned: the finetuned models keep 96-97% of their accuracy without them, while the pretrained model keeps 66\\% - less than the lexical baseline does - and on structurally divergent pairs the finetuned models are better without identifiers than with them.	arXiv:2611.11711v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: C-to-Rust migration is an active agenda, but no dataset pairs the two languages at the level of whole solutions to a shared problem. We present CRUSTPairs, 1857 C/Rust implementation pairs drawn from competitive programming archives, normalised, deduplicated, and stratified into difficulty tiers by zero-shot cross-language embedding similarity, with perrecord provenance and licensing. Cross-language retrieval is hard on this data, and harder than a single baseline would suggest: TF-IDF over shared identifiers reaches MRR 0.21 and so beats a pretrained code model at 0.19, which on the hardest quarter of the data scores 1.7x chance. Finetuning reaches 0.79, and lifts that hardest quarter 10x. Renaming every user identifier settles what is being learned: the finetuned models keep 96-97% of their accuracy without them, while the pretrained model keeps 66\\% - less than the lexical baseline does - and on structurally divergent pairs the finetuned models are better without identifiers than with them.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.614915+00	\N	\N	2d29e190e6e4cd31bba0f5758180811c13893b82ac4a0e5645b9b3ef1f49e5a8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.615269+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.61527+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
710db04d-4823-4cf8-9723-8d29ae0b75f6	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/our-response-to-the-tanstack-npm-supply-chain-attack	Our response to the TanStack npm supply chain attack	https://openai.com/index/our-response-to-the-tanstack-npm-supply-chain-attack	https://openai.com/index/our-response-to-the-tanstack-npm-supply-chain-attack	\N	OpenAI details its response to the TanStack “Mini Shai-Hulud” supply chain attack, outlines protections taken to secure systems and signing certificates, and explains why macOS users must update OpenAI apps by June 12, 2026. Learn what happened, what was affected, and how OpenAI is strengthening defenses against evolving software supply chain threats.	OpenAI details its response to the TanStack “Mini Shai-Hulud” supply chain attack, outlines protections taken to secure systems and signing certificates, and explains why macOS users must update OpenAI apps by June 12, 2026. Learn what happened, what was affected, and how OpenAI is strengthening defenses against evolving software supply chain threats.	news	en	2026-05-13 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.764188+00	4	\N	5382e0beeaf8e1851dc71819975db4bd624dd16bd2c926b7b60a4fd2f332d5b1	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.764663+00	2026-11-10 14:23:19.840995+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8e345eb0-e052-4242-9a48-fea2d1ae8409	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11780v1	Planners Exploit Deterministic Error: Dithered Low-Bit World Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11780	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11780	Yong Hwi Song, Keon Myung Lee	arXiv:2611.11780v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Sampling-based planners such as MPPI and CEM query a learned world model thousands of times per control step, so low-bit world models are an obvious way to make model-based control cheaper on a robot. With round-to-nearest activation quantization and no calibration, we find that planning can collapse, by up to 100 points of success, while the world model's latent one-step prediction error stays no larger than that of a stochastic rounding scheme that plans well. At the same bit width, what decides the outcome is not the size of the rounding error but whether it persists across planner iterations and is shared across candidates. Round-to-nearest leaves an error that persists, and an iterating planner drifts toward the candidates that this error favors; in TD-MPC2, holding the same dither fixed within a step or an episode brings the drift back. Dither shared by all candidates and redrawn at every iteration removes most of the damage at the same bit width and without training: at least half of it in all 19 affected Meta-World cells (task $\\times$ bit width) of TD-MPC2 (median 95%), in 4 of 5 DINO-WM cells, and in all 8 affected tasks of a 317M-parameter multi-task TD-MPC2. Captured as CUDA graphs on an Ampere GPU, a W8A4 integer TD-MPC2 world model runs its planning rollouts 1.9-3.3$\\times$ and the whole planning step 1.3-1.5$\\times$ faster than FP32, though not faster than BF16 with pre-converted weights; shared dither adds at most 2.3% latency. In TD-MPC2, block-scaled NVFP4 removes most of the damage and MXFP4 part of it, which ties the failure to coarse grids with a shared scale. Hypotheses, thresholds and analysis code were fixed before the data that test them, and the hypotheses that failed are reported.	arXiv:2611.11780v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Sampling-based planners such as MPPI and CEM query a learned world model thousands of times per control step, so low-bit world models are an obvious way to make model-based control cheaper on a robot. With round-to-nearest activation quantization and no calibration, we find that planning can collapse, by up to 100 points of success, while the world model's latent one-step prediction error stays no larger than that of a stochastic rounding scheme that plans well. At the same bit width, what decides the outcome is not the size of the rounding error but whether it persists across planner iterations and is shared across candidates. Round-to-nearest leaves an error that persists, and an iterating planner drifts toward the candidates that this error favors; in TD-MPC2, holding the same dither fixed within a step or an episode brings the drift back. Dither shared by all candidates and redrawn at every iteration removes most of the damage at the same bit width and without training: at least half of it in all 19 affected Meta-World cells (task $\\times$ bit width) of TD-MPC2 (median 95%), in 4 of 5 DINO-WM cells, and in all 8 affected tasks of a 317M-parameter multi-task TD-MPC2. Captured as CUDA graphs on an Ampere GPU, a W8A4 integer TD-MPC2 world model runs its planning rollouts 1.9-3.3$\\times$ and the whole planning step 1.3-1.5$\\times$ faster than FP32, though not faster than BF16 with pre-converted weights; shared dither adds at most 2.3% latency. In TD-MPC2, block-scaled NVFP4 removes most of the damage and MXFP4 part of it, which ties the failure to coarse grids with a shared scale. Hypotheses, thresholds and analysis code were fixed before the data that test them, and the hypotheses that failed are reported.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.627059+00	\N	\N	0a690f7417667b10f47871d5edd71e67a704f4301ad9fd99af7c11e5ab856cac	pending	2026-