a4daba28-eff1-48e7-8f2a-7dd926c882d1	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12755v1	From Granger causality to causal structure in dynamical systems	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12755	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12755	V\\'ictor Elvira	arXiv:2611.12755v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: The idea of testing whether the past of one signal improves the prediction of another can be traced back to Wiener. It was Granger who formalized the concept. Granger causality has become one of the most widely used tools to infer directed interactions between the components of a system. The name however can be misleading: it detects predictive relations among the measured signals, not causal relations among the mechanisms that generate them. This article starts by reviewing recent advances in observation-space Granger analysis. It then follows a path to directed structure in latent dynamical models, and asks under which conditions that structure can be read causally.	arXiv:2611.12755v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: The idea of testing whether the past of one signal improves the prediction of another can be traced back to Wiener. It was Granger who formalized the concept. Granger causality has become one of the most widely used tools to infer directed interactions between the components of a system. The name however can be misleading: it detects predictive relations among the measured signals, not causal relations among the mechanisms that generate them. This article starts by reviewing recent advances in observation-space Granger analysis. It then follows a path to directed structure in latent dynamical models, and asks under which conditions that structure can be read causally.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.758273+00	\N	\N	54f044044afdd3ae2934172eff98bce9c2ab98aab12e07be7069227f5ac79a01	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.758698+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.7587+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a35f156a-d354-4682-b8dc-a2ae246353fc	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2504.08897v3	On the Adversarial Robustness of Spiking Neural Networks Trained by Local Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.08897	https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.08897	Jiaqi Lin, Abhronil Sengupta	arXiv:2504.08897v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent research has shown the vulnerability of Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) under adversarial examples that are nearly indistinguishable from clean data in the context of frame-based and event-based information. The majority of these studies are constrained in generating adversarial examples using Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT), a gradient-based method which lacks biological plausibility. In contrast, local learning methods, which relax many of BPTT's constraints, remain under-explored in the context of adversarial attacks. With the increasing real-world deployment of local learning methods in SNNs, it is critical to investigate whether these approaches introduce adversarial vulnerabilities that could compromise reliability. To address this problem, we examine adversarial robustness in SNNs through the framework of four types of training algorithms. We provide an in-depth analysis showing that direct gradient-based attacks are ineffective for locally trained models. To overcome these limitations, we introduce a hybrid adversarial attack paradigm that leverages the transferability of adversarial instances. The proposed hybrid approach demonstrates superior performance, outperforming existing adversarial attack methods. Furthermore, the generalizability of the method is assessed under multi-step adversarial attacks, adversarial attacks in black-box scenarios, and within the non-spiking domain.	arXiv:2504.08897v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent research has shown the vulnerability of Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) under adversarial examples that are nearly indistinguishable from clean data in the context of frame-based and event-based information. The majority of these studies are constrained in generating adversarial examples using Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT), a gradient-based method which lacks biological plausibility. In contrast, local learning methods, which relax many of BPTT's constraints, remain under-explored in the context of adversarial attacks. With the increasing real-world deployment of local learning methods in SNNs, it is critical to investigate whether these approaches introduce adversarial vulnerabilities that could compromise reliability. To address this problem, we examine adversarial robustness in SNNs through the framework of four types of training algorithms. We provide an in-depth analysis showing that direct gradient-based attacks are ineffective for locally trained models. To overcome these limitations, we introduce a hybrid adversarial attack paradigm that leverages the transferability of adversarial instances. The proposed hybrid approach demonstrates superior performance, outperforming existing adversarial attack methods. Furthermore, the generalizability of the method is assessed under multi-step adversarial attacks, adversarial attacks in black-box scenarios, and within the non-spiking domain.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.808093+00	\N	\N	7eb18c35ed07174cbfb3d01abb4221b7b836a92d64d41c5fa7bde6f953c22402	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.808486+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.808488+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
41a8ce46-df5a-4032-8a77-3d96fe2ddef4	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2408.08812v3	TRAM: Test-Time Risk Adaptation with Mixture of Agents	https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.08812	https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.08812	Mohamad Chehade, Amrit Singh Bedi, Souradip Chakraborty, Amy Zhang, Hao Zhu	arXiv:2408.08812v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Deployed reinforcement learning agents often face safety requirements that are specified only after training: new hazard maps, revised risk thresholds, or behavioral alignment constraints. We study zero-update deployment-time adaptation in which a fixed library of risk-neutral source policies must be reused under a newly specified reward-risk tradeoff. We propose TRAM (Test-time Risk Adaptation with Mixture of Agents), a source-scored composition rule that evaluates each source under the target reward and an occupancy-based deployment risk, then selects actions using risk-adjusted source scores. Unlike training-time risk-sensitive methods tied to a fixed surrogate such as return variance, TRAM supports spatial barrier exposure, divergence to a reference behavior, and local volatility risks specified at test time. We make the surrogate nature of the method explicit: TRAM is not claimed to solve the full occupancy-control problem of the stitched policy, but it admits a measurable source-hull mismatch term connecting source-scored risk to realized risk. Experiments in gridworlds, MuJoCo Reacher, Safety-Gymnasium, and an inference-time LLM alignment setting show that TRAM improves deployment risk while preserving reward and requires no parameter updates at test time.	arXiv:2408.08812v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Deployed reinforcement learning agents often face safety requirements that are specified only after training: new hazard maps, revised risk thresholds, or behavioral alignment constraints. We study zero-update deployment-time adaptation in which a fixed library of risk-neutral source policies must be reused under a newly specified reward-risk tradeoff. We propose TRAM (Test-time Risk Adaptation with Mixture of Agents), a source-scored composition rule that evaluates each source under the target reward and an occupancy-based deployment risk, then selects actions using risk-adjusted source scores. Unlike training-time risk-sensitive methods tied to a fixed surrogate such as return variance, TRAM supports spatial barrier exposure, divergence to a reference behavior, and local volatility risks specified at test time. We make the surrogate nature of the method explicit: TRAM is not claimed to solve the full occupancy-control problem of the stitched policy, but it admits a measurable source-hull mismatch term connecting source-scored risk to realized risk. Experiments in gridworlds, MuJoCo Reacher, Safety-Gymnasium, and an inference-time LLM alignment setting show that TRAM improves deployment risk while preserving reward and requires no parameter updates at test time.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.786839+00	\N	\N	29cdcc6251c0c061242df55a25b923fe4ad7337202b0d0e7657362d8b8cc0ea1	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.787161+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.787163+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b57c7db0-7dec-4f08-8677-fd2de7dc5f77	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/trustworthy-third-party-evaluations-foundations	A shared playbook for trustworthy third party evaluations	https://openai.com/index/trustworthy-third-party-evaluations-foundations	https://openai.com/index/trustworthy-third-party-evaluations-foundations	\N	OpenAI shares guidance on third-party AI evaluations, covering how to assess model capabilities, safeguards, and validity for frontier systems.	OpenAI shares guidance on third-party AI evaluations, covering how to assess model capabilities, safeguards, and validity for frontier systems.	other	en	2026-05-29 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.701591+00	4	\N	f33bc27509425b954bf2b8b500120b1502fc8092684724da44a8b22fe9422c00	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.702159+00	2026-11-10 14:24:38.796958+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2291628c-f63d-46d4-877c-05e7a7e7d6be	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2412.18212v2	Accelerating AIGC Services with Latent Action Diffusion Scheduling in Edge Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.18212	https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.18212	Changfu Xu, Jianxiong Guo, Wanyu Lin, Haodong Zou, Yuzhu Liang, Xiaowen Chu, Jiannong Cao, Tian Wang	arXiv:2412.18212v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Artificial Intelligence Generated Content (AIGC) has gained significant popularity for creating diverse content. However, current AIGC models primarily focus on content quality within a centralized framework, resulting in a high service delay and negative user experiences. The workload of an AIGC task is determined by model complexity and multi-layered encoder architectures, posing significant challenges for its modeling, deployment, and scheduling within resource-constrained edge networks. We model an offloading problem among edges for providing real AIGC services and propose \\textbf{LAD-TS}, a novel Latent Action Diffusion-based Task Scheduling method that orchestrates multiple edge servers for expedited AIGC services by leveraging the diffusion model's conditional guidance capability and the reinforcement learning's environment interaction ability. We first design an innovative Latent Action Diffusion Network (LADN) model based on traditional diffusion models. We then incorporate the LADN model into deep reinforcement learning, forming an effective LAD-TS method for task scheduling in distributed edge networks. Meanwhile, we design a latent action diffusion strategy to achieve near-optimal decisions quickly by utilizing historical action probabilities instead of Gaussian noise. Furthermore, we implement a refined AIGC model and develop \\textbf{DEdgeAI}, a prototype edge system with the refined AIGC model deployment to evaluate our LAD-TS method. DEdgeAI provides a real AIGC service for users, demonstrating up to $7.36\\%-18.11\\%$ and $34.07\\%-94.96\\%$ shorter service delays than the state-of-the-art methods and representative commercial platforms, respectively. We release our open-source code at https://github.com/ChangfuXu/DEdgeAI/	arXiv:2412.18212v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Artificial Intelligence Generated Content (AIGC) has gained significant popularity for creating diverse content. However, current AIGC models primarily focus on content quality within a centralized framework, resulting in a high service delay and negative user experiences. The workload of an AIGC task is determined by model complexity and multi-layered encoder architectures, posing significant challenges for its modeling, deployment, and scheduling within resource-constrained edge networks. We model an offloading problem among edges for providing real AIGC services and propose \\textbf{LAD-TS}, a novel Latent Action Diffusion-based Task Scheduling method that orchestrates multiple edge servers for expedited AIGC services by leveraging the diffusion model's conditional guidance capability and the reinforcement learning's environment interaction ability. We first design an innovative Latent Action Diffusion Network (LADN) model based on traditional diffusion models. We then incorporate the LADN model into deep reinforcement learning, forming an effective LAD-TS method for task scheduling in distributed edge networks. Meanwhile, we design a latent action diffusion strategy to achieve near-optimal decisions quickly by utilizing historical action probabilities instead of Gaussian noise. Furthermore, we implement a refined AIGC model and develop \\textbf{DEdgeAI}, a prototype edge system with the refined AIGC model deployment to evaluate our LAD-TS method. DEdgeAI provides a real AIGC service for users, demonstrating up to $7.36\\%-18.11\\%$ and $34.07\\%-94.96\\%$ shorter service delays than the state-of-the-art methods and representative commercial platforms, respectively. We release our open-source code at https://github.com/ChangfuXu/DEdgeAI/	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.801159+00	\N	\N	a8048271474c69394c8bcb63a308cbeda8894f4551b35e79528d4b0663fd6b58	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.801482+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.801483+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ddb3d7b0-e530-476c-b123-70b1be7a93df	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2503.05970v4	Partially Decentralized Multi-Agent Q-Learning via Digital Cousins for Wireless Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05970	https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05970	Talha Bozkus, Urbashi Mitra	arXiv:2503.05970v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Q-learning is a widely used reinforcement learning (RL) algorithm for optimizing wireless networks, but faces challenges with large state-spaces. Recently proposed multi-environment mixed Q-learning (MEMQ) algorithm addresses these challenges by employing multiple Q-learning algorithms across multiple synthetically generated, distinct but structurally related environments, so-called digital cousins. In this paper, we propose a novel multi-agent MEMQ (M-MEMQ) for cooperative decentralized wireless networks with multiple networked transmitters (TXs) and base stations (BSs). TXs do not have access to global information (joint state and actions). The new concept of coordinated and uncoordinated states is introduced. In uncoordinated states, TXs act independently to minimize their individual costs and update local Q-functions. In coordinated states, TXs use a Bayesian approach to estimate the joint state and update the joint Q-functions. The cost of information-sharing scales linearly with the number of TXs and is independent of the joint state-action space size. Several theoretical guarantees, including deterministic and probabilistic convergence, bounds on estimation error variance, and the probability of misdetecting the joint states, are given. Numerical simulations show that M-MEMQ outperforms several decentralized and centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) multi-agent RL algorithms by achieving 60% lower average policy error (APE), 40% faster convergence, 45% reduced runtime complexity, and 40% less sample complexity. Furthermore, M-MEMQ achieves comparable APE with significantly lower complexity than centralized methods. Simulations validate the theoretical analyses.	arXiv:2503.05970v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Q-learning is a widely used reinforcement learning (RL) algorithm for optimizing wireless networks, but faces challenges with large state-spaces. Recently proposed multi-environment mixed Q-learning (MEMQ) algorithm addresses these challenges by employing multiple Q-learning algorithms across multiple synthetically generated, distinct but structurally related environments, so-called digital cousins. In this paper, we propose a novel multi-agent MEMQ (M-MEMQ) for cooperative decentralized wireless networks with multiple networked transmitters (TXs) and base stations (BSs). TXs do not have access to global information (joint state and actions). The new concept of coordinated and uncoordinated states is introduced. In uncoordinated states, TXs act independently to minimize their individual costs and update local Q-functions. In coordinated states, TXs use a Bayesian approach to estimate the joint state and update the joint Q-functions. The cost of information-sharing scales linearly with the number of TXs and is independent of the joint state-action space size. Several theoretical guarantees, including deterministic and probabilistic convergence, bounds on estimation error variance, and the probability of misdetecting the joint states, are given. Numerical simulations show that M-MEMQ outperforms several decentralized and centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) multi-agent RL algorithms by achieving 60% lower average policy error (APE), 40% faster convergence, 45% reduced runtime complexity, and 40% less sample complexity. Furthermore, M-MEMQ achieves comparable APE with significantly lower complexity than centralized methods. Simulations validate the theoretical analyses.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.805848+00	\N	\N	99492f28aedd6019bc7aa2710e2b23caac57f0b17e1f284fa24056946d8f4808	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.806171+00	