cfc5e2e1-73b5-48fa-870c-6624a8d5eaf4	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.07148v2	Agentic Planning with Reasoning for Image Styling via Offline RL	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07148	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07148	Subhojyoti Mukherjee, Stefano Petrangeli, Branislav Kveton, Trung Bui, Franck Dernoncourt, Arko Mukherjee	arXiv:2603.07148v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Direct prompt-based editing often fails on complex transformations because vague and subjective prompts often require nuanced understanding of what should be changed in the image. Our core intuition is that leveraging compositional image editing tools rather than direct prompting profits from structured agent-level planning with explicit reasoning, leading to better results. This structured planning framework enables efficient offline RL post-training on quality-scored trajectories to improve performance. We present a tool-based agentic RL post-training framework that addresses this through structured planning with chain-of-thought reasoning. Our key contributions include: (1) A tool-based agentic planning methodology that combines a compositional library of orthogonal primitive transformations, structured context representation, and explicit per-step reasoning to decompose complex styling into interpretable tool sequences. (2) A synthetic data generation pipeline producing three large-scale datasets (each $\\sim$10K trajectories) with reasoning chains, plans, and quality scores, as no existing datasets provide such supervision. Our datasets and code are publicly available at the HuggingFace repository. (3) Offline RL training methods for learning planners with reasoning as our core algorithmic contributions, which consistently improve over the Edit-Only baseline in visual quality and instruction following. (4) Comprehensive evaluation across 4B and 8B parameter Qwen3-VL models showing that our methods outperform other baselines in the majority of compositional tasks, validated by human evaluations.	arXiv:2603.07148v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Direct prompt-based editing often fails on complex transformations because vague and subjective prompts often require nuanced understanding of what should be changed in the image. Our core intuition is that leveraging compositional image editing tools rather than direct prompting profits from structured agent-level planning with explicit reasoning, leading to better results. This structured planning framework enables efficient offline RL post-training on quality-scored trajectories to improve performance. We present a tool-based agentic RL post-training framework that addresses this through structured planning with chain-of-thought reasoning. Our key contributions include: (1) A tool-based agentic planning methodology that combines a compositional library of orthogonal primitive transformations, structured context representation, and explicit per-step reasoning to decompose complex styling into interpretable tool sequences. (2) A synthetic data generation pipeline producing three large-scale datasets (each $\\sim$10K trajectories) with reasoning chains, plans, and quality scores, as no existing datasets provide such supervision. Our datasets and code are publicly available at the HuggingFace repository. (3) Offline RL training methods for learning planners with reasoning as our core algorithmic contributions, which consistently improve over the Edit-Only baseline in visual quality and instruction following. (4) Comprehensive evaluation across 4B and 8B parameter Qwen3-VL models showing that our methods outperform other baselines in the majority of compositional tasks, validated by human evaluations.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.002124+00	\N	\N	5a6feed6c5b7d87866f335d4b75ed1d78f51ecb3f0cdf9e2d25cd1f60786308f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.002459+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.00246+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d7331541-1e09-4aa3-b14f-ac62dad59f32	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.20568v3	Amortized Vine Copulas for High-Dimensional Density and Information Estimation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.20568	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.20568	Houman Safaai	arXiv:2604.20568v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modeling high-dimensional dependencies while keeping likelihoods tractable re- mains challenging. Classical vine copulas provide an explicit dependence structure but require repeated, costly bivariate fitting. We propose Vine Denoising Copula (VDC)1, an amortized estimator for simplified vines on continuous data. VDC trains a single bivariate denoising network on simulated copulas and reuses it across all vine edges. For each edge, the network maps an empirical histogram to a density grid, and iterative proportional fitting approximately enforces uniform grid marginals. The predicted densities and cached conditional transforms support stan- dard vine recursion, likelihood evaluation, sampling, and information estimation. This retains the classical vine factorization while replacing repeated per-edge fitting with reusable neural inference. Across synthetic and real-data benchmarks, VDC combines accurate bivariate density estimation, competitive information estimates, and faster high-dimensional vine fitting after pretraining. Performance varies across datasets; finite grid resolution and conditional prediction remain limitations. The explicit fitted structure supports edge- and tree-level information decomposition, making repeated dependence analysis more practical.	arXiv:2604.20568v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modeling high-dimensional dependencies while keeping likelihoods tractable re- mains challenging. Classical vine copulas provide an explicit dependence structure but require repeated, costly bivariate fitting. We propose Vine Denoising Copula (VDC)1, an amortized estimator for simplified vines on continuous data. VDC trains a single bivariate denoising network on simulated copulas and reuses it across all vine edges. For each edge, the network maps an empirical histogram to a density grid, and iterative proportional fitting approximately enforces uniform grid marginals. The predicted densities and cached conditional transforms support stan- dard vine recursion, likelihood evaluation, sampling, and information estimation. This retains the classical vine factorization while replacing repeated per-edge fitting with reusable neural inference. Across synthetic and real-data benchmarks, VDC combines accurate bivariate density estimation, competitive information estimates, and faster high-dimensional vine fitting after pretraining. Performance varies across datasets; finite grid resolution and conditional prediction remain limitations. The explicit fitted structure supports edge- and tree-level information decomposition, making repeated dependence analysis more practical.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.056973+00	\N	\N	89fd9aeacd6b8f489e40b6c7e2d3e0717d83fe057608da0a280550b855ede4fe	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.057277+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.057278+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
52e25979-2489-400e-b043-698ae5568cde	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.09257v2	Transductive Generalization for GNNs via Optimal Transport	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09257	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09257	MoonJeong Park, Seungbeom Lee, Kyungmin Kim, Jaeseung Heo, Seunghyuk Cho, Shouheng Li, Sangdon Park, Dongwoo Kim	arXiv:2603.09257v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Analyzing the generalization of graph neural networks (GNNs) on node classification is challenging: message passing induces dependencies that break the i.i.d. assumption underlying standard inductive generalization theory. While distribution-free transductive theory resolves this dependency issue, existing classical bounds are either intractable or correlate poorly with empirical generalization. To fill this gap, we propose a transductive generalization bound based on optimal transport, connecting generalization to intra-class concentration and inter-class separation in the feature space. Empirically, our bound aligns strongly with the generalization gap. Building on this representation-based approach, we analyze how feature geometry evolves under repeated message passing. This explains the non-monotonic relationship between GNN depth and generalization, providing new insights into oversmoothing research. Practically, our bound serves as a geometry-aware regularizer that corrects the harmful behavior of message passing, yielding consistent performance improvements. The code is available at https://github.com/ml-postech/Transductive-OT-Gen-Bound.	arXiv:2603.09257v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Analyzing the generalization of graph neural networks (GNNs) on node classification is challenging: message passing induces dependencies that break the i.i.d. assumption underlying standard inductive generalization theory. While distribution-free transductive theory resolves this dependency issue, existing classical bounds are either intractable or correlate poorly with empirical generalization. To fill this gap, we propose a transductive generalization bound based on optimal transport, connecting generalization to intra-class concentration and inter-class separation in the feature space. Empirically, our bound aligns strongly with the generalization gap. Building on this representation-based approach, we analyze how feature geometry evolves under repeated message passing. This explains the non-monotonic relationship between GNN depth and generalization, providing new insights into oversmoothing research. Practically, our bound serves as a geometry-aware regularizer that corrects the harmful behavior of message passing, yielding consistent performance improvements. The code is available at https://github.com/ml-postech/Transductive-OT-Gen-Bound.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.006863+00	\N	\N	a8f88c656d24dfb90f1df5c589779a196dde880ff408cf9a2aa84636776da335	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.007225+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.007226+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1076d8fe-4cf8-4b8d-b5ff-6a439e298e29	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.25687v2	On Neural Scaling Laws for Weather Emulation through Continual Training	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25687	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25687	Shashank Subramanian, Alexander Kiefer, Arnur Nigmetov, Amir Gholami, Dmitriy Morozov, Michael W. Mahoney	arXiv:2603.25687v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Neural scaling laws, which in some domains can predict the performance of large neural networks as a function of model, data, and compute scale, are the cornerstone of building foundation models in Natural Language Processing and Computer Vision. We study neural scaling in Scientific Machine Learning, focusing on models for weather forecasting. To analyze scaling behavior in as simple a setting as possible, we adopt a minimal, scalable, general-purpose Swin Transformer architecture, and we use continual training with constant learning rates and periodic cooldowns as an efficient training strategy. We show that models trained in this minimalist way follow predictable scaling trends and can outperform standard cosine learning rate schedules. Cooldown phases can be repurposed to improve downstream performance, e.g., enabling accurate multi-step rollouts over longer forecast horizons or sharper predictions through spectral loss adjustments. We also systematically explore a wide range of model and dataset sizes under various compute budgets to construct IsoFLOP curves, and we identify compute-optimal training regimes. Extrapolating these trends to larger scales highlights potential performance limits, demonstrating that neural scaling can serve as an important diagnostic for efficient resource allocation. We open-source our code for reproducibility	arXiv:2603.25687v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Neural scaling laws, which in some domains can predict the performance of large neural networks as a function of model, data, and compute scale, are the cornerstone of building foundation models in Natural Language Processing and Computer Vision. We study neural scaling in Scientific Machine Learning, focusing on models for weather forecasting. To analyze scaling behavior in as simple a setting as possible, we adopt a minimal, scalable, general-purpose Swin Transformer architecture, and we use continual training with constant learning rates and periodic cooldowns as an efficient training strategy. We show that models trained in this minimalist way follow predictable scaling trends and can outperform standard cosine learning rate schedules. Cooldown phases can be repurposed to improve downstream performance, e.g., enabling accurate multi-step rollouts over longer forecast horizons or sharper predictions through spectral loss adjustments. We also systematically explore a wide range of model and dataset sizes under various compute budgets to construct IsoFLOP curves, and we identify compute-optimal training regimes. Extrapolating these trends to larger scales highlights potential performance limits, demonstrating that neural scaling can serve as an important diagnostic for efficient resource allocation. We open-source our code for reproducibility	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.018759+00	\N	\N	cbf92ee6d72e2512e429272fc224e3f0d75303bfefc2a75f846af54ac8203fc9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.019086+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.019087+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5d05b89a-c637-41d5-93fa-f767c6a44f00	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.00686v2	Full-Gradient Successor Feature Learning for Multi-Task Transfer in Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00686	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00686	Ritish Shrirao, Aditya Priyadarshi, Raghuram Bharadwaj Diddigi	arXiv:2604.00686v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Successor Features (SFs) provide a principled framework for multi-task transfer in Reinforcement Learning (RL) by decoupling environment dynamics from reward functions. By learning representations of expected future feature occupancies, SFs enable knowledge acquired in one task to be transferred to other tasks with different reward functions. However, conventional SF learning relies on semi-gradient Temporal Difference (TD) updates, which, when combined with nonlinear function approximation, generally lack convergence guarantees and can exhibit instability. This motivates exploring alternative learning objectives that offer stronger theoretical properties. Inspired by Full-Gradient DQN, we propose Full-Gradient Successor Feature Representations Q-Learning (FG-SFRQL), which extends full-gradient optimization to successor feature learning by minimizing the squared SF Bellman residual and differentiating through both the prediction and the bootstrapped target. We establish almost-sure convergence of FG-SFRQL under randomized task sampling, characterize the objective it minimizes, and show that averaging the bootstrapped target over $N$ next-state transitions reduces double-sampling bias in proportion to $1/N$. Through experiments in both discrete- and continuous-state domains, we demonstrate that full-gradient learning achieves lower Bellman residuals and improved transfer performance compared to semi-gradient baselines, with FG-SFRQL attaining the highest final return across all five evaluated domains.	arXiv:2604.00686v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Successor Features (SFs) provide a principled framework for multi-task transfer in Reinforcement Learning (RL) by decoupling environment dynamics from reward functions. By learning representations of expected future feature occupancies, SFs enable knowledge acquired in one task to be transferred to other tasks with different reward functions. However, conventional SF learning relies on semi-gradient Temporal Difference (TD) updates, which, when combined with nonlinear function approximation, generally lack convergence guarantees and can exhibit instability. This motivates exploring alternative learning objectives that offer stronger theoretical properties. Inspired by Full-Gradient DQN, we propose Full-Gradient Successor Feature Representations Q-Learning (FG-SFRQL), which extends full-gradient optimization to successor feature learning by minimizing the squared SF Bellman residual and differentiating through both the prediction and the bootstrapped target. We establish almost-sure convergence of FG-SFRQL under randomized task sampling, characterize the objective it minimizes, and show that averaging the bootstrapped target over $N$ next-state transitions reduces double-sampling bias in proportion to $1/N$. Through experiments in both discrete- and continuous-state domains, we demonstrate that full-gradient learning achieves lower Bellman residuals and improved transfer performance compared to semi-gradient baselines, with FG-SFRQL attaining the highest final return across all five evaluated domains.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.032461+00	\N	\N	66fa6bc3047970bc877a7b1cbde3a0d3d8fffcda4c79df35effc3fa8f882305a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.032817+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.03282+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3110967f-9590-4064-86cc-b1e11ede825e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.07383v3	SCOT: Soft Correspondence via Optimal Transport for Multi-Source Cross-City Transfer	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07383	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07383	Yuyao Wang, Min Yang, Meng Chen, Weiming Huang, Yilong Yin, Yongshun Gong	arXiv:2604.07383v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Cross-city transfer uses labeled data from well-instrumented cities to improve prediction in label-scarce ones. The task is difficult when cities have incompatible region partitions and no ground-truth correspondence. Existing methods either rely on heuristic anchors, which can create hubness, or align global distributions, which can over-mix heterogeneous regions. We propose SCOT (Soft Correspondence via Optimal Transport), an optimal transport-based framework for learning explicit, mass-controlled soft correspondences between unequal region sets. SCOT combines an optimal transport-weighted contrastive loss, a one-sided cycle regularizer, and a shared prototype hub for multi-source transfer. Across real-world cities and tasks, SCOT consistently outperforms strong baselines, reducing MAE and MAPE by 5-50%. Additional analyses show that the learned correspondences are selective, region-specific, and robust to heterogeneous settings.	arXiv:2604.07383v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Cross-city transfer uses labeled data from well-instrumented cities to improve prediction in label-scarce ones. The task is difficult when cities have incompatible region partitions and no ground-truth correspondence. Existing methods either rely on heuristic anchors, which can create hubness, or align global distributions, which can over-mix heterogeneous regions. We propose SCOT (Soft Correspondence via Optimal Transport), an optimal transport-based framework for learning explicit, mass-controlled soft correspondences between unequal region sets. SCOT combines an optimal transport-weighted contrastive loss, a one-sided cycle regularizer, and a shared prototype hub for multi-source transfer. Across real-world cities and tasks, SCOT consistently outperforms strong baselines, reducing MAE and MAPE by 5-50%. Additional analyses show that the learned correspondences are selective, region-specific, and robust to heterogeneous settings.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.043124+00	\N	\N	946c7b2e6bc29d12fde82e675ff7a12989ca20d01a5ac297b112599ce57de318	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.043742+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.043746+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
89d3453f-2cc3-447e-9d35-755c2bfcf141	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.19530v3	Calibrating Scientific Foundation Models with Inference-Time Stochastic Attention	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19530	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19530	Akash Yadav, Taiwo A. Adebiyi, Ruda Zhang	arXiv:2604.19530v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Transformer-based scientific foundation models are increasingly deployed in high-stakes settings, but current architectures give deterministic outputs and provide limited support for calibrated predictive uncertainty. We propose Stochastic Attention, a lightweight sample-average inference-time modification that randomizes attention by replacing softmax weights with normalized multinomial samples controlled by a single concentration parameter, and produces predictive ensembles without retraining. To set this parameter, we introduce a calibration objective that matches the stochastic attention output with the target, yielding an efficient univariate post-hoc tuning problem. We evaluate this mechanism on scientific foundation models for weather and time-series forecasting, as well as a regression task. Across benchmarks against baseline methods, Stochastic Attention achieves the strongest native calibration and the sharpest prediction intervals at comparable calibration, with adaptation costs nearly three orders of magnitude lower than the next-best baseline. Code is available at https://github.com/UQUH/StochasticAttention.	arXiv:2604.19530v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Transformer-based scientific foundation models are increasingly deployed in high-stakes settings, but current architectures give deterministic outputs and provide limited support for calibrated predictive uncertainty. We propose Stochastic Attention, a lightweight sample-average inference-time modification that randomizes attention by replacing softmax weights with normalized multinomial samples controlled by a single concentration parameter, and produces predictive ensembles without retraining. To set this parameter, we introduce a calibration objective that matches the stochastic attention output with the target, yielding an efficient univariate post-hoc tuning problem. We evaluate this mechanism on scientific foundation models for weather and time-series forecasting, as well as a regression task. Across benchmarks against baseline methods, Stochastic Attention achieves the strongest native calibration and the sharpest prediction intervals at comparable calibration, with adaptation costs nearly three orders of magnitude lower than the next-best baseline. Code is available at https://github.com/UQUH/StochasticAttention.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.054748+00	\N	\N	fcf748aac73be991884dd11177a2890884479a457fa52be0ab5acb51b106bae4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.055065+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.055067+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
