c9be9eb1-e251-4446-8a15-91cab451b87f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.06281v2	INEUS: Iterative Neural Solver for High-Dimensional PIDEs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06281	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06281	Jean-Loup Dupret, Davide Gallon, Patrick Cheridito	arXiv:2605.06281v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: In this paper, we introduce INEUS, a meshfree iterative neural solver for partial integro-differential equations (PIDEs). The method replaces the explicit evaluation of nonlocal jump integrals with single-jump sampling and reformulates PIDE solving as a sequence of recursive regression problems. Like Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), INEUS learns global solutions over the entire space-time domain, yet it offers a more efficient treatment of nonlocal terms and avoids the computationally expensive differentiation of full PIDE residuals. These features make INEUS particularly well suited for high-dimensional PDEs and PIDEs. Supported by a contraction-based convergence proof for linear PIDEs, our numerical experiments show that INEUS delivers accurate and scalable solutions for various high-dimensional linear and nonlinear examples.	arXiv:2605.06281v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: In this paper, we introduce INEUS, a meshfree iterative neural solver for partial integro-differential equations (PIDEs). The method replaces the explicit evaluation of nonlocal jump integrals with single-jump sampling and reformulates PIDE solving as a sequence of recursive regression problems. Like Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), INEUS learns global solutions over the entire space-time domain, yet it offers a more efficient treatment of nonlocal terms and avoids the computationally expensive differentiation of full PIDE residuals. These features make INEUS particularly well suited for high-dimensional PDEs and PIDEs. Supported by a contraction-based convergence proof for linear PIDEs, our numerical experiments show that INEUS delivers accurate and scalable solutions for various high-dimensional linear and nonlinear examples.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.088264+00	\N	\N	8853edc3ae84491878581cf1879f77a74c34fece9b06e91fd4b0abdde7c71d8f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.088682+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.088684+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4d1e5d18-d5cf-4464-91fc-427476dc73c4	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/advancing-youth-safety-and-opportunity-through-global-leadership	Advancing youth safety and opportunity through global leadership	https://openai.com/index/advancing-youth-safety-and-opportunity-through-global-leadership	https://openai.com/index/advancing-youth-safety-and-opportunity-through-global-leadership	\N	OpenAI calls for global action on youth AI safety, proposing an international institute to strengthen safeguards, standards, and opportunities for young people.	OpenAI calls for global action on youth AI safety, proposing an international institute to strengthen safeguards, standards, and opportunities for young people.	opinion	en	2026-06-02 07:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.677463+00	3	\N	3605432906164c141b2e2c060a747a6959378575cbe682e64f31b15b96bcf601	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.677824+00	2026-11-10 14:25:05.500501+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5e948d14-f9d1-4941-bc7b-73ef4de1af0b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.07270v2	bispectrum: Selective G-Bispectra Made Practical	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07270	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07270	Johan Mathe, Adele Myers, Simon Mataigne, Nina Miolane	arXiv:2605.07270v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Many machine learning tasks are invariant under transformations such as translations and rotations. The $G$-bispectrum is a complete invariant that determines a signal up to a group action, but its computation is costly and existing implementations each cover a single group. We present bispectrum, an open-source, fully unit-tested PyTorch library providing efficient, differentiable selective $G$-bispectra for seven group actions. Selectivity reduces the number of coefficients from $O(|G|^2)$ to $O(|G|)$ for finite groups, from $O(m^{5/2})$ to $O(m)$ for planar rotations on the disk with $m$ harmonics, and from $\\Theta(L^3)$ to $\\Theta(L^2)$ for spherical 3D rotations using our augmented selective spherical bispectrum at band limit $L$. The library is invariant up to floating-point error and computes selective $G$-bispectra in sub-millisecond time on GPU. We evaluate them as pooling layers on PatchCamelyon and OrganMNIST3D against five baselines. Across 46 comparisons, bispectral pooling remains competitive, matching all baselines on PCam and improving on max pooling by 5.0 percentage points on OrganMNIST3D. On Spherical MNIST, our method with an MLP head reaches 95.8% on rotated test digits, matching a spherical CNN at $4\\times$ lower latency and coming within 0.3 percentage points of a tensor-product network at $364\\times$ lower latency. Selective $G$-bispectra provide fast, competitive invariant layers for finite and continuous groups.	arXiv:2605.07270v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Many machine learning tasks are invariant under transformations such as translations and rotations. The $G$-bispectrum is a complete invariant that determines a signal up to a group action, but its computation is costly and existing implementations each cover a single group. We present bispectrum, an open-source, fully unit-tested PyTorch library providing efficient, differentiable selective $G$-bispectra for seven group actions. Selectivity reduces the number of coefficients from $O(|G|^2)$ to $O(|G|)$ for finite groups, from $O(m^{5/2})$ to $O(m)$ for planar rotations on the disk with $m$ harmonics, and from $\\Theta(L^3)$ to $\\Theta(L^2)$ for spherical 3D rotations using our augmented selective spherical bispectrum at band limit $L$. The library is invariant up to floating-point error and computes selective $G$-bispectra in sub-millisecond time on GPU. We evaluate them as pooling layers on PatchCamelyon and OrganMNIST3D against five baselines. Across 46 comparisons, bispectral pooling remains competitive, matching all baselines on PCam and improving on max pooling by 5.0 percentage points on OrganMNIST3D. On Spherical MNIST, our method with an MLP head reaches 95.8% on rotated test digits, matching a spherical CNN at $4\\times$ lower latency and coming within 0.3 percentage points of a tensor-product network at $364\\times$ lower latency. Selective $G$-bispectra provide fast, competitive invariant layers for finite and continuous groups.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.098825+00	\N	\N	5bb0b3c131f71d7883174f167cafbb759b5749d35b947e2cc602671ce75b8fad	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.099174+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.099176+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5112d0d3-e7a5-40b8-b072-4c25e5433c76	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.08007v2	Interpreting Reinforcement Learning Agents with Susceptibilities	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08007	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08007	Chris Elliott, Einar Urdshals, David Quarel, Daniel Murfet	arXiv:2605.08007v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Susceptibilities are a technique for neural network interpretability that studies the response of posterior expectation values of observables to perturbations of the loss. We generalize this construction to the setting of the regret in deep reinforcement learning and investigate the utility of susceptibilities in a simple gridworld model that nevertheless exhibits non-trivial stagewise development. We argue that susceptibilities reveal internal features of the development of the model in parameter space that one cannot detect purely by studying the development of the learned policy. We validate these results with activation-steering, and discuss the framework's extension to RLHF post-training.	arXiv:2605.08007v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Susceptibilities are a technique for neural network interpretability that studies the response of posterior expectation values of observables to perturbations of the loss. We generalize this construction to the setting of the regret in deep reinforcement learning and investigate the utility of susceptibilities in a simple gridworld model that nevertheless exhibits non-trivial stagewise development. We argue that susceptibilities reveal internal features of the development of the model in parameter space that one cannot detect purely by studying the development of the learned policy. We validate these results with activation-steering, and discuss the framework's extension to RLHF post-training.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.106002+00	\N	\N	a835a071a2c6bc31ea68b60975762d2573115aa6e13b7b024e543653f45c2710	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.106409+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03