ceed44a4-51be-4859-ae15-c5c12355011b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.14029v2	Utility-Constrained Policy Optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14029	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14029	Mehrdad Moghimi, Bernardo Avila Pires	arXiv:2606.14029v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Constrained MDPs (CMDPs) are a widely adopted framework for incorporating safety into RL agents; however, the framework does not support risk-sensitive constraints. This can be problematic: For example, CMDPs allow for optimal solutions that, in order to satisfy the risk-neutral constraints, mix infrequent catastrophic behaviors and frequent, overly conservative ones. Moreover, prior empirical results suggest that enforcing stricter, risk-sensitive constraints can improve performance even under risk-neutral evaluation. The natural framework to incorporate risk-sensitive constraints is utility-constrained MDPs (UCMDPs), but no practical solutions for this problem existed. In this work, we introduce a simple yet powerful methodology for UCMDPs and constrained RL. Besides allowing for risk-sensitive constraints, our framework does not require us to fix constraint limits in advance of training the agent, provided that a sensible range is known. This increases policy flexibility and, in practice, allows for adjustments to these limits at no extra training cost. Besides benefiting from the generality of the framework, our agent shows strong performance in practice, consistently matching or outperforming existing baselines in several Safety Gymnasium benchmark tasks.	arXiv:2606.14029v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Constrained MDPs (CMDPs) are a widely adopted framework for incorporating safety into RL agents; however, the framework does not support risk-sensitive constraints. This can be problematic: For example, CMDPs allow for optimal solutions that, in order to satisfy the risk-neutral constraints, mix infrequent catastrophic behaviors and frequent, overly conservative ones. Moreover, prior empirical results suggest that enforcing stricter, risk-sensitive constraints can improve performance even under risk-neutral evaluation. The natural framework to incorporate risk-sensitive constraints is utility-constrained MDPs (UCMDPs), but no practical solutions for this problem existed. In this work, we introduce a simple yet powerful methodology for UCMDPs and constrained RL. Besides allowing for risk-sensitive constraints, our framework does not require us to fix constraint limits in advance of training the agent, provided that a sensible range is known. This increases policy flexibility and, in practice, allows for adjustments to these limits at no extra training cost. Besides benefiting from the generality of the framework, our agent shows strong performance in practice, consistently matching or outperforming existing baselines in several Safety Gymnasium benchmark tasks.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.245379+00	\N	\N	5dc4b3c62c761b73c71aab20dae1f3abe9b1f5111cc5031b87e8f2b34caccb12	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.245696+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.245698+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b181101d-9d88-484e-b2fb-659dcd37b831	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/openai-for-india	Introducing OpenAI for India	https://openai.com/index/openai-for-india	https://openai.com/index/openai-for-india	\N	OpenAI for India expands AI access across the country—building local infrastructure, powering enterprises, and advancing workforce skills.	OpenAI for India expands AI access across the country—building local infrastructure, powering enterprises, and advancing workforce skills.	product_update	en	2026-02-18 21:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:43.106273+00	4	\N	c8bddf6cd8c73fe680367ae4567c05b9660f7074a545a24f228a9f71b52d5434	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:43.106671+00	2026-11-10 14:16:10.897472+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2f695e7b-0559-443f-aaff-dd9ca00a3313	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.10458v2	QT-Net: Rethinking Evaluation of AI Models in Atomic Chemical Space	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10458	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10458	Pablo Mart\\'inez Crespo, Stefano Ribes, Richard Beckmann, Marisa Gliege, Santiago Miret, Robert S. Jordan, Vijay Kris Narasimhan, Martin Rahm, Roc\\'io Mercado	arXiv:2605.10458v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Atomic properties such as partial charges or multipoles encode chemically meaningful information that can inform downstream molecular property prediction, but their evaluation as machine learning targets has been complicated by the absence of a principled out-of-distribution evaluation protocol at the atomic level. In this work, we propose a held-out evaluation protocol that clusters atomic environments by SOAP descriptors and computes metrics accounting only for cluster labels unseen during training. Following this procedure, we use 5$\\times$5 cross-validation and Tukey's HSD to run a statistically rigorous comparison of E(3)-equivariant against non-equivariant, rotationally augmented models for predicting electron populations and multipoles of H, C, N, and O atoms. Building on our results, we introduce the Quantum Topological Neural Network (QT-Net), a rotationally augmented, non-equivariant graph neural network. We show that QT-Net can be used to infer properties of atoms in molecules from QM9 outside our training set, and that the atomic environments seen during training strongly affect the performance of molecular property prediction tasks. To further validate the framework, molecular dipole moments computed from QT-Net's per-atom outputs recover the ground-truth values reported in QM9. We release all code and data, including a JAX implementation of QT-Net.	arXiv:2605.10458v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Atomic properties such as partial charges or multipoles encode chemically meaningful information that can inform downstream molecular property prediction, but their evaluation as machine learning targets has been complicated by the absence of a principled out-of-distribution evaluation protocol at the atomic level. In this work, we propose a held-out evaluation protocol that clusters atomic environments by SOAP descriptors and computes metrics accounting only for cluster labels unseen during training. Following this procedure, we use 5$\\times$5 cross-validation and Tukey's HSD to run a statistically rigorous comparison of E(3)-equivariant against non-equivariant, rotationally augmented models for predicting electron populations and multipoles of H, C, N, and O atoms. Building on our results, we introduce the Quantum Topological Neural Network (QT-Net), a rotationally augmented, non-equivariant graph neural network. We show that QT-Net can be used to infer properties of atoms in molecules from QM9 outside our training set, and that the atomic environments seen during training strongly affect the performance of molecular property prediction tasks. To further validate the framework, molecular dipole moments computed from QT-Net's per-atom outputs recover the ground-truth values reported in QM9. We release all code and data, including a JAX implementation of QT-Net.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.123769+00	\N	\N	9985ef8415216d449538b886b0ff591578a3bb2ae62bda9041d4081aeb3adb0a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.124144+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.124145+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0d0007a1-9c07-413b-8234-f5158651da4d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.11289v2	Quotient-Categorical Representations for Bellman-Compatible Average-Reward Distributional Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11289	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11289	Ege C. Kaya, Aliasghar Pourghani, Vijay Gupta, Abolfazl Hashemi	arXiv:2605.11289v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Average-reward reinforcement learning requires estimating the gain and a state-dependent bias, which solves the Poisson equation only up to an additive constant. Distributional analogues of the bias inherit this same ambiguity. We introduce a quotient-space formulation in which state-indexed laws are identified up to a common translation, together with a categorical parameterization that respects this symmetry. On this space, we define a projected average-reward distributional operator and show that it is well-defined, non-expansive in a coordinate Cram\\'er metric, and admits fixed points. We interpret the projected fixed points through geometrically-stopped differential returns, whose categorical operators are contractive and whose fixed points converge in the Abel sense to a canonical fixed point of the projected undiscounted operator. We show that the means of the projected fixed points match the scalar bias up to an error caused by endpoint clipping of the categorical projections, and we bound this error. Finally, we establish almost-sure convergence and finite-iteration residual bounds for temporal-difference recursions with gain-centered rewards under i.i.d. and Markovian sampling. When the gain is unknown, we couple the recursion with an online gain estimator and prove the same guarantees under Markovian sampling, with a bound on the gain error.	arXiv:2605.11289v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Average-reward reinforcement learning requires estimating the gain and a state-dependent bias, which solves the Poisson equation only up to an additive constant. Distributional analogues of the bias inherit this same ambiguity. We introduce a quotient-space formulation in which state-indexed laws are identified up to a common translation, together with a categorical parameterization that respects this symmetry. On this space, we define a projected average-reward distributional operator and show that it is well-defined, non-expansive in a coordinate Cram\\'er metric, and admits fixed points. We interpret the projected fixed points through geometrically-stopped differential returns, whose categorical operators are contractive and whose fixed points converge in the Abel sense to a canonical fixed point of the projected undiscounted operator. We show that the means of the projected fixed points match the scalar bias up to an error caused by endpoint clipping of the categorical projections, and we bound this error. Finally, we establish almost-sure convergence and finite-iteration residual bounds for temporal-difference recursions with gain-centered rewards under i.i.d. and Markovian sampling. When the gain is unknown, we couple the recursion with an online gain estimator and prove the same guarantees under Markovian sampling, with a bound on the gain error.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.133396+00	\N	\N	6feb62138dc4541d21233bdb767455266b46527d0ddc7b965cf3237815616cbc	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.133753+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.133755+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5819f5bc-0d81-4f0e-82f3-781433722768	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.12492v2	Pion: A Spectrum-Preserving Optimizer via Orthogonal Equivalence Transformation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12492	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12492	Kexuan Shi, Hanxuan Li, Zeju Qiu, Yandong Wen, Simon Buchholz, Weiyang Liu	arXiv:2605.12492v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce Pion, a spectrum-preserving optimizer for large language model (LLM) training based on orthogonal equivalence transformation. Unlike additive optimizers such as Adam and Muon, Pion updates each weight matrix through left and right orthogonal transformations, preserving its singular values throughout training. This yields an optimization mechanism that modulates the geometry of weight matrices while keeping their spectral norm fixed. We derive the Pion update rule, systematically examine its design choices, and analyze its convergence behavior along with several key properties. Empirical results show that Pion offers a stable and competitive alternative to standard optimizers for both LLM pretraining and finetuning.	arXiv:2605.12492v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce Pion, a spectrum-preserving optimizer for large language model (LLM) training based on orthogonal equivalence transformation. Unlike additive optimizers such as Adam and Muon, Pion updates each weight matrix through left and right orthogonal transformations, preserving its singular values throughout training. This yields an optimization mechanism that modulates the geometry of weight matrices while keeping their spectral norm fixed. We derive the Pion update rule, systematically examine its design choices, and analyze its convergence behavior along with several key properties. Empirical results show that Pion offers a stable and competitive alternative to standard optimizers for both LLM pretraining and finetuning.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.138781+00	\N	\N	8329ef97797e51817e494