52b1151f-cd8c-430c-9d78-f1d1deeb8392	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.23357v3	SOAP-Bubbles $\\circ$\\ding{109} Structured Weight Uncertainty for Neural Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.23357	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.23357	Adrian Robert Minut, Nico Daheim, Marco Miani, Mohammad Emtiyaz Khan, Wu Lin, Thomas M\\"ollenhoff	arXiv:2606.23357v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Structured weight-uncertainty can improve many aspects of deep learning, but it remains costly to estimate and difficult to implement. Here, we show that these issues can be addressed by adapting the SOAP optimizer. Our key idea is to run IVON, an existing diagonal-covariance variational method, in the eigenspace of SOAP's preconditioner and then use the preconditioner to transform the diagonal estimate into a non-diagonal covariance. The resulting method has costs similar to those of SOAP and requires no drastic changes to training pipelines. We call the posteriors obtained in this way SOAP-Bubbles and our new optimizer Eigenspace-VON (EVON). We show that, for logistic regression, EVON can match the full Gaussian covariance and that, for language model pretraining, it yields significantly better results than existing diagonal-covariance methods. Our work makes it easier to estimate more expressive posterior distributions for deep learning at scale.	arXiv:2606.23357v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Structured weight-uncertainty can improve many aspects of deep learning, but it remains costly to estimate and difficult to implement. Here, we show that these issues can be addressed by adapting the SOAP optimizer. Our key idea is to run IVON, an existing diagonal-covariance variational method, in the eigenspace of SOAP's preconditioner and then use the preconditioner to transform the diagonal estimate into a non-diagonal covariance. The resulting method has costs similar to those of SOAP and requires no drastic changes to training pipelines. We call the posteriors obtained in this way SOAP-Bubbles and our new optimizer Eigenspace-VON (EVON). We show that, for logistic regression, EVON can match the full Gaussian covariance and that, for language model pretraining, it yields significantly better results than existing diagonal-covariance methods. Our work makes it easier to estimate more expressive posterior distributions for deep learning at scale.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.260182+00	\N	\N	5aa6f011b844fc059e0461d95728a5d7d745ff316d42d610439dca8bb4718b65	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.260493+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.260495+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a6cbdd39-2ae2-4bd4-814f-5827f6fee236	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.19235v2	GAE Falls Short in Imperfect-Information Self-Play Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19235	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19235	Zhiyuan Fan, Gabriele Farina	arXiv:2605.19235v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Competitive multi-agent reinforcement learning in imperfect-information games requires agents to act under partial observability and against adversarial opponents, necessitating stochastic policies. While self-play reinforcement learning with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) has achieved strong empirical success, its standard advantage estimator, generalized advantage estimation, suffers from additional variance due to the sampling of stochastic future actions. This variance is amplified in equilibrium self-play because of the stochastic nature of the equilibrium policy and persists even when the critic is exact. We address this bottleneck by introducing $Q$-boosting, a variance-reduced advantage estimator based on a centralized action-value critic, and propose Variance-Reduced Policy Optimization (VRPO), incorporating this new estimator. The algorithm replaces sampled multi-step backups with a multi-step Expected SARSA$(\\lambda)$ trace, computing policy expectations at each step to average out action-sampling noise, while retaining PPO's clipped objective and on-policy actor updates. Empirically, VRPO achieves strong performance in various games: In mid-sized games where exploitability is computable, VRPO lowers the exact exploitability by more than 15% compared to tuned baselines. In Dou Dizhu, it sets a new state of the art over PerfectDou. In Heads-Up No-Limit Texas Hold'em, it posts a positive win rate against Slumbot without any blueprint raise sizes, search, or subgame solving at test time.	arXiv:2605.19235v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Competitive multi-agent reinforcement learning in imperfect-information games requires agents to act under partial observability and against adversarial opponents, necessitating stochastic policies. While self-play reinforcement learning with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) has achieved strong empirical success, its standard advantage estimator, generalized advantage estimation, suffers from additional variance due to the sampling of stochastic future actions. This variance is amplified in equilibrium self-play because of the stochastic nature of the equilibrium policy and persists even when the critic is exact. We address this bottleneck by introducing $Q$-boosting, a variance-reduced advantage estimator based on a centralized action-value critic, and propose Variance-Reduced Policy Optimization (VRPO), incorporating this new estimator. The algorithm replaces sampled multi-step backups with a multi-step Expected SARSA$(\\lambda)$ trace, computing policy expectations at each step to average out action-sampling noise, while retaining PPO's clipped objective and on-policy actor updates. Empirically, VRPO achieves strong performance in various games: In mid-sized games where exploitability is computable, VRPO lowers the exact exploitability by more than 15% compared to tuned baselines. In Dou Dizhu, it sets a new state of the art over PerfectDou. In Heads-Up No-Limit Texas Hold'em, it posts a positive win rate against Slumbot without any blueprint raise sizes, search, or subgame solving at test time.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.177748+00	\N	\N	e96be005217785accd8efdc96fbac6b489a885030c86beffe14743956fd8f74b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.178094+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.178096+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
decbd4c9-ac5a-4204-9c46-f6669d5694eb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.21699v2	X-Token: Projection-Guided Cross-Tokenizer Knowledge Distillation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21699	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21699	Sharath Turuvekere Sreenivas, Adithyakrishna Venkatesh Hanasoge, Mingyu Yang, Ali Taghibakhshi, Saurav Muralidharan, Ashwath Aithal, Pavlo Molchanov	arXiv:2605.21699v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Cross-tokenizer knowledge distillation allows a student to learn from teachers with incompatible vocabularies while preserving its tokenizer and inference architecture. Existing logit-based methods either supervise only the observed token or match full distributions through strict token partitions and heuristic ranking. We identify two limitations: an uncommon-token failure, where task-critical tokens outside the shared vocabulary are suppressed, and over-conservative matching, where exact string equality discards useful correspondences. We propose X-Token, which addresses both through a sparse projection initialized from tokenizer-level string rules and optionally refined during distillation. P-KL removes the partition and projects the student distribution into the teacher vocabulary; H-KL retains the hybrid loss while relaxing matching through the projection. A token-coverage audit guides the choice between them, and both extend naturally to heterogeneous teachers. Across Llama, OLMo, and Gemma students, X-Token achieves the highest average accuracy in all four teacher-student settings among all evaluated cross-tokenizer methods. Complementary teachers provide a further 1.30-point gain, and both variants are faster than the evaluated cross-tokenizer baselines in matched profiling.	arXiv:2605.21699v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Cross-tokenizer knowledge distillation allows a student to learn from teachers with incompatible vocabularies while preserving its tokenizer and inference architecture. Existing logit-based methods either supervise only the observed token or match full distributions through strict token partitions and heuristic ranking. We identify two limitations: an uncommon-token failure, where task-critical tokens outside the shared vocabulary are suppressed, and over-conservative matching, where exact string equality discards useful correspondences. We propose X-Token, which addresses both through a sparse projection initialized from tokenizer-level string rules and optionally refined during distillation. P-KL removes the partition and projects the student distribution into the teacher vocabulary; H-KL retains the hybrid loss while relaxing matching through the projection. A token-coverage audit guides the choice between them, and both extend naturally to heterogeneous teachers. Across Llama, OLMo, and Gemma students, X-Token achieves the highest average accuracy in all four teacher-student settings among all evaluated cross-tokenizer methods. Complementary teachers provide a further 1.30-point gain, and both variants are faster than the evaluated cross-tokenizer baselines in matched profiling.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.187136+00	\N	\N	185149403786519f9f5cac7348619d11c5cd67baa6f35816b41fea682a536082	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.187476+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.187478+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
aadd0029-9a03-4cb6-a706-49294a0e7da8	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.24274v3	A lift for input-convex neural net training	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24274	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24274	Ali Siahkoohi	arXiv:2605.24274v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Input-convex neural nets parametrize the convex potentials of density models and transport maps, and their convexity requires the inter-layer weights to be non-negative. Projected gradient descent enforces this by projecting after each step, and due to mini-batch noise the boundary is re-crossed indefinitely, which leads to an active set the projection never identifies. The differentiable alternative, direct softplus, optimizes a free latent weight through a softplus positivity map. Where that latent weight is well below zero, the derivative of the map is exponentially small and scales down both the gradient and its noise. We call this region the shoulder, and a weight that reaches it therefore barely moves and stays for an exponentially long time. We propose the lift, which replaces the free latent weight by a learnable slack plus the output of a hypernetwork that reads a permutation-invariant summary of the training batch. The latent weight then varies with the same batch that forms the gradient, so the two are coupled. We show that this coupling adds a term to the variance of the update at first order, and that the slack, the batch dependence, and the shared batch are each needed for it. Where the coupling aligns positively with the loss curvature, a weight on the shoulder crosses the barriers of the loss sooner under the lift than under direct softplus, also under preconditioned stochastic gradient descent. We compare the lift with the two existing methods on several applications. Where a constrained weight of direct softplus reaches the shoulder, the lift fits the target more closely than direct softplus and reaches the same reconstruction about three times sooner. Where almost none reaches it, the methods agree.	arXiv:2605.24274v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Input-convex neural nets parametrize the convex potentials of density models and transport maps, and their convexity requires the inter-layer weights to be non-negative. Projected gradient descent enforces this by projecting after each step, and due to mini-batch noise the boundary is re-crossed indefinitely, which leads to an active set the projection never identifies. The differentiable alternative, direct softplus, optimizes a free latent weight through a softplus positivity map. Where that latent weight is well below zero, the derivative of the map is exponentially small and scales down both the gradient and its noise. We call this region the shoulder, and a weight that reaches it therefore barely moves and stays for an exponentially long time. We propose the lift, which replaces the free latent weight by a learnable slack plus the output of a hypernetwork that reads a permutation-invariant summary of the training batch. The latent weight then varies with the same batch that forms the gradient, so the two are coupled. We show that this coupling adds a term to the variance of the update at first order, and that the slack, the batch dependence, and the shared batch are each needed for it. Where the coupling aligns positively with the loss curvature, a weight on the shoulder crosses the barriers of the loss sooner under the lift than under direct softplus, also under preconditioned stochastic gradient descent. We compare the lift with the two existing methods on several applications. Where a constrained weight of direct softplus reaches the shoulder, the lift fits the target more closely than direct softplus and reaches the same reconstruction about three times sooner. Where almost none reaches it, the methods agree.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.196509+00	\N	\N	d55083e4e9c7ef333f8293ee4a426a20bbe0725906371c191435c5213c5fad7c	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.19688