10583085-dce3-48d1-b27c-d749806353b3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.10944v4	Express Language Modeling	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.10944	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.10944	Albert Gong, Annabelle Michael Carrell, Raaz Dwivedi, Lester Mackey	arXiv:2606.10944v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce a new tool, Express, for converting a non-causal attention approximation into a causal approximation with matching approximation guarantees. When combined with the state-of-the-art Thinformer approximation, Express improves upon the best known causal attention guarantees, delivering $\\log^{3/2}(n)/s$ approximation error with only $O(s)$ memory and $O(s^2 \\log^2(n))$ compression overhead for a sequence of length $n$. We pair these developments with an efficient I/O-aware Triton implementation, demonstrate substantial speedups over FlashAttention 2, and use Express to overcome four resource bottlenecks in the language modeling pipeline: long-context prefill, KV cache compression, long-form memory-constrained decoding, and long-form compute-constrained decoding.	arXiv:2606.10944v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce a new tool, Express, for converting a non-causal attention approximation into a causal approximation with matching approximation guarantees. When combined with the state-of-the-art Thinformer approximation, Express improves upon the best known causal attention guarantees, delivering $\\log^{3/2}(n)/s$ approximation error with only $O(s)$ memory and $O(s^2 \\log^2(n))$ compression overhead for a sequence of length $n$. We pair these developments with an efficient I/O-aware Triton implementation, demonstrate substantial speedups over FlashAttention 2, and use Express to overcome four resource bottlenecks in the language modeling pipeline: long-context prefill, KV cache compression, long-form memory-constrained decoding, and long-form compute-constrained decoding.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.23842+00	\N	\N	3d3c357d404a90313abfb933204cf85a38c17d8d40cb94147e3c31903ddcd153	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.238764+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.238765+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4a4ffe12-2a7a-470a-83a7-67c6333cab40	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.11711v2	Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711	Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy	arXiv:2606.11711v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Online learning with delayed feedback typically assumes that the learner can track all pending rounds until their feedback arrives. In practice, tracking resources are finite, and feedback from rounds that cannot be tracked is permanently lost. In this paper, we study delayed online convex optimization (OCO) under a hard capacity constraint, where at most $C$ pending rounds can be tracked at any time. To model delay information, we introduce a semi-clairvoyant model that refines the clairvoyant assumption from prior work: rather than requiring delays to be known at prediction time, the learner observes delay expirations online, consistent with the classical unconstrained delayed setting. Our approach proceeds via a reduction to a novel ``delayed and weighted'' OCO problem, using a scheduler that randomizes which rounds are tracked and importance-weights the resulting observations. For the base problem, we propose and analyze Delayed-Weighted FTRL and its bandit analogue, obtaining regret bounds that characterize how weights interact with delayed feedback in both settings. Combining these bounds with our schedulers yields regret guarantees for capacity-constrained OCO under convex and strongly convex losses, for both first-order and bandit feedback. For first-order feedback, capacity $C = \\Omega(\\log T)$ suffices to recover the standard delayed OCO rates up to logarithmic factors. For bandit feedback, the standard delayed BCO rates are instead modulated by $(1 + \\sigma_{\\text{max}}/C)$ factors, where $\\sigma_{\\text{max}}$ is the maximum number of outstanding observations. This allows the regret bound to degrade gracefully when $C < \\sigma_{\\text{max}}$. We complement our analysis with numerical experiments.	arXiv:2606.11711v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Online learning with delayed feedback typically assumes that the learner can track all pending rounds until their feedback arrives. In practice, tracking resources are finite, and feedback from rounds that cannot be tracked is permanently lost. In this paper, we study delayed online convex optimization (OCO) under a hard capacity constraint, where at most $C$ pending rounds can be tracked at any time. To model delay information, we introduce a semi-clairvoyant model that refines the clairvoyant assumption from prior work: rather than requiring delays to be known at prediction time, the learner observes delay expirations online, consistent with the classical unconstrained delayed setting. Our approach proceeds via a reduction to a novel ``delayed and weighted'' OCO problem, using a scheduler that randomizes which rounds are tracked and importance-weights the resulting observations. For the base problem, we propose and analyze Delayed-Weighted FTRL and its bandit analogue, obtaining regret bounds that characterize how weights interact with delayed feedback in both settings. Combining these bounds with our schedulers yields regret guarantees for capacity-constrained OCO under convex and strongly convex losses, for both first-order and bandit feedback. For first-order feedback, capacity $C = \\Omega(\\log T)$ suffices to recover the standard delayed OCO rates up to logarithmic factors. For bandit feedback, the standard delayed BCO rates are instead modulated by $(1 + \\sigma_{\\text{max}}/C)$ factors, where $\\sigma_{\\text{max}}$ is the maximum number of outstanding observations. This allows the regret bound to degrade gracefully when $C < \\sigma_{\\text{max}}$. We complement our analysis with numerical experiments.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.240641+00	\N	\N	f6a381e57be1ca9f421ef28323f301c39512db5bc5bad2d427c59f500e46d1b9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.240952+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.240953+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
77089db0-f246-4a2c-9241-7e70edd6b01a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.14095v3	Lyapunov-Based Sample Complexity Analysis for Weakly-Coupled MDPs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14095	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14095	Tianhao Wu, Matthew Zurek, Weina Wang, Qiaomin Xie	arXiv:2606.14095v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study the sample complexity of learning in average-reward weakly-coupled Markov decision processes (WCMDPs) and Restless Bandits (RBs) under a generative model. Naive reduction to a tabular MDP leads to high complexity bounds as the state-action space is exponentially large in the number of arms $N$. By exploiting the weakly coupled structure, we show that near-optimal policies can be learned with sample and computational complexities that are polynomial in $N$. Specifically, we analyze the plug-in approach, which applies an efficient planning algorithm to an empirical model estimated from data. For fully heterogeneous WCMDPs, we establish the first finite-sample PAC guarantee with polynomial complexity and an $O(1/\\sqrt{N})$ optimality gap. For homogeneous RBs, we further prove that a smaller optimality gap is achievable under mild structural assumptions. A primary technical contribution is a novel Lyapunov-based analysis framework. Unlike classical approaches that rely on the difficult-to-control bias function, our framework uses an explicitly constructed Lyapunov function along with a drift transfer technique between the true and empirical models. A key step of independent interest in our framework is a fine-grained perturbation analysis for the underlying linear programming (LP) relaxation, which provides a general tool for analyzing LP-based policies and weakly-coupled systems.	arXiv:2606.14095v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study the sample complexity of learning in average-reward weakly-coupled Markov decision processes (WCMDPs) and Restless Bandits (RBs) under a generative model. Naive reduction to a tabular MDP leads to high complexity bounds as the state-action space is exponentially large in the number of arms $N$. By exploiting the weakly coupled structure, we show that near-optimal policies can be learned with sample and computational complexities that are polynomial in $N$. Specifically, we analyze the plug-in approach, which applies an efficient planning algorithm to an empirical model estimated from data. For fully heterogeneous WCMDPs, we establish the first finite-sample PAC guarantee with polynomial complexity and an $O(1/\\sqrt{N})$ optimality gap. For homogeneous RBs, we further prove that a smaller optimality gap is achievable under mild structural assumptions. A primary technical contribution is a novel Lyapunov-based analysis framework. Unlike classical approaches that rely on the difficult-to-control bias function, our framework uses an explicitly constructed Lyapunov function along with a drift transfer technique between the true and empirical models. A key step of independent interest in our framework is a fine-grained perturbation analysis for the underlying linear programming (LP) relaxation, which provides a general tool for analyzing LP-based policies and weakly-coupled systems.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.247949+00	\N	\N	1ebc56a11e21be3d11317ff332a04956400e08f63ddfb735ff176635b64a155f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.248291+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.248292+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8cf103d6-a40a-4a24-a26a-3f754e03ed7b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.20820v3	CELEUS: Certifiable and Efficient LLM Evaluation via E-Processes	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.20820	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.20820	Zhijian Zhou, Zesheng Ye, Zhaorun Chen, Bo Li, Feng Liu	arXiv:2606.20820v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Can we trust evaluation scores to capture an LLM's true real-world performance? Certifiable evaluation answers this question by providing a confidence interval (CI) that covers the LLM's true performance with high probability (e.g., 95%). In particular, existing methods sequentially select evaluation samples from a fixed pool and keep updating the CI until certain conditions are satisfied, e.g., the CI width reaches a target precision. However, existing methods are not generally anytime-valid: the claimed coverage (e.g., 95%) may fail when CIs are repeatedly updated and used to decide when to stop, leaving a gap between theoretical rigor and practice. This paper bridges this gap by proposing CELEUS, a Certifiable framework for Efficient LLM evaluation, which leverages E-processes to build anytime-valid CIs. Concretely, we propose a new signal that combines two ingredients: (i) Uncertainty-guided sampling to select informative samples for evaluation, and (ii) Surrogate-assisted approximations for unevaluated samples. We prove that the new signal remains unbiased for the evaluation score conditional on the past, enabling statistically-grounded and anytime-valid e-process CIs. More importantly, the two ingredients reduce estimation variance and help reach the target precision with fewer evaluated samples. We also prove that CIs obtained by CELEUS can shrink at a near-parametric rate up to logarithmic factors and analyze the oracle variance-optimal sampling rule that motivates the empirical uncertainty-guided one. Experiments show that CELEUS reaches the target precision using 54-62% fewer evaluated samples than baselines, while preserving anytime-valid coverage. Code is available at https://github.com/zyecs/celeus.	arXiv:2606.20820v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Can we trust evaluation scores to capture an LLM's true real-world performance? Certifiable evaluation answers this question by providing a confidence interval (CI) that covers the LLM's true performance with high probability (e.g., 95%). In particular, existing methods sequentially select evaluation samples from a fixed pool and keep updating the CI until certain conditions are satisfied, e.g., the CI width reaches a target precision. However, existing methods are not generally anytime-valid: the claimed coverage (e.g., 95%) may fail when CIs are repeatedly updated and used to decide when to stop, leaving a gap between theoretical rigor and practice. This paper bridges this gap by proposing CELEUS, a Certifiable framework for Efficient LLM evaluation, which leverages E-processes to build anytime-valid CIs. Concretely, we propose a new signal that combines two ingredients: (i) Uncertainty-guided sampling to select informative samples for evaluation, and (ii) Surrogate-assisted approximations for unevaluated samples. We prove that the new signal remains unbiased for the evaluation score conditional on the past, enabling statistically-grounded and anytime-valid e-process CIs. More importantly, the two ingredients reduce estimation variance and help reach the target precision with fewer evaluated samples. We also prove that CIs obtained by CELEUS can shrink at a near-parametric rate up to logarithmic factors and analyze the oracle variance-optimal sampling rule that motivates the empirical uncertainty-guided one. Experiments show that CELEUS reaches the target precision using 54-62% fewer evaluated samples than baselines, while preserving anytime-valid coverage. Code is available at https://github.com/zyecs/celeus.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.255443+00	\N	\N	5156c3cca216f92bb673d026c1e08aedf5833c91fed4b8c483b18ec650f487c5	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.255756+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.255757+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d898e04d-8a1f-4e7c-9fd0-0e2973850d54	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09898v1	Generative AI and Oral Proficiency: How ChatGPT Is Transforming English Language Speaking Practice	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09898	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09898	Mohammad Mousazadeh	arXiv:2611.09898v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: The integration of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) into language learning contexts presents potential for developing oral proficiency, though empirical evidence regarding its efficacy remains limited. This quasi-experimental study investigated the impact of ChatGPT-mediated speaking practice on English oral proficiency among Iranian learners. A total of 380 participants (190 female, 190 male; M(age) = 21.4, SD = 4.7) from Tehran and Isfahan were assigned to experimental (ChatGPT practice; n = 190) or control (human pair work; n = 190) groups. Both groups completed an 8-week intervention with three 30-minute weekly sessions. Oral proficiency was assessed using a Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR)-aligned Oral Proficiency Interview (OPI) scored on fluency, pronunciation, lexical resource, grammatical accuracy, and interactive communication (total score: 0-20). Multilevel growth modeling revealed a significant time-by-group interaction, F(1, 376) = 48.73, p < .001, partial eta-squared = .115. The experimental group demonstrated greater gains (M change = 2.90, SD = 1.60) than controls (M change = 0.75, SD = 1.80), d = 1.20, 95% CI [0.95, 1.45]. Gender moderated this effect, with females in the experimental group showing the largest improvements (M change = 2.50). Mediation analysis confirmed that increased integrative motivation partially mediated proficiency gains (indirect effect = 0.28, 95% CI [0.20, 0.36]). Findings indicate that structured ChatGPT practice yields modest but meaningful enhancements in oral proficiency, particularly when leveraging affective factors. Implications for AI-augmented speaking pedagogy in resource-constrained contexts are discussed.	arXiv:2611.09898v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: The integration of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) into language learning contexts presents potential for developing oral proficiency, though empirical evidence regarding its efficacy remains limited. This quasi-experimental study investigated the impact of ChatGPT-mediated speaking practice on English oral proficiency among Iranian learners. A total of 380 participants (190 female, 190 male; M(age) = 21.4, SD = 4.7) from Tehran and Isfahan were assigned to experimental (ChatGPT practice; n = 190) or control (human pair work; n = 190) groups. Both groups completed an 8-week intervention with three 30-minute weekly sessions. Oral proficiency was assessed using a Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR)-aligned Oral Proficiency Interview (OPI) scored on fluency, pronunciation, lexical resource, grammatical accuracy, and interactive communication (total score: 0-20). Multilevel growth modeling revealed a significant time-by-group interaction, F(1, 376) = 48.73, p < .001, partial eta-squared = .115. The experimental group demonstrated greater gains (M change = 2.90, SD = 1.60) than controls (M change = 0.75, SD = 1.80), d = 1.20, 95% CI [0.95, 1.45]. Gender moderated this effect, with females in the experimental group showing the largest improvements (M change = 2.50). Mediation analysis confirmed that increased integrative motivation partially mediated proficiency gains (indirect effect = 0.28, 95% CI [0.20, 0.36]). Findings indicate that structured ChatGPT practice yields modest but meaningful enhancements in oral proficiency, particularly when leveraging affective factors. Implications for AI-augmented speaking pedagogy in resource-constrained contexts are discussed.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.952624+00	\N	\N	e8a43eed5e9326a2990ec04ecd7fe6d4bc37ca38cf7efc6030e0821c13d32989	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.952911+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.952912+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
45422a30-de0b-45f5-9026-0d69c585337f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2607.01571v2	Geometric Signatures of Reasoning: A Spectral Perspective on Task Hardness	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01571	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01571	Aria Masoomi, Mahsa Bazzaz, Adel Javanmard, Vahab Mirrokni	arXiv:2607.01571v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning enables large language models (LLMs) to solve complex problems by generating intermediate reasoning steps. While much attention has been paid to the length and content of these reasoning chains, far less is known about their internal geometry. We study the geometry of CoT trajectories in the hidden state space of transformer models, formalizing each reasoning chain as a discrete curve in $\\mathbb{R}^d$ and characterizing it through spectral, positional, and kinematic geometric functionals. We introduce the effective dimension $d_\\rho$ as a measure of trajectory complexity and empirically show that harder tasks exhibit greater effective dimension. Lastly, we explore how kinematic features of the trajectory, mean position, positional dispersion, initial and final hidden states, mean velocity, mean speed, and speed dispersion, can be used to predict solution correctness before generation is complete, and may inform future early-stopping strategies. Experimentally, on 86 mathematical reasoning problems from the MATH500 dataset and three Qwen2.5 model scales (0.5B, 1.5B, and 7B), $d_\\rho$ achieves difficulty-prediction AUROC of $0.90$, $0.94$, and $0.96$, respectively. Seven kinematic and positional features predict correctness from only the first $20\\%$ of generated tokens, with AUROC between $0.77$ and $0.88$ across scales. These correctness signatures transfer across held-out questions, establishing that the shape of a model's internal reasoning trajectory is a principled window into both task hardness and solution quality.	arXiv:2607.01571v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning enables large language models (LLMs) to solve complex problems by generating intermediate reasoning steps. While much attention has been paid to the length and content of these reasoning chains, far less is known about their internal geometry. We study the geometry of CoT trajectories in the hidden state space of transformer models, formalizing each reasoning chain as a discrete curve in $\\mathbb{R}^d$ and characterizing it through spectral, positional, and kinematic geometric functionals. We introduce the effective dimension $d_\\rho$ as a measure of trajectory complexity and empirically show that harder tasks exhibit greater effective dimension. Lastly, we explore how kinematic features of the trajectory, mean position, positional dispersion, initial and final hidden states, mean velocity, mean speed, and speed dispersion, can be used to predict solution correctness before generation is complete, and may inform future early-stopping strategies. Experimentally, on 86 mathematical reasoning problems from the MATH500 dataset and three Qwen2.5 model scales (0.5B, 1.5B, and 7B), $d_\\rho$ achieves difficulty-prediction AUROC of $0.90$, $0.94$, and $0.96$, respectively. Seven kinematic and positional features predict correctness from only the first $20\\%$ of generated tokens, with AUROC between $0.77$ and $0.88$ across scales. These correctness signatures transfer across held-out questions, establishing that the shape of a model's internal reasoning trajectory is a principled window into both task hardness and solution quality.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.279891+00	\N	\N	be6981f693fe87a6a7a759578f294eb9fe5c52957f4c68d8fb681a8a5c68ae18	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.280235+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.280236+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
df197679-0ccd-4ab7-a687-227cdf5a4b00	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/openai-to-acquire-ona	OpenAI to acquire Ona	https://openai.com/index/openai-to-acquire-ona	https://openai.com/index/openai-to-acquire-ona	\N	OpenAI plans to acquire Ona to expand Codex with secure, persistent cloud environments, enabling long-running AI agents across enterprise workflows.	OpenAI plans to acquire Ona to expand Codex with secure, persistent cloud environments, enabling long-running AI agents across enterprise workflows.	news	en	2026-06-11 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.622743+00	4	\N	54eed7465d454c076c1b99e662faf4a3ad66c15a77d0845d609e3466572feebc	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.623122+00	2026-11-10 14:26:12.765851+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
71def9c4-4c45-40d5-8120-e2b0d97049fb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2607.18516v2	Signed Rectified Flow: Negativity-Controlled Generation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18516	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18516	Runlong Liao, Baiyu Su, Lizhang Chen, Qiang Liu	arXiv:2607.18516v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce Signed Rectified Flow (Signed RF), a generalization of Rectified Flow that targets the signed measure $\\pi^{sign} = (1+\\alpha)\\pi^+ - \\alpha\\pi^-$, where $\\alpha>0$, $\\pi^+$ is the distribution to promote, and $\\pi^-$ is the distribution to suppress. Although direct sampling from a signed measure is not well-defined, Signed RF induces a valid generative process that concentrates probability in regions where the signed measure is positive while provably excluding regions dominated by its negative component. It therefore provides a principled framework for incorporating negative information and exclusion constraints into generative modeling. We analyze the signed continuity equation underlying Signed RF and use a charged-particle interpretation to explain how negative mass forms exclusion barriers. This theory further motivates practical adaptive guidance algorithms. Across several applications, Signed RF improves the fidelity-diversity trade-off on ImageNet, reduces nearest-neighbor similarity in anti-memorization experiments, and reduces nudity induced by adversarial prompts in Stable Diffusion 3.5 while preserving CLIP and aesthetic scores.	arXiv:2607.18516v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce Signed Rectified Flow (Signed RF), a generalization of Rectified Flow that targets the signed measure $\\pi^{sign} = (1+\\alpha)\\pi^+ - \\alpha\\pi^-$, where $\\alpha>0$, $\\pi^+$ is the distribution to promote, and $\\pi^-$ is the distribution to suppress. Although direct sampling from a signed measure is not well-defined, Signed RF induces a valid generative process that concentrates probability in regions where the signed measure is positive while provably excluding regions dominated by its negative component. It therefore provides a principled framework for incorporating negative information and exclusion constraints into generative modeling. We analyze the signed continuity equation underlying Signed RF and use a charged-particle interpretation to explain how negative mass forms exclusion barriers. This theory further motivates practical adaptive guidance algorithms. Across several applications, Signed RF improves the fidelity-diversity trade-off on ImageNet, reduces nearest-neighbor similarity in anti-memorization experiments, and reduces nudity induced by adversarial prompts in Stable Diffusion 3.5 while preserving CLIP and aesthetic scores.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.300593+00	\N	\N	aabe031accf5719801f6801586a890266f77554c7405a6cf9d94061808d36838	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.300931+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.300933+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d5bde6be-3970-4ddd-af6b-4d43cc3eaadc	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2607.18642v3	Spaghetti Architect: A Controlled Code Benchmark Generator, and What Its Own Comments Do to Model Scores	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18642	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18642	Yuxiang Ji	arXiv:2607.18642v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Code corpora mined from the wild cannot change one thing at a time: a program's behaviour, messiness and size vary together, and nothing marks the correct answer. We present Spaghetti Architect, a generator that turns a small JSON description of a program into deliberately messy programs in five languages. It compiles and runs every program, checks the output against a reference computed from the description, and labels each program by problem size and by messiness. This control lets us change one property at a time and still know the correct answer. The generator writes comments into its own output, so we first re-render identical code without them. Removing the comments lowers the refactoring score of all four models we tested, and lowers the weakest model's about ten times as much as the strongest model's; a pre-registered replication confirms the difference at a smaller magnitude. The comments also hide an effect: messiness leaves scores flat while they are present and lowers them on both tasks once they are gone. We therefore recommend evaluating on the comment-free rendering. Changing only problem size drives direct-answer aggregation accuracy to zero for every model, and changing only literal values moves scores by less than 0.012. The generator, the dataset and the evaluation records are open source.	arXiv:2607.18642v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Code corpora mined from the wild cannot change one thing at a time: a program's behaviour, messiness and size vary together, and nothing marks the correct answer. We present Spaghetti Architect, a generator that turns a small JSON description of a program into deliberately messy programs in five languages. It compiles and runs every program, checks the output against a reference computed from the description, and labels each program by problem size and by messiness. This control lets us change one property at a time and still know the correct answer. The generator writes comments into its own output, so we first re-render identical code without them. Removing the comments lowers the refactoring score of all four models we tested, and lowers the weakest model's about ten times as much as the strongest model's; a pre-registered replication confirms the difference at a smaller magnitude. The comments also hide an effect: messiness leaves scores flat while they are present and lowers them on both tasks once they are gone. We therefore recommend evaluating on the comment-free rendering. Changing only problem size drives direct-answer aggregation accuracy to zero for every model, and changing only literal values moves scores by less than 0.012. The generator, the dataset and the evaluation records are open source.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.303112+00	\N	\N	2427255042c8bae32d8c8f7bee1f8a892ab75e181a4ef46364972914aae81faa	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.30344+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.303441+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
07fd7e3e-bd13-4fb5-a067-18d3328b2f35	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.01692v4	Beckmann Transport Models: From Autonomous Flows to One-Step Maps	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01692	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01692	Lee Cheuk-Kit, Florentin Coeurdoux, Yuyuan Chen, Sophia Tang, Peter Potaptchik, Yilun Du, Michael Samuel Albergo, Eric Vanden-Eijnden	arXiv:2608.01692v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We propose an instantiation of flow matching that relies on a time-independent velocity field---an autonomous flow---to exactly map between two distributions when the target is supported on a sufficiently lower-dimensional data manifold. We also show that the associated one-step generative map is constant in the direction of the autonomous velocity field and therefore satisfies a simple conservation equation, which motivates learning the map directly from samples. These autonomous flows and maps give a dynamical meaning to the flux constraint of Beckmann's transportation problem, and we show that, under suitable conditions, the construction extends beyond flow matching to other probability fluxes satisfying the same constraint. The resulting framework includes, for instance, a closed-form Coulomb transport closely related to Poisson Flow and a flow-matching-consistent version of Equilibrium Matching. We illustrate these distinctions and demonstrate the effectiveness of the autonomous flow and the one-step map on ImageNet $256\\times256$.	arXiv:2608.01692v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We propose an instantiation of flow matching that relies on a time-independent velocity field---an autonomous flow---to exactly map between two distributions when the target is supported on a sufficiently lower-dimensional data manifold. We also show that the associated one-step generative map is constant in the direction of the autonomous velocity field and therefore satisfies a simple conservation equation, which motivates learning the map directly from samples. These autonomous flows and maps give a dynamical meaning to the flux constraint of Beckmann's transportation problem, and we show that, under suitable conditions, the construction extends beyond flow matching to other probability fluxes satisfying the same constraint. The resulting framework includes, for instance, a closed-form Coulomb transport closely related to Poisson Flow and a flow-matching-consistent version of Equilibrium Matching. We illustrate these distinctions and demonstrate the effectiveness of the autonomous flow and the one-step map on ImageNet $256\\times256$.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.315456+00	\N	\N	de88270e6faebaa27781ddefd9fbe12782b4a4dea8986222b95f56354d9edcd5	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.315775+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.315776+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
522315ac-ab97-4d1f-bd44-571ccd5f5c37	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.17070v3	Certified but Private: Scalable Zero-Knowledge Proofs for Neural Network Guarantees	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17070	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17070	Youwei Zhong, Ben Merbaum, Timos Antonopoulos, Ning Luo, Charalampos Papamanthou, Katerina Sotiraki, Ruzica Piskac	arXiv:2608.17070v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: With the growing deployment of machine learning models, formal guarantees of the robustness and fairness of these models have become increasingly important in safety-critical and legal-compliance settings. However, model parameters are often commercial secrets that cannot be disclosed to auditors or end users. To this end, we present PANDA, a scalable system that uses zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) to prove the robustness and fairness properties of a model without revealing its private parameters. PANDA is built on top of CROWN, an efficient robustness certification framework that is used in many state-of-the-art formal verification tools for neural networks. The core contribution of PANDA is a novel algorithm for proving linear relaxation bounds for non-linear activation layers, yielding simple, lightweight proofs. Remarkably, our implementation can generate proofs of local robustness for neural networks with more than 2.9M parameters in 5 minutes, and can verify them in 10 seconds. Prior ZKP-based robustness system rely on exponential-time algorithms that cannot scale to nontrivial networks. In contrast, PANDA scales polynomially in the number of neurons in a network, allowing us to support neural networks 4 orders of magnitude larger than previous approaches with significantly reduced prover overhead.	arXiv:2608.17070v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: With the growing deployment of machine learning models, formal guarantees of the robustness and fairness of these models have become increasingly important in safety-critical and legal-compliance settings. However, model parameters are often commercial secrets that cannot be disclosed to auditors or end users. To this end, we present PANDA, a scalable system that uses zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) to prove the robustness and fairness properties of a model without revealing its private parameters. PANDA is built on top of CROWN, an efficient robustness certification framework that is used in many state-of-the-art formal verification tools for neural networks. The core contribution of PANDA is a novel algorithm for proving linear relaxation bounds for non-linear activation layers, yielding simple, lightweight proofs. Remarkably, our implementation can generate proofs of local robustness for neural networks with more than 2.9M parameters in 5 minutes, and can verify them in 10 seconds. Prior ZKP-based robustness system rely on exponential-time algorithms that cannot scale to nontrivial networks. In contrast, PANDA scales polynomially in the number of neurons in a network, allowing us to support neural networks 4 orders of magnitude larger than previous approaches with significantly reduced prover overhead.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.325398+00	\N	\N	c78fe979b885fdb8b208d59422a92c3c93225436b64d40cd1a67d377431f34b4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.325779+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.325781+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3a2c78f5-daff-4ea8-90ac-8d863738a35d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.22007v3	The Communication Map of a Transformer	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22007	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22007	Richard Zhe Wang	arXiv:2608.22007v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: The components of a transformer communicate by writing to and reading from a shared residual stream, and the mechanistic interpretability literature has mapped these connections by hand, one circuit at a time. We present the communication map, which charts every potential communication channel from the geometry of the model's weights alone. Its unit of analysis is the whole transformer rather than the individual head or circuit. Every ordered writer-reader pair, across all 18 connection classes from head-to-head to neuron-to-neuron, receives a coupling coefficient that generalizes the composition score of Elhage et al. (2021), with an exact chance level. The census finds that 70-89% of head pairs are oriented far from chance, some coupled strongly and others actively avoiding each other. The map recovers the known induction circuits blind from the strongest head-to-head couplings and groups the heads into communities, and ablating one such community destroys the model's in-context copying. As an application, we pool the coupling coefficients of every head to identify a two-dimensional residual stream subspace, distinct from those found by activation PCA or outlier dimensions, whose deletion abolishes induction in six models up to Pythia-6.9B. We release the map, the statistical machinery, and the intervention suite.	arXiv:2608.22007v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: The components of a transformer communicate by writing to and reading from a shared residual stream, and the mechanistic interpretability literature has mapped these connections by hand, one circuit at a time. We present the communication map, which charts every potential communication channel from the geometry of the model's weights alone. Its unit of analysis is the whole transformer rather than the individual head or circuit. Every ordered writer-reader pair, across all 18 connection classes from head-to-head to neuron-to-neuron, receives a coupling coefficient that generalizes the composition score of Elhage et al. (2021), with an exact chance level. The census finds that 70-89% of head pairs are oriented far from chance, some coupled strongly and others actively avoiding each other. The map recovers the known induction circuits blind from the strongest head-to-head couplings and groups the heads into communities, and ablating one such community destroys the model's in-context copying. As an application, we pool the coupling coefficients of every head to identify a two-dimensional residual stream subspace, distinct from those found by activation PCA or outlier dimensions, whose deletion abolishes induction in six models up to Pythia-6.9B. We release the map, the statistical machinery, and the intervention suite.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.337528+00	\N	\N	ef5b76c746a03b9dea80f274b4e1266bf1ca31b119cb84cc91a6dc5d50f067c0	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.337886+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.337887+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f5230004-f12e-475c-a9c0-a10ae7cbd06b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.04134v2	Prospective Coding Improves Learning in Deep Continuous-Time Recurrent Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04134	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04134	Shivang Rawat, Mirko Morello, Flaviano Morone, David J. Heeger	arXiv:2609.04134v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Temporal integration gives continuous-time recurrent networks memory, but in deep stacks it also delays bottom-up signals and attenuates top-down errors. We compensate for this delay by making each layer's bottom-up input prospective, and we develop Recursive Quadrature Filters (RQFs), biologically motivated temporal band-pass filters that are a special case of diagonal state-space models (SSMs). Starting from an energy model, we derive the RQF dynamics and show that each RQF is a band-pass filter whose learnable parameters control its tuning frequency and bandwidth. We then make each layer's bottom-up input prospective using a parameter-free two-tap update that leaves the recurrent transition and parallel scan unchanged. We extend this correction to general diagonal SSMs and show that it mitigates depth-dependent gradient attenuation when temporal gradients are truncated, i.e., spatial-only backpropagation. We evaluate the performance of RQFs, S5, and ORGaNICs (a nonlinear gated RNN with built-in attentional modulation and recurrent normalization) trained using full backpropagation through time (BPTT) and spatial-only backpropagation. Under full BPTT, the prospective variants of these models match or outperform their non-prospective controls in every configuration. A non-residual width-32 six-layer RQF achieves 96.09% accuracy on raw-audio Speech Commands with 31.9k parameters; a width-64 six-layer RQF achieves 83.36% on the 16,384-step Path-X task. We also introduce selective RQFs (sRQFs), which make each filter's input gain and, optionally, its bandwidth and tuning frequency input-dependent while retaining the parallel scan. With the input gain alone, the same six-layer model achieves 86.31% on Path-X; with all three input-dependent, a four-layer prospective sRQF solves Selective Copying (99.83%).	arXiv:2609.04134v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Temporal integration gives continuous-time recurrent networks memory, but in deep stacks it also delays bottom-up signals and attenuates top-down errors. We compensate for this delay by making each layer's bottom-up input prospective, and we develop Recursive Quadrature Filters (RQFs), biologically motivated temporal band-pass filters that are a special case of diagonal state-space models (SSMs). Starting from an energy model, we derive the RQF dynamics and show that each RQF is a band-pass filter whose learnable parameters control its tuning frequency and bandwidth. We then make each layer's bottom-up input prospective using a parameter-free two-tap update that leaves the recurrent transition and parallel scan unchanged. We extend this correction to general diagonal SSMs and show that it mitigates depth-dependent gradient attenuation when temporal gradients are truncated, i.e., spatial-only backpropagation. We evaluate the performance of RQFs, S5, and ORGaNICs (a nonlinear gated RNN with built-in attentional modulation and recurrent normalization) trained using full backpropagation through time (BPTT) and spatial-only backpropagation. Under full BPTT, the prospective variants of these models match or outperform their non-prospective controls in every configuration. A non-residual width-32 six-layer RQF achieves 96.09% accuracy on raw-audio Speech Commands with 31.9k parameters; a width-64 six-layer RQF achieves 83.36% on the 16,384-step Path-X task. We also introduce selective RQFs (sRQFs), which make each filter's input gain and, optionally, its bandwidth and tuning frequency input-dependent while retaining the parallel scan. With the input gain alone, the same six-layer model achieves 86.31% on Path-X; with all three input-dependent, a four-layer prospective sRQF solves Selective Copying (99.83%).	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.345285+00	\N	\N	ca798ae1921141106b81fa399e4bc6fa46c8c4d0d2cdff2fd9f842fe7240e8fc	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.345609+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.345611+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
