df7607a2-c155-44ec-9fa6-b0c293a8711a	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-26-new-countries	ChatGPT Ads expands to 26 new countries as over 100,000 advertisers have used the platform	https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-26-new-countries	https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-26-new-countries	\N	From local businesses to global brands, more advertisers can now reach people as they explore, compare and decide what to buy in ChatGPT.	From local businesses to global brands, more advertisers can now reach people as they explore, compare and decide what to buy in ChatGPT.	news	en	2026-10-21 13:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.010213+00	3	\N	469a88c567fe08814707d34a4db84650be13221ccf2bf7e68360c8a6cd2faa12	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.010667+00	2026-11-10 14:39:21.111805+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f32b06b9-02bb-49ed-ad53-809e37614081	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.06228v2	Separation Is a Riesz Energy: How Many Environments Does ICA Need?	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06228	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06228	Yuda Bi, Yubo Guo, Jiaxiao Cai, Xiaonai Li, Zhuolin Li, Vince D. Calhoun	arXiv:2611.06228v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: How many environments and samples does it take to separate $d$ Gaussian sources whose variances change across environments? Each source has a signature, its vector of variances. The Riesz $2$-energy $E_j$ of source $j$ sums the inverse Fisher informations for rotating it towards other sources. With known whitening, we prove a worst-case squared-angle risk lower bound of order $E_j/(n+E_j)$ for orthogonal estimators at every $n$ and $d$. A quadratic pencil given the signatures attains $E_j/n$ up to a constant above a separation-dependent sample threshold, even with estimated whitening. For well-spread signatures at fixed $K$, the energy grows as $d^2$, $d\\log d$ and $d$ for $K=2$, $3$ and $K\\ge4$, respectively. A design with $K\\asymp\\log d$ and pairwise Fisher information bounded below by a constant makes $d\\log d$ samples sufficient. The eigenvectors of a single fixed linear combination have an average first-order error coefficient of at least order $d^2$. Simulations up to $d=1000$ support the first-order predictions, and on task fMRI the energy helps rank split-half instability.	arXiv:2611.06228v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: How many environments and samples does it take to separate $d$ Gaussian sources whose variances change across environments? Each source has a signature, its vector of variances. The Riesz $2$-energy $E_j$ of source $j$ sums the inverse Fisher informations for rotating it towards other sources. With known whitening, we prove a worst-case squared-angle risk lower bound of order $E_j/(n+E_j)$ for orthogonal estimators at every $n$ and $d$. A quadratic pencil given the signatures attains $E_j/n$ up to a constant above a separation-dependent sample threshold, even with estimated whitening. For well-spread signatures at fixed $K$, the energy grows as $d^2$, $d\\log d$ and $d$ for $K=2$, $3$ and $K\\ge4$, respectively. A design with $K\\asymp\\log d$ and pairwise Fisher information bounded below by a constant makes $d\\log d$ samples sufficient. The eigenvectors of a single fixed linear combination have an average first-order error coefficient of at least order $d^2$. Simulations up to $d=1000$ support the first-order predictions, and on task fMRI the energy helps rank split-half instability.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.50489+00	\N	\N	579619c3078634f891a385ee2d561c501bb940d535464d2ae6a13a69b66a679f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.505214+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.505215+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6f66cee4-321a-4016-8dd0-863d8d982986	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.06348v2	Flexible Intensities Matter: A Comprehensive Re-evaluation of Hawkes Processes and Neural Temporal Point Processes	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06348	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06348	Hendrik Mehrtens, Florian Buettner, Oliver Stegle	arXiv:2611.06348v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Temporal point processes (TPPs) are the canonical model class for event sequences in continuous time, with applications ranging from seismology to electronic health records. Recent benchmarks report that neural TPPs substantially outperform their classical counterparts, such as Hawkes processes. However, these comparisons set flexible neural models against Hawkes processes in their most restricted forms and covered only a narrow range of domains. We revisit this comparison under autoregressive next-event prediction, evaluating Poisson and Hawkes processes of increasing flexibility against neural TPPs on five established benchmarks and two large-scale medical tasks derived from UK Biobank disease histories and MIMIC-IV ICU records. First, once Hawkes processes are equipped with flexible, learnable baselines and kernels, the previously reported gap to neural TPPs largely closes. Second, neural TPPs are likewise limited by their intensity parametrization: a Transformer with a spline-based intensity head outperforms previous intensity-based neural TPPs and matches or exceeds the intensity-free model. Decomposing the log-likelihood into time and type terms shows that this gain lies almost entirely in modeling when events occur (log p(t)) rather than which type occurs (log p(d|t)). Third, only on MIMIC-IV do neural TPPs retain an advantage in predicting event types, underscoring the value of complex datasets for evaluating TPPs. We release TorchPP, an open-source PyTorch framework with unified implementations of classical and neural TPPs.	arXiv:2611.06348v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Temporal point processes (TPPs) are the canonical model class for event sequences in continuous time, with applications ranging from seismology to electronic health records. Recent benchmarks report that neural TPPs substantially outperform their classical counterparts, such as Hawkes processes. However, these comparisons set flexible neural models against Hawkes processes in their most restricted forms and covered only a narrow range of domains. We revisit this comparison under autoregressive next-event prediction, evaluating Poisson and Hawkes processes of increasing flexibility against neural TPPs on five established benchmarks and two large-scale medical tasks derived from UK Biobank disease histories and MIMIC-IV ICU records. First, once Hawkes processes are equipped with flexible, learnable baselines and kernels, the previously reported gap to neural TPPs largely closes. Second, neural TPPs are likewise limited by their intensity parametrization: a Transformer with a spline-based intensity head outperforms previous intensity-based neural TPPs and matches or exceeds the intensity-free model. Decomposing the log-likelihood into time and type terms shows that this gain lies almost entirely in modeling when events occur (log p(t)) rather than which type occurs (log p(d|t)). Third, only on MIMIC-IV do neural TPPs retain an advantage in predicting event types, underscoring the value of complex datasets for evaluating TPPs. We release TorchPP, an open-source PyTorch framework with unified implementations of classical and neural TPPs.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.509691+00	\N	\N	d1c81959a7dfffc0ad4c3165169981f14f304ec8049b00d7eec015cf78d24e35	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.509997+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.509998+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
40837996-84b8-464e-8a58-d2be1da1f974	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.06682v2	SecureGARD: Dynamic Ontology-Guided Governance for Secure Tool-Using LLM Agents	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06682	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06682	Guoqin Xiong, Shengtao Wen, Xiang Chen, Xinyi Zeng, Zhaoxin Fan, Wei Ye, Xiaofeng Cao, Sheng-Jun Huang	arXiv:2611.06682v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Tool-using large language model (LLM) agents translate model outputs into consequential actions, turning indirect prompt injection into an execution-security problem. Existing defenses constrain tool calls, trajectories, or information flows, but often leave action semantics, state dependencies, and evidence relations implicit, limiting generalization across heterogeneous tools and complicating safe updates. We present SecureGARD, a dynamic ontology-guided governance framework. SecureGARD grounds heterogeneous calls in a shared semantic representation and combines an executable ontology with episode-level task state to derive deterministic ready, waiting, and forbidden decisions. A waiting decision identifies an unmet but recoverable obligation, enabling bounded replanning without expanding the agent's authorization boundary. SecureGARD evolves its ontology offline from verified training executions. An offline LLM proposes instance-independent action-state axioms, which are accepted only if they address ontology errors exposed by training failures, preserve logical consistency and protected entailments, and pass held-out validation before the ontology is frozen. We evaluate SecureGARD on AgentDojo, ASB, and AgentDyn under benign and indirect prompt-injection settings. SecureGARD achieves a 0% attack success rate (ASR) across all three benchmarks while improving task utility under attack compared with existing baselines. These results show that explicit semantic governance can strengthen execution security without indiscriminate blocking.	arXiv:2611.06682v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Tool-using large language model (LLM) agents translate model outputs into consequential actions, turning indirect prompt injection into an execution-security problem. Existing defenses constrain tool calls, trajectories, or information flows, but often leave action semantics, state dependencies, and evidence relations implicit, limiting generalization across heterogeneous tools and complicating safe updates. We present SecureGARD, a dynamic ontology-guided governance framework. SecureGARD grounds heterogeneous calls in a shared semantic representation and combines an executable ontology with episode-level task state to derive deterministic ready, waiting, and forbidden decisions. A waiting decision identifies an unmet but recoverable obligation, enabling bounded replanning without expanding the agent's authorization boundary. SecureGARD evolves its ontology offline from verified training executions. An offline LLM proposes instance-independent action-state axioms, which are accepted only if they address ontology errors exposed by training failures, preserve logical consistency and protected entailments, and pass held-out validation before the ontology is frozen. We evaluate SecureGARD on AgentDojo, ASB, and AgentDyn under benign and indirect prompt-injection settings. SecureGARD achieves a 0% attack success rate (ASR) across all three benchmarks while improving task utility under attack compared with existing baselines. These results show that explicit semantic governance can strengthen execution security without indiscriminate blocking.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.51206+00	\N	\N	f35486ff861ba33d512547646578f4de8d7c21c3982989baab9d0d6dcd8d2df6	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.512378+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.512379+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4b3d61e3-2ba7-4e3c-b332-427165fae391	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.07130v2	Teaching Agents the "Principle", Not the "Reward": Prescriptive Policy Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07130	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07130	Shan Yang	arXiv:2611.07130v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Reinforcement learning has achieved strong performance in many tasks by letting agents learn from nothing more than a reward signal. However, in more complex tasks, agents still struggle to learn good, generalizable policies, even with carefully designed rewards and abundant samples. We argue that this difficulty stems from the reward decoding burden: designers encode their knowledge of the desired behavior into rewards and penalties, and agents must infer this knowledge from long sequences of noisy scalars. To address this burden, we propose prescriptive policy learning (PPL), which directly teaches agents higher-level knowledge about the tasks, termed principles of behavior, such as how to maintain balance, how to accumulate energy, and how to land smoothly. Through appropriately selected key quantities and references, PPL can directly indicate at every step whether the agent follows the principles and to what extent. Our experiments show that agents can learn good, generalizable policies without any reward. Taught only simple heuristic principles, PPL agents outperform reward-based methods with far fewer samples. Their policies also transfer directly to harder settings and keep performing well, demonstrating their strong generalization ability. In summary, PPL provides a new approach in which rough knowledge of the desired behavior is enough for agents to learn good, generalizable policies, even when the system dynamics cannot be accurately modeled.	arXiv:2611.07130v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Reinforcement learning has achieved strong performance in many tasks by letting agents learn from nothing more than a reward signal. However, in more complex tasks, agents still struggle to learn good, generalizable policies, even with carefully designed rewards and abundant samples. We argue that this difficulty stems from the reward decoding burden: designers encode their knowledge of the desired behavior into rewards and penalties, and agents must infer this knowledge from long sequences of noisy scalars. To address this burden, we propose prescriptive policy learning (PPL), which directly teaches agents higher-level knowledge about the tasks, termed principles of behavior, such as how to maintain balance, how to accumulate energy, and how to land smoothly. Through appropriately selected key quantities and references, PPL can directly indicate at every step whether the agent follows the principles and to what extent. Our experiments show that agents can learn good, generalizable policies without any reward. Taught only simple heuristic principles, PPL agents outperform reward-based methods with far fewer samples. Their policies also transfer directly to harder settings and keep performing well, demonstrating their strong generalization ability. In summary, PPL provides a new approach in which rough knowledge of the desired behavior is enough for agents to learn good, generalizable policies, even when the system dynamics cannot be accurately modeled.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.519223+00	\N	\N	db1ad6ff0930a872f52db2f5319c281c788677a36a157c547f358aa7fbcdf55d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.519574+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.519575+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
02db393b-5069-474a-9ea8-5d3203471fde	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.07750v2	Foresee to Trade: Forecast-Augmented Policy Optimization for Portfolio Management	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07750	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07750	Zeyu Liu, Xuanzhi Feng, Haoran Xu, Lai Sing Kwong, Yang Li, Hualei Zhang, Hao Chen, Jie Zhang, Xiaoyi Pang, Song Guo	arXiv:2611.07750v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Online investment services must translate asset opportunities into investment outcomes for users' accounts, yet the same market opportunity can warrant different account actions. We study how supervision from future asset opportunities can strengthen account-level trading decisions and propose Forecast-Augmented Relative Policy Optimization (FRPO). FRPO separately supervises asset opportunities and account decisions through a jointly learned representation: a Financial State Head (FSH) learns future relative-return targets to provide asset-level opportunity supervision, while Same-Path Group-Relative Optimization compares trading trajectories under the same market path and initial account to provide account-level relative-outcome supervision. An Adaptive Gate coordinates auxiliary supervision using predictive variance and gradient alignment. FSH is used only during training; inference uses the encoder and policy for trading. Across DJIA30, TOPIX Core30, and three investment-style portfolios, ten-seed evaluations show that FRPO achieves the highest mean cumulative and annualized returns among the compared methods in all five settings. These results support strengthening investment agents' trading decisions through joint supervision from asset opportunities and trading outcomes.	arXiv:2611.07750v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Online investment services must translate asset opportunities into investment outcomes for users' accounts, yet the same market opportunity can warrant different account actions. We study how supervision from future asset opportunities can strengthen account-level trading decisions and propose Forecast-Augmented Relative Policy Optimization (FRPO). FRPO separately supervises asset opportunities and account decisions through a jointly learned representation: a Financial State Head (FSH) learns future relative-return targets to provide asset-level opportunity supervision, while Same-Path Group-Relative Optimization compares trading trajectories under the same market path and initial account to provide account-level relative-outcome supervision. An Adaptive Gate coordinates auxiliary supervision using predictive variance and gradient alignment. FSH is used only during training; inference uses the encoder and policy for trading. Across DJIA30, TOPIX Core30, and three investment-style portfolios, ten-seed evaluations show that FRPO achieves the highest mean cumulative and annualized returns among the compared methods in all five settings. These results support strengthening investment agents' trading decisions through joint supervision from asset opportunities and trading outcomes.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.524327+00	\N	\N	0951b2352457243bd966ddbcfce33989b75e7293c21482c41d46f1f88b71cb5c	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.52466+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.524661+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
90a538e3-0f09-46a8-9864-9fd5c05a4960	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-criminal-scam-operation	Disrupting a Cambodia-based criminal scam operation	https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-criminal-scam-operation	https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-criminal-scam-operation	\N	OpenAI disrupted a Cambodia-based scam operation using ChatGPT to support investment, romance, gambling, and impersonation schemes.	OpenAI disrupted a Cambodia-based scam operation using ChatGPT to support investment, romance, gambling, and impersonation schemes.	news	en	2026-07-31 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.456476+00	4	\N	12a15706021352ae0c6c8be8ac66113b8ad7e4fd711f88ff3cdc0ab2d8cc79f3	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.456992+00	2026-11-10 14:29:45.342636+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
11ab1f47-c834-4581-b1ad-b4b3e6c44ad6	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2510.00666v3	Blind Image Restoration via Merging the Manifold Hypothesis, Kernel Method and a Diffusion-like Flow	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00666	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00666	Leah Bar, Liron Mor Yosef, Shai Zucker, Neta Shoham, Inbar Seroussi, Nir Sochen	arXiv:2510.00666v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Blind Image Restoration (BIR) aims to restore degraded images without knowledge of either the type or severity of the degradation. This is a challenging ill-posed inverse problem for which an image prior is essential. We view the prior of an image class as a probability distribution supported on a low-dimensional manifold embedded in the high-dimensional image space. Existing generative approaches primarily model the probability distribution, while the geometric structure is implicit or disregarded. Conversely, geometric approaches do not account for the non-uniform probability distribution on the manifold. We propose a geometric-probabilistic framework for BIR that models these aspects. The probability distribution on the manifold is approximated using a kernel-based method, while a distance from the manifold function induces a geometry-based probability function on the ambient space. This formulation leads to the (Latent) Manifold-Probabilistic Projection Model ((L)MPPM), a restoration model defined in both image and latent spaces. (L)MPPM induces a flow guided by a score field that jointly incorporates geometric and probabilistic information. Evaluated on image restoration tasks and datasets, (L)MPPM consistently outperforms existing approaches, providing empirical evidence for the benefit of incorporating both geometric and probabilistic information into the image prior.	arXiv:2510.00666v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Blind Image Restoration (BIR) aims to restore degraded images without knowledge of either the type or severity of the degradation. This is a challenging ill-posed inverse problem for which an image prior is essential. We view the prior of an image class as a probability distribution supported on a low-dimensional manifold embedded in the high-dimensional image space. Existing generative approaches primarily model the probability distribution, while the geometric structure is implicit or disregarded. Conversely, geometric approaches do not account for the non-uniform probability distribution on the manifold. We propose a geometric-probabilistic framework for BIR that models these aspects. The probability distribution on the manifold is approximated using a kernel-based method, while a distance from the manifold function induces a geometry-based probability function on the ambient space. This formulation leads to the (Latent) Manifold-Probabilistic Projection Model ((L)MPPM), a restoration model defined in both image and latent spaces. (L)MPPM induces a flow guided by a score field that jointly incorporates geometric and probabilistic information. Evaluated on image restoration tasks and datasets, (L)MPPM consistently outperforms existing approaches, providing empirical evidence for the benefit of incorporating both geometric and probabilistic information into the image prior.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.609765+00	\N	\N	8f46b9c31c041f91839fd59931a123cafcb6d8ccda88537a2617c89034d9cd79	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.610079+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.61008+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2f0c665b-dfd3-4514-9d9a-76783e11b213	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2302.12670v2	Personalized Pricing with Invalid Instrumental Variables: Identification, Estimation, and Policy Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12670	https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12670	Rui Miao, Zhengling Qi, Cong Shi, Lin Lin	arXiv:2302.12670v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Pricing based on individual customer characteristics is widely used to maximize sellers' revenues. We study how a firm can learn such a personalized pricing policy from historical transactions when unobserved factors affect both the prices it charged and the revenue it earned. Standard instrumental variable (IV) methods either focus on a discrete treatment space or require that the IV affect revenue only through price. This exclusion restriction is difficult to justify in pricing, where the most natural candidate IVs are price-setting levers that may also affect revenue directly. We propose PRINT (Personalized pRicing using Invalid iNsTrumental variables), a policy learning method for continuous prices that uses such candidate IVs. Relying on structural models of revenue and price, we show that the optimal pricing policy can be identified with invalid IV that shifts higher moments of historical prices, when the unobserved factors that drive customers' price sensitivity are separated from those used by the historical pricing rule. Based on this identification, which leads to conditional moment restrictions with generalized residual functions, we construct an adversarial min-max estimator that accommodates flexible machine learning models and establish regret bounds for the learned pricing policy. Simulation studies and an application to a U.S. online auto lender illustrate the method's performance. Our results give a principled way to learn personalized prices from observational data using imperfect price-setting levers, and clarify when doing so is justified.	arXiv:2302.12670v2 Announce Type: re