8796f3d0-9ee6-402b-8e91-1589fe57d809	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.19882v2	Multimodal Concept Bottleneck Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19882	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19882	Tongqing Shi, Ge Yan, Tuomas Oikarinen, Tsui-Wei Weng	arXiv:2606.19882v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Concept Bottleneck Models (CBMs) enhance the interpretability of deep neural networks by aligning the features extracted from images with human-interpretable concepts. However, conventional CBMs typically use a linear classifier that maps concept activations to a fixed set of predefined classes, limiting their flexibility in open-vocabulary settings. In this paper, we propose Multimodal Concept Bottleneck Model (MM-CBM) to address these issues and extend CBMs into CLIP structure. MM-CBM utilizes dual Concept Bottleneck Layers (CBLs) to align both the image and text embeddings into interpretable features. This allows us to perform new vision tasks like zero-shot classification or image retrieval in an interpretable way. Compared to existing methods, MM-CBM achieves 23.28% accuracy improvement on average across four standard benchmarks. Our method maintains competitive performance compared with black-box CLIP model while offering interpretability. Under classification tasks, MM-CBM's accuracy is on average 3.94% lower than CLIP in the target-dataset adaptation setting and 9.84% in the zero-shot transfer setting. Under image-text retrieval tasks, it achieves an average gap of 2.48% for image retrieval and 9.22% for text retrieval.	arXiv:2606.19882v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Concept Bottleneck Models (CBMs) enhance the interpretability of deep neural networks by aligning the features extracted from images with human-interpretable concepts. However, conventional CBMs typically use a linear classifier that maps concept activations to a fixed set of predefined classes, limiting their flexibility in open-vocabulary settings. In this paper, we propose Multimodal Concept Bottleneck Model (MM-CBM) to address these issues and extend CBMs into CLIP structure. MM-CBM utilizes dual Concept Bottleneck Layers (CBLs) to align both the image and text embeddings into interpretable features. This allows us to perform new vision tasks like zero-shot classification or image retrieval in an interpretable way. Compared to existing methods, MM-CBM achieves 23.28% accuracy improvement on average across four standard benchmarks. Our method maintains competitive performance compared with black-box CLIP model while offering interpretability. Under classification tasks, MM-CBM's accuracy is on average 3.94% lower than CLIP in the target-dataset adaptation setting and 9.84% in the zero-shot transfer setting. Under image-text retrieval tasks, it achieves an average gap of 2.48% for image retrieval and 9.22% for text retrieval.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.840732+00	\N	\N	5ca740d2723c7aade93e85e26aae64e312fd55fbce4098c84c4add110ce03bb7	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.84107+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.841072+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ae39ad3f-7220-4a46-ad28-127818fbd3b4	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.27895v2	Mosaic: A Benchmark Suite for Differentiable Physics Solvers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27895	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27895	Andrin Rehmann, Heiko Zimmermann, Dion H\\"afner	arXiv:2606.27895v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Differentiable partial differential equation (PDE) solvers underpin solver-in-the-loop MLtraining, gradient-based optimal control, and inverse problems, yet the practical cost of obtaining correct, usable gradients from a given solver on a given problem is largely undocumented. Integration effort, computational cost, memory, gradient accuracy, and numerical stability vary widely across solvers and are discoverable only by trial and error. We introduce Mosaic, an extensible benchmarking framework for differentiable PDE solvers that standardizes access to solver gradients. Each solver is packaged as a containerized component (Tesseract) exposing a uniform gradient API regardless of language or automatic differentiation (AD) strategy, enabling researchers to evaluate, compare, and build on non-trivial physical solvers. Our evaluation of 14 solvers across fluid dynamics, structural mechanics, and heat transfer demonstrates that the benchmark surfaces practically relevant differences: order-of-magnitude variation in computational cost and memory, alongside setup incompatibilities and instabilities that rule out solvers for specific tasks. Despite this variation, all solvers that produce gradients converge to similar optima on our tasks, indicating that the practical barriers are memory limits, numerical stability, and setup compatibility rather than gradient accuracy alone. Mosaic is open-source and available at github.com/pasteurlabs/mosaic.	arXiv:2606.27895v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Differentiable partial differential equation (PDE) solvers underpin solver-in-the-loop MLtraining, gradient-based optimal control, and inverse problems, yet the practical cost of obtaining correct, usable gradients from a given solver on a given problem is largely undocumented. Integration effort, computational cost, memory, gradient accuracy, and numerical stability vary widely across solvers and are discoverable only by trial and error. We introduce Mosaic, an extensible benchmarking framework for differentiable PDE solvers that standardizes access to solver gradients. Each solver is packaged as a containerized component (Tesseract) exposing a uniform gradient API regardless of language or automatic differentiation (AD) strategy, enabling researchers to evaluate, compare, and build on non-trivial physical solvers. Our evaluation of 14 solvers across fluid dynamics, structural mechanics, and heat transfer demonstrates that the benchmark surfaces practically relevant differences: order-of-magnitude variation in computational cost and memory, alongside setup incompatibilities and instabilities that rule out solvers for specific tasks. Despite this variation, all solvers that produce gradients converge to similar optima on our tasks, indicating that the practical barriers are memory limits, numerical stability, and setup compatibility rather than gradient accuracy alone. Mosaic is open-source and available at github.com/pasteurlabs/mosaic.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.843536+00	\N	\N	3b8a46c3db960788f84d92476be235baa98bd2d5588ce162173e13ab2f63491d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.843906+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.843908+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4a724eac-b2be-4408-b943-0b9356e00e8c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2607.09645v2	Deep Gaussian Processes on Directed Acyclic Graphs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09645	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09645	Federico L. Perlino, Oliver Hamelijnck, Adam M. Johansen, Theodoros Damoulas	arXiv:2607.09645v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Many real-world processes can be represented as compositions of functions along a directed acyclic graph (DAG). In causal modelling, these correspond to the underlying mechanisms; in engineering, to multiple fidelity levels; and in gene-regulatory networks, to transcription factors. These functions are partially observed across the DAG, with noisy and heterogeneously sampled measurements, posing significant challenges for reconstruction, uncertainty propagation, and inference. To tackle these challenges, we place priors over functions and naturally arrive at Deep Gaussian Processes over DAGs. We theoretically study their prior-collapse behaviour and the effect of graph topology and intermediate observations on the preservation of information. We obtain almost-sure lower bounds on the asymptotic frequency of depths at which the distinction between inputs is preserved, identify broad kernel classes for which these hold, and prove an observation by \\citep{dunlop2018} on the role of input connections. We offer a structured variational approximation that retains graph dependencies, preserves compositional uncertainty, and captures the explaining-away behaviour of colliders. Finally, we empirically validate our theory and methodology at scale and under DAG misspecification. We then model a latent-collider DAG, a protein signalling network, and a multi-fidelity \\mbox{heavy-ion} collision emulation task, attaining state-of-the-art performance while recovering low-fidelity contributions and yielding interpretability over the simulator hierarchy.	arXiv:2607.09645v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Many real-world processes can be represented as compositions of functions along a directed acyclic graph (DAG). In causal modelling, these correspond to the underlying mechanisms; in engineering, to multiple fidelity levels; and in gene-regulatory networks, to transcription factors. These functions are partially observed across the DAG, with noisy and heterogeneously sampled measurements, posing significant challenges for reconstruction, uncertainty propagation, and inference. To tackle these challenges, we place priors over functions and naturally arrive at Deep Gaussian Processes over DAGs. We theoretically study their prior-collapse behaviour and the effect of graph topology and intermediate observations on the preservation of information. We obtain almost-sure lower bounds on the asymptotic frequency of depths at which the distinction between inputs is preserved, identify broad kernel classes for which these hold, and prove an observation by \\citep{dunlop2018} on the role of input connections. We offer a structured variational approximation that retains graph dependencies, preserves compositional uncertainty, and captures the explaining-away behaviour of colliders. Finally, we empirically validate our theory and methodology at scale and under DAG misspecification. We then model a latent-collider DAG, a protein signalling network, and a multi-fidelity \\mbox{heavy-ion} collision emulation task, attaining state-of-the-art performance while recovering low-fidelity contributions and yielding interpretability over the simulator hierarchy.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.858729+00	\N	\N	97af1b9426534cb48ad15c011cdfda4bb70673b4a8bf8f90ed2e665056285f52	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.859061+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.859062+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
58376ab2-8671-45fc-985a-e00acf070a26	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.06250v2	Minimax Optimal Early-Stopped Gradient Descent for Gaussian Mixture Classification	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.06250	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.06250	Alex Buna, Shirley Xiaoqi Liu, Patrick Rebeschini	arXiv:2608.06250v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: In overparameterised classification, training data can be linearly separable even when the underlying distribution is not. In this setting, gradient descent (GD) on the logistic loss diverges in norm while converging in direction to a max-margin interpolating classifier, whose implicit bias can be statistically suboptimal. In this work, we show that early stopping can overcome this suboptimality: in a Gaussian mixture model with label-flipping noise, GD stopped at an appropriate oracle time achieves minimax-optimal excess zero-one risk for covariance spectra with fast and continuous decay, including polynomial and exponential spectral decays. Our analysis combines a sharp upper bound for the early-stopped iterate with a matching statistical lower bound over arbitrary classifiers, yielding optimal rates that are validated by experiments. A central technical contribution is a new calibration result that converts excess logistic risk into excess zero-one risk; it handles the model misspecification induced by the label-flipping noise, and removes the square-root rate in standard bounds. We also establish a lower bound for linear interpolators, showing that interpolation can require exponentially more samples than early stopping to achieve the same excess risk.	arXiv:2608.06250v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: In overparameterised classification, training data can be linearly separable even when the underlying distribution is not. In this setting, gradient descent (GD) on the logistic loss diverges in norm while converging in direction to a max-margin interpolating classifier, whose implicit bias can be statistically suboptimal. In this work, we show that early stopping can overcome this suboptimality: in a Gaussian mixture model with label-flipping noise, GD stopped at an appropriate oracle time achieves minimax-optimal excess zero-one risk for covariance spectra with fast and continuous decay, including polynomial and exponential spectral decays. Our analysis combines a sharp upper bound for the early-stopped iterate with a matching statistical lower bound over arbitrary classifiers, yielding optimal rates that are validated by experiments. A central technical contribution is a new calibration result that converts excess logistic risk into excess zero-one risk; it handles the model misspecification induced by the label-flipping noise, and removes the square-root rate in standard bounds. We also establish a lower bound for linear interpolators, showing that interpolation can require exponentially more samples than early stopping to achieve the same excess risk.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.881153+00	\N	\N	97ce053cce52124230e47dc10975223373af586b3ddfab95ca4fb6c8dcb3c1b4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.881473+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.881475+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
47597127-109c-4b8d-9458-c01dc15e2799	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.25005v2	The Imperfective Paradox Is Not Necessarily in Large Language Models: A Benchmark Failure Before a Model Failure	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25005	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25005	Kaiqiao Han, Katarzyna Pru\\'s, Mark Steedman, Yizhou Sun	arXiv:2608.25005v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Recent work uses an NLI benchmark of the imperfective paradox to argue that large language models exhibit a Teleological Bias and Calibration Crisis. We identify three validity concerns in benchmark construction, including aspectual reduction, semantic bias, and relation bias. Under a strict NLI standard, 76% of the Group A instances do not explicitly rule out culmination; in native-speaker annotation, 38% of Group A and 29% of Group C examples permit an alternative interpretation. To better isolate the semantic contrast, we construct Lexically Matched Minimal Pairs. Moreover, by formulating event-semantic NLI as a multi-step reasoning problem, we evaluate intermediate semantic judgments and use oracle-guided settings. We find that incorrect NLI entailment does not fully support teleological bias. Prompting often changes final NLI decisions without reliably improving the underlying semantic judgments, offering a possible explanation for the Calibration Crisis.	arXiv:2608.25005v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Recent work uses an NLI benchmark of the imperfective paradox to argue that large language models exhibit a Teleological Bias and Calibration Crisis. We identify three validity concerns in benchmark construction, including aspectual reduction, semantic bias, and relation bias. Under a strict NLI standard, 76% of the Group A instances do not explicitly rule out culmination; in native-speaker annotation, 38% of Group A and 29% of Group C examples permit an alternative interpretation. To better isolate the semantic contrast, we construct Lexically Matched Minim