9c2ce519-425a-4aca-bdb6-1268432a09a9	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.29211v2	On the gradient conjecture at infinity: Finite spherical length for gradient trajectories of definable potentials	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29211	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29211	Thang Do, Arnulf Jentzen, Benno Kuckuck	arXiv:2610.29211v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We prove that for every open $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ the spherical projection of every gradient trajectory $\\theta(t)$, $t \\geq 0$, of a definable $C^1$-potential $F \\colon U \\to \\mathbb{R}$ has finite length and, in particular, converges (directional convergence). In other words, every gradient trajectory of a definable $C^1$-potential has finite total angular motion. Here, definable means definable in an arbitrary o-minimal structure expanding the real field.\n  Notably, the gradient trajectory in this result is an arbitrary solution of the gradient flow differential equation (whose solutions may not be unique) with no further assumptions: The gradient trajectory may be unbounded and diverge to infinity or it may be convergent. The established theorem therefore provides affirmative answers to both the gradient conjecture at infinity for definable potentials and the definable Thom gradient conjecture.\n  We extend our conclusions from the Euclidean metric to a general class of ambient metrics including Riemannian metrics and we establish uniform local upper bounds for the lengths of the spherical projections of gradient flow trajectories. We finally apply our findings to the training of deep neural networks (DNNs) to conclude that, in the case of definable and continuously differentiable activation and loss functions, the spherical projection of the DNN training flow must always converge (directional convergence).	arXiv:2610.29211v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We prove that for every open $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ the spherical projection of every gradient trajectory $\\theta(t)$, $t \\geq 0$, of a definable $C^1$-potential $F \\colon U \\to \\mathbb{R}$ has finite length and, in particular, converges (directional convergence). In other words, every gradient trajectory of a definable $C^1$-potential has finite total angular motion. Here, definable means definable in an arbitrary o-minimal structure expanding the real field.\n  Notably, the gradient trajectory in this result is an arbitrary solution of the gradient flow differential equation (whose solutions may not be unique) with no further assumptions: The gradient trajectory may be unbounded and diverge to infinity or it may be convergent. The established theorem therefore provides affirmative answers to both the gradient conjecture at infinity for definable potentials and the definable Thom gradient conjecture.\n  We extend our conclusions from the Euclidean metric to a general class of ambient metrics including Riemannian metrics and we establish uniform local upper bounds for the lengths of the spherical projections of gradient flow trajectories. We finally apply our findings to the training of deep neural networks (DNNs) to conclude that, in the case of definable and continuously differentiable activation and loss functions, the spherical projection of the DNN training flow must always converge (directional convergence).	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.995941+00	\N	\N	6b286130978ca9d7411a17084684818d1c7af66105b4403a528cbd7741c9b4b9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.996264+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.996266+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f3dcbc32-c495-48e4-9a41-cfae8e095e34	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.33143v2	Learning Pathology-Sensitive Representations for Unsupervised Retinal OCT Anomaly Detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33143	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33143	Tania Haghighi, Sina Gholami, Behafarin Emam, Jennifer I Lim, Theodore Leng, Atalie C Thompson, Hamed Tabkhi, Minhaj Nur Alam	arXiv:2610.33143v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Deep learning approaches for retinal optical coherence tomography (OCT) predominantly rely on predefined disease categories, limiting their ability to detect rare or previously unseen abnormalities. While visual foundation models provide transferable representations, their effectiveness in identifying pathological deviations from normal retinal anatomy remains insufficiently understood. In this work, we introduce an unsupervised framework for retinal OCT anomaly detection and localization that adapts pretrained visual representations to normal retinal anatomy through anatomy-aware reconstruction and contrastive triplet learning guided by synthetic structural perturbations, without requiring disease-specific annotations. We systematically investigate the influence of pretraining strategies, anatomy-aware representation adaptation, and anomaly scoring mechanisms on the detection and localization of unseen retinal abnormalities. Our findings demonstrate that adapting pretrained representations to normal retinal structure improves anomaly sensitivity, while pixel-space reconstruction errors and feature-space deviations provide complementary signals for identifying pathological changes. These findings underscore the importance of anatomy-aware representation learning and offer insights into developing generalizable retinal anomaly detection frameworks beyond predefined disease categories.	arXiv:2610.33143v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Deep learning approaches for retinal optical coherence tomography (OCT) predominantly rely on predefined disease categories, limiting their ability to detect rare or previously unseen abnormalities. While visual foundation models provide transferable representations, their effectiveness in identifying pathological deviations from normal retinal anatomy remains insufficiently understood. In this work, we introduce an unsupervised framework for retinal OCT anomaly detection and localization that adapts pretrained visual representations to normal retinal anatomy through anatomy-aware reconstruction and contrastive triplet learning guided by synthetic structural perturbations, without requiring disease-specific annotations. We systematically investigate the influence of pretraining strategies, anatomy-aware representation adaptation, and anomaly scoring mechanisms on the detection and localization of unseen retinal abnormalities. Our findings demonstrate that adapting pretrained representations to normal retinal structure improves anomaly sensitivity, while pixel-space reconstruction errors and feature-space deviations provide complementary signals for identifying pathological changes. These findings underscore the importance of anatomy-aware representation learning and offer insights into developing generalizable retinal anomaly detection frameworks beyond predefined disease categories.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.003406+00	\N	\N	bed5a843e73b2f78757856645e1657cdcf795f4c7cbb705e53683f0fbec218cd	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.003753+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.003755+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
01c22698-d366-48d1-a0e6-8ea5c00c55ee	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/advisory-group-on-mathematics-and-ai	Advisory Group on Mathematics and Artificial Intelligence	https://openai.com/index/advisory-group-on-mathematics-and-ai	https://openai.com/index/advisory-group-on-mathematics-and-ai	\N	OpenAI is working with an independent Advisory Group on Mathematics and Artificial Intelligence to guide the review and communication of emerging AI results.	OpenAI is working with an independent Advisory Group on Mathematics and Artificial Intelligence to guide the review and communication of emerging AI results.	news	en	2026-09-21 12:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.183317+00	3	\N	079c217c94aeb350aa98701f0f24d009ecd746cd2c30ef5c9424daf89cb2c977	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.183673+00	2026-11-10 14:35:32.842682+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d20a7a9d-258d-481d-a307-73a00f9de2a0	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10319v1	Prove What You Can: Hybrid Neurosymbolic Reasoning for Logical Natural Language Inference	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10319	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10319	Ada Cano\\u{g}lu, Sadiq Qara	arXiv:2611.10319v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Logical reasoning over natural language requires both semantic interpretation and deductive search. Large language models (LLMs) can propose plausible intermediate derivations, but they often fail to compose them into a complete proof and overpredict uncertainty. Neurosymbolic methods improve search by translating an entire problem into first-order logic and handing it to a prover, but a single bad clause can make the whole translation nonexecutable. We present Hybrid Reasoning, a three-stage pipeline that decouples proposing a proof, validating its steps, and deciding a label. An LLM proposes a structured proof tree with a raw verdict; a theorem prover validates each derivation locally, keeping only the subset it can prove; and an uncertainty-gated repair attempts to re-derive the label from the premises plus the validated derivations, when the raw verdict is UNCERTAIN. Across four benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, ProverQA, PrOntoQA) and five contemporary LLMs, Hybrid Reasoning is the top-performing method on the two hardest datasets, improving on the strongest baseline by 5.3 points on FOLIO and 4.9 on ProverQA, and reaches ceiling-level accuracy on the easiest. The repair stage operates at 78-98% precision, producing 326 correct relabellings against 19 regressions.	arXiv:2611.10319v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Logical reasoning over natural language requires both semantic interpretation and deductive search. Large language models (LLMs) can propose plausible intermediate derivations, but they often fail to compose them into a complete proof and overpredict uncertainty. Neurosymbolic methods improve search by translating an entire problem into first-order logic and handing it to a prover, but a single bad clause can make the whole translation nonexecutable. We present Hybrid Reasoning, a three-stage pipeline that decouples proposing a proof, validating its steps, and deciding a label. An LLM proposes a structured proof tree with a raw verdict; a theorem prover validates each derivation locally, keeping only the subset it can prove; and an uncertainty-gated repair attempts to re-derive the label from the premises plus the validated derivations, when the raw verdict is UNCERTAIN. Across four benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, ProverQA, PrOntoQA) and five contemporary LLMs, Hybrid Reasoning is the top-performing method on the two hardest datasets, improving on the strongest baseline by 5.3 points on FOLIO and 4.9 on ProverQA, and reaches ceiling-level accuracy on the easiest. The repair stage operates at 78-98% precision, producing 326 correct relabellings against 19 regressions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.693449+00	\N	\N	11a7519707709d738ac52a4e8de13c9f7c666bd524bd4f4a12a71fc29c80b7ad	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.693777+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.693778+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
293920bd-2a82-4942-af69-56672bbdad05	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10327v1	Policy Over Purpose: Auditing LLM Content Moderation of Reclaimed Slur Usage	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10327	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10327	Christina Chance, Kai-Wei Chang	arXiv:2611.10327v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As Perspective API is deprecated, platforms are turning to large language models for content moderation, encoding policy as natural language that the model must interpret at inference time. Reclaimed slurs test this design directly. The same term can express in-group pride or target the community that reclaimed it, and misreading it silences the speech moderation is meant to protect. We audit four general-purpose LLMs and four moderation-specific systems, with Perspective API as a legacy baseline, on IYKYK, a corpus of reclaimed and derogatory usage of three slurs annotated by community members. Across the policy, decision, reasoning, disparity, and steering layers of this audit, we find that whether a system was purpose-built for moderation explains little about how it handles reclaimed language. No system reliably agrees with community judgment, and all fail most on language reclaimed within Black communities. What separates systems is instead how their policy is represented. Only systems whose policy is expressed in the prompt can be steered at all, and even then, explicit definitions change how models justify a decision more than how they make it. We argue that policy transparency determines whether moderation failures can be corrected, but that correcting them requires contextual judgment that definitions supplied at inference time cannot provide.	arXiv:2611.10327v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As Perspective API is deprecated, platforms are turning to large language models for content moderation, encoding policy as natural language that the model must interpret at inference time. Reclaimed slurs test this design directly. The same term can express in-group pride or target the community that reclaimed it, and misreading it silences the speech moderation is meant to protect. We audit four general-purpose LLMs and four moderation-specific systems, with Perspective API as a legacy baseline, on IYKYK, a corpus of reclaimed and derogatory usage of three slurs annotated by community members. Across the policy, decision, reasoning, disparity, and steering layers of this audit, we find that whether a system was purpose-built for moderation explains little about how it handles reclaimed language. No system reliably agrees with community judgment, and all fail most on language reclaimed within Black communities. What separates systems is instead how their policy is represented. Only systems whose policy is expressed in the prompt can be steered at all, and even then, explicit definitions change how models justify a decision more than how they make it. We argue that policy transparency determines whether moderation failures can be corrected, but that correcting them requires contextual judgment that definitions supplied at inference time cannot provide.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.723359+00	\N	\N	4496546ed117c1bc7511094ec453b4430c577c175e43106998564bd0de94ab9e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.723862+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.723864+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
414d3934-8013-4d1e-9f38-efd450c92993	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10555v1	YRC-Bench: Benchmarking Culturally Aware Machine Translation for Historical Heritage via Tri-Level Semantic Bridging	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10555	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10555	Huang Yan, Aobo Li, Jiayi Wang, Huidong Zhu	arXiv:2611.10555v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Translating historical cultural heritage poses profound challenges for machine translation systems due to the acute semantic asymmetry across eras and cultures, where terse classical syntax frequently obscures domain-specific cultural knowledge. While Large Language Models have demonstrated remarkable prowess in generic translation benchmarks, they struggle to negotiate Culturally-Specific Items and diachronic shifts in domain-rich historical texts, such as Yellow River cultural records. To bridge this critical gap, we introduce YRC-Bench, the first human-curated, multi-parallel benchmark dedicated to historical heritage translation across Classical Chinese, Modern Chinese, and nine target foreign languages. YRC-Bench establishes a tri-level parallel corpus incorporating modern explanatory glosses as explicit semantic bridges and operationalizes translation strategies through paired conserved versus substituted CSI variants categorized by cultural translatability. Systematic evaluations of nine leading LLMs across bidirectional translation directions reveal that leveraging modern Chinese glosses - especially human-curated ones - dramatically elevates both lexical fidelity and semantic adequacy. Furthermore, multidimensional human preference audits by native experts unveil distinct cross-cultural preferences between Sinographic-tradition and non-Sinographic language groups.	arXiv:2611.10555v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Translating historical cultural heritage poses profound challenges for machine translation systems due to the acute semantic asymmetry across eras and cultures, where terse classical syntax frequently obscures domain-specific cultural knowledge. While Large Language Models have demonstrated remarkable prowess in generic translation benchmarks, they struggle to negotiate Culturally-Specific Items and diachronic shifts in domain-rich historical texts, such as Yellow River cultural records. To bridge this critical gap, we introduce YRC-Bench, the first human-curated, multi-parallel benchmark dedicated to historical heritage translation across Classical Chinese, Modern Chinese, and nine target foreign languages. YRC-Bench establishes a tri-level parallel corpus incorporating modern explanatory glosses as explicit semantic bridges and operationalizes translation strategies through paired conserved versus substituted CSI variants categorized by cultural translatability. Systematic evaluations of nine leading LLMs across bidirectional translation directions reveal that leveraging modern Chinese glosses - especially human-curated ones - dramatically elevates both lexical fidelity and semantic adequacy. Furthermore, multidimensional human preference audits by native experts unveil distinct cross-cultural preferences between Sinographic-tradition and non-Sinographic language groups.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.748574+00	\N	\N	fbc4a9b53a6c8cac02815a77963fb58221c4ddbe71d47e7a2ce91e3da7b48e99	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.748882+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.748883+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9dd97043-9ce3-4e4a-96f2-27a3f4248efb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.16262v2	Compute-Optimal Pretrain--Fine-tune in Ridge Gradient Descent	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16262	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16262	Alex Buna, Fanghui Liu, Patrick Rebeschini	arXiv:2609.16262v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Pretraining followed by fine-tuning introduces a compute-allocation problem: under a fixed training budget, compute spent improving the upstream objective reduces the compute available for downstream adaptation. Despite its practical importance, this trade-off is not yet well understood theoretically, even in simple models. In this paper, we cast this allocation as a compute-split problem under a two-stage pretrain--fine-tune procedure with fixed total optimisation budget, using regularised least squares trained by gradient descent as a tractable setting. We characterise the optimal split under data-dependent evaluation geometries induced by the fine-tuning problem. Our results show that the allocation depends on how pretraining directions affect fine-tuning predictions and how fine-tuning shifts are seen through downstream data geometry. In particular, the relevant quantities are determined by prediction-relevant spectral components of the pretraining and fine-tuning empirical covariances. Technically, the analysis relies on a basis-invariant, eigenspace-level spectral decomposition, together with perturbative control of the non-commuting pretraining and fine-tuning dynamics.	arXiv:2609.16262v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Pretraining followed by fine-tuning introduces a compute-allocation problem: under a fixed training budget, compute spent improving the upstream objective reduces the compute available for downstream adaptation. Despite its practical importance, this trade-off is not yet well understood theoretically, even in simple models. In this paper, we cast this allocation as a compute-split problem under a two-stage