32d8c911-0107-474c-ba75-9efc350727eb	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/introducing-the-rosalind-academic-plan	Bringing GPT-Rosalind to academic research labs	https://openai.com/index/introducing-the-rosalind-academic-plan	https://openai.com/index/introducing-the-rosalind-academic-plan	\N	Introducing the Rosalind Academic Plan, offering eligible academic labs sponsored access to GPT-Rosalind and tools to advance life sciences research.	Introducing the Rosalind Academic Plan, offering eligible academic labs sponsored access to GPT-Rosalind and tools to advance life sciences research.	product_update	en	2026-10-15 19:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.045341+00	4	\N	7ddef110008164729345b05b831d14daef7988740aaf5994b60206ad30f82bec	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.04577+00	2026-11-10 14:38:32.379107+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
18900351-ed63-4e5b-9ea9-cd68ced72100	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10740v1	Evaluating Physically Grounded Procedural Reasoning in VLMs through Real-World Execution: Crochet as a Testbed	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10740	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10740	Maximiliano Wardle	arXiv:2611.10740v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Vision-Language Models (VLMs) can generate procedures that look convincing on paper, but do they work in practice? In this paper, we propose crochet as an ideal testbed for physically grounded procedural reasoning in VLMs. Crochet cannot be reliably simulated or automated: the only way to know what a pattern produces is to make it by hand, yet verification is tangible and cheap, requiring nothing but a hook and a ball of yarn. We introduce FoundationChain, a deceptively simple test of procedural reasoning: given an image of a basic crochet object, can VLMs infer how it was made and generate a pattern that produces the same result? We crochet 40 patterns generated by four VLMs by hand. Even at this scale, physical execution not only reveals failures that automated proxies cannot detect, but also enables a more precise diagnosis of their causes and consequences: while models write fluent, convincing patterns, only four are free of errors, and our analysis exposes deficiencies in visual, geometric and procedural reasoning.	arXiv:2611.10740v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Vision-Language Models (VLMs) can generate procedures that look convincing on paper, but do they work in practice? In this paper, we propose crochet as an ideal testbed for physically grounded procedural reasoning in VLMs. Crochet cannot be reliably simulated or automated: the only way to know what a pattern produces is to make it by hand, yet verification is tangible and cheap, requiring nothing but a hook and a ball of yarn. We introduce FoundationChain, a deceptively simple test of procedural reasoning: given an image of a basic crochet object, can VLMs infer how it was made and generate a pattern that produces the same result? We crochet 40 patterns generated by four VLMs by hand. Even at this scale, physical execution not only reveals failures that automated proxies cannot detect, but also enables a more precise diagnosis of their causes and consequences: while models write fluent, convincing patterns, only four are free of errors, and our analysis exposes deficiencies in visual, geometric and procedural reasoning.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.76493+00	\N	\N	1d2056dcf374859beb3892ee3180bca7f6014bf64136bf2a66af1405a56f284a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.765235+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.765237+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
95644a5a-a362-44b4-8cfc-d27963e02e06	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10765v1	Cascading Circle Loss: Order-Preserving Contrastive Learning for Graded Legal Retrieval	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10765	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10765	Rayane Kimouche, Ahmed Touila	arXiv:2611.10765v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: This paper introduces Cascading Circle Loss (CasCL), a contrastive learning framework for legal retrieval tasks with graded relevance. CasCL combines an InfoNCE objective for positive-negative separation with a cascade of ranking boundaries that model ordered relevance levels between queries and legal clauses. The framework also includes a negative-aware self-paced optimization mechanism and a relevance gain weighting strategy to improve ranking quality. Experiments on legal passage retrieval benchmarks show that CasCL consistently outperforms existing methods, including InfoNCE, Circle Loss, MNRL and Margin MSE, across both binary retrieval and graded ranking metrics.	arXiv:2611.10765v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: This paper introduces Cascading Circle Loss (CasCL), a contrastive learning framework for legal retrieval tasks with graded relevance. CasCL combines an InfoNCE objective for positive-negative separation with a cascade of ranking boundaries that model ordered relevance levels between queries and legal clauses. The framework also includes a negative-aware self-paced optimization mechanism and a relevance gain weighting strategy to improve ranking quality. Experiments on legal passage retrieval benchmarks show that CasCL consistently outperforms existing methods, including InfoNCE, Circle Loss, MNRL and Margin MSE, across both binary retrieval and graded ranking metrics.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.766796+00	\N	\N	b0ad8b56777e6cb133e9df92380bf2ef9132e3ed9c1bee72935b46caa0ac7697	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.767109+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.76711+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
68ee4e12-86cf-4d17-b942-927c8a7ecc32	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10840v1	Simple Test-Time Refinement for Plot-to-Code Generation via Visual-Code Diagnostics	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10840	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10840	Peiming Guo, Jinpeng Huang, Yangbin Sun, Gaoxiong Cao, Ensheng Shi, Yuchi Ma, Yu Zhang, Meishan Zhang, Baotian Hu, Min Zhang	arXiv:2611.10840v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Plot-to-code generation has been greatly advanced by recent multimodal large language models (MLLMs). However, existing studies mainly focus on one-pass generation or iterative sampling guided by verifier-based filtering, which does not align with the typical human programming workflow of generate first, then iteratively error-location and fix. This naturally motivates a test-time refinement paradigm for plot-to-code generation. In this work, we present the first study on plot-to-code generation via iterative test-time refinement. We propose a Visual-Code Diagnostics framework that enables refinement through visual feedback from source code. Specifically, we first categorize generation errors into four coarse-grained types, and then train lightweight discriminators to recognize and localize these errors based on the rendered visual outputs and corresponding code. Unlike traditional compiler- or debugger-based verification, our approach performs iterative refinement guided by one diagnostics system, enabling more effective semantic and visual correction during test time. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method consistently improves generation quality across diverse generators and significantly outperforms strong test-time scaling baselines.	arXiv:2611.10840v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Plot-to-code generation has been greatly advanced by recent multimodal large language models (MLLMs). However, existing studies mainly focus on one-pass generation or iterative sampling guided by verifier-based filtering, which does not align with the typical human programming workflow of generate first, then iteratively error-location and fix. This naturally motivates a test-time refinement paradigm for plot-to-code generation. In this work, we present the first study on plot-to-code generation via iterative test-time refinement. We propose a Visual-Code Diagnostics framework that enables refinement through visual feedback from source code. Specifically, we first categorize generation errors into four coarse-grained types, and then train lightweight discriminators to recognize and localize these errors based on the rendered visual outputs and corresponding code. Unlike traditional compiler- or debugger-based verification, our approach performs iterative refinement guided by one diagnostics system, enabling more effective semantic and visual correction during test time. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method consistently improves generation quality across diverse generators and significantly outperforms strong test-time scaling baselines.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.775263+00	\N	\N	e4da08c394cb393c8d2369d15b52e816c1dfc0e6142c0d6e19ce44c3800faa96	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.775577+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.775578+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1909fe55-1aaa-4539-b312-20dbde167045	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10848v1	MultiTurnRoute: A Platform for Building and Evaluating Multi-Turn LLM Routers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10848	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10848	Jiaqi Xue, Mengxin Zheng, Heng Huang, Qian Lou	arXiv:2611.10848v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In a multi-turn conversation, the choice of language model at one turn can affect which model should answer the next. The selected model's reply shapes the context for the next query, and its cached prompt prefix can reduce the cost of using that model again. We introduce MultiTurnRoute, a platform for building and evaluating multi-turn LLM routers. A shared interface gives built-in and user-defined routers the current query, dialogue history, and an estimate of each model's reusable prefix length. Our routing algorithm, Chain-of-Route (CoR), uses a learned gate over recent and recurrent dialogue history to predict response quality and estimates cost from each model's reusable prefix. MultiTurnRoute Arena ranks routers on recorded conversations and compares session quality and cost in interactive runs where a simulated user responds to each reply. On a benchmark drawn from WildChat and LMSYS-Chat-1M with per-turn quality annotations for five LLMs, CoR reduces the normalized cost required to match the strongest model's quality by up to 20.3% relative to the strongest routing baseline, while matching or improving peak response quality.	arXiv:2611.10848v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In a multi-turn conversation, the choice of language model at one turn can affect which model should answer the next. The selected model's reply shapes the context for the next query, and its cached prompt prefix can reduce the cost of using that model again. We introduce MultiTurnRoute, a platform for building and evaluating multi-turn LLM routers. A shared interface gives built-in and user-defined routers the current query, dialogue history, and an estimate of each model's reusable prefix length. Our routing algorithm, Chain-of-Route (CoR), uses a learned gate over recent and recurrent dialogue history to predict response quality and estimates cost from each model's reusable prefix. MultiTurnRoute Arena ranks routers on recorded conversations and compares session quality and cost in interactive runs where a simulated user responds to each reply. On a benchmark drawn from WildChat and LMSYS-Chat-1M with per-turn quality annotations for five LLMs, CoR reduces the normalized cost required to match the strongest model's quality by up to 20.3% relative to the strongest routing baseline, while matching or improving peak response quality.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.777198+00	\N	\N	1bb70871859d4caa791018905d0af36fbca266b0727efce4bd49bddd9831dc06	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.777521+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.777522+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2a755b50-3f00-4666-af38-f980986c3c9d	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/helping-older-adults-use-ai-in-everyday-life	Helping older adults use AI in everyday life	https://openai.com/index/helping-older-adults-use-ai-in-everyday-life	https://openai.com/index/helping-older-adults-use-ai-in-everyday-life	\N	OpenAI and AARP are bringing free, hands-on ChatGPT workshops to 1,000 older adults across 10 U.S. cities to build practical AI skills safely.	OpenAI and AARP are bringing free, hands-on ChatGPT workshops to 1,000 older adults across 10 U.S. cities to build practical AI skills safely.	news	en	2026-09-16 16:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.199187+00	3	\N	517b7abc8755bca6864486d9645947708f5e2ff8b0427954f2107e31c148a894	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.199855+00	2026-11-10 14:34:58.102799+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a2422260-3204-4acc-8156-95920bf1eb5e	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10931v1	Gendered Misclassification from Surname-Based Race Imputation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10931	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10931	Noah Dasanaike	arXiv:2611.10931v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Researchers increasingly estimate political behavior based on race and gender from voter files, where race is imputed from surnames and geography. This only works where surnames suggest group membership, an assumption violated where surnames follow norms around marriage. Imputation is therefore biased against women who adopt their husband's surname, especially those who racially inter-marry, an outcome itself endogenous to partisanship. Using six US states recording self-reported race, I show that imputation is less accurate for Asian and Hispanic women, particularly those sharing the surname of a partner of another race, who differ politically from other women of their race. This violates assumptions behind downstream inference approaches, which nearly double the gender gap in partisanship among Asian voters. I derive an expression showing the bias is largest where a group is small. Using maiden rather than marriage-adopted surnames in North Carolina raises accuracy up to fourfold for women married across racial lines. Recovering maiden surnames, or omitting the married surname, removes much of the bias. In a national replication of a study of gender gaps in turnout, the correction more than triples the gap among Asian voters. In Lebanon, where wives register in their husband's locality, the error enters through geography.	arXiv:2611.10931v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Researchers increasingly estimate political behavior based on race and gender from voter files, where race is imputed from surnames and geography. This only works where surnames suggest group membership, an assumption violated where surnames follow norms around marriage. Imputation is therefore biased against women who adopt their husband's surname, especially those who racially inter-marry, an outcome itself endogenous to partisanship. Using six US states recording self-reported race, I show that imputation is less accurate for Asian and Hispanic women, particularly those sharing the surname of a partner of another race, who differ politically from other women of their race. This violates assumptions behind downstream inference approaches, which nearly double the gender gap in partisanship among Asian voters. I derive an expression showing the bias is largest where a group is small. Using maiden rather than marriage-adopted surnames in North Carolina raises accuracy up to fourfold for women married across racial lines. Recovering maiden surnames, or omitting the married surname, removes much of the bias. In a national replication of a study of gender gaps in turnout, the correction more than triples the gap among Asian voters. In Lebanon, where wives register in their husband's locality, the error enters through geography.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.787635+00	\N	\N	a43df2410b160a00a7084380a59d727438502cfe5954eb941f189a03f732bf94	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.787961+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.787962+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
7b192640-7052-4441-b3b6-e9d8c151e6e1	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10971v1	When Safety Becomes An Outlier: Understanding the Retention of LLM Safety Behaviors	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10971	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10971	Binchi Zhang, Hadi Abdullah, Yiwei Cai, Jundong Li	arXiv:2611.10971v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Safety-aligned large language models often lose their safety behaviors after fine-tuning, even when safety data are included. While this fragility is well documented, its underlying cause remains unclear. In this paper, we propose that formulaic refusal strings can function as outlier modes: narrow, weakly grounded response patterns that are easy to fit but vulnerable to subsequent updates. Controlled experiments support this account: fixed refusals are acquired much like arbitrary constant strings, in contrast to prompt-grounded natural responses. Motivated by these findings, we introduce Naturalness-Enhanced Safety Targets (NEST), a safety alignment framework that improves safety retention by constructing natural, context-aware safety targets that keep safety responses within the model's existing output distribution and grounded in semantics. We instantiate NEST in both supervised and preference-based alignment settings, using perplexity under the original model as a proxy for distributional support. Across multiple models and alignment regimes, natural safety targets improve both immediate safety and its retention under continued fine-tuning without sacrificing helpfulness. These findings suggest a practical principle for more durable safety alignment: construct safety targets that draw on the model's existing language capabilities and remain grounded in the meaning of each request.	arXiv:2611.10971v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Safety-aligned large language models often lose their safety behaviors after fine-tuning, even when safety data are included. While this fragility is well documented, its underlying cause remains unclear. In this paper, we propose that formulaic refusal strings can function as outlier modes: narrow, weakly grounded response patterns that are easy to fit but vulnerable to subsequent updates. Controlled experiments support this account: fixed refusals are acquired much like arbitrary constant strings, in contrast to prompt-grounded natural responses. Motivated by these findings, we introduce Naturalness-Enhanced Safety Targets (NEST), a safety alignment framework that improves safety retention by constructing natural, context-aware safety targets that keep safety responses within the model's existing output distribution and grounded in semantics. We instantiate NEST in both supervised and preference-based alignment settings, using perplexity under the original model as a proxy for distributional support. Across multiple models and alignment regimes, natural safety targets improve both immediate safety and its retention under continued fine-tuning without sacrificing helpfulness. These findings suggest a practical principle for more durable safety alignment: construct safety targets that draw on the model's existing language capabilities and remain grounded in the meaning of each request.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.792899+00	\N	\N	386583de2871f63bc23f9687835660894da2c6ca2f7fff6a255b71101745c556	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.793221+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.793223+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1fb03a83-6cfc-448f-be5c-cc5df0148d07	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/astra-for-law	Introducing Astra for Law	https://openai.com/index/astra-for-law	https://openai.com/index/astra-for-law	\N	OpenAI for Law brings frontier intelligence for law, custom firm workflows, connected legal data sources, and legal-grade controls for confidential client work.	OpenAI for Law brings frontier intelligence for law, custom firm workflows, connected legal data sources, and legal-grade controls for confidential client work.	product_update	en	2026-09-17 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.196695+00	4	\N	e39ca2efb804909e6a51eb6b4e2008d309a065fe7adf1eef45c34eb073681284	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.197097+00	2026-11-10 14:35:03.050464+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cfccb497-2769-4535-ab6b-1f24fe81026c	d58a9c86-f9be-4f8b-bd34-60f063fd344d	\N	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/6/nilay-patel/	Quoting Nilay Patel	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/6/nilay-patel/	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/6/nilay-patel/	\N	The products aren't good enough to sell people on the costs. You cannot tell people that you have to listen to the whine of a data center in their backyard for AI overviews and some of the most hilarious deep fake slop in history. It's not worth it. It just isn't worth it to anyone. And agents aren't real and they might not be being used the way that anyone wants them to yet. All these companies are spending more than they're making on these products. Refrigerators holding blood storage in Europe are being hacked. The costs are too high. The benefits are not real. And if you connect that to corruption, you're like, in order to impose these costs on me, these CEOs are in the White House shaking Trump's hand, renaming it to the Gulf of America, paying for this ballroom that we don't want. It's obvious crypto-corruption, and it's still slop. The AI industry will not get out of it until they make better products . Until everyone can see without needing to be convinced, without needing an ad campaign, without any of this stuff that just using these products makes your life better. — Nilay Patel , The Vergecast: Big Tech lost big in the 2026 election Tags: nilay-patel , slop , ai , generative-ai , ai-ethics	The products aren't good enough to sell people on the costs. You cannot tell people that you have to listen to the whine of a data center in their backyard for AI overviews and some of the most hilarious deep fake slop in history. It's not worth it. It just isn't worth it to anyone. And agents aren't real and they might not be being used the way that anyone wants them to yet. All these companies are spending more than they're making on these products. Refrigerators holding blood storage in Europe are being hacked. The costs are too high. The benefits are not real. And if you connect that to corruption, you're like, in order to impose these costs on me, these CEOs are in the White House shaking Trump's hand, renaming it to the Gulf of America, paying for this ballroom that we don't want. It's obvious crypto-corruption, and it's still slop. The AI industry will not get out of it until they make better products . Until everyone can see without needing to be convinced, without needing an ad campaign, without any of this stuff that just using these products makes your life better. — Nilay Patel , The Vergecast: Big Tech lost big in the 2026 election Tags: nilay-patel , slop , ai , generative-ai , ai-ethics	opinion	en	2026-11-06 17:13:12+00	2026-11-09 17:12:46.812873+00	3	\N	192a3c56e3b8169e41c873b66d33aa2bfcac90a82a6b70a7f8b0a38230c96da2	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:46.813544+00	2026-11-10 14:44:42.293196+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
384fbcaa-f80b-43bf-8e9e-18ec8e41545f	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11284v1	WarBias: A Bilingual LLM Benchmark of Stereotypes About Internally Displaced People and Veterans	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11284	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11284	Viktoriia Makovska, Tetiana Zakharchenko, Oleksandra Ostafiichuk	arXiv:2611.11284v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: This paper contains examples of stereotypes about veterans and displaced people that may be offensive or distressing. Social bias benchmarks for language models commonly examine categories such as gender, race, and religion, often drawn from lists of legally protected characteristics. Veterans and internally displaced people (IDPs) fall outside these lists, and stereotypes about them, which can undermine access to employment, housing, and social participation, remain largely absent from language model evaluation. We introduce WarBias, a Ukrainian--English benchmark of stereotypes about these two groups. Built from a taxonomy of 120 stereotype claims grounded in research and monitoring reports, WarBias contains 1,007 stereotype--counter-stereotype--unrelated sentence triplets and 240 multiple-choice questions per language. A Ukrainian language model selected in a blinded human pilot generated the Ukrainian triplets. At least two independent annotators reviewed each triplet in the annotation cohort. The authors revised items in response to the reviews, and the final release was machine-translated into English. Each claim carries a human severity rating. In a baseline evaluation of six models, four prefer stereotypical Ukrainian sentences about at least one group, most strongly about IDPs, while English scores are mostly balanced. WarBias supports complementary evaluations of stereotypical preferences and context-sensitive judgments, and provides paired forget/retain material for unlearning-based bias mitigation. We document the benchmark's construction, intended uses, and limitations, and offer its construction method as a template for analogous benchmarks in other conflict-affected societies.	arXiv:2611.11284v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: This paper contains examples of stereotypes about veterans and displaced people that may be offensive or distressing. Social bias benchmarks for language models commonly examine categories such as gender, race, and religion, often drawn from lists of legally protected characteristics. Veterans and internally displaced people (IDPs) fall outside these lists, and stereotypes about them, which can undermine access to employment, housing, and social participation, remain largely absent from language model evaluation. We introduce WarBias, a Ukrainian--English benchmark of stereotypes about these two groups. Built from a taxonomy of 120 stereotype claims grounded in research and monitoring reports, WarBias contains 1,007 stereotype--counter-stereotype--unrelated sentence triplets and 240 multiple-choice questions per language. A Ukrainian language model selected in a blinded human pilot generated the Ukrainian triplets. At least two independent annotators reviewed each triplet in the annotation cohort. The authors revised items in response to the reviews, and the final release was machine-translated into English. Each claim carries a human severity rating. In a baseline evaluation of six models, four prefer stereotypical Ukrainian sentences about at least one group, most strongly about IDPs, while English scores are mostly balanced. WarBias supports complementary evaluations of stereotypical preferences and context-sensitive judgments, and provides paired forget/retain material for unlearning-based bias mitigation. We document the benchmark's construction, intended uses, and limitations, and offer its construction method as a template for analogous benchmarks in other conflict-affected societies.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.817555+00	\N	\N	5a6f2746068c8df0f92888c4b2fe77621afcbcd5e3e0c80fd02bad0d359560a5	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.81788+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.817882+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4751b4be-4f83-4941-a001-10d627c2ec23	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11349v1	AOP-Smart-Finder: A High-Throughput Human-AI Hybrid Framework for AOP Discovery and Evidence Mapping from Large-Scale Literature	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11349	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11349	Qinjiang Niu, Lu Yan	arXiv:2611.11349v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The rapid growth of scientific literature and the high heterogeneity of biological terminology and mechanistic descriptions make it difficult for researchers to integrate scattered research evidence, identify connections among biological events, and construct mechanistic networks. Here, we developed a high-throughput human-AI hybrid framework, AOP-Smart-Finder, for biological event discovery, relationship mining, and Adverse Outcome Pathway (AOP) construction and evidence mapping from large-scale literature. This framework integrates Large Language Models (LLMs), AOP-Smart retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) technology, and Python-based automation, supporting two analysis modes: one based on a single compound and the other based on a predefined set of biological events. In the di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP) case, the system processed 12,983 articles within 30 min and included 1,093 of them, from which it extracted 1,589 biological events (including 458 official key events [KEs] and 1,131 candidate KEs) and 2,519 key event relationships (KERs). In the amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) case, the system identified 55 stressors from 437 articles and extracted 278 biological events and 548 event relationships. Following manual curation and Weight of Evidence (WoE) evaluation, a mechanistic network comprising 16 KEs and 23 KERs was ultimately constructed. This framework can either use existing AOPs as knowledge guidance for mechanistic expansion and evidence supplementation, or discover novel event connections and construct mechanistic networks around user-specified research topics. It provides a high-throughput, traceable, human-AI hybrid methodology for complex biological mechanism elucidation and AOP discovery driven by large-scale literature.	arXiv:2611.11349v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The rapid growth of scientific literature and the high heterogeneity of biological terminology and mechanistic descriptions make it difficult for researchers to integrate scattered research evidence, identify connections among biological events, and construct mechanistic networks. Here, we developed a high-throughput human-AI hybrid framework, AOP-Smart-Finder, for biological event discovery, relationship mining, and Adverse Outcome Pathway (AOP) construction and evidence mapping from large-scale literature. This framework integrates Large Language Models (LLMs), AOP-Smart retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) technology, and Python-based automation, supporting two analysis modes: one based on a single compound and the other based on a predefined set of biological events. In the di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP) case, the system processed 12,983 articles within 30 min and included 1,093 of them, from which it extracted 1,589 biological events (including 458 official key events [KEs] and 1,131 candidate KEs) and 2,519 key event relationships (KERs). In the amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) case, the system identified 55 stressors from 437 articles and extracted 278 biological events and 548 event relationships. Following manual curation and Weight of Evidence (WoE) evaluation, a mechanistic network comprising 16 KEs and 23 KERs was ultimately constructed. This framework can either use existing AOPs as knowledge guidance for mechanistic expansion and evidence supplementation, or discover novel event connections and construct mechanistic networks around user-specified research topics. It provides a high-throughput, traceable, human-AI hybrid methodology for complex biological mechanism elucidation and AOP discovery driven by large-scale literature.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.823651+00	\N	\N	09d83a89a6bda474b1bc179f49c339618fc0c02f19b1acf67611b7c3db663eae	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.824049+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.824051+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
dfa94d96-db99-45bf-90df-492e8838eb4b	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11396v1	Search-by-Answer Bias in Retrieval Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11396	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11396	Adiel Avigad, Shauli Ravfogel, Yoav Goldberg	arXiv:2611.11396v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Dense retrievers are meant to select passages that match a query's semantic specification. We show they suffer from search-by-answer bias: they favor passages that mention answer objects the model knows (scores as likely), over passages that support valid alternative answers. We test this with controlled factual contrasts from LAMA/TREx. For each retriever, we measure how strongly the model scores the canonical object over an alternative object using a token-prediction head, and ask whether this score predicts passage rankings. Across eight retrievers, including DPR, Spider variants, Contriever, E5, and Qwen3-Embedding, larger token-prediction scores consistently predict higher retrieval scores for passages containing the corresponding object. This holds when passages differ only in the answer object, and even when the passage that mentions the high-scoring object expresses a wrong relation. Dense retrieval thus exhibits search-by-answer bias during passage selection, shaping which evidence is retrieved.	arXiv:2611.11396v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Dense retrievers are meant to select passages that match a query's semantic specification. We show they suffer from search-by-answer bias: they favor passages that mention answer objects the model knows (scores as likely), over passages that support valid alternative answers. We test this with controlled factual contrasts from LAMA/TREx. For each retriever, we measure how strongly the model scores the canonical object over an alternative object using a token-prediction head, and ask whether this score predicts passage rankings. Across eight retrievers, including DPR, Spider variants, Contriever, E5, and Qwen3-Embedding, larger token-prediction scores consistently predict higher retrieval scores for passages containing the corresponding object. This holds when passages differ only in the answer object, and even when the passage that mentions the high-scoring object expresses a wrong relation. Dense retrieval thus exhibits search-by-answer bias during passage selection, shaping which evidence is retrieved.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.827601+00	\N	\N	fa36a776a2f2a223cbf8b7f6c26101b11f32955abf859f90e1017abd1996c8b8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.827917+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.827918+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c24c45f7-08e7-4a0f-aeea-49351d5d303e	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11487v1	THEATRON: A Benchmark for Evaluating Persona Persistence in Long-Horizon LLM Decision-Making	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11487	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11487	Yuyan Wang, Yuechen Jiang, Zhiwei Liu, Jimin Huang, Sophia Ananiadou	arXiv:2611.11487v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Existing evaluations often test persona expression rather than whether persona information persists in extended decision-making. We introduce THEATRON, a controlled benchmark that uses financial replay to evaluate persona persistence in long-horizon sequential decision-making. In these environments, models conditioned on fixed behavioral-finance personas observe shared daily market evidence and trajectory-specific account-state feedback and make sequential decisions over multi-month trajectories. We evaluate Zero-Shot, One-Shot, and Chain-of-Thought persona injection across four LLMs, using cumulative-return and action trajectories to diagnose within-persona stability, cross-persona separability, and action collapse. Persona conditioning produces systematic behavioral shifts beyond a no-persona baseline, and persona-level separation generally exceeds the variation induced by semantically equivalent descriptions. Nevertheless, these shifts do not reliably form coherent persona-specific policies: apparently stable separation can arise from stereotyped action collapse, while diverse policies can exhibit noisy trajectory switching. These findings expose a gap between observable persona-induced behavior and persistent, context-sensitive persona-conditioned decision policies. Code and benchmark data are publicly available at: https://github.com/yuyan12138/theatron	arXiv:2611.11487v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Existing evaluations often test persona expression rather than whether persona information persists in extended decision-making. We introduce THEATRON, a controlled benchmark that uses financial replay to evaluate persona persistence in long-horizon sequential decision-making. In these environments, models conditioned on fixed behavioral-finance personas observe shared daily market evidence and trajectory-specific account-state feedback and make sequential decisions over multi-month trajectories. We evaluate Zero-Shot, One-Shot, and Chain-of-Thought persona injection across four LLMs, using cumulative-return and action trajectories to diagnose within-persona stability, cross-persona separability, and action collapse. Persona conditioning produces systematic behavioral shifts beyond a no-persona baseline, and persona-level separation generally exceeds the variation induced by semantically equivalent descriptions. Nevertheless, these shifts do not reliably form coherent persona-specific policies: apparently stable separation can arise from stereotyped action collapse, while diverse policies can exhibit noisy trajectory switching. These findings expose a gap between observable persona-induced behavior and persistent, context-sensitive persona-conditioned decision policies. Code and benchmark data are publicly available at: https://github.com/yuyan12138/theatron	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.836624+00	\N	\N	11be0d0079dbc98798f22fac7cabaa417c37b999a462921f4e8b9eb644aa2af8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.836952+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.836953+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a9938261-674e-45c8-8a04-abe02d68d0fd	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11502v1	Does Merge Form a Hopf Algebra? Coproducts, Traces, and the Prospects of Algebraic Minimalism	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11502	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11502	Daisuke Bekki (Ochanomizu University)	arXiv:2611.11502v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Marcolli, Chomsky, and Berwick (2025; henceforth MCB) formulate the Merge operation of Minimalism on a Hopf algebra of Connes-Kreimer type, and claim that language and physics obey the same algebraic structure. This paper separates their program into the working hypothesis H, that Merge on workspaces can be described as a Hopf algebra, and the claim P placed on top of it, that language and physics obey the same principle, and examines the validity of H. MCB employs two coproducts in formulating Merge, corresponding to two different translations of movement (Internal Merge): $\\Delta_d$, which deletes the lower copy, and $\\Delta_c$, which leaves a trace. MCB uses the former in syntax and the latter at the syntax-semantics interface, but $\\Delta_d$ is not coassociative, and $\\Delta_c$ is not connected under the gradings MCB gives and, moreover, not coassociative as long as the labels of traces are retained. Consequently, MCB's algebra is not a Hopf algebra, and the Birkhoff factorization is not a consequence of the Hopf-algebra theorem but of a weaker statement that requires neither coassociativity nor connectedness. There are four ways to recover a Hopf algebra, but none of them can distinguish different traces, and hence none is compatible with Heim-Kratzer-style formal semantics, which reads traces as indexed variables. Thus H does not hold at present, and under the current formulation, making Minimalism a Hopf algebra and preserving its connection with formal semantics are mutually exclusive. Finally, we indicate three directions in which algebraic Minimalism may proceed.	arXiv:2611.11502v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Marcolli, Chomsky, and Berwick (2025; henceforth MCB) formulate the Merge operation of Minimalism on a Hopf algebra of Connes-Kreimer type, and claim that language and physics obey the same algebraic structure. This paper separates their program into the working hypothesis H, that Merge on workspaces can be described as a Hopf algebra, and the claim P placed on top of it, that language and physics obey the same principle, and examines the validity of H. MCB employs two coproducts in formulating Merge, corresponding to two different translations of movement (Internal Merge): $\\Delta_d$, which deletes the lower copy, and $\\Delta_c$, which leaves a trace. MCB uses the former in syntax and the latter at the syntax-semantics interface, but $\\Delta_d$ is not coassociative, and $\\Delta_c$ is not connected under the gradings MCB gives and, moreover, not coassociative as long as the labels of traces are retained. Consequently, MCB's algebra is not a Hopf algebra, and the Birkhoff factorization is not a consequence of the Hopf-algebra theorem but of a weaker statement that requires neither coassociativity nor connectedness. There are four ways to recover a Hopf algebra, but none of them can distinguish different traces, and hence none is compatible with Heim-Kratzer-style formal semantics, which reads traces as indexed variables. Thus H does not hold at present, and under the current formulation, making Minimalism a Hopf algebra and preserving its connection with formal semantics are mutually exclusive. Finally, we indicate three directions in which algebraic Minimalism may proceed.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.840827+00	\N	\N	0aa2b4faba3896261c88230ea1ef76785010cd38bbb32481fddf6ba0683337a9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.841144+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.841145+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b867f127-a848-40e7-8fa4-ff88302e2f08	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/stack-overflow-chatgpt-plugin	Stack Overflow for Agents triples signups with ChatGPT plugin	https://openai.com/index/stack-overflow-chatgpt-plugin	https://openai.com/index/stack-overflow-chatgpt-plugin	\N	Two engineers shipped Stack Overflow for Agents in a few weeks with Codex, and its ChatGPT plugin helped drive up to 10× growth in agent creation.	Two engineers shipped Stack Overflow for Agents in a few weeks with Codex, and its ChatGPT plugin helped drive up to 10× growth in agent creation.	product_update	en	2026-10-16 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.040081+00	3	\N	1d670f55b7e8bf7faa686f3d10acc953b3a571af975bff92f77e1e17162dfa93	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.040635+00	2026-11-10 14:38:40.99927+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a6e834f9-648c-455f-96c4-3321ff0bb760	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11573v1	Beyond the Transcript: Measuring and Improving the Use of Acoustic Information in Audio Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11573	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11573	Linkai Peng, Baorian Nuchged, Yuyang Yao	arXiv:2611.11573v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Audio language model benchmarks provide comprehensive evaluation across diverse tasks. However, some tasks can be answered from the transcript or even the question alone, and benchmark scores can therefore confound acoustic understanding with language modeling ability. We distinguish lexical tasks, for which the transcript usually suffices, from global-attribute and within-recording-structure tasks, which require the model to analyse the recording beyond its words, either as a whole, for properties such as the speaker and emotion, or within it, for patterns such as stress and intonation. We evaluated 19 open models and Gemini 3.1 Pro on 92 tasks from 6 benchmarks and estimated the recording gain, the accuracy advantage of the original recording over references that retain its words, by comparing the recording with synthetic readings of its transcript and by adding it to the transcript. On attribute and structure tasks, the open models fall behind Gemini by 28.6 and 41.9 percentage points, and about half or more of each deficit lies in the recording gain. Nevertheless, linear probes in Gemma-4 12B and Qwen2.5-Omni-7B show that the acoustic information needed for answering is already readable in early language-model layers, while the readout of many cues declines in the layers that follow. We therefore constructed training data and trained LoRA adapters in these layers, with a content anchor, which raises attribute and structure accuracy by 12.8 to 44.4 points in the two models and keeps mean lexical accuracy within 1 point of the base model. Most of the improvement appears as larger recording gains. These results suggest that training improves the use of acoustic information that the models already encode.	arXiv:2611.11573v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Audio language model benchmarks provide comprehensive evaluation across diverse tasks. However, some tasks can be answered from the transcript or even the question alone, and benchmark scores can therefore confound acoustic understanding with language modeling ability. We distinguish lexical tasks, for which the transcript usually suffices, from global-attribute and within-recording-structure tasks, which require the model to analyse the recording beyond its words, either as a whole, for properties such as the speaker and emotion, or within it, for patterns such as stress and intonation. We evaluated 19 open models and Gemini 3.1 Pro on 92 tasks from 6 benchmarks and estimated the recording gain, the accuracy advantage of the original recording over references that retain its words, by comparing the recording with synthetic readings of its transcript and by adding it to the transcript. On attribute and structure tasks, the open models fall behind Gemini by 28.6 and 41.9 percentage points, and about half or more of each deficit lies in the recording gain. Nevertheless, linear probes in Gemma-4 12B and Qwen2.5-Omni-7B show that the acoustic information needed for answering is already readable in early language-model layers, while the readout of many cues declines in the layers that follow. We therefore constructed training data and trained LoRA adapters in these layers, with a content anchor, which raises attribute and structure accuracy by 12.8 to 44.4 points in the two models and keeps mean lexical accuracy within 1 point of the base model. Most of the improvement appears as larger recording gains. These results suggest that training improves the use of acoustic information that the models already encode.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.844755+00	\N	\N	4baf2ecc47e96680f3cd1399368023c0016725ff5c220