23e24cbd-0848-4e74-a169-229d7814d81d	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11597v1	Out of Parameters, Saved by Syntax: UD-PrMPT for Parser Augmented Low Resource Machine Translation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11597	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11597	Emma Thronson, Abhishek Purushothama, Amir Zeldes	arXiv:2611.11597v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Recent work advancing machine translation quality using LLMs for very low resource languages has relied on the injection of lexicon-based prompt components to expose vocabulary to models. However, these approaches run into a ceiling when models must guess sentence meaning purely from plausible combinations of possible word senses mapped to strings. In this paper we test UD-PrMPT, an approach using syntactic analyses in the Universal Dependencies formalism as prompt components to improve MT in five diverse low resource languages: Beja, Chintang, Chukchi, Coptic, and Xibe. Our results show additive improvements which stack on top of the lexicon based approach which are stronger for higher quality manual analyses but consistent for predicted parses as well.	arXiv:2611.11597v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Recent work advancing machine translation quality using LLMs for very low resource languages has relied on the injection of lexicon-based prompt components to expose vocabulary to models. However, these approaches run into a ceiling when models must guess sentence meaning purely from plausible combinations of possible word senses mapped to strings. In this paper we test UD-PrMPT, an approach using syntactic analyses in the Universal Dependencies formalism as prompt components to improve MT in five diverse low resource languages: Beja, Chintang, Chukchi, Coptic, and Xibe. Our results show additive improvements which stack on top of the lexicon based approach which are stronger for higher quality manual analyses but consistent for predicted parses as well.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30: