5064d8b6-1806-429b-85fc-d00cf64a364c	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/how-to-connect-ai-usage-to-business-value	How to connect AI usage to business value	https://openai.com/index/how-to-connect-ai-usage-to-business-value	https://openai.com/index/how-to-connect-ai-usage-to-business-value	\N	Learn how ChatGPT Work and Codex analytics help teams understand AI usage and spend, identify training needs, and connect adoption to business outcomes.	Learn how ChatGPT Work and Codex analytics help teams understand AI usage and spend, identify training needs, and connect adoption to business outcomes.	tutorial	en	2026-09-16 12:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.20698+00	3	\N	bb799286f57ba563bd19915731a0f0ae2a483f2d154b5b27b7f4df450d4429bb	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.207357+00	2026-11-10 14:34:52.68735+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
10194afc-e102-43bc-bc5f-7db34fea7220	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09012v1	Towards Robust Reinforcement Learning Market Making: Lessons from Loss Function Experimentation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09012	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09012	Siddharth Nand, Bora Guney	arXiv:2611.09012v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Market makers play a vital role in ensuring efficient trade within financial markets. Recent literature has delved into training market-making systems using reinforcement learning. This paper delves into the impact of various loss function formulations on risk management behavior within reinforcement-based market making. Through empirical evaluation, we contrast profit-maximizing strategies with risk-averse approaches, finding that penalties for inventory and cash management foster more resilient and adaptable market-making strategies. Our results underscore the significance of striking a balance between profit maximization and risk management in algorithmic trading, providing actionable insights for refining strategies in practical settings.	arXiv:2611.09012v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Market makers play a vital role in ensuring efficient trade within financial markets. Recent literature has delved into training market-making systems using reinforcement learning. This paper delves into the impact of various loss function formulations on risk management behavior within reinforcement-based market making. Through empirical evaluation, we contrast profit-maximizing strategies with risk-averse approaches, finding that penalties for inventory and cash management foster more resilient and adaptable market-making strategies. Our results underscore the significance of striking a balance between profit maximization and risk management in algorithmic trading, providing actionable insights for refining strategies in practical settings.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.38812+00	3	\N	666ae4c91d3b1b09a0cd3a6b3cdadcf32d6d48130c7f6ae227a34be816b7e699	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.388592+00	2026-11-10 14:48:52.662513+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
057a3aa4-17bb-4d1d-af96-f2ff58bb4fe3	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.21954v2	Are Multilingual Models Actually Improving? Revisiting Cross-Lingual Transfer	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21954	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21954	Prasoon Bajpai, Eleftheria Briakou, Colin Cherry, Preethi Jyothi, Vihari Piratla	arXiv:2606.21954v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Cross-lingual transfer is a model's ability to generalize capabilities from well-represented source languages to under-represented target languages. Existing measures of a model's transfer strength conflate improvements in transfer with general improvements to accuracy in the source language. We advocate for an alternate metric that reliably captures transfer strength called Hardness Adjusted Transfer (HAT) Score, and use it to derive multiple insights on factors influencing transfer strength. Our analysis across twenty diverse language models and three popular mainstream multilingual benchmarks argues that 1) transfer in small models is not broken, 2) we are making slower than expected progress in cross-lingual transfer with model size, and 3) we have made clear progress over time.	arXiv:2606.21954v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Cross-lingual transfer is a model's ability to generalize capabilities from well-represented source languages to under-represented target languages. Existing measures of a model's transfer strength conflate improvements in transfer with general improvements to accuracy in the source language. We advocate for an alternate metric that reliably captures transfer strength called Hardness Adjusted Transfer (HAT) Score, and use it to derive multiple insights on factors influencing transfer strength. Our analysis across twenty diverse language models and three popular mainstream multilingual benchmarks argues that 1) transfer in small models is not broken, 2) we are making slower than expected progress in cross-lingual transfer with model size, and 3) we have made clear progress over time.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.192515+00	\N	\N	7568c561f383ee8cf31ec12acb563d0f58acdc10221260d73ffe2274a21c55bb	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.192766+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.192767+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f5343001-c4ac-41e0-beb5-28afccf54f8e	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.29858v3	Smooth Scaling Laws Hide Stepwise Token Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29858	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29858	Pingjie Wang, Zechen Hu, Peiru Yang, Fu Guo, Debing Zhang	arXiv:2606.29858v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Language model loss follows remarkably regular scaling laws over model and data size, yet it remains unclear why the aggregate loss should exhibit a power-law form. Existing explanations often attribute this regularity to a heavy-tailed spectrum of pattern difficulty in natural language, but this view has not been directly validated at token-level granularity in large-scale real-data training. We present a token-level framework that decomposes scaling laws into localized learning events of individual contextualized tokens. By fitting token loss trajectories with sigmoids, we show that token learning is concentrated in localized transitions, giving rise to a learning-time spectrum that dominates the scaling-law shape. Across more than one hundred pre-training runs on large and diverse real-language corpora with modern LLM architectures, scaling up to 6B parameters and 300B training tokens, the measured learning-time spectrum quantitatively reconstructs the validation loss derivative along the training-step $T$, data-scale $D$, and model-scale $M$ axes. We further show that the same signal is actionable: by reshaping the training distribution according to when tokens become learnable, we alter the optimization trajectory and achieve 11\\% faster validation-loss reduction. These results provide direct empirical evidence that scaling laws are governed primarily by the distribution of token-level learning times, and that this distribution can be used not only to explain scaling behavior but also to improve training performance.	arXiv:2606.29858v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Language model loss follows remarkably regular scaling laws over model and data size, yet it remains unclear why the aggregate loss should exhibit a power-law form. Existing explanations often attribute this regularity to a heavy-tailed spectrum of pattern difficulty in natural language, but this view has not been directly validated at token-level granularity in large-scale real-data training. We present a token-level framework that decomposes scaling laws into localized learning events of individual contextualized tokens. By fitting token loss trajectories with sigmoids, we show that token learning is concentrated in localized transitions, giving rise to a learning-time spectrum that dominates the scaling-law shape. Across more than one hundred pre-training runs on large and diverse real-language corpora with modern LLM architectures, scaling up to 6B parameters and 300B training tokens, the measured learning-time spectrum quantitatively reconstructs the validation loss derivative along the training-step $T$, data-scale $D$, and model-scale $M$ axes. We further show that the same signal is actionable: by reshaping the training distribution according to when tokens become learnable, we alter the optimization trajectory and achieve 11\\% faster validation-loss reduction. These results provide direct empirical evidence that scaling laws are governed primarily by the distribution of token-level learning times, and that this distribution can be used not only to explain scaling behavior but also to improve training performance.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.196613+00	\N	\N	da1433353074ec816c7b2f171f8f00d50f1e79b9a776b96d309a28b5868f7a1a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.196967+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.196968+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5c1a909f-ea58-4bc4-94ce-2b6bb1667458	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2607.11070v2	MJ: Multi-turn LLM Jailbreaking via Decomposed Credit Assignment	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.11070	https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.11070	Junyoung Park, Namgyu Park, Sechan Lee, Yoon-Chan Jhi, Jihoon Cho, Sangdon Park	arXiv:2607.11070v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern large language models (LLMs) operate in interactive multi-turn settings, making multi-turn jailbreaking a realistic threat model and an important setting for automated red teaming. A core challenge in learning multi-turn jailbreak attackers is credit assignment: different turns contribute differently to the final outcome, yet existing learning signals are often too coarse to identify their individual contributions.\n  We propose decomposed credit GRPO (DC-GRPO), a unified turn-level credit assignment framework for Group Relative Policy Optimization in multi-turn jailbreak learning. DC-GRPO assigns a separate group-relative learning signal to each turn by combining immediate and future credit, avoiding the credit misassignment induced by broadcasting a single trajectory-level score across the dialogue. We instantiate this framework with static and dynamic weighting rules that differ in how the two credit sources are balanced while sharing the same turn-level structure.\n  Across four victim LLMs and three benchmarks, DC-GRPO achieves up to 98.26% average ASR_5@3, substantially outperforming the state-of-the-art methods, including SEMA (86.58%) and TROJail (86.23%), demonstrating the effectiveness of our turn-level group-relative credit assignment.\n  Warning: This paper contains examples of harmful content.	arXiv:2607.11070v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern large language models (LLMs) operate in interactive multi-turn settings, making multi-turn jailbreaking a realistic threat model and an important setting for automated red teaming. A core challenge in learning multi-turn jailbreak attackers is credit assignment: different turns contribute differently to the final outcome, yet existing learning signals are often too coarse to identify their individual contributions.\n  We propose decomposed credit GRPO (DC-GRPO), a unified turn-level credit assignment framework for Group Relative Policy Optimization in multi-turn jailbreak learning. DC-GRPO assigns a separate group-relative learning signal to each turn by combining immediate and future credit, avoiding the credit misassignment induced by broadcasting a single trajectory-level score across the dialogue. We instantiate this framework with static and dynamic weighting rules that differ in how the two credit sources are balanced while sharing the same turn-level structure.\n  Across four victim LLMs and three benchmarks, DC-GRPO achieves up to 98.26% average ASR_5@3, substantially outperforming the state-of-the-art methods, including SEMA (86.58%) and TROJail (86.23%), demonstrating the effectiveness of our turn-level group-relative credit assignment.\n  Warning: This paper contains examples of harmful content.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.201502+00	\N	\N	5f2fed9809c78c25946c01752c84cb0c8fc0906ed7ddeafcbee66e32a3f9f32a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.201784+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.201785+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
943507ad-e653-4763-81c4-fd793a8ddeb3	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.11441v2	DonorRank: Donor Language Selection for Low-Resource Cross-Lingual Speech Recognition	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11441	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11441	Akriti Dhasmana, Aarohi Srivastava, David Chiang	arXiv:2608.11441v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Low-resource automatic speech recognition (ASR) often relies on cross-lingual transfer from higher-resource languages due to limited availability of transcribed speech. However, selecting an effective donor language is a nontrivial issue, particularly in settings with spontaneous speech, linguistic variation, and uneven resource availability. We present DonorRank, a learning-to-rank framework that uses linguistic and dataset-level features along with previously observed transfer outcomes to predict promising donor languages for zero-shot ASR. We evaluate DonorRank on two multilingual speech corpora of Indic and African languages. DonorRank produces informative donor rankings across both collections, outperforming common heuristics in the more closely related Indic setting while remaining competitive in the more linguistically diverse African setting. Our analysis reveals that effective donors are not always the closest linguistic relatives and that the most informative predictors vary across language collections. These findings highlight the value of learned donor selection for prioritizing transfer experiments in low-resource ASR. We will also release all of our evaluation results, feature matrices for all languages, scripts for training the DonorRank framework, and pairwise transfer performance matrices for our datasets.	arXiv:2608.11441v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Low-resource automatic speech recognition (ASR) often relies on cross-lingual transfer from higher-resource languages due to limited availability of transcribed speech. However, selecting an effective donor language is a nontrivial issue, particularly in settings with spontaneous speech, linguistic variation, and uneven resource availability. We present DonorRank, a learning-to-rank framework that uses linguistic and dataset-level features along with previously observed transfer outcomes to predict promising donor languages for zero-shot ASR. We evaluate DonorRank on two multilingual speech corpora of Indic and African languages. DonorRank produces informative donor rankings across both collections, outperforming common heuristics in the more closely related Indic setting while remaining competitive in the more linguistically diverse African setting. Our analysis reveals that effective donors are not always the closest linguistic relatives and that the most informative predictors vary across language collections. These findings highlight the value of learned donor selection for prioritizing transfer experiments in low-resource ASR. We will also release all of our evaluation results, feature matrices for all languages, scripts for training the DonorRank framework, and pairwise transfer performance matrices for our datasets.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.214816+00	\N	\N	f0564ac6011ff61970153a704e41f6b4d4514c61b9621b247ce2a3261932433e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.215127+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.215128+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6f773b0b-2c3b-407e-aa22-f02026871d4b	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.19134v2	ScienceIDE: Turning World's Scientific Codebase into Agent Learnable Environments	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19134	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19134	Hejia Geng, Zesen Huang, Haoyang Li, Wenbin Li, Koutian Wu, Zihan Zhou, Yuanbo Pang, Weihao Liu, Zigong Xu, Zhiping Li, Zongzheng Zhang, Chuanfei Dong, Jiankai Sun, Tianzhe Zheng, Fengyu Xie, Yue Ma, Yueheng Shi, Junkai Wang, Tong Xie, Zonglin Di, Xianrong Liu, Qucheng Gao, Yimin Liu, Jiaming Pan, Sheng Huang, Xiao-Han Ma, Lanqing Yuan, Zhenlin Zhu, Ziang Liu, Ziyang Xu, Kangkai Liang, Jiayi Xian, Zehong Zhao, Taigao Ma, Fuming Chang, Yuanzhe Hu, Liuwei Xu, Xianzhe Tang, Jingxu Xie, Peijin Zhang, Qiang Gao, Chengyi Xing, Zhe Zhao, Xi Wang, Leo Wang, Fang Wu, Jiacheng Ge, Weixuan Liao, Jungang Chen, Shuozhe Li, Yi Daniel Lu, Xu Pan, Shuhang Lin, Peihan Tu, Nan Liu, Zelin Tan, Yunze Xiao, Hao Xiao, Yaopeng Xing, Xing Meng, Zhenfei Yin, Yingcheng Wu, Ling Yang	arXiv:2609.19134v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Scientific code repositories encode decades of human knowledge in executable models, methods, and tools. Yet fragmented toolchains, implicit domain conventions, and specialized correctness criteria make this knowledge difficult to convert into reliable learning experience-a challenge we call the scientific experience bottleneck. We introduce ScienceIDE, infrastructure for turning the world's scientific code into programmable environments for scientific agents. Guided by expert-defined scientific cases and acceptance criteria, agents transform repositories into executable environments that support task generation, execution, and scientific verification. These environments provide a shared foundation for supervised fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, and evaluation. Using verified interaction trajectories, we train PhAI-IDE-72B/9B/4B. The model family shows gains in held-out scientific-code repair and across selected general-purpose benchmarks in code, reasoning, and knowledge, providing evidence of positive transfer from scientific experience to broader capabilities. ScienceIDE lays the foundation for an integrated workspace for agent learning and scientific practice, making humanity's scientific software a shared substrate for developing scientific intelligence. Code: https://github.com/aitofound/ScienceIDE	arXiv:2609.19134v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Scientific code repositories encode decades of human knowledge in executable models, methods, and tools. Yet fragmented toolchains, implicit domain conventions, and specialized correctness criteria make this knowledge difficult to convert into reliable learning experience-a challenge we call the scientific experience bottleneck. We introduce ScienceIDE, infrastructure for turning the world's scientific code into programmable environments for scientific agents. Guided by expert-defined scientific cases and acceptance criteria, agents transform repositories into executable environments that support task generation, execution, and scientific verification. These environments provide a shared foundation for supervised fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, and evaluation. Using verified interaction trajectories, we train PhAI-IDE-72B/9B/4B. The model family shows gains in held-out scientific-code repair and across selected general-purpose benchmarks in code, reasoning, and knowledge, providing evidence of positive transfer from scientific experience to broader capabilities. ScienceIDE lays the foundation for an integrated workspace for agent learning and scientific practice, making humanity's scientific software a shared substrate for developing scientific intelligence. Code: https://github.com/aitofound/ScienceIDE	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.230085+00	\N	\N	9dcebc209c6ced70bdb068c0ff745fc57aaa6ce16982ba38374024405658b72b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.230474+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.230476+00	pending	\