a66e223d-bad4-43d6-a7bc-fa91b205824f	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2505.10877v2	Graph and Simplicial Complex Prediction Gaussian Process via the Hodgelet Representations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10877	https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10877	Mathieu Alain, So Takao, Xiaowen Dong, Bastian Rieck, Emmanuel Noutahi	arXiv:2505.10877v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Predicting labels for graph-structured data is crucial in many scientific applications. Recently, Gaussian processes with graph-level inputs have been proposed as flexible non-parametric models for classification tasks. In this work, we extend this framework to regression tasks and to simplicial complexes, enabling the modelling of attributes defined on edges and higher-order simplices. Drawing on Hodge theory, we construct representations based on the Hodge decomposition, allowing the model to distinguish between exact, co-exact, and harmonic signal components. We introduce Hodgelet representations as rich, learnable topological descriptors of simplicial complexes and show that our framework improves predictive performance across a range of applications. Our work broadens the applicability of Gaussian processes to prediction tasks at the graph and simplicial-complex levels.	arXiv:2505.10877v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Predicting labels for graph-structured data is crucial in many scientific applications. Recently, Gaussian processes with graph-level inputs have been proposed as flexible non-parametric models for classification tasks. In this work, we extend this framework to regression tasks and to simplicial complexes, enabling the modelling of attributes defined on edges and higher-order simplices. Drawing on Hodge theory, we construct representations based on the Hodge decomposition, allowing the model to distinguish between exact, co-exact, and harmonic signal components. We introduce Hodgelet representations as rich, learnable topological descriptors of simplicial complexes and show that our framework improves predictive performance across a range of applications. Our work broadens the applicability of Gaussian processes to prediction tasks at the graph and simplicial-complex levels.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.296073+00	3	\N	0c5ff03f4ed026791533ff289e8c126b2edfbcabd855bc4a70f17d674f493679	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:56.296391+00	2026-11-10 16:34:45.4383+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3860f5f0-0297-4574-b275-a3ae6ba9927d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11946v1	Decision Aware Origin-Destination Demand Estimation with Structurally Constrained Data	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11946	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11946	Xianzhe Wang, Yueyue Fan, Johannes Royset	arXiv:2611.11946v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Origin-destination (OD) demand plays an important role in transportation system design and management applications. Conventional OD estimation methods typically judge estimation quality based on how estimated demand aligns with observed traffic data and historical demand patterns. We argue that data alignment alone is insufficient; how errors in OD estimates influence downstream decision quality should also be considered. This paper develops a decision aware OD demand estimation framework that leverages structurally constrained traffic data to characterize an admissible demand region, within which we identify OD estimates that would lead to least regret in the downstream decision. Therefore, our OD estimates are both data-consistent and decision-relevant. Methodologically, we approximate the admissible demand region induced by physical network constraints through a geometric representation, which regularizes the search over feasible OD estimates. Within this regularized region, we use a convex surrogate of downstream regret to obtain a tractable decision aware optimization problem. Numerical experiments show that the proposed framework consistently reduces decision regret relative to conventional estimation benchmarks, demonstrating the value of aligning OD demand estimation with downstream decision quality.	arXiv:2611.11946v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Origin-destination (OD) demand plays an important role in transportation system design and management applications. Conventional OD estimation methods typically judge estimation quality based on how estimated demand aligns with observed traffic data and historical demand patterns. We argue that data alignment alone is insufficient; how errors in OD estimates influence downstream decision quality should also be considered. This paper develops a decision aware OD demand estimation framework that leverages structurally constrained traffic data to characterize an admissible demand region, within which we identify OD estimates that would lead to least regret in the downstream decision. Therefore, our OD estimates are both data-consistent and decision-relevant. Methodologically, we approximate the admissible demand region induced by physical network constraints through a geometric representation, which regularizes the search over feasible OD estimates. Within this regularized region, we use a convex surrogate of downstream regret to obtain a tractable decision aware optimization problem. Numerical experiments show that the proposed framework consistently reduces decision regret relative to conventional estimation benchmarks, demonstrating the value of aligning OD demand estimation with downstream decision quality.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.078121+00	3	\N	8634f47e3fe116d3b0589dc0397db6438a30fab2aaf577e3e2d20f973d645464	processed	2026-11-10 16:30:02.078484+00	2026-11-10 16:34:48.671096+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
be075b71-21e3-4671-b4c9-0548d322c364	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11926v1	Text Alone Predicts Which Classes a Segmentation Readout Keeps	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11926	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11926	David Yan	arXiv:2611.11926v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: An open-vocabulary segmentation readout silently loses a class deployed beyond its training categories. It still emits masks with high confidence, but the class never wins a single pixel. The training loss falls anyway, and mIoU can even rise while classes die. We show that which classes collapse is written in the class-name list alone. Before any image is seen, we rank each class's collapse risk from its text similarity to the training vocabulary alone. The ranking holds at AUROC 0.69-0.96 across nine model families, twelve backbones and eight pretraining recipes. Two further families, measured only after the predictions were written down, land in the same band. What the list writes, it can rewrite. With identical images and a class's counterpart moved in or out of the training list, retraining flips that class's gain over the frozen baseline by 21 IoU points; swapping in unrelated names changes nothing. We find a vocabulary-distance mechanism behind the collapse. Collapsed classes lose every vote to near-name neighbors, but in the frozen features they survive. Turning down the readout's amplitude, or stopping training early, brings them back, up to +16 points, without retraining; trained with its amplitude held down, the readout never collapses.	arXiv:2611.11926v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: An open-vocabulary segmentation readout silently loses a class deployed beyond its training categories. It still emits masks with high confidence, but the class never wins a single pixel. The training loss falls anyway, and mIoU can even rise while classes die. We show that which classes collapse is written in the class-name list alone. Before any image is seen, we rank each class's collapse risk from its text similarity to the training vocabulary alone. The ranking holds at AUROC 0.69-0.96 across nine model families, twelve backbones and eight pretraining recipes. Two further families, measured only after the predictions were written down, land in the same band. What the list writes, it can rewrite. With identical images and a class's counterpart moved in or out of the training list, retraining flips that class's gain over the frozen baseline by 21 IoU points; swapping in unrelated names changes nothing. We find a vocabulary-distance mechanism behind the collapse. Collapsed classes lose every vote to near-name neighbors, but in the frozen features they survive. Turning down the readout's amplitude, or stopping training early, brings them back, up to +16 points, without retraining; trained with its amplitude held down, the readout never collapses.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:55.74046+00	3	\N	4c08bd4e9ef6f9ee7c7c00955a54ca3a13da617e65f7d21f86eb6209a91bc78a	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:55.74077+00	2026-11-10 16:34:56.179221+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
