6c8e504e-c20b-4cdd-a2da-034fc7738922	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10628v1	Towards Identity-Preserving LLM Agents under Model Transition	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10628	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10628	Yihuang Kang, Pei-Cing Huang, Chan-Tung Ku, Chan Hsu, Pei-Ju Lee, I-Ling Cheng	arXiv:2611.10628v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: When an LLM agent's backend model is upgraded or replaced, operators typically reload the same persona, memory, and rules onto the new model and assume the agent will behave as before. We show this assumption requires empirical validation-and that whether identity is preserved depends on the specific model pairing. Across 5,760 compliance-testing runs in 12 regulated domains with three open-weight models, we find that some transitions produce significant behavioral drift while others do not, even when the agent's external state remains identical. Crucially, this drift is invisible to verbal verification: every model claims full policy compliance, yet actual decisions under pressure differ across models. The same external state can reorder which model is most compliant-a large positive effect on one substrate, negligible on the other two. Practitioners should treat model transitions as behavioral changes requiring action-level empirical testing, not as transparent infrastructure operations.	arXiv:2611.10628v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: When an LLM agent's backend model is upgraded or replaced, operators typically reload the same persona, memory, and rules onto the new model and assume the agent will behave as before. We show this assumption requires empirical validation-and that whether identity is preserved depends on the specific model pairing. Across 5,760 compliance-testing runs in 12 regulated domains with three open-weight models, we find that some transitions produce significant behavioral drift while others do not, even when the agent's external state remains identical. Crucially, this drift is invisible to verbal verification: every model claims full policy compliance, yet actual decisions under pressure differ across models. The same external state can reorder which model is most compliant-a large positive effect on one substrate, negligible on the other two. Practitioners should treat model transitions as behavioral changes requiring action-level empirical testing, not as transparent infrastructure operations.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:54.833025+00	4	\N	a8e7e4d9f05a7fe0fe316e196d8ae1cb9ee788638a4768ce007a8e0de0bbe8d4	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:54.833363+00	2026-11-10 16:35:28.329381+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
92d5d04c-c3c6-444a-b08d-95ddc24731dd	110c8b74-5d9a-4479-9380-aab19486cc0c	\N	https://deepmind.google/blog/accelerating-antimicrobial-discovery/	Accelerating the search for antimicrobial peptides with Google Antigravity	https://deepmind.google/blog/accelerating-antimicrobial-discovery/	https://deepmind.google/blog/accelerating-antimicrobial-discovery/	\N	Accelerating the search for novel antimicrobial peptides using Google Antigravity for Science.	Accelerating the search for novel antimicrobial peptides using Google Antigravity for Science.	research	en	2026-10-15 16:08:03+00	2026-11-09 17:12:35.113336+00	4	\N	11bd3f22d465daf9fd01d58d91148df72c0b671eeceb410f45379481fef97961	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:35.11395+00	2026-11-10 13:24:49.76309+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6d230f7d-28a4-42f5-bfe0-8d0d4d727cdd	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12750v1	The Narrative Gap: Can LLMs Help Us Navigate Diverse Narratives Across Languages?	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12750	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12750	Nikhil Sharma, Kelly Marchisio, Kenton Murray, Ziang Xiao	arXiv:2611.12750v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Large language models (LLMs) increasingly mediate how people seek and consume information, including accounts that differ across languages and regions, especially on contested topics. Supporting diverse information seeking requires models to help users navigate competing narratives across languages and information streams. We define six capabilities necessary for diverse multilingual information seeking: three foundational capabilities for narrative inclusion, narrative exclusion, and missing narrative identification; and three higher-level capabilities for presenting diverse narratives, preserving narrative coverage under confirmatory queries, and maintaining information parity across languages. To guide the development of those information seeking capabilities, we introduce \\textsc{NarrativeBench}, a benchmark comprising 1,333 news articles in 23 publication languages across 23 contested topics and 460 Reddit-grounded queries. Evaluating 14 LLMs across 24 query languages, we find that current models lack the capabilities needed to reliably navigate diverse narratives, limiting users' control over the perspectives they receive and leaving substantial gaps in exposure to competing accounts even when diversity is explicitly requested. Information access also differs across query framings and languages, risking echo chambers, linguistic filter bubbles, and information disparities that weaken the common ground needed for democratic discourse. \\textsc{NarrativeBench} provides a foundation for developing information systems that give users across languages and regions agency to navigate diverse narratives.	arXiv:2611.12750v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Large language models (LLMs) increasingly mediate how people seek and consume information, including accounts that differ across languages and regions, especially on contested topics. Supporting diverse information seeking requires models to help users navigate competing narratives across languages and information streams. We define six capabilities necessary for diverse multilingual information seeking: three foundational capabilities for narrative inclusion, narrative exclusion, and missing narrative identification; and three higher-level capabilities for presenting diverse narratives, preserving narrative coverage under confirmatory queries, and maintaining information parity across languages. To guide the development of those information seeking capabilities, we introduce \\textsc{NarrativeBench}, a benchmark comprising 1,333 news articles in 23 publication languages across 23 contested topics and 460 Reddit-grounded queries. Evaluating 14 LLMs across 24 query languages, we find that current models lack the capabilities needed to reliably navigate diverse narratives, limiting users' control over the perspectives they receive and leaving substantial gaps in exposure to competing accounts even when diversity is explicitly requested. Information access also differs across query framings and languages, risking echo chambers, linguistic filter bubbles, and information disparities that weaken the common ground needed for democratic discourse. \\textsc{NarrativeBench} provides a foundation for developing information systems that give users across languages and regions agency to navigate diverse narratives.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:55.894931+00	4	\N	b93b27b2ff83e2b07b3bd0dc79e8beb39bec6d0d2494356da388b0972b6210d8	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:55.895275+00	2026-11-10 16:35:48.705238+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c43c22b5-21d7-4c31-8c32-531ea50919b2	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10697v1	Hallucination-Guided Unlearning: Using Hallucination Traces to Reveal Overfitted Memories	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10697	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10697	Jiayu Zhang, Ziqi Zhong, Yuliang Gai, Canran Xiao	arXiv:2611.10697v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Selective unlearning is increasingly important for the safety and compliance of large language models. Harmful or memorized content is often entangled with useful knowledge and difficult to enumerate. We use the model's own failures to identify candidates for unlearning. We propose Hallucination-Guided Unlearning (HGU), which uses hallucinations as diagnostic signals. HGU localizes unsupported spans via evidence and internal consensus, traces a sparse set of high-responsibility MLP neurons, and edits them with compact gates under a utility-preserving KL constraint. On TOFU, HGU achieves the best MU-FE trade-off, with Avg scores of 0.7221 (forget05) and 0.7699 (forget10), and the highest SepS H-Avg (0.4382/0.4715) under mixed forget/retain prompts. On the WMDP auxiliary task, HGU yields the strongest forgetting (e.g., 14.2 on Economics) while improving Retain (52.7) and MMLU (57.2). These results suggest that tracing hallucinations enables targeted, post-hoc unlearning that better balances forgetting efficacy and general utility without full retraining.	arXiv:2611.10697v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Selective unlearning is increasingly important for the safety and compliance of large language models. Harmful or memorized content is often entangled with useful knowledge and difficult to enumerate. We use the model's own failures to identify candidates for unlearning. We propose Hallucination-Guided Unlearning (HGU), which uses hallucinations as diagnostic signals. HGU localizes unsupported spans via evidence and internal consensus, traces a sparse set of high-responsibility MLP neurons, and edits them with compact gates under a utility-preserving KL constraint. On TOFU, HGU achieves the best MU-FE trade-off, with Avg scores of 0.7221 (forget05) and 0.7699 (forget10), and the highest SepS H-Avg (0.4382/0.4715) under mixed forget/retain prompts. On the WMDP auxiliary task, HGU yields the strongest forgetting (e.g., 14.2 on Economics) while improving Retain (52.7) and MMLU (57.2). These results suggest that tracing hallucinations enables targeted, post-hoc unlearning that better balances forgetting efficacy and general utility without full retraining.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:54.853751+00	4	\N	a0670187191fc28eb2bdc08630198036500a0e2aefabfab7688303eb4be5273c	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:54.854068+00	2026-11-10 16:35:52.16129+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
