03c38695-ba19-4b23-bd5e-26fad9f3d288	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.01301v2	CuriousTogether: Implications of Curiosity and Cultural Grounding in Multi-Agent LLM Interactions	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01301	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01301	Angana Borah, Zoha Hayat Bhatti, Zhijing Jin, Rada Mihalcea	arXiv:2611.01301v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in multi-agent settings, exhibiting emergent collective behaviors, yet the role of curiosity as a driver of such dynamics remains understudied. We introduce CuriousTogether, a framework evaluating curiosity-driven multi-agent LLM interactions across three complementary dimensions: novelty-seeking, epistemic and social curiosity. Across eight tasks spanning reasoning, creative generation, and social bias mitigation, curiosity-guided multi-agent settings yield improvements over non-curious ones on seven out of eight tasks, with gains of up to 20.6% in story generation (semantic diversity) and a 14.9 pp reduction in stereotype rates. Furthermore, country-conditioned cultural grounding show benefits for story generation and social tasks, but can hurt precision-oriented reasoning. Finally, we show that inference-time multi-agent curiosity-based approach offers favorable quality-token trade-offs, compared with single-agent baselines. Together, these findings support curiosity-guided interaction as a practical mechanism for improving multi-agent collaboration, with downstream benefits for reasoning, creative, and societal tasks.	arXiv:2611.01301v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in multi-agent settings, exhibiting emergent collective behaviors, yet the role of curiosity as a driver of such dynamics remains understudied. We introduce CuriousTogether, a framework evaluating curiosity-driven multi-agent LLM interactions across three complementary dimensions: novelty-seeking, epistemic and social curiosity. Across eight tasks spanning reasoning, creative generation, and social bias mitigation, curiosity-guided multi-agent settings yield improvements over non-curious ones on seven out of eight tasks, with gains of up to 20.6% in story generation (semantic diversity) and a 14.9 pp reduction in stereotype rates. Furthermore, country-conditioned cultural grounding show benefits for story generation and social tasks, but can hurt precision-oriented reasoning. Finally, we show that inference-time multi-agent curiosity-based approach offers favorable quality-token trade-offs, compared with single-agent baselines. Together, these findings support curiosity-guided interaction as a practical mechanism for improving multi-agent collaboration, with downstream benefits for reasoning, creative, and societal tasks.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.214131+00	3	\N	478cee1914c1d029c134ce80dfdec44757e01418e6070c18807c21a6bf1e6b0c	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.21451+00	2026-11-10 15:43:30.619038+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
