6eae28b9-5c81-450b-b376-54c148ef7362	f8782f3e-cbda-4809-b208-87367f54d3c8	\N	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/doordash-says-outside-ai-agents-have-only-led-to-hundreds-of-orders-so-far/	Basically no one is using AI agents for their DoorDash orders	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/doordash-says-outside-ai-agents-have-only-led-to-hundreds-of-orders-so-far/	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/doordash-says-outside-ai-agents-have-only-led-to-hundreds-of-orders-so-far/	Kyle Orland	But it's still early days for the relatively new e-commerce use case.	As AI tools and agents have continued their march across all aspects of digital life, many companies are trying to use LLMs to improve the experience of purchasing goods and services on the Internet. Thus far, though, a vanishingly small number of people seem to be taking advantage of those capabilities, at least when it comes to using the popular food delivery app DoorDash with an outside AI agent. In a third-quarter earnings call Thursday , DoorDash co-founder and CEO Tony Xu responded to an analyst question by noting that "AI agents are generating several hundred orders [on DoorDash] across the time that they've been up and running." In a marked understatement, Xu called this a "very low use case" for a company that handled just shy of 1 billion orders total in the third quarter of 2026 alone. Speaking generally, Xu said he sees the potential for AI agents to be genuinely helpful for "long consideration purchases" and major decisions where agents would be "doing work on your behalf proactively in the background." However, Xu added, "when it comes to a task that's as simple as ordering food, we tend to see that come directly to DoorDash." Read full article Comments	news	en	2026-10-30 19:30:52+00	2026-11-09 17:12:50.363928+00	2	\N	9e28a72ecdd2ab58e6964498aa6a9c8a0b537d2da983652ecc299fba48bdc86d	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:50.364319+00	2026-11-10 14:41:28.583886+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
