6de5d6a8-fb7e-405f-bf39-215d6611f641	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.05557v2	Support Vector Machines Directly Minimise KL Divergence	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05557	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05557	Ivan Silajev	arXiv:2611.05557v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Support vector machines (SVMs) are fundamentally grounded in geometric margin maximisation and currently lack a mainstream probabilistic interpretation. This keeps them theoretically isolated from probabilistic machine learning models that are naturally comparable and combinable. In this paper, we bridge this gap by proving that the dual objectives of both support vector classification and support vector regression are exact, constrained Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence minimisation tasks between two Gaussian measures. We further show that this same divergence formulation recovers the exact objective function for kernel ridge regression when domain constraints are removed and $L^2$ regularisation is applied. By demonstrating that SVM duals directly minimise relative entropy, our results provide a unified information-theoretic model for standard kernel machines and lay the foundation for unifying broader kernel-based algorithms under probability theory.	arXiv:2611.05557v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Support vector machines (SVMs) are fundamentally grounded in geometric margin maximisation and currently lack a mainstream probabilistic interpretation. This keeps them theoretically isolated from probabilistic machine learning models that are naturally comparable and combinable. In this paper, we bridge this gap by proving that the dual objectives of both support vector classification and support vector regression are exact, constrained Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence minimisation tasks between two Gaussian measures. We further show that this same divergence formulation recovers the exact objective function for kernel ridge regression when domain constraints are removed and $L^2$ regularisation is applied. By demonstrating that SVM duals directly minimise relative entropy, our results provide a unified information-theoretic model for standard kernel machines and lay the foundation for unifying broader kernel-based algorithms under probability theory.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.306567+00	3	\N	bb270db316b31dd104e2550b476a1b92263b1165a440e7ffcd40bf5bd187af06	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.307002+00	2026-11-10 15:29:58.824089+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b5dca995-c255-4d64-be0e-c773e8c38e30	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08767v1	MEDALLION: Manifold Embedding Distillation and Attributions for Low-dimensional Latent InterpretatiON	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08767	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08767	Vicky Jung, Claire M. He, Irene Chang, Genevera I. Allen	arXiv:2611.08767v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Dimensionality reduction is widely used to visualize high-dimensional data and guide scientific discovery, yet interpreting which original features contribute to low-dimensional representations remains challenging, particularly for black-box methods such as t-SNE and UMAP. We introduce \\textbf{MEDALLION} (Manifold Embedding Distillation and Attributions for Low-dimensional Latent InterpretatiON), a novel framework for interpreting low-dimensional embeddings through knowledge distillation and neural feature attribution. MEDALLION distills any given embedding with a neural encoder which we explain via a broad range of existing neural feature attribution methods. Through experiments on MNIST and applications in single-cell transcriptomics, we show MEDALLION provides informative feature attributions that cast additional insights into the underlying embedding and compare its performance with existing interpretability approaches.	arXiv:2611.08767v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Dimensionality reduction is widely used to visualize high-dimensional data and guide scientific discovery, yet interpreting which original features contribute to low-dimensional representations remains challenging, particularly for black-box methods such as t-SNE and UMAP. We introduce \\textbf{MEDALLION} (Manifold Embedding Distillation and Attributions for Low-dimensional Latent InterpretatiON), a novel framework for interpreting low-dimensional embeddings through knowledge distillation and neural feature attribution. MEDALLION distills any given embedding with a neural encoder which we explain via a broad range of existing neural feature attribution methods. Through experiments on MNIST and applications in single-cell transcriptomics, we show MEDALLION provides informative feature attributions that cast additional insights into the underlying embedding and compare its performance with existing interpretability approaches.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.324004+00	3	\N	c9b59186a50bf17b93dded399c61e8c349d957d8ac554431886ba4a51ccbefa8	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.32458+00	2026-11-10 14:47:28.615936+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
88c7b679-0c9d-43fc-b257-e05bffeba10f	d58a9c86-f9be-4f8b-bd34-60f063fd344d	\N	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/4/datasette-apps/	datasette-apps 0.3a0	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/4/datasette-apps/	https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/4/datasette-apps/	\N	Release: datasette-apps 0.3a0 Datasette Apps adds support for hosting and editing custom HTML applications inside of Datasette. These can be created by hand, but it's easier to create them using LLMs via Datasette Agent . This release improves that integration: Apps can now be tagged in Datasette Agent chat messages by typing # , which autocompletes from the apps the user has permission to view. New app_add_stored_queries() and app_remove_stored_queries() tools let the agent manage which stored queries an app is allowed to call. Granting a write query requires approval from the user first. app_create() now checks that each requested stored query exists and that the user can view it, and asks the user to approve any write queries before the app is created. The "View app" buttons shown by the agent tools, and the links on # app references, now open the app full screen in the Datasette Agent panel. Tags: datasette , datasette-agent , datasette-apps	Release: datasette-apps 0.3a0 Datasette Apps adds support for hosting and editing custom HTML applications inside of Datasette. These can be created by hand, but it's easier to create them using LLMs via Datasette Agent . This release improves that integration: Apps can now be tagged in Datasette Agent chat messages by typing # , which autocompletes from the apps the user has permission to view. New app_add_stored_queries() and app_remove_stored_queries() tools let the agent manage which stored queries an app is allowed to call. Granting a write query requires approval from the user first. app_create() now checks that each requested stored query exists and that the user can view it, and asks the user to approve any write queries before the app is created. The "View app" buttons shown by the agent tools, and the links on # app references, now open the app full screen in the Datasette Agent panel. Tags: datasette , datasette-agent , datasette-apps	release	en	2026-11-04 00:29:36+00	2026-11-09 17:12:46.824485+00	3	\N	b70011612921d4e2d9713b1527cbb7c26cda7fb394e43c164f2ef1432d9a6b4f	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:46.824992+00	2026-11-10 14:43:19.423927+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b87af4f5-7056-4e4a-9df9-ce73c9d6fd27	eff33c27-9fc1-4abb-a69e-99f9ef74c427	\N	https://www.technologyreview.com/?p=1147596	Securing the agentic enterprise with data lineage	https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/11/04/1147596/securing-the-agentic-enterprise-with-data-lineage/	https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/11/04/1147596/securing-the-agentic-enterprise-with-data-lineage/	MIT Technology Review Insights	A sales representative asks an AI agent for customer pricing, and the agent pulls data from multiple sources: restricted pricing systems, internal communications, and archived quotes. The output looks accurate to the agent, so a second agent uses the data to draft the proposal for the sales rep. No human checks either handoff. When it’s…	A sales representative asks an AI agent for customer pricing, and the agent pulls data from multiple sources: restricted pricing systems, internal communications, and archived quotes. The output looks accurate to the agent, so a second agent uses the data to draft the proposal for the sales rep. No human checks either handoff. When it’s discovered that the proposal includes unapproved, confidential, and sensitive information, no one can trace where the problematic data entered the workflow or how it spread across systems, files, and agents. DOWNLOAD THE REPORT This scenario illustrates why AI governance needs to evolve, rapidly and dynamically. As more enterprises begin to use agentic AI in daily workflows, they’re realizing that they must manage more than static files; they must manage moving and fragmented data that is continuously ingested, copied, summarized, transformed, reused, and redistributed in new forms. In short, AI security isn’t a tool problem; it’s a data visibility problem. Consequently, traditional security approaches built solely around applications, systems, networks, or endpoints are no longer sufficient. Securing agentic AI at scale requires understanding how data moves. Teams, therefore, need to establish data lineage in order to effectively manage governance, accountability, and trust across human, agentic, and hybrid workflows. Download the report This content was produced by Insights, MIT Technology Review’s custom content arm, not its editorial staff. It was researched and written by humans, with any AI tools that may have been used limited to production processes under human oversight.	news	en	2026-11-04 13:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.531435+00	4	\N	903225873c4405e2079e691fc7911022fd5e9f4143bd9a53493317e2dd615efc	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.532613+00	2026-11-10 14:43:26.771521+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f46d082a-15af-419c-b9e0-6456d5c61479	eff33c27-9fc1-4abb-a69e-99f9ef74c427	\N	https://www.technologyreview.com/?p=1148043	Texas was the new epicenter of the AI data center buildout. That might be kaput.	https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/11/04/1148043/texas-was-the-new-epicenter-of-the-ai-data-center-buildout-that-might-be-kaput/	https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/11/04/1148043/texas-was-the-new-epicenter-of-the-ai-data-center-buildout-that-might-be-kaput/	James O'Donnell	Texas is the fastest-growing data center market in the US and ground zero for Big Tech’s largest AI buildouts. But the blue wave that swept the Lone Star State may jeopardize that position. Democrat James Talarico won the US Senate seat against Ken Paxton. Gina Hinojosa won the race for governor against the Republican incumbent,…	Texas is the fastest-growing data center market in the US and ground zero for Big Tech’s largest AI buildouts. But the blue wave that swept the Lone Star State may jeopardize that position. Democrat James Talarico won the US Senate seat against Ken Paxton. Gina Hinojosa won the race for governor against the Republican incumbent, Greg Abbott. At the state level, Democrats will take control of the Texas House for the first time in 24 years. Talarico and Hinojosa made data centers a central issue, discussing them constantly on the campaign trail. Even before the polls closed, insiders in the data center industry noticed that the times were changing. At the industry’s DCD conference in Ashburn, Virginia, on Tuesday, panelists lamented that Texas had been the easiest place to build for years—until September. That’s when Abbott, perhaps seeing the increasingly bipartisan distaste for these facilities , began changing course himself. Developers said the governor’s office had stopped responding to their emails. On September 21, Abbott directed a key state regulator to halt data center permits. Many panelists at the conference were expecting this to mean that existing projects might be slowed and new ones could be halted altogether. The governor-elect indeed seems eager to do that. Hinojosa supported tax breaks for data centers in 2023 as a way to boost construction jobs, but during her campaign she began pushing for a full-fledged moratorium. That would be a problem for the hyperscalers of Big Tech, which have ambitious plans for AI training facilities in the state. The scale of projects already underway in Texas is massive. The Stargate Frontier data center in Shackelford County is projected to serve 1,820 megawatts—about as much as the nation’s largest data center, SpaceX’s Colossus 2 in Tennessee, and more than twice as much as the largest data center currently operating in Texas. Nexus Hubbard, also under construction and intended mostly to serve Anthropic, has a planned size of 6,400 megawatts and is the second-largest data center being built in the US, behind only Meta’s Hyperion project in Louisiana. The potential for Democrats to order delays to these projects or moratoriums on new ones adds to growing anxiety that the AI buildout could stall. Banks and lenders are already getting pickier about the projects they back. And given the extent to which the data center buildout is underpinning growth in the economy right now, those forces combined could have ripple effects. Voters around the country, though, were unambiguous in the midterms, sending a similar message on everything from state ballot questions to national offices. Voters supported efforts to protect ratepayers from increases in their electricity bills caused by data centers, and they favored some larger bans on new data centers altogether. If we needed any more convincing that Big Tech has fumbled its effort to sell voters on the idea that AI-powered abundance will make health care better, goods cheaper, and scientific breakthroughs more common, this was it. A key question is whether the Texas counties with the largest data center presence were part of the reason these Democrats got elected, or if the anti-data-center vote came from elsewhere. It’s complicated: The counties where much of the data center buildout is happening are around Dallas. Dallas County and others closest to it predictably went blue, but nearby Ellis County, which leads in permitting requests for data centers, went for Greg Abbott and Ken Paxton. Cameron County, near the state’s southern border, is a hot spot for data centers and voted blue in the midterms, which was a shift. But Mitchell County and Scurry County, where a bunch of projects in west Texas are located, went red. This is a big deal, because no single office in the state decides whether a project goes ahead or is halted. Decisions often reflect an interplay between city governments, county officials, and state offices. Local officials who have some say over data center development may resist sweeping restrictions from the state. And Democrats pushing for a moratorium might also face pushback from the construction unions that supported some of their campaigns. So while the blue wave spells a death knell for Texas data centers in theory, election winners may find that following through on their opposition once in office is much more complicated than campaigning on it.	news	en	2026-11-04 18:50:55+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.527776+00	3	\N	b6e2d388ab37d01069b03146c9926340cc89dfd4ced462cea84478f759d1b99d	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.528292+00	2026-11-10 14:43:37.193096+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e5271c13-2d98-49dc-b77d-a3dca9d88f99	eb530fbb-88dd-42d2-a6be-0b352a46d09a	\N	https://the-decoder.com/?p=42602	The brain-as-computer metaphor shaped modern AI but may be misleading the consciousness debate	https://the-decoder.com/the-brain-as-computer-metaphor-shaped-modern-ai-but-may-be-misleading-the-consciousness-debate/	https://the-decoder.com/the-brain-as-computer-metaphor-shaped-modern-ai-but-may-be-misleading-the-consciousness-debate/	Manuel Uth	Consciousness researchers Anil Seth, Ned Block, and Giulio Tononi argue in a Nature commentary that today's AI systems aren't conscious and won't become so on silicon hardware. They consider the assumption that consciousness depends on computation alone "fatally flawed" and warn against granting rights to AI systems. The article The brain-as-computer metaphor shaped modern AI but may be misleading the consciousness debate appeared first on The Decoder .	Consciousness researchers Anil Seth, Ned Block, and Giulio Tononi argue in a Nature commentary that today's AI systems aren't conscious and won't become so on silicon hardware. They consider the assumption that consciousness depends on computation alone "fatally flawed" and warn against granting rights to AI systems. The article The brain-as-computer metaphor shaped modern AI but may be misleading the consciousness debate appeared first on The Decoder .	opinion	en	2026-11-08 13:49:21+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.208788+00	3	\N	5275f0d590199f05e0db0cafc6276face6e86779e82ef0aa9bc2fd08e91cac95	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.209243+00	2026-11-10 14:45:32.160102+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1c0408e8-5ab7-46bf-9703-cb79dab18d96	eb530fbb-88dd-42d2-a6be-0b352a46d09a	\N	https://the-decoder.com/?p=42607	Andrej Karpathy built a curated video platform in a weekend using Claude and 20 years of Hacker News	https://the-decoder.com/andrej-karpathy-built-a-curated-video-platform-in-a-weekend-using-claude-and-20-years-of-hacker-news/	https://the-decoder.com/andrej-karpathy-built-a-curated-video-platform-in-a-weekend-using-claude-and-20-years-of-hacker-news/	Matthias Bastian	OpenAI co-founder Andrej Karpathy used Claude's Opus 5.5 to filter 43 million Hacker News comments into 6,454 curated videos. The result beats YouTube's own recommendations, he says. The article Andrej Karpathy built a curated video platform in a weekend using Claude and 20 years of Hacker News appeared first on The Decoder .	OpenAI co-founder Andrej Karpathy used Claude's Opus 5.5 to filter 43 million Hacker News comments into 6,454 curated videos. The result beats YouTube's own recommendations, he says. The article Andrej Karpathy built a curated video platform in a weekend using Claude and 20 years of Hacker News appeared first on The Decoder .	news	en	2026-11-08 14:23:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.205378+00	3	\N	ec27c2ef939efc730fb7d6e50d147b4ddb87328f39e6bfce3340be59974c3a15	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.206288+00	2026-11-10 14:45:35.282107+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
abcdb884-2b4a-48fc-b8e9-1b7d40342174	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08737v1	On Differentially Private Mechanisms for Linear Regression	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08737	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08737	Bardiya Aryanfard, Monika Henzinger, Farhood Rostamkhani	arXiv:2611.08737v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We study the classic least-squares linear regression problem under differential privacy (DP) in the batch and the continual observation setting. In the latter setting, the mechanism receives a stream of $n$ data points $(x_i,y_i)$ ($x_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$ and $y_i\\in\\mathbb{R}$) and must output an estimate of the optimal parameters at each time step $i$. Although differentially private linear regression has been extensively studied in the batch setting, the problem remains underexplored for the continual setting, with prior work being restricted to constrained linear regression and update time polynomial in $n$.\n  In this work, we introduce ContinualAdaSSP, the first mechanism for unconstrained linear regression under continual differential privacy. Our mechanism's suboptimality gap is proportional to $\\sqrt{d}\\operatorname{polylog}(n)$. It requires $O(d^2\\log{n})$ space and $O(d^2\\log{n}+d^{\\omega})$ update time, where $d$ is the dimension of the input space.\n  We also study fundamental limits of differentially private linear regression by showing the first lower bounds for the box-constrained least-squares linear regression problem. Our lower bounds are proportional to $\\min(n,\\frac{\\sqrt{d}}{\\log^{1.5}d})$ for $(1,O(\\frac{1}{n}))$-DP, and $\\min(n,\\frac{d}{\\epsilon})$ for $\\epsilon$-DP, and are derived using a reduction from a fingerprinting code problem from [Peter et al., 2024], providing new insights into the inherent hardness of this canonical task.\n  Our experimental evaluation on real-world datasets shows that the accuracy of our continual observation mechanism clearly outperforms any other known differentially private mechanism for this problem such as rerunning the batch algorithm at each time step.	arXiv:2611.08737v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We study the classic least-squares linear regression problem under differential privacy (DP) in the batch and the continual observation setting. In the latter setting, the mechanism receives a stream of $n$ data points $(x_i,y_i)$ ($x_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$ and $y_i\\in\\mathbb{R}$) and must output an estimate of the optimal parameters at each time step $i$. Although differentially private linear regression has been extensively studied in the batch setting, the problem remains underexplored for the continual setting, with prior work being restricted to constrained linear regression and update time polynomial in $n$.\n  In this work, we introduce ContinualAdaSSP, the first mechanism for unconstrained linear regression under continual differential privacy. Our mechanism's suboptimality gap is proportional to $\\sqrt{d}\\operatorname{polylog}(n)$. It requires $O(d^2\\log{n})$ space and $O(d^2\\log{n}+d^{\\omega})$ update time, where $d$ is the dimension of the input space.\n  We also study fundamental limits of differentially private linear regression by showing the first lower bounds for the box-constrained least-squares linear regression problem. Our lower bounds are proportional to $\\min(n,\\frac{\\sqrt{d}}{\\log^{1.5}d})$ for $(1,O(\\frac{1}{n}))$-DP, and $\\min(n,\\frac{d}{\\epsilon})$ for $\\epsilon$-DP, and are derived using a reduction from a fingerprinting code problem from [Peter et al., 2024], providing new insights into the inherent hardness of this canonical task.\n  Our experimental evaluation on real-world datasets shows that the accuracy of our continual observation mechanism clearly outperforms any other known differentially private mechanism for this problem such as rerunning the batch algorithm at each time step.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.318852+00	4	\N	708b491a7e48d1d505334ed5bf99e1