b3322807-9703-4ed6-aea3-3506fe4bc0e4	f8782f3e-cbda-4809-b208-87367f54d3c8	\N	https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/big-tech-claims-voters-ai-backlash-is-anti-trump-not-anti-tech/	Big Tech claims voters’ AI backlash is “anti-Trump” not “anti-tech”	https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/big-tech-claims-voters-ai-backlash-is-anti-trump-not-anti-tech/	https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/big-tech-claims-voters-ai-backlash-is-anti-trump-not-anti-tech/	Ashley Belanger	Tech industry in denial as US overwhelmingly votes to restrict AI, data centers.	Donald Trump’s big, rapid American AI buildout now faces a major slowdown as voters in yesterday’s elections overwhelmingly supported candidates and measures that favored increasing restrictions on AI and data centers nationwide. One resource from a polling firm, called Redbud Consulting, tracked data center initiatives on the ballot and reported that out of 44 measures across 10 states seeking to restrict data centers, 38 passed so far. Those measures included dozens of moratoriums and bans. Analysts suggested those won’t impact data centers already under construction or approved through 2027, instead largely impacting future projects planned for 2028. Only one measure passed allowing a data center to proceed. But even that small win represented pushback on the AI industry—requiring data center developers in a North Dakota city to pay for independent reviews and hold public hearings when expanding or proposing projects. In addition to increasing regulations on data centers, voters also backed anti-AI candidates, electing officials vowing crackdowns in key states building out the most data center capacity in the US. Read full article Comments	news	en	2026-11-04 20:55:50+00	2026-11-09 17:12:50.341549+00	4	\N	c76938876429b834de9c0b1af7b619210d17497964c3224126b16813c97b8898	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:50.341975+00	2026-11-10 14:43:39.769673+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
75613831-6036-4ae7-8b2b-8ff3c39bc06c	f8782f3e-cbda-4809-b208-87367f54d3c8	\N	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/agility-robotics-nasdaq-debut-invites-scrutiny-of-humanoid-robot-market/	Agility Robotics' Nasdaq debut invites scrutiny of humanoid robot market	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/agility-robotics-nasdaq-debut-invites-scrutiny-of-humanoid-robot-market/	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/agility-robotics-nasdaq-debut-invites-scrutiny-of-humanoid-robot-market/	Jeremy Hsu	US leader in deploying humanoid robots goes public on the Nasdaq stock exchange.	Humanoid robots remain unproven at scale and have not yet achieved widespread commercialization, compared with industrial robots that have decades of proven track records in warehouses and factories. That frank acknowledgment is among the risks outlined in a securities filing by Agility Robotics—the first dedicated developer of humanoid robots to become a publicly traded company in the United States. The robotics company, headquartered in Salem, Oregon, previously became the first to achieve full-time commercial deployment of humanoid robots at a customer warehouse in 2024. Now, Agility’s public listing on the Nasdaq stock exchange as of November 5 has come with financial disclosures detailing how the company plans to build a business around renting out or selling humanoid robots to perform manual labor. “When we are doing robots as a service, it is much lower risk for customers to use it because they are getting a return on investment on day one,” Jonathan Hurst , co-founder and chief robot officer at Agility Robotics, told Ars. "Because they're paying a rate that is comparable to the human labor rate, and that makes the economics pretty straightforward.” Read full article Comments	news	en	2026-11-05 12:00:41+00	2026-11-09 17:12:50.338914+00	3	\N	e0529d189d025d1ea3d1c81f4ded9973aa5c500083984af8754df09e34ebaa83	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:50.339356+00	2026-11-10 14:43:55.462566+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
