9f76bf32-5909-4378-bbe5-189938dfed0d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08512v1	Interpretable Multifidelity Learning with Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks for Spatiotemporal Nuclear Reactor Flow Modeling	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08512	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08512	Meredith Eaheart, Majdi I. Radaideh	arXiv:2611.08512v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: High-fidelity computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations of nuclear reactor systems are computationally expensive, motivating machine learning methods that reduce cost while preserving accuracy. Multifidelity surrogate modeling addresses this by combining inexpensive low-fidelity data with limited high-fidelity data. This work investigates whether the high-fidelity data required to predict the spatiotemporal mass flow rate across all 193 fuel assemblies of a pressurized water reactor (PWR) core can be reduced, comparing black-box multifidelity feedforward neural networks (MFNNs) with interpretable multifidelity Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (MFKANs). Cross-fidelity correlation across the core is weak, making this a challenging multifidelity problem, and neither architecture predicts the transient particularly well in absolute terms. MFKAN nonetheless performs comparably to MFNN for one-time-step-forward prediction while also providing closed-form symbolic equations unavailable with MFNN. To address the scarcity of CFD transients, with only one available per fidelity level, TabDDPM-generated synthetic data was used to augment cross-fidelity forecasting. KAN and FNN achieve comparable self-evaluation accuracy (Pearson correlation of roughly 0.73--0.81), but both perform poorly at true cross-fidelity forecasting (correlations of 0.03--0.06). Assembly-level analysis shows that each assembly's forecast correlation tracks its real cross-fidelity correlation for both architectures ($r=0.840$--$0.896$ for KAN, $r=0.804$--$0.903$ for FNN), confirming that forecasting accuracy is fundamentally limited by how correlated the fidelity levels already are. Overall, KAN-based multifidelity modeling achieves accuracy consistently comparable to MFNN across all settings, while additionally providing symbolic equations that match or exceed the spline representation's accuracy.	arXiv:2611.08512v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: High-fidelity computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations of nuclear reactor systems are computationally expensive, motivating machine learning methods that reduce cost while preserving accuracy. Multifidelity surrogate modeling addresses this by combining inexpensive low-fidelity data with limited high-fidelity data. This work investigates whether the high-fidelity data required to predict the spatiotemporal mass flow rate across all 193 fuel assemblies of a pressurized water reactor (PWR) core can be reduced, comparing black-box multifidelity feedforward neural networks (MFNNs) with interpretable multifidelity Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (MFKANs). Cross-fidelity correlation across the core is weak, making this a challenging multifidelity problem, and neither architecture predicts the transient particularly well in absolute terms. MFKAN nonetheless performs comparably to MFNN for one-time-step-forward prediction while also providing closed-form symbolic equations unavailable with MFNN. To address the scarcity of CFD transients, with only one available per fidelity level, TabDDPM-generated synthetic data was used to augment cross-fidelity forecasting. KAN and FNN achieve comparable self-evaluation accuracy (Pearson correlation of roughly 0.73--0.81), but both perform poorly at true cross-fidelity forecasting (correlations of 0.03--0.06). Assembly-level analysis shows that each assembly's forecast correlation tracks its real cross-fidelity correlation for both architectures ($r=0.840$--$0.896$ for KAN, $r=0.804$--$0.903$ for FNN), confirming that forecasting accuracy is fundamentally limited by how correlated the fidelity levels already are. Overall, KAN-based multifidelity modeling achieves accuracy consistently comparable to MFNN across all settings, while additionally providing symbolic equations that match or exceed the spline representation's accuracy.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.279221+00	3	\N	99925bdc8552c5e9fdafd373c581899fb3248ea13387e63f6e5a8ffd00c60758	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.279668+00	2026-11-10 14:46:33.576771+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
725c0f31-b5d0-4b8a-ba5d-3b957ee5bb7e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08551v1	How Small Can We Go? Calibrating Activation-Cache Compression for SAE Training	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08551	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08551	Abraham Yeung	arXiv:2611.08551v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: To train a sparse autoencoder (SAE), you need a massive cache of model activations. We look into the effects that compression has on an SAE in two ways: we keep the original fp32-trained SAE and pass the compressed cache into the same SAE to see how many feature activations stay the same (the frozen-SAE test), and we retrain an SAE on the compressed cache and see how many of the original features still exist (the retraining test). We found that in the retraining test, there is no clear scale for retained features. On a GPT-2 cache, rounding everything to fp16 keeps 60.6% of features, but changing one value in a million by the smallest step fp32 allows (a change eight orders of magnitude smaller) keeps only 70.5%. Meanwhile, changing the random seed keeps 23.0%. For the frozen-SAE test, the frozen SAE's correlation with the original increases in order of how precise the codecs are, but plain $L_2$ distance gives nearly the same ordering. We also see the same frozen reading correspond to different percentages retained at different sites. This leaves the question of the right measurement. We build one by calibrating: retraining on identical bytes sets the ceiling, and a reseed sets the floor. We then measure the effect of each compressed codec against its own site's floor. With TopK SAEs on GPT-2 small and Pythia-1.4B at research-scale budgets, na\\"ive per-token int4 loses more features than a reseed everywhere we tested, as do PCA and random projection on GPT-2. Standardizing each channel before quantizing brings int4 back above the floor in terms of retained features, at the same raw file size. At our smallest budget, a bf16 forward pass, which some pipelines use to save memory, loses slightly more features than rotated 8-bit storage. Calibrating a site costs four training runs and two evaluation passes.	arXiv:2611.08551v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: To train a sparse autoencoder (SAE), you need a massive cache of model activations. We look into the effects that compression has on an SAE in two ways: we keep the original fp32-trained SAE and pass the compressed cache into the same SAE to see how many feature activations stay the same (the frozen-SAE test), and we retrain an SAE on the compressed cache and see how many of the original features still exist (the retraining test). We found that in the retraining test, there is no clear scale for retained features. On a GPT-2 cache, rounding everything to fp16 keeps 60.6% of features, but changing one value in a million by the smallest step fp32 allows (a change eight orders of magnitude smaller) keeps only 70.5%. Meanwhile, changing the random seed keeps 23.0%. For the frozen-SAE test, the frozen SAE's correlation with the original increases in order of how precise the codecs are, but plain $L_2$ distance gives nearly the same ordering. We also see the same frozen reading correspond to different percentages retained at different sites. This leaves the question of the right measurement. We build one by calibrating: retraining on identical bytes sets the ceiling, and a reseed sets the floor. We then measure the effect of each compressed codec against its own site's floor. With TopK SAEs on GPT-2 small and Pythia-1.4B at research-scale budgets, na\\"ive per-token int4 loses more features than a reseed everywhere we tested, as do PCA and random projection on GPT-2. Standardizing each channel before quantizing brings int4 back above the floor in terms of retained features, at the same raw file size. At our smallest budget, a bf16 forward pass, which some pipelines use to save memory, loses slightly more features than rotated 8-bit storage. Calibrating a site costs four training runs and two evaluation passes.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.287985+00	3	\N	4f007236e5f1323d6b5af30eea00ad3cc1271ddba929f84b1144c646cac2ab39	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.288441+00	2026-11-10 14:46:43.158212+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
69e062cf-0a14-4d39-9185-508dbb992c17	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.20103v5	Spectral Alignment in Forward-Backward Representations via Temporal Abstraction	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.20103	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.20103	Seyed Mahdi B. Azad, Jasper Hoffmann, Iman Nematollahi, Hao Zhu, Abhinav Valada, Joschka Boedecker	arXiv:2603.20103v5 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Forward-backward (FB) representations provide a powerful framework for learning the successor representation (SR) in continuous spaces by enforcing a low-rank factorization. However, a fundamental spectral mismatch often exists between the high-rank transition dynamics of continuous environments and the low-rank bottleneck of the FB architecture, making accurate low-rank representation learning difficult. In this work, we analyze temporal abstraction as a mechanism to mitigate this mismatch. By characterizing the spectral properties of the transition operator, we show that temporal abstraction acts analogously to a low-pass filter that suppresses high-frequency spectral components. This suppression reduces the effective rank of the induced SR while preserving a formal bound on the resulting value function error. Empirically, we show that this alignment is a key factor for stable FB learning, particularly at high discount factors where bootstrapping becomes error-prone. Our results identify temporal abstraction as a principled mechanism for shaping the spectral structure of the underlying MDP and enabling effective long-horizon representations in continuous control.	arXiv:2603.20103v5 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Forward-backward (FB) representations provide a powerful framework for learning the successor representation (SR) in continuous spaces by enforcing a low-rank factorization. However, a fundamental spectral mismatch often exists between the high-rank transition dynamics of continuous environments and the low-rank bottleneck of the FB architecture, making accurate low-rank representation learning difficult. In this work, we analyze temporal abstraction as a mechanism to mitigate this mismatch. By characterizing the spectral properties of the transition operator, we show that temporal abstraction acts analogously to a low-pass filter that suppresses high-frequency spectral components. This suppression reduces the effective rank of the induced SR while preserving a formal bound on the resulting value function error. Empirically, we show that this alignment is a key factor for stable FB learning, particularly at high discount factors where bootstrapping becomes error-prone. Our results identify temporal abstraction as a principled mechanism for shaping the spectral structure of the underlying MDP and enabling effective long-horizon representations in continuous control.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.058493+00	3	\N	2e2164335f9dbd5c19626130c78bcae02b86e4f7c5cfc29c7bd3799850e8b1fa	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.058894+00	2026-11-10 15:25:47.506219+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ac225a9a-ada8-4f41-bb3b-e9dacf3e5150	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/the-eternal-complement	The eternal complement	https://openai.com/index/the-eternal-complement	https://openai.com/index/the-eternal-complement	\N	Advanced AI may matter most for the routine work behind breakthrough ideas. Explore why execution could shape the next economy and the pace of progress.	Advanced AI may matter most for the routine work behind breakthrough ideas. Explore why execution could shape the next economy and the pace of progress.	opinion	en	2026-10-01 17:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.108009+00	3	\N	827c3927c0968265f53ae99a53ed78d202905ba78491ffd6d5b77e71b5e4be86	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.108368+00	2026-11-10 14:37:01.597392+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
10a64d28-c883-4495-8e99-c63b355c587e	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/chatham-financial	Chatham scales its capital markets expertise with OpenAI	https://openai.com/index/chatham-financial	https://openai.com/index/chatham-financial	\N	Chatham Financial uses Codex and GPT-5.6 to build technology and redesign workflows, cutting trade validation from 30 minutes to under 4.	Chatham Financial uses Codex and GPT-5.6 to build technology and redesign workflows, cutting trade validation from 30 minutes to under 4.	product_update	en	2026-10-02 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.10601+00	4	\N	deae4947564c70825e67de5e8b074234e6a18d456aa7247ec0067dafb0094848	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.106375+00	2026-11-10 14:37:04.267298+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c677bcb1-282d-4c97-a203-537478b0de36	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08558v1	Minimum-Sufficient Knowledge and Computation for Wind Energy Predictive Analysis	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08558	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08558	Purbak Sengupta, Aparna S. varde, Emil J{\\o}rgensen Njor	arXiv:2611.08558v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Resource-constrained cyber-physical decision systems often apply fixed sensing, knowledge, and computational pipelines despite state-dependent information needs. We introduce Minimum-Sufficient Knowledge and Computation (MSKC), a resource-aware computing principle: select the least-costly route meeting decision-risk and deployment constraints, escalating only when cheaper routes are insufficient. As proof of concept, we isolate adaptive knowledge routing, the decision underlying Knowledge-Guided Machine Learning (KGML), which conventionally injects external knowledge unconditionally rather than only where needed. We instantiate this for wind ramp-event predictive analysis, an edge-oriented decision-support task, evaluating local, relational, and physics-informed routes with a state-aware gate on publicly available real data from two wind farms. We establish conditions for feasibility preservation, cost dominance, and strict expected savings over always-on routing. At a 35.9% relational-knowledge injection rate, adaptive routing is statistically indistinguishable from always-on injection on the reported metrics while reducing knowledge calls by 64%, supporting sustainable, Green-IT-aligned deployment. These results support MSKC's premise that uniformly maximal intelligence is unnecessary. This offers intelligent data analysis for big data streams: the analysis itself decides, per state, how much knowledge and computation the data warrant.	arXiv:2611.08558v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Resource-constrained cyber-physical decision systems often apply fixed sensing, knowledge, and computational pipelines despite state-dependent information needs. We introduce Minimum-Sufficient Knowledge and Computation (MSKC), a resource-aware computing principle: select the least-costly route meeting decision-risk and deployment constraints, escalating only when cheaper routes are insufficient. As proof of concept, we isolate adaptive knowledge routing, the decision underlying Knowledge-Guided Machine Learning (KGML), which conventionally injects external knowledge unconditionally rather than only where needed. We instantiate this for wind ramp-event predictive analysis, an edge-oriented decision-support task, evaluating local, relational, and physics-informed routes with a state-aware gate on publicly available real data from two wind farms. We establish conditions for feasibility preservation, cost dominance, and strict expected savings over always-on routing. At a 35.9% relational-knowledge injection rate, adaptive routing is statistically indistinguishable from always-on injection on the reported metrics while reducing knowledge calls by 64%, supporting sustainable, Green-IT-aligned deployment. These results support MSKC's premise that uniformly maximal intelligence is unnecessary. This offers intelligent data analysis for big data streams: the analysis itself decides, per state, how much knowledge and computation the data warrant.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.290688+00	3	\N	aa34df1070bcaec86857785bdea70702a4d839b71bd3c8f5053fc86fb139e11f	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.291131+00	2026-11-10 14:46:46.17259+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4297a291-7a22-4519-b30b-eefb37df9607	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.25508v2	Dyna-Style Safety Augmented Reinforcement Learning: Staying Safe in the Face of Uncertainty	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.25508	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.25508	Artur Eisele, Bernd Frauenknecht, Friedrich Solowjow, Sebastian Trimpe	arXiv:2604.25508v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Safety is a key problem in reinforcement learning, especially during exploration. While safety filters are promising to address this issue, they are ill-suited for high- dimensional systems with unknown dynamics. We propose Dyna-style Safety Augmented Reinforcement Learning (Dyna-SAuR), a novel algorithm that learns a control policy as well as a safety filter requiring minimal domain knowledge by leveraging a learned uncertainty-aware dynamics model. A novel filter learning formulation requires the filter to avoid failures and areas of high model uncertainty. As joint deployment of the control policy and filter allows to safely collect data and improve the dynamics model, certain areas grow and filter conservatism decreases over time. Dyna-SAuR reduces failures compared to established safe RL baselines by two orders of magnitude on goal-reaching CartPole and MuJoCo Walker.	arXiv:2604.25508v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Safety is a key problem in reinforcement learning, especially during exploration. While safety filters are promising to address this issue, they are ill-suited for high- dimensional systems with unknown dynamics. We propose Dyna-style Safety Augmented Reinforcement Learning (Dyna-SAuR), a novel algorithm that learns a control policy as well as a safety filter requiring minimal domain knowledge by leveraging a learned uncertainty-aware dynamics model. A novel filter learning formulation requires the filter to avoid failures and areas of high model uncertainty. As joint deployment of the control policy and filter allows to safely collect data and improve the dynamics model, certain areas grow and filter conservatism decreases over time. Dyna-SAuR reduces failures compared to established safe RL baselines by two orders of magnitude on goal-reaching CartPole and MuJoCo Walker.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.082452+00	4	\N	96116ec04087e72a13cf9b3df5dbda142988a472bb179c107ef4b424e0b3abf3	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.082922+00	2026-11-10 15:26:03.90295+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
71b763e8-33af-4b5d-a8b0-69077875396f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08569v1	Not All Misalignment Is Alike: A Decomposed Benchmark for Emergent Misalignment	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08569	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08569	Afonso Domingues, Mubashara Akhtar, Bhada Yun, Xin Chen, Lukas Fluri, Anna Hedstr\\"om	arXiv:2611.08569v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Emergent misalignment (EM), in which fine-tuning on a narrow task induces misaligned behaviour on unrelated prompts, has been demonstrated across a wide range of datasets, model families, and training setups. Yet, most studies continue to rely on the evaluation approach introduced in the original EM paper, producing rates that are sensitive to the small set of evaluation questions used, the choice of judge, and the thresholds applied. These evaluations can also conflate misalignment with capability loss and offer little insight into the specific ways misalignment manifests. Differences in datasets, models, and fine-tuning configurations further make findings difficult to compare across studies. We introduce EMERGE, a decomposed benchmark that tracks changes in eight distinct misaligned behaviours from base to fine-tuned models. It evaluates these behaviours on out-of-domain prompts against matched benign controls and tracks capability, coherence, and refusal to account for potential confounders. Applying EMERGE to 8 open-weight models and 13 fine-tuning datasets, we find that models with similar broad misalignment rates can exhibit very different behavioural profiles that a single score cannot capture. We hope EMERGE can help establish a shared and robust standard for measuring EM, making findings more comparable and helping move research from detecting the phenomenon towards understanding it.	arXiv:2611.08569v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Emergent misalignment (EM), in which fine-tuning on a narrow task induces misaligned behaviour on unrelated prompts, has been demonstrated across a wide range of datasets, model families, and training setups. Yet, most studies continue to rely on the evaluation approach introduced in the original EM paper, producing rates that are sensitive to the small set of evaluation questions used, the choice of judge, and the thresholds applied. These evaluations can also conflate misalignment with capability loss and offer little insight into the specific ways misalignment manifests. Differences in datasets, models, and fine-tuning configurations further make findings difficult to compare across studies. We introduce EMERGE, a decomposed benchmark that tracks changes in eight distinct misaligned behaviours from base to fine-tuned models. It evaluates these behaviours on out-of-domain prompts against matched benign controls and tracks capability, coherence, and refusal to account for potential confounders. Applying EMERGE to 8 open-weight models and 13 fine-tuning datasets, we find that models with similar broad misalignment rates can exhibit very different behavioural profiles that a single score cannot capture. We hope EMERGE can help establish a shared and robust standard for measuring EM, making findings more comparable and helping move research from detecting the phenomenon towards understanding it.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.296139+00	4	\N	b842b3cf433e4a7b3796503c36bd9ba6b6728e992e953b43de430e0c34d29b40	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.296599+00	2026-11-10 14:46:55.297982+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a2833695-bc70-487c-9cdc-92799036f3d8	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/practical-guide-building-gpt-6	A model guide for the GPT-6 family	https://openai.com/index/practical-guide-building-gpt-6	https://openai.com/index/practical-guide-building-gpt-6	\N	Learn how startups can choose GPT-6 models, tune reasoning effort, improve prompts and skills, coordinate tools, and prepare workflows for production.	Learn how startups can choose GPT-6 models, tune reasoning effort, improve prompts and skills, coordinate tools, and prepare workflows for production.	tutorial	en	2026-10-02 16:15:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.103459+00	3	\N	ba4440266f6d4d3fe2537c893b6811c53f68bf494fdac98ae06eb3b340306952	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.104179+00	2026-11-10 14:37:09.736032+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b6546f32-3b94-4161-9f31-9f1a27b5b5ca	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08634v1	Benchmarking Machine Learning for Chaotic Three-Body Dynamics in Astrophysics	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08634	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08634	Sheng Wan, Pavan Vynatheya, Duo Xu, Li Kevin Wenliang, Ye Zhu	arXiv:2611.08634v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Machine learning (ML) and generative AI have recently made impressive progress in understanding physics. We explore a hierarchy of prediction problems stemming from chaotic three-body systems and benchmark existing ML approaches, including general-purpose prediction algorithms and those that build on top of foundational physics models. We first simulate the trajectories of triple systems near the critical boundary of stable/unstable initial conditions. We then design a hierarchy of learning tasks, focusing mainly on two broad problems: stability prediction and trajectory prediction. The former classifies whether a given triple system has diverged or not up to a specific horizon, while the latter predicts the exact trajectory, both given suitable input features. We benchmark a range of ML approaches, from simple regression methods to state-of-the-art foundational physics models, across these tasks, and compare them against astrophysical methods such as the Mardling-Aarseth stability criterion. Our experiments reveal a clear order of task difficulties in chaotic three-body dynamics: early dynamics enable strong stability prediction, while precise long-horizon trajectory forecasting remains difficult. Code and dataset are publicly available at https://github.com/Simonwan03/ThreeBodyMLBench.	arXiv:2611.08634v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Machine learning (ML) and generative AI have recently made impressive progress in understanding physics. We explore a hierarchy of prediction problems stemming from chaotic three-body systems and benchmark existing ML approaches, including general-purpose prediction algorithms and those that build on top of foundational physics models. We first simulate the trajectories of triple systems near the critical boundary of stable/unstable initial conditions. We then design a hierarchy of learning tasks, focusing mainly on two broad problems: stability prediction and trajectory prediction. The former classifies whether a given triple system has diverged or not up to a specific horizon, while the latter predicts the exact trajectory, both given suitable input features. We benchmark a range of ML approaches, from simple regression methods to state-of-the-art foundational physics models, across these tasks, and compare them against astrophysical methods such as the Mardling-Aarseth stability criterion. Our experiments reveal a clear order of task difficulties in chaotic three-body dynamics: early dynamics enable strong stability prediction, while precise long-horizon trajectory forecasting remains difficult. Code and dataset are publicly available at https://github.com/Simonwan03/ThreeBodyMLBench.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.298835+00	3	\N	6cbedc6c12cd1d3b462d947a62ac2b568d8db48bf367c0682ec4acc26dde9657	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.299272+00	2026-11-10 14:46:58.2665+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a7f8062b-45d1-41b2-8d42-c1fd4ad643e0	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08703v1	Foundation-Model Residuals as Signals of Urban Bus Irregularity	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08703	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08703	Csaba I. Sidl\\'o, Jamil J. Ghazal, Domonkos R\\'ozsay, Rita R\\'onai, Andr\\'as J. Moln\\'ar	arXiv:2611.08703v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Pretrained trajectory models encode mobility patterns learned across cities, but their errors when transferred to a new city are usually treated only as performance limitations. We instead ask whether these residuals reveal systematic local conditions. Architectures such as UniTraj and TrajFM learn transferable trajectory representations, although cross-context transfer remains challenging. Using the NetMob26 bus location, ticketing, weather, network, and socioeconomic data for Niteroi, we analyze spatial and travel-time residuals of these recent trajectory foundation models. We test whether high residuals concentrate spatially, whether spatial and travel-time residuals overlap, and which urban-context variables explain their distribution.\n  We interpret high residuals as candidate proxy signals of transit irregularity rather than direct evidence of service disruption, and analyze their contextual associations using Explainable AI (XAI). Insights into such varied environments are increasingly valuable for evaluating new urban paradigms; for instance, the 15-minute-city perspective serves as a key motivation for our approach, guiding our methods and expanding the analysis beyond standard mobility metrics.	arXiv:2611.08703v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Pretrained trajectory models encode mobility patterns learned across cities, but their errors when transferred to a new city are usually treated only as performance limitations. We instead ask whether these residuals reveal systematic local conditions. Architectures such as UniTraj and TrajFM learn transferable trajectory representations, although cross-context transfer remains challenging. Using the NetMob26 bus location, ticketing, weather, network, and socioeconomic data for Niteroi, we analyze spatial and travel-time residuals of these recent trajectory foundation models. We test whether high residuals concentrate spatially, whether spatial and travel-time residuals overlap, and which urban-context variables explain their distribution.\n  We interpret high residuals as candidate proxy signals of transit irregularity rather than direct evidence of service disruption, and analyze their contextual associations using Explainable AI (XAI). Insights into such varied environments are increasingly valuable for evaluating new urban paradigms; for instance, the 15-minute-city perspective serves as a key motivation for our approach, guiding our methods and expanding the analysis beyond standard mobility metrics.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.308462+00	3	\N	f8259ae7dff13becfac9fc9de7bdca225618837a2377871c24f2ab563bc19f12	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.309005+00	2026-11-10 14:47:07.080022+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0af93337-eeda-46b3-aec5-ca15fd9ba016	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08775v1	On Lipschitz Explosion in Deep Neural Networks with Normalization: Consequences for Optimization and Adversarial Robustness	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08775	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08775	Ashkan Soleymani, Reyhaneh Hosseinpourkhoshkbari, Hadi Daneshmand, Patrick Jaillet	arXiv:2611.08775v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Can a network look stable at every layer and still be exponentially unstable as a whole? The Lipschitz constant of a neural network is a basic stability quantity appearing in optimization guarantees, generalization bounds, and robustness certificates. Deep networks naturally admit two such notions, input Lipschitzness, measuring sensitivity to data perturbations, and parameter Lipschitzness, measuring sensitivity to weight perturbations. We prove that, for deep networks with normalization layers, with batch normalization as the canonical example, both Lipschitz constants can grow exponentially with depth and hence with parameter dimension, even under strong per-layer norm control such as $\\|W_\\ell\\|_2\\le 1$, and already for linear activations. Thus, a mechanism widely used to stabilize training can create severe worst-case instabilities that are invisible locally, i.e., from layerwise norm bounds alone. Parameter-Lipschitz explosion turns Lipschitz-dependent nonsmooth optimization guarantees into exponential-in-dimension bounds for deep normalized networks, while input-Lipschitz explosion makes worst-case Lipschitz-based generalization and robustness certificates vacuous. The latter also yields a concrete rank-separation mechanism for adversarial vulnerability. The theory predicts that perturbations introducing new singular directions should be amplified much more strongly than perturbations at the same noise level that remain within the input's original singular subspace. Experiments on MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 support this prediction, showing that rank-creating perturbations cause substantially sharper drops in accuracy, confidence, and margins than same-subspace perturbations.	arXiv:2611.08775v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Can a network look stable at every layer and still be exponentially unstable as a whole? The Lipschitz constant of a neural network is a basic stability quantity appearing in optimization guarantees, generalization bounds, and robustness certificates. Deep networks naturally admit two such notions, input Lipschitzness, measuring sensitivity to data perturbations, and parameter Lipschitzness, measuring sensitivity to weight perturbations. We prove that, for deep networks with normalization layers, with batch normalization as the canonical example, both Lipschitz constants can grow exponentially with depth and hence with parameter dimension, even under strong per-layer norm control such as $\\|W_\\ell\\|_2\\le 1$, and already for linear activations. Thus, a mechanism widely used to stabilize training can create severe worst-case instabilities that are invisible locally, i.e., from layerwise norm bounds alone. Parameter-Lipschitz explosion turns Lipschitz-dependent nonsmooth optimization guarantees into exponential-in-dimension bounds for deep normalized networks, while input-Lipschitz explosion makes worst-case Lipschitz-based generalization and robustness certificates vacuous. The latter also yields a concrete rank-separation mechanism for adversarial vulnerability. The theory predicts that perturbations introducing new singular directions should be amplified much more strongly than perturbations at the same noise level that remain within the input's original singular subspace. Experiments on MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 support this prediction, showing that rank-creating perturbations cause substantially sharper drops in accuracy, confidence, and margins than same-subspace perturbations.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.326787+00	4	\N	835e9d6