b45993b4-7b4f-4ff1-9a4d-1c63ff19628a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08706v1	Dual-Stream LLM-Guided Reward Design for Reject-Inference-Aware Reinforcement Learning in E-Commerce Payment Fraud Detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08706	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08706	Bo Qu, Zhurong Wang, Frank Zahradnik, Ming Ouyang	arXiv:2611.08706v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Production fraud-detection models learn from a censored slice of their own traffic: transactions a legacy system blocked never receive fraud labels, so models train on a biased, approved-only distribution. We extend LLM-guided reward design, the framework of Qu et al. (2025) in which an LLM evolves the reward of a two-step REINFORCE fraud-blocking policy, to attack this reject-inference problem inside the reward. A single LLM designs a dual-stream reward: stream 1 applies the standard dollar-weighted precision objective to labeled transactions; stream 2 scores legacy-blocked, unlabeled transactions by contrasting the newest gradient-boosted risk signal against the legacy scores that drove the original block. Rather than prescribe which signals matter, we expose a superset of evidence and let the LLM decide for itself which to use, guided by evaluation feedback alone; because it writes the reward as code, that choice is directly auditable. On a held-out four-month out-of-time test, the dual-stream agent beats the prior labeled-only agent at every target recall, by +4.7, +2.4 and +3.0 points of dollar-weighted precision at 80%, 85% and 90% recall, and the production baseline by +6.8 points on average. The resulting policy is architecturally identical to that of Qu et al. (2025) and adds no inference cost.	arXiv:2611.08706v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Production fraud-detection models learn from a censored slice of their own traffic: transactions a legacy system blocked never receive fraud labels, so models train on a biased, approved-only distribution. We extend LLM-guided reward design, the framework of Qu et al. (2025) in which an LLM evolves the reward of a two-step REINFORCE fraud-blocking policy, to attack this reject-inference problem inside the reward. A single LLM designs a dual-stream reward: stream 1 applies the standard dollar-weighted precision objective to labeled transactions; stream 2 scores legacy-blocked, unlabeled transactions by contrasting the newest gradient-boosted risk signal against the legacy scores that drove the original block. Rather than prescribe which signals matter, we expose a superset of evidence and let the LLM decide for itself which to use, guided by evaluation feedback alone; because it writes the reward as code, that choice is directly auditable. On a held-out four-month out-of-time test, the dual-stream agent beats the prior labeled-only agent at every target recall, by +4.7, +2.4 and +3.0 points of dollar-weighted precision at 80%, 85% and 90% recall, and the production baseline by +6.8 points on average. The resulting policy is architecturally identical to that of Qu et al. (2025) and adds no inference cost.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.311218+00	4	\N	464089a0b49e9d4c9afe6a18031d4e9623ee09e6cb9e0830dc870de7c529fb68	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.311626+00	2026-11-10 14:47:10.620301+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1daf64b8-1646-4c18-95ca-861268c5f388	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08712v1	An Educated Guess: Deriving Statistically Aligned Gradient Estimates for Zeroth-Order Optimisation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08712	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08712	Justus Huebotter, Serge Thill, Marcel van Gerven, Nasir Ahmad	arXiv:2611.08712v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As novel hardware architectures are explored for the purposes of machine learning, including neuromorphic chips, asynchronous systems, and ASICs, effective learning algorithms are highly desirable. While backpropagation is the standard for differentiation on standard hardware, its requirement for global synchrony and exact differentiability limits the exploration of exotic algorithms on non-traditional substrates and devices. Zeroth-order (ZO) optimisation methods, such as SPSA and weight perturbation, offer a compelling alternative by estimating gradients through inference-only passes; however, these methods have historically suffered due to the `curse of dimensionality', where random perturbations become increasingly misaligned with the true gradient in high-dimensional spaces. In this work, we move beyond blind random noise by deriving zeroth-order search directions from activation geometry available during the forward pass. This leads to a family of Spectral Zeroth-Order methods, SZO-$\\kappa$, whose directions solve local response-based gradient-guessing objectives under explicit stochastic assumptions. These methods explore feature-supported directions that are more likely to produce informative loss responses. Using multilayer and convolutional networks, we analyse gradient alignment, bias-variance structure, and training stability of these methods relative to standard perturbation baselines and backpropagation.	arXiv:2611.08712v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As novel hardware architectures are explored for the purposes of machine learning, including neuromorphic chips, asynchronous systems, and ASICs, effective learning algorithms are highly desirable. While backpropagation is the standard for differentiation on standard hardware, its requirement for global synchrony and exact differentiability limits the exploration of exotic algorithms on non-traditional substrates and devices. Zeroth-order (ZO) optimisation methods, such as SPSA and weight perturbation, offer a compelling alternative by estimating gradients through inference-only passes; however, these methods have historically suffered due to the `curse of dimensionality', where random perturbations become increasingly misaligned with the true gradient in high-dimensional spaces. In this work, we move beyond blind random noise by deriving zeroth-order search directions from activation geometry available during the forward pass. This leads to a family of Spectral Zeroth-Order methods, SZO-$\\kappa$, whose directions solve local response-based gradient-guessing objectives under explicit stochastic assumptions. These methods explore feature-supported directions that are more likely to produce informative loss responses. Using multilayer and convolutional networks, we analyse gradient alignment, bias-variance structure, and training stability of these methods relative to standard perturbation baselines and backpropagation.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.313713+00	3	\N	d91728bc216f8e2e90864d65e74bf83282775fa7d50d05ffe5b51e30a21ce57b	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.314158+00	2026-11-10 14:47:13.984791+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0f0fadca-1fee-47d9-b871-8aabc27581c6	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution	On the Navier–Stokes Millennium Prize Problem	https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution	https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution	\N	We’re sharing an AI-generated solution to the Navier–Stokes Millennium Prize Problem, including a writeup and a formal proof in Lean.	We’re sharing an AI-generated solution to the Navier–Stokes Millennium Prize Problem, including a writeup and a formal proof in Lean.	release	en	2026-09-08 10:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.256632+00	5	\N	849d74b4694876fe92bb6f43b891ef887baa75b7deaf6d7da673f61b31ca5d96	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.257098+00	2026-11-10 14:33:54.191974+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3a14d84d-358d-453d-84df-8f332103b029	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.27239v2	Analytical Correction for Subsampling Bias in Drifting Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.27239	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.27239	Jiaru Zhang, Zeyun Deng, Juanwu Lu, Ziran Wang, Ruqi Zhang	arXiv:2604.27239v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Drifting models are capable one-step generative models trained to follow a drifting field. The field combines attractive and repulsive softmax-weighted centroids over the data and current-generator distributions. In practice, only a minibatch of $n$ samples from each distribution is available, and each centroid is approximated by an empirical estimate. In this paper, we begin by showing that the minibatch centroid is in general a biased estimator of the target centroid, with a pointwise $O(1/n)$ bias arising from softmax self-normalization. Correcting this bias requires the expectation over the full distribution, which is intractable. We instead approximate the leading bias term from in-batch statistics and propose Analytical Bias Correction (ABC), a closed-form plug-in adjustment. We prove that ABC reduces the bias from $O(1/n)$ to $O(1/n^2)$, introduces no first-order increase in total variance, and preserves convex-hull containment of the corrected centroid. In practice, ABC requires only two additional lines of code and has negligible wall-time overhead under compiled execution. Toy experiments confirm the theoretical $O(1/n)$ and $O(1/n^2)$ scaling. On CIFAR-10, ABC reduces FID and trains faster, with the largest gains at small $n$, where the bias is most significant.	arXiv:2604.27239v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Drifting models are capable one-step generative models trained to follow a drifting field. The field combines attractive and repulsive softmax-weighted centroids over the data and current-generator distributions. In practice, only a minibatch of $n$ samples from each distribution is available, and each centroid is approximated by an empirical estimate. In this paper, we begin by showing that the minibatch centroid is in general a biased estimator of the target centroid, with a pointwise $O(1/n)$ bias arising from softmax self-normalization. Correcting this bias requires the expectation over the full distribution, which is intractable. We instead approximate the leading bias term from in-batch statistics and propose Analytical Bias Correction (ABC), a closed-form plug-in adjustment. We prove that ABC reduces the bias from $O(1/n)$ to $O(1/n^2)$, introduces no first-order increase in total variance, and preserves convex-hull containment of the corrected centroid. In practice, ABC requires only two additional lines of code and has negligible wall-time overhead under compiled execution. Toy experiments confirm the theoretical $O(1/n)$ and $O(1/n^2)$ scaling. On CIFAR-10, ABC reduces FID and trains faster, with the largest gains at small $n$, where the bias is most significant.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.08564+00	3	\N	a7acd4f63ea2071df021f2fdac44eaee808463520e28105a0c5b8f731628fdf0	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.086438+00	2026-11-10 15:26:07.203544+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b7749340-74bc-451d-93ea-07983a0d8bac	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08753v1	Random Symbolic Boosting: A Geometric Approach to Symbolic Regression	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08753	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08753	Jos\\'e R. Ortegaa, Jos\\'e Manuel Mu\\~noz Contreras, Leonardo Trujillo	arXiv:2611.08753v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Symbolic models offer interpretability, but their construction through generational evolution can be computationally expensive. This article presents Random Symbolic Boosting (RSB), a method that constructs explicit additive symbolic models via boosting over populations of randomly generated syntax trees, eliminating the need for generational evolution. The idea combines two observations: high-dimensional spaces can be spanned by enough linearly independent vectors, and the semantics of syntax trees can be directly manipulated as such vectors. It is shown that, for Ordinary Least Squares, the boosting process guarantees a monotonic reduction of the training error for both regression and classification. Furthermore, an adaptive geometric early stopping mechanism is introduced. It is based on the angle between the prediction and target vectors, is independent of the problems scale, and requires no adjustments across tasks. Evaluated on a standard benchmark for symbolic regression, the method is located at the Pareto-optimal point between predictive accuracy and training time, ranking first on the real-world datasets. Additionally, it was tested on a large tabular classification benchmark with 200 problems, achieving competitive results.	arXiv:2611.08753v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Symbolic models offer interpretability, but their construction through generational evolution can be computationally expensive. This article presents Random Symbolic Boosting (RSB), a method that constructs explicit additive symbolic models via boosting over populations of randomly generated syntax trees, eliminating the need for generational evolution. The idea combines two observations: high-dimensional spaces can be spanned by enough linearly independent vectors, and the semantics of syntax trees can be directly manipulated as such vectors. It is shown that, for Ordinary Least Squares, the boosting process guarantees a monotonic reduction of the training error for both regression and classification. Furthermore, an adaptive geometric early stopping mechanism is introduced. It is based on the angle between the prediction and target vectors, is independent of the problems scale, and requires no adjustments across tasks. Evaluated on a standard benchmark for symbolic regression, the method is located at the Pareto-optimal point between predictive accuracy and training time, ranking first on the real-world datasets. Additionally, it was tested on a large tabular classification benchmark with 200 problems, achieving competitive results.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.321385+00	3	\N	2f61b956f07191d6bdb63a62b3399768e77a094f4abe7abbe7fe366ff4bda11c	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.321855+00	2026-11-10 14:47:25.507507+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
27fcdfdb-633a-433f-a5d7-79109960819c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.00222v3	CompleteRXN: Toward Completing Open Chemical Reaction Databases	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.00222	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.00222	Gabriel Vogel, Minouk Noordsij, Evgeny Pidko, Jana M. Weber	arXiv:2605.00222v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Chemical reaction datasets such as USPTO suffer from substantial incompleteness, frequently missing byproducts, co-reactants, and stoichiometric coefficients. This limits their applicability and reliability in downstream applications. Here, we introduce CompleteRXN, a large-scale supervised benchmark for reaction completion under realistic missing-data conditions. We construct a dataset of aligned incomplete and atom-balanced reactions by mapping USPTO records to curated mechanistic reactions. We evaluate representative baselines, including a novel encoder-decoder reaction completion model with constrained decoding, the Constrained Reaction Balancer (CRB), and a recent algorithmic method, SynRBL. On our CompleteRXN benchmark, the CRB achieves high performance across splits of increasing difficulty, reaching 99.28% equivalence accuracy on the random split and 91.71% on the extreme out-of-distribution split. SynRBL produces many balanced and chemically plausible completions, but with lower accuracy on the benchmark test splits. Across all methods, performance degrades with increasing incompleteness. We observe a substantial drop when evaluating on reactions outside the benchmark (full uncurated USPTO), highlighting the gap between benchmark performance and practical robustness and motivating future work. Data, code and models can be found at: https://github.com/Intelligent-molecular-systems/CompleteRxn-Benchmarking.	arXiv:2605.00222v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Chemical reaction datasets such as USPTO suffer from substantial incompleteness, frequently missing byproducts, co-reactants, and stoichiometric coefficients. This limits their applicability and reliability in downstream applications. Here, we introduce CompleteRXN, a large-scale supervised benchmark for reaction completion under realistic missing-data conditions. We construct a dataset of aligned incomplete and atom-balanced reactions by mapping USPTO records to curated mechanistic reactions. We evaluate representative baselines, including a novel encoder-decoder reaction completion model with constrained decoding, the Constrained Reaction Balancer (CRB), and a recent algorithmic method, SynRBL. On our CompleteRXN benchmark, the CRB achieves high performance across splits of increasing difficulty, reaching 99.28% equivalence accuracy on the random split and 91.71% on the extreme out-of-distribution split. SynRBL produces many balanced and chemically plausible completions, but with lower accuracy on the benchmark test splits. Across all methods, performance degrades with increasing incompleteness. We observe a substantial drop when evaluating on reactions outside the benchmark (full uncurated USPTO), highlighting the gap between benchmark performance and practical robustness and motivating future work. Data, code and models can be found at: https://github.com/Intelligent-molecular-systems/CompleteRxn-Benchmarking.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.095797+00	3	\N	d97acefc88f909d8b39af886b1a5afe99fa69fc58fcff6a4cdacd7aadddc6d24	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.096254+00	2026-11-10 15:26:18.646309+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1ddab0c4-31f0-41cc-967f-7a50a0514b17	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/new-chatgpt-ads-format-and-measurement	Building advertising for the way people use AI	https://openai.com/index/new-chatgpt-ads-format-and-measurement	https://openai.com/index/new-chatgpt-ads-format-and-measurement	\N	OpenAI introduces a new visual ad format in ChatGPT and expands measurement tools, attribution partnerships, and brand suitability for advertisers.	OpenAI introduces a new visual ad format in ChatGPT and expands measurement tools, attribution partnerships, and brand suitability for advertisers.	product_update	en	2026-10-05 10:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.100467+00	3	\N	f214efe038e44c535b7f6c44fa69adf9db30d7e44e2ebacee6c86557c410dece	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.100972+00	2026-11-10 14:37:13.013626+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1b08588f-c8cb-4744-a616-e8b71657b428	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2509.00761v5	L-MARS: Legal Multi-Agent System with Agentic Search and Citation-Faithfulness Audit	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00761	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00761	Boqin Yuan, Ziqi Wang	arXiv:2509.00761v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Large language models are increasingly deployed for legal question answering, where evaluations typically focus on multiple-choice accuracy. This measure overlooks a common failure: whether the citation source attached to an answer exists and supports the rule the system attributes to it. We present L-MARS, an open multi-agent legal QA system with agentic search and judge-driven evidence checks, and audit it claim by claim against its cited source. Each atomic claim is labelled with a six-class taxonomy and scored with strict-ALCE under cross-provider judging, where the answerer and verifier come from different model families. On a stratified 100-question Bar Exam audit, retrieval barely moves accuracy, yet the multi-turn judge loop lifts strict citation F1 from 0.13 (naive RAG) to 0.25 and cuts the no-citation rate from 34% to 13%. We further introduce Faith-Search, a post-draft step that re-verifies and repairs unreachable citations; it drops the unreachable rate below 1% but does not improve F1 over the multi-turn loop, so we report it as a targeted reachability intervention rather than a faithfulness breakthrough. A 50-question LegalSearchQA case study confirms the picture: retrieve-then-draft pipelines saturate near 0.75 citation F1, while a single-agent web-search baseline collapses to 0.22 under external audit.	arXiv:2509.00761v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Large language models are increasingly deployed for legal question answering, where evaluations typically focus on multiple-choice accuracy. This measure overlooks a common failure: whether the citation source attached to an answer exists and supports the rule the system attributes to it. We present L-MARS, an open multi-agent legal QA system with agentic search and judge-driven evidence checks, and audit it claim by claim against its cited source. Each atomic claim is labelled with a six-class taxonomy and scored with strict-ALCE under cross-provider judging, where the answerer and verifier come from different model families. On a stratified 100-question Bar Exam audit, retrieval barely moves accuracy, yet the multi-turn judge loop lifts strict citation F1 from 0.13 (naive RAG) to 0.25 and cuts the no-citation rate from 34% to 13%. We further introduce Faith-Search, a post-draft step that re-verifies and repairs unreachable citations; it drops the unreachable rate below 1% but does not improve F1 over the multi-turn loop, so we report it as a targeted reachability intervention rather than a faithfulness breakthrough. A 50-question LegalSearchQA case study confirms the picture: retrieve-then-draft pipelines saturate near 0.75 citation F1, while a single-agent web-search baseline collapses to 0.22 under external audit.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.231207+00	4	\N	9a0aefd324fae6f6402208f412774c31c7072fa487766b2a0e88fd854c74e196	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.231595+00	2026-11-10 15:43:51.797269+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c5bdd81d-5695-423f-aa71-aa6fd654de26	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08788v1	Not All Latents Are Equal: Importance-Weighted Latent Graph Generation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08788	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08788	Biraaj Rout, Jay Revolinsky, Han Liu, Harry Shomer	arXiv:2611.08788v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Graph generation has shown tremendous effectiveness in modeling complex scientific tasks ranging from molecule generation to optimized circuit design. Most recent advancements have developed models that learn to denoise noisy graphs using either discrete diffusion or flow matching. However, discrete graph diffusion scales quadratically with the number of nodes, making it difficult to use such models on larger graphs. On the other hand, latent graph generation instead seeks to denoise noisy graph latents, which scale better to more nodes due to their lower dimension. However, in practice, latent graph generation models lag behind their discrete counterparts in performance. We study this problem, finding that it is caused by the unequal distribution of that different latent dimensions have on graph reconstruction. the impact. We observe that the vast majority of impact is contained in only a smaller number of latent dimensions. Since equal emphasis is placed on reconstructing the entire latent in the loss function, existing models are optimized to reconstruct information irrelevant to the downstream task. To tackle this problem, we propose Latent Importance Reweighting ({\\bf LIR)} which weights each latent dimensions via it's importance to final graph reconstruction. Importance is calculated as the relative influence of a single latent unit on graph reconstruction, which is represented via the Jacobian of the decoder relative to the latent. Our method is model-agnostic and requires no additional model parameters or hyperparameters. Experiments show that LIR can boost performance and lead to faster training convergence when applied to two different autoencoders on various benchmark datasets. Code: https://github.com/biraaj/GraphGenLatentReweight	arXiv:2611.08788v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Graph generation has shown tremendous effectiveness in modeling complex scientific tasks ranging from molecule generation to optimized circuit design. Most recent advancements have developed models that learn to denoise noisy graphs using either discrete diffusion or flow matching. However, discrete graph diffusion scales quadratically with the number of nodes, making it difficult to use such models on larger graphs. On the other hand, latent graph generation instead seeks to denoise noisy graph latents, which scale better to more nodes due to their lower dimension. However, in practice, latent graph generation models lag behind their discrete counterparts in performance. We study this problem, finding that it is caused by the unequal distribution of that different latent dimensions have on graph reconstruction. the impact. We observe that the vast majority of impact is contained in only a smaller number of latent dimensions. Since equal emphasis is placed on reconstructing the entire latent in the loss function, existing models are optimized to reconstruct information irrelevant to the downstream task. To tackle this problem, we propose Latent Importance Reweighting ({\\bf LIR)} which weights each latent dimensions via it's importance to final graph reconstruction. Importance is calculated as the relative influence of a single latent unit on graph reconstruction, which is represented via the Jacobian of the decoder relative to the latent. Our method is model-agnostic and requires no additional model parameters or hyperparameters. Experiments show that LIR can boost performance and lead to faster training convergence when applied to two different autoencoders on various benchmark datasets. Code: https://github.com/biraaj/GraphGenLatentReweight	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.332003+00	3	\N	1b0ac978e2152f2be8bbfcd1bc435ff3e5780aed5ed6d3a91af666756011cc76	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.332465+00	2026-11-10 14:47:43.76333+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5c31c1f5-73c9-4222-9d36-365eb95001b9	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08789v1	TopoSHAP: Shapley for Explainable and Efficient Topological Deep Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08789	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08789	Mathilde Papillon, Nina Miolane	arXiv:2611.08789v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Topological deep learning (TDL) extends learning on graphs, such as molecules or social networks, to higher-order structures, such as rings in a molecule or groups in a social network. Explaining which of these structures drive a model's predictions, and which architectural components matter most for its performance, remains challenging. We introduce TopoSHAP, an efficient and general explanation framework that addresses both questions across topological domains and model architectures. TopoSHAP-cell assigns Shapley values to topological data cells and extracts compact explanations. These explanations match or outperform graph-based counterparts on GraphXAI benchmark data while running up to 112$\\times$ faster. They also reveal advantageous topological feature design, leading to measured improvements in explanation recovery and predictive accuracy. On the architecture side, TopoSHAP-neighbor explains how neighborhoods contribute to model performance, and turns these evaluations into a backward-elimination search that identifies smaller, competitive architectures early in training. This is up to $11.3\\times$ cheaper than traditional TDL training sweeps.	arXiv:2611.08789v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Topological deep learning (TDL) extends learning on graphs, such as molecules or social networks, to higher-order structures, such as rings in a molecule or groups in a social network. Explaining which of these structures drive a model's predictions, and which architectural components matter most for its performance, remains challenging. We introduce TopoSHAP, an efficient and general explanation framework that addresses both questions across topological domains and model architectures. TopoSHAP-cell assigns Shapley values to topological data cells and extracts compact explanations. These explanations match or outperform graph-based counterparts on GraphXAI benchmark data while running up to 112$\\times$ faster. They also reveal advantageous topological feature design, leading to measured improvements in explanation recovery and predictive accuracy. On the architecture side, TopoSHAP-neighbor explains how neighborhoods contribute to model performance, and turns these evaluations into a backward-elimination search that identifies smaller, competitive architectures early in training. This is up to $11.3\\times$ cheaper than traditional TDL training sweeps.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.334758+00	4	\N	f77a1827428430287dfcbc3d4abf59d6b486f23f842f11e83e0822e212644854	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.335135+00	2026-11-10 14:47:46.696213+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2667e8cf-4962-4fd8-83aa-3493459753a8	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08840v1	What Matters for LLM Persona Simulation: Signal Form in Cold-Start E-Commerce Advertising	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08840	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08840	Jiwoo Choi, In Ik Lee, Woo Sung Seo, Minho Lee, Yun Young Choi	arXiv:2611.08840v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Estimating advertising performance in cold-start settings is challenging because historical logs are unavailable before launch. Large language models (LLMs) offer a potential alternative by simulating persona-conditioned user responses, but it remains unclear which design choices actually drive LLM-based persona simulation. Using real Google Ads e-commerce campaign data across five demographic segments, we identify three signal-form dimensions that affect simulation quality: (i) a demographic anchor without noisy rich attributes, (ii) a relative qualitative banner-blindness signal rather than quantitative anchors, and (iii) prompt phrasing. On this campaign, our method attains an MAE of 24.9 and a MAPE of 26.1%, a 51.3% lower MAE than the best tested non-LLM baseline and 77.3% lower than an LLM configuration using explicit CTR anchors. Systematic ablations show that minimal demographic descriptions achieve lower mean error than richer persona configurations, while relative qualitative cues substantially outperform the tested quantitative anchors. We additionally compare these configurations with direct-prior and rule-based baselines and with alternative LLM configurations.	arXiv:2611.08840v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Estimating advertising performance in cold-start settings is challenging because historical logs are unavailable before launch. Large language models (LLMs) offer a potential alternative by simulating persona-conditioned user responses, but it remains unclear which design choices actually drive LLM-based persona simulation. Using real Google Ads e-commerce campaign data across five demographic segments, we identify three signal-form dimensions that affect simulation quality: (i) a demographic anchor without noisy rich attributes, (ii) a relative qualitative banner-blindness signal rather than quantitative anchors, and (iii) prompt phrasing. On this campaign, our method attains an MAE of 24.9 and a MAPE of 26.1%, a 51.3% lower MAE than the best tested non-LLM baseline and 77.3% lower than an LLM configuration using explicit CTR anchors. Systematic ablations show that minimal demographic descriptions achieve lower mean error than richer persona configurations, while relative qualitative cues substantially outperform the tested quantitative anchors. We additionally compare these configurations with direct-prior and rule-based baselines and with alternative LLM configurations.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.345322+00	3	\N	1d4d8ebe2cad724c0f215d7e25418b19d78cf8585a1a7aa98e4d8a09953c947a	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.345895+00	2026-11-10 14:47:59.738556+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6d8e2941-cdff-41d1-b337-10334be9bdfb	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/eu-text-provenance	Our approach to EU text provenance rules	https://openai.com/index/eu-text-provenance	https://openai.com/index/eu-text-provenance	\N	How OpenAI is approaching text watermarking under EU rules. Learn where watermarks apply, how detection works, and why access starts with researchers.	How OpenAI is approaching text watermarking under EU rules. Learn where watermarks apply, how detection works, and why access starts with researchers.	product_update	en	2026-10-05 15:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.098234+00	4	\N	513466985439b39b910e0f64413305fc7b92a7624319e894fade2608df6890b8	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.098683+00	2026-11-10 14:37:15.986731+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2a3ba013-fc58-4a28-a349-d3599349dbc7	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.06467v2	No Triangulation Without Representation: Generalization in Topological Deep Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06467	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06467	Johannes S. Schmidt, Martin Carrasco, Ernst R\\"oell, Guy Wolf, Nello Blaser, Bastian Rieck	arXiv:2605.06467v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Despite an ever-increasing interest in topological deep learning models that target higher-order datasets, there is no consensus on how to evaluate such models. This is exacerbated by the fact that topological objects permit operations, such as structural refinements, that are not appropriate for graph data. In this work, we extend MANTRA, a benchmark dataset containing manifold triangulations, to a larger class of manifolds with more diverse homeomorphism types. We show that, contrary to prior claims, graph neural networks can saturate the benchmark, while higher-order message passing methods lag behind. However, we find that this is contingent on the right representation and feature assignment, emphasizing their importance in baseline models. Hence, we present a novel evaluation protocol based on representational diversity and triangulation refinement. Even when including refinement-like changes in the training data, existing models fail to generalize beyond the combinatorial structure of the data. This points towards a research gap in developing models that understand topological structure independent of scale. Our work thus provides the necessary scaffolding to evaluate future models and enable the development of topology-aware inductive bias	arXiv:2605.06467v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Despite an ever-increasing interest in topological deep learning models that target higher-order datasets, there is no consensus on how to evaluate such models. This is exacerbated by the fact that topological objects permit operations, such as structural refinements, that are not appropriate for graph data. In this work, we extend MANTRA, a benchmark dataset containing manifold triangulations, to a larger class of manifolds with more diverse homeomorphism types. We show that, contrary to prior claims, graph neural networks can saturate the benchmark, while higher-order message passing methods lag behind. However, we find that this is contingent on the right representation and feature assignment, emphasizing their importance in baseline models. Hence, we present a novel evaluation protocol based on representational diversity and triangulation refinement. Even when including refinement-like changes in the training data, existing models fail to generalize beyond the combinatorial structure of the data. This points towards a research gap in developing models that understand topological structure independent of scale. Our work thus provides the necessary scaffolding to evaluate future models and enable the development of topology-aware inductive bias	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.098911+00	4	\N	9cd11dab2188ac2f1fd24d29d856e01c90d1fc2567a94ac93ca26ac8ed9c4523	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.099318+00	2026-11-10 15:26:25.449407+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d664be79-d923-4360-905d-6d784837da3e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08883v1	Learning to Restart: Differentiable Restart Policies for Optimization Dynamics	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08883	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08883	Yiman Fong, Zhonglin Xie, Zaiwen Wen	arXiv:2611.08883v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Adaptive restart mechanisms, which reset the momentum or other auxiliary state of an iterative optimizer, play a central role in the empirical performance of accelerated first-order methods, yet existing restart rules remain almost entirely hand-designed. The obstruction to learning restart policies is that, unlike the typical Learning to Optimize (L2O) setting, the discrete restart events make the resulting trajectory non-differentiable in the algorithm parameters and thus block gradient-based optimization of the restart policy. We resolve this obstruction by developing a differentiable framework for restart policies through their continuous-time limit. We introduce surrogate gradients for the restart events and optimization trajectories that are efficient to compute and provably converge to their continuous-time counterparts. We validate the effectiveness of our surrogate gradients in L2O settings, training restart policies that improve upon standard handcrafted adaptive restart criteria on quadratic and linear programs, and that transfer zero-shot from synthetic quadratics to logistic regression on real datasets.	arXiv:2611.08883v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Adaptive restart mechanisms, which reset the momentum or other auxiliary state of an iterative optimizer, play a central role in the empirical performance of accelerated first-order methods, yet existing restart rules remain almost entirely hand-designed. The obstruction to learning restart policies is that, unlike the typical Learning to Optimize (L2O) setting, the discrete restart events make the resulting trajectory non-differentiable in the algorithm parameters and thus block gradient-based optimization of the restart policy. We resolve this obstruction by developing a differentiable framework for restart policies through their continuous-time limit. We introduce surrogate gradients for the restart events and optimization trajectories that are efficient to compute and provably converge to their continuous-time counterparts. We validate the effectiveness of our surrogate gradients in L2O settings, training restart policies that improve upon standard handcrafted adaptive restart criteria on quadratic and linear programs, and that transfer zero-shot from synthetic quadratics to logistic regression on real datasets.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.352308+00	3	\N	003027a301eed019609e7971ab03a0b8384f0730f3abff5fe6b4043608fbfe15	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.352913+00	2026-11-10 14:48:06.004424+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9f4bbd53-7a5e-4ef7-ade7-76dc0de2044b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08918v1	Composable Causality: A Toolkit for Systematic Time-Series Causal Discovery and Treatment-Effect Benchmarking	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08918	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08918	Muhammad Hasan Ferdous, Md Osman Gani	arXiv:2611.08918v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Real-world multivariate time series rarely come with verified ground-truth causal structure, so research on time-series causal discovery and treatment-effect estimation depends on synthetic data. Existing tools are either fixed datasets that cannot be modified after release or script-based generators in which each script fixes one combination of mechanism, confounding, and missingness. Neither supports controlled experiments that vary a single property, such as sparsity, noise distribution, or missingness mechanism, while holding the others fixed. Counterfactual evaluation has the same gap, since existing simulators are built per paper with hardcoded mechanisms or produce observational data only. We introduce DTCD (Datasets and Toolkit for Causal Discovery), a data generation library that produces multivariate time series with ground-truth contemporaneous and lagged adjacency matrices. Topology, mechanism, noise, observational mask, and intervention policy are independent components that compose through configuration alone. Paired counterfactual twins, produced by caching the stochastic realization during the factual rollout and re-injecting it during the interventional rollout, recover individual treatment effects to numerical precision. A reference benchmark of eight discovery algorithms on eighty-one configurations and four treatment-effect estimators on eighteen configurations exposes failure modes that aggregate metrics hide. These include the inability of standard discovery methods to handle missing values, regardless of whether the missingness mechanism is informative, and a marginal-variance shortcut (varsortability) that we measure and remove by standardization. Code and data: https://github.com/hferdous/Composable_Causality	arXiv:2611.08918v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Real-world multivariate time series rarely come with verified ground-truth causal structure, so research on time-series causal discovery and treatment-effect estimation depends on synthetic data. Existing tools are either fixed datasets that cannot be modified after release or script-based generators in which each script fixes one combination of mechanism, confounding, and missingness. Neither supports controlled experiments that vary a single property, such as sparsity, noise distribution, or missingness mechanism, while holding the others fixed. Counterfactual evaluation has the same gap, since existing simulators are built per paper with hardcoded mechanisms or produce observational data only. We introduce DTCD (Datasets and Toolkit for Causal Discovery), a data generation library that produces multivariate time series with ground-truth contemporaneous and lagged adjacency matrices. Topology, mechanism, noise, observational mask, and intervention policy are independent components that compose through configuration alone. Paired counterfactual twins, produced by caching the stochastic realization during the factual rollout and re-injecting it during the interventional rollout, recover individual treatment effects to numerical precision. A reference benchmark of eight discovery algorithms on eighty-one configurations and four treatment-effect estimators on eighteen configurations exposes failure modes that aggregate metrics hide. These include the inability of standard discovery methods to handle missing values, regardless of whether the missingness mechanism is informative, and a marginal-variance shortcut (varsortability) that we measure and remove by standardization. Code and data: https://github.com/hferdous/Composable_Causality	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.357738+00	4	\N	adf839f2bed10077dfcee1c5ba75093e71cf0abbc5bed94aa4a9207395353668	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.358139+00	2026-11-10 14:48:17.369393+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
eac06f10-61f9-4235-a8bf-5c330e233a3d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08924v1	ResQAdam: Residual-Preserving Adaptive Optimization for Memory-Efficient Quantization Aware Training	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08924	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08924	Liqun Ma, Shengkun Tang, Liangyu Wang, Lin Zhao, Wei Luo, Xinjun Niu, Spandan Tiwari, Ashish Sirasao, Kun Zhang, Zhiqiang Shen	arXiv:2611.08924v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Quantization-aware training (QAT) is the most reliable way to obtain accurate low-bit large language models (LLMs), but training with AdamW keeps two dense FP32 states for every weight matrix. We find that low-rank memory-efficient optimizers such as GaLore and LDAdam degrade substantially in QAT. A considerable part of the QAT momentum lies outside its top-r singular subspace, and projection-based methods discard this part at every step. We further observe that this residual remains useful when only its sign is kept. Based on these observations, we propose ResQAdam. It keeps a single BF16 momentum, applies Adam only to the r x r coordinates of the principal subspace, and adds a normalized sign update computed from the residual. We evaluate ResQAdam with two from-scratch QAT methods and three QAT fine-tuning pipelines, with weights quantized down to 2 bits. It reduces optimizer-state memory by 71% to 74% relative to AdamW, stays close to AdamW in most settings, and lowers training throughput by less than 2%.	arXiv:2611.08924v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Quantization-aware training (QAT) is the most reliable way to obtain accurate low-bit large language models (LLMs), but training with AdamW keeps two dense FP32 states for every weight matrix. We find that low-rank memory-efficient optimizers such as GaLore and LDAdam degrade substantially in QAT. A considerable part of the QAT momentum lies outside its top-r singular subspace, and projection-based methods discard this part at every step. We further observe that this residual remains useful when only its sign is kept. Based on these observations, we propose ResQAdam. It keeps a single BF16 momentum, applies Adam only to the r x r coordinates of the principal subspace, and adds a normalized sign update computed from the residual. We evaluate ResQAdam with two from-scratch QAT methods and three QAT fine-tuning pipelines, with weights quantized down to 2 bits. It reduces optimizer-state memory by 71% to 74% relative to AdamW, stays close to AdamW in most settings, and lowers training throughput by less than 2%.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.360153+00	4	\N	d8c6050a781a4ebeb37062990601f48c065a66834dc98ebf46e357d785417699	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.360701+00	2026-11-10 14:48:20.699546+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
