529a1c9c-430c-4188-9895-bdc33806d35a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08787v1	Training Stability under Adaptive Curricula	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08787	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08787	Johan Obando-Ceron, Walter Mayor, Michael Dennis, Aaron Courville, Pablo Samuel Castro	arXiv:2611.08787v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Unsupervised Environment Design (UED) approaches train reinforcement learning (RL) agents on diverse curricula that may evolve with their capabilities. While prior work has largely focused on how these curricula are constructed, we study whether their students remain trainable under the resulting distributions. Across multiple UED mechanisms, environment complexities, and extended training horizons, we identify recurring learner-side pathologies, including parameter growth, degraded value--outcome ordering, and loss of representational diversity, that accompany stagnation and performance degradation. These findings connect limitations in UED to optimization failures previously identified in conventional deep RL, while showing how adaptive curricula couple the learner's optimization state to its future training distribution. Controlled interventions show that mitigating selected pathologies improves learning. Stabilizing the learner improves long-horizon performance and zero-shot generalization without changing the curriculum rule or increasing environment interaction. Our results establish learner trainability as a complementary design principle for UED. Effective curricula require students that remain capable of learning from the distributions they produce.	arXiv:2611.08787v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Unsupervised Environment Design (UED) approaches train reinforcement learning (RL) agents on diverse curricula that may evolve with their capabilities. While prior work has largely focused on how these curricula are constructed, we study whether their students remain trainable under the resulting distributions. Across multiple UED mechanisms, environment complexities, and extended training horizons, we identify recurring learner-side pathologies, including parameter growth, degraded value--outcome ordering, and loss of representational diversity, that accompany stagnation and performance degradation. These findings connect limitations in UED to optimization failures previously identified in conventional deep RL, while showing how adaptive curricula couple the learner's optimization state to its future training distribution. Controlled interventions show that mitigating selected pathologies improves learning. Stabilizing the learner improves long-horizon performance and zero-shot generalization without changing the curriculum rule or increasing environment interaction. Our results establish learner trainability as a complementary design principle for UED. Effective curricula require students that remain capable of learning from the distributions they produce.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.329377+00	3	\N	9d43d9610a4efb99d7230436f42929cf59bf0cb3ea52b6ffaf5a9f1a1065ba84	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.329844+00	2026-11-10 14:47:40.521268+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8b8a6fb5-2856-488a-a090-e470c4032e2a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.09289v4	Intention Driven Identification of In-Possession Match Phases in Association Football through Temporal Graph Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.09289	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.09289	Yuesen Li, Daniel Link	arXiv:2606.09289v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recognising intentions from continuous movement trajectories is an important problem in spatiotemporal data mining. This becomes difficult when behaviours change over time and their boundaries cannot be directly observed. Football provides a useful setting for studying this problem because tactical intentions emerge from the coordinated movements of multiple players during possession. Existing methods for match phase recognition often classify individual frames, use predefined spatial rules, or identify only selected tactical patterns. They therefore cannot fully describe continuous possession sequences in which tactical intentions change over time. We address this gap by treating the identification of the in-possession match phase as a hierarchical sequence segmentation task. The framework includes three tactical intentions (Invade Opponent Space, Keep Possession, and Scoring) and six phases (Build Up, Progression, Counter Attack, Maintenance, Sustained Threat, and Finishing). We propose a Temporal Graph Attention Network (T-GAN) that combines player interaction graphs, contextual features, and Transformer based temporal modelling. The framework was evaluated on seven Bundesliga matches using leave-one-match-out validation and compared with rule-based, Random Forest, GNN, and Transformer baselines at both frame and sequence levels. The results show that temporal modelling is important for coherent phase segmentation, while graph modelling provides additional value for transition phases such as Counter Attack. The framework provides an interpretable method for discovering intention based behavioural segments from continuous player tracking data and supports automated tactical analysis in football.	arXiv:2606.09289v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recognising intentions from continuous movement trajectories is an important problem in spatiotemporal data mining. This becomes difficult when behaviours change over time and their boundaries cannot be directly observed. Football provides a useful setting for studying this problem because tactical intentions emerge from the coordinated movements of multiple players during possession. Existing methods for match phase recognition often classify individual frames, use predefined spatial rules, or identify only selected tactical patterns. They therefore cannot fully describe continuous possession sequences in which tactical intentions change over time. We address this gap by treating the identification of the in-possession match phase as a hierarchical sequence segmentation task. The framework includes three tactical intentions (Invade Opponent Space, Keep Possession, and Scoring) and six phases (Build Up, Progression, Counter Attack, Maintenance, Sustained Threat, and Finishing). We propose a Temporal Graph Attention Network (T-GAN) that combines player interaction graphs, contextual features, and Transformer based temporal modelling. The framework was evaluated on seven Bundesliga matches using leave-one-match-out validation and compared with rule-based, Random Forest, GNN, and Transformer baselines at both frame and sequence levels. The results show that temporal modelling is important for coherent phase segmentation, while graph modelling provides additional value for transition phases such as Counter Attack. The framework provides an interpretable method for discovering intention based behavioural segments from continuous player tracking data and supports automated tactical analysis in football.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.189045+00	3	\N	be9e105bfc11eec99db2ec5d853b118cd97fc751f414a206bdb33c4a3c051d58	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.189458+00	2026-11-10 15:27:55.443025+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
33da8136-f485-4ae1-bab8-c2389b83d4ea	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.12289v4	The Standard Interpretable Model: Design interpretable machine learning methods from elementary symmetries	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12289	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12289	Pietro Barbiero, Giovanni De Felice, Mateo Espinosa Zarlenga, Francesco Giannini, Filippo Bonchi, Mateja Jamnik, Giuseppe Marra, Ruggero Noris	arXiv:2606.12289v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: As Artificial Intelligence models grow in complexity, interpretability has become an indispensable tool for understanding, debugging, and controlling their computations. However, interpretability lacks general theories to deductively design interpretable methods. This gap between theories and methods results in a fragmented literature and inconsistent evaluation protocols.\n  To fill this gap, we introduce the Standard Interpretable Model (SIM), a general theory that enables the deductive design of interpretable methods from first principles. Specifically, the SIM summarises, in a set of premises, what interpretability is for a target entity. From these premises, the SIM systematically derives interpretability symmetries and corresponding constraints. One can then build interpretable models by either introducing constraints in the optimisation problem or by compiling constraints into an interpretable architecture.\n  The most important finding, both theoretical and empirical, is that full expressivity and interpretability symmetries can coexist in the same model, showing that the trade-off between accuracy and interpretability is an artefact of model design. We demonstrate this on language models (LMs), where SIM-based LMs match the performance of equivalent opaque LMs without making any compromise on interpretability. Beyond this, the SIM shows how to improve the interpretability of existing pre-trained models, highlights underexplored research directions, and unifies a discipline that has long been fragmented, offering pedagogical grounding for interpretability curricula.	arXiv:2606.12289v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: As Artificial Intelligence models grow in complexity, interpretability has become an indispensable tool for understanding, debugging, and controlling their computations. However, interpretability lacks general theories to deductively design interpretable methods. This gap between theories and methods results in a fragmented literature and inconsistent evaluation protocols.\n  To fill this gap, we introduce the Standard Interpretable Model (SIM), a general theory that enables the deductive design of interpretable methods from first principles. Specifically, the SIM summarises, in a set of premises, what interpretability is for a target entity. From these premises, the SIM systematically derives interpretability symmetries and corresponding constraints. One can then build interpretable models by either introducing constraints in the optimisation problem or by compiling constraints into an interpretable architecture.\n  The most important finding, both theoretical and empirical, is that full expressivity and interpretability symmetries can coexist in the same model, showing that the trade-off between accuracy and interpretability is an artefact of model design. We demonstrate this on language models (LMs), where SIM-based LMs match the performance of equivalent opaque LMs without making any compromise on interpretability. Beyond this, the SIM shows how to improve the interpretability of existing pre-trained models, highlights underexplored research directions, and unifies a discipline that has long been fragmented, offering pedagogical grounding for interpretability curricula.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.191768+00	4	\N	3bb2b8ddf6652a6f49aa49579cb34c45f7ee736c4204bf4ff4c926341f3c9a3e	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.192316+00	2026-11-10 15:27:58.822192+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1097eca1-25db-44cb-8265-d5d0c87e0e2c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08797v1	MotionBench-XAI: Benchmarking What Shapes Shapley Explanations in Spatiotemporal Machine Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08797	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08797	Mathilde Papillon, Louisa Cornelis, Nina Miolane	arXiv:2611.08797v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Predicting outcomes from spatiotemporal data, such as assessing Parkinson's severity from human motion, often requires explainability in addition to accuracy. Practitioners need to know what drove the prediction, whether it be spatial (a body joint), temporal (a specific window), or both. Shapley values are attributions computed by treating input features as players in a cooperative game and measuring how the model output changes as coalitions of players are removed. Computing them involves choosing what counts as players, how absent players are imputed, and how the game is evaluated. How these choices affect explanation quality is poorly understood for spatiotemporal data, and real data carries no ground-truth attributions to check against. We present MotionBench-XAI, a benchmark built on i) five synthetic spatiotemporal datasets with exact ground-truth Shapley values, ii) three real datasets across clinical gait, electrocardiogram, and audio, iii) ten attribution methods across three player sets, and iv) three trained architectures. We show that grouping features into players, across time, space, or both, matters more than the choice of imputer. Generative imputers improve explanations only when their conditional models are accurate. On real data, the standard proxy metrics select contradictory best methods. We distill these findings into practical insights and provide an open-source pipeline.	arXiv:2611.08797v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Predicting outcomes from spatiotemporal data, such as assessing Parkinson's severity from human motion, often requires explainability in addition to accuracy. Practitioners need to know what drove the prediction, whether it be spatial (a body joint), temporal (a specific window), or both. Shapley values are attributions computed by treating input features as players in a cooperative game and measuring how the model output changes as coalitions of players are removed. Computing them involves choosing what counts as players, how absent players are imputed, and how the game is evaluated. How these choices affect explanation quality is poorly understood for spatiotemporal data, and real data carries no ground-truth attributions to check against. We present MotionBench-XAI, a benchmark built on i) five synthetic spatiotemporal datasets with exact ground-truth Shapley values, ii) three real datasets across clinical gait, electrocardiogram, and audio, iii) ten attribution methods across three player sets, and iv) three trained architectures. We show that grouping features into players, across time, space, or both, matters more than the choice of imputer. Generative imputers improve explanations only when their conditional models are accurate. On real data, the standard proxy metrics select contradictory best methods. We distill these findings into practical insights and provide an open-source pipeline.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.337089+00	3	\N	7840479ec77a5677c25bf28771aca50a739fd16ff03f6a7a0d8141da651f4b50	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.337545+00	2026-11-10 14:47:49.634259+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
7e0d2ea8-4142-4e9c-a455-eb9aff8221dc	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/lenfest-ai-collaborative-expansion	The Lenfest Institute grows landmark program with expanded OpenAI support	https://openai.com/index/lenfest-ai-collaborative-expansion	https://openai.com/index/lenfest-ai-collaborative-expansion	\N	OpenAI is expanding the Lenfest AI Collaborative and Fellowship Program with $5 million in funding and up to $5 million in software credits and engineering support.	OpenAI is expanding the Lenfest AI Collaborative and Fellowship Program with $5 million in funding and up to $5 million in software credits and engineering support.	news	en	2026-09-28 07:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.130829+00	3	\N	368192eb5668dee9c3cd205801a2d662e36c7b2cce609d21cae1b8481217a45c	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.131228+00	2026-11-10 14:36:31.825171+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ae339971-bffb-4351-bf26-8afc002a1187	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08800v1	On the Few-Shot Adaptation of Vision-Language Models: A Few Gradient Signs Are All You Need	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08800	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08800	Nairouz Mrabah, Ismail Ben Ayed, Mahdi S. Hosseini, Eric Granger	arXiv:2611.08800v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The limited supervision available for the few-shot adaptation of vision-language models makes gradient estimation particularly noisy. We find that the gradient magnitudes are severely miscalibrated, while the signs of a small top-magnitude subset remain informative. This motivates SignSparseSGD and DynSignSparseSGD, two state-free optimizers that use gradient magnitudes only to select where to update and fixed-magnitude signs to decide how to update. The static variant fixes the support after the first minibatch. The dynamic variant refreshes it at every step. Across 11 datasets, three CLIP backbones, and five shot levels, the proposed methods are competitive with or outperform parameter-efficient and optimization-based baselines on average, while exhibiting markedly lower sensitivity to long training schedules. They introduce no task-specific modules, maintain no first- or second-moment optimizer states, and use one shared configuration across datasets, backbones, and shot levels. Our code is included in the supplementary materials and will be released upon publication.	arXiv:2611.08800v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The limited supervision available for the few-shot adaptation of vision-language models makes gradient estimation particularly noisy. We find that the gradient magnitudes are severely miscalibrated, while the signs of a small top-magnitude subset remain informative. This motivates SignSparseSGD and DynSignSparseSGD, two state-free optimizers that use gradient magnitudes only to select where to update and fixed-magnitude signs to decide how to update. The static variant fixes the support after the first minibatch. The dynamic variant refreshes it at every step. Across 11 datasets, three CLIP backbones, and five shot levels, the proposed methods are competitive with or outperform parameter-efficient and optimization-based baselines on average, while exhibiting markedly lower sensitivity to long training schedules. They introduce no task-specific modules, maintain no first- or second-moment optimizer states, and use one shared configuration across datasets, backbones, and shot levels. Our code is included in the supplementary materials and will be released upon publication.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.339944+00	3	\N	90f1649302fd0f98c4042f0280886cefba820fd68f4dc07d760b79011c807227	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.340335+00	2026-11-10 14:47:52.651156+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2915b7ce-d82a-4e3b-ba65-74defbe9c2d1	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08831v1	Global Solar Siting from Geospatial Embeddings	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08831	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08831	Abdullah Rehman, Quentin Paletta, Rui Song	arXiv:2611.08831v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Global solar photovoltaic (PV) capacity is rapidly growing, helping decrease our emissions and dependence on fossil fuels. However, optimally siting a solar plant remains challenging, requiring anticipation of geographical, urban, and infrastructure needs. Current methods rely on multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) using hand-picked geophysical layers and solar-resource products, however these strategies are not geographically transferable and do not capture the practical requirements of solar sites. We use geospatial embeddings, dense vectors representing geophysical properties of a location, to analyse 38,591 existing solar sites and the land they were built on to learn the latent solar suitability signal. We assess a set of linear and non-linear models and select a soft-voting ensemble of a random forest and a multilayer perceptron (RF+MLP ensemble), chosen for accuracy and large scale deployability. Validated against future installations in thirteen countries, the ensemble separates existing solar sites from random land with a median ROC-AUC of 0.90. Compared with published MCDA strategies, the ensemble increases ROC-AUC by 0.06-0.30 in every country. The ranking also concentrates real deployment: the most-suitable 20% of land holds 84% of estimated generation (annual energy yield) and 67% of installed capacity (potential power output). After applying standard siting constraints, 5.6% of global land (9.8% if cropland is permitted) remains highly suited to solar. The global 10 m resolution solar suitability dataset and code can be found here: https://github.com/Abdullahr29/Global-Solar-Siting.	arXiv:2611.08831v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Global solar photovoltaic (PV) capacity is rapidly growing, helping decrease our emissions and dependence on fossil fuels. However, optimally siting a solar plant remains challenging, requiring anticipation of geographical, urban, and infrastructure needs. Current methods rely on multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) using hand-picked geophysical layers and solar-resource products, however these strategies are not geographically transferable and do not capture the practical requirements of solar sites. We use geospatial embeddings, dense vectors representing geophysical properties of a location, to analyse 38,591 existing solar sites and the land they were built on to learn the latent solar suitability signal. We assess a set of linear and non-linear models and select a soft-voting ensemble of a random forest and a multilayer perceptron (RF+MLP ensemble), chosen for accuracy and large scale deployability. Validated against future installations in thirteen countries, the ensemble separates existing solar sites from random land with a median ROC-AUC of 0.90. Compared with published MCDA strategies, the ensemble increases ROC-AUC by 0.06-0.30 in every country. The ranking also concentrates real deployment: the most-suitable 20% of land holds 84% of estimated generation (annual energy yield) and 67% of installed capacity (potential power output). After applying standard siting constraints, 5.6% of global land (9.8% if cropland is permitted) remains highly suited to solar. The global 10 m resolution solar suitability dataset and code can be found here: https://github.com/Abdullahr29/Global-Solar-Siting.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.342556+00	4	\N	561163286d111a850a07404b663ac61849928edc749e8a7b06d25902a5f3684f	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.343028+00	2026-11-10 14:47:55.982006+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
768d30f4-e489-4910-836a-0ae9f896c32a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09197v1	Safe Reinforcement Learning via Probabilistic Option Shielding	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09197	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09197	Gian Luca Ascari, Isidoro Tamassia, Ying Jiao, Luc De Raedt, Giuseppe Marra	arXiv:2611.09197v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Shielding is a safe reinforcement learning technique that provides safety guarantees while learning, by blocking unsafe actions. Traditional shielding relies on a complete model of the environment to provide long-term guarantees. Probabilistic logic shielding (PLS) lifts the requirement of having such model, but only considers safety in the short term, typically looking only one or few steps ahead. We propose Probabilistic Option Shielding (POS), which extends PLS to the option level, recovering long-horizon safety without reintroducing the need for a complete environment model. POS learns a model-free critic directly from one-step PLS signals, to estimate the probability that an entire option will complete safely under uncertainty. We evaluate POS on safe navigation tasks in the LogiCity environment, and show that it achieves considerably safer training while matching the task performance of standard step-level PLS and other safety related reinforcement learning baselines.	arXiv:2611.09197v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Shielding is a safe reinforcement learning technique that provides safety guarantees while learning, by blocking unsafe actions. Traditional shielding relies on a complete model of the environment to provide long-term guarantees. Probabilistic logic shielding (PLS) lifts the requirement of having such model, but only considers safety in the short term, typically looking only one or few steps ahead. We propose Probabilistic Option Shielding (POS), which extends PLS to the option level, recovering long-horizon safety without reintroducing the need for a complete environment model. POS learns a model-free critic directly from one-step PLS signals, to estimate the probability that an entire option will complete safely under uncertainty. We evaluate POS on safe navigation tasks in the LogiCity environment, and show that it achieves considerably safer training while matching the task performance of standard step-level PLS and other safety related reinforcement learning baselines.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.787275+00	3	\N	a7f96513a4e1da7e786e67df3198962c275bffd99c46d0a4167951748e13b888	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.788046+00	2026-11-10 14:48:12.507077+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
072156f2-ba98-48e4-b4dc-59b36ce388c6	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.23276v3	Exposing the Illusion of Erasure in Knowledge Editing for LLMs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.23276	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.23276	Advik Raj Basani, Anshuman Chhabra	arXiv:2606.23276v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Knowledge Editing (KE) has emerged as a frontier for updating specific facts in LLMs without costly retraining, but its reliability and underlying mechanisms remain poorly understood. In this work, we examine KE from an adversarial elicitation perspective, revealing that edited knowledge is often not fully erased and continues to surface, with consistent failures observed across diverse model architectures. To explain this behavior, we conduct a mechanistic analysis of popular KE methods. We show that low-rank updates do not overwrite existing knowledge but instead redistribute it within the model's representation space. Furthermore, we find that these methods act as targeted suppression mechanisms that reduce the likelihood of expressing original facts, rather than removing them from the model. Analysis of the loss landscape reveals that edited knowledge lies in narrow, anisotropic regions that are highly sensitive to perturbations, making them highly vulnerable to indirect prompting and adversarial attacks. By exposing these profound architectural vulnerabilities, our work proves that KE algorithms are inherently bypassable and motivates a fundamental reevaluation of how we deploy post-hoc updates in several LLM applications.	arXiv:2606.23276v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Knowledge Editing (KE) has emerged as a frontier for updating specific facts in LLMs without costly retraining, but its reliability and underlying mechanisms remain poorly understood. In this work, we examine KE from an adversarial elicitation perspective, revealing that edited knowledge is often not fully erased and continues to surface, with consistent failures observed across diverse model architectures. To explain this behavior, we conduct a mechanistic analysis of popular KE methods. We show that low-rank updates do not overwrite existing knowledge but instead redistribute it within the model's representation space. Furthermore, we find that these methods act as targeted suppression mechanisms that reduce the likelihood of expressing original facts, rather than removing them from the model. Analysis of the loss landscape reveals that edited knowledge lies in narrow, anisotropic regions that are highly sensitive to perturbations, making them highly vulnerable to indirect prompting and adversarial attacks. By exposing these profound architectural vulnerabilities, our work proves that KE algorithms are inherently bypassable and motivates a fundamental reevaluation of how we deploy post-hoc updates in several LLM applications.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.199114+00	4	\N	94a2c3fcd80e710ac9c759bebacd4094b0d5e9b6318508bdb0bc4dc499fba8f5	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.199577+00	2026-11-10 15:28:06.693616+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
62610a58-7651-4fa1-a0fb-b210b95410da	eb530fbb-88dd-42d2-a6be-0b352a46d09a	\N	https://the-decoder.com/?p=42640	Meta launches Muse Ember, a small AI model built for the repetitive work agents actually do	https://the-decoder.com/meta-launches-muse-ember-a-small-ai-model-built-for-the-repetitive-work-agents-actually-do/	https://the-decoder.com/meta-launches-muse-ember-a-small-ai-model-built-for-the-repetitive-work-agents-actually-do/	Matthias Bastian	Meta is introducing Muse Ember, its smallest and most efficient AI model, optimized for repetitive agent tasks such as tool calls, routing, and data extraction. According to Meta, it delivers more intelligence per dollar than Claude Haiku 5.5 or GPT-6 Luna. But it falls short when it comes to actual programming tasks. The article Meta launches Muse Ember, a small AI model built for the repetitive work agents actually do appeared first on The Decoder .	Meta is introducing Muse Ember, its smallest and most efficient AI model, optimized for repetitive agent tasks such as tool calls, routing, and data extraction. According to Meta, it delivers more intelligence per dollar than Claude Haiku 5.5 or GPT-6 Luna. But it falls short when it comes to actual programming tasks. The article Meta launches Muse Ember, a small AI model built for the repetitive work agents actually do appeared first on The Decoder .	product_update	en	2026-11-09 19:41:51+00	2026-11-10 16:29:45.860193+00	3	\N	3eade002bf870c5ed7a24e846795dbe4859ed367fc818eeefd849f8cb969e40e	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:45.860628+00	2026-11-10 16:30:48.776595+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
