d67ed3f6-3a67-47a1-8363-73394906b927	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-mathematical-discovery	GPT-5 and the future of mathematical discovery	https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-mathematical-discovery	https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-mathematical-discovery	\N	UCLA Professor Ernest Ryu and GPT-5 solved a key question in optimization theory, showcasing AI’s role in accelerating mathematical discovery.	UCLA Professor Ernest Ryu and GPT-5 solved a key question in optimization theory, showcasing AI’s role in accelerating mathematical discovery.	news	en	2025-11-24 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:43.417399+00	4	\N	1a7c781a28dfd46a9306e5cd1aa510d50ad9437d31de96e8c0d8f8d4aa6bd00d	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:43.417831+00	2026-11-10 14:09:55.10293+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c3794f4b-693d-4505-9309-11ebfd39b0a7	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.02945v2	Continual Graph Memory and Community Infrastructure for AI-Assisted Mathematical Discovery	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02945	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02945	Eric Hanchen Jiang, Junyi Zhang, Jinxi Yu, Jiachen Lu, Zhi Zhang, Michael Simon, Justin Baker, Richard Cai, Sneha Agarwal, Yubei Li, Hyunsik Chae, Xinjie He, Kyle Zheng, Vigyan Sahai, Ethan Ji, Alexander K Taylor, Yiwen Kou, Kyle Hess, Xiao Liang, Yingnian Wu, J. D. Zamfirescu-Pereira, Andrea Bertozzi, Kai-Wei Chang, Eunice Jun, Raghu Meka, Nanyun Peng, Amit Sahai, Matthew Sottile, Terence Tao, Wei Wang	arXiv:2610.02945v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Mathematical discovery requires preserving partial ideas and the evidence supporting their reuse. We present Ansatz, a research harness whose continual graph memory separates model-accepted claims and dependencies from curated exploration, and Ansatze, a community platform for inspecting, guiding and checking this work. On First Proof, Ansatz recorded nine local verifier closures in each of two ten-problem batches; a partial result in the Second Batch also counted as a pass under the study's criterion. These model-assessed outcomes include continued searches and differ from expert validation. Component studies and re-review expose disagreements and their dependency reach. Open-problem investigations produced candidate results, with five Lean formalizations reported. The platform connects problem discovery, research records, manuscript analysis and formal checking while preserving distinct evidence states. Together, these systems support cumulative human-AI research through persistent investigation and inspectable contributions.	arXiv:2610.02945v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Mathematical discovery requires preserving partial ideas and the evidence supporting their reuse. We present Ansatz, a research harness whose continual graph memory separates model-accepted claims and dependencies from curated exploration, and Ansatze, a community platform for inspecting, guiding and checking this work. On First Proof, Ansatz recorded nine local verifier closures in each of two ten-problem batches; a partial result in the Second Batch also counted as a pass under the study's criterion. These model-assessed outcomes include continued searches and differ from expert validation. Component studies and re-review expose disagreements and their dependency reach. Open-problem investigations produced candidate results, with five Lean formalizations reported. The platform connects problem discovery, research records, manuscript analysis and formal checking while preserving distinct evidence states. Together, these systems support cumulative human-AI research through persistent investigation and inspectable contributions.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.256548+00	4	\N	d2e0902402a33ae47195f6778ba6068999531abaa3eb62d896206f3fb920947a	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.257073+00	2026-11-10 15:44:30.218655+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
231ce7f8-785a-43ce-ac24-cbb150f12ab3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08538v1	Subliminal Learning in Vision-Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08538	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08538	Arman Hatami, Romina Aalishah, Ilya E. Monosov	arXiv:2611.08538v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Subliminal learning is a phenomenon in large language models (LLMs) where a teacher passes a hidden preference to a student through training data unrelated to that preference. We study whether subliminal learning also occurs in vision-language models (VLMs). We finetune a teacher VLM to prefer a specific animal or facial attribute and use a control teacher from the same base model. Both teachers generate numbers for an unrelated visual task involving geometric shapes. Two students initialized from the same base VLM are finetuned on the same images, using numbers generated by either the biased or control teacher. Across VLMs ranging from 2B to 7B parameters, we find that both animal and facial preferences can transfer through this unrelated task. Students trained on biased-teacher outputs select the target more often than control students, with a difference of up to $\\sim$10 percentage points in the animal preference task. Transfer can become stronger with more training data and varies with the target preference, model, and how the numbers are generated. Beyond preferences, we show that the biased student's explanations use more of the same words and phrases as the biased teacher's explanations than those of the control student, even when all three models select the same image. These results matter for training VLMs and show that training on unrelated data may change both a VLM's image preferences and how it explains its choices, and that transfer can become stronger with more training samples.	arXiv:2611.08538v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Subliminal learning is a phenomenon in large language models (LLMs) where a teacher passes a hidden preference to