063a843b-8840-4948-a88e-efaabb2ea2b3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09373v1	Calibrated Uncertainty Quantification on TDC ADMET: What Can Be Resolved Across 22 Datasets?	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09373	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09373	Laurits Fredsgaard, Michael Riis Andersen, Mikkel N. Schmidt	arXiv:2611.09373v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, and toxicity (ADMET) models often rely on small, heterogeneous datasets, yet their predictions may determine which compounds proceed to experimental testing. Useful evaluations must therefore assess both predictive performance and the quality of reported probabilities or intervals. We ask which common uncertainty-quantification methods perform best on the 22 Therapeutics Data Commons (TDC) ADMET datasets and what this benchmark can resolve. We compare eleven calibrated pipelines using nested five-fold cross-validation across ten chemical partitions and five model seeds. Ensemble Monte Carlo (MC) dropout has the best average rank for TDC performance, Brier score, and 90% interval score. For TDC performance, however, it is not separated from the other ensemble pipelines; for Brier and interval score, LightGBM is also not ruled out as best. LightGBM wins more individual datasets than any other method for TDC performance and Brier score, but performs poorly on several endpoints. Smaller evaluations can identify a different leader and often fail to recover the broader comparison. These results make LightGBM and an ensemble neural model complementary baselines. Comparative claims should be checked across multiple chemical partitions using statistical analyses matched to the question being asked.	arXiv:2611.09373v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, and toxicity (ADMET) models often rely on small, heterogeneous datasets, yet their predictions may determine which compounds proceed to experimental testing. Useful evaluations must therefore assess both predictive performance and the quality of reported probabilities or intervals. We ask which common uncertainty-quantification methods perform best on the 22 Therapeutics Data Commons (TDC) ADMET datasets and what this benchmark can resolve. We compare eleven calibrated pipelines using nested five-fold cross-validation across ten chemical partitions and five model seeds. Ensemble Monte Carlo (MC) dropout has the best average rank for TDC performance, Brier score, and 90% interval score. For TDC performance, however, it is not separated from the other ensemble pipelines; for Brier and interval score, LightGBM is also not ruled out as best. LightGBM wins more individual datasets than any other method for TDC performance and Brier score, but performs poorly on several endpoints. Smaller evaluations can identify a different leader and often fail to recover the broader comparison. These results make LightGBM and an ensemble neural model complementary baselines. Comparative claims should be checked across multiple chemical partitions using statistical analyses matched to the question being asked.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.525017+00	3	\N	88a319c59afdfb7ccc2f588839bb6c706be6f8e0b5b2dbec9836e5b191935c2e	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.52547+00	2026-11-10 14:51:20.753179+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
088a7ccc-4601-49ec-94ee-2a4b70dfeb56	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09388v1	k-Volume: a Unified Active Learning Strategy	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09388	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09388	Eliot Tron, Alain Celisse, Christophe Denis, Alice Le Brigant	arXiv:2611.09388v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In the classification context where revealing the label of a sample is costly, we propose a new Active Learning (AL) strategy named k-Volume (kVol). It randomly selects a sample to be labeled according to the volume of the smallest ball containing its k nearest neighbors. kVol is highly versatile: it can be applied either in the input space or in the output space, and it enjoys the desirable properties of the three main families of AL strategies: density, diversity, and (when performed in the output space) uncertainty. From a theoretical perspective, we establish that kVol is asymptotically equivalent to uniform sampling over the support of the (input or output) data distribution. Based on experiments on various synthetic and real-life tabular datasets and images, we observe that kVol is surprisingly stable: whatever the classifier or the experimental setup, it still lies among the best AL strategies unlike other competitors that fail dramatically. In particular, kVol outperforms state-of-the-art competitors such as CoreSet, TypiClust or Uncertainty on several real-life datasets, at a low computational cost.	arXiv:2611.09388v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In the classification context where revealing the label of a sample is costly, we propose a new Active Learning (AL) strategy named k-Volume (kVol). It randomly selects a sample to be labeled according to the volume of the smallest ball containing its k nearest neighbors. kVol is highly versatile: it can be applied either in the input space or in the output space, and it enjoys the desirable properties of the three main families of AL strategies: density, diversity, and (when performed in the output space) uncertainty. From a theoretical perspective, we establish that kVol is asymptotically equivalent to uniform sampling over the support of the (input or output) data distribution. Based on experiments on various synthetic and real-life tabular datasets and images, we observe that kVol is surprisingly stable: whatever the classifier or the experimental setup, it still lies among the best AL strategies unlike other competitors that fail dramatically. In particular, kVol outperforms state-of-the-art competitors such as CoreSet, TypiClust or Uncertainty on several real-life datasets, at a low computational cost.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.527646+00	3	\N	f5a07f454f4e83b20b04dc9bf7f806b6deb7c9be084428a33fc6d5bf0027b2cf	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.528024+00	2026-11-10 14:51:24.808083+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1e7eb49e-c63f-498d-a7b8-a20cdb5efbb6	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08669v1	ChainFlow: Few-Step Latent Flow Matching for Speculative Decoding	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08669	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08669	Selim Aktas, Yusuf Erdem Erdem, Ahmed Zeer, Himmet Toprak Kesgin, Muzaffer Kaan Yuce, Mehmet Fatih Amasyali	arXiv:2611.08669v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies. We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states. ChainFlow treats drafting as a short transport over hidden states, needing one or two flow steps instead of the many denoising steps of generation from noise. A variational autoencoder compresses these states so drafting cost barely grows with target size, lightweight feedback heads restore inter-token dependencies, and tree drafting raises acceptance. On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively. These gains further widen as drafters are adapted to new languages sequentially. Our ablations cover drafter design and concurrent serving, and we open-source our code and drafter models.	arXiv:2611.08669v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies. We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states. ChainFlow treats drafting as a short transport over hidden states, needing one or two flow steps instead of the many denoising steps of generation from noise. A variational autoencoder compresses these states so drafting cost barely grows with target size, lightweight feedback heads restore inter-token dependencies, and tree drafting raises acceptance. On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively. These gains further widen as drafters are adapted to new languages sequentially. Our ablations cover drafter design and concurrent serving, and we open-source our code and drafter models.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.617789+00	4	\N	056c8b1eb96a8c7b19f2122b8ef21c25048c8773ef25ba377fe7ce99f50b4ee3	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.618308+00	2026-11-10 15:45:04.690431+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
06b05739-aa4d-4272-acca-d4ac7ee9b528	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09424v1	MINE: Entropy-coded Memory-efficient Inference for Large Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09424	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09424	Emil Sch\\"atzle, Markus P\\"uschel	arXiv:2611.09424v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We present MINE (Memory-efficient INference Engine), a lossless entropy coding approach that accelerates single-stream inference of Large Language Models (LLMs), where existing lossless compression methods yield speedups only for specific data types such as \\texttt{BF16} and under additional assumptions. Single-stream decoding is strongly memory-bound, since every weight must be streamed from memory once per generated token and requests cannot be batched in local or low-latency deployments. At the core of MINE is dtANS-LLM, a GPU entropy coding scheme adapted from the prior dtANS to the needs of LLM inference. It exploits the non-uniform distribution of weights, which persists even after quantization, and exposes hyper-parameters that trade compression ratio against decoding cost. We auto-tune these parameters for each tensor and GPU to obtain the Pareto front between model size and decoding latency. MINE integrates into vLLM as a plugin and selects the best kernel configuration for a given memory budget. On Llama~3.1~8B~Instruct with a 2.1-bit EntQuant checkpoint, MINE achieves speedups of 2.52$\\times$ and 1.48$\\times$ over the fastest baseline on an RTX~4060~Ti and an RTX~5090, at effective bitrates of 2.24 and 3.43~bits per weight. MINE is available as open source.	arXiv:2611.09424v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We present MINE (Memory-efficient INference Engine), a lossless entropy coding approach that accelerates single-stream inference of Large Language Models (LLMs), where existing lossless compression methods yield speedups only for specific data types such as \\texttt{BF16} and under additional assumptions. Single-stream decoding is strongly memory-bound, since every weight must be streamed from memory once per generated token and requests cannot be batched in local or low-latency deployments. At the core of MINE is dtANS-LLM, a GPU entropy coding scheme adapted from the prior dtANS to the needs of LLM inference. It exploits the non-uniform distribution of weights, which persists even after quantization, and exposes hyper-parameters that trade compression ratio against decoding cost. We auto-tune these parameters for each tensor and GPU to obtain the Pareto front between model size and decoding latency. MINE integrates into vLLM as a plugin and selects the best kernel configuration for a given memory budget. On Llama~3.1~8B~Instruct with a 2.1-bit EntQuant checkpoint, MINE achieves speedups of 2.52$\\times$ and 1.48$\\times$ over the fastest baseline on an RTX~4060~Ti and an RTX~5090, at effective bitrates of 2.24 and 3.43~bits per weight. MINE is available as open source.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.53528+00	4	\N	0d07eb89077f1c53077cabb292a97d866f5fbf4613a6fa525f5d57649f68a7b8	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.535774+00	2026-11-10 14:51:35.258652+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2177f889-4afd-4501-8283-8a69a690c409	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09433v1	DexDICE: Reconciling Safety and Performance via Dual-Expert Offline Imitation Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09433	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09433	Seokin Seo, Sung Kuk Shyn, Minji Seo, Yisoo Lee, Jongmin Lee, Hongseok Yang, Kee-Eung Kim	arXiv:2611.09433v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Ensuring high returns while providing strong safety guarantees in offline imitation learning (IL) is a fundamental challenge: existing approaches either rely on explicitly specified cost functions, which are difficult to design for complex real-world safety constraints, or require expert demonstrations that are both safe and high-performing, which are often too costly to collect. We introduce dual-expert imitation learning, which leverages two complementary data sources: (i) safety-compliant demonstrations that faithfully follow safety guidelines but may be suboptimal, and (ii) performance-oriented demonstrations that achieve high returns but may violate safety guidelines. To address this problem, we propose DexDICE (Dual-expert offline imitation learning via stationary DIstribution Correction Estimation). Instead of conventional distribution constraints, DexDICE combines DICE-style offline learning with safe support constraints, enabling agents to exploit high-return behaviors while remaining within safety-compliant regions. We prove that the ideal solution satisfying this constraint exactly has zero unsafe-region visitation with high probability. We evaluate DexDICE on a re-curated DSRL benchmark and validate it on a real-world mobile-robot navigation task trained directly from human teleoperation demonstrations, where it substantially outperforms baselines and remains robust to data scarcity and quality degradation.	arXiv:2611.09433v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Ensuring high returns while providing strong safety guarantees in offline imitation learning (IL) is a fundamental challenge: existing approaches either rely on explicitly specified cost functions, which are difficult to design for complex real-world safety constraints, or require expert demonstrations that are both safe and high-performing, which are often too costly to collect. We introduce dual-expert imitation learning, which leverages two complementary data sources: (i) safety-compliant demonstrations that faithfully follow safety guidelines but may be suboptimal, and (ii) performance-oriented demonstrations that achieve high returns but may violate safety guidelines. To address this problem, we propose DexDICE (Dual-expert offline imitation learning via stationary DIstribution Correction Estimation). Instead of conventional distribution constraints, DexDICE combines DICE-style offline learning with safe support constraints, enabling agents to exploit high-return behaviors while remaining within safety-compliant regions. We prove that the ideal solution satisfying this constraint exactly has zero unsafe-region visitation with high probability. We evaluate DexDICE on a re-curated DSRL benchmark and validate it on a real-world mobile-robot navigation task trained directly from human teleoperation demonstrations, where it substantially outperforms baselines and remains robust to data scarcity and quality degradation.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.53918+00	4	\N	e8242c59c1131dbed5a2960530199a00f9bc86128202525d1c41e6849c3e76fd	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.539606+00	2026-11-10 14:51:38.474314+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a7dbd910-ab01-4cd8-9771-aad3a23b3c4b	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/devday-2026-recap	DevDay 2026 Recap	https://openai.com/index/devday-2026-recap	https://openai.com/index/devday-2026-recap	\N	Explore more than 20 announcements from OpenAI DevDay 2026, including GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT, Codex, APIs, security, and new tools for builders.	Explore more than 20 announcements from OpenAI DevDay 2026, including GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT, Codex, APIs, security, and new tools for builders.	news	en	2026-09-29 10:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.122218+00	5	\N	6eb530af650a68c5f128e874c46ec1066143bbfc221d495feb0ec3a091df38a0	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.122583+00	2026-11-10 14:36:42.763957+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a12cd229-1f49-4b6b-8969-3d6c92d4193e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.07297v2	Spectrum-Adaptive Generalization Bounds for Trained Deep Transformers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07297	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07297	Mana Sakai, Masaaki Imaizumi	arXiv:2605.07297v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Understanding why trained Transformers generalize well is a fundamental problem in modern machine learning theory, and complexity-based generalization bounds provide a principled way to study this question. While existing norm-based bounds for Transformers remove the explicit polynomial dependence on the hidden dimension, they typically impose fixed norm constraints specified a priori and can exhibit unfavorable exponential dependence on depth. In this paper, we derive spectrum-adaptive post hoc generalization bounds for multi-layer Transformers. Under layerwise spectral norm control, the bounds are expressed in terms of layerwise Schatten quantities of the query-key, value, and feedforward weight matrices. Since the Schatten indices need not be fixed a priori and can instead be selected after training, separately for each matrix type and layer, the bounds adaptively trade off spectral complexity against the dimension- and depth-dependent factors according to the learned singular-value profiles. Empirical comparisons of BERT-adapted proxies for the leading complexity factors suggest that the proxies induced by our bounds grow more slowly with depth and hidden dimension than the corresponding norm-based proxies. Overall, our results provide a complexity-based perspective on how the spectral structure of trained Transformers is reflected in generalization analyses.	arXiv:2605.07297v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Understanding why trained Transformers generalize well is a fundamental problem in modern machine learning theory, and complexity-based generalization bounds provide a principled way to study this question. While existing norm-based bounds for Transformers remove the explicit polynomial dependence on the hidden dimension, they typically impose fixed norm constraints specified a priori and can exhibit unfavorable exponential dependence on depth. In this paper, we derive spectrum-adaptive post hoc generalization bounds for multi-layer Transformers. Under layerwise spectral norm control, the bounds are expressed in terms of layerwise Schatten quantities of the query-key, value, and feedforward weight matrices. Since the Schatten indices need not be fixed a priori and can instead be selected after training, separately for each matrix type and layer, the bounds adaptively trade off spectral complexity against the dimension- and depth-dependent factors according to the learned singular-value profiles. Empirical comparisons of BERT-adapted proxies for the leading complexity factors suggest that the proxies induced by our bounds grow more slowly with depth and hidden dimension than the corresponding norm-based proxies. Overall, our results provide a complexity-based perspective on how the spectral structure of trained Transformers is reflected in generalization analyses.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.438341+00	3	\N	7603440459ae7033cfee3d34b8dcaaa768a2202c5ff7be4f6a78fe1e6dbb7afc	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.438745+00	2026-11-10 15:30:51.051395+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9fa1eca8-5f40-402f-8b02-3ade8e2d9a5a	c9402267-ab70-45fa-b843-f47b6189bd14	\N	https://research.google/blog/a-connectomics-milestone-mapping-the-complete-male-fruit-fly-brain/	A connectomics milestone: Mapping the complete male fruit fly brain	https://research.google/blog/a-connectomics-milestone-mapping-the-complete-male-fruit-fly-brain/	https://research.google/blog/a-connectomics-milestone-mapping-the-complete-male-fruit-fly-brain/	\N	General Science	General Science	research	en	2026-09-03 16:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.006991+00	4	\N	ebe99148751da44a8e23b691bc08ade059012fb146a8765a8c3e195a9ff9478d	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.007387+00	2026-11-10 14:33:34.446458+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
79dfb1dc-f534-437c-b0dd-89c52aaba998	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2509.04899v5	Encoding of musical structures in hidden units of restricted Boltzmann machines	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04899	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04899	Mutsumi Kobayashi, Hiroshi Watanabe	arXiv:2509.04899v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Restricted Boltzmann machines (RBMs) are energy-based models originating from statistical physics. In this study, we investigate how musical structures are represented in the hidden layer of a Bernoulli-Bernoulli RBM trained on binary piano-roll representations of J.~S.~Bach's music. By examining the visible patterns induced by individual hidden units, we find that the hidden layer primarily captures local temporal and pitch-statistical features rather than explicit musical concepts such as melodies, chords, or keys. Free-energy comparisons show that the trained RBM assigns higher probabilities to the examined musical inputs than to digit or noise patterns. Analysis using t-SNE, cosine similarity, and PCA reveals that hidden representations are sensitive to absolute pitch and input overlap, with transposed versions of the same piece not necessarily mapped close together. Generated samples exhibit local pitch organization but limited long-range coherence. These findings illustrate how a simple statistical-physics model captures certain regularities in structured musical data while highlighting its limitations in representing higher-level musical relationships.	arXiv:2509.04899v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Restricted Boltzmann machines (RBMs) are energy-based models originating from statistical physics. In this study, we investigate how musical structures are represented in the hidden layer of a Bernoulli-Bernoulli RBM trained on binary piano-roll representations of J.~S.~Bach's music. By examining the visible patterns induced by individual hidden units, we find that the hidden layer primarily captures local temporal and pitch-statistical features rather than explicit musical concepts such as melodies, chords, or keys. Free-energy comparisons show that the trained RBM assigns higher probabilities to the examined musical inputs than to digit or noise patterns. Analysis using t-SNE, cosine similarity, and PCA reveals that hidden representations are sensitive to absolute pitch and input overlap, with transposed versions of the same piece not necessarily mapped close together. Generated samples exhibit local pitch organization but limited long-range coherence. These findings illustrate how a simple statistical-physics model captures certain regularities in structured musical data while highlighting its limitations in representing higher-level musical relationships.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.355054+00	3	\N	93e86563ee035e9b31f2fd40ee9dfe9999520a171b4ea6d7c32fbd3f23f84c0d	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.35547+00	2026-11-10 15:30:54.141008+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9799410e-c1c3-46e1-a4e7-f21b99ba344c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09496v1	Mosaic:montage-agnostic self-distilled graph learning for subject-independent ieeg seizure detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09496	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09496	Arya Jalali, Mahsa Shoaran	arXiv:2611.09496v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Intracranial EEG provides high signal-to-noise ratio and spatial resolution for detecting epileptic activity, but patient-specific electrode placement leads to substantial variation in channel count and montage across subjects. This heterogeneity limits the generalization of existing detectors under subject-independent evaluation. We propose MOSAIC (MOntage-agnostic Self-distilled Adaptive Inter-channel Connectivity learning), a lightweight graph-learning framework for subject-independent iEEG seizure detection. MOSAIC pairs a shared convolutional channel encoder with a patient-agnostic channel-attention module that infers a weighted inter-channel adjacency matrix from the encoded features, requiring no fixed montage graph. An exponential moving average self-distillation objective regularizes each prediction toward a slowly updated per-patient teacher. Across four public iEEG corpora (SWEC-ETHZ long- and short-term, Mayo, FNUSA), MOSAIC outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in F2 and Cohen's $\\kappa$ while learning more stable inter-channel adjacencies, demonstrating that self-distilled connectivity learning transfers across unseen implantations.	arXiv:2611.09496v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Intracranial EEG provides high signal-to-noise ratio and spatial resolution for detecting epileptic activity, but patient-specific electrode placement leads to substantial variation in channel count and montage across subjects. This heterogeneity limits the generalization of existing detectors under subject-independent evaluation. We propose MOSAIC (MOntage-agnostic Self-distilled Adaptive Inter-channel Connectivity learning), a lightweight graph-learning framework for subject-independent iEEG seizure detection. MOSAIC pairs a shared convolutional channel encoder with a patient-agnostic channel-attention module that infers a weighted inter-channel adjacency matrix from the encoded features, requiring no fixed montage graph. An exponential moving average self-distillation objective regularizes each prediction toward a slowly updated per-patient teacher. Across four public iEEG corpora (SWEC-ETHZ long- and short-term, Mayo, FNUSA), MOSAIC outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in F2 and Cohen's $\\kappa$ while learning more stable inter-channel adjacencies, demonstrating that self-distilled connectivity learning transfers across unseen implantations.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.549391+00	4	\N	907a7ba12105c8af5f61f39d8ebbfa2b8d75e2da792046f9db52beed20fd000a	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.549804+00	2026-11-10 14:51:53.943047+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
29fd04e0-5bf4-4eb8-8a65-b2c1d8fc2215	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2509.22738v4	Enabling Approximate Joint Sampling in Diffusion LMs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22738	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22738	Parikshit Bansal, Sujay Sanghavi	arXiv:2509.22738v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: In autoregressive language models, each token is sampled by conditioning on all the past tokens; the overall string has thus been sampled from the correct underlying joint distribution represented by the model. In contrast, masked diffusion language models generate text by unmasking tokens out of order and potentially in parallel. Generating an overall string sampled from the correct underlying joint distribution would (again) require exactly one token unmasking in every full-model forward pass. The more tokens unmasked in parallel, the further away the string is from the true joint; this can be seen in the resulting drop in accuracy (but, increase in speed). In this paper we devise a way to {\\em approximately} sample multiple tokens from the joint distribution in a single full-model forward pass; we do so by developing a new lightweight single-layer ``sampler" on top of an existing large diffusion LM. One forward pass of the full model can now be followed by multiple forward passes of only this sampler layer, to yield multiple unmasked tokens. Our sampler is trained to mimic exact joint sampling from the (frozen) full model. We show the effectiveness of our approximate joint sampling for both pretrained-only (Dream-7B-Base, Llada-7B-Base) and instruction-tuned (Dream-7B-Instruct, Dream-7B-Coder) models on language modeling and math \\& coding tasks. When four tokens are unmasked for each full-model denoising step, our sampling algorithm achieves a MAUVE score of 0.87 (vs marginal baseline of 0.31) with respect to the true joint distribution.	arXiv:2509.22738v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: In autoregressive language models, each token is sampled by conditioning on all the past tokens; the overall string has thus been sampled from the correct underlying joint distribution represented by the model. In contrast, masked diffusion language models generate text by unmasking tokens out of order and potentially in parallel. Generating an overall string sampled from the correct underlying joint distribution would (again) require exactly one token unmasking in every full-model forward pass. The more tokens unmasked in parallel, the further away the string is from the true joint; this can be seen in the resulting drop in accuracy (but, increase in speed). In this paper we devise a way to {\\em approximately} sample multiple tokens from the joint distribution in a single full-model forward pass; we do so by developing a new lightweight single-layer ``sampler" on top of an existing large diffusion LM. One forward pass of the full model can now be followed by multiple forward passes of only this sampler layer, to yield multiple unmasked tokens. Our sampler is trained to mimic exact joint sampling from the (frozen) full model. We show the effectiveness of our approximate joint sampling for both pretrained-only (Dream-7B-Base, Llada-7B-Base) and instruction-tuned (Dream-7B-Instruct, Dream-7B-Coder) models on language modeling and math \\& coding tasks. When four tokens are unmasked for each full-model denoising step, our sampling algorithm achieves a MAUVE score of 0.87 (vs marginal baseline of 0.31) with respect to the true joint distribution.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.35787+00	4	\N	2573abe882a68eb4178aa2997a301b633f2bd997f562dea3a66351f89ee05b92	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.358294+00	2026-11-10 15:30:57.571456+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
76994a6b-3327-4007-aa1e-9c645ba1ae6f	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-6-1-sol	Introducing GPT-6.1 Sol	https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-6-1-sol	https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-6-1-sol	\N	Meet GPT-6.1 Sol: near-Astra intelligence for coding, computer use, and professional work at one-fifth of Astra’s standard API input and output token prices.	Meet GPT-6.1 Sol: near-Astra intelligence for coding, computer use, and professional work at one-fifth of Astra’s standard API input and output token prices.	product_update	en	2026-09-29 17:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.119379+00	4	\N	d1517ea2f2a29007dca93a51e75a11966e1b3df0b7137912bf9bec88751314d0	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.119749+00	2026-11-10 14:36:46.024316+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1da2bc3e-4694-4b08-a7e7-9002800c1c0c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2510.02789v3	Align Your Query: Representation Alignment for Multimodality Medical Object Detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02789	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02789	Ara Seo, Bryan Sangwoo Kim, Hyungjin Chung, Jong Chul Ye	arXiv:2510.02789v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Medical object detection suffers when a single detector is trained on mixed medical modalities (e.g., CXR, CT, MRI) due to heterogeneous statistics and disjoint representation spaces. To address this challenge, we turn to representation alignment, an approach that has proven effective for bringing features from different sources into a shared space. Specifically, we target the representations of DETR-style object queries and propose a simple, detector-agnostic framework to align them with modality context. First, we define modality tokens: compact, text-derived embeddings encoding imaging modality that are lightweight and require no extra annotations. We integrate the modality tokens into the detection process via Multimodality Context Attention (MoCA), mixing object-query representations via self-attention to propagate modality context within the query set. This preserves DETR-style architectures and adds negligible latency while injecting modality cues into object queries. We further introduce QueryREPA, a short pretraining stage that aligns query representations to their modality tokens using a task-specific contrastive objective with modality-balanced batches. Together, MoCA and QueryREPA produce modality-aware, class-faithful queries that transfer effectively to downstream training. Across diverse modalities trained altogether, the proposed approach consistently improves AP with minimal overhead and no architectural modifications, offering a practical path toward robust multimodality medical object detection.	arXiv:2510.02789v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Medical object detection suffers when a single detector is trained on mixed medical modalities (e.g., CXR, CT, MRI) due to heterogeneous statistics and disjoint representation spaces. To address this challenge, we turn to representation alignment, an approach that has proven effective for bringing features from different sources into a shared space. Specifically, we target the representations of DETR-style object queries and propose a simple, detector-agnostic framework to align them with modality context. First, we define modality tokens: compact, text-derived embeddings encoding imaging modality that are lightweight and require no extra annotations. We integrate the modality tokens into the detection process via Multimodality Context Attention (MoCA), mixing object-query representations via self-attention to propagate modality context within the query set. This preserves DETR-style architectures and adds negligible latency while injecting modality cues into object queries. We further introduce QueryREPA, a short pretraining stage that aligns query representations to their modality tokens using a task-specific contrastive objective with modality-balanced batches. Together, MoCA and QueryREPA produce modality-aware, class-faithful queries that transfer effectively to downstream training. Across diverse modalities trained altogether, the proposed approach consistently improves AP with minimal overhead and no architectural modifications, offering a practical path toward robust multimodality medical object detection.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.361812+00	4	\N	9744709a87b2624d8806ca86a4aad259920125283292d1aba58c3e5e00a3e596	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.362495+00	2026-11-10 15:31:00.820118+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
fa788bc2-c016-475a-98cf-b689f3fa6758	c9402267-ab70-45fa-b843-f47b6189bd14	\N	https://research.google/blog/transfer-learning-for-genomic-prediction-in-underrepresented-populations/	Transfer learning for genomic prediction in underrepresented populations	https://research.google/blog/transfer-learning-for-genomic-prediction-in-underrepresented-populations/	https://research.google/blog/transfer-learning-for-genomic-prediction-in-underrepresented-populations/	\N	General Science	General Science	research	en	2026-09-03 18:20:31+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.004245+00	4	\N	633695fecb9a95d4e146c36b3c1ae7df7b61f9513d40bb460fccc4977166531a	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.004757+00	2026-11-10 14:33:36.887565+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
98c25723-babd-4e83-9e58-e24b2a7cf334	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2510.12082v2	Enhancing Neural Code Representation with Additional Context	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12082	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12082	Huy Nguyen, Christoph Treude, Patanamon Thongtanunam	arXiv:2510.12082v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Automated program comprehension underpins many software engineering tasks, from code summarisation to clone detection. While recent deep learning models have achieved strong results, they typically rely on source code alone, overlooking contextual signals such as version history or structural relationships. We conduct an empirical study of how enriching code representations with such signals affects model performance on four tasks: code clone detection, code classification, code summarisation, and vulnerability detection, using three datasets (SeSaMe, CodeSearchNet-Java, Vul4J). We evaluate five foundational code models (CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, CodeT5, PLBART, ASTNN) and seven code-specialised LLMs (CodeLlama, Qwen2.5-Coder, DeepSeek-Coder), comparing prompt-based context injection against fine-tuned use of context. Context improves classification for all five foundational models and, after fine-tuning, for most LLMs. For summarisation, the effect depends on the model family: version history improves all four foundational models on every metric, whereas prompt-level context helps two of the seven LLMs and hurts the other five. Combining context types reaches up to +21.48% macro-F1 in code classification and +15.04% F1 in clone detection. A human evaluation of CodeT5 summaries on 100 Java snippets ranks context-augmented summaries significantly higher for Accuracy and Content Adequacy. An exploratory vulnerability detection evaluation provides initial evidence of transfer to security-oriented tasks.	arXiv:2510.12082v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Automated program comprehension underpins many software engineering tasks, from code summarisation to clone detection. While recent deep learning models have achieved strong results, they typically rely on source code alone, overlooking contextual signals such as version history or structural relationships. We conduct an empirical study of how enriching code representations with such signals affects model performance on four tasks: code clone detection, code classification, code summarisation, and vulnerability detection, using three datasets (SeSaMe, CodeSearchNet-Java, Vul4J). We evaluate five foundational code models (CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, CodeT5, PLBART, ASTNN) and seven code-specialised LLMs (CodeLlama, Qwen2.5-Coder, DeepSeek-Coder), comparing prompt-based context injection against fine-tuned use of context. Context improves classification for all five foundational models and, after fine-tuning, for most LLMs. For summarisation, the effect depends on the model family: version history improves all four foundational models on every metric, whereas prompt-level context helps two of the seven LLMs and hurts the other five. Combining context types reaches up to +21.48% macro-F1 in code classification and +15.04% F1 in clone detection. A human evaluation of CodeT5 summaries on 100 Java snippets ranks context-augmented summaries significantly higher for Accuracy and Content Adequacy. An exploratory vulnerability detection evaluation provides initial evidence of transfer to security-oriented tasks.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.501821+00	4	\N	3d898f0a34179fabb8558df228c2c8c85b5177a0be1a4cdf84caa2717896bd2e	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.502227+00	2026-11-10 16:03:00.388043+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
af8362a3-a5f4-4baf-be91-7bea1b0d4124	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09560v1	Solver-Free Decision-Focused Learning via Neural Optimization Layers for Edge Selection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09560	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09560	Xinda Zheng, Frits de Nijs, Edward Lam	arXiv:2611.09560v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Decision-focused learning trains prediction models to improve downstream decisions, but many methods require costly solver calls or optimal-solution labels. We propose a solver-free decision-focused learning framework for NP-hard edge-selection problems with uncertain parameters in the objective, the constraints, or both. A neural optimization layer is first trained without solution labels, using problem objectives and constraint penalties. Graph-informed edge scoring and iterative refinement encourage concentrated and structurally consistent continuous decisions. The layer is then frozen, and a prediction model is trained through its continuous output using a problem-specific loss evaluated under observed parameters. At deployment, the predictor, optimization layer, and a lightweight decoder produce decisions without solving the original NP-hard problem. Experiments on the asymmetric traveling salesperson and endpoint-free orienteering problems show consistently better decision quality than prediction-focused learning and improvements over most evaluated decision-focused baselines. Our framework requires less training time than the evaluated decision-focused baselines, even when optimization-layer pretraining is included. Neural deployment also produces decisions faster than solver-based deployment, offering a better quality-efficiency trade-off. The layer also reduces training cost in existing decision-focused methods when used as an approximate optimization oracle.	arXiv:2611.09560v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Decision-focused learning trains prediction models to improve downstream decisions, but many methods require costly solver calls or optimal-solution labels. We propose a solver-free decision-focused learning framework for NP-hard edge-selection problems with uncertain parameters in the objective, the constraints, or both. A neural optimization layer is first trained without solution labels, using problem objectives and constraint penalties. Graph-informed edge scoring and iterative refinement encourage concentrated and structurally consistent continuous decisions. The layer is then frozen, and a prediction model is trained through its continuous output using a problem-specific loss evaluated under observed parameters. At deployment, the predictor, optimization layer, and a lightweight decoder produce decisions without solving the original NP-hard problem. Experiments on the asymmetric traveling salesperson and endpoint-free orienteering problems show consistently better decision quality than prediction-focused learning and improvements over most evaluated decision-focused baselines. Our framework requires less training time than the evaluated decision-focused baselines, even when optimization-layer pretraining is included. Neural deployment also produces decisions faster than solver-based deployment, offering a better quality-efficiency trade-off. The layer also reduces training cost in existing decision-focused methods when used as an approximate optimization oracle.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.563749+00	4	\N	3a28bfb1bfb29925fcca59048a63a00140454922086e951d667238db1ca7a490	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.564328+00	2026-11-10 14:52:10.600247+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
532226d5-e683-4970-9ea3-2945db0d878c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09596v1	Coordinatewise Epsilon for Adam under Heavy-Tailed Noise	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09596	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09596	Jeonseong Kim, Chenglin Fan	arXiv:2611.09596v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Adam estimates second moments coordinatewise, but typically adds the same scalar $\\varepsilon$ to every denominator. We study how this shared $\\varepsilon$ affects convergence when noise and curvature vary across coordinates. Under coordinatewise finite-$p$ noise moments, $p\\in(1,2]$, choosing $\\varepsilon_i$ from the noise and curvature bounds for each coordinate attains the heavy-tailed $\\ell_1$-stationarity benchmark $O(T^{-\\frac{p-1}{3p-2}})$, matching the known optimal time exponent for smooth nonconvex stochastic optimization. For $1 <2$, Adam with a shared $\\varepsilon_t$ admits a lower bound of order $\\Omega(T^{-\\frac{p-1}{2p}})$. This lower bound holds for every predictable scalar choice of $\\eta_t$, $\\beta_{1,t}$, $\\beta_{2,t}$, and $\\varepsilon_t$, precluding a dimension-uniform benchmark rate for this class.	arXiv:2611.09596v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Adam estimates second moments coordinatewise, but typically adds the same scalar $\\varepsilon$ to every denominator. We study how this shared $\\varepsilon$ affects convergence when noise and curvature vary across coordinates. Under coordinatewise finite-$p$ noise moments, $p\\in(1,2]$, choosing $\\varepsilon_i$ from the noise and curvature bounds for each coordinate attains the heavy-tailed $\\ell_1$-stationarity benchmark $O(T^{-\\frac{p-1}{3p-2}})$, matching the known optimal time exponent for smooth nonconvex stochastic optimization. For $1 <2$, Adam with a shared $\\varepsilon_t$ admits a lower bound of order $\\Omega(T^{-\\frac{p-1}{2p}})$. This lower bound holds for every predictable scalar choice of $\\eta_t$, $\\beta_{1,t}$, $\\beta_{2,t}$, and $\\varepsilon_t$, precluding a dimension-uniform benchmark rate for this class.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.566761+00	3	\N	e6417d5aac3419cfb4f6bfc9ed8554de6dbb47987095806d1ca4a57ab46d98b3	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.567215+00	2026-11-10 14:52:13.540378+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c41bb1cd-ff55-4558-b69d-b813947e1411	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2511.20798v3	Physics Steering: Causal Control of Cross-Domain Concepts in a Physics Foundation Model	https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20798	https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20798	Rio Alexa Fear, Payel Mukhopadhyay, Michael McCabe, Alberto Bietti, Miles Cranmer	arXiv:2511.20798v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Foundation models trained on text and images have been shown to develop abstract internal features that align with human concepts and that can be directly manipulated via activation steering to alter model behaviour. Whether scientific foundation models learn similarly abstract and domain-general representations has remained an open question. Inspired by recent work identifying single directions in activation space that control complex behaviours in large language models, we show that Walrus, a large physics foundation model, learns linearly steerable representations of physical phenomena. By computing the difference between activations representing contrasting physical regimes, we identify single directions in activation space that correspond to vorticity, diffusion, and even temporal progression. We find that injecting these concept directions back into the model during inference enables fine-grained causal control: vortices can be induced or removed, diffusion enhanced or suppressed, and simulations sped up or slowed down. Moreover, directions derived from one physical system also steer the behaviour of other physical systems, suggesting that they are domain-general. These results provide early evidence that scientific foundation models can learn general representations of physical principles, and further support for the Linear Representation Hypothesis.	arXiv:2511.20798v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Foundation models trained on text and images have been shown to develop abstract internal features that align with human concepts and that can be directly manipulated via activation steering to alter model behaviour. Whether scientific foundation models learn similarly abstract and domain-general representations has remained an open question. Inspired by recent work identifying single directions in activation space that control complex behaviours in large language models, we show that Walrus, a large physics foundation model, learns linearly steerable representations of physical phenomena. By computing the difference between activations representing contrasting physical regimes, we identify single directions in activation space that correspond to vorticity, diffusion, and even temporal progression. We find that injecting these concept directions back into the model during inference enables fine-grained causal control: vortices can be induced or removed, diffusion enhanced or suppressed, and simulations sped up or slowed down. Moreover, directions derived from one physical system also steer the behaviour of other physical systems, suggesting that they are domain-general. These results provide early evidence that scientific foundation models can learn general representations of physical principles, and further support for the Linear Representation Hypothesis.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.509816+00	4	\N	b6ffc61249fb7eb1edfc5f37c93f8de246c9712c252f28f6d96ab08ea62fa7ab	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.510231+00	2026-11-10 16:03:11.746834+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2dacfd27-6310-46ca-9b07-193d3c2feec2	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.14885v3	Embedding stochastic dynamics in latent manifolds of recurrent neural networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14885	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14885	Ram\\'on Nartallo-Kaluarachchi, Renaud Lambiotte, Alain Goriely	arXiv:2602.14885v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) provide a theoretical framework for understanding computation in biological neural circuits through their ability to support rich time-dependent behaviour. Whilst high-dimensional by construction, they can be used to encode low-dimensional dynamical systems, for example by restricting to low-rank connectivity, hence mirroring the emergence of latent neural manifolds found in empirical neural data. Here, we exploit a relationship between RNNs and a class of universal approximators to train networks that can represent arbitrary, nonlinear stochastic differential equations (SDEs), with given drift and diffusion coefficients, within a low-dimensional latent subspace. We show that RNNs can faithfully embed the drift and diffusion of a given SDE, including nonlinear and nonequilibrium dynamics such as chaotic attractors. We apply this approach by constructing RNN realisations of stochastic systems that transiently explore various attractors through both input-driven switching and autonomous transitions driven by nonequilibrium currents, which we interpret as models of associative and sequential (episodic) memory. To elucidate how these dynamics are encoded in the network, we introduce decompositions of the RNN based on its asymmetric connectivity and its time-irreversibility. Our results show that neural populations can implement a broad class of stochastic dynamics within low-dimensional manifolds, unifying ideas from the dynamics of neural computation, the theory of neural manifolds, associative memory, and nonequilibrium statistical mechanics.	arXiv:2602.14885v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) provide a theoretical framework for understanding computation in biological neural circuits through their ability to support rich time-dependent behaviour. Whilst high-dimensional by construction, they can be used to encode low-dimensional dynamical systems, for example by restricting to low-rank connectivity, hence mirroring the emergence of latent neural manifolds found in empirical neural data. Here, we exploit a relationship between RNNs and a class of universal approximators to train networks that can represent arbitrary, nonlinear stochastic differential equations (SDEs), with given drift and diffusion coefficients, within a low-dimensional latent subspace. We show that RNNs can faithfully embed the drift and diffusion of a given SDE, including nonlinear and nonequilibrium dynamics such as chaotic attractors. We apply this approach by constructing RNN realisations of stochastic systems that transiently explore various attractors through both input-driven switching and autonomous transitions driven by nonequilibrium currents, which we interpret as models of associative and sequential (episodic) memory. To elucidate how these dynamics are encoded in the network, we introduce decompositions of the RNN based on its asymmetric connectivity and its time-irreversibility. Our results show that neural populations can implement a broad class of stochastic dynamics within low-dimensional manifolds, unifying ideas from the dynamics of neural computation, the theory of neural manifolds, associative memory, and nonequilibrium statistical mechanics.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.392429+00	4	\N	6fd52ca3badb759ba17609f47ff3f4d7a073a5cf7b2dec8b35daaa241a97f0ae	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.3929+00	2026-11-10 15:31:41.232246+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
437fff79-fb57-4b6e-95e1-f1ba13588c2f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2512.04992v2	Evolutionary Architecture Search through Grammar-Based Sequence Alignment	https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.04992	https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.04992	Adri G\\'omez Mart\\'in, Felix M\\"oller, Steven McDonagh, Monica Abella, Manuel Desco, Elliot J. Crowley, Aaron Klein, Linus Ericsson	arXiv:2512.04992v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Neural architecture search (NAS) in expressive search spaces is a computationally hard problem, but it also holds the potential to automatically discover completely novel and performant architectures. To achieve this we need effective search algorithms that can identify powerful components and reuse them in new candidate architectures. In this paper, we introduce two adapted variants of the Smith-Waterman algorithm for local sequence alignment and use them to compute the edit distance in a grammar-based evolutionary architecture search. These algorithms enable us to efficiently calculate a distance metric for neural architectures and to generate a set of hybrid offspring from two parent models. This facilitates the deployment of crossover-based search heuristics, allows us to perform a thorough analysis on the architectural loss landscape, and track population diversity during search. We highlight how our method vastly improves computational complexity over previous work and enables us to efficiently compute shortest paths between architectures. When instantiating the crossover in evolutionary searches, we achieve competitive results, outperforming competing methods. Future work can build upon this new tool, discovering novel components that can be used more broadly across neural architecture design, and broadening its applications beyond NAS.	arXiv:2512.04992v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Neural architecture search (NAS) in expressive search spaces is a computationally hard problem, but it also holds the potential to automatically discover completely novel and performant architectures. To achieve this we need effective search algorithms that can identify powerful components and reuse them in new candidate architectures. In this paper, we introduce two adapted variants of the Smith-Waterman algorithm for local sequence alignment and use them to compute the edit distance in a grammar-based evolutionary architecture search. These algorithms enable us to efficiently calculate a distance metric for neural architectures and to generate a set of hybrid offspring from two parent models. This facilitates the deployment of crossover-based search heuristics, allows us to perform a thorough analysis on the architectural loss landscape, and track population diversity during search. We highlight how our method vastly improves computational complexity over previous work and enables us to efficiently compute shortest paths between architectures. When instantiating the crossover in evolutionary searches, we achieve competitive results, outperforming competing methods. Future work can build upon this new tool, discovering novel components that can be used more broadly across neural architecture design, and broadening its applications beyond NAS.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.374697+00	4	\N	755a3ec576eff7b34082ae42141eea7c29fd89ac20e0f0c08d0acf494a7f25c9	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.375164+00	2026-11-10 15:31:13.303761+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
65c2400c-a99d-4d57-b4fd-58c8f1892256	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09614v1	Know What You Need: Efficient Activation Checkpointing	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09614	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09614	Danyal Ahmed (MLIA), Edouard Oyallon (MLIA)	arXiv:2611.09614v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Large Language Model training is increasingly constrained by memory capacity. Activation Checkpointing reduces activation memory by avoiding storing selected activations during the forward pass and rematerializing them during backpropagation, thereby trading memory savings for additional computation. Although this technique is essential for scaling model training, rematerialization can be prohibitively expensive, particularly for Transformer linear layers. Approximate computation could reduce this overhead, but conventional approaches either introduce errors that accumulate over the backward pass or fail to exploit information available from the initial forward pass. We introduce a novel approximate rematerialization method that can yield unbiased gradient estimates: using information collected during the initial forward pass, the method selectively recomputes only the most important layer components, either through greedy selection or randomized sketching. Ablation studies show that the resulting gradients closely match the exact gradients. When applied to feed-forward layers, attention layers, or both, our method can recompute only two-thirds of the layer components in a Llama-488M model, while increasing perplexity by just 1.7%, 1.2%, and 2.5%, respectively.	arXiv:2611.09614v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Large Language Model training is increasingly constrained by memory capacity. Activation Checkpointing reduces activation memory by avoiding storing selected activations during the forward pass and rematerializing them during backpropagation, thereby trading memory savings for additional computation. Although this technique is essential for scaling model training, rematerialization can be prohibitively expensive, particularly for Transformer linear layers. Approximate computation could reduce this overhead, but conventional approaches either introduce errors that accumulate over the backward pass or fail to exploit information available from the initial forward pass. We introduce a novel approximate rematerialization method that can yield unbiased gradient estimates: using information collected during the initial forward pass, the method selectively recomputes only the most important layer components, either through greedy selection or randomized sketching. Ablation studies show that the resulting gradients closely match the exact gradients. When applied to feed-forward layers, attention layers, or both, our method can recompute only two-thirds of the layer components in a Llama-488M model, while increasing perplexity by just 1.7%, 1.2%, and 2.5%, respectively.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.573245+00	4	\N	62a5f1d3361d64b940d4a8e67721e1a3ea5cfe59460a5f8c7831cbcae023d721	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.573645+00	2026-11-10 14:52:24.012585+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a0c43d74-f1b5-4652-b0cb-37adeff38af8	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09625v1	Encoding Memory in World Models with Test-Time Model-Based Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09625	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09625	Maxime Burchi, Fayaz Ali Dharejo, Radu Timofte	arXiv:2611.09625v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Model-based reinforcement learning (MBRL) approaches have demonstrated strong performance across diverse domains in recent years, learning accurate world models from agent trajectories. However, despite this progress, current approaches still struggle to surpass human performance on tasks that require long-term memory and planning. In this work, we address the problem of long-term memory in MBRL by moving beyond the commonly studied paradigm of architecture design. We introduce Test-Time Model-Based Reinforcement Learning (TT-MBRL), using gradient descent at test time to encode the memory of episode observations into world model parameters. This enables the agent to learn a distinct world model for each episode, improving memory capabilities compared to traditional approaches that solely rely on contextual memory. We first establish a proof of concept on a simplified two-dimensional variant of the Memory Maze environment, which we refer to as Memory Maze 2D, showing consistent improvements across different world model architectures. We then demonstrate the effectiveness of TT-MBRL in the 3D domain, achieving new state-of-the-art results on the Memory Maze benchmark. Finally, we show that TT-MBRL maintains strong performance on non-memory benchmarks such as DeepMind Control and Atari 100k.	arXiv:2611.09625v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Model-based reinforcement learning (MBRL) approaches have demonstrated strong performance across diverse domains in recent years, learning accurate world models from agent trajectories. However, despite this progress, current approaches still struggle to surpass human performance on tasks that require long-term memory and planning. In this work, we address the problem of long-term memory in MBRL by moving beyond the commonly studied paradigm of architecture design. We introduce Test-Time Model-Based Reinforcement Learning (TT-MBRL), using gradient descent at test time to encode the memory of episode observations into world model parameters. This enables the agent to learn a distinct world model for each episode, improving memory capabilities compared to traditional approaches that solely rely on contextual memory. We first establish a proof of concept on a simplified two-dimensional variant of the Memory Maze environment, which we refer to as Memory Maze 2D, showing consistent improvements across different world model architectures. We then demonstrate the effectiveness of TT-MBRL in the 3D domain, achieving new state-of-the-art results on the Memory Maze benchmark. Finally, we show that TT-MBRL maintains strong performance on non-memory benchmarks such as DeepMind Control and Atari 100k.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.578202+00	4	\N	9070a1149d6de63f2bcb74ae3ddb71b5dcacbc8e1a4215d43a46d09752feec63	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.578698+00	2026-11-10 14:52:31.907326+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
