b974bf4d-c922-43ef-9577-bf0085c03d44	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09815v1	The Cost of Coverage: Conformalising Native Forecast Uncertainty for GB Wind Power	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09815	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09815	Carl Somers	arXiv:2611.09815v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Uncertainty bands on wind forecasts help inform reserve sizing and commitments, but often fail to deliver their stated coverage. We apply conformal prediction to native bands to see what it adds and what it costs, comparing six forecasters, from ARIMA to a tabular foundation model, at two wind farms in Great Britain (GB) under operational conditions. We show that calibration repairs empirical marginal coverage with little or no Winkler Score penalty, but this apparent success conceals under-coverage in high winds. Calibrating by forecast wind regime turns marginal coverage into regime-level coverage, cutting day-ahead out-of-band volume in high winds by over 40% relative to pooled calibration while narrowing bands overall.	arXiv:2611.09815v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Uncertainty bands on wind forecasts help inform reserve sizing and commitments, but often fail to deliver their stated coverage. We apply conformal prediction to native bands to see what it adds and what it costs, comparing six forecasters, from ARIMA to a tabular foundation model, at two wind farms in Great Britain (GB) under operational conditions. We show that calibration repairs empirical marginal coverage with little or no Winkler Score penalty, but this apparent success conceals under-coverage in high winds. Calibrating by forecast wind regime turns marginal coverage into regime-level coverage, cutting day-ahead out-of-band volume in high winds by over 40% relative to pooled calibration while narrowing bands overall.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.616749+00	3	\N	50223ab5d6037a70bd55ced50383c757a96d5d858590fc0dac72b1331c87f0f5	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.617197+00	2026-11-10 14:53:20.381559+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8c3bf970-d21a-4753-9cb4-8d7a79880a05	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08799v1	The Matthews Correlation Coefficient: Fifty years	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08799	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08799	Jesus S. Aguilar-Ruiz	arXiv:2611.08799v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Fifty years after its introduction, the Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC) remains central -and debated- in binary classification. This survey offers a comprehensive retrospective and an in-depth analysis of MCC, covering its historical development, mathematical foundations, and implications for practice. We clarify long-standing misconceptions, formalize its equivalences (to Pearson's correlation and to Chi2 in 2x2 tables), and examine its behavior under class imbalance. Beyond MCC, we develop analytical (derivatives) and graphical (contour plots) frameworks that generalize to any confusion-matrix-based metric. These frameworks expose structural properties -bias, robustness, sensitivity- that scalar summaries conceal, and they provide actionable guidance for selecting and interpreting performance measures in applications where reliability and fairness matter. Together, our results position MCC within a broader toolkit while clarifying when it is informative -and when alternatives may be preferable.	arXiv:2611.08799v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Fifty years after its introduction, the Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC) remains central -and debated- in binary classification. This survey offers a comprehensive retrospective and an in-depth analysis of MCC, covering its historical development, mathematical foundations, and implications for practice. We clarify long-standing misconceptions, formalize its equivalences (to Pearson's correlation and to Chi2 in 2x2 tables), and examine its behavior under class imbalance. Beyond MCC, we develop analytical (derivatives) and graphical (contour plots) frameworks that generalize to any confusion-matrix-based metric. These frameworks expose structural properties -bias, robustness, sensitivity- that scalar summaries conceal, and they provide actionable guidance for selecting and interpreting performance measures in applications where reliability and fairness matter. Together, our results position MCC within a broader toolkit while clarifying when it is informative -and when alternatives may be preferable.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.643801+00	3	\N	a1dc059a40bfcf64230aae96cd4a03908651a4061e3df96cb6512af970a924b4	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.644217+00	2026-11-10 15:45:37.109076+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
