6bcd6313-1593-4e77-ba62-b9bd55f3620f	c9402267-ab70-45fa-b843-f47b6189bd14	\N	https://research.google/blog/how-diffusion-controller-unifies-and-simplifies-ai-image-generation/	How Diffusion Controller unifies and simplifies AI image generation	https://research.google/blog/how-diffusion-controller-unifies-and-simplifies-ai-image-generation/	https://research.google/blog/how-diffusion-controller-unifies-and-simplifies-ai-image-generation/	\N	Algorithms & Theory	Algorithms & Theory	research	en	2026-09-29 18:38:27+00	2026-11-09 17:12:40.985594+00	4	\N	45ee6d4a16b230aada93381846c34c0ec21062333b7069c5b2d19de94db5bfff	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:40.98631+00	2026-11-10 14:36:49.053724+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bbf25292-44e7-4726-a84d-880820b383b3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08937v1	Surrogate Quality Score: A Novel Composite Performance Metric for Model Selection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08937	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08937	Maaz Ahmad, Iftekhar A. Karimi	arXiv:2611.08937v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The growing reliance on high-fidelity, state-of-the-art machine learning based models for engineering decision-making necessitates robust and systematic approaches for model selection. While high predictive accuracy at untrained points is a necessary trait in the final model, other attributes such as model complexity, forecastability, explainability, scalability, and physics-awareness may be equally important or relevant criteria depending on application context and user requirements. In this work, we propose a novel composite performance metric, Surrogate Quality Score (SQS) for model selection based on predictive accuracy and model complexity simultaneously. SQS heuristically integrates these two attributes through a weighted product of normalized measures, yielding a unified score to reflect overall model quality. A user-specified parameter in SQS enables explicit control over the accuracy-complexity trade-off, thereby incorporating human judgement and preference in the model selection process. We extensively discuss and justify the formulation of SQS and demonstrate its utility through an illustrative example. By combining predictive accuracy and model complexity, SQS offers a systematic and objective approach for model selection based on both attributes.	arXiv:2611.08937v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The growing reliance on high-fidelity, state-of-the-art machine learning based models for engineering decision-making necessitates robust and systematic approaches for model selection. While high predictive accuracy at untrained points is a necessary trait in the final model, other attributes such as model complexity, forecastability, explainability, scalability, and physics-awareness may be equally important or relevant criteria depending on application context and user requirements. In this work, we propose a novel composite performance metric, Surrogate Quality Score (SQS) for model selection based on predictive accuracy and model complexity simultaneously. SQS heuristically integrates these two attributes through a weighted product of normalized measures, yielding a unified score to reflect overall model quality. A user-specified parameter in SQS enables explicit control over the accuracy-complexity trade-off, thereby incorporating human judgement and preference in the model selection process. We extensively discuss and justify the formulation of SQS and demonstrate its utility through an illustrative example. By combining predictive accuracy and model complexity, SQS offers a systematic and objective approach for model selection based on both attributes.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.366665+00	3	\N	b781e640489a4b196e97fcbbf354c90d1e2c98d1c2fc6dad9900e8411370f59b	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.367086+00	2026-11-10 14:48:26.903391+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6c32e18d-4222-4cec-a4d5-b20d64078020	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.27218v3	Unsupervised Evaluation of Deep Audio Embeddings for Music Structure Analysis	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.27218	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.27218	Axel Marmoret	arXiv:2603.27218v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Music Structure Analysis (MSA) aims to uncover the high-level organization of musical pieces. State-of-the-art methods are often based on supervised deep learning, but these methods are bottlenecked by the need for heavily annotated data and inherent structural ambiguities. In this paper, we propose an unsupervised evaluation of nine open-source, generic pre-trained deep audio models, on MSA. For each model, we extract barwise embeddings and segment them using three unsupervised segmentation algorithms (Foote's checkerboard kernels, spectral clustering, and Correlation Block-Matching (CBM)), focusing exclusively on boundary retrieval. Our results demonstrate that modern, generic deep embeddings generally outperform traditional spectrogram-based baselines, but not systematically. Furthermore, our unsupervised boundary estimation methodology generally yields stronger performance than recent linear probing baselines. Among the evaluated techniques, the CBM algorithm consistently emerges as the most effective downstream segmentation method. Finally, we highlight the artificial inflation of standard evaluation metrics and advocate for the systematic adoption of ``trimming'', or even ``double trimming'' annotations to establish more rigorous MSA evaluation standards.	arXiv:2603.27218v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Music Structure Analysis (MSA) aims to uncover the high-level organization of musical pieces. State-of-the-art methods are often based on supervised deep learning, but these methods are bottlenecked by the need for heavily annotated data and inherent structural ambiguities. In this paper, we propose an unsupervised evaluation of nine open-source, generic pre-trained deep audio models, on MSA. For each model, we extract barwise embeddings and segment them using three unsupervised segmentation algorithms (Foote's checkerboard kernels, spectral clustering, and Correlation Block-Matching (CBM)), focusing exclusively on boundary retrieval. Our results demonstrate that modern, generic deep embeddings generally outperform traditional spectrogram-based baselines, but not systematically. Furthermore, our unsupervised boundary estimation methodology generally yields stronger performance than recent linear probing baselines. Among the evaluated techniques, the CBM algorithm consistently emerges as the most effective downstream segmentation method. Finally, we highlight the artificial inflation of standard evaluation metrics and advocate for the systematic adoption of ``trimming'', or even ``double trimming'' annotations to establish more rigorous MSA evaluation standards.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.410214+00	3	\N	b9164b97cd371fa80cc37ca966c962e4ed12c47df03fc94f8a8bc1be79937632	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.410611+00	2026-11-10 15:32:03.62602+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
29bc7123-0dcb-492e-9b18-8d0addbb48d3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08501v1	Electoral Intelligence: A Neural Zero-Inflated Negative Binomial Model for Forecasting Swedish Municipal Elections	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08501	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08501	Haodong Qi, Stefano M. Iacus, Pieter Bevelander, Derek S. Hutcheson	arXiv:2611.08501v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Forecasting local elections is difficult because electoral competition is geographically heterogeneous, highly overdispersed, and characterized by many party-municipality combinations with little or no electoral activity. This article develops a bounded neural zero-inflated negative binomial (ZINB) model for forecasting party-level vote counts in Swedish municipal elections. The model combines a count component for expected votes, a zero-inflation component for structural electoral absence, municipality-specific dispersion, and learned embeddings capturing latent party, municipality, and party-municipality structure. Model training relies on data from the 2014, 2018, and 2022 Swedish municipal elections and performance is evaluated on a held-out set of municipalities. Testing results show strong predictive accuracy in both vote counts and vote shares. Explanatory diagnostics indicate that latent party structure, local party-municipality relationships, electoral persistence, organizational capacity, and candidate-electorate representational similarity are among the most important determinants of predictive performance. Following model evaluation and diagnostics, the selected specification is refit on all historical data and applied to generate forecasts for the 2026 municipal elections. The proposed framework provides a flexible approach for studying local electoral competition in multiparty systems characterized by heterogeneous party presence and substantial geographic variation.	arXiv:2611.08501v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Forecasting local elections is difficult because electoral competition is geographically heterogeneous, highly overdispersed, and characterized by many party-municipality combinations with little or no electoral activity. This article develops a bounded neural zero-inflated negative binomial (ZINB) model for forecasting party-level vote counts in Swedish municipal elections. The model combines a count component for expected votes, a zero-inflation component for structural electoral absence, municipality-specific dispersion, and learned embeddings capturing latent party, municipality, and party-municipality structure. Model training relies on data from the 2014, 2018, and 2022 Swedish municipal elections and performance is evaluated on a held-out set of municipalities. Testing results show strong predictive accuracy in both vote counts and vote shares. Explanatory diagnostics indicate that latent party structure, local party-municipality relationships, electoral persistence, organizational capacity, and candidate-electorate representational similarity are among the most important determinants of predictive performance. Following model evaluation and diagnostics, the selected specification is refit on all historical data and applied to generate forecasts for the 2026 municipal elections. The proposed framework provides a flexible approach for studying local electoral competition in multiparty systems characterized by heterogeneous party presence and substantial geographic variation.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.636051+00	3	\N	6b0fbcb5318d07e375c8255ec50b4895492b9ad4a09f611e0aed74d536aa8226	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.636554+00	2026-11-10 14:53:45.036+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6b6d75c5-da07-4bab-8f52-dd74977d7e9d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.03203v2	PR3DICTR: A modular AI framework for medical 3D image-based detection and outcome prediction	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03203	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03203	Daniel C. MacRae, Luuk van der Hoek, Robert van der Wal, Suzanne P. M. de Vette, Hendrike Neh, Baoqiang Ma, Peter M. A. van Ooijen, Lisanne V. van Dijk	arXiv:2604.03203v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Three-dimensional medical image data and computer-aided decision making, particularly using deep learning, are becoming increasingly important in the medical field. To aid in these developments we introduce PR3DICTR: Platform for Research in 3D Image Classification and sTandardised tRaining. Built using community-standard distributions (PyTorch and MONAI), PR3DICTR provides an open-access, flexible and convenient framework for prediction model development, with an explicit focus on classification using three-dimensional medical image data. By combining modular design principles and standardization, it aims to alleviate developmental burden whilst retaining adjustability. It provides users with a wealth of pre-established functionality, for instance in model architecture design options, hyper-parameter solutions and training methodologies, but still gives users the opportunity and freedom to ``plug in'' their own solutions or modules. PR3DICTR can be applied to any binary or event-based three-dimensional classification task and can work with as little as two lines of code.	arXiv:2604.03203v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Three-dimensional medical image data and computer-aided decision making, particularly using deep learning, are becoming increasingly important in the medical field. To aid in these developments we introduce PR3DICTR: Platform for Research in 3D Image Classification and sTandardised tRaining. Built using community-standard distributions (PyTorch and MONAI), PR3DICTR provides