2a8151f5-5f99-429f-bea3-11f5c6ea1acd	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-southeast-asia-taiwan	ChatGPT Ads expands to Southeast Asia and Taiwan	https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-southeast-asia-taiwan	https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-southeast-asia-taiwan	\N	ChatGPT Ads is expanding to Southeast Asia and Taiwan, giving eligible businesses new ways to reach people across more than 60 countries.	ChatGPT Ads is expanding to Southeast Asia and Taiwan, giving eligible businesses new ways to reach people across more than 60 countries.	news	en	2026-09-23 02:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.162904+00	3	\N	d8d47699141cd99c5753cd42bc8009c4b2833e9153222b1059dea4e9c87979e1	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.163267+00	2026-11-10 14:35:54.063274+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f9b4b314-0a30-4f83-b293-91c4f7fc1694	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.18261v2	DeepRitzSplit: A Variational Neural Operator Framework for Gradient Flows with Application to Dendritic Growth	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18261	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18261	Beno\\^it Appolaire, Ludovick Gagnon, Chih-Kang Huang, Miha Zalo\\v{z}nik	arXiv:2604.18261v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Phase-field models are widely used to simulate microstructure evolution but their numerical solution remains computationally demanding. Neural operator surrogates provide a promising alternative, yet existing approaches are data-driven, requiring large datasets and exhibiting limited robustness when extrapolating to unseen configurations.\n  We introduce DeepRitzSplit, an unsupervised neural operator framework for gradient-flow systems based on a variational formulation of energy-stable splitting schemes. It learns the time evolution semigroup associated with a convex-concave free-energy decomposition by minimizing the underlying variational problem at each time step. It is designed to inherit the dissipative structure of the underlying gradient flow without requiring trajectory datasets.\n  We further introduce a Reaction-Diffusion Neural Operator (RDNO) architecture motivated by splitting schemes, with separate reaction and diffusion components to improve training efficiency and interpretability.\n  We assess the approach on the Allen-Cahn equation and apply it to a phase-field model of anisotropic dendritic growth during solidification. In the Allen-Cahn benchmark, DeepRitzSplit shows better generalization than the supervised baseline on the out-of-distribution configurations tested, using 180 training configurations. In dendritic growth, the method captures the overall phase morphology and dendrite tip velocity well, while the tip radius is more sensitive to the training strategy. Refinement based on the residual of the splitting scheme markedly improves this second-order morphological quantity. The trained operators retain good predictive capabilities on configurations substantially different from the training set.\n  These results demonstrate the potential of variationally trained neural operators as physically informed surrogates for accelerated microstructure simulations.	arXiv:2604.18261v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Phase-field models are widely used to simulate microstructure evolution but their numerical solution remains computationally demanding. Neural operator surrogates provide a promising alternative, yet existing approaches are data-driven, requiring large datasets and exhibiting limited robustness when extrapolating to unseen configurations.\n  We introduce DeepRitzSplit, an unsupervised neural operator framework for gradient-flow systems based on a variational formulation of energy-stable splitting schemes. It learns the time evolution semigroup associated with a convex-concave free-energy decomposition by minimizing the underlying variational problem at each time step. It is designed to inherit the dissipative structure of the underlying gradient flow without requiring trajectory datasets.\n  We further introduce a Reaction-Diffusion Neural Operator (RDNO) architecture motivated by splitting schemes, with separate reaction and diffusion components to improve training efficiency and interpretability.\n  We assess the approach on the Allen-Cahn equation and apply it to a phase-field model of anisotropic dendritic growth during solidification. In the Allen-Cahn benchmark, DeepRitzSplit shows better generalization than the supervised baseline on the out-of-distribution configurations tested, using 180 training configurations. In dendritic growth, the method captures the overall phase morphology and dendrite tip velocity well, while the tip radius is more sensitive to the training strategy. Refinement based on the residual of the splitting scheme markedly improves this second-order morphological quantity. The trained operators retain good predictive capabilities on configurations substantially different from the training set.\n  These results demonstrate the potential of variationally trained neural operators as physically informed surrogates for accelerated microstructure simulations.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.421486+00	4	\N	7320d410404de90afb313bb3f6126ec00ccfec575bfc440856f84aa3eea702c2	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.421933+00	2026-11-10 15:32:17.477448+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c2e8939e-a63c-4c6b-85ff-e6fc44dd2f79	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08510v1	Online and Survey Representations of Well-Being: Coherence and Cross-Source Correspondence	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08510	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08510	Stefano M. Iacus, Giuseppe Porro, Haodong Qi	arXiv:2611.08510v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Digital traces may complement population surveys in the measurement of well-being, but the two sources do not necessarily capture identical constructs. We compare the Human Flourishing Geographic Index (HFGI) with World Values Survey and European Social Survey measures across three domains using structural equation models. The results show strong within-source coherence and construct-specific cross-source correspondence. Cross-source convergence varies across domains; the observed pattern is consistent with stronger correspondence where the underlying indicators are more closely aligned conceptually. Near-perfect agreement would be unexpected, since surveys elicit standardized self-reports, whereas HFGI captures naturally occurring public expressions. HFGI should therefore be viewed as a distinct but empirically connected measurement system that complements survey-based evidence on well-being.	arXiv:2611.08510v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Digital traces may complement population surveys in the measurement of well-being, but the two sources do not necessarily capture identical constructs. We compare the Human Flourishing Geographic Index (HFGI) with World Values Survey and European Social Survey measures across three domains using structural equation models. The results show strong within-source coherence and construct-specific cross-source correspondence. Cross-source convergence varies across domains; the observed pattern is consistent with stronger correspondence where the underlying indicators are more closely aligned conceptually. Near-perfect agreement would be unexpected, since surveys elicit standardized self-reports, whereas HFGI captures naturally occurring public expressions. HFGI should therefore be viewed as a distinct but empirically connected measurement system that complements survey-based evidence on well-being.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.645417+00	3	\N	b945e73a1b26b40643f0e1eba27bf8bad8d972277d35f49b03a7be8fb9fc11da	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.645858+00	2026-11-10 14:53:55.008597+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
29681919-3522-458f-ba4d-063408bcf863	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.18540v2	Duality for the Adversarial Total Variation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18540	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18540	Leon Bungert, Lucas Schmitt	arXiv:2604.18540v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Adversarial training of binary classifiers can be reformulated as regularized risk minimization involving a nonlocal total variation. Building on this perspective, we establish a characterization of the subdifferential of this total variation using duality techniques. To achieve this, we derive a dual representation of the nonlocal total variation and a related integration-by-parts formula, involving a nonlocal gradient and divergence. We provide such duality statements both in the space of continuous functions vanishing at infinity on proper metric spaces and for the space of essentially bounded functions on separable metric spaces. Furthermore, under some additional conditions we provide characterizations of the subdifferential in these settings.	arXiv:2604.18540v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Adversarial training of binary classifiers can be reformulated as regularized risk minimization involving a nonlocal total variation. Building on this perspective, we establish a characterization of the subdifferential of this total variation using duality techniques. To achieve this, we derive a dual representation of the nonlocal total variation and a related integration-by-parts formula, involving a nonlocal gradient and divergence. We provide such duality statements both in the space of continuous functions vanishing at infinity on proper metric spaces and for the space of essentially bounded functions on separable metric spaces. Furthermore, under some additional conditions we provide characterizations of the subdifferential in these settings.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.424211+00	3	\N	54bac74769bbd974559de4eff9aa324fe54f50c3d6896c8305