02e29d68-4c3f-46e4-926b-8d05262c9fad	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2512.04992v2	Evolutionary Architecture Search through Grammar-Based Sequence Alignment	https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.04992	https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.04992	Adri G\\'omez Mart\\'in, Felix M\\"oller, Steven McDonagh, Monica Abella, Manuel Desco, Elliot J. Crowley, Aaron Klein, Linus Ericsson	arXiv:2512.04992v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Neural architecture search (NAS) in expressive search spaces is a computationally hard problem, but it also holds the potential to automatically discover completely novel and performant architectures. To achieve this we need effective search algorithms that can identify powerful components and reuse them in new candidate architectures. In this paper, we introduce two adapted variants of the Smith-Waterman algorithm for local sequence alignment and use them to compute the edit distance in a grammar-based evolutionary architecture search. These algorithms enable us to efficiently calculate a distance metric for neural architectures and to generate a set of hybrid offspring from two parent models. This facilitates the deployment of crossover-based search heuristics, allows us to perform a thorough analysis on the architectural loss landscape, and track population diversity during search. We highlight how our method vastly improves computational complexity over previous work and enables us to efficiently compute shortest paths between architectures. When instantiating the crossover in evolutionary searches, we achieve competitive results, outperforming competing methods. Future work can build upon this new tool, discovering novel components that can be used more broadly across neural architecture design, and broadening its applications beyond NAS.	arXiv:2512.04992v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Neural architecture search (NAS) in expressive search spaces is a computationally hard problem, but it also holds the potential to automatically discover completely novel and performant architectures. To achieve this we need effective search algorithms that can identify powerful components and reuse them in new candidate architectures. In this paper, we introduce two adapted variants of the Smith-Waterman algorithm for local sequence alignment and use them to compute the edit distance in a grammar-based evolutionary architecture search. These algorithms enable us to efficiently calculate a distance metric for neural architectures and to generate a set of hybrid offspring from two parent models. This facilitates the deployment of crossover-based search heuristics, allows us to perform a thorough analysis on the architectural loss landscape, and track population diversity during search. We highlight how our method vastly improves computational complexity over previous work and enables us to efficiently compute shortest paths between architectures. When instantiating the crossover in evolutionary searches, we achieve competitive results, outperforming competing methods. Future work can build upon this new tool, discovering novel components that can be used more broadly across neural architecture design, and broadening its applications beyond NAS.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.517387+00	4	\N	755a3ec576eff7b34082ae42141eea7c29fd89ac20e0f0c08d0acf494a7f25c9	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.517781+00	2026-11-10 16:03:22.42511+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f4adaf23-59f4-416b-80ce-9da700f1e59d	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12017v1	Grasp-Taxonomy Conditioning as a Mode Switch for Dexterous Grasping of Climbing Holds	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12017	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12017	Rumi Loghmani, Abraham George, Amir Barati Farimani	arXiv:2611.12017v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Dexterous grasping in robotics relies on distinct hand configurations depending on object geometry. We introduce BetaGrasp, a 3D diffusion policy that conditions a DP3-style point-cloud policy on a four-class grasp label (crimp, sloper, pinch, and jug), running on a Franka arm with a 16-DoF LEAP hand. We also introduce a climbing-hold benchmark: a spring testbed whose ratchet gives a slip-force reading in newtons. In our analysis the policy's point-cloud encoder did not separate the four holds, so the scan alone gave it little to choose a grasp from. Without the label (a DP3-style baseline), simulated rollouts from the robot's reset pose all end in a jug grasp, whatever the hold (25% correct grasp type, versus 94% for BetaGrasp). The label works as a switch for which grasp the policy performs. On 80 paired hardware trials, BetaGrasp raises average slip force from 9.6 N to 14.3 N, and grasps slipping fully off the hold drop from 44% of trials to 10%.	arXiv:2611.12017v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Dexterous grasping in robotics relies on distinct hand configurations depending on object geometry. We introduce BetaGrasp, a 3D diffusion policy that conditions a DP3-style point-cloud policy on a four-class grasp label (crimp, sloper, pinch, and jug), running on a Franka arm with a 16-DoF LEAP hand. We also introduce a climbing-hold benchmark: a spring testbed whose ratchet gives a slip-force reading in newtons. In our analysis the policy's point-cloud encoder did not separate the four holds, so the scan alone gave it little to choose a grasp from. Without the label (a DP3-style baseline), simulated rollouts from the robot's reset pose all end in a jug grasp, whatever the hold (25% correct grasp type, versus 94% for BetaGrasp). The label works as a switch for which grasp the policy performs. On 80 paired hardware trials, BetaGrasp raises average slip force from 9.6 N to 14.3 N, and grasps slipping fully off the hold drop from 44% of trials to 10%.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:55.764142+00	3	\N	933d1768616bf21d7ab3894d75cc8d9cd3314db3d0610b8d26568f5e9d05b67e	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:55.764466+00	2026-11-10 16:34:33.757748+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2d884b95-1923-4094-86b2-c48178574bba	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08682v1	Transporting the Past: An Optimal Transport View of Backtracking Counterfactuals	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08682	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08682	Zhangyi Liu, Pouria Fatemi, Suvrit Sra	arXiv:2611.08682v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study backtracking counterfactual inference for Structural Causal Models (SCMs) through the lens of optimal transport. In SCMs, a factual observation generally induces a posterior distribution over exogenous variables, making counterfactual inference inherently distributional. We introduce two complementary frameworks that transport this posterior onto the set of exogenous configurations satisfying a desired counterfactual query. The first constructs a natural pointwise projection and is suitable when the transported exogenous distribution can be used directly. The second enforces mutual independence among counterfactual exogenous variables, so that the transported variables remain interpretable as genuine exogenous noises of the same SCM. We show that both frameworks are consistent with the original backtracking counterfactual principle, and we propose marginal, penalty-based, and hard-constrained solvers for finite-posterior settings. Synthetic experiments on non-bijective SCMs illustrate the geometry of the problem and reveal trade-offs among transport cost, counterfactual constraint satisfaction, and preservation of exogenous independence.	arXiv:2611.08682v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study backtracking counterfactual inference for Structural Causal Models (SCMs) through the lens of optimal transport. In SCMs, a factual observation generally induces a posterior distribution over exogenous variables, making counterfactual inference inherently distributional. We introduce two complementary frameworks that transport this posterior onto the set of exogenous configurations satisfying a desired counterfactual query. The first constructs a natural pointwise projection and is suitable when the transported exogenous distribution can be used directly. The second enforces mutual independence among counterfactual exogenous variables, so that the transported variables remain interpretable as genuine exogenous noises of the same SCM. We show that both frameworks are consistent with the original backtracking counterfactual principle, and we propose marginal, penalty-based, and hard-constrained solvers for finite-posterior settings. Synthetic experiments on non-bijective SCMs illustrate the geometry of the problem and reveal trade-offs among transport cost, counterfactual constraint satisfaction, and preservation of exogenous independence.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.68556+00	3	\N	93b9179ef05f73f2901d5a810c3ebce7080b93d182da40b569a91cd8980f526e	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.686318+00	2026-11-10 14:54:38.783529+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d52a0591-259c-4d79-b93b-ce8a77f39afd	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.09319v5	PGID: Progressive Guided Inversion and Denoising for Robust Watermark Detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09319	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09319	Minh Quoc Duong, Chun Tong Lei, Chun Pong Lau	arXiv:2605.09319v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: With the proliferation of AI-generated images, digital watermarking has become an essential safeguard for protecting intellectual property and mitigating malicious exploitation. Recent works on semantic watermarking have enabled efficient copyright protection for diffusion models. However, the dependence of semantic watermarking on diffusion inversion for watermark detection creates a critical vulnerability. Imprint removal and forgery attacks exploit this weakness to produce deceptive results. Our analysis reveals that these attacks succeed by displacing watermarked latents into the unwatermarked region, while guiding unwatermarked latents into the watermarked region. Based on that, we propose Progressive Guided Inversion and Denoising (PGID), the first plug-and-play, training-free noise extraction framework designed to defend against both attack strategies. PGID effectively defends by projecting perturbed latents back to the region where they originally belong. The projection is achieved by eliminating intermediate latent deflections and mitigating adversarial perturbations through progressive inversion-denoising cycles. Comprehensive evaluations across multiple schemes demonstrate that PGID successfully restores detection reliability by recovering removed watermarks and identifying forged instances.	arXiv:2605.09319v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: With the proliferation of AI-generated images, digital watermarking has become an essential safeguard for protecting intellectual property and mitigating malicious exploitation. Recent works on semantic watermarking have enabled efficient copyright protection for diffusion models. However, the dependence of semantic watermarking on diffusion inversion for watermark detection creates a critical vulnerability. Imprint removal and forgery attacks exploit this weakness to produce deceptive results. Our analysis reveals that these attacks succeed by displacing watermarked latents into the unwatermarked region, while guiding unwatermarked latents into the watermarked region. Based on that, we propose Progressive Guided Inversion and Denoising (PGID), the first plug-and-play, training-free noise extraction framework designed to defend against both attack strategies. PGID effectively defends by projecting perturbed latents back to the region where they originally belong. The projection is achieved by eliminating intermediate latent deflections and mitigating adversarial perturbations through progressive inversion-denoising cycles. Comprehensive evaluations across multiple schemes demonstrate that PGID successfully restores detection reliability by recovering removed watermarks and identifying forged instances.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.446787+00	4	\N	bf1100a6f89663bf7091404b067613d052405c8984331478414712e8ea8eda9d	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.447177+00	2026-11-10 15:32:39.280092+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f2f5ca3a-029f-42b8-bda6-8e39bc2307b1	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.09630v2	Scratchpad Patching: Decoupling Compute from Patch Size in Byte-Level Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09630	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09630	Lin Zheng, Vasilisa Bashlovkina, Timothy Dozat, Dan Garrette, Laura Rimell, Joshua Maynez	arXiv:2605.09630v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Tokenizer-free language models eliminate the tokenizer step of the language modeling pipeline by operating directly on bytes; patch-based variants further aggregate contiguous byte spans into patches for efficiency. However, the average patch size chosen at the model design stage governs a tight trade-off: larger patches reduce compute and KV-cache footprint, but degrade modeling quality. We trace this trade-off to patch lag: until a patch is fully observed, byte predictions within it must rely on a stale representation from the previous patch to preserve causality; this lag widens as patches grow larger. We introduce Scratchpad Patching (SP), which inserts transient scratchpads inside each patch to aggregate the bytes seen so far and refresh patch-level context for subsequent predictions. SP triggers scratchpads using next-byte prediction entropy, selectively allocating compute to information-dense regions and enabling post-hoc adjustment of inference-time compute. Across experiments on natural language and code, SP improves model quality at the same patch size; for example, even at $16$ bytes per patch, SP-augmented models match or closely approach the byte-level baseline on downstream evaluations while using a $16\\times$ smaller KV-cache over patches and requiring roughly $3$-$4\\times$ less inference compute than the byte-level baseline.	arXiv:2605.09630v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Tokenizer-free language models eliminate the tokenizer step of the language modeling pipeline by operating directly on bytes; patch-based variants further aggregate contiguous byte spans into patches for efficiency. However, the average patch size chosen at the model design stage governs a tight trade-off: larger patches reduce compute and KV-cache footprint, but degrade modeling quality. We trace this trade-off to patch lag: until a patch is fully observed, byte predictions within it must rely on a stale representation from the previous patch to preserve causality; this lag widens as patches grow larger. We introduce Scratchpad Patching (SP), which inserts transient scratchpads inside each patch to aggregate the bytes seen so far and refresh patch-level context for subsequent predictions. SP triggers scratchpads using next-byte prediction entropy, selectively allocating compute to information-dense regions and enabling post-hoc adjustment of inference-time compute. Across experiments on natural language and code, SP improves model quality at the same patch size; for example, even at $16$ bytes per patch, SP-augmented models match or closely approach the byte-level baseline on downstream evaluations while using a $16\\times$ smaller KV-cache over patches and requiring roughly $3$-$4\\times$ less inference compute than the byte-level baseline.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.449274+00	4	\N	da8fdcb99da1997343735f378767f95f1968a97872a177b78b7521996be7ea39	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.449654+00	2026-11-10 15:32:42.511872+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
56c7477a-e100-43cd-9365-89ffd699dc58	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08855v1	Learning Whom to Trust About the Unseen: Source Credibility and Reliability Transfer in Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08855	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08855	Ruijia Guan	arXiv:2611.08855v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: A source who has been right before deserves attention, but about what? We ask how far a demonstrated record of reliability travels in language models when the new claim cannot be checked, and whether the answer depends on the kind of claim. Three behavioral studies across six models approach the question from three angles. Study 1 adapts the Einstein-effect paradigm and finds that models, unlike the human sample, favor a spiritual leader over a scientist for the same obscure statement, and that the preference largely reverses when the statement is reworded in the vocabulary of physics. Study 2 adapts a paradigm in which a trusted expert calls a scientific or religious explanation unknown or a mystery; models reproduce the human domain asymmetry in exaggerated form. Study 3, the central test, gives two fictional informants different records on checkable river forecasts and then has them endorse matched claims about hidden natural and spiritual causes of the same village events. Most models carry the record to natural causes and barely at all to spiritual ones; one model transfers it weakly to either. Telling the model that the reliable informant forecasts by spiritual guidance rather than by measurement lowers that informant's influence on natural claims in every model and produces no comparable gain on spiritual claims in any. Demonstrated reliability thus has a scope in these models, and the account a source gives of how they came to be reliable can narrow it. Whether this reflects a judgment about relevance or the low prior credence of spiritual claims is the question the design leaves open, and we outline the experiment that would settle it.	arXiv:2611.08855v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: A source who has been right before deserves attention, but about what? We ask how far a demonstrated record of reliability travels in language models when the new claim cannot be checked, and whether the answer depends on the kind of claim. Three behavioral studies across six models approach the question from three angles. Study 1 adapts the Einstein-effect paradigm and finds that models, unlike the human sample, favor a spiritual leader over a scientist for the same obscure statement, and that the preference largely reverses when the statement is reworded in the vocabulary of physics. Study 2 adapts a paradigm in which a trusted expert calls a scientific or religious explanation unknown or a mystery; models reproduce the human domain asymmetry in exaggerated form. Study 3, the central test, gives two fictional informants different records on checkable river forecasts and then has them endorse matched claims about hidden natural and spiritual causes of the same village events. Most models carry the record to natural causes and barely at all to spiritual ones; one model transfers it weakly to either. Telling the model that the reliable informant forecasts by spiritual guidance rather than by measurement lowers that informant's influence on natural claims in every model and produces no comparable gain on spiritual claims in any. Demonstrated reliability thus has a scope in these models, and the account a source gives of how they came to be reliable can narrow it. Whether this reflects a judgment about relevance or the low prior credence of spiritual claims is the question the design leaves open, and we outline the experiment that would settle it.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.658743+00	3	\N	8ca1d9eb2ada6b6982b7af6fb965de84f7553c086a186e7b95d1137d4967b2ad	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.659158+00	2026-11-10 15:45:55.890313+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6291eaa0-d2a0-4c7f-8fe1-9743fdf023a5	c9402267-ab70-45fa-b843-f47b6189bd14	\N	https://research.google/blog/the-future-of-practice-enabling-teachers-to-create-learning-interactives-with-generative-ui/	The future of practice: Enabling teachers to create learning interactives with generative UI	https://research.google/blog/the-future-of-practice-enabling-teachers-to-create-learning-interactives-with-generative-ui/	https://research.google/blog/the-future-of-practice-enabling-teachers-to-create-learning-interactives-with-generative-ui/	\N	Education Innovation	Education Innovation	research	en	2026-09-17 20:45:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:40.994728+00	3	\N	a55c1118a53f939d8f80bec0635c281595da9982f2da1a93cc6d085528ae4f88	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:40.995143+00	2026-11-10 14:35:13.469284+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1440e77b-d527-442c-af15-a39b43c53f7c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08779v1	Jacobian-Causal Memory Control for Persistent Memory in Language Model Systems	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08779	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08779	Marvin Danig (Achiral Research)	arXiv:2611.08779v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Persistent-memory systems choose not only which records are retrieved but which prior information can alter current inference. We propose Jacobian-causal memory control (JCMC), which represents each authorized memory intervention by a predicted influence signature in a shared behavioral measurement space, selects interventions against a target effect and side-effect budget, and compares predicted with observed effects before retaining or rolling back the intervention. Under explicit smoothness and bounded-estimation assumptions, we prove a quadratic local-approximation bound, characterize the target effects reachable by a set of signatures, bound selection regret under signature error, and bound non-additive interactions between simultaneous memories. The paper reports no benchmark measurements; it defines a paired counterfactual estimator and an evaluation protocol comparing JCMC with relevance, graph, and policy-filtered retrieval baselines. The central claim is narrowed or falsified if those simpler baselines match effect targeting, side-effect control, and rollback utility under equal model, memory, policy, and cost budgets.	arXiv:2611.08779v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Persistent-memory systems choose not only which records are retrieved but which prior information can alter current inference. We propose Jacobian-causal memory control (JCMC), which represents each authorized memory intervention by a predicted influence signature in a shared behavioral measurement space, selects interventions against a target effect and side-effect budget, and compares predicted with observed effects before retaining or rolling back the intervention. Under explicit smoothness and bounded-estimation assumptions, we prove a quadratic local-approximation bound, characterize the target effects reachable by a set of signatures, bound selection regret under signature error, and bound non-additive interactions between simultaneous memories. The paper reports no benchmark measurements; it defines a paired counterfactual estimator and an evaluation protocol comparing JCMC with relevance, graph, and policy-filtered retrieval baselines. The central claim is narrowed or falsified if those simpler baselines match effect targeting, side-effect control, and rollback utility under equal model, memory, policy, and cost budgets.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.696302+00	3	\N	93876d956bdd6d96d35c57281a109a73baf5d55fb3e2152e426105b26ed29f91	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.696886+00	2026-11-10 14:54:52.527454+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c7e68e55-8ab6-4ab1-a214-243cb2dce97c	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08860v1	Mind the Divergence: Divergence-Guided Reliability-Aware Fusion and Adaptive LLM Prompting for Conversational Emotion Recognition	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08860	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08860	Jingwen Wang, Jiao Li, Yuhang Qian, Yuyin Wang, Min Zhang	arXiv:2611.08860v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Multimodal emotion recognition in conversations (MERC) integrates textual, acoustic, and visual cues to identify utterance-level emotions. However, existing approaches generally do not explicitly adapt multimodal processing to the degree of inter-modal predictive disagreement. In real conversations, semantic incongruity, concealed emotion, noisy observations, and ambiguity can affect modality-specific emotional evidence differently, while the reliability of individual modalities varies across samples. Rather than identifying each source of inconsistency separately, we model their common consequence for emotion recognition as predictive disagreement among unimodal emotion distributions over the same emotion-label set. Such disagreement makes indiscriminate fusion prone to propagating unreliable or competing evidence. Inspired by the Brunswik Lens Model, we propose MiD-ERC, a divergence-centric framework that quantifies inter-modal predictive disagreement using a normalized divergence score and estimates sample-specific modality reliability. MiD-ERC uses these signals to coordinate divergence-guided cross-modal gating, divergence-modulated reliability weighting, and divergence-conditioned adaptive LLM prompting. The framework is trained end-to-end using only standard emotion annotations, without requiring additional conflict labels, auxiliary data construction, or external retrieval modules. Experiments on IEMOCAP and MELD demonstrate that MiD-ERC achieves state-of-the-art performance, with particularly pronounced improvements on high-divergence samples.	arXiv:2611.08860v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Multimodal emotion recognition in conversations (MERC) integrates textual, acoustic, and visual cues to identify utterance-level emotions. However, existing approaches generally do not explicitly adapt multimodal processing to the degree of inter-modal predictive disagreement. In real conversations, semantic incongruity, concealed emotion, noisy observations, and ambiguity can affect modality-specific emotional evidence differently, while the reliability of individual modalities varies across samples. Rather than identifying each source of inconsistency separately, we model their common consequence for emotion recognition as predictive disagreement among unimodal emotion distributions over the same emotion-label set. Such disagreement makes indiscriminate fusion prone to propagating unreliable or competing evidence. Inspired by the Brunswik Lens Model, we propose MiD-ERC, a divergence-centric framework that quantifies inter-modal predictive disagreement using a normalized divergence score and estimates sample-specific modality reliability. MiD-ERC uses these signals to coordinate divergence-guided cross-modal gating, divergence-modulated reliability weighting, and divergence-conditioned adaptive LLM prompting. The framework is trained end-to-end using only standard emotion annotations, without requiring additional conflict labels, auxiliary data construction, or external retrieval modules. Experiments on IEMOCAP and MELD demonstrate that MiD-ERC achieves state-of-the-art performance, with particularly pronounced improvements on high-divergence samples.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.661912+00	4	\N	368363e62119c168fc2276667234c8cf4dff4f9e339e9f846e5cd90222223b27	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.662385+00	2026-11-10 15:45:59.109375+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bfa26ad2-b8cd-4d77-a31c-fbdfed92fc99	c9402267-ab70-45fa-b843-f47b6189bd14	\N	https://research.google/blog/introducing-groundsource-turning-news-reports-into-data-with-gemini/	Introducing Groundsource: Turning news reports into data with Gemini	https://research.google/blog/introducing-groundsource-turning-news-reports-into-data-with-gemini/	https://research.google/blog/introducing-groundsource-turning-news-reports-into-data-with-gemini/	\N	Climate & Sustainability	Climate & Sustainability	product_update	en	2026-03-12 10:42:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.162388+00	3	\N	9a7a58b2d1283a51fcba3123fb0ad7fb1c963b8f4d2d8d66af50102aa2d18fc5	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.162817+00	2026-11-10 14:18:06.367447+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1f919966-70da-4e1d-a5f2-244f2421b425	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08833v1	Beyond the Lower-Star Filtration: Alternative Filtration Methods for Persistent Homology	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08833	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08833	Innocent Abaa, Umar Islambekov	arXiv:2611.08833v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: In the lower-star filtration (LSF), simplices are assigned filtration values according to the maximum-vertex rule, with each simplex entering the filtration when its highest-valued vertex appears. A limitation of this construction is that some higher-dimensional simplices may enter simultaneously with their boundary faces, causing certain topological features to be filled immediately upon formation and thus not appear in the resulting persistence diagram. To address this limitation, we propose three alternative filtration methods: the \\(\\varepsilon\\)-lower-star filtration (ELSF), the \\(L_p\\) filtration (LpF), and the softmax filtration (SMF). {ELSF introduces a dimension-dependent delay to the maximum-vertex rule,} allowing higher-dimensional simplices to enter the filtration after their boundary faces, whereas {LpF and SMF use aggregation functions that depend on all vertex values rather} than solely on their maximum. By modifying how filtration values are assigned, these methods allow simplices to enter the filtration more progressively, so that topological features such as loops and voids may persist over longer filtration intervals rather than being filled immediately. Consequently, the resulting persistence diagrams may include persistence features that are not observed under LSF, potentially providing useful information for distinguishing data populations. Experimental results on simulated and real-world datasets demonstrate that the proposed filtration methods are computationally efficient and can provide greater sensitivity to structural differences in the data than LSF.	arXiv:2611.08833v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: In the lower-star filtration (LSF), simplices are assigned filtration values according to the maximum-vertex rule, with each simplex entering the filtration when its highest-valued vertex appears. A limitation of this construction is that some higher-dimensional simplices may enter simultaneously with their boundary faces, causing certain topological features to be filled immediately upon formation and thus not appear in the resulting persistence diagram. To address this limitation, we propose three alternative filtration methods: the \\(\\varepsilon\\)-lower-star filtration (ELSF), the \\(L_p\\) filtration (LpF), and the softmax filtration (SMF). {ELSF introduces a dimension-dependent delay to the maximum-vertex rule,} allowing higher-dimensional simplices to enter the filtration after their boundary faces, whereas {LpF and SMF use aggregation functions that depend on all vertex values rather} than solely on their maximum. By modifying how filtration values are assigned, these methods allow simplices to enter the filtration more progressively, so that topological features such as loops and voids may persist over longer filtration intervals rather than being filled immediately. Consequently, the resulting persistence diagrams may include persistence features that are not observed under LSF, potentially providing useful information for distinguishing data populations. Experimental results on simulated and real-world datasets demonstrate that the proposed filtration methods are computationally efficient and can provide greater sensitivity to structural differences in the data than LSF.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.706997+00	3	\N	015a00cdbaf94d28689d81e910698f0bfdec14cc71d4ecc60a7c427253af8e0f	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.707492+00	2026-11-10 14:55:02.234317+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
