e34d9821-23d7-418c-a7c7-2f7dc61213e2	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.01930v4	MCGI: Manifold-Consistent Graph Indexing for Billion-Scale Disk-Resident Vector Search	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.01930	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.01930	Dongfang Zhao	arXiv:2601.01930v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Graph-based Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search often suffers from performance degradation in high-dimensional spaces due to the Euclidean-Geodesic mismatch, where greedy routing diverges from the underlying data manifold. To address this challenge, this paper presents Manifold-Consistent Graph Indexing (MCGI), a geometry-aware and disk-resident indexing method that leverages Local Intrinsic Dimensionality (LID) to allocate the search effort of a graph index according to the intrinsic geometry of data. Specifically, MCGI estimates the LID around each query from the distances that its own search has already computed and resizes the candidate list of the query once, so that queries in high-LID regions receive longer lists while queries in low-LID regions terminate earlier; the estimate itself costs no additional disk reads. Theoretical analysis shows that the success probability of any fixed search list decays exponentially with the LID of the query. At matched high recall, MCGI raises the query throughput of DiskANN on the same standard Vamana index by up to $1.43\\times$ on three million-scale benchmarks and by up to $1.24\\times$ on the billion-scale SIFT1B and Deep1B.	arXiv:2601.01930v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Graph-based Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search often suffers from performance degradation in high-dimensional spaces due to the Euclidean-Geodesic mismatch, where greedy routing diverges from the underlying data manifold. To address this challenge, this paper presents Manifold-Consistent Graph Indexing (MCGI), a geometry-aware and disk-resident indexing method that leverages Local Intrinsic Dimensionality (LID) to allocate the search effort of a graph index according to the intrinsic geometry of data. Specifically, MCGI estimates the LID around each query from the distances that its own search has already computed and resizes the candidate list of the query once, so that queries in high-LID regions receive longer lists while queries in low-LID regions terminate earlier; the estimate itself costs no additional disk reads. Theoretical analysis shows that the success probability of any fixed search list decays exponentially with the LID of the query. At matched high recall, MCGI raises the query throughput of DiskANN on the same standard Vamana index by up to $1.43\\times$ on three million-scale benchmarks and by up to $1.24\\times$ on the billion-scale SIFT1B and Deep1B.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.52227+00	3	\N	c11bd96675e8c75041c0a83c3e986f1c612cee0766487d897fa58b0c1d60c1df	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.522649+00	2026-11-10 16:03:29.887577+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
