c5d6f53f-49be-45da-bb69-cbe1da6d7478	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08921v1	Frame: Form-Restricted Authoring with Model-Based Evaluation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08921	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08921	Nan Jiang, Mohammed Mohsin Hussain, Onkar Tiwari, Ankit Jain, Thong Q. Nguyen, Abhishek Agarwal, Vishal Chowdhary	arXiv:2611.08921v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Many professional reports reuse the structure of prior editions while updating the content. We formalize this setting as form-restricted report generation: given a sample report and new grounding documents, generate a report that preserves the sample's form while accurately reflecting the new evidence. We introduce FRAME, a benchmark of 120 EDGAR- and arXiv-derived instances across Financial Quarterly, Financial Annual, Academic Simple, and Academic Complex categories, built with a temporal three-snapshot pipeline. We evaluate outputs with three human-validated metrics: structural similarity via semantic tree-edit distance, coverage via information unit recall, and faithfulness via claim-level grounding verification. Across five LLMs, open-source models nearly match proprietary models on structure but lag on coverage and faithfulness; all models perform better on financial than academic reports, and performance declines with longer outputs and more complex grounding.	arXiv:2611.08921v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Many professional reports reuse the structure of prior editions while updating the content. We formalize this setting as form-restricted report generation: given a sample report and new grounding documents, generate a report that preserves the sample's form while accurately reflecting the new evidence. We introduce FRAME, a benchmark of 120 EDGAR- and arXiv-derived instances across Financial Quarterly, Financial Annual, Academic Simple, and Academic Complex categories, built with a temporal three-snapshot pipeline. We evaluate outputs with three human-validated metrics: structural similarity via semantic tree-edit distance, coverage via information unit recall, and faithfulness via claim-level grounding verification. Across five LLMs, open-source models nearly match proprietary models on structure but lag on coverage and faithfulness; all models perform better on financial than academic reports, and performance declines with longer outputs and more complex grounding.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:00.670639+00	3	\N	66aa8d0a7616b9c4c31661012254c19db486cf77ca60d5ddc8f64e3efa23bf0c	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:00.671092+00	2026-11-10 15:46:09.067471+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
48f80bd4-972e-430a-945c-b4cd9a977ba6	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/daybreak-red-aws-govcloud	Daybreak Red is now available in AWS GovCloud (US-East)	https://openai.com/index/daybreak-red-aws-govcloud	https://openai.com/index/daybreak-red-aws-govcloud	\N	Daybreak Red brings GPT-5.6 Cyber to approved customers in AWS GovCloud (US-East) through Amazon Bedrock for authorized cybersecurity work.	Daybreak Red brings GPT-5.6 Cyber to approved customers in AWS GovCloud (US-East) through Amazon Bedrock for authorized cybersecurity work.	product_update	en	2026-11-04 07:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.895265+00	4	\N	13716133f047ab14516e1413446e24d56c5bd1238ce44cdeeaba3909500ae20c	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.895685+00	2026-11-10 14:43:21.956837+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
850db20d-9723-4214-9425-0ebb27e279ed	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.17778v2	Self-Distillation is Optimal Among Spectral Shrinkage Estimators	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17778	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17778	Radu Lecoiu, Debarghya Mukherjee, Pragya Sur	arXiv:2605.17778v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Self-distillation has emerged as a promising technique for improving model performance in modern machine learning systems. We develop the statistical foundations of self-distillation in high-dimensional linear regression by introducing and analyzing a broad class of estimators, namely spectral shrinkage estimators. We consider both spiked and general covariance matrices. For the former, we establish that when the covariance has $s$ distinct spikes, $s$-step self-distillation achieves optimal prediction among spectral shrinkage estimators, outperforming well-known estimators in statistics and machine learning. Moreover, we show that $s$ steps are necessary for optimality: any $(s-k)$-step distilled estimator is strictly suboptimal for $1 \\leq k \\leq s$. For general covariance matrices, we show that self-distillation approaches the optimal limiting prediction risk among continuous spectral shrinkage estimators as the number of distillation steps increases. We further develop a generalized cross-validation procedure for selecting the distillation parameters from data and establish an asymptotic oracle guarantee for the resulting estimator. We also study a federated approach where multiple data centers share spectral shrinkage estimators and a common server seeks to aggregate them to achieve optimal performance. In this case, we find that the best local rule again takes the form of self-distillation, though it differs from the optimal rule when data are hosted centrally on a single server. Together, our results explain why self-distillation improves predictive performance and provide a broader statistical framework that connects it to classical shrinkage-based methods.	arXiv:2605.17778v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Self-distillation has emerged as a promising technique for improving model performance in modern machine learning systems. We develop the statistical foundations of self-distillation in high-dimensional linear regression by introducing and analyzing a broad class of estimators, namely spectral shrinkage estimators. We consider both spiked and general covariance matrices. For the former, we establish that when the covariance has $s$ distinct spikes, $s$-step self-distillation achieves optimal prediction among spectral shrinkage estimators, outperforming well-known estimators in statistics and machine learning. Moreover, we show that $s$ steps are necessary for optimality: any $(s-k)$-step distilled estimator is strictly suboptimal for $1 \\leq k \\leq s$. For general covariance matrices, we show that self-distillation approaches the optimal limiting prediction risk among continuous spectral shrinkage estimators as the number of distillation steps increases. We further develop a generalized cross-validation procedure for selecting the distillation parameters from data and establish an asymptotic oracle guarantee for the resulting estimator. We also study a federated approach where multiple data centers share spectral shrinkage estimators and a common server seeks to aggregate them to achieve optimal performance. In this case, we find that the best local rule again takes the form of self-distillation, though it differs from the optimal rule when data are hosted centrally on a single server. Together, our results explain why self-distillation improves predictive performance and provide a broader statistical framework that connects it to classical shrinkage-based methods.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.475343+00	3	\N	72f9c36aae5b63ce8e4becbaa9cd0f82111be9ece9d46690eef251f6cd2f17e7	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.475734+00	2026-11-10 15:33:16.328453+00	pen