c50d1c51-a577-40f3-b65b-45cff927a639	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/advancing-ai-safety-through-state-and-federal-action	The US is advancing AI safety through state and federal action	https://openai.com/index/advancing-ai-safety-through-state-and-federal-action	https://openai.com/index/advancing-ai-safety-through-state-and-federal-action	\N	OpenAI outlines a “reverse federalism” approach to AI governance, where state laws help build a national framework for safe, democratic AI.	OpenAI outlines a “reverse federalism” approach to AI governance, where state laws help build a national framework for safe, democratic AI.	news	en	2026-07-15 12:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.51936+00	4	\N	2eb3e99b9324a7a5bfd537ff7fe445ae5fbdde3d78de41e6ce25a04fff9853e5	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.519922+00	2026-11-10 14:28:37.818833+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b134f64c-cec8-4ab6-a152-bb9c94f83659	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.06840v5	Characterize Then Distill: Mechanistic Reasoning in Large Output Spaces	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06840	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06840	Debjyoti Saha Roy	arXiv:2606.06840v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Reasoning-trained language models can select, zero-shot, a small set of relevant labels from thousands to hundreds of thousands of candidates. We ask how they do it mechanistically, and whether the mechanism can be distilled. We treat each decision as a token-level event scored by the model's own margin: the token that picks a coarse region of the label space, the tokens that pick a label within it, and the token where the output departs from a near-miss named earlier. Attribution, exact mean-ablation, knock-in and a null calibration give individual attention heads causal standing. On clinical coding of MIMIC-IV discharge summaries, with all 5,651 candidate codes in context, a small, global, phase-structured set of heads is necessary and sufficient on essentially every summary; distinct head families read the candidate region and the earlier near-miss, and blocking what they read removes their effect; for mentions elicitable in the reasoning, a disjoint mid-layer set makes the region elicitable several tokens before the code. The structure recurs in a second reasoner. We introduce MISTILL, which, unlike chain-of-thought distillation, also supervises the student's pooled attention at these decision events. Read on heads found after training, it nearly doubles the causal recovery of the contrastive decision in a cross-family student and adds a small, seed-stable gain in one that already carries most of it, with no detected task difference at matched precision; under matched selection it exceeds a frozen random-head pool on both recovery and task score. On two corpora with human-annotated evidence (one read zero-shot, one in domain), a MISTILL student's decision heads attend to each code's evidence more, and more specifically, than a chain-of-thought-distilled student's. A pre-registered evidence-flip test detected no improvement in evidence-dependent decisions through the tested routes.	arXiv:2606.06840v5 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Reasoning-trained language models can select, zero-shot, a small set of relevant labels from thousands to hundreds of thousands of candidates. We ask how they do it mechanistically, and whether the mechanism can be distilled. We treat each decision as a token-level event scored by the model's own margin: the token that picks a coarse region of the label space, the tokens that pick a label within it, and the token where the output departs from a near-miss named earlier. Attribution, exact mean-ablation, knock-in and a null calibration give individual attention heads causal standing. On clinical coding of MIMIC-IV discharge summaries, with all 5,651 candidate codes in context, a small, global, phase-structured set of heads is necessary and sufficient on essentially every summary; distinct head families read the candidate region and the earlier near-miss, and blocking what they read removes their effect; for mentions elicitable in the reasoning, a disjoint mid-layer set makes the region elicitable several tokens before the code. The structure recurs in a second reasoner. We introduce MISTILL, which, unlike chain-of-thought distillation, also supervises the student's pooled attention at these decision events. Read on heads found after training, it nearly doubles the causal recovery of the contrastive decision in a cross-family student and adds a small, seed-stable gain in one that already carries most of it, with no detected task difference at matched precision; under matched selection it exceeds a frozen random-head pool on both recovery and task score. On two corpora with human-annotated evidence (one read zero-shot, one in domain), a MISTILL student's decision heads attend to each code's evidence more, and more specifically, than a chain-of-thought-distilled student's. A pre-registered evidence-flip test detected no improvement in evidence-dependent decisions through the tested routes.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.496769+00	3	\N	d6e6ed19d03deff177c3ad3fd78f985f0df481cebed3ce72cad39a1204dbcd2c	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.49734+00	2026-11-10 15:33:49.628108+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
