29ca920b-1743-49c6-8d7a-7250082b9047	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09173v1	TOFS: Feature Selection with Swap Supervision for Semantic Communication over Fading Channels	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09173	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09173	Zhiyi Zhu, Eiji Takimoto, Patrick Finnerty, Chikara Ohta	arXiv:2611.09173v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Task-oriented semantic communication transmits task-relevant features to support receiver inference under limited communication resources and adverse channel conditions. For image classification, the usefulness of a feature depends on the other transmitted features and the distortion introduced by the wireless channel. Effective feature prioritization should therefore account for classification performance after transmission of the selected features. This paper proposes a Task-Oriented Feature Selection (\\textit{TOFS}) framework and method, which evaluates feature replacements using receiver classification loss. Loss differences confirmed under independent transmission samples provide pairwise ranking supervision for a selector network. The trained selector predicts priorities in a single forward pass. CIFAR-10 simulations demonstrate improved classification accuracy over an importance-based feature selection baseline at the same transmitted feature count and average transmit power. The proposed \\textit{TOFS} framework shows 84\\% accuracy performance under Rayleigh fading and Gaussian noise, compared with the 68\\% performance of a SOTA method.	arXiv:2611.09173v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Task-oriented semantic communication transmits task-relevant features to support receiver inference under limited communication resources and adverse channel conditions. For image classification, the usefulness of a feature depends on the other transmitted features and the distortion introduced by the wireless channel. Effective feature prioritization should therefore account for classification performance after transmission of the selected features. This paper proposes a Task-Oriented Feature Selection (\\textit{TOFS}) framework and method, which evaluates feature replacements using receiver classification loss. Loss differences confirmed under independent transmission samples provide pairwise ranking supervision for a selector network. The trained selector predicts priorities in a single forward pass. CIFAR-10 simulations demonstrate improved classification accuracy over an importance-based feature selection baseline at the same transmitted feature count and average transmit power. The proposed \\textit{TOFS} framework shows 84\\% accuracy performance under Rayleigh fading and Gaussian noise, compared with the 68\\% performance of a SOTA method.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.439992+00	3	\N	279966dbbf3be2156bb9c026f105f9a09da08ea9bc44ee160d73354de35476ea	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.441308+00	2026-11-10 14:49:52.806229+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ff5bff22-8d7a-4946-bf08-9aed47363e18	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09175v1	SIFR: Stable Signed Flow Reinforcement	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09175	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09175	Runlong Liao, Xingqian Xu, Lizhang Chen, Baiyu Su, Yufan Huang, Zhonghao Wang, Akash Gokul, Ming-Yu Liu, Qiang Liu, Yogesh Balaji	arXiv:2611.09175v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Reward-based fine-tuning of flow models requires effectively promoting high-reward and suppressing low-reward samples, while maintaining training stability over many rounds of iterative updates. Iterative reward fine-tuning provides a simple starting point, but the positive reward weights give no explicit unfitting signal, and repeated reweighting concentrates the distribution and causes collapse. We propose Stable Signed Flow Reinforcement (SIFR), which replaces the reward weights by signed advantages to supply the missing unfitting signal, and constrains the velocity drift from a reference model to stabilize the updates, with a Lagrange multiplier that adapts the regularization strength to a prescribed drift budget. We further show that existing flow RL methods fall within this framework: they implicitly minimize the signed loss, differing in their regularization choices. We validate SIFR on SD3.5 Medium (2.5B) and FLUX.2 Klein (9B) and scale it to Cosmos 3 Super Text2Image (64B): across all 12 backbone--reward pairs with preference and rule-based rewards, it matches or exceeds existing methods, reaches their peak rewards much faster, and keeps improving over long training where they plateau or collapse.	arXiv:2611.09175v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Reward-based fine-tuning of flow models requires effectively promoting high-reward and suppressing low-reward samples, while maintaining training stability over many rounds of iterative updates. Iterative reward fine-tuning provides a simple starting point, but the positive reward weights give no explicit unfitting signal, and repeated reweighting concentrates the distribution and causes collapse. We propose Stable Signed Flow Reinforcement (SIFR), which replaces the reward weights by signed advantages to supply the missing unfitting signal, and constrains the velocity drift from a reference model to stabilize the updates, with a Lagrange multiplier that adapts the regularization strength to a prescribed drift budget. We further show that existing flow RL methods fall within this framework: they implicitly minimize the signed loss, differing in their regularization choices. We validate SIFR on SD3.5 Medium (2.5B) and FLUX.2 Klein (9B) and scale it to Cosmos 3 Super Text2Image (64B): across all 12 backbone--reward pairs with preference and rule-based rewards, it matches or exceeds existing methods, reaches their peak rewards much faster, and keeps improving over long training where they plateau or collapse.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.443524+00	4	\N	6d6528c0a8ce6a29c0fd227847edcaf03f39c36a235dded0d0c23c903a35892e	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.443931+00	2026-11-10 14:49:56.071866+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
