065c9351-075c-4923-9764-c0f5340e9289	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09248v1	Topology as a Design Variable: Joint Architecture-Sizing Optimization for SRAM	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09248	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09248	Can Wang, Junhao Ma, Yikai Wang, Shan Shen, Wei W. Xing	arXiv:2611.09248v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: SRAM's power-performance-area-stability trade-off depends on bitcell topology, a discrete architectural choice that interacts non-separably with continuous transistor sizing. However, no open-source SRAM tool treats topology as an optimizable variable. OpenRAM generates SRAM layouts but performs no optimization, while OpenYield supports high-fidelity evaluation but restricts optimization to scalar sizing of a fixed 6T cell. OpenYieldV2 closes this gap through three integrated contributions. First, a fully parameterized compiler generates complete 6T and 10T SRAM macros with peripheral circuits and replica-bitline self-timing, enabling topology comparison under identical simulation conditions. Second, a few-shot TabPFN surrogate learns topology-dependent PPA responses with limited full-macro SPICE samples. Third, a coarse-to-refined multi-objective optimizer combines global exploration of discrete architectural choices with local refinement of continuous transistor sizes. Experiments across varying design conditions reveal a topology decision boundary that shifts with operating constraints rather than following a fixed design rule. Jointly optimizing across this boundary improves hypervolume by up to 17.3\\% over the strongest single-topology baseline. OpenYieldV2 is open-source to support reproducible SRAM design research.	arXiv:2611.09248v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: SRAM's power-performance-area-stability trade-off depends on bitcell topology, a discrete architectural choice that interacts non-separably with continuous transistor sizing. However, no open-source SRAM tool treats topology as an optimizable variable. OpenRAM generates SRAM layouts but performs no optimization, while OpenYield supports high-fidelity evaluation but restricts optimization to scalar sizing of a fixed 6T cell. OpenYieldV2 closes this gap through three integrated contributions. First, a fully parameterized compiler generates complete 6T and 10T SRAM macros with peripheral circuits and replica-bitline self-timing, enabling topology comparison under identical simulation conditions. Second, a few-shot TabPFN surrogate learns topology-dependent PPA responses with limited full-macro SPICE samples. Third, a coarse-to-refined multi-objective optimizer combines global exploration of discrete architectural choices with local refinement of continuous transistor sizes. Experiments across varying design conditions reveal a topology decision boundary that shifts with operating constraints rather than following a fixed design rule. Jointly optimizing across this boundary improves hypervolume by up to 17.3\\% over the strongest single-topology baseline. OpenYieldV2 is open-source to support reproducible SRAM design research.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.467615+00	3	\N	128e319efd04c5c5042921f397f6c22c4ef1a28d62e98cb02afb3f7becbf95f3	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.468066+00	2026-11-10 14:50:30.923713+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
