99c92cbb-7f25-4aa5-bb7a-b9a4ee2f82ea	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09344v1	Probably Safe Sim-to-Real Transfer with Lifetime Validity	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09344	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09344	Rohan Tangri, Jan-Peter Calliess	arXiv:2611.09344v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We present Dejavu, a method that makes sim-to-real transfer of reinforcement learning policies in constrained Markov decision processes probably safe over an unbounded sequence of policy updates. An anytime valid test using per-decision importance sampling and a betting confidence sequence certifies each candidate trained in simulation before deployment using only trajectories collected by a safe baseline policy operating in production. Simulation quality does not affect the safety guarantee, only the production sample efficiency of the method. Finally, a summable per-test failure probability yields the lifetime valid property. Empirical results across three sim-to-real benchmarks support the theoretical work.	arXiv:2611.09344v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We present Dejavu, a method that makes sim-to-real transfer of reinforcement learning policies in constrained Markov decision processes probably safe over an unbounded sequence of policy updates. An anytime valid test using per-decision importance sampling and a betting confidence sequence certifies each candidate trained in simulation before deployment using only trajectories collected by a safe baseline policy operating in production. Simulation quality does not affect the safety guarantee, only the production sample efficiency of the method. Finally, a summable per-test failure probability yields the lifetime valid property. Empirical results across three sim-to-real benchmarks support the theoretical work.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.513391+00	4	\N	b68408985e1a6852163b38e6a340231d943d627720783a19fadc71a8ba9d2800	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.513814+00	2026-11-10 14:51:06.476512+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
319944b8-9eed-4d3b-a392-6e87ea316246	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11821v1	Direct Trajectory Optimization for Inference-Time Alignment of Text-to-Image Diffusion Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11821	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11821	Huixuan Zhang, Xiaojun Wan	arXiv:2611.11821v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Alignment of text-to-image diffusion models with reward functions is a key challenge in controllable generation. Existing approaches typically rely on optimizing model inputs or fine-tuning denoising networks, which requires computationally expensive backpropagation through large denoising models. In this work, we propose Direct Trajectory Optimization (DTO), a training-free framework that performs alignment by directly optimizing the sampling trajectory of diffusion models. Specifically, DTO treats intermediate denoising scores and sampled noise as the optimization variables, eliminating the need for gradients through the denoising network. To mitigate the out-of-distribution problem, we introduce Reward-Optimized Score Guidance, which interpolates between the optimized scores and the pretrained model predictions to stabilize sampling, while providing a provable theoretical guarantee of reward improvement under constrained distributions. Empirically, DTO improves alignment performance across three diffusion models on two benchmarks, outperforming existing inference-time alignment methods while maintaining high efficiency. These results suggest that trajectory-level optimization is a promising direction for efficient and effective diffusion model alignment.	arXiv:2611.11821v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Alignment of text-to-image diffusion models with reward functions is a key challenge in controllable generation. Existing approaches typically rely on optimizing model inputs or fine-tuning denoising networks, which requires computationally expensive backpropagation through large denoising models. In this work, we propose Direct Trajectory Optimization (DTO), a training-free framework that performs alignment by directly optimizing the sampling trajectory of diffusion models. Specifically, DTO treats intermediate denoising scores and sampled noise as the optimization variables, eliminating the need for gradients through the denoising network. To mitigate the out-of-distribution problem, we introduce Reward-Optimized Score Guidance, which interpolates between the optimized scores and the pretrained model predictions to stabilize sampling, while providing a provable theoretical guarantee of reward improvement under constrained distributions. Empirically, DTO improves alignment performance across three diffusion models on two benchmarks, outperforming existing inference-time alignment methods while maintaining high efficiency. These results suggest that trajectory-level optimization is a promising direction for efficient and effective diffusion model alignment.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:55.717776+00	\N	\N	2001e3917e68e86106cabb7d68ae668ea713ce64ae0908a6251a5efb5b76dcd9	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:55.718089+00	2026-11-10 16:29:55.71809+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
