73b7df3a-4038-41a7-974a-62ae2b4e261d	eb530fbb-88dd-42d2-a6be-0b352a46d09a	\N	https://the-decoder.com/?p=42615	Nvidia espionage scandal weakens Huang's lobby against tighter AI chip export controls	https://the-decoder.com/nvidia-espionage-scandal-weakens-huangs-lobby-against-tighter-ai-chip-export-controls/	https://the-decoder.com/nvidia-espionage-scandal-weakens-huangs-lobby-against-tighter-ai-chip-export-controls/	Maximilian Schreiner	Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has admitted to an extramarital affair with a woman US intelligence agencies classify as a suspected Chinese informant. The board has launched an independent investigation. The affair weakens Huang's lobbying efforts against stricter AI chip export controls, with a newly elected Democratic Congress poised to ramp up scrutiny of AI chip smuggling to China and push for tighter restrictions. The article Nvidia espionage scandal weakens Huang's lobby against tighter AI chip export controls appeared first on The Decoder .	Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has admitted to an extramarital affair with a woman US intelligence agencies classify as a suspected Chinese informant. The board has launched an independent investigation. The affair weakens Huang's lobbying efforts against stricter AI chip export controls, with a newly elected Democratic Congress poised to ramp up scrutiny of AI chip smuggling to China and push for tighter restrictions. The article Nvidia espionage scandal weakens Huang's lobby against tighter AI chip export controls appeared first on The Decoder .	news	en	2026-11-09 11:38:43+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.200991+00	4	\N	c8dec1f1462faf84a752602087e10b123f93a8ba94cf34968e9dcd2cadc50220	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.202316+00	2026-11-10 16:08:48.445792+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1bc5fcfb-baab-4f04-b403-3deefeeae372	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.00967v2	TrajWiki: Source-Grounded Memory Trajectories for Long-Horizon Dialogue Agents	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00967	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00967	Jingyu Sun, Yuyang Xue, Mingyang Li, Zhengtao Yao, Jiachen Li, Yang Cui, Wenhao Cai, Haozhe Liu, Fangying Wang, Magdalene Katharina Montgomery, Syed Murtuza Baker, Hongpeng Zhou	arXiv:2608.00967v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language model agents have shown strong capabilities in generating coherent and contextually appropriate responses, yet robust long-horizon dialogue remains limited by the lack of external memory that is traceable, updatable, and diagnostically transparent. Existing memory-augmented agents often store memories as isolated records or overwritable states, making it difficult to preserve how information originates, evolves, conflicts, or becomes obsolete over time. We propose \\textbf{TrajWiki}, a trajectory-based memory framework for long-horizon conversational agents. Instead of treating memory as static entries, TrajWiki represents each memory as a source-grounded evolution trajectory, maintained through immutable episodic snapshots and claim-level operations such as \\texttt{ADD}, \\texttt{REVISE}, and \\texttt{DEPRECATE}. To reduce fragmentation and retrieval cost, TrajWiki further introduces \\textbf{Memory Wiki}, a persistent intermediate layer that incrementally compiles dialogue history into structured and interlinked wiki pages capturing salient entities, events, quantities, topics, and conflicts. At inference time, queries are routed hierarchically from relevant wiki pages to linked memory trajectories, then to corresponding snapshots and source messages for evidence-grounded answer synthesis. Experiments on LoCoMo and MedMT show that TrajWiki improves long-horizon dialogue performance across both open-source and closed-source LLM backbones, while providing greater interpretability and diagnostic visibility into memory evolution, retrieval failures, and answer generation.	arXiv:2608.00967v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language model agents have shown strong capabilities in generating coherent and contextually appropriate responses, yet robust long-horizon dialogue remains limited by the lack of external memory that is traceable, updatable, and diagnostically transparent. Existing memory-augmented agents often store memories as isolated records or overwritable states, making it difficult to preserve how information originates, evolves, conflicts, or becomes obsolete over time. We propose \\textbf{TrajWiki}, a trajectory-based memory framework for long-horizon conversational agents. Instead of treating memory as static entries, TrajWiki represents each memory as a source-grounded evolution trajectory, maintained through immutable episodic snapshots and claim-level operations such as \\texttt{ADD}, \\texttt{REVISE}, and \\texttt{DEPRECATE}. To reduce fragmentation and retrieval cost, TrajWiki further introduces \\textbf{Memory Wiki}, a persistent intermediate layer that incrementally compiles dialogue history into structured and interlinked wiki pages capturing salient entities, events, quantities, topics, and conflicts. At inference time, queries are routed hierarchically from relevant wiki pages to linked memory trajectories, then to corresponding snapshots and source messages for evidence-grounded answer synthesis. Experiments on LoCoMo and MedMT show that TrajWiki improves long-horizon dialogue performance across both open-source and closed-source LLM backbones, while providing greater interpretability and diagnostic visibility into memory evolution, retrieval failures, and answer generation.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.112625+00	\N	\N	626d32a078f1de4984b9442e0e0fad392c2c3b5214743791aff59116bcbcf137	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.112936+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.112937+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a11236fc-2269-43ee-92cc-3ede61a57dd5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.06333v2	Subspace-Aware Sparse Autoencoders for Effective Mechanistic Interpretability	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06333	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06333	Seyed Arshan Dalili, Mehrdad Mahdavi	arXiv:2606.06333v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are widely used for mechanistic interpretability in large language models, yet their standard formulation assigns each latent feature a single decoder direction, implicitly assuming features to be one-dimensional. We show that this assumption can induce feature splitting when model features are multi-dimensional. Geometrically, accurately reconstructing a feature under a fixed co-activation budget can require exponentially many decoder directions as its dimension grows. From an optimization perspective, adding decoder directions can strictly lower the $\\ell_1$-regularized population risk, even when a basis can already reconstruct the feature exactly. Motivated by these results, we introduce Subspace-Aware Sparse Autoencoders (SASA), which replace single-vector decoders with learned subspaces, enforce sparsity through Top-$s$ group gating, and adapt group rank with nuclear-norm regularization. Under an idealized single-feature model, we prove that the SASA objective is minimized by a single group whose reconstruction map recovers the feature subspace. We also derive polynomial sample complexity bounds for subspace estimation within a group and a lower bound on the samples needed to observe every atom in a fixed fragmented dictionary. Experiments on GPT-2 and Mistral-7B show reduced absorption compared with SAEs. Matched-budget GPT-2 comparisons show lower absorption for concept families and more accurate prediction of their members using a small number of latent coordinates, at a reconstruction cost. The code is available at https://github.com/arshandalili/sasa.	arXiv:2606.06333v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are widely used for mechanistic interpretability in large language models, yet their standard formulation assigns each latent feature a single decoder direction, implicitly assuming features to be one-dimensional. We show that this assumption can induce feature splitting when model features are multi-dimensional. Geometrically, accurately reconstructing a feature under a fixed co-activation budget can require exponentially many decoder directions as its dimension grows. From an optimization perspective, adding decoder directions can strictly lower the $\\ell_1$-regularized population risk, even when a basis can already reconstruct the feature exactly. Motivated by these results, we introduce Subspace-Aware Sparse Autoencoders (SASA), which replace single-vector decoders with learned subspaces, enforce sparsity through Top-$s$ group gating, and adapt group rank with nuclear-norm regularization. Under an idealized single-feature model, we prove that the SASA objective is minimized by a single group whose reconstruction map recovers the feature subspace. We also derive polynomial sample complexity bounds for subspace estimation within a group and a lower bound on the samples needed to observe every atom in a fixed fragmented dictionary. Experiments on GPT-2 and Mistral-7B show reduced absorption compared with SAEs. Matched-budget GPT-2 comparisons show lower absorption for concept families and more accurate prediction of their members using a small number of latent coordinates, at a reconstruction cost. The code is available at https://github.com/arshandalili/sasa.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.185354+00	4	\N	710b3820246dad16a1e727336eea37993610641826bce530e12654d67159fc2c	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.185983+00	2026-11-10 15:27:51.499557+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
528b6866-23ec-41b1-907c-0365ba365b27	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09703v1	Empirical Study of Post-Quantum Cryptography Adoption Status in SSH Infrastructure	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09703	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09703	Md Ariful Haque, Sander Vinberg, Ben Edwards, Jo\\~ao Cruz	arXiv:2611.09703v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: With the anticipated arrival of Q-day, quantum computers threaten current cryptographic schemes for encryption and digital signatures. In response, major content delivery networks and cloud service providers are already adopting Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC). The current state-of-the-art focuses on benchmarking the operational performance of implementing PQC, but the real-world adoption status remains under-researched. This work utilizes large-scale SSH fingerprint data from a proprietary scanning tool to analyze PQC adoption across 1.52 million hosts and 27,012 entities. Our study shows that the PQC transition in the SSH protocol is underway but highly polarized: 45.2% of SSH hosts on port 22 support PQC, while 54.8% remain classical-only. At the entity level, 39.9% have enabled PQC on at least one host, whereas 60.1% have no PQC support. By correlating PQC adoption with industry, geography, and organizational size (digital and financial), this study provides a comprehensive empirical assessment of PQC adoption status in SSH infrastructure.	arXiv:2611.09703v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: With the anticipated arrival of Q-day, quantum computers threaten current cryptographic schemes for encryption and digital signatures. In response, major content delivery networks and cloud service providers are already adopting Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC). The current state-of-the-art focuses on benchmarking the operational performance of implementing PQC, but the real-world adoption status remains under-researched. This work utilizes large-scale SSH fingerprint data from a proprietary scanning tool to analyze PQC adoption across 1.52 million hosts and 27,012 entities. Our study shows that the PQC transition in the SSH protocol is underway but highly polarized: 45.2% of SSH hosts on port 22 support PQC, while 54.8% remain classical-only. At the entity level, 39.9% have enabled PQC on at least one host, whereas 60.1% have no PQC support. By correlating PQC adoption with industry, geography, and organizational size (digital and financial), this study provides a comprehensive empirical assessment of PQC adoption status in SSH infrastructure.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.275656+00	3	\N	82eb23c1ea60b9b3fda87b3bf4fceb08642277ca7de70fc680f50bb045c21f57	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.276114+00	2026-11-10 15:58:16.88071+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
53e9ccf3-48d8-42cd-aee8-1d3f2aa7565b	eb530fbb-88dd-42d2-a6be-0b352a46d09a	\N	https://the-decoder.com/?p=42618	Google ships four TPU variants in one year as it ramps up competition with Nvidia	https://the-decoder.com/google-ships-four-tpu-variants-in-one-year-as-it-ramps-up-competition-with-nvidia/	https://the-decoder.com/google-ships-four-tpu-variants-in-one-year-as-it-ramps-up-competition-with-nvidia/	Maximilian Schreiner	Google is expanding its TPU 8 family with two specialized variants: the TPU 8tx keeps sensitive data encrypted even during processing, while the TPU 8i Max doubles compute power and memory by placing two chips on a single package. With nearly 100 external customers now on board, Google is increasingly positioning its in-house chips as an alternative to Nvidia's GPUs. The article Google ships four TPU variants in one year as it ramps up competition with Nvidia appeared first on The Decoder .	Google is expanding its TPU 8 family with two specialized variants: the TPU 8tx keeps sensitive data encrypted even during processing, while the TPU 8i Max doubles compute power and memory by placing two chips on a single package. With nearly 100 external customers now on board, Google is increasingly positioning its in-house chips as an alternative to Nvidia's GPUs. The article Google ships four TPU variants in one year as it ramps up competition with Nvidia appeared first on The Decoder .	news	en	2026-11-09 12:59:02+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.196816+00	4	\N	4d36d4dee4402e1b3e88ef9d0cf3a56339edb4fa8a45534a4dc85878c90262e9	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.197265+00	2026-11-10 16:08:52.035996+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
722885d2-c1ac-493a-be9f-b7dee12e8bb9	eff33c27-9fc1-4abb-a69e-99f9ef74c427	\N	https://www.technologyreview.com/?p=1147654	Innovating with AI in the special chemicals industry	https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/11/09/1147654/innovating-with-ai-in-the-special-chemicals-industry/	https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/11/09/1147654/innovating-with-ai-in-the-special-chemicals-industry/	MIT Technology Review Insights	As companies look to accelerate innovation with artificial intelligence, cloud computing, and advanced modeling, the primary challenge is connecting new technologies to reliable data, scientific expertise, and the practical realities of bringing products to market. For specialty chemicals manufacturer Lubrizol, digital transformation is all about giving researchers the tools to test ideas faster, make better-informed…	As companies look to accelerate innovation with artificial intelligence, cloud computing, and advanced modeling, the primary challenge is connecting new technologies to reliable data, scientific expertise, and the practical realities of bringing products to market. For specialty chemicals manufacturer Lubrizol, digital transformation is all about giving researchers the tools to test ideas faster, make better-informed decisions, and develop more sustainable materials without compromising performance. Girish Rao, vice president of information technology at Lubrizol, says the company is building a digital foundation that connects business processes, modern data platforms, and cloud infrastructure to support its research and development (R&D) teams. Their goal is to combine decades of accumulated technical data with the deep expertise of chemists and engineers who understand customer needs as well as the physical constraints of their work. Data scientists can help identify patterns, test new hypotheses, and model possibilities before researchers conduct physical experiments. But, Rao emphasizes, “it’s not about replacing scientific judgment; it is about giving our scientists better tools.” Scientists, data scientists, and IT teams approach problems from different perspectives, prioritizing experimental validity, model performance, and platform security and scalability, respectively. By bringing these groups together in “fusion teams,” Lubrizol aligns them around shared objectives, starting with small pilots to build trust. “Once the trust is built between the team members and they know what value they’re about to create,” Rao says, “things go a lot better.” Cloud infrastructure also gives the company flexibility to access computing resources and modern tools without making the substantial investments required to build and maintain its own data centers. These capabilities could prove especially valuable in advancing sustainability. Digital modeling can help researchers evaluate more formulations, explore materials such as starch and corn-based alternatives, and assess trade-offs across a product’s life cycle. Promising a material in a laboratory is only the first step. Scaling production while meeting performance expectations, regulatory requirements, and financial constraints is still a tightrope walk, but AI’s ability to model multiple variables could help teams navigate those competing demands. Looking to the future, Rao expects AI, automation, advanced modeling, digital twins, and connected data to converge as laboratories become more intelligent and manufacturing plants more efficient. The opportunity, he suggests, lies in bringing these capabilities together responsibly and at scale. “It’s not just a single technology. I think it’s the combination of all of these, and it’ll be the ability to connect all these processes that are going to generate tremendous amounts of data.” This webcast is produced in partnership with Infosys. This content was produced by Insights, MIT Technology Review’s custom content arm, not its editorial staff. It was researched and written by humans, with any AI tools that may have been used limited to production processes under human oversight.	news	en	2026-11-09 13:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:48.519162+00	3	\N	e69ebeed49d44aec29cd0c14c8b4dadad72bf42c28526d0e469ceea7b82652b3	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:48.519957+00	2026-11-10 16:08:54.962324+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
595ed441-215d-46e3-a886-6309d6bfd6fb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09612v1	Kernel-Based Learning of Analytic Operators and Parametric PDEs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09612	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09612	Giovanni S. Alberti, Markus Holzleitner, Lorenzo Rosasco, Matteo Santacesaria	arXiv:2611.09612v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study kernel-based learning of analytic operators from finite samples, with a focus on how the regularity of the target should determine the choice of the kernel. We establish embeddings of analytic operators into vector-valued reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces and derive finite-sample, high-probability guarantees that quantify the uncertainty due to random sampling: in the noiseless setting, suitable analytic kernels yield rates exponential in a fractional power of the sample size, while Sobolev-type kernels yield polynomial rates. We apply the theory to the solution operator of a parametric elliptic PDE, where the training data are themselves finite-element approximations, and obtain bounds that separate the sampling and discretization contributions to the error and prescribe how the mesh size should be coupled to the sample size. The results are illustrated numerically.	arXiv:2611.09612v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We study kernel-based learning of analytic operators from finite samples, with a focus on how the regularity of the target should determine the choice of the kernel. We establish embeddings of analytic operators into vector-valued reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces and derive finite-sample, high-probability guarantees that quantify the uncertainty due to random sampling: in the noiseless setting, suitable analytic kernels yield rates exponential in a fractional power of the sample size, while Sobolev-type kernels yield polynomial rates. We apply the theory to the solution operator of a parametric elliptic PDE, where the training data are themselves finite-element approximations, and obtain bounds that separate the sampling and discretization contributions to the error and prescribe how the mesh size should be coupled to the sample size. The results are illustrated numerically.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.889634+00	3	\N	7987fc9913f7f3f04279c989112e3a3e2a76a79e00f8a18e41da45d5a45d5a5d	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.890119+00	2026-11-10 15:22:24.952806+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
affa1cc2-5645-4ded-9d5c-5ba188435d12	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09615v1	Contextual Search and Bilateral Trade with a Trace-Square-Root Potential	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09615	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09615	Romain Cosson, Federico Fusco, Anupam Gupta, Stefano Leonardi, Renato Paes Leme, Matteo Russo	arXiv:2611.09615v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We present a unified potential method for contextual search and bilateral trade with one-bit feedback and obtain order-tight algorithms for both problems. Our framework starts from a Bayesian perspective in which the unknown target (for contextual search) and the unknown pair of valuations (for bilateral trade) are sampled from a known prior distribution. In that model, the decision maker bases its prediction on the posterior distribution, obtained by conditioning on feedback received in previous rounds.\n  For Bayesian contextual search, we present an algorithm with a total loss bounded by $O(\\mathrm{Tr} \\sqrt \\Sigma)$, where $\\Sigma$ is the covariance matrix of the target's prior; and for contextual bilateral trade, our algorithm's regret is bounded by $O(\\mathrm{Tr} \\sqrt{\\Sigma_S}+\\mathrm{Tr} \\sqrt{\\Sigma_B})$, where $\\Sigma_S$ and $\\Sigma_B$ are the covariance matrices of the seller's and buyer's valuations.\n  We then use ideas from this Bayesian setting and adapt a ``dual'' of this trace-square-root potential to the classical worst-case setting, where the target and valuations are chosen adversarially from the unit Euclidean ball. For both contextual search and bilateral trade, we obtain tight $O(\\sqrt d)$-regret algorithms. These results match the existing lower bounds up to a constant factor and improve on the previous guarantees of $O(d\\log d)$ for contextual search and of $O(d6^d)$ for bilateral trade.	arXiv:2611.09615v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: We present a unified potential method for contextual search and bilateral trade with one-bit feedback and obtain order-tight algorithms for both problems. Our framework starts from a Bayesian perspective in which the unknown target (for contextual search) and the unknown pair of valuations (for bilateral trade) are sampled from a known prior distribution. In that model, the decision maker bases its prediction on the posterior distribution, obtained by conditioning on feedback received in previous rounds.\n  For Bayesian contextual search, we present an algorithm with a total loss bounded by $O(\\mathrm{Tr} \\sqrt \\Sigma)$, where $\\Sigma$ is the covariance matrix of the target's prior; and for contextual bilateral trade, our algorithm's regret is bounded by $O(\\mathrm{Tr} \\sqrt{\\Sigma_S}+\\mathrm{Tr} \\sqrt{\\Sigma_B})$, where $\\Sigma_S$ and $\\Sigma_B$ are the covariance matrices of the seller's and buyer's valuations.\n  We then use ideas from this Bayesian setting and adapt a ``dual'' of this trace-square-root potential to the classical worst-case setting, where the target and valuations are chosen adversarially from the unit Euclidean ball. For both contextual search and bilateral trade, we obtain tight $O(\\sqrt d)$-regret algorithms. These results match the existing lower bounds up to a constant factor and improve on the previous guarantees of $O(d\\log d)$ for contextual search and of $O(d6^d)$ for bilateral trade.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:58.892989+00	3	\N	c2b716bb7ac9609ecd577ef68d65c1ccefd8c0b7a76bb4b66c71e471f4f752cf	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:58.893495+00	2026-11-10 15:22:28.205399+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f69ff528-0f5f-47b0-a62e-b51958ce5e56	f8782f3e-cbda-4809-b208-87367f54d3c8	\N	https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/11/deepmind-reveals-gemini-robotics-2-5-and-open-safety-benchmark-asimov-jailbreak/	DeepMind reveals Gemini Robotics 2.5 and open safety benchmark "ASIMOV-jailbreak"	https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/11/deepmind-reveals-gemini-robotics-2-5-and-open-safety-benchmark-asimov-jailbreak/	https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/11/deepmind-reveals-gemini-robotics-2-5-and-open-safety-benchmark-asimov-jailbreak/	Ryan Whitwam	The new model is up to twice as successful at some tasks.	Google DeepMind has revealed a new generation of the AI-powered Gemini Robotics platform. In the 2.5 release, Google says its robotic AI is getting tantalizingly close to completing real-world tasks as well as humans do. In fact, some Gemini robots were able to get actual work done in Mercedes-Benz factories, and the team is preparing for a day when robots operate alongside humans with an open source safety benchmark. DeepMind's physical AI platform has several computational layers between user input and robotic action. There's the vision-language model piece, which is Gemini Embodied Reasoning. Essentially, it takes your instructions and uses live video to plan how the robot can accomplish the task. The other main component is the vision-language-action (VLA) that actually generates robot actions, like moving an actuator—that's Gemini Robotics, which has been bumped to version 2.5. DeepMind says that Gemini Robotics 2.5 is much better at tasks that require a high degree of dexterity. The model was evaluated across more than 200 activities, including serving food with tongs and using a pipette, as you would see in a laboratory. Google also showed that Apptronik’s Apollo 2 humanoid robot with Gemini Robotics 2.5 can now tie a bow with its five-fingered hands, a notable feat, because deformable objects (like ribbon) are notoriously difficult for robots to manipulate. Read full article Comments	article	en	2026-11-10 15:00:39+00	2026-11-10 16:29:52.947232+00	\N	\N	93a37857bde4969ab0d04970e1f817dd90bf9ce25c2a26c75f093200a6e2935e	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:52.94769+00	2026-11-10 16:29:52.947692+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8428030b-1922-4e1f-b09d-ecdade4909c7	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09712v1	ICare-DT: a digital twin for continuous forecasting of intensive care patients	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09712	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09712	Yanke Li, Yahang Qi, Yumi Kim, Diego Paez-Granados	arXiv:2611.09712v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Digital twins of intensive care patients should update forecasts from new data and predict treatment responses, but few studies test whether routine records support either. We propose ICare-DT, a Transformer-based twin that updates a latent patient state hourly from new measurements without retraining. We trained it on 140,473 eICU-CRD stays to forecast vital signs crossing their training-set means. On test data, later forecasts beat the twin's own hour-6 forecasts of the same windows in all 12 settings (+0.073 to +0.153 AUROC). On validation data, controls that withheld or swapped the new hours traced the gain to the patient's own measurements. Without refitting, the model kept its gain on a second database (8 of 9 settings, +0.025 to +0.114 AUROC) in a prespecified evaluation of the previously analyzed MIMIC-IV test split. Refitted within MIMIC-IV, a twin state fine-tuned on clinical endpoints gave gradient boosting a significant gain for acute kidney injury (+0.0035 AUROC; hypotension +0.0010, not significant). To test response prediction, we built a benchmark that replaces the treatments in real records with simulated doses and injects responses of known size, visible only at measurement times. Ridge models supervised with injected effects recovered them from real records only through frequently measured variables (heart rate) read at their measurement times. We asked standard learners to predict these effects for a dosing regime no patient had received. No standard learner outperformed predicting zero effect at an out-of-support query. When told how the simulated responses were generated, a separate learner recovered the effects from the same records without seeing their true values (R2 0.30 to 0.40 at one effect size). Routine records support forecasts that improve with each patient's new data, and our benchmark is a testbed for models that learn how responses are generated.	arXiv:2611.09712v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Digital twins of intensive care patients should update forecasts from new data and predict treatment responses, but few studies test whether routine records support either. We propose ICare-DT, a Transformer-based twin that updates a latent patient state hourly from new measurements without retraining. We trained it on 140,473 eICU-CRD stays to forecast vital signs crossing their training-set means. On test data, later forecasts beat the twin's own hour-6 forecasts of the same windows in all 12 settings (+0.073 to +0.153 AUROC). On validation data, controls that withheld or swapped the new hours traced the gain to the patient's own measurements. Without refitting, the model kept its gain on a second database (8 of 9 settings, +0.025 to +0.114 AUROC) in a prespecified evaluation of the previously analyzed MIMIC-IV test split. Refitted within MIMIC-IV, a twin state fine-tuned on clinical endpoints gave gradient boosting a significant gain for acute kidney injury (+0.0035 AUROC; hypotension +0.0010, not significant). To test response prediction, we built a benchmark that replaces the treatments in real records with simulated doses and injects responses of known size, visible only at measurement times. Ridge models supervised with injected effects recovered them from real records only through frequently measured variables (heart rate) read at their measurement times. We asked standard learners to predict these effects for a dosing regime no patient had received. No standard learner outperformed predicting zero effect at an out-of-support query. When told how the simulated responses were generated, a separate learner recovered the effects from the same records without seeing their true values (R2 0.30 to 0.40 at one effect size). Routine records support forecasts that improve with each patient's new data, and our benchmark is a testbed for models that learn how responses are generated.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.281837+00	4	\N	2083fb540060a9fb26233b33b3fc26cac2dc08f2382be4919b50010e65c729a1	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.282564+00	2026-11-10 15:58:25.420651+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ae831af0-b9d4-462c-9354-7a1b6de37ee3	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09721v1	MemoryDojo: Benchmarking Poisoning Through LLM Agent Memory	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09721	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09721	David H. Yang, Blake Bullwinkel, Amanda Minnich, Mohammad Mohammadi Amiri, Giorgio Severi	arXiv:2611.09721v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: LLM agents benefit from using memory systems to improve performance on long-horizon, multi-session tasks. However, memory introduces a critical attack surface. Malicious instructions encountered in one session may be stored and propagated through memory before being retrieved and acted upon in later sessions. Because memory systems differ in how they process and retrieve information, the downstream effects of poisoning can vary significantly across systems. To quantify the effects of poisoning across different memory architectures, we introduce MemoryDojo, a benchmark for tracing the lifecycle of poisoned information through agent memory and measuring its influence on subsequent agent behavior. MemoryDojo is easily extensible, consists of 155 multi-session long-horizon trajectories covering four domains, and supports five different memory systems. Through our evaluation, we find that all memory systems are susceptible to memory poisoning, with substantial variation in downstream effects. We further propose a memory-aware poison optimization process that increases the poisoning efficacy, highlighting differences between memory architectures. We hope that MemoryDojo will support research on understanding and mitigating agentic memory risks.	arXiv:2611.09721v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: LLM agents benefit from using memory systems to improve performance on long-horizon, multi-session tasks. However, memory introduces a critical attack surface. Malicious instructions encountered in one session may be stored and propagated through memory before being retrieved and acted upon in later sessions. Because memory systems differ in how they process and retrieve information, the downstream effects of poisoning can vary significantly across systems. To quantify the effects of poisoning across different memory architectures, we introduce MemoryDojo, a benchmark for tracing the lifecycle of poisoned information through agent memory and measuring its influence on subsequent agent behavior. MemoryDojo is easily extensible, consists of 155 multi-session long-horizon trajectories covering four domains, and supports five different memory systems. Through our evaluation, we find that all memory systems are susceptible to memory poisoning, with substantial variation in downstream effects. We further propose a memory-aware poison optimization process that increases the poisoning efficacy, highlighting differences between memory architectures. We hope that MemoryDojo will support research on understanding and mitigating agentic memory risks.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.285866+00	4	\N	76e1e3eb986d727b1265bcd5d155a17c42c366a258c659fe1c365831ec0c7d89	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.286308+00	2026-11-10 15:58:28.68438+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
