619ad81f-3d8b-4a03-b8a5-24558a34efc4	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08967v1	Using an LLM to Tackle Difficult Cases in NER for Nepali Tweets	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08967	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08967	Claire Wang	arXiv:2611.08967v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Named entity recognition (NER) is challenging for social media data in a low-resource language. LLMs often lag behind pretrained language models (PLMs) in NER performance, but they show promise in handling cases that PLMs struggle with. Using a dataset of Nepali tweets, we split the test set into "easy" and "hard" cases using token probabilities from a fine-tuned pretrained language model (PLM). We then evaluate an LLM on the hard test cases using 16 different prompt configurations. We find that selecting examples based on semantic similarity and increasing the number of examples improves F1 score by almost 10 points from the baseline configuration. Although the LLM underperforms the PLM on the full and easy test sets, it surpasses the PLM on the hard test set, resulting in an improved score when the two models are combined.	arXiv:2611.08967v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Named entity recognition (NER) is challenging for social media data in a low-resource language. LLMs often lag behind pretrained language models (PLMs) in NER performance, but they show promise in handling cases that PLMs struggle with. Using a dataset of Nepali tweets, we split the test set into "easy" and "hard" cases using token probabilities from a fine-tuned pretrained language model (PLM). We then evaluate an LLM on the hard test cases using 16 different prompt configurations. We find that selecting examples based on semantic similarity and increasing the number of examples improves F1 score by almost 10 points from the baseline configuration. Although the LLM underperforms the PLM on the full and easy test sets, it surpasses the PLM on the hard test set, resulting in an improved score when the two models are combined.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.891744+00	3	\N	57eb4cb20f297057f9c4f5b44116a2f152ce6bd2cebcfce41e819c1486204a02	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.89222+00	2026-11-10 15:23:36.175348+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
82862d5c-54aa-438e-86c8-77aa09dc203f	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2509.23178v2	Limit Analysis for Symbolic Multi-step Reasoning Tasks with Information Propagation Rules Based on Transformers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.23178	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.23178	Tian Qin, Yuhan Chen, Zhiwei Wang, Zhi-Qin John Xu	arXiv:2509.23178v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Transformers are able to perform reasoning tasks, however the intrinsic mechanism remains widely open. In this paper we propose a set of information propagation rules based on Transformers and utilize symbolic reasoning tasks to theoretically analyze the limit reasoning steps. We show that the limit number of reasoning steps is between $2^{L-1}-1$ and $\\frac{3^{L-1}-1}{2}$ for a model with $L$ attention layers in a single-pass. In addition, we find that, for problems with a fixed number of reasoning steps, Transformers exhibit a preference for generating sequences that satisfy certain ordering relations. Ultimately, we construct a Transformer and prove that the limiting reasoning depth is achievable.	arXiv:2509.23178v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Transformers are able to perform reasoning tasks, however the intrinsic mechanism remains widely open. In this paper we propose a set of information propagation rules based on Transformers and utilize symbolic reasoning tasks to theoretically analyze the limit reasoning steps. We show that the limit number of reasoning steps is between $2^{L-1}-1$ and $\\frac{3^{L-1}-1}{2}$ for a model with $L$ attention layers in a single-pass. In addition, we find that, for problems with a fixed number of reasoning steps, Transformers exhibit a preference for generating sequences that satisfy certain ordering relations. Ultimately, we construct a Transformer and prove that the limiting reasoning depth is achievable.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.322086+00	3	\N	c2dce9a75abcbd0ddf116811ddcffb5acd38c45f048532d7a244896d0baee75a	pr