95961997-1a06-427a-9c7b-7d5bb01efcc8	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.16222v2	How I learned to stop worrying and love StopGrads: Stationarity, Convergence, and a case study on Flow Map Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16222	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16222	Max W. Shen, Mark Goldstein, Zichu Wang, Aahlad Puli, Rajesh Ranganath	arXiv:2609.16222v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Stopgrads are widely used in training machine learning models, but stopgrads can alter the gradient, stationary points and convergence guarantees of the original objective, which can make stopgrad training theoretically ungrounded. We introduce a stopgrad regression principle, which identifies a general template for stopgrad objectives with a closed-form characterization of stationary points and their uniqueness, unifying stopgrad objectives for flow maps, reinforcement learning, and diffusion samplers. We provide theoretical grounding for optimizing stopgrad flow map objectives by showing their unique stationary point is the true flow map, and showing positive convergence results for Eulerian and Lagrangian objectives, including MeanFlow and improved MeanFlow. Remarkably, we show that under functional semi-gradient flow, the learned flow map has a closed-form expression composing the initial flow map and the true flow map. We additionally use our stopgrad regression principle to propose modified stopgrad placements for flow map objectives which reduce training memory by 2x.	arXiv:2609.16222v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Stopgrads are widely used in training machine learning models, but stopgrads can alter the gradient, stationary points and convergence guarantees of the original objective, which can make stopgrad training theoretically ungrounded. We introduce a stopgrad regression principle, which identifies a general template for stopgrad objectives with a closed-form characterization of stationary points and their uniqueness, unifying stopgrad objectives for flow maps, reinforcement learning, and diffusion samplers. We provide theoretical grounding for optimizing stopgrad flow map objectives by showing their unique stationary point is the true flow map, and showing positive convergence results for Eulerian and Lagrangian objectives, including MeanFlow and improved MeanFlow. Remarkably, we show that under functional semi-gradient flow, the learned flow map has a closed-form expression composing the initial flow map and the true flow map. We additionally use our stopgrad regression principle to propose modified stopgrad placements for flow map objectives which reduce training memory by 2x.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.23331+00	3	\N	5b0eca6e651e18a84334b93a3af4fc9635cba93d7387b0d83bad4dd46f7d78a0	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.233685+00	2026-11-10 15:28:49.950681+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
979ecc05-d655-4d17-bc87-a33a47424f65	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08177v2	BabyTheorist: A Benchmark for Learning to Theorize the World from Observation Alone	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08177	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08177	Doojin Baek, Junyeob Baek, Mingyu Jo, Hosung Lee, Taegu Kang, Gyubin Lee, Sungjin Ahn	arXiv:2611.08177v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: How would we know whether a machine truly understands the world? We argue that one key capability is observational theory learning: acquiring reusable theories from pure observations and applying them to new situations. Current visual reasoning benchmarks focus primarily on the latter, presenting each task as a few-shot puzzle grouped by a common rule. We introduce BabyTheorist, a benchmark for controlled training and evaluation of observational theory learning. BabyTheorist generates continual streams of before-after visual observations from hidden compositional programs; during training, learners see only the resulting transitions, with no program labels, task identities, or support sets. To support theory learning and evaluation, BabyTheorist controls the program language, a curriculum over program complexity, recurring frequent subprograms, and a continual train-test protocol with level skipping. Seven representative baselines plateau well below ceiling, with the gap widening as the curriculum advances---evidence that BabyTheorist isolates a capability current program-learning approaches do not yet address.	arXiv:2611.08177v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: How would we know whether a machine truly understands the world? We argue that one key capability is observational theory learning: acquiring reusable theories from pure observations and applying them to new situations. Current visual reasoning benchmarks focus primarily on the latter, presenting each task as a few-shot puzzle grouped by a common rule. We introduce BabyTheorist, a benchmark for controlled training and evaluation of observational theory learning. BabyTheorist generates continual streams of before-after visual observations from hidden compositional programs; during training, learners see only the resulting transitions, with no program labels, task identities, or support sets. To support theory learning and evaluation, BabyTheorist controls the program language, a curriculum over program complexity, recurring frequent subprograms, and a continual train-test protocol with level skipping. Seven representative baselines plateau well below ceiling, with the gap widening as the curriculum advances---evidence that BabyTheorist isolates a capability current program-learning approaches do not yet address.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:23:01.474654+00	3	\N	6c958733b44309ac33cc3ff9578e706cc697fa0d8c8f62f4c42cd484a82da9a1	processed	2026-11-09 17:23:01.475085+00	2026-11-10 16:02:22.036099+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
33dd5ee8-88be-4179-9ced-8a28d67591a8	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11588v1	FinCutBench: A Continually Updated Benchmark for Measuring the Temporal Boundaries of LLM Financial Knowledge	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11588	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11588	Siyuan Li, Ke Wan, Boyong Hou, Yujie Deng, Zixuan Yuan	arXiv:2611.11588v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In financial backtesting and other point-in-time tasks, external inputs can be restricted by date, but model parameters may retain financial facts disclosed later, introducing parametric look-ahead bias. We introduce FinCutBench, a continually updated monthly financial knowledge benchmark for measuring how far a fixed model's financial knowledge extends over calendar time. The current dataset contains 9,000 Chinese and English four-option multiple-choice questions from January 2023 to September 2026, covering Chinese and U.S. markets and four categories of financial information. We construct monthly knowledge profiles using the approximate months in which the tested facts became public. After a profile passes the decline test and knot checks, we define the FinCut Boundary as the first month when the fitted profile reaches its lower level, and report the stability of the estimated date under resampling. Boundary dates and localization precision vary across models. Using the independently measured Transition Start and Boundary as fixed references for downstream sample grouping, we find clear relative declines after the Boundary in U.S. asset ranking and earnings recall for some models, while Chinese-market ranking estimates vary across models and all eight disclosure-period-paired forecast-error comparisons have unadjusted $p$ values above 0.10. The FinCut Boundary thus provides an empirical reference for the temporal scope of parametric knowledge in point-in-time financial evaluation, supporting evaluation-window selection and result interpretation.	arXiv:2611.11588v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In financial backtesting and other point-in-time tasks, external inputs can be restricted by date, but model parameters may retain financial facts disclosed later, introducing parametric look-ahead bias. We introduce FinCutBench, a continually updated monthly financial knowledge benchmark for measuring how far a fixed model's financial knowledge extends over calendar time. The current dataset contains 9,000 Chinese and English four-option multiple-choice questions from January 2023 to September 2026, covering Chinese and U.S. markets and four categories of financial information. We construct monthly knowledge profiles using the approximate months in which the tested facts became public. After a profile passes the decline test and knot checks, we define the FinCut Boundary as the first month when the fitted profile reaches its lower level, and report the stability of the estimated date under resampling. Boundary dates and localization precision vary across models. Using the independently measured Transition Start and Boundary as fixed references for downstream sample grouping, we find clear relative declines after the Boundary in U.S. asset ranking and earnings recall for some models, while Chinese-market ranking estimates vary across models and all eight disclosure-period-paired forecast-error comparisons have unadjusted $p$ values above 0.10. The FinCut Boundary thus provides an empirical reference for the temporal scope of parametric knowledge in point-in-time financial evaluation, supporting evaluation-window selection and result interpretation.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:54.951459+00	\N	\N	a54fbb028b03804dbaa3e1d531200ad35e9ab73109dfaea79d0400353c19442b	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:54.951888+00	2026-11-10 16:29:54.95189+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
