d3d45218-eca4-46de-9390-d5031acaeef5	5d593eb7-b3c4-4600-aefa-56c14415c194	\N	https://huggingface.co/blog/eee-community-evals	Featuring Every Eval Ever Results on Hugging Face Model Pages	https://huggingface.co/blog/eee-community-evals	https://huggingface.co/blog/eee-community-evals	\N	\N	\N	product_update	en	2026-06-30 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:36.250312+00	3	\N	850d1f28fd90fe9b5a303187bda7ab4bf71259ba84a9531afdf864b5bb482323	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:36.250717+00	2026-11-10 13:32:19.215079+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
dfb70126-bc5e-464c-a01e-a9d902ef3137	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09239v1	DUET: Dual-Sparse Inference for Block Diffusion Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09239	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09239	Siyuan Yan, Shen Gu, Xiaoxing Ma, Chun Cao, Jingwei Xu	arXiv:2611.09239v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Diffusion language models (DLMs) enable parallel token generation through iterative denoising, yet their inference remains costly due to repeated attention over a growing historical prefix and repeated processing of the entire current generation block. Historical attention becomes increasingly expensive as generation progresses, while all current-block positions are propagated through the Transformer at every denoising step even though only a small fraction are typically resolved at that step. In this paper, we introduce Duet, a training-free dual-sparse inference framework that addresses these challenges through prefix Key-Value (KV) sparsity and early query sparsity, respectively. Specifically, Duet exploits the concentration and cross-step stability of historical attention within each generation block to construct a compact historical KV subset that is reused across denoising steps. For query sparsity, Duet predicts update positions from shallow representations and propagates only the corresponding queries through the suffix Transformer layers, avoiding deep computation at positions unlikely to be updated in the current step. Experiments show that Duet maintains competitive accuracy while achieving 1.34$\\times$/2.83$\\times$ end-to-end/per-step speedups on LLaDA2.1-mini and 2.41$\\times$/4.72$\\times$ on SDAR-8B-Chat-b32 at a 32K context length.	arXiv:2611.09239v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Diffusion language models (DLMs) enable parallel token generation through iterative denoising, yet their inference remains costly due to repeated attention over a growing historical prefix and repeated processing of the entire current generation block. Historical attention becomes increasingly expensive as generation progresses, while all current-block positions are propagated through the Transformer at every denoising step even though only a small fraction are typically resolved at that step. In this paper, we introduce Duet, a training-free dual-sparse inference framework that addresses these challenges through prefix Key-Value (KV) sparsity and early query sparsity, respectively. Specifically, Duet exploits the concentration and cross-step stability of historical attention within each generation block to construct a compact historical KV subset that is reused across denoising steps. For query sparsity, Duet predicts update positions from shallow representations and propagates only the corresponding queries through the suffix Transformer layers, avoiding deep computation at positions unlikely to be updated in the current step. Experiments show that Duet maintains competitive accuracy while achieving 1.34$\\times$/2.83$\\times$ end-to-end/per-step speedups on LLaDA2.1-mini and 2.41$\\times$/4.72$\\times$ on SDAR-8B-Chat-b32 at a 32K context length.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.924932+00	4	\N	c62007a78e30516551c80d5f1e391818b161ad09949d15c31c525fc7ae7bb4c2	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.925422+00	2026-11-10 15:37:23.196587+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ac7a0472-afaf-44c9-842d-228273cb87a2	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09296v1	Multilingual Artificial Datasets for AI-Driven Cardiology: the CliTe and ParaCliTe corpora	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09296	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09296	Lorenzo Pratesi, Eulalia Farre-Maduell, Machteld Boonstra, Michele Miranda, Jonas Mack, Eduard Rodenas-Alesina, Jonathan Kossoff, Corinne Allaart, S\\'ebastien Brati\\`eres, Manuela-Daniela Danu, Isabella Fumarulo, Milou Gamage, Antonio Iaconelli, Pavel Leinveber, Felix Lindberg, Pavel Mach\\'a\\v{c}, Vladim\\'ira Mac\\'ikov\\'a, Gianluigi Savarese, Raffaele Scorza, Bram van Es, Emma Weiss, Andrea Guala, Martin Krallinger, the DataTools4Heart NLP group	arXiv:2611.09296v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Background and Aims. Comparable, multilingual, validated cardiology corpora are critical to advance trustworthy natural language processing (NLP) models for cardiology but such resources are scarce due to privacy concerns. This study aimed to cover this gap by presenting suitable artificial corpus creation guidelines and by releasing 3 multilanguage corpora. Methods. Following purpose-specific publicly-available corpus construction guidelines, cardiologists from 7 European hospitals generated artificial clinical records in Czech, Dutch, English, Italian, Romanian, Spanish and Swedish, ensuring clinical diversity and sex balance, resulting in the CliTe corpus. A CliTe subset underwent a multi-stage manual translation into the other 6 languages, resulting in the parallel ParaCliTe corpus, while the remaining reports were automatically translated, constituting ParaCliTe Silver. Results. CliTe is a collection of 699 artificial cardiology reports, mostly outpatient notes (340 documents), discharge reports (263), and inpatient notes (89), describing fictional patients with ischaemic heart disease (199 patients), heart failure (188), and valvular disease (79), among others. ParaCliTe contains reports from 94 fictitious patients, each available in seven languages with sentence-level alignment, resulting in 658 documents. The corpus mainly comprises discharge reports (322 documents), and outpatient (196) and inpatient (140) notes, describing patients with heart failure (399 documents), ischaemic heart disease (287), and valvular disease (238). Empirical results illustrate the utility of ParaCliTe and highlight the limitations of state-of-the-art NLP models adapted to the cardiology domain. Conclusions. The creation of multilingual, equitable and realistic corpora of artificial patients capable of strengthening NLP performance for cardiology is feasible when supported by detailed guidelines.	arXiv:2611.09296v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Background and Aims. Comparable, multilingual, validated cardiology corpora are critical to advance trustworthy natural language processing (NLP) models for cardiology but such resources are scarce due to privacy concerns. This study aimed to cover this gap by presenting suitable artificial corpus creation guidelines and by releasing 3 multilanguage corpora. Methods. Following purpose-specific publicly-available corpus construction guidelines, cardiologists from 7 European hospitals generated artificial clinical records in Czech, Dutch, English, Italian, Romanian, Spanish and Swedish, ensuring clinical diversity and sex balance, resulting in the CliTe corpus. A CliTe subset underwent a multi-stage manual translation into the other 6 languages, resulting in the parallel ParaCliTe corpus, while the remaining reports were automatically translated, constituting ParaCliTe Silver. Results. CliTe is a collection of 699 artificial cardiology reports, mostly outpatient notes (340 documents), discharge reports (263), and inpatient notes (89), describing fictional patients with ischaemic heart disease (199 patients), heart failure (188), and valvular disease (79), among others. ParaCliTe contains reports from 94 fictitious patients, each available in seven languages with sentence-level alignment, resulting in 658 documents. The corpus mainly comprises discharge reports (322 documents), and outpatient (196) and inpatient (140) notes, describing patients with heart failure (399 documents), ischaemic heart disease (287), and valvular disease (238). Empirical results illustrate the utility of ParaCliTe and highlight the limitations of state-of-the-art NLP models adapted to the cardiology domain. Conclusions. The creation of multilingual, equitable and realistic corpora of artificial patients capable of strengthening NLP performance for cardiology is feasible when supported by detailed guidelines.	research	en	2026-11-09 05:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:22:59.927548+00	3	\N	f9f18c0748e8efe96973641e810800ba8cabab0a42b93784a21dbc02b567f883	processed	2026-11-09 17:22:59.928012+00	2026-11-10 15:37:26.147044+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1052e5bd-277a-407d-91fc-1e1f06675ab5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12160v1	Beyond Flat Walks: Compositional Abstraction for Autoregressive Graph Generation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12160	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12160	Kaiwen Bian, Andrew H. Yang, Yusu Wang, Ali Parviz, Gal Mishne	arXiv:2611.12160v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Autoregressive graph generators typically linearize graphs through flat node-edge walks, which can obscure higher-order structure in large and complex graphs. We introduce a hierarchical graph abstraction and coarse-to-fine generation pipeline that separates coarsening, tokenization, and decoding. This exposes three design axes: community coarsening, hierarchical tokenization, and order randomization. Across these axes, traversal-based hierarchy consistently improves distributional and structural fidelity, while its V.U.N.\\ cost depends strongly on the coarsening and randomization policy. In particular, the fidelity--V.U.N.\\ trade-off observed under within-community randomization is substantially reduced when traversal order is randomized across the full hierarchy. With a fixed backbone, training protocol, and matched training time across five molecular and five non-molecular benchmarks, hierarchical serialization yields consistent distributional and structural gains on all molecular datasets, while the non-molecular results further show that these gains depend on how well the supplied hierarchy aligns with structure in the target distribution. Together, these results characterize when and how hierarchical serialization benefits autoregressive graph generation.	arXiv:2611.12160v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Autoregressive graph generators typically linearize graphs through flat node-edge walks, which can obscure higher-order structure in large and complex graphs. We introduce a hierarchical graph abstraction and coarse-to-fine generation pipeline that separates coarsening, tokenization, and decoding. This exposes three design axes: community coarsening, hierarchical tokenization, and order randomization. Across these axes, traversal-based hierarchy consistently improves distributional and structural fidelity, while its V.U.N.\\ cost depends strongly on the coarsening and randomization policy. In particular, the fidelity--V.U.N.\\ trade-off observed under within-community randomization is substantially reduced when traversal order is randomized across the full hierarchy. With a fixed backbone, training protocol, and matched training time across five molecular and five non-molecular benchmarks, hierarchical serialization yields consistent distributional and structural gains on all molecular datasets, while the non-molecular results further show that these gains depend on how well the supplied hierarchy aligns with structure in the target distribution. Together, these results characterize when and how hierarchical serialization benefits autoregressive graph generation.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.143455+00	\N	\N	47b2fb20cc26062d83d1e506cd784c41ac24fa00d9c42b4e4048984823b4f094	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.143765+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.143766+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
092779a9-3dd0-44c1-b6ab-270e3f3456c4	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10884v1	Keeping LLM Agents on Procedure: Executable Finite-State Control for Long-Horizon Instruction Fidelity	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10884	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10884	Yutao Sun, Haokun Li, Mingshuai Chen, Guanyan Li, Zhongyi Wang, Shengchao Qin, Jianwei Yin	arXiv:2611.10884v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Much of the automation handed to language-model agents is procedural: each step needs little intelligence, but a long unattended run must follow a skill or SOP stated once at the start. We show that agents drift from such procedures over long runs, and that restating the instructions or resuming a stopped agent only moves the drift to another shortcut. We study an executable finite-state machine (FSM) that keeps the workflow state outside the model's context and enforces it at three points: tool calls, state transitions and the end of the agent's turn. In a pre-registered experiment on 200-account SOP-Bench batches, Claude Haiku 4.5 with the FSM decided on average 139 accounts correctly and by the procedure, against 48 for the strongest baseline (95% CI of the difference [46, 130]), and kept this rate to the end; across three models, the gain tracked how much the plain agent drifted. Because the runtime, not the model, supplies this long-horizon reliability, the FSM can let small models carry procedural work: Haiku 4.5 with it averaged more such decisions than plain Claude Sonnet 4.6 at 38% of the cost. Analyses on SWE-bench Verified, $\\tau^2$-bench, and staged and cross-context workflows further suggest that reliable long-horizon automation can come from stronger execution control, not only stronger models.	arXiv:2611.10884v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Much of the automation handed to language-model agents is procedural: each step needs little intelligence, but a long unattended run must follow a skill or SOP stated once at the start. We show that agents