a9b1fbf8-87d9-4469-952a-353c89ee0453	5d593eb7-b3c4-4600-aefa-56c14415c194	\N	https://huggingface.co/blog/PaddlePaddle/pp-ocrv6	PP-OCRv6 on Hugging Face: 50-Language OCR from 1.5M to 34.5M Parameters	https://huggingface.co/blog/PaddlePaddle/pp-ocrv6	https://huggingface.co/blog/PaddlePaddle/pp-ocrv6	\N	\N	\N	release	en	2026-06-22 13:18:56+00	2026-11-09 17:12:36.265768+00	3	\N	f4f5aba6ff44d0d3fcd106b1fb0aef323eb8db28b7de65b4972efca2b737bc95	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:36.266186+00	2026-11-10 13:32:37.528293+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4f8d9ddb-6e2a-4c89-9c99-5d3849f3cee5	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.00249v2	SANEval: Open-Vocabulary Compositional Benchmarks with Failure-mode Diagnosis	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00249	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00249	Rishav Pramanik, Ian E. Nielsen, Jeff Smith, Saurav Pandit, Ravi P. Ramachandran, Zhaozheng Yin	arXiv:2602.00249v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Text-to-image (T2I) models struggle with compositional prompts requiring multiple objects, bound attributes, accurate counts, or spatial relations. Existing benchmarks face limits from fixed detector vocabularies, aggregated scores, and varying human-validation protocols. We introduce SANEval (Spatial, Attribute, and Numeracy Evaluation), an open-vocabulary compositional benchmark with two key contributions: (i) a public dataset of ~5,250 prompts paired with images from six state-of-the-art T2I models, split into algorithmically generated Simple and fully human-written Hard partitions across five compositional tasks, and (ii) a modular evaluation pipeline combining an LLM prompt parser, an open-vocabulary detector with LLM synonym expansion, and scorers for attribute binding, spatial relations, and numeracy. Each scorer emits numerical scores and diagnostic feedback for missing, extra, or mis-bound elements. Validated against human ratings (n = 500), SANEval achieves positive Pearson correlations across all categories (overall r = 0.222), outperforming CompBench++ (-0.016) and GenEval (-0.030). A manual audit of 24,012 detections shows 96.58% precision, with ablation confirming that synonym expansion is critical to pipeline performance. Dataset: [https://huggingface.co/datasets/saneval/saneval-release](https://huggingface.co/datasets/saneval/saneval-release). Pipeline: [https://github.com/nielseni6/saneval](https://github.com/nielseni6/saneval).	arXiv:2602.00249v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Text-to-image (T2I) models struggle with compositional prompts requiring multiple objects, bound attributes, accurate counts, or spatial relations. Existing benchmarks face limits from fixed detector vocabularies, aggregated scores, and varying human-validation protocols. We introduce SANEval (Spatial, Attribute, and Numeracy Evaluation), an open-vocabulary compositional benchmark with two key contributions: (i) a public dataset of ~5,250 prompts paired with images from six state-of-the-art T2I models, split into algorithmically generated Simple and fully human-written Hard partitions across five compositional tasks, and (ii) a modular evaluation pipeline combining an LLM prompt parser, an open-vocabulary detector with LLM synonym expansion, and scorers for attribute binding, spatial relations, and numeracy. Each scorer emits numerical scores and diagnostic feedback for missing, extra, or mis-bound elements. Validated against human ratings (n = 500), SANEval achieves positive Pearson correlations across all categories (overall r = 0.222), outperforming CompBench++ (-0.016) and GenEval (-0.030). A manual audit of 24,012 detections shows 96.58% precision, with ablation confirming that synonym expansion is critical to pipeline performance. Dataset: [https://huggingface.co/datasets/saneval/saneval-release](https://huggingface.co/datasets/saneval/saneval-release). Pipeline: [https://github.com/nielseni6/saneval](https://github.com/nielseni6/saneval).	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.407552+00	\N	\N	994938c510a32473e1d853ad622fef330a1145ab5eba841b56b73fd94a8aa556	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.407932+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.407935+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e2288e3b-60c1-4267-b55f-2d0d4f794737	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.04937v2	Linear Model Merging Unlocks Simple and Scalable Multimodal Data Mixture Optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04937	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04937	Davide Berasi, Matteo Farina, Massimiliano Mancini, Elisa Ricci	arXiv:2602.04937v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Selecting the best data mixture is critical for successful Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) of Multimodal Large Language Models. However, determining the optimal mixture weights across multiple domain-specific datasets remains a significant bottleneck due to the combinatorial search space and the high cost associated with even a single training run. This is the so-called Data Mixture Optimization (DMO) problem. On the other hand, model merging unifies domain-specific experts through parameter interpolation. This strategy is efficient, as it requires only a single training run per domain, yet it often leads to suboptimal models. In this work, we take the best of both worlds, studying model merging as an efficient strategy for estimating the performance of different data mixtures. We train domain-specific multimodal experts and evaluate their weighted parameter-space combinations to estimate the efficacy of corresponding data mixtures. We conduct extensive experiments on 14 multimodal benchmarks, and empirically demonstrate that the merged proxy models consistently select near-optimal mixtures. This decouples the search for optimal mixtures from the resource-intensive training process, thereby providing a scalable and efficient strategy for navigating the complex landscape of mixture weights. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/BerasiDavide/mLLMs_merging_4_DMO.	arXiv:2602.04937v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Selecting the best data mixture is critical for successful Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) of Multimodal Large Language Models. However, determining the optimal mixture weights across multiple domain-specific datasets remains a significant bottleneck due to the combinatorial search space and the high cost associated with even a single training run. This is the so-called Data Mixture Optimization (DMO) problem. On the other hand, model merging unifies domain-specific experts through parameter interpolation. This strategy is efficient, as it requires only a single training run per domain, yet it often leads to suboptimal models. In this work, we take the best of both worlds, studying model merging as an efficient strategy for estimating the performance of different data mixtures. We train domain-specific multimodal experts and evaluate their weighted parameter-space combinations to estimate the efficacy of corresponding data mixtures. We conduct extensive experiments on 14 multimodal benchmarks, and empirically demonstrate that the merged proxy models consistently select near-optimal mixtures. This decouples the search for optimal mixtures from the resource-intensive training process, thereby providing a scalable and efficient strategy for navigating the complex landscape of mixture weights. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/BerasiDavide/mLLMs_merging_4_DMO.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.412852+00	\N	\N	326507693fcd1454c8dab774b56af64163b7e3b056eb2729ff0a39c3871ffa48	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.41318+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.413182+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1bd8efe9-996d-465b-8fdd-ed390d80ff3e	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.05026v2	Laws of Learning Dynamics and the Core of Learners	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05026	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05026	Inkee Jung, Siu Cheong Lau	arXiv:2602.05026v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We formulate an algorithm-independent theory of learning dynamics for individual models and ensembles. We establish a conservation law, the vanishing of predictive entropy under successful learning, quantitative entropy and core-expansion bounds, and calibration and finite-sample validation results for identifying reliable predictions. These results motivate a two-generation ensemble that uses Jensen-Shannon (JS) disagreement to route uncertain inputs to specialized learners. CIFAR-10 experiments examine controlled perturbations, distribution shifts, and adversarial attacks. On a heterogeneous test environment, routing improves accuracy over probability averaging while keeping core accuracy near 95%. For adversarial defense, we consider a query-only black-box setting: the attacker can query the overall prediction function but does not know a particular internal decomposition into learners and routing rules. Our current attacks use a fixed-initial-route gradient, which is accurate for a single-step attack when the route remains unchanged, but may deviate from actual multi-step attacks, especially for large perturbations. We evaluate our two-layer defense against this approximation; whether additional adversarially trained layers improve resistance to stronger multi-step attacks remains for future study.	arXiv:2602.05026v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We formulate an algorithm-independent theory of learning dynamics for individual models and ensembles. We establish a conservation law, the vanishing of predictive entropy under successful learning, quantitative entropy and core-expansion bounds, and calibration and finite-sample validation results for identifying reliable predictions. These results motivate a two-generation ensemble that uses Jensen-Shannon (JS) disagreement to route uncertain inputs to specialized learners. CIFAR-10 experiments examine controlled perturbations, distribution shifts, and adversarial attacks. On a heterogeneous test environment, routing improves accuracy over probability averaging while keeping core accuracy near 95%. For adversarial defense, we consider a query-only black-box setting: the attacker can query the overall prediction function but does not know a particular internal decomposition into learners and routing rules. Our current attacks use a fixed-initial-route gradient, which is accurate for a single-step attack when the route remains unchanged, but may deviate from actual multi-step attacks, especially for large perturbations. We evaluate our two-layer defense against this approximation; whether additional adversarially trained layers improve resistance to stronger multi-step attacks remains for future study.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.415118+00	\N	\N	a57f45549b0bf484759fa7c4a7946722e4736f7266bb960ad7a7e1dcc6e533dc	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.415422+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.415423+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c06d3daa-d43f-4ff7-af54-24e02a40e119	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.06208v2	Emergent Low-Rank Training Dynamics in MLPs with Smooth Activations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06208	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06208	Alec S. Xu, Can Yaras, Matthew Asato, Qing Qu, Laura Balzano	arXiv:2602.06208v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Recent empirical evidence has demonstrated that the training dynamics of large-scale deep neural networks occur within low-dimensional subspaces. While this has inspired new research into low-rank training, compression, and adaptation, theoretical justification for these dynamics in nonlinear networks remains limited. %compared to deep linear settings. To address this gap, this paper analyzes the learning dynamics of multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs) under gradient descent (GD). We demonstrate that the weight dynamics concentrate within invariant low-dimensional subspaces throughout training. Theoretically, we precisely characterize these invariant subspaces for two-layer networks with smooth nonlinear activations, providing insight into their emergence. Experimentally, we validate that this phenomenon extends beyond our theoretical assumptions. Leveraging these insights, we empirically show there exists a low-rank MLP parameterization that, when initialized within the appropriate subspaces, matches the classification performance of fully-parameterized counterparts on a variety of classification tasks.	arXiv:2602.06208v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Recent empirical evidence has demonstrated that the training dynamics of large-scale deep neural networks occur within low-dimensional subspaces. While this has inspired new research into low-rank training, compression, and adaptation, theoretical justification for these dynamics in nonlinear networks remains limited. %compared to deep linear settings. To address this gap, this paper analyzes the learning dynamics of multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs) under gradient descent (GD). We demonstrate that the weight dynamics concentrate within invariant low-dimensional subspaces throughout training. Theoretically, we precisely characterize these invariant subspaces for two-layer networks with smooth nonlinear activations, providing insight into their emergence. Experimentally, we validate that this phenomenon extends beyond our theoretical assumptions. Leveraging these insights, we empirically show there exists a low-rank MLP parameterization that, when initialized within the appropriate subspaces, matches the classification performance of fully-parameterized counterparts on a variety of classification tasks.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.417611+00	\N	\N	a9196f2c5508475f0a0844e70946638100fecfeae181463da7fed0592291b7cb	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.417933+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.417935+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6fe8fff5-59f9-46b8-a74b-b3e2860d37a9	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.06643v3	HuMI: Humanoid Whole-Body Manipulation from Humanoid-Free Demonstrations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06643	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06643	Ruiqian Nai, Boyuan Zheng, Junming Zhao, Haodong Zhu, Sicong Dai, Zunhao Chen, Yihang Hu, Yingdong Hu, Tong Zhang, Chuan Wen, Yang Gao	arXiv:2602.06643v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Current approaches for humanoid whole-body manipulation, primarily relying on teleoperation or visual sim-to-real reinforcement learning, are hindered by hardware logistics and complex reward engineering. Consequently, demonstrated autonomous skills remain limited and are typically restricted to controlled environments. In this paper, we present the Humanoid Manipulation Interface (HuMI), a portable and efficient framework for learning diverse whole-body manipulation tasks across various environments. HuMI enables humanoid-free data collection by capturing rich whole-body motion using portable hardware. This data drives a hierarchical learning pipeline that translates human motions into dexterous and feasible humanoid skills. Extensive experiments across five whole-body tasks--including kneeling, squatting, tossing, walking, and bimanual manipulation--demonstrate that HuMI achieves a 3x increase in data collection efficiency compared to teleoperation and attains a 70% success rate in unseen environments.	arXiv:2602.06643v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Current approaches for humanoid whole-body manipulation, primarily relying on teleoperation or visual sim-to-real reinforcement learning, are hindered by hardware logistics and complex reward engineering. Consequently, demonstrated autonomous skills remain limited and are typically restricted to controlled environments. In this paper, we present the Humanoid Manipulation Interface (HuMI), a portable and efficient framework for learning diverse whole-body manipulation tasks across various environments. HuMI enables humanoid-free data collection by capturing rich whole-body motion using portable hardware. This data drives a hierarchical learning pipeline that translates human motions into dexterous and feasible humanoid skills. Extensive experiments across five whole-body tasks--including kneeling, squatting, tossing, walking, and bimanual manipulation--demonstrate that HuMI achieves a 3x increase in data collection efficiency compared to teleoperation and attains a 70% success rate in unseen environments.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.420034+00	\N	\N	28d3137b85cd67d7a585124f813956635afd7c99e3a65b30f0c126d27c2fcff6	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.420342+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.420343+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
aa411b2d-ece0-4fb7-a282-1b3c23f12609	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.07738v3	Learnable Chernoff Baselines for Inference-Time Alignment	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07738	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07738	Sunil Madhow, Yuchen Liang, Ness Shroff, Yingbin Liang, Yu-Xiang Wang	arXiv:2602.07738v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study inference-time reward-guided alignment for generative models. Existing methods often rely on either architecture-specific adaptations or computationally costly inference procedures. We introduce Learnable Chernoff Baselines (LCBs) as a method for efficiently and approximately sampling from the exponentially tilted kernels that arise from KL-regularized reward alignment. Using only black-box sampling access to the pretrained model, LCBs implement a form of rejection sampling with adaptively selected acceptance probabilities, which allows fine-grained control over inference-compute scaling. We establish total-variation guarantees to the ideal aligned model, and demonstrate in both continuous and discrete diffusion settings that LCB sampling closely matches ideal rejection sampling while using substantially fewer queries to the pretrained model.	arXiv:2602.07738v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study inference-time reward-guided alignment for generative models. Existing methods often rely on either architecture-specific adaptations or computationally costly inference procedures. We introduce Learnable Chernoff Baselines (LCBs) as a method for efficiently and approximately sampling from the exponentially tilted kernels that arise from KL-regularized reward alignment. Using only black-box sampling access to the pretrained model, LCBs implement a form of rejection sampling with adaptively selected acceptance probabilities, which allows fine-grained control over inference-compute scaling. We establish total-variation guarantees to the ideal aligned model, and demonstrate in both continuous and discrete diffusion settings that LCB sampling closely matches ideal rejection sampling while using substantially fewer queries to the pretrained model.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.422254+00	\N	\N	6d4dbb5870eeda6fba48c195c8ae68e26e9b7b94fffc71809ad8126bdbd898db	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.42264+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.422676+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
690f0de1-669c-4013-84db-8799e3907e82	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.08616v3	Breaking the Grid: Distance-Guided Reinforcement Learning in Large Discrete Action Spaces	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08616	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08616	Heiko Hoppe, Fabian Akkerman, Wouter van Heeswijk, Maximilian Schiffer	arXiv:2602.08616v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Reinforcement Learning (RL) is increasingly applied to large-scale decision-making problems like logistics, scheduling, and recommender systems, but existing algorithms struggle with the curse of dimensionality in such large discrete action spaces. We propose Distance-Guided Reinforcement Learning (DGRL), combining Sampled Dynamic Neighborhoods and Distance-Based Updates to enable efficient RL in problems with up to $10^{36}$ actions. Unlike prior methods, DGRL performs stochastic volumetric exploration and transforms policy optimization into a stable regression task, decoupling gradient variance from action space cardinality. On structured tasks, DGRL provably guarantees local value improvement. DGRL naturally handles action spaces without regular semantic structure and hybrid continuous-discrete action spaces. We demonstrate performance improvements of up to 66% against state-of-the-art benchmarks across regularly and irregularly structured environments, while simultaneously improving convergence speed and computational complexity.	arXiv:2602.08616v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Reinforcement Learning (RL) is increasingly applied to large-scale decision-making problems like logistics, scheduling, and recommender systems, but existing algorithms struggle with the curse of dimensionality in such large discrete action spaces. We propose Distance-Guided Reinforcement Learning (DGRL), combining Sampled Dynamic Neighborhoods and Distance-Based Updates to enable efficient RL in problems with up to $10^{36}$ actions. Unlike prior methods, DGRL performs stochastic volumetric exploration and transforms policy optimization into a stable regression task, decoupling gradient variance from action space cardinality. On structured tasks, DGRL provably guarantees local value improvement. DGRL naturally handles action spaces without regular semantic structure and hybrid continuous-discrete action spaces. We demonstrate performance improvements of up to 66% against state-of-the-art benchmarks across regularly and irregularly structured environments, while simultaneously improving convergence speed and computational complexity.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.427105+00	\N	\N	8e7c9f5cc2780d2125d1aeaf105c82c7644c7820c05005517b3be2dc37bbd148	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.427415+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.427417+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1e1d46f1-7f0e-4d3d-8ca1-3ff8cfdfecf7	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.12413v2	Soft Contamination Means Benchmarks Test Shallow Generalization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12413	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12413	Ari Spiesberger, Juan J. Vazquez, Nicky Pochinkov, Tom\\'a\\v{s} Gaven\\v{c}iak, Peli Grietzer, Gavin Leech, Nandi Schoots	arXiv:2602.12413v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Training on benchmark test items is a classic way to overestimate generalization to unseen problems. Standard decontamination methods miss semantic duplicates (SemDs): training items equivalent in content to a test item, but distant in string space. We call training on such items soft contamination; when the resulting gains extend to held-out items of the same benchmark (but not to related benchmarks), we call the result shallow generalization. We first embed Olmo 3's entire post-training dataset and search it for SemDs of benchmark items; we then finetune Olmo 3 and Qwen models on natural and synthetic semantic duplicates. We find (1) that soft contamination is widespread: the Olmo corpus contains SemDs of 76% of the CodeForces test set and 100% of MBPP's; (2) that finetuning on SemDs of a benchmark raises accuracy on its held-out items by up to 22pp in controlled experiments and 10-11pp at realistic contamination rates, though the effect varies across benchmarks; (3) that the gains from training on SemDs mostly do not transfer to conceptually similar benchmarks, i.e. we find shallow generalization; (4) that finetuning on items with similar embeddings that are not SemDs produces no such gain, indicating that the mechanism is not mere topical similarity; and (5) that at realistic contamination rates, soft contamination can raise held-out performance about as much as exact duplicates (or more). Benchmark gains here are therefore confounded - in a way that standard decontamination does not address.	arXiv:2602.12413v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Training on benchmark test items is a classic way to overestimate generalization to unseen problems. Standard decontamination methods miss semantic duplicates (SemDs): training items equivalent in content to a test item, but distant in string space. We call training on such items soft contamination; when the resulting gains extend to held-out items of the same benchmark (but not to related benchmarks), we call the result shallow generalization. We first embed Olmo 3's entire post-training dataset and search it for SemDs of benchmark items; we then finetune Olmo 3 and Qwen models on natural and synthetic semantic duplicates. We find (1) that soft contamination is widespread: the Olmo corpus contains SemDs of 76% of the CodeForces test set and 100% of MBPP's; (2) that finetuning on SemDs of a benchmark raises accuracy on its held-out items by up to 22pp in controlled experiments and 10-11pp at realistic contamination rates, though the effect varies across benchmarks; (3) that the gains from training on SemDs mostly do not transfer to conceptually similar benchmarks, i.e. we find shallow generalization; (4) that finetuning on items with similar embeddings that are not SemDs produces no such gain, indicating that the mechanism is not mere topical similarity; and (5) that at realistic contamination rates, soft contamination can raise held-out performance about as much as exact duplicates (or more). Benchmark gains here are therefore confounded - in a way that standard decontamination does not address.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.429376+00	\N	\N	c5b39d55bbc8754cb80d15f2f3712db2003ee30e80797be7cd016eb3893db45e	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.429677+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.429678+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d48b06d8-3c0d-4734-b1df-0e5aeb4e306b	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.12517v2	Bench-MFG: A Benchmark Suite for Learning in Stationary Mean Field Games	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12517	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12517	Lorenzo Magnino, Jiacheng Shen, Matthieu Geist, Olivier Pietquin, Mathieu Lauri\\`ere	arXiv:2602.12517v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: The rise of Mean Field Games (MFGs) has fostered a growing family of algorithms designed to solve large-scale multi-agent systems. However, the field currently lacks a standardized evaluation protocol, forcing researchers to rely on bespoke, isolated, and often simplistic environments. This fragmentation makes it difficult to assess the robustness, generalization, and failure modes of emerging methods. To address this gap, we propose a comprehensive benchmark suite for MFGs, focusing on the discrete-time, discrete-space, stationary setting. We introduce a taxonomy of problem classes, ranging from no-interaction and monotone games to potential and dynamics-coupled games, and provide prototypical environments for each. Furthermore, we present MF-Garnets, a method for generating random MFG instances to facilitate rigorous statistical testing. We benchmark a variety of learning algorithms across these environments, including a novel population-based approach (MF-PSO). To our knowledge, this is the first benchmark for MFGs.	arXiv:2602.12517v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: The rise of Mean Field Games (MFGs) has fostered a growing family of algorithms designed to solve large-scale multi-agent systems. However, the field currently lacks a standardized evaluation protocol, forcing researchers to rely on bespoke, isolated, and often simplistic environments. This fragmentation makes it difficult to assess the robustness, generalization, and failure modes of emerging methods. To address this gap, we propose a comprehensive benchmark suite for MFGs, focusing on the discrete-time, discrete-space, stationary setting. We introduce a taxonomy of problem classes, ranging from no-interaction and monotone games to potential and dynamics-coupled games, and provide prototypical environments for each. Furthermore, we present MF-Garnets, a method for generating random MFG instances to facilitate rigorous statistical testing. We benchmark a variety of learning algorithms across these environments, including a novel population-based approach (MF-PSO). To our knowledge, this is the first benchmark for MFGs.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.431807+00	\N	\N	cce9da074ebcc71e488cecb0732ce67b1288f354ab0f3dab2cbc446c5d544383	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.432138+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.432139+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
fcb172d5-d09b-413d-8e83-ff7945e2a9cb	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.14344v3	Zero-Shot Instruction Following in RL via Structured LTL Representations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14344	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14344	Mathias Jackermeier, Mattia Giuri, Jacques Cloete, Alessandro Abate	arXiv:2602.14344v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study instruction following in multi-task reinforcement learning, where an agent must zero-shot execute novel tasks not seen during training. In this setting, linear temporal logic (LTL) has recently been adopted as a powerful framework for specifying structured, temporally extended tasks. While existing approaches successfully train generalist policies, they often struggle to effectively capture the rich logical and temporal structure inherent in LTL specifications. In this work, we address these concerns with a novel approach to learn structured task representations that facilitate training and generalisation. Our method conditions the policy on sequences of Boolean formulae constructed from a finite automaton of the task. We propose a hierarchical neural architecture to encode the logical structure of these formulae, and introduce an attention mechanism that enables the policy to reason about future subgoals. Experiments in a variety of complex environments demonstrate the strong generalisation capabilities and superior performance of our approach.	arXiv:2602.14344v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study instruction following in multi-task reinforcement learning, where an agent must zero-shot execute novel tasks not seen during training. In this setting, linear temporal logic (LTL) has recently been adopted as a powerful framework for specifying structured, temporally extended tasks. While existing approaches successfully train generalist policies, they often struggle to effectively capture the rich logical and temporal structure inherent in LTL specifications. In this work, we address these concerns with a novel approach to learn structured task representations that facilitate training and generalisation. Our method conditions the policy on sequences of Boolean formulae constructed from a finite automaton of the task. We propose a hierarchical neural architecture to encode the logical structure of these formulae, and introduce an attention mechanism that enables the policy to reason about future subgoals. Experiments in a variety of complex environments demonstrate the strong generalisation capabilities and superior performance of our approach.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.434647+00	\N	\N	058392fb20de99c6e97f98ff4df3c0a9d7c3a6d00be8e3e9bfdc83cf576415ad	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.43513+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.435132+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1ac69483-37b3-41eb-89ce-ecf25c3a59e1	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.14917v2	BFS-PO: Best-First Search for Large Reasoning Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14917	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14917	Fiorenzo Parascandolo, Wenhui Tan, Enver Sangineto, Xiaoyi Yu, Ruihua Song, Rita Cucchiara	arXiv:2602.14917v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) have shown excellent performance in reasoning tasks using long Chains of Thought (CoTs). However, this has also led to a significant increase of computational costs and the generation of verbose output, a phenomenon known as overthinking. The tendency to overthink is often exacerbated by Reinforcement Learning (RL) algorithms such as GRPO/DAPO. In this paper, we propose BFS-PO, an RL algorithm which alleviates this problem using a Best-First Search exploration strategy. Specifically, the reasoning chains generated at training time by BFS-PO are organized into a search tree, within which the shortest correct sequence is selected as the best and expanded using a backtracking mechanism based on maximum entropy nodes. In this way, we bias the exploration of the solution space towards the search for increasingly shorter solutions, training the LRM to generate progressively more concise answers. Using different benchmarks and base LRMs, we show that BFS-PO can simultaneously increase the LRM accuracy and shorten its reasoning chains.	arXiv:2602.14917v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) have shown excellent performance in reasoning tasks using long Chains of Thought (CoTs). However, this has also led to a significant increase of computational costs and the generation of verbose output, a phenomenon known as overthinking. The tendency to overthink is often exacerbated by Reinforcement Learning (RL) algorithms such as GRPO/DAPO. In this paper, we propose BFS-PO, an RL algorithm which alleviates this problem using a Best-First Search exploration strategy. Specifically, the reasoning chains generated at training time by BFS-PO are organized into a search tree, within which the shortest correct sequence is selected as the best and expanded using a backtracking mechanism based on maximum entropy nodes. In this way, we bias the exploration of the solution space towards the search for increasingly shorter solutions, training the LRM to generate progressively more concise answers. Using different benchmarks and base LRMs, we show that BFS-PO can simultaneously increase the LRM accuracy and shorten its reasoning chains.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.437284+00	\N	\N	9921939933d1ba2434b3486fc7c9bdd7c08214407ab6c7e3f0fbe4a055219bbb	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.4376+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.437602+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b4dbe9d3-5298-4081-9a37-d6e6e74df36b	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.18308v3	JPmHC Dynamical Isometry via Orthogonal Hyper-Connections	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18308	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18308	Biswa Sengupta, Jinhua Wang, Leo Brunswic	arXiv:2602.18308v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: The hyper-connection paradigm separates the skip-connections of transformers into $n$ parallel streams on which a linear mixer acts.\n  Constraining the mixer to bistochastic matrices has proven effective in preventing gradient explosion but risk collapsing onto low-rank configurations, limiting expressivity.\n  We therefore propose JPmHC (Jacobian-spectrum Preserving manifold-constrained Hyper-Connections).\n  Our framework triages manifold constraints by computing the Jacobian singular spectrum of the end-to-end network in the mean-field limit and their spectral stability. It consists of: (i) an operator-valued free-probability library that predicts the Jacobian singular spectrum; (ii) an orthogonal mixer computed by a fixed-point Cayley iteration, which enables dynamical isometry; (iii) implicit differentiation of the Sinkhorn projection of the bistochastic baseline, with $O(1)$ backward operations; and (iv) a sensitivity theory giving the training budget before a bistochastic mixer collapses.\n  The library is written in Lean, which certifies the mathematics and the algorithms.\n  We validate JPmHC with TRM on ARC-AGI and nano-GPT on FineWeb-Edu: the theory predicts spectral collapse for TRM and stability for nano-GPT as observed in our experiments.	arXiv:2602.18308v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: The hyper-connection paradigm separates the skip-connections of transformers into $n$ parallel streams on which a linear mixer acts.\n  Constraining the mixer to bistochastic matrices has proven effective in preventing gradient explosion but risk collapsing onto low-rank configurations, limiting expressivity.\n  We therefore propose JPmHC (Jacobian-spectrum Preserving manifold-constrained Hyper-Connections).\n  Our framework triages manifold constraints by computing the Jacobian singular spectrum of the end-to-end network in the mean-field limit and their spectral stability. It consists of: (i) an operator-valued free-probability library that predicts the Jacobian singular spectrum; (ii) an orthogonal mixer computed by a fixed-point Cayley iteration, which enables dynamical isometry; (iii) implicit differentiation of the Sinkhorn projection of the bistochastic baseline, with $O(1)$ backward operations; and (iv) a sensitivity theory giving the training budget before a bistochastic mixer collapses.\n  The library is written in Lean, which certifies the mathematics and the algorithms.\n  We validate JPmHC with TRM on ARC-AGI and nano-GPT on FineWeb-Edu: the theory predicts spectral collapse for TRM and stability for nano-GPT as observed in our experiments.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.442095+00	\N	\N	513269200ada364b26ca4e92605baad68bb1e17c2ef403a43344f05a1cc03201	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.442423+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.442424+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2e26a914-e386-4418-89e0-5f4f0b718975	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.18851v2	Rank-Aware Spectral Bounds on Attention Logits for Stable Low-Precision Training	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18851	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18851	Seyed Morteza Emadi	arXiv:2602.18851v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Attention scores in transformers are bilinear forms $S_{ij} = x_i^\\top M x_j / \\sqrt{d_h}$ whose maximum magnitude governs overflow risk in low-precision training. We derive a rank-aware concentration inequality: when the interaction matrix $M = W^Q W^{K\\top}$ has rank $r \\ll d$, tail probabilities for $\\max_{i,j}|S_{ij}|$ decay as $\\exp(-d^{2}\\alpha^{2}/(\\gamma r))$ rather than $\\exp(-d\\alpha^{2})$, where $\\gamma > 1$. For transformer attention where $r = d_h$, this yields $8$ to $28\\times$ tighter concentration than rank-agnostic bounds in modern architectures. We apply this result to FP8 training, deriving geometry-aware scale factors that provide principled overflow guarantees without observing activations. The method computes per-layer scales from the spectral norm $\\|W^Q W^{K\\top}\\|_2$ via implicit power iteration, includes a grouped query attention formulation that avoids key expansion, and remains compatible with fused attention kernels. Across GPT-2 XL to Llama-2-70B, geometry-aware scaling eliminates overflows in transient scenarios where delayed scaling fails, while achieving comparable downstream MMLU accuracy.	arXiv:2602.18851v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Attention scores in transformers are bilinear forms $S_{ij} = x_i^\\top M x_j / \\sqrt{d_h}$ whose maximum magnitude governs overflow risk in low-precision training. We derive a rank-aware concentration inequality: when the interaction matrix $M = W^Q W^{K\\top}$ has rank $r \\ll d$, tail probabilities for $\\max_{i,j}|S_{ij}|$ decay as $\\exp(-d^{2}\\alpha^{2}/(\\gamma r))$ rather than $\\exp(-d\\alpha^{2})$, where $\\gamma > 1$. For transformer attention where $r = d_h$, this yields $8$ to $28\\times$ tighter concentration than rank-agnostic bounds in modern architectures. We apply this result to FP8 training, deriving geometry-aware scale factors that provide principled overflow guarantees without observing activations. The method computes per-layer scales from the spectral norm $\\|W^Q W^{K\\top}\\|_2$ via implicit power iteration, includes a grouped query attention formulation that avoids key expansion, and remains compatible with fused attention kernels. Across GPT-2 XL to Llama-2-70B, geometry-aware scaling eliminates overflows in transient scenarios where delayed scaling fails, while achieving comparable downstream MMLU accuracy.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.444718+00	\N	\N	c6a8fe4ee47e7e1b9112ca134085a98b0b5549f1ca2a1b259d2c1390a980c508	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:56.445067+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.445068+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
