877464c9-0fa5-4e2b-a7ea-e8c66e76cb16	5d593eb7-b3c4-4600-aefa-56c14415c194	\N	https://huggingface.co/blog/cybersecurity-openness	AI and the Future of Cybersecurity: Why Openness Matters	https://huggingface.co/blog/cybersecurity-openness	https://huggingface.co/blog/cybersecurity-openness	\N	\N	\N	opinion	en	2026-04-21 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:36.345898+00	3	\N	74b85a1fe345ff0301a58680cf2967a53cffd4fda51688bdcf09b618d68fc0ea	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:36.346265+00	2026-11-10 13:34:14.190316+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e5f43ddc-a20b-4a4b-a82f-40c58133a955	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11905v1	On the Global Convergence of Stochastic Adam for Physics-Informed Neural Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11905	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11905	Yihang Gao	arXiv:2611.11905v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The Adam optimizer is widely used to train neural networks with highly nonlinear and nonconvex objective functions. Remarkably, Adam often drives the empirical training loss nearly to zero. This phenomenon has been partially explained by neural tangent kernel (NTK) analyses of gradient descent (GD) and stochastic gradient descent (SGD). However, analyzing Adam presents additional challenges. Its first-moment momentum couples stochastic gradients across iterations, while its adaptive second-moment preconditioner depends on the same stochastic gradient used in the update. In this paper, we establish linear convergence guarantees for Adam in training sufficiently wide two-layer neural networks with either full-batch or minibatch gradients. For full-batch Adam, we construct a Lyapunov function to control the effect of historical momentum. For minibatch Adam, our analysis builds on an exact virtual-iterate representation, a conditional analysis of the dependence between stochastic gradients and adaptive preconditioners, and uniform concentration bounds for the second-moment estimates. We prove geometric decay of the empirical loss for full-batch Adam and geometric decay in restricted expectation on a high-probability event for minibatch Adam. Beyond regression, we extend our convergence analysis to physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) for solving the Poisson equation, where differential operators and separate sampling of interior and boundary points introduce additional challenges. We establish analogous global convergence guarantees for both deterministic and stochastic Adam under explicit conditions on network width and Adam's parameters, characterizing the effects of minibatch size on convergence. These results help bridge the gap between Adam's empirical success and its theoretical understanding, providing sharper convergence guarantees for PINNs than existing analyses.	arXiv:2611.11905v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The Adam optimizer is widely used to train neural networks with highly nonlinear and nonconvex objective functions. Remarkably, Adam often drives the empirical training loss nearly to zero. This phenomenon has been partially explained by neural tangent kernel (NTK) analyses of gradient descent (GD) and stochastic gradient descent (SGD). However, analyzing Adam presents additional challenges. Its first-moment momentum couples stochastic gradients across iterations, while its adaptive second-moment preconditioner depends on the same stochastic gradient used in the update. In this paper, we establish linear convergence guarantees for Adam in training sufficiently wide two-layer neural networks with either full-batch or minibatch gradients. For full-batch Adam, we construct a Lyapunov function to control the effect of historical momentum. For minibatch Adam, our analysis builds on an exact virtual-iterate representation, a conditional analysis of the dependence between stochastic gradients and adaptive preconditioners, and uniform concentration bounds for the second-moment estimates. We prove geometric decay of the empirical loss for full-batch Adam and geometric decay in restricted expectation on a high-probability event for minibatch Adam. Beyond regression, we extend our convergence analysis to physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) for solving the Poisson equation, where differential operators and separate sampling of interior and boundary points introduce additional challenges. We establish analogous global convergence guarantees for both deterministic and stochastic Adam under explicit conditions on network width and Adam's parameters, characterizing the effects of minibatch size on convergence. These results help bridge the gap between Adam's empirical success and its theoretical understanding, providing sharper convergence guarantees for PINNs than existing analyses.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.061891+00	\N	\N	3cd27b640e8f0431274cb3b8a5f4ae255a7a5fd0ddbbd020d842d653b59414a2	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.062264+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.062266+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c8b681d3-161e-4431-aee3-12669c6b2b92	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11976v1	ShiftCache: Fast and Exact Indel Effect Prediction by Reusing Shifted Reference Encodings	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11976	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11976	Sina Barazandeh, Arth Banka, Jose Lugo-Martinez	arXiv:2611.11976v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Sequence-to-function models such as AlphaGenome and Borzoi read up to a megabase of DNA and score a genetic variant by comparing their predictions for the reference and alternate sequences. Insertions and deletions (indels) are costly to score, because an indel usually shifts the unchanged DNA after it onto a different pooling grid, which prevents direct reuse of the reference's encoder activations. The AlphaGenome Atlas scored more than 100 million indels with three full model passes each, although the DNA after an indel matches the reference shifted by the indel's length. We present ShiftCache, an exact reuse system that encodes each shifted reference once and shares it among all variants scored in the same window with the same shift. ShiftCache copies every encoder output a variant can reuse and recomputes the rest in batched tiles. On held-out AlphaGenome windows shared by many indels, in strict fp32 on one A6000, ShiftCache is 3.5 to 6.5$\\times$ faster than deduplicated dense execution and 1.6 to 2.7$\\times$ faster than our adaptation of layer-by-layer reuse to indels. It is 3.5 to 4.1$\\times$ faster than dense reimplementations of two published analyses with Borzoi and AlphaGenome, and 2.6$\\times$ faster than dense execution with Enformer. Scoring each variant in its own centered 131 kb window makes ShiftCache only 1.36$\\times$ faster than deduplicated dense execution, and highly diverse insertion lengths or many dispersed edits can make it slower than layer-by-layer reuse or dense execution. For encoders that meet our conditions, ShiftCache computes the same predictions as dense execution in exact arithmetic. On an A6000, every checked output of the default AlphaGenome and Borzoi runs matches dense execution within an absolute and relative tolerance of $10^{-5}$. With larger tile batches, with Enformer or on an H100, a small fraction of values exceeds this tolerance.	arXiv:2611.11976v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Sequence-to-function models such as AlphaGenome and Borzoi read up to a megabase of DNA and score a genetic variant by comparing their predictions for the reference and alternate sequences. Insertions and deletions (indels) are costly to score, because an indel usually shifts the unchanged DNA after it onto a different pooling grid, which prevents direct reuse of the reference's encoder activations. The AlphaGenome Atlas scored more than 100 million indels with three full model passes each, although the DNA after an indel matches the reference shifted by the indel's length. We present ShiftCache, an exact reuse system that encodes each shifted reference once and shares it among all variants scored in the same window with the same shift. ShiftCache copies every encoder output a variant can reuse and recomputes the rest in batched tiles. On held-out AlphaGenome windows shared by many indels, in strict fp32 on one A6000, ShiftCache is 3.5 to 6.5$\\times$ faster than deduplicated dense execution and 1.6 to 2.7$\\times$ faster than our adaptation of layer-by-layer reuse to indels. It is 3.5 to 4.1$\\times$ faster than dense reimplementations of two published analyses with Borzoi and AlphaGenome, and 2.6$\\times$ faster than dense execution with Enformer. Scoring each variant in its own centered 131 kb window makes ShiftCache only 1.36$\\times$ faster than deduplicated dense execution, and highly diverse insertion lengths or many dispersed edits can make it slower than layer-by-layer reuse or dense execution. For encoders that meet our conditions, ShiftCache computes the same predictions as dense execution in exact arithmetic. On an A6000, every checked output of the default AlphaGenome and Borzoi runs matches dense execution within an absolute and relative tolerance of $10^{-5}$. With larger tile batches, with Enformer or on an H100, a small fraction of values exceeds this tolerance.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.087467+00	\N	\N	54e0310c6f2209354df1d9f01cdbb34695d36a421a02d7ca4ffbf6c556af87e3	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.087884+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.087886+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3fb4bd67-d510-4a20-99d5-b88b4685781f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11998v1	Reward-Estimated Hypergradient for Bilevel Reinforcement Learning with Black-Box Follower	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11998	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11998	Shigeki Kusaka, Mikoto Kudo, Takumi Tanabe, Akifumi Wachi, Youhei Akimoto	arXiv:2611.11998v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Hypergradient (HG) methods for bilevel reinforcement learning (RL) typically assume a white-box follower whose reward is known. In practice, however, the leader rarely knows the follower's reward function and can only observe its trajectories. We address this black-box setting and propose Reward-Estimated Hypergradients (RE-HG), which recovers the follower's reward from these trajectories via inverse RL (IRL). Yet IRL identifies the reward only up to potential-based shaping, a transformation that leaves the follower's behavior unchanged but biases the HG. We prove that, for linear rewards, this bias cancels when IRL estimates are aggregated across sufficiently diverse dynamics, which the leader's own updates naturally supply. To handle finite samples, RE-HG truncates small eigenvalues of the aggregated feature covariance, restoring the identifiable subspace and stabilizing the estimation. Experiments show that RE-HG estimates gradients aligned with a white-box Oracle and achieves comparable performance; empirically, the truncation also suppresses HG explosions, letting RE-HG avoid local optima that trap the Oracle in sharp reward landscapes.	arXiv:2611.11998v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Hypergradient (HG) methods for bilevel reinforcement learning (RL) typically assume a white-box follower whose reward is known. In practice, however, the leader rarely knows the follower's reward function and can only observe its trajectories. We address this black-box setting and propose Reward-Estimated Hypergradients (RE-HG), which recovers the follower's reward from these trajectories via inverse RL (IRL). Yet IRL identifies the reward only up to potential-based shaping, a transformation that leaves the follower's behavior unchanged but biases the HG. We prove that, for linear rewards, this bias cancels when IRL estimates are aggregated across sufficiently diverse dynamics, which the leader's own updates naturally supply. To handle finite samples, RE-HG truncates small eigenvalues of the aggregated feature covariance, restoring the identifiable subspace and stabilizing the estimation. Experiments show that RE-HG estimates gradients aligned with a white-box Oracle and achieves comparable performance; empirically, the truncation also suppresses HG explosions, letting RE-HG avoid local optima that trap the Oracle in sharp reward landscapes.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.096129+00	\N	\N	2ca63d08f4c925168d96c29232285efa6ae1678738edc85d05a724b26e0fd7c2	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.09644+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.096442+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
7da733aa-0edf-4f6f-b093-3abaac9aefc0	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/safety-bug-bounty	Introducing the OpenAI Safety Bug Bounty program	https://openai.com/index/safety-bug-bounty	https://openai.com/index/safety-bug-bounty	\N	OpenAI launches a Safety Bug Bounty program to identify AI abuse and safety risks, including agentic vulnerabilities, prompt injection, and data exfiltration.	OpenAI launches a Safety Bug Bounty program to identify AI abuse and safety risks, including agentic vulnerabilities, prompt injection, and data exfiltration.	news	en	2026-03-25 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.986782+00	4	\N	2c51f295139e43efde7a849cee095f9d339abba9c357a7ecd17fc208f153f859	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.987371+00	2026-11-10 14:18:52.428948+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e4344470-22dd-4155-9cc0-571957739d30	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12000v1	HyPLM Technical Report	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12000	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12000	Haowei Lin, Cai Zhou, Xiangxin Zhou	arXiv:2611.12000v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Protein language models (PLMs) are standard tools for variant-effect prediction, protein annotation and design, but the compute needed to train and serve them keeps growing. We present HyPLM-Flash, an efficient and open PLM trained by Hyra 2.0, the research agent developed by our team. HyPLM-Flash is a 650-million-parameter masked language model trained on 726 billion tokens, 0.73 times the data of ESM-2 650M and a fifth to an eighth of the data of E1-600M and ESM C 600M. It runs on an inference engine with fused kernels and INT8 matrix multiplication, embedding about 130,000 residues per second on a single NVIDIA H20 GPU, 1.9 to 2.7 times faster than other 600M-class encoders. In a matched evaluation against 28 public checkpoints on 32 tasks, HyPLM-Flash reaches a ProteinGym zero-shot Spearman correlation of 0.409, statistically tied with ESM-2 650M, ESM C 600M and E1-600M, and the highest organismal-fitness score among self-supervised single-sequence models. It gives the best long-range contact precision of all models evaluated, including ESM-2 15B and ESM C 6B, and a lower masked-residue loss than ESM-2 650M, ESM C 600M and E1-600M on held-out sequences and on recently released PDB chains. Matching state-of-the-art accuracy at a fraction of the training and inference cost, HyPLM-Flash sets a new Pareto frontier for protein language models. We have released the model weights and the inference engine at https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/hyra-results/tree/main.	arXiv:2611.12000v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Protein language models (PLMs) are standard tools for variant-effect prediction, protein annotation and design, but the compute needed to train and serve them keeps growing. We present HyPLM-Flash, an efficient and open PLM trained by Hyra 2.0, the research agent developed by our team. HyPLM-Flash is a 650-million-parameter masked language model trained on 726 billion tokens, 0.73 times the data of ESM-2 650M and a fifth to an eighth of the data of E1-600M and ESM C 600M. It runs on an inference engine with fused kernels and INT8 matrix multiplication, embedding about 130,000 residues per second on a single NVIDIA H20 GPU, 1.9 to 2.7 times faster than other 600M-class encoders. In a matched evaluation against 28 public checkpoints on 32 tasks, HyPLM-Flash reaches a ProteinGym zero-shot Spearman correlation of 0.409, statistically tied with ESM-2 650M, ESM C 600M and E1-600M, and the highest organismal-fitness score among self-supervised single-sequence models. It gives the best long-range contact precision of all models evaluated, including ESM-2 15B and ESM C 6B, and a lower masked-residue loss than ESM-2 650M, ESM C 600M and E1-600M on held-out sequences and on recently released PDB chains. Matching state-of-the-art accuracy at a fraction of the training and inference cost, HyPLM-Flash sets a new Pareto frontier for protein language models. We have released the model weights and the inference engine at https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/hyra-results/tree/main.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.098248+00	\N	\N	2e0ec066161119df8c03726bf754cc5589898847aa49367ccbcbfc5326bb088f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.09862+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.098622+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
40676270-4049-4530-863a-3ac96668e63e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12024v1	FocusBranch: Combinatorial Branch-and-Bound for $\\ell_0$ Neural Network Robustness Verification	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12024	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12024	Renwei Deng, Yang He, Linyi Li, Yuepeng Wang	arXiv:2611.12024v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Verifying the local robustness of neural networks under $\\ell_0$ perturbations is inherently combinatorial, as it must reason over all sparse subsets of $\\epsilon$ perturbed pixels. This paper presents FocusBranch, a combinatorial branch-and-bound framework for $\\ell_0$ robustness verification. The key insight is to make the $\\ell_0$-ball decomposable and branchable using a node abstraction, which consists of a focus pixel set $\\mathcal{S}$ and a level $l$, permitting at most $\\epsilon-l$ pixels outside $\\mathcal{S}$. This abstraction unifies two branching mechanisms: concrete nodes branch through upper shadows to fix additional perturbed locations, while super nodes branch through covering designs to cover many perturbation cases with fewer child nodes. FocusBranch proves node safety by concretizing linear relaxations over node-constrained $\\ell_0$-balls and recursively branches on unknown nodes. Rather than relying on a fixed verification plan, it samples nodes across levels to estimate safe rates and costs, then selects a starting level and branching strategy predicted to minimize verification time. Experiments on fully connected and convolutional classifiers for MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 show that FocusBranch verifies $\\ell_0$ robustness effectively across architectures and outperforms state-of-the-art verifiers. The code is available at: https://github.com/simpl-group/FocusBranch.	arXiv:2611.12024v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Verifying the local robustness of neural networks under $\\ell_0$ perturbations is inherently combinatorial, as it must reason over all sparse subsets of $\\epsilon$ perturbed pixels. This paper presents FocusBranch, a combinatorial branch-and-bound framework for $\\ell_0$ robustness verification. The key insight is to make the $\\ell_0$-ball decomposable and branchable using a node abstraction, which consists of a focus pixel set $\\mathcal{S}$ and a level $l$, permitting at most $\\epsilon-l$ pixels outside $\\mathcal{S}$. This abstraction unifies two branching mechanisms: concrete nodes branch through upper shadows to fix additional perturbed locations, while super nodes branch through covering designs to cover many perturbation cases with fewer child nodes. FocusBranch proves node safety by concretizing linear relaxations over node-constrained $\\ell_0$-balls and recursively branches on unknown nodes. Rather than relying on a fixed verification plan, it samples nodes across levels to estimate safe rates and costs, then selects a starting level and branching strategy predicted to minimize verification time. Experiments on fully connected and convolutional classifiers for MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 show that FocusBranch verifies $\\ell_0$ robustness effectively across architectures and outperforms state-of-the-art verifiers. The code is available at: https://github.com/simpl-group/FocusBranch.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.104964+00	\N	\N	c79ce949a219cea45ed90e03bbfa5deea9b0c7eaea22c042b559070cff1c76ee	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.105359+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.105361+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8f790cb9-02c8-43b4-9733-311ba86d4352	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12835v1	Mitigating Agentic Deception via Evaluation-Side Partially Observable Games	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12835	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12835	Hanxiao Li, Shutong Liu, Yongjia Ma, Jiaming Ji, Juntao Dai, Shuning Zhang, Kejian Shi, Kai Chen, Pu Feng, Yuqing Ma, Qi Dou, Yaodong Yang, Xianglong Liu, Simin Li	arXiv:2611.12835v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As large language models become more capable agents, they can exhibit deceptive behaviors, cheating human for higher rewards while appearing helpful. We attribute this failure to evaluation-side partial observability, where models are evaluated mainly from final outcome, without checking the faithfulness of underlying reasoning, tool use, and environmental effects. Prior mitigations address this partial observability by predefined evaluator latent belief space or one-shot monitoring, which do not extend to free-form language space and vulnerable to stronger deceptions that evade simple monitoring. In this work, we formulate deception mitigation as an Evaluation-Side Partially Observable Extensive Form Game (e-PoG), a max-min framework in which an agent maximizes reward, a monitor minimizes the agent's payoff by exposing deception from the full interaction trajectory, and a fixed verifier validates the monitor's claims and converts them into penalties. We define the solution concept as a Subgame-Perfect Oversight Equilibrium (SPOE) between agent and monitor, and prove its existence in finite games. With a perfect verifier, every SPOE is faithful; with an imperfect verifier, the guarantee persists if verifier errors do not reverse the ranking of monitor actions. To achieve faithful SPOE, we derive a two-timescale policy gradient algorithm and enhance the pipeline empirically with NLI verification and deterministic checks. Experiments with Gemma3-4B-IT and Qwen3-4B/8B on Agentic Role-Playing, SearchQA, and WebShop show that e-PoG mitigates multiple emergent deception modes, outperforming the strongest baselines by 26.0% in deception mitigation and by up to 39.8% in task success. These gains persist under weak-to-strong supervision. Code is available at https://github.com/Toyoid/deceptive-agent.	arXiv:2611.12835v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As large language models become more capable agents, they can exhibit deceptive behaviors, cheating human for higher rewards while appearing helpful. We attribute this failure to evaluation-side partial observability, where models are evaluated mainly from final outcome, without checking the faithfulness of underlying reasoning, tool use, and environmental effects. Prior mitigations address this partial observability by predefined evaluator latent belief space or one-shot monitoring, which do not extend to free-form language space and vulnerable to stronger deceptions that evade simple monitoring. In this work, we formulate deception mitigation as an Evaluation-Side Partially Observable Extensive Form Game (e-PoG), a max-min framework in which an agent maximizes reward, a monitor minimizes the agent's payoff by exposing deception from the full interaction trajectory, and a fixed verifier validates the monitor's claims and converts them into penalties. We define the solution concept as a Subgame-Perfect Oversight Equilibrium (SPOE) between agent and monitor, and prove its existence in finite games. With a perfect verifier, every SPOE is faithful; with an imperfect verifier, the guarantee persists if verifier errors do not reverse the ranking of monitor actions. To achieve faithful SPOE, we derive a two-timescale policy gradient algorithm and enhance the pipeline empirically with NLI verification and deterministic checks. Experiments with Gemma3-4B-IT and Qwen3-4B/8B on Agentic Role-Playing, SearchQA, and WebShop show that e-PoG mitigates multiple emergent deception modes, outperforming the strongest baselines by 26.0% in deception mitigation and by up to 39.8% in task success. These gains persist under weak-to-strong supervision. Code is available at https://github.com/Toyoid/deceptive-agent.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.251515+00	\N	\N	cc874310b78edaad938c0d2e5ff982f2abfa289f980de10d828c37dcd5cd4dba	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.251845+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.251846+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
76806923-6f9a-40ef-8803-ad7b88f3e217	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12026v1	MambaPD: Bidirectional Selective State Space Model for Parkinson's Disease Detection from Resting-State EEG	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12026	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12026	Ayesha Zulfiqar	arXiv:2611.12026v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Typical diagnosis of Parkinson's disease (PD) occurs only after significant dopaminergic loss, while resting-state EEG abnormalities like alpha slowing and reduced coherence occur earlier. While many published EEG-based PD detectors claim accuracy greater than 95%, many use epoch-level splits which leak subject identity. I introduce MambaPD, a bidirectional selective state space model for resting-state EEG-based PD detection, blending the channel mixing, patch tokenization and bidirectional Mamba encoder. It is compared with four baselines (TCN, BiLSTM with attention, EEGNet and a patch Transformer) in two public datasets (UNM, SanDiego) using subject-independent 5-fold cross-validation (checkpoints were selected on the held-out fold, so absolute estimates are optimistic). On UNM the accuracy is between 0.67 and 0.71, and MambaPD (0.714) is not statistically distinguishable from the Transformer (0.715), and TCN (0.708), with the highest sensitivity (0.82), while the lowest specificity (0.52). For the smaller SanDiego cohort, accuracy is in the range of 0.60 to 0.70, with the highest scores (0.703) from TCN and the lowest (0.601) from EEGNet, while MambaPD scores 0.631, with the lowest specificity (0.46) among the five models. The differences among the models on UNM are minor compared to the differences among folds. I report the whole pipeline, per-fold results, and the shortcomings of the protocol.	arXiv:2611.12026v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Typical diagnosis of Parkinson's disease (PD) occurs only after significant dopaminergic loss, while resting-state EEG abnormalities like alpha slowing and reduced coherence occur earlier. While many published EEG-based PD detectors claim accuracy greater than 95%, many use epoch-level splits which leak subject identity. I introduce MambaPD, a bidirectional selective state space model for resting-state EEG-based PD detection, blending the channel mixing, patch tokenization and bidirectional Mamba encoder. It is compared with four baselines (TCN, BiLSTM with attention, EEGNet and a patch Transformer) in two public datasets (UNM, SanDiego) using subject-independent 5-fold cross-validation (checkpoints were selected on the held-out fold, so absolute estimates are optimistic). On UNM the accuracy is between 0.67 and 0.71, and MambaPD (0.714) is not statistically distinguishable from the Transformer (0.715), and TCN (0.708), with the highest sensitivity (0.82), while the lowest specificity (0.52). For the smaller SanDiego cohort, accuracy is in the range of 0.60 to 0.70, with the highest scores (0.703) from TCN and the lowest (0.601) from EEGNet, while MambaPD scores 0.631, with the lowest specificity (0.46) among the five models. The differences among the models on UNM are minor compared to the differences among folds. I report the whole pipeline, per-fold results, and the shortcomings of the protocol.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.107654+00	\N	\N	a3275c8d706c93f8d7e538868a9c169e72f13a7e120dc11bbf9d9ef1165f5524	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.107973+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.107974+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5de8591d-7830-4c1e-b337-ed71c2fe4b10	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12058v1	Exact Natarajan Dimension of Homogeneous Multiclass Linear Predictors	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12058	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12058	Jiahua Liu, Benchong Li	arXiv:2611.12058v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The Natarajan dimension is a fundamental measure of the capacity of multiclass hypothesis classes and plays an important role in analyzing their generalization ability. In this paper, by establishing lower and upper bounds, we show that the exact Natarajan dimension of homogeneous multiclass linear predictors with pairwise distinct class weights is \\(n(k-1)-1\\), where $n$ is the dimension of the feature space and $k$ is the number of classes, for \\(n,k\\ge3\\), \\(n,k\\in\\mathbb N\\).	arXiv:2611.12058v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The Natarajan dimension is a fundamental measure of the capacity of multiclass hypothesis classes and plays an important role in analyzing their generalization ability. In this paper, by establishing lower and upper bounds, we show that the exact Natarajan dimension of homogeneous multiclass linear predictors with pairwise distinct class weights is \\(n(k-1)-1\\), where $n$ is the dimension of the feature space and $k$ is the number of classes, for \\(n,k\\ge3\\), \\(n,k\\in\\mathbb N\\).	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.114552+00	\N	\N	867c032bb5a052bc2af1487b07a1fbe7a7a50039a4124b678534274fdfa7dc5c	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.114929+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.114931+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
49b65ba7-0f8b-4e7c-a221-127b518bb2bd	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12062v1	Translating KV Caches Between Hybrid-Attention Language Models: MLA Latents, Linear-Attention States and Sparse-Attention Indexers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12062	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12062	Povilas Kvedaras, Tomas Rasymas	arXiv:2611.12062v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: A model that takes over another model's conversation must normally re-read the whole history; a text hand-off shortens the re-read only by dropping content. KV-cache translation instead maps the source's cache into the target's own format, so the target continues from the full context without re-reading it. It works for grouped-query attention (GQA), but open-weights frontier models now cache compressed latents (MLA), sparse-attention indexer keys and fixed-size linear-attention states. We replicate KV-Lingo on a GQA pair and then give, for each of these objects, where to capture and inject it and a mechanism that reaches native quality. We study two hybrid pairs at 16-48B scale in both directions, Kimi-VL-A3B - Kimi-Linear-48B (MLA - MLA + Kimi Delta Attention) and Qwen3.5-27B - Qwen3.8-27B (Gated DeltaNet hybrids), plus a sparse-attention target (Qwen3.8-27B - Qwen3.8-Flash-Next). MLA latents translate with token-wise linear maps; recurrent states are copied with a closed-form correction or synthesised by replaying the target's own recurrence. Exact long-range recall needs token identity: decoding token ids from the source's layer-0 MLA latent raises needle recall from 0.09 to 0.99 at 2-32K and to 0.83-1.00 at 64K, eight times the longest training prompt. The hybrid full-MLA direction is within the paired confidence interval of native quality on every task metric, and same-shape GDN hybrids reach the native level in both directions (apart from 6% refusals inherited from the source), with time to first token 18.7 and 46.8 times shorter than a re-read at 64K tokens. Segment-wise multi-turn transfer stays at the re-prefill level; naive re-translation collapses. On the sparse-attention target, predicted indexer keys recall the needle as well as the target's own keys (one seed). We state as a hypothesis how the recipes transfer to 300B+ hybrid flagships.	arXiv:2611.12062v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: A model that takes over another model's conversation must normally re-read the whole history; a text hand-off shortens the re-read only by dropping content. KV-cache translation instead maps the source's cache into the target's own format, so the target continues from the full context without re-reading it. It works for grouped-query attention (GQA), but open-weights frontier models now cache compressed latents (MLA), sparse-attention indexer keys and fixed-size linear-attention states. We replicate KV-Lingo on a GQA pair and then give, for each of these objects, where to capture and inject it and a mechanism that reaches native quality. We study two hybrid pairs at 16-48B scale in both directions, Kimi-VL-A3B - Kimi-Linear-48B (MLA - MLA + Kimi Delta Attention) and Qwen3.5-27B - Qwen3.8-27B (Gated DeltaNet hybrids), plus a sparse-attention target (Qwen3.8-27B - Qwen3.8-Flash-Next). MLA latents translate with token-wise linear maps; recurrent states are copied with a closed-form correction or synthesised by replaying the target's own recurrence. Exact long-range recall needs token identity: decoding token ids from the source's layer-0 MLA latent raises needle recall from 0.09 to 0.99 at 2-32K and to 0.83-1.00 at 64K, eight times the longest training prompt. The hybrid full-MLA direction is within the paired confidence interval of native quality on every task metric, and same-shape GDN hybrids reach the native level in both directions (apart from 6% refusals inherited from the source), with time to first token 18.7 and 46.8 times shorter than a re-read at 64K tokens. Segment-wise multi-turn transfer stays at the re-prefill level; naive re-translation collapses. On the sparse-attention target, predicted indexer keys recall the needle as well as the target's own keys (one seed). We state as a hypothesis how the recipes transfer to 300B+ hybrid flagships.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.116692+00	\N	\N	52ffabb52d6eac265334ad5b5519557198f1106f83cb8fd17f5a7b3f5b6b6495	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.117017+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.117018+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f9915230-494d-4bbd-87f7-6e074f709f47	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12475v1	Learning to Select Sparse Linear Solvers with Convolutional Neural Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12475	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12475	Artemis Pados, Alan Edelman, Emmanuel Lujan, Daniel Pickard, Felipe Tome, Christopher Rackauckas	arXiv:2611.12475v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We investigate convolutional-neural-network (CNN) based automatic solver selection for sparse linear systems, a problem of practical importance where solver performance varies dramatically with matrix sparsity structure. A CNN is trained to regress log timing ratios between candidate solvers using spy plot images of matrix nonzero structure as input, achieving strong prediction accuracy on both held-out SuiteSparse matrices and real-world matrices from high-performance simulation codes.	arXiv:2611.12475v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We investigate convolutional-neural-network (CNN) based automatic solver selection for sparse linear systems, a problem of practical importance where solver performance varies dramatically with matrix sparsity structure. A CNN is trained to regress log timing ratios between candidate solvers using spy plot images of matrix nonzero structure as input, achieving strong prediction accuracy on both held-out SuiteSparse matrices and real-world matrices from high-performance simulation codes.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.186607+00	\N	\N	ef61097deb14cb7363e8e68da470d517f6ace1c6079972b7eaa0b6cfa3cb3f13	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.187009+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.187011+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
38c42785-a2cb-46aa-ac66-0ebf1f05102a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12067v1	XposedMIA: Exploiting Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) for One-Shot Membership Inference Attacks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12067	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12067	Omar Hamdache, Serdar Kara, K{\\i}van\\c{c} Kuzey Dikici, Abdullah \\c{C}a\\u{g}lar \\"Oks\\"uz, Anisa Halimi, Erman Ayday, Sinem Sav	arXiv:2611.12067v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Machine learning (ML) services increasingly expose explainability outputs alongside model predictions. While such explanations are intended to improve transparency, they may also reveal information about the underlying training data. In this work, we show that the local surrogate geometry employed by Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) can be repurposed as a proxy for a sample's distance to the model's decision boundary. We extend this boundary-distance view to tabular counterfactual explanations and, for the first time, to visual counterfactual explanations for image classifiers. We present XposedMIA, a one-shot, explanation-based black-box membership inference attack (MIA) framework that converts the returned explanation into a proxy for the queried sample's distance to the model decision boundary. After a one-time calibration step on a small labeled calibration set of as few as 30 samples, our attack requires only a single query for each target sample. Unlike prior label-only attacks requiring iterative queries or shadow training, our method relies solely on the predicted label and its accompanying explanation. Our evaluation indicates that XposedMIA achieves high effectiveness, with success rates of up to 0.95 using only a single query per target, revealing that releasing explanations introduces an additional channel for membership leakage.	arXiv:2611.12067v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Machine learning (ML) services increasingly expose explainability outputs alongside model predictions. While such explanations are intended to improve transparency, they may also reveal information about the underlying training data. In this work, we show that the local surrogate geometry employed by Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) can be repurposed as a proxy for a sample's distance to the model's decision boundary. We extend this boundary-distance view to tabular counterfactual explanations and, for the first time, to visual counterfactual explanations for image classifiers. We present XposedMIA, a one-shot, explanation-based black-box membership inference attack (MIA) framework that converts the returned explanation into a proxy for the queried sample's distance to the model decision boundary. After a one-time calibration step on a small labeled calibration set of as few as 30 samples, our attack requires only a single query for each target sample. Unlike prior label-only attacks requiring iterative queries or shadow training, our method relies solely on the predicted label and its accompanying explanation. Our evaluation indicates that XposedMIA achieves high effectiveness, with success rates of up to 0.95 using only a single query per target, revealing that releasing explanations introduces an additional channel for membership leakage.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.118945+00	\N	\N	67e2d88c5229b89165dd905e45dae39ee59fa94a2cc854dce1aa25b63019e1ac	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.119282+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.119284+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
85682554-73a0-4459-9647-232e34fb3c48	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12079v1	Correct First, Fast Second: What a Local 7B Code Model Can and Cannot Do for GPU Kernel Optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12079	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12079	Jui-Chien Tsou, Po-Yi Wu	arXiv:2611.12079v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We study how much the interaction strategy around a fixed, locally hosted 7B-parameter code model (Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct) affects its ability to replace PyTorch operators with custom CUDA kernels. On 30 KernelBench tasks (levels 1-3, first 10 each, evaluated on a V100), we compare four regimes of increasing structure: zero-shot prompting, profiling-guided prompting, iterative refinement on execution feedback, and a multi-agent loop (analysis, retrieval, generation, evaluation, feedback triage) in which the same model fills four roles around a non-LLM evaluator. Three findings stand out. (1) Correctness, not speed, is the binding constraint: even correct generated kernels average 0.97-0.99x the speed of eager PyTorch, because a 7B model cannot out-write cuBLAS/cuDNN on dense GEMM and convolution; the only real wins are fused memory-bound epilogues. (2) The best strategy depends on difficulty: profiling-guided prompting is strongest overall (43% correct, 27% faster than baseline), but the agent loop is the only method that solves any level-2 fusion problem (3/10 vs. 0/10 for all others). (3) Naive agentic scaffolding is actively harmful: our first agent scored 3% correct, below zero-shot's 30%, and the decisive repairs were matching the agent's ambition to the model's competence and spending compute on best-of-n sampling under a real execution oracle (3% to 33% correct from n=1 to n=8), not on more reasoning steps.	arXiv:2611.12079v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: We study how much the interaction strategy around a fixed, locally hosted 7B-parameter code model (Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct) affects its ability to replace PyTorch operators with custom CUDA kernels. On 30 KernelBench tasks (levels 1-3, first 10 each, evaluated on a V100), we compare four regimes of increasing structure: zero-shot prompting, profiling-guided prompting, iterative refinement on execution feedback, and a multi-agent loop (analysis, retrieval, generation, evaluation, feedback triage) in which the same model fills four roles around a non-LLM evaluator. Three findings stand out. (1) Correctness, not speed, is the binding constraint: even correct generated kernels average 0.97-0.99x the speed of eager PyTorch, because a 7B model cannot out-write cuBLAS/cuDNN on dense GEMM and convolution; the only real wins are fused memory-bound epilogues. (2) The best strategy depends on difficulty: profiling-guided prompting is strongest overall (43% correct, 27% faster than baseline), but the agent loop is the only method that solves any level-2 fusion problem (3/10 vs. 0/10 for all others). (3) Naive agentic scaffolding is actively harmful: our first agent scored 3% correct, below zero-shot's 30%, and the decisive repairs were matching the agent's ambition to the model's competence and spending compute on best-of-n sampling under a real execution oracle (3% to 33% correct from n=1 to n=8), not on more reasoning steps.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.121265+00	\N	\N	d6c002eb211c58fe561ed37255f5cd2abdcee8d2df84d30b7c7448f3a8212252	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.121586+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.121587+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6c9943c1-8fa9-4e3a-947a-11a01934f2df	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12090v1	TSFFormer: A Frequency-Domain Spike Transformer for Green Human Activity Recognition	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12090	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12090	Jiale Deng, Shuiying Xiang	arXiv:2611.12090v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Wearable Inertial Measurement Units (IMUs) enable pervasive Human Activity Recognition (HAR), but traditional Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) suffer from an "arithmetic-latency deadlock" due to dense floating-point MAC operations. While Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) offer ultra-low power, existing ones face spatial blind spots, rigid neurons, and gradient degradation. To address these, we propose TSFFormer, a purely spiking Fourier Transformer for Green HAR. It introduces two key innovations: (1) a MultiHeadFourier attention that performs frequency-domain global mixing, reducing complexity from \\(O(V^2)\\) to \\(O(V\\log V)\\), eliminating spatial blind spots, and saving \\(\\sim\\)50% GPU memory; and (2) a dual-compartment Temporal Spiking LIF (TSLIFNode) that utilizes dynamic thresholds to adapt to non-stationary rhythms and mitigate gradient degradation. Extensive experiments on 8 public IMU benchmarks demonstrate that TSFFormer surpasses state-of-the-art SNNs and even the classic DeepConvLSTM, achieving a \\(\\sim\\)3\\(\\times\\) faster training speed with an energy footprint of 0.1 pJ. Ablation studies confirm the synergistic effect of our modules, providing a highly accurate, ultra-low-power event-driven paradigm for wearable sensing.	arXiv:2611.12090v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Wearable Inertial Measurement Units (IMUs) enable pervasive Human Activity Recognition (HAR), but traditional Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) suffer from an "arithmetic-latency deadlock" due to dense floating-point MAC operations. While Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) offer ultra-low power, existing ones face spatial blind spots, rigid neurons, and gradient degradation. To address these, we propose TSFFormer, a purely spiking Fourier Transformer for Green HAR. It introduces two key innovations: (1) a MultiHeadFourier attention that performs frequency-domain global mixing, reducing complexity from \\(O(V^2)\\) to \\(O(V\\log V)\\), eliminating spatial blind spots, and saving \\(\\sim\\)50% GPU memory; and (2) a dual-compartment Temporal Spiking LIF (TSLIFNode) that utilizes dynamic thresholds to adapt to non-stationary rhythms and mitigate gradient degradation. Extensive experiments on 8 public IMU benchmarks demonstrate that TSFFormer surpasses state-of-the-art SNNs and even the classic DeepConvLSTM, achieving a \\(\\sim\\)3\\(\\times\\) faster training speed with an energy footprint of 0.1 pJ. Ablation studies confirm the synergistic effect of our modules, providing a highly accurate, ultra-low-power event-driven paradigm for wearable sensing.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.12569+00	\N	\N	919133854ba12a4ea840622a291f07fa90b467a87af928442ecde3c74dc5d05d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.12602+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.126022+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
40e17292-364c-4e75-9bb3-112728940ef0	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12097v1	Calibrating Document-Local Boundaries for Contextual Chunk Retrieval	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12097	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12097	Yuanxiang Wang, Hui Jiang, Da Zhu, Zhihua Chang	arXiv:2611.12097v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Late Chunking encodes an entire document before pooling at chunk boundaries, preserving document context in local retrieval units. Chunk-level post-training improves context-dependent retrieval, but shared context can make semantically similar same-document negatives difficult to distinguish. We study the local decision boundary between a gold chunk and its hardest sibling. Starting from InSeNT, we analyze InSeNT's within-document gradients and show that the entropy of the conditional negative allocation decreases monotonically with the scaling factor at fixed logits. We propose a two-part framework for calibrating document-local chunk boundaries. The first component adapts the discriminative scale from within-document margins, while the second exposes chunk partitions to attention during document encoding. Experiments on ConTEB and NanoBEIR show that the resulting model improves contextual chunk retrieval across the evaluated encoder settings while retaining competitive passage-level retrieval quality; ablations further characterize the distinct roles of adaptive scaling and chunk-aware routing.	arXiv:2611.12097v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Late Chunking encodes an entire document before pooling at chunk boundaries, preserving document context in local retrieval units. Chunk-level post-training improves context-dependent retrieval, but shared context can make semantically similar same-document negatives difficult to distinguish. We study the local decision boundary between a gold chunk and its hardest sibling. Starting from InSeNT, we analyze InSeNT's within-document gradients and show that the entropy of the conditional negative allocation decreases monotonically with the scaling factor at fixed logits. We propose a two-part framework for calibrating document-local chunk boundaries. The first component adapts the discriminative scale from within-document margins, while the second exposes chunk partitions to attention during document encoding. Experiments on ConTEB and NanoBEIR show that the resulting model improves contextual chunk retrieval across the evaluated encoder settings while retaining competitive passage-level retrieval quality; ablations further characterize the distinct roles of adaptive scaling and chunk-aware routing.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.127894+00	\N	\N	65772039e2bdc0bb263e95f87b44dc5e307dc4bafb582d2c9c61cf6ea73a23e6	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.128201+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.128203+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5a901549-3db6-4fa6-8ad7-47f8f73d7c55	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12178v1	Replication of Thermal Comfort Cohort Models: No Support for Improved Thermal Preference Prediction	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12178	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12178	Thomas Hirsch, Katja Ceplak, Veronika Windisch, Samina Kadkhoda Masoumali, Matias Quintana, Clayton Miller, Stefano Schiavon, Gerald Schweiger	arXiv:2611.12178v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Cohort comfort models (CCMs) aim to overcome the extensive individual data requirements of personal comfort models (PCMs) by grouping occupants based on demographic and psychometric characteristics (cold start) or similar thermal preferences (warm start). We evaluated the validity of the reported CCM performance gains through (i) a methodological assessment of the original study, (ii) a replication using one of the two original datasets (DORN: 20 participants, 6 months) under a revised experimental protocol, and (iii) a replication using an independently collected dataset (PersonAI: 72 participants, 4 weeks). Classification performance was evaluated using F1-micro, as in the original study, and F1-macro to account for class imbalance. Under F1-micro, a trivial baseline that always outputs the majority class performed on par with cohort models. Under F1-macro, all cohort models achieved lower median scores than a simple baseline that makes random predictions based on the estimated distribution of thermal preferences. In our methodological assessment we identified the following key issues: Limited data per participant and per minority class, reliance on a metric insensitive to class imbalance, missing trivial baselines, inadequate cross validation strategy, and multiple occurrences of information leakage. Our findings challenge the original study's conclusion that CCMs improve thermal preference prediction. CCMs did not outperform simple class-distribution baselines. Future studies should improve experimental designs, including controlled thermal conditions, to obtain sufficient observations across thermal preference classes.	arXiv:2611.12178v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Cohort comfort models (CCMs) aim to overcome the extensive individual data requirements of personal comfort models (PCMs) by grouping occupants based on demographic and psychometric characteristics (cold start) or similar thermal preferences (warm start). We evaluated the validity of the reported CCM performance gains through (i) a methodological assessment of the original study, (ii) a replication using one of the two original datasets (DORN: 20 participants, 6 months) under a revised experimental protocol, and (iii) a replication using an independently collected dataset (PersonAI: 72 participants, 4 weeks). Classification performance was evaluated using F1-micro, as in the original study, and F1-macro to account for class imbalance. Under F1-micro, a trivial baseline that always outputs the majority class performed on par with cohort models. Under F1-macro, all cohort models achieved lower median scores than a simple baseline that makes random predictions based on the estimated distribution of thermal preferences. In our methodological assessment we identified the following key issues: Limited data per participant and per minority class, reliance on a metric insensitive to class imbalance, missing trivial baselines, inadequate cross validation strategy, and multiple occurrences of information leakage. Our findings challenge the original study's conclusion that CCMs improve thermal preference prediction. CCMs did not outperform simple class-distribution baselines. Future studies should improve experimental designs, including controlled thermal conditions, to obtain sufficient observations across thermal preference classes.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.145605+00	\N	\N	723ebefc343d74b1fd293bb7da14b08bc64a856cc4b6d2a091fc4b4d60da3fe6	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.14592+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.145921+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cf294f3f-5a5b-4aac-9189-a1fb31a5aedb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12187v1	Harnessing Vision-Language Models for Federated Unsupervised Visual Recognition	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12187	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12187	Jian Liang, Kuangpu Guo, Ran He, Tieniu Tan	arXiv:2611.12187v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Federated learning (FL) is a promising research paradigm that enables collaborative model training across distributed clients without data sharing. To handle unlabeled data in local clients, existing unsupervised FL research has primarily focused on tasks like clustering and self-supervised representation learning, leaving the critical problem of unsupervised visual recognition largely unexplored. To bridge this gap, we introduce FedUR, the first framework designed for unsupervised recognition by leveraging the zero-shot capabilities of pre-trained vision-language models like CLIP. Specifically, FedUR addresses data heterogeneity through a pseudo-labeling mechanism with a class-aware data selection strategy. This strategy first estimates a global pseudo-label distribution, then allocates pseudo-labeled data to clients accordingly. To handle the uncertainty of conventional positive pseudo-labels, FedUR further incorporates class weights and a negative pseudo-labeling strategy. Furthermore, FedUR proposes a personalized prompt aggregation protocol where general visual prompts are aggregated on the server, and personalized text prompts encoding local knowledge are retained on clients. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FedUR consistently outperforms baseline methods under both label-level and feature-level distribution shifts. We believe this work paves the way for further advances in federated unsupervised recognition. Code will be available at {github.com/tim-learn/FedUR/}}.	arXiv:2611.12187v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Federated learning (FL) is a promising research paradigm that enables collaborative model training across distributed clients without data sharing. To handle unlabeled data in local clients, existing unsupervised FL research has primarily focused on tasks like clustering and self-supervised representation learning, leaving the critical problem of unsupervised visual recognition largely unexplored. To bridge this gap, we introduce FedUR, the first framework designed for unsupervised recognition by leveraging the zero-shot capabilities of pre-trained vision-language models like CLIP. Specifically, FedUR addresses data heterogeneity through a pseudo-labeling mechanism with a class-aware data selection strategy. This strategy first estimates a global pseudo-label distribution, then allocates pseudo-labeled data to clients accordingly. To handle the uncertainty of conventional positive pseudo-labels, FedUR further incorporates class weights and a negative pseudo-labeling strategy. Furthermore, FedUR proposes a personalized prompt aggregation protocol where general visual prompts are aggregated on the server, and personalized text prompts encoding local knowledge are retained on clients. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FedUR consistently outperforms baseline methods under both label-level and feature-level distribution shifts. We believe this work paves the way for further advances in federated unsupervised recognition. Code will be available at {github.com/tim-learn/FedUR/}}.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.150308+00	\N	\N	f094d15e045096f21450f2ec1967e385293b0c378872de1d79971d1582239c18	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.150625+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.150627+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
7ccd3369-3d92-4878-8933-1676f16cf50a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12491v1	Out-of-the-Box Tabular Foundation Models for Bayesian Optimization in Materials Discovery	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12491	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12491	Klemens Fl\\"oge, Simon Bing, Leo Grinsztajn, Eliott Kalfon, Lennart Purucker, Noah Hollmann, Frank Hutter	arXiv:2611.12491v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Bayesian optimization is widely used in active learning loops for materials science. Gaussian processes serve as the de-facto surrogate model family, but require task-dependent kernel choices and can lack the modeling capacity for complex relationships. Tabular foundation models (TFMs) have recently emerged as the state-of-the-art for predictive tasks on structured data, without requiring bespoke kernels or hyperparameter tuning. We evaluate two TFMs, TabPFN and TabICL, as out-of-the-box surrogate models on two standard materials science benchmarks, comparing against a large field of baselines encompassing Gaussian processes, random forests, and CatBoost. Our findings indicate that TFMs are highly competitive surrogate models across the two benchmarks, without task-specific training for active learning or GP kernel selection. Our results motivate developing TFMs further as surrogates for active learning in materials discovery.	arXiv:2611.12491v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Bayesian optimization is widely used in active learning loops for materials science. Gaussian processes serve as the de-facto surrogate model family, but require task-dependent kernel choices and can lack the modeling capacity for complex relationships. Tabular foundation models (TFMs) have recently emerged as the state-of-the-art for predictive tasks on structured data, without requiring bespoke kernels or hyperparameter tuning. We evaluate two TFMs, TabPFN and TabICL, as out-of-the-box surrogate models on two standard materials science benchmarks, comparing against a large field of baselines encompassing Gaussian processes, random forests, and CatBoost. Our findings indicate that TFMs are highly competitive surrogate models across the two benchmarks, without task-specific training for active learning or GP kernel selection. Our results motivate developing TFMs further as surrogates for active learning in materials discovery.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.189069+00	\N	\N	aaeff6fe7ea9c7b932a8f77270ebd8dbb07f2fe4d70e6d4fd98d42e06236468e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.189499+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.189501+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d428d540-ccad-4f95-bc44-375254fc2187	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12509v1	UniHam: A Large-Scale SOC-Complete Dataset and Benchmark for Hamiltonian Learning in Materials	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12509	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12509	Yuewen Huang, Pin Chen, Yutong Lu	arXiv:2611.12509v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Accurate prediction of electronic Hamiltonians would enable broad property inference while avoiding the high computational cost of Density Functional Theory (DFT). However, progress toward general-purpose materials foundation models is limited by a data bottleneck: existing Hamiltonian datasets are typically small, lack structural diversity, and often omit essential relativistic physics such as spin--orbit coupling (SOC). We therefore construct UniHam, a large-scale Hamiltonian dataset and benchmark suite comprising 100,000+ DFT-computed complex-valued Hermitian Hamiltonians with full SOC, covering 72 elements and a wide range of crystal geometries and symmetries (spanning diverse lattice types and space-group families). Building on UniHam, we benchmark two representative state-of-the-art models under a standardized protocol and introduce complementary evaluation metrics that jointly assess three dimensions: (i) Hamiltonian reconstruction accuracy, (ii) out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization across composition/symmetry shifts, and (iii) the ability to support downstream property prediction from the predicted Hamiltonians. Experiments on UniHam demonstrate that the proposed benchmark and metrics effectively differentiate model capabilities, revealing intrinsic SOC- and element-dependent failure modes, large variations in compositional OOD robustness, and the necessity of spectral-level evaluation to assess whether Hamiltonian predictions reliably support downstream electronic-structure properties. Overall, UniHam provides a reproducible, SOC-complete benchmark that can sharpen model comparisons and accelerate the development of next-generation foundation models for quantum materials.	arXiv:2611.12509v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Accurate prediction of electronic Hamiltonians would enable broad property inference while avoiding the high computational cost of Density Functional Theory (DFT). However, progress toward general-purpose materials foundation models is limited by a data bottleneck: existing Hamiltonian datasets are typically small, lack structural diversity, and often omit essential relativistic physics such as spin--orbit coupling (SOC). We therefore construct UniHam, a large-scale Hamiltonian dataset and benchmark suite comprising 100,000+ DFT-computed complex-valued Hermitian Hamiltonians with full SOC, covering 72 elements and a wide range of crystal geometries and symmetries (spanning diverse lattice types and space-group families). Building on UniHam, we benchmark two representative state-of-the-art models under a standardized protocol and introduce complementary evaluation metrics that jointly assess three dimensions: (i) Hamiltonian reconstruction accuracy, (ii) out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization across composition/symmetry shifts, and (iii) the ability to support downstream property prediction from the predicted Hamiltonians. Experiments on UniHam demonstrate that the proposed benchmark and metrics effectively differentiate model capabilities, revealing intrinsic SOC- and element-dependent failure modes, large variations in compositional OOD robustness, and the necessity of spectral-level evaluation to assess whether Hamiltonian predictions reliably support downstream electronic-structure properties. Overall, UniHam provides a reproducible, SOC-complete benchmark that can sharpen model comparisons and accelerate the development of next-generation foundation models for quantum materials.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.191559+00	\N	\N	0953c7dd38bffb9e878801c81309c566c9f03315901ef52d9078fe8be923105a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.191886+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.191887+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
11d03760-e106-46c9-8986-972f9a83eb37	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12521v1	Can the Confidence and Decisions of System 1 Models Be Trusted? An Explainability and Perturbation Audit of Jev-Style Decision Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12521	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12521	Julien Delaunay	arXiv:2611.12521v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: "System 1" decision models (pioneered by TypeSafe AI's Jev) replace autoregressive text generation with a single forward pass returning typed probability distributions over declared schemas (Choice, Score, Noul). While deployed for low-latency routing and automated triage, their statistical calibration and post-hoc explainability remain uncharacterized. We introduce an open, deterministic audit benchmark evaluating System 1 decision models across calibration under label-preserving perturbations, cross-question interference, and post-hoc attribution faithfulness. Evaluating five open-weight models (0.8B to 27B) alongside generative System 2 baselines -- both single-token logprob scoring and structured JSON/CoT decoding -- across NLI (e-SNLI), adversarial syntactic shortcuts (HANS), synthetic clinical triage (N=900), prompt injection defense (Tensor Trust, N=250), and an 86-case logic-gate trigger suite yields five findings: (1) provider confidence is an exact closed-form dispersion transform (1 - H(p)/ln K on sub-10B models; normalized margin on Clef-27B) rather than empirical accuracy; (2) bundling multiple questions in one request induces directional cross-question interference (11.7% to 22.4% binary flips on Tev1-0.8B, vs. 0.1% to 0.2% on Tev1-4B); (3) System 1 models with >= 4B parameters are faster and better calibrated under syntactic shifts (ECE_w = 0.030 to 0.087) than logprob (0.144) and JSON (0.378) baselines; (4) for prompt-injection defense (N=250), >= 4B models achieve 0.988 to 0.998 AUROC with up to 95.2% recall and 0.0% false alarms at 48 to 60 ms; and (5) single-pass inference makes 512-coalition XAI interactive (1.3 to 3.2 s/input), while KernelSHAP with a logit link (S1-SHAP) mitigates softmax saturation on disjunctive OR gates (89.3% to 100.0% gate recovery vs. 57.1% to 82.1% in prob space; p_adj = 0.004).	arXiv:2611.12521v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: "System 1" decision models (pioneered by TypeSafe AI's Jev) replace autoregressive text generation with a single forward pass returning typed probability distributions over declared schemas (Choice, Score, Noul). While deployed for low-latency routing and automated triage, their statistical calibration and post-hoc explainability remain uncharacterized. We introduce an open, deterministic audit benchmark evaluating System 1 decision models across calibration under label-preserving perturbations, cross-question interference, and post-hoc attribution faithfulness. Evaluating five open-weight models (0.8B to 27B) alongside generative System 2 baselines -- both single-token logprob scoring and structured JSON/CoT decoding -- across NLI (e-SNLI), adversarial syntactic shortcuts (HANS), synthetic clinical triage (N=900), prompt injection defense (Tensor Trust, N=250), and an 86-case logic-gate trigger suite yields five findings: (1) provider confidence is an exact closed-form dispersion transform (1 - H(p)/ln K on sub-10B models; normalized margin on Clef-27B) rather than empirical accuracy; (2) bundling multiple questions in one request induces directional cross-question interference (11.7% to 22.4% binary flips on Tev1-0.8B, vs. 0.1% to 0.2% on Tev1-4B); (3) System 1 models with >= 4B parameters are faster and better calibrated under syntactic shifts (ECE_w = 0.030 to 0.087) than logprob (0.144) and JSON (0.378) baselines; (4) for prompt-injection defense (N=250), >= 4B models achieve 0.988 to 0.998 AUROC with up to 95.2% recall and 0.0% false alarms at 48 to 60 ms; and (5) single-pass inference makes 512-coalition XAI interactive (1.3 to 3.2 s/input), while KernelSHAP with a logit link (S1-SHAP) mitigates softmax saturation on disjunctive OR gates (89.3% to 100.0% gate recovery vs. 57.1% to 82.1% in prob space; p_adj = 0.004).	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.19414+00	\N	\N	bfa3b914dfd246fbb689d2cff7f56bd73dc31fc77ab24d80d0afb7934a263467	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.194466+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.194467+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
079afb7b-fdab-4b02-b932-7549c7e87b05	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12285v1	Learning Evidence-Grounded Reasoning for Context-Aware Time Series Forecasting	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12285	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12285	Xingpeng Gao, Mingyue Cheng, Daoyu Wang, Huajian Zhang, Xiaoyu Tao	arXiv:2611.12285v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: LLM-driven time series forecasting has become increasingly promising by leveraging contextual features to generate future values directly or refine forecast priors. However, incorporating contextual features alone does not ensure that models can identify which features matter for forecasting and understand what they suggest about future dynamics. We argue that the fundamental challenge is how to extract predictive evidence from contextual features and when and how to leverage it to guide forecasting decisions. In this paper, we propose CastReasoner, an evidence-grounded reasoning model for context-aware time series forecasting. Specifically, CastReasoner uses a structured chain of thought (CoT) to extract predictive evidence from contextual features, infer the future dynamics suggested by this evidence, and assess these dynamics against the forecast prior to guide adaptive forecasting decisions. To enable this capability, we design a reasoning-centric two-stage post-training framework including supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement learning (RL). First, we synthesize reasoning trajectories through controllable multi-teacher guidance and select high-quality ones using a novel student-aware difficulty metric. Second, we design fine-grained multi-objective rewards to further improve reasoning and forecasting. To address heterogeneous learning needs across domains in both stages, we introduce a convergence-guided data balancing strategy. Through this two-stage training, CastReasoner improves both reasoning quality and predictive accuracy. Extensive experiments across multi-domain forecasting benchmarks demonstrate that CastReasoner achieves superior forecasting performance.	arXiv:2611.12285v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: LLM-driven time series forecasting has become increasingly promising by leveraging contextual features to generate future values directly or refine forecast priors. However, incorporating contextual features alone does not ensure that models can identify which features matter for forecasting and understand what they suggest about future dynamics. We argue that the fundamental challenge is how to extract predictive evidence from contextual features and when and how to leverage it to guide forecasting decisions. In this paper, we propose CastReasoner, an evidence-grounded reasoning model for context-aware time series forecasting. Specifically, CastReasoner uses a structured chain of thought (CoT) to extract predictive evidence from contextual features, infer the future dynamics suggested by this evidence, and assess these dynamics against the forecast prior to guide adaptive forecasting decisions. To enable this capability, we design a reasoning-centric two-stage post-training framework including supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement learning (RL). First, we synthesize reasoning trajectories through controllable multi-teacher guidance and select high-quality ones using a novel student-aware difficulty metric. Second, we design fine-grained multi-objective rewards to further improve reasoning and forecasting. To address heterogeneous learning needs across domains in both stages, we introduce a convergence-guided data balancing strategy. Through this two-stage training, CastReasoner improves both reasoning quality and predictive accuracy. Extensive experiments across multi-domain forecasting benchmarks demonstrate that CastReasoner achieves superior forecasting performance.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.166319+00	\N	\N	cd6eb9278e8e11656f84727b5436684062e8548e937dcdad1527139ff9195e86	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.166693+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.166695+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b03dd6db-54b7-43fe-9dd3-d880909fef2f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12419v1	Optimal Rates for Adaptive Private $k$-PCA	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12419	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12419	Johanna D\\"ungler, Amartya Sanyal	arXiv:2611.12419v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Given $n$ i.i.d. random matrices $A_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$ with common expectation $\\Sigma$, the goal of \\emph{Differentially Private Stochastic PCA} is to identify a $k$-dimensional subspace capturing the leading variance directions of $\\Sigma$, while preserving differential privacy~(DP) for each individual sample $A_i$. \\citet{dungler2025iterative} introduced \\kdppca, the first algorithm to simultaneously (1) achieve sample complexity $n=\\widetilde O(d)$ for sub-Gaussian data, (2) adapt its privacy noise to the intrinsic randomness of the data, and (3) extend seamlessly to any target dimension $k\\le d$. However, its sample complexity has a suboptimal dependence on $k$. We propose the first algorithm that achieves optimal sample complexity in both $d$ and $k$, while retaining properties (2) and (3) of \\kdppca. Additionally, prior utility guarantees require a minimum sample size with exponential dependence on the eigengap. Our method removes this dependence. It also improves the dependence on spectral parameters, matching known lower bounds for the spiked covariance model. Unlike deflation-based approaches like \\kdppca, our algorithm updates the full $d\\times k$ subspace jointly rather than one eigenvector at a time. This non-deflation structure simplifies the algorithm, reduces the number of hyper-parameters, and improves computational efficiency, particularly when using block linear algebra libraries.	arXiv:2611.12419v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Given $n$ i.i.d. random matrices $A_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$ with common expectation $\\Sigma$, the goal of \\emph{Differentially Private Stochastic PCA} is to identify a $k$-dimensional subspace