e81f05b2-c458-4e34-8914-56e01bf45d90	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2509.22574v2	Machine learning approaches to seismic event classification in the Ostrava region	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22574	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22574	Michael Skotnica, Marek Pecha, Jana Ru\\v{s}ajov\\'a, Bohdan Rieznikov, V\\'it Wandrol, Mark\\'eta R\\"osnerov\\'a, Jarom\\'ir Knejzl\\'ik	arXiv:2509.22574v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: The north-eastern part of the Czech Republic, including the Czech part of the Upper Silesian Coal Basin, is among the most seismically active areas of the country. The seismic stations in the region record mining-induced events, which continue after the cessation of mining, natural earthquakes, which are rare but may originate in the same area, and quarry blasts. A rapid and reliable distinction between the three types is required for the routine evaluation of the continuously recorded data. We study their automatic discrimination with neural networks from short three-component windows starting one second before the P-wave onset. The main data set is the archive of the Seismic Polygon Fren\\v{s}t\\'{a}t (1992--2002), a triggered network whose records were recovered from the original format, cleaned with deterministic and learned quality checks and completed with automatically picked P-wave arrivals; it contains about 40,000 records of all three event types. Among five compared architectures, convolutional networks operating on short-time spectrograms outperform recurrent networks operating on magnitude spectra or on the raw waveform. The best model, EfficientNet-B0 applied to 24-s windows, reaches a macro-averaged F1 score of 0.96, and blasts and natural earthquakes are practically never confused with each other; class weighting and undersampling, the usual remedies for the strong class imbalance, lower the score. Finally, we examine the transfer to the station OKC, which monitors the region today with different instruments. A model trained on the archive alone fails on the modern records, whereas a single model trained on both data sets reaches a macro-F1 score of 0.96 on the archive and 0.92 on the modern station, where the accuracy is limited by the small number of catalogued natural earthquakes rather than by the model.	arXiv:2509.22574v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: The north-eastern part of the Czech Republic, including the Czech part of the Upper Silesian Coal Basin, is among the most seismically active areas of the country. The seismic stations in the region record mining-induced events, which continue after the cessation of mining, natural earthquakes, which are rare but may originate in the same area, and quarry blasts. A rapid and reliable distinction between the three types is required for the routine evaluation of the continuously recorded data. We study their automatic discrimination with neural networks from short three-component windows starting one second before the P-wave onset. The main data set is the archive of the Seismic Polygon Fren\\v{s}t\\'{a}t (1992--2002), a triggered network whose records were recovered from the original format, cleaned with deterministic and learned quality checks and completed with automatically picked P-wave arrivals; it contains about 40,000 records of all three event types. Among five compared architectures, convolutional networks operating on short-time spectrograms outperform recurrent networks operating on magnitude spectra or on the raw waveform. The best model, EfficientNet-B0 applied to 24-s windows, reaches a macro-averaged F1 score of 0.96, and blasts and natural earthquakes are practically never confused with each other; class weighting and undersampling, the usual remedies for the strong class imbalance, lower the score. Finally, we examine the transfer to the station OKC, which monitors the region today with different instruments. A model trained on the archive alone fails on the modern records, whereas a single model trained on both data sets reaches a macro-F1 score of 0.96 on the archive and 0.92 on the modern station, where the accuracy is limited by the small number of catalogued natural earthquakes rather than by the model.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.841597+00	\N	\N	19b5deb7941ac9342db4e322512340b4884bad253c288b70907db9efdedb1c91	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.841935+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.841936+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3847ba43-0da0-4646-9b18-72215e2bcd35	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.16906v2	The Trajectory Alignment Coefficient in Two Acts: From Reward Tuning to Reward Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.16906	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.16906	Calarina Muslimani, Yunshu Du, Kenta Kawamoto, Kaushik Subramanian, Peter Stone, Peter Wurman	arXiv:2601.16906v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: The success of RL is fundamentally tied to having a reward function that accurately reflects the task objective. Yet, designing reward functions is time-consuming and prone to misspecification. To address this, our first goal is to understand how to support RL practitioners in specifying reward functions. We leverage the Trajectory Alignment Coefficient (TAC), a metric that evaluates how closely a reward function's induced preferences match those of a domain expert. To test whether TAC provides meaningful support, we evaluated its impact in a user study where practitioners designed reward functions for Lunar Lander. We found that iterative TAC feedback allowed participants to design more performant reward functions while reporting lower cognitive workload than unguided reward design. However, the study also underscored that manual reward design, even with TAC, remains labor-intensive. This motivated our second goal: to learn a reward model that maximizes TAC directly. Specifically, we propose Soft-TAC, a differentiable approximation of TAC that can be used as a loss function to train reward models from human preferences. Our theoretical analysis guarantees that under Soft-TAC optimization: (1) the optimal reward function belongs exclusively to the set of human-aligned reward functions, and (2) this optimal reward remains invariant whether trained on clean or noisy preference data. Empirically, we first evaluate reward models trained with Soft-TAC across three robotic tasks, demonstrating that they consistently match or outperform those trained with the Cross-Entropy loss. Next, we evaluate Soft-TAC in the GT7 driving simulator, and find that Soft-TAC-trained reward models more successfully capture preference-specific driving styles. Ultimately, this work demonstrates that TAC can serve as both a practical tool for guiding reward design and a reward learning objective in complex domains.	arXiv:2601.16906v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: The success of RL is fundamentally tied to having a reward function that accurately reflects the task objective. Yet, designing reward functions is time-consuming and prone to misspecification. To address this, our first goal is to understand how to support RL practitioners in specifying reward functions. We leverage the Trajectory Alignment Coefficient (TAC), a metric that evaluates how closely a reward function's induced preferences match those of a domain expert. To test whether TAC provides meaningful support, we evaluated its impact in a user study where practitioners designed reward functions for Lunar Lander. We found that iterative TAC feedback allowed participants to design more performant reward functions while reporting lower cognitive workload than unguided reward design. However, the study also underscored that manual reward design, even with TAC, remains labor-intensive. This motivated our second goal: to learn a reward model that maximizes TAC directly. Specifically, we propose Soft-TAC, a differentiable approximation of TAC that can be used as a loss function to train reward models from human preferences. Our theoretical analysis guarantees that under Soft-TAC optimization: (1) the optimal reward function belongs exclusively to the set of human-aligned reward functions, and (2) this optimal reward remains invariant whether trained on clean or noisy preference data. Empirically, we first evaluate reward models trained with Soft-TAC across three robotic tasks, demonstrating that they consistently match or outperform those trained with the Cross-Entropy loss. Next, we evaluate Soft-TAC in the GT7 driving simulator, and find that Soft-TAC-trained reward models more successfully capture preference-specific driving styles. Ultimately, this work demonstrates that TAC can serve as both a practical tool for guiding reward design and a reward learning objective in complex domains.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.891845+00	\N	\N	7f3eba9cd3fa5f105b38b3f411844133e494a412e3b68d494fa454736ddf7f7d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.892187+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.892189+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bdf2e4dd-3fff-4933-82f5-b104844759a2	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.20154v3	Spectral Ghost in Representation Learning: A Unified View from Component Analysis to Self-Supervised Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.20154	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.20154	Bo Dai, Na Li, Dale Schuurmans	arXiv:2601.20154v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Self-supervised learning (SSL) extracts representations from massive unlabeled data that transfer to a variety of downstream tasks. Its empirical success has produced a dizzying array of objectives and procedures, yet a clear understanding of what representation is desired, and why these seemingly different methods work, is still missing. In this paper, we develop a unified spectral framework for representation learning. We first show that a representation is sufficient for arbitrary downstream prediction with a linear head if it spans the spectral decomposition of the pointwise mutual information, which can be extracted purely from unlabeled data. This view organizes representation learning along two axes, the parametrization of the spectral representation (linear, energy-based, denoising, nonlinear, latent-variable, and multi-modal) and the learning objective for fitting it, under which a broad range of existing methods, including SimCLR, MoCo, BYOL, Barlow Twins, VICReg, DINO, MAE, JEPA, BERT, GPT, and CLIP, as well as classic PCA, CCA, Laplacian eigenmaps, LLE, and SNE, are recovered as particular combinations. The framework also clarifies optimization behaviors: the batch-size requirement is determined by whether the objective is unbiased, a consistent surrogate, or biased, rather than by contrastive vs. non-contrastive losses. Finally, the framework inspires new scalable algorithms and extends spectral representations to causal inference and reinforcement learning.	arXiv:2601.20154v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Self-supervised learning (SSL) extracts representations from massive unlabeled data that transfer to a variety of downstream tasks. Its empirical success has produced a dizzying array of objectives and procedures, yet a clear understanding of what representation is desired, and why these seemingly different methods work, is still missing. In this paper, we develop a unified spectral framework for representation learning. We first show that a representation is sufficient for arbitrary downstream prediction with a linear head if it spans the spectral decomposition of the pointwise mutual information, which can be extracted purely from unlabeled data. This view organizes representation learning along two axes, the parametrization of the spectral representation (linear, energy-based, denoising, nonlinear, latent-variable, and multi-modal) and the learning objective for fitting it, under which a broad range of existing methods, including SimCLR, MoCo, BYOL, Barlow Twins, VICReg, DINO, MAE, JEPA, BERT, GPT, and CLIP, as well as classic PCA, CCA, Laplacian eigenmaps, LLE, and SNE, are recovered as particular combinations. The framework also clarifies optimization behaviors: the batch-size requirement is determined by whether the objective is unbiased, a consistent surrogate, or biased, rather than by contrastive vs. non-contrastive losses. Finally, the framework inspires new scalable algorithms and extends spectral representations to causal inference and reinforcement learning.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.894145+00	\N	\N	f766a4e6e6182b77a54e216e50bc9c4ec82bfd3db816ec6ed9074b68e01259cc	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.894474+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.894476+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
26ffff83-f763-4469-8228-a360e193ebde	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.22230v2	JURIST: Training Small Data-Reweighted Reasoning Judges for Test-Time Scaling in Code Generation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22230	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22230	Peijia Qin, Ruiyi Zhang, Qi Cao, Pengtao Xie	arXiv:2601.22230v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Best-of-N selection for code generation is only as good as the judge that picks among the candidates, and the judges accurate enough for this role are costly frontier LLMs. A small judge can be trained on abundant earlier data labeled by unit tests, but it is deployed on newer problems: the earlier problems are easier and teach it little, their tasks differ, and their candidates come from weaker policies, while data close to deployment are scarce. We present JURIST, a 1.7B reasoning judge that uses a small meta set close to deployment to learn how much each earlier training sample should count. In our experiments, we find that training on the meta set alone, or adding it to the training data, does not help, while using it to weight the earlier data through a bi-level objective adds 3.9 Hard points over adding it to the training data. On LiveCodeBench, JURIST reaches 84.7% Overall and matches Claude Sonnet 4.6 on Hard problems (73.8%) at 463x lower cost; on BigCodeBench, it surpasses all test-time scaling baselines (35.9%). Abundant earlier data, guided by a small deployment-aligned meta set, can thus train a compact judge that selects accurately at low cost.	arXiv:2601.22230v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Best-of-N selection for code generation is only as good as the judge that picks among the candidates, and the judges accurate enough for this role are costly frontier LLMs. A small judge can be trained on abundant earlier data labeled by unit tests, but it is deployed on newer problems: the earlier problems are easier and teach it little, their tasks differ, and their candidates come from weaker policies, while data close to deployment are scarce. We present JURIST, a 1.7B reasoning judge that uses a small meta set close to deployment to learn how much each earlier training sample should count. In our experiments, we find that training on the meta set alone, or adding it to the training data, does not help, while using it to weight the earlier data through a bi-level objective adds 3.9 Hard points over adding it to the training data. On LiveCodeBench, JURIST reaches 84.7% Overall and matches Claude Sonnet 4.6 on Hard problems (73.8%) at 463x lower cost; on BigCodeBench, it surpasses all test-time scaling baselines (35.9%). Abundant earlier data, guided by a small deployment-aligned meta set, can thus train a compact judge that selects accurately at low cost.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.903939+00	\N	\N	85445d3d106ed96a0f6f7b1be1b0e31ae97218a559861569cb0b0b77c15683ea	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.904293+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.904295+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b22a2c68-2991-4c24-b473-0c8d158ad20f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.23180v2	TriSpec: Ternary Speculative Decoding via Lightweight Proxy Verification	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.23180	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.23180	Haoyun Jiang, Junqi He, Feng Hong, Xinlong Yang, Jianwei Zhang, Zhengyang Zhuge, Zheng Li, Xiaofeng Cao, Zhiyong Chen, Bo Han, Junyang Lin, Jiangchao Yao	arXiv:2601.23180v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Inference efficiency in Large Language Models (LLMs) is fundamentally limited by their serial, autoregressive generation, especially as reasoning becomes a key capability and response sequences grow longer. Speculative decoding (SD) offers a powerful solution, providing significant speed-ups through its lightweight drafting and parallel verification mechanism. While existing work has nearly saturated improvements in draft effectiveness and efficiency, this paper advances SD from a new yet critical perspective: the verification cost. We propose TriSpec, a novel ternary SD framework that, at its core, introduces a lightweight proxy to significantly reduce computational cost by approving easily verifiable draft sequences and engaging the full target model only when encountering uncertain tokens. TriSpec can be integrated with state-of-the-art SD methods like EAGLE-3 to further reduce verification costs, achieving greater acceleration. Extensive experiments on the Qwen3 and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen/LLaMA families show that TriSpec achieves up to 35% speedup over standard SD, with up to 50% fewer target model invocations while maintaining comparable accuracy.	arXiv:2601.23180v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Inference efficiency in Large Language Models (LLMs) is fundamentally limited by their serial, autoregressive generation, especially as reasoning becomes a key capability and response sequences grow longer. Speculative decoding (SD) offers a powerful solution, providing significant speed-ups through its lightweight drafting and parallel verification mechanism. While existing work has nearly saturated improvements in draft effectiveness and efficiency, this paper advances SD from a new yet critical perspective: the verification cost. We propose TriSpec, a novel ternary SD framework that, at its core, introduces a lightweight proxy to significantly reduce computational cost by approving easily verifiable draft sequences and engaging the full target model only when encountering uncertain tokens. TriSpec can be integrated with state-of-the-art SD methods like EAGLE-3 to further reduce verification costs, achieving greater acceleration. Extensive experiments on the Qwen3 and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen/LLaMA families show that TriSpec achieves up to 35% speedup over standard SD, with up to 50% fewer target model invocations while maintaining comparable accuracy.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.906207+00	\N	\N	7ab5893095112c68efba9ee42739a76d5d5f746bb3ade18207bab2fa6de9309b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.9066+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.906601+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4bef6380-eb3b-4ed3-a55c-d80cffb4f62e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.01849v2	Self-Rewarding Sequential Monte Carlo for Masked Diffusion Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01849	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01849	Ziwei Luo, Ziqi Jin, Lei Wang, Lidong Bing, Thomas B. Sch\\"on	arXiv:2602.01849v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This work presents self-rewarding sequential Monte Carlo (SMC), an inference-time scaling algorithm enabling effective sampling of masked diffusion language models (MDLMs). Our algorithm stems from the observation that most existing MDLMs rely on a confidence-based sampling strategy, where only tokens with the highest prediction confidence are preserved at each step. This restricts the generation to a noise-sensitive, greedy decoding paradigm, resulting in an inevitable collapse in the diversity of possible paths. We address this problem by launching multiple interacting diffusion processes in parallel, referred to as particles, for trajectory exploration. Importantly, we introduce the trajectory-level confidence as a self-rewarding signal for assigning particle importance weights. During sampling, particles are iteratively weighted and resampled to systematically steer generation towards globally confident, high-quality samples. Our self-rewarding SMC is verified on various masked diffusion language models and benchmarks, achieving significant improvement without extra training or reward guidance, while effectively converting parallel inference capacity into improved sampling quality. Our code is available at https://github.com/Algolzw/self-rewarding-smc.	arXiv:2602.01849v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This work presents self-rewarding sequential Monte Carlo (SMC), an inference-time scaling algorithm enabling effective sampling of masked diffusion language models (MDLMs). Our algorithm stems from the observation that most existing MDLMs rely on a confidence-based sampling strategy, where only tokens with the highest prediction confidence are preserved at each step. This restricts the generation to a noise-sensitive, greedy decoding paradigm, resulting in an inevitable collapse in the diversity of possible paths. We address this problem by launching multiple interacting diffusion processes in parallel, referred to as particles, for trajectory exploration. Importantly, we introduce the trajectory-level confidence as a self-rewarding signal for assigning particle importance weights. During sampling, particles are iteratively weighted and resampled to systematically steer generation towards globally confident, high-quality samples. Our self-rewarding SMC is verified on various masked diffusion language models and benchmarks, achieving significant improvement without extra training or reward guidance, while effectively converting parallel inference capacity into improved sampling quality. Our code is available at https://github.com/Algolzw/self-rewarding-smc.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.918607+00	\N	\N	cc9aaa65c94bd0fd0076d4c0ba3e911af54aa3f7414fca03d16279dcced002d9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.919003+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.919004+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
586552cd-fe5d-422f-a12a-e410ba69fa3e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.03780v2	Task Vector Geometry Underlies Dual Modes of Task Inference in Transformers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03780	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03780	Hao Yan, Haolin Yang, Yiqiao Zhong	arXiv:2605.03780v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Transformers are effective at inferring the latent task from context via two inference modes: recognizing a task seen during training, and adapting to a novel one. Recent interpretability studies have identified from middle-layer representations task-specific directions, or task vectors, that steer model behavior. However, a lack of rigorous foundations hinders connecting internal representations to external model behavior: existing work fails to explain how task-vector geometry is shaped by the training distribution, and what geometry enables out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization. In this paper, we study these questions in a controlled synthetic setting by training small transformers from scratch on latent-task sequence distributions, which allows a principled mathematical characterization. We show that two inference modes can coexist within a single model. In-distribution behavior is governed by Bayesian task retrieval, implemented internally through convex combinations of learned task vectors. OOD behavior, by contrast, arises through extrapolative task learning, whose representations occupy a subspace nearly orthogonal to the task-vector subspace. Taken together, our results suggest that task-vector geometry, training distributions, and generalization behaviors are closely related.	arXiv:2605.03780v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Transformers are effective at inferring the latent task from context via two inference modes: recognizing a task seen during training, and adapting to a novel one. Recent interpretability studies have identified from middle-layer representations task-specific directions, or task vectors, that steer model behavior. However, a lack of rigorous foundations hinders connecting internal representations to external model behavior: existing work fails to explain how task-vector geometry is shaped by the training distribution, and what geometry enables out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization. In this paper, we study these questions in a controlled synthetic setting by training small transformers from scratch on latent-task sequence distributions, which allows a principled mathematical characterization. We show that two inference modes can coexist within a single model. In-distribution behavior is governed by Bayesian task retrieval, implemented internally through convex combinations of learned task vectors. OOD behavior, by contrast, arises through extrapolative task learning, whose representations occupy a subspace nearly orthogonal to the task-vector subspace. Taken together, our results suggest that task-vector geometry, training distributions, and generalization behaviors are closely related.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.076389+00	\N	\N	540addac9c5015543c6b0e6fcf25a284235e1ab749b89f66000b679384e95b9d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.07671+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.076711+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5877f007-76a1-40ad-a8ad-fac51b4e37b2	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.02634v2	A Reduction from Delayed to Immediate Feedback for Online Convex Optimization with Improved Guarantees	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02634	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02634	Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy	arXiv:2602.02634v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This paper develops a reduction-based framework for online learning with delayed feedback that recovers and improves upon existing results for first-order and bandit convex optimization. We introduce a continuous-time model that enables an observation-centric rather than prediction-centric analysis. Under this model, regret decomposes into a delay-independent learning term and a delay-induced drift term, yielding a delay-adaptive reduction that converts any online linear optimization algorithm into one that handles round-dependent delays. For bandit convex optimization, we significantly improve existing regret bounds, with delay-dependent terms matching optimal first-order rates. For first-order feedback, we recover optimal regret bounds via a simpler, unified analysis.\n  Quantitatively, for bandit convex optimization we obtain $O(\\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}} + T^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\sqrt{k})$ regret, improving the delay-dependent term from $O(\\min\\{\\sqrt{T d_{\\text{max}}},(Td_{\\text{tot}})^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\})$ in prior work to $O(\\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}})$. Here, $k$, $T$, $d_{\\text{max}}$, and $d_{\\text{tot}}$ denote the dimension, time horizon, maximum and total delay, respectively. Under strong convexity, we achieve $O(\\min\\{\\sigma_{\\text{max}} \\ln T, \\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}}\\} + (T^2\\ln T)^{\\frac{1}{3}} {k}^{\\frac{2}{3}})$, improving the delay-dependent term from $O(d_{\\text{max}} \\ln T)$ to $O(\\min\\{\\sigma_{\\text{max}} \\ln T, \\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}}\\})$, where $\\sigma_{\\text{max}}$ denotes the maximum number of outstanding observations and may be considerably smaller than $d_{\\text{max}}$.	arXiv:2602.02634v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This paper develops a reduction-based framework for online learning with delayed feedback that recovers and improves upon existing results for first-order and bandit convex optimization. We introduce a continuous-time model that enables an observation-centric rather than prediction-centric analysis. Under this model, regret decomposes into a delay-independent learning term and a delay-induced drift term, yielding a delay-adaptive reduction that converts any online linear optimization algorithm into one that handles round-dependent delays. For bandit convex optimization, we significantly improve existing regret bounds, with delay-dependent terms matching optimal first-order rates. For first-order feedback, we recover optimal regret bounds via a simpler, unified analysis.\n  Quantitatively, for bandit convex optimization we obtain $O(\\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}} + T^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\sqrt{k})$ regret, improving the delay-dependent term from $O(\\min\\{\\sqrt{T d_{\\text{max}}},(Td_{\\text{tot}})^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\})$ in prior work to $O(\\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}})$. Here, $k$, $T$, $d_{\\text{max}}$, and $d_{\\text{tot}}$ denote the dimension, time horizon, maximum and total delay, respectively. Under strong convexity, we achieve $O(\\min\\{\\sigma_{\\text{max}} \\ln T, \\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}}\\} + (T^2\\ln T)^{\\frac{1}{3}} {k}^{\\frac{2}{3}})$, improving the delay-dependent term from $O(d_{\\text{max}} \\ln T)$ to $O(\\min\\{\\sigma_{\\text{max}} \\ln T, \\sqrt{d_{\\text{tot}}}\\})$, where $\\sigma_{\\text{max}}$ denotes the maximum number of outstanding observations and may be considerably smaller than $d_{\\text{max}}$.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.92124+00	\N	\N	8dd10f5f15722e2410c466be4855d38b7e7333230dc8d49d69929a42fad7084a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.921572+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.921574+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9d90ad06-7b0a-4cbc-a877-2ec6874c6ecb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.03461v2	Soft-Radial Projection for Constrained End-to-End Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03461	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03461	Philipp J. Schneider, Daniel Kuhn	arXiv:2602.03461v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Constrained end-to-end learning trains neural networks whose outputs must satisfy feasibility constraints, such as resource, budget, or operational limits. While projection and optimization layers guarantee constraint satisfaction, boundary-based projections can introduce unfavorable optimization geometry: regions of the unconstrained output space are mapped to the same active face of the feasible set. This makes the resulting map locally rank-deficient, suppressing gradients in constrained directions and potentially impairing optimization - a bottleneck known as gradient saturation. We propose Soft-Radial Projection (SRP), a differentiable reparameterization layer that maps unconstrained network outputs into the relative interior of a convex feasible set by smoothly rescaling rays from a strictly feasible anchor point. Unlike hard radial or orthogonal projections, the resulting map is one-to-one and has a full-rank Jacobian almost everywhere, while guaranteeing strict feasibility. We prove that constrained networks equipped with SRP retain universal approximation guarantees. For polytopes and balls, the layer admits a closed-form forward pass, while for general convex sets, the ray-boundary intersection reduces to a one-dimensional search. Experiments on decision-focused learning benchmarks demonstrate strong solution quality at low computational cost, with particularly favorable results in portfolio optimization and substantially lower inference latency than differentiable optimization layers in the evaluated settings.	arXiv:2602.03461v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Constrained end-to-end learning trains neural networks whose outputs must satisfy feasibility constraints, such as resource, budget, or operational limits. While projection and optimization layers guarantee constraint satisfaction, boundary-based projections can introduce unfavorable optimization geometry: regions of the unconstrained output space are mapped to the same active face of the feasible set. This makes the resulting map locally rank-deficient, suppressing gradients in constrained directions and potentially impairing optimization - a bottleneck known as gradient saturation. We propose Soft-Radial Projection (SRP), a differentiable reparameterization layer that maps unconstrained network outputs into the relative interior of a convex feasible set by smoothly rescaling rays from a strictly feasible anchor point. Unlike hard radial or orthogonal projections, the resulting map is one-to-one and has a full-rank Jacobian almost everywhere, while guaranteeing strict feasibility. We prove that constrained networks equipped with SRP retain universal approximation guarantees. For polytopes and balls, the layer admits a closed-form forward pass, while for general convex sets, the ray-boundary intersection reduces to a one-dimensional search. Experiments on decision-focused learning benchmarks demonstrate strong solution quality at low computational cost, with particularly favorable results in portfolio optimization and substantially lower inference latency than differentiable optimization layers in the evaluated settings.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.923618+00	\N	\N	00bc5c0f20a2d35230a907ad366c6ae747f4d86c3e42fc390d9819373d767e13	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.923975+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.923977+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cb208c38-a691-450c-82b3-fef0471b143a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.09969v3	Causal Multi-Task Demand Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09969	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09969	Varun Gupta, Vijay Kamble	arXiv:2602.09969v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study multi-task demand learning for retail pricing, where firms seek to estimate heterogeneous linear price-response functions across many decision contexts. Each context has rich covariates but limited price variation, motivating transfer across tasks. However, such transfer is complicated by endogeneity: prices may be arbitrarily correlated with unobserved task-level demand determinants.\n  We propose a meta-learning framework that identifies the conditional mean of task-specific causal demand parameters despite this confounding, provided each task contains at least two distinct, locally exogenous price points. The key is to carefully design the information available to the learner. We condition on the full price history to address cross-task confounding, but withhold the demand outcomes associated with two locally exogenous price points to preserve identifiability. These withheld outcomes provide supervision through a randomized query.\n  We establish that this information design is maximally uniformly valid for the squared-loss objective: any refinement of the conditioning set that reveals information about the withheld outcomes or the query index admits a data-generating process under which the objective no longer identifies the conditional mean causal target. Synthetic experiments demonstrate improved estimation of causal demand parameters under strong confounding, while retail experiments show improved held-out demand prediction relative to standard transfer-learning baselines.	arXiv:2602.09969v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study multi-task demand learning for retail pricing, where firms seek to estimate heterogeneous linear price-response functions across many decision contexts. Each context has rich covariates but limited price variation, motivating transfer across tasks. However, such transfer is complicated by endogeneity: prices may be arbitrarily correlated with unobserved task-level demand determinants.\n  We propose a meta-learning framework that identifies the conditional mean of task-specific causal demand parameters despite this confounding, provided each task contains at least two distinct, locally exogenous price points. The key is to carefully design the information available to the learner. We condition on the full price history to address cross-task confounding, but withhold the demand outcomes associated with two locally exogenous price points to preserve identifiability. These withheld outcomes provide supervision through a randomized query.\n  We establish that this information design is maximally uniformly valid for the squared-loss objective: any refinement of the conditioning set that reveals information about the withheld outcomes or the query index admits a data-generating process under which the objective no longer identifies the conditional mean causal target. Synthetic experiments demonstrate improved estimation of causal demand parameters under strong confounding, while retail experiments show improved held-out demand prediction relative to standard transfer-learning baselines.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.959912+00	\N	\N	245a5c822620c604a528a4e95db269175858c6c9555396bf79b60e2d2793fa38	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.960233+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.960235+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
29556c82-9d1e-449d-9902-1cb99128ed19	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.03562v3	NPCNet: Navigator-Driven Pseudo Text for Deep Clustering of Early Sepsis Phenotyping	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03562	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03562	Pi-Ju Tsai, Charkkri Limbud, Kuan-Fu Chen, Yi-Ju Tseng	arXiv:2602.03562v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Sepsis is a heterogeneous syndrome. Identifying clinically distinct phenotypes may enable more precise treatment strategies. In recent years, many researchers have applied clustering algorithms to sepsis patients. However, many existing approaches require aggregating or imputing temporal Electronic Health Records (EHRs), which can limit the model's ability to capture disease progression. Furthermore, existing clustering processes seldom incorporate domain-driven constraints, often resulting in phenotypes that lack clear clinical distinction. We propose a novel clustering network, NPCNet, that comprises a text embedding generator, a clustering operator, and a target navigator. We first transformed EHRs into pseudo texts by discretizing continuous clinical measurements and integrated them with static variables to construct the embeddings. The target navigator then infused clinical knowledge into the embeddings through auxiliary tasks, constraining clustering results to better align sepsis phenotypes with clinical significance. Finally, the clustering operator employed an iterative refinement mechanism to jointly optimize phenotype centroids and patient representations under domain-driven constraints. Extensive experiments on public datasets demonstrated that NPCNet achieved superior performance on both internal clustering and clinical metrics. We identify four distinct sepsis phenotypes with divergent SOFA trajectories. Notably, although {\\alpha} and {\\delta} exhibit similarly severe conditions initially, NPCNet distinguishes patients likely to improve ({\\alpha}) from those at risk of deterioration ({\\delta}). Furthermore, treatment effect analysis suggests that early vasopressor administration may be associated with improved outcomes in the {\\alpha}, \\{beta}, and {\\delta} phenotypes. These findings highlight NPCNet's potential for phenotype-specific treatment strategies.	arXiv:2602.03562v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Sepsis is a heterogeneous syndrome. Identifying clinically distinct phenotypes may enable more precise treatment strategies. In recent years, many researchers have applied clustering algorithms to sepsis patients. However, many existing approaches require aggregating or imputing temporal Electronic Health Records (EHRs), which can limit the model's ability to capture disease progression. Furthermore, existing clustering processes seldom incorporate domain-driven constraints, often resulting in phenotypes that lack clear clinical distinction. We propose a novel clustering network, NPCNet, that comprises a text embedding generator, a clustering operator, and a target navigator. We first transformed EHRs into pseudo texts by discretizing continuous clinical measurements and integrated them with static variables to construct the embeddings. The target navigator then infused clinical knowledge into the embeddings through auxiliary tasks, constraining clustering results to better align sepsis phenotypes with clinical significance. Finally, the clustering operator employed an iterative refinement mechanism to jointly optimize phenotype centroids and patient representations under domain-driven constraints. Extensive experiments on public datasets demonstrated that NPCNet achieved superior performance on both internal clustering and clinical metrics. We identify four distinct sepsis phenotypes with divergent SOFA trajectories. Notably, although {\\alpha} and {\\delta} exhibit similarly severe conditions initially, NPCNet distinguishes patients likely to improve ({\\alpha}) from those at risk of deterioration ({\\delta}). Furthermore, treatment effect analysis suggests that early vasopressor administration may be associated with improved outcomes in the {\\alpha}, \\{beta}, and {\\delta} phenotypes. These findings highlight NPCNet's potential for phenotype-specific treatment strategies.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.926101+00	\N	\N	b28e415948cb08dcb757700aea3f57301d80f1646ff887a4f8c6c7a02f44eed1	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.926489+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.926491+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e21b3b40-b49b-4d64-aee3-6f11ad10bc3a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.04042v3	Partition Tree: Conditional Density Estimation over General Outcome Spaces	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04042	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04042	Felipe Angelim, Alessandro Leite	arXiv:2602.04042v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We propose Partition Tree, a tree-based framework for conditional density estimation over general outcome spaces that supports both continuous and categorical variables within a unified formulation. Our approach models conditional distributions as piecewise-constant densities on data-adaptive partitions and learns trees by greedily minimizing a surrogate for conditional log-loss. This yields a scalable alternative to existing probabilistic trees that does not make parametric assumptions about the target distribution. We further introduce Partition Forest, an ensemble extension obtained by averaging conditional densities. Empirically, we demonstrate improved probabilistic prediction over CART-style trees and competitive performance compared to state-of-the-art probabilistic tree methods and Random Forests.	arXiv:2602.04042v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We propose Partition Tree, a tree-based framework for conditional density estimation over general outcome spaces that supports both continuous and categorical variables within a unified formulation. Our approach models conditional distributions as piecewise-constant densities on data-adaptive partitions and learns trees by greedily minimizing a surrogate for conditional log-loss. This yields a scalable alternative to existing probabilistic trees that does not make parametric assumptions about the target distribution. We further introduce Partition Forest, an ensemble extension obtained by averaging conditional densities. Empirically, we demonstrate improved probabilistic prediction over CART-style trees and competitive performance compared to state-of-the-art probabilistic tree methods and Random Forests.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.928472+00	\N	\N	49a4bdc6dcdf6d90904217f05dd226fbcb798d01017a1bbed5ad70621bbfefac	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.928811+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.928813+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3e661903-7c09-4a40-8bbe-ecb343d5b5b2	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.07732v2	Efficient Adaptive Data Analysis over Dense Distributions	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07732	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07732	Joon Suk Huh	arXiv:2602.07732v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern data workflows are inherently adaptive, repeatedly querying the same dataset to refine and validate sequential decisions, but such adaptivity can lead to overfitting and invalid statistical inference. Adaptive Data Analysis (ADA) mechanisms address this challenge; however, there is a fundamental tension between computational efficiency and sample complexity. For $T$ rounds of adaptive analysis, computationally efficient algorithms typically incur suboptimal $O(\\sqrt{T})$ sample complexity, whereas for arbitrary distributions, algorithms with $O(\\log T)$ sample complexity are computationally intractable under standard cryptographic assumptions. In this work, we shed light on this trade-off by identifying a natural class of data distributions under which both computational efficiency and logarithmic sample complexity in $T$ are achievable. We propose a computationally efficient ADA mechanism that attains $O(\\log T)$ sample complexity, optimal in its dependence on $T$, when the data distribution is dense with respect to a known prior. This setting includes, in particular, feature-label data distributions arising in distribution-specific learning. As a consequence, our mechanism also yields a sample-efficient (i.e., $O(\\log T)$ samples) statistical query oracle in the distribution-specific setting. Moreover, although our algorithm is not based on differential privacy, it satisfies a relaxed privacy notion known as Predicate Singling Out (PSO) security (Cohen and Nissim 2020) whenever the number of its possible update transcripts is polynomial in the sample size. Our results thus reveal an inherent connection between adaptive data analysis and privacy beyond differential privacy.	arXiv:2602.07732v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern data workflows are inherently adaptive, repeatedly querying the same dataset to refine and validate sequential decisions, but such adaptivity can lead to overfitting and invalid statistical inference. Adaptive Data Analysis (ADA) mechanisms address this challenge; however, there is a fundamental tension between computational efficiency and sample complexity. For $T$ rounds of adaptive analysis, computationally efficient algorithms typically incur suboptimal $O(\\sqrt{T})$ sample complexity, whereas for arbitrary distributions, algorithms with $O(\\log T)$ sample complexity are computationally intractable under standard cryptographic assumptions. In this work, we shed light on this trade-off by identifying a natural class of data distributions under which both computational efficiency and logarithmic sample complexity in $T$ are achievable. We propose a computationally efficient ADA mechanism that attains $O(\\log T)$ sample complexity, optimal in its dependence on $T$, when the data distribution is dense with respect to a known prior. This setting includes, in particular, feature-label data distributions arising in distribution-specific learning. As a consequence, our mechanism also yields a sample-efficient (i.e., $O(\\log T)$ samples) statistical query oracle in the distribution-specific setting. Moreover, although our algorithm is not based on differential privacy, it satisfies a relaxed privacy notion known as Predicate Singling Out (PSO) security (Cohen and Nissim 2020) whenever the number of its possible update transcripts is polynomial in the sample size. Our results thus reveal an inherent connection between adaptive data analysis and privacy beyond differential privacy.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.947938+00	\N	\N	f52055049b75d1f9c92e34a8e8a5b66a0b0fd1977b8a390d15e918b9cd1aa596	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.948337+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.948339+00	pending	\N	\N	pend