c90bc6a7-c7f8-4f55-a0d2-0e02b3e81a5b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.26994v2	ImmSET: Sequence-Based Predictor of TCR-pMHC Specificity at Scale	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.26994	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.26994	Marco Garcia Noceda, Matthew T Noakes, Andrew FigPope, Daniel E Mattox, Bryan Howie, Harlan Robins	arXiv:2603.26994v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: T cells are a critical component of the adaptive immune system, playing a role in infectious disease, autoimmunity, and cancer. T cell function is mediated by the T cell receptor (TCR) protein, a highly diverse receptor targeting specific peptides presented by the major histocompatibility complex (pMHCs). Predicting the specificity of TCRs for their cognate pMHCs is central to understanding adaptive immunity and enabling personalized therapies. However, accurate prediction of this protein-protein interaction remains challenging due to the extreme diversity of both TCRs and pMHCs. Here, we present ImmSET (Immune Synapse Encoding Transformer), a novel sequence-based architecture designed to model interactions among sets of variable-length biological sequences. We train this model across a range of dataset sizes and compositions and study the resulting models' generalization to pMHC targets. We describe a failure mode in prior sequence-based approaches that inflates previously reported performance on this task and show that ImmSET remains robust under stricter evaluation. In systematically testing the scaling behavior of ImmSET with training data, we show that performance scales consistently with data volume across multiple data types and compares favorably with the pre-trained protein language model ESM2 fine-tuned on the same datasets. Finally, we demonstrate that ImmSET can outperform AlphaFold2 and AlphaFold3-based pipelines on TCR-pMHC specificity prediction when provided sufficient training data. This work establishes ImmSET as a scalable modeling paradigm for multi-sequence interaction problems, demonstrated in the TCR-pMHC setting but generalizable to other biological domains where high-throughput sequence-driven reasoning complements structure prediction and experimental mapping.	arXiv:2603.26994v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: T cells are a critical component of the adaptive immune system, playing a role in infectious disease, autoimmunity, and cancer. T cell function is mediated by the T cell receptor (TCR) protein, a highly diverse receptor targeting specific peptides presented by the major histocompatibility complex (pMHCs). Predicting the specificity of TCRs for their cognate pMHCs is central to understanding adaptive immunity and enabling personalized therapies. However, accurate prediction of this protein-protein interaction remains challenging due to the extreme diversity of both TCRs and pMHCs. Here, we present ImmSET (Immune Synapse Encoding Transformer), a novel sequence-based architecture designed to model interactions among sets of variable-length biological sequences. We train this model across a range of dataset sizes and compositions and study the resulting models' generalization to pMHC targets. We describe a failure mode in prior sequence-based approaches that inflates previously reported performance on this task and show that ImmSET remains robust under stricter evaluation. In systematically testing the scaling behavior of ImmSET with training data, we show that performance scales consistently with data volume across multiple data types and compares favorably with the pre-trained protein language model ESM2 fine-tuned on the same datasets. Finally, we demonstrate that ImmSET can outperform AlphaFold2 and AlphaFold3-based pipelines on TCR-pMHC specificity prediction when provided sufficient training data. This work establishes ImmSET as a scalable modeling paradigm for multi-sequence interaction problems, demonstrated in the TCR-pMHC setting but generalizable to other biological domains where high-throughput sequence-driven reasoning complements structure prediction and experimental mapping.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.021009+00	\N	\N	690d015d2165cc42d2303b1e713983ec149c2fccbab09aaf0294149fecdafe4d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.021326+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.021328+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
04bedc4a-7672-407d-bafb-aefde5a789f5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2603.29264v3	Linear Generator Networks for Nonlinear Partial Differential Equations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29264	https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29264	Shafayeth Jamil, Rehan Kapadia	arXiv:2603.29264v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Most physical systems are governed by nonlinear partial differential equations, yet the analytical tools that make linear systems transparent, such as eigenspectra, dispersion relations and modal decomposition, remain unavailable in nonlinear settings. We introduce Linear Generator Network-Koopman (LGN-KM), a neural operator that learns a nonlinear lift and a continuous-time linear generator for the lifted dynamics through the decomposition $L_k = S - D_k$, where $S$ is skew-symmetric (conservative coupling) and $D_k$ is positive-definite diagonal (mode-selective dissipation). The decomposition guarantees stability by construction and exposes the eigenstructure directly, making linear-systems analysis available on a nonlinear PDE. On 2D Navier-Stokes turbulence, the generator yields a complete multi-branch dispersion relation and models trained independently at viscosities $100\\times$ apart learn the same coupling spectrum ($R^2 = 0.87$). The same architecture shows the corresponding diffusive damping on FitzHugh-Nagumo reaction-diffusion, and for pulse propagation in optical fibers, the oscillation frequencies of the learned generator match the fiber's dispersion relation, giving the group-velocity dispersion to within $3.1\\%$ with no fitted constant. The architecture additionally enables long-horizon stability, $O(1)$ continuous-time evaluation, and physics-informed cross-regime model transfer.	arXiv:2603.29264v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Most physical systems are governed by nonlinear partial differential equations, yet the analytical tools that make linear systems transparent, such as eigenspectra, dispersion relations and modal decomposition, remain unavailable in nonlinear settings. We introduce Linear Generator Network-Koopman (LGN-KM), a neural operator that learns a nonlinear lift and a continuous-time linear generator for the lifted dynamics through the decomposition $L_k = S - D_k$, where $S$ is skew-symmetric (conservative coupling) and $D_k$ is positive-definite diagonal (mode-selective dissipation). The decomposition guarantees stability by construction and exposes the eigenstructure directly, making linear-systems analysis available on a nonlinear PDE. On 2D Navier-Stokes turbulence, the generator yields a complete multi-branch dispersion relation and models trained independently at viscosities $100\\times$ apart learn the same coupling spectrum ($R^2 = 0.87$). The same architecture shows the corresponding diffusive damping on FitzHugh-Nagumo reaction-diffusion, and for pulse propagation in optical fibers, the oscillation frequencies of the learned generator match the fiber's dispersion relation, giving the group-velocity dispersion to within $3.1\\%$ with no fitted constant. The architecture additionally enables long-horizon stability, $O(1)$ continuous-time evaluation, and physics-informed cross-regime model transfer.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.025381+00	\N	\N	0d72088771902e4bee4bc475dd88b27a3ba17a69e3a4d35240e6ed86632a341e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.025693+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.025694+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ac0b4242-30eb-4109-a4d1-17a4227fb16a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.01342v3	Massively Parallel Exact Inference for Hawkes Processes	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01342	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01342	Ahmer Raza, Hudson Smith	arXiv:2604.01342v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Multivariate Hawkes processes are a widely used class of self-exciting point processes, but maximum likelihood estimation naively scales as $O(N^2)$ in the number of events. The canonical linear exponential Hawkes process admits a faster $O(N)$ recurrence, but prior work evaluates this recurrence sequentially, without exploiting parallelization on modern GPUs. We show that the Hawkes process intensity can be expressed as a product of sparse transition matrices admitting a linear-time associative multiply, enabling computation via a parallel prefix scan. This yields a massively parallelizable algorithm for estimation of linear exponential Hawkes processes. Our method reduces the computational complexity to approximately $O(N/P)$ with $P$ parallel processors, and naturally yields a batching scheme to maintain constant memory usage, avoiding GPU memory constraints. Importantly, it computes the exact likelihood without any additional assumptions or approximations, preserving the simplicity and interpretability of the model. We demonstrate orders-of-magnitude speedups on simulated and real datasets, scaling to thousands of nodes and tens of millions of events, substantially beyond scales reported in prior work. We provide an open-source PyTorch library implementing our optimizations.	arXiv:2604.01342v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Multivariate Hawkes processes are a widely used class of self-exciting point processes, but maximum likelihood estimation naively scales as $O(N^2)$ in the number of events. The canonical linear exponential Hawkes process admits a faster $O(N)$ recurrence, but prior work evaluates this recurrence sequentially, without exploiting parallelization on modern GPUs. We show that the Hawkes process intensity can be expressed as a product of sparse transition matrices admitting a linear-time associative multiply, enabling computation via a parallel prefix scan. This yields a massively parallelizable algorithm for estimation of linear exponential Hawkes processes. Our method reduces the computational complexity to approximately $O(N/P)$ with $P$ parallel processors, and naturally yields a batching scheme to maintain constant memory usage, avoiding GPU memory constraints. Importantly, it computes the exact likelihood without any additional assumptions or approximations, preserving the simplicity and interpretability of the model. We demonstrate orders-of-magnitude speedups on simulated and real datasets, scaling to thousands of nodes and tens of millions of events, substantially beyond scales reported in prior work. We provide an open-source PyTorch library implementing our optimizations.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.034764+00	\N	\N	7b5b08ce49839c72152e9fc16026331a9205c3854c7893ccf9cc87e6aa0bf0ea	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.03508+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.035082+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5cef26fc-be6c-42fb-ab99-ba5db51c5ff3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.02577v2	ROMAN: A Multiscale Routing Operator for Convolutional Time Series Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02577	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02577	Gonzalo Uribarri	arXiv:2604.02577v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce ROMAN (ROuting Multiscale representAtioN), a deterministic operator for time series that maps temporal scale and coarse temporal position into an explicit channel structure while reducing sequence length. ROMAN builds an anti-aliased multiscale pyramid, extracts fixed-length windows from each scale, and stacks them as pseudochannels. ROMAN provides a training-free mechanism to control the inductive bias of downstream models: it can reduce temporal invariance, make temporal pooling implicitly coarse-position-aware, and expose multiscale interactions through channel mixing, while often improving computational efficiency by shortening the processed time axis. We formally analyze the operator and evaluate it as preprocessing for four convolutional classifiers: MiniRocket, MultiRocket, a standard CNN-based classifier, and a fully convolutional network (FCN) classifier. Synthetic mechanism studies that isolate coarse position, long-range correlation, multiscale interaction, and full positional invariance show that ROMAN behaves as intended, and comparisons with coordinate augmentation and single-scale routing distinguish the benefits of positional routing from those of the multiscale cascade across the evaluated backbones. On long-sequence subsets of the UCR and UEA archives, ROMAN's effect on accuracy varies across datasets while its effect on efficiency is often favorable. At \\(S=4\\) on UCR, accuracy changes are positively correlated across the pooled backbones, consistent with the proposed role of task structure. We give practical guidance for choosing the operator's two hyperparameters and illustrate its use on very long sequences, where it is both more accurate and several times cheaper, and as a source of representation diversity for ensembles. The ROMAN operator library is available at \\href{https://github.com/gon-uri/ROMAN}{github.com/gon-uri/ROMAN	arXiv:2604.02577v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce ROMAN (ROuting Multiscale representAtioN), a deterministic operator for time series that maps temporal scale and coarse temporal position into an explicit channel structure while reducing sequence length. ROMAN builds an anti-aliased multiscale pyramid, extracts fixed-length windows from each scale, and stacks them as pseudochannels. ROMAN provides a training-free mechanism to control the inductive bias of downstream models: it can reduce temporal invariance, make temporal pooling implicitly coarse-position-aware, and expose multiscale interactions through channel mixing, while often improving computational efficiency by shortening the processed time axis. We formally analyze the operator and evaluate it as preprocessing for four convolutional classifiers: MiniRocket, MultiRocket, a standard CNN-based classifier, and a fully convolutional network (FCN) classifier. Synthetic mechanism studies that isolate coarse position, long-range correlation, multiscale interaction, and full positional invariance show that ROMAN behaves as intended, and comparisons with coordinate augmentation and single-scale routing distinguish the benefits of positional routing from those of the multiscale cascade across the evaluated backbones. On long-sequence subsets of the UCR and UEA archives, ROMAN's effect on accuracy varies across datasets while its effect on efficiency is often favorable. At \\(S=4\\) on UCR, accuracy changes are positively correlated across the pooled backbones, consistent with the proposed role of task structure. We give practical guidance for choosing the operator's two hyperparameters and illustrate its use on very long sequences, where it is both more accurate and several times cheaper, and as a source of representation diversity for ensembles. The ROMAN operator library is available at \\href{https://github.com/gon-uri/ROMAN}{github.com/gon-uri/ROMAN	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.037074+00	\N	\N	943e6aad533c7570f837a08a7705651cea42eeee6a2e2b6c557b54cb7e19f97f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.037451+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.037453+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4ec90a11-f061-489e-9148-905100238769	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.07962v2	We Should Distinguish Unlearning From Untraining	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07962	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07962	Eleni Triantafillou, Ahmed Imtiaz Humayun, Monica Ribero, Alexander Matt Turner, Michael C. Mozer, Georgios Kaissis	arXiv:2604.07962v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: There has been a recent surge in interest about the question of how we can ``delete'' specific data points or behaviours from a trained model, a goal referred to as ``machine unlearning''. We argue that the umbrella term ``unlearning'' actually spans two distinct problem formulations, but the distinction between them has not yet been observed in literature. This causes ambiguity around when an unlearning algorithm is expected to work, leads to the use of inappropriate metrics and baselines when comparing algorithms to one another, difficulty in interpreting results, and missed opportunities for pursuing critical research directions. In this paper, we argue for the position that a fundamental distinction must be made between two notions that we refer to as unlearning and untraining. On one hand, untraining aims to reverse the effect of having trained on a given ``forget set'', i.e. to remove the influence that that specific set of examples had on the model during training. On the other hand, unlearning aims not just to remove the influence of those given examples, but also of the entire underlying subdistribution from which they were sampled (and thus e.g. the concept or model behaviour that those examples represent). We discuss technical definitions of these problems and map problem settings studied in the literature to each notion. By disambiguating technical definitions, our work aims to accelerate progress in this important field.	arXiv:2604.07962v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: There has been a recent surge in interest about the question of how we can ``delete'' specific data points or behaviours from a trained model, a goal referred to as ``machine unlearning''. We argue that the umbrella term ``unlearning'' actually spans two distinct problem formulations, but the distinction between them has not yet been observed in literature. This causes ambiguity around when an unlearning algorithm is expected to work, leads to the use of inappropriate metrics and baselines when comparing algorithms to one another, difficulty in interpreting results, and missed opportunities for pursuing critical research directions. In this paper, we argue for the position that a fundamental distinction must be made between two notions that we refer to as unlearning and untraining. On one hand, untraining aims to reverse the effect of having trained on a given ``forget set'', i.e. to remove the influence that that specific set of examples had on the model during training. On the other hand, unlearning aims not just to remove the influence of those given examples, but also of the entire underlying subdistribution from which they were sampled (and thus e.g. the concept or model behaviour that those examples represent). We discuss technical definitions of these problems and map problem settings studied in the literature to each notion. By disambiguating technical definitions, our work aims to accelerate progress in this important field.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.046233+00	\N	\N	d3b086559461b99e43cc47c411204571c505e8445ac18d02ccb09812c517e177	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.046607+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.046608+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
706c42dd-37d5-46f5-a265-4e5b6fbd2ae9	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.10009v2	Towards Multi-Source Domain Generalization for Sleep Staging with Noisy Labels	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.10009	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.10009	Kening Wang, Di Wen, Yufan Chen, Ruiping Liu, Junwei Zheng, Jiale Wei, Kailun Yang, Rainer Stiefelhagen, Kunyu Peng	arXiv:2604.10009v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Automatic sleep staging is a multimodal learning problem involving heterogeneous physiological signals such as EEG and EOG, which often suffer from domain shifts across institutions, devices, and populations. In practice, these data are also affected by noisy annotations, yet label-noise-robust multi-source domain generalization remains underexplored. We present the first benchmark for Noisy Labels in Multi-Source Domain-Generalized Sleep Staging (NL-DGSS) and show that existing noisy-label learning methods degrade substantially when domain shifts and label noise coexist. To address this challenge, we propose FF-TRUST, a domain-invariant multimodal sleep staging framework with Joint Time-Frequency Early Learning Regularization (JTF-ELR). By jointly exploiting temporal and spectral consistency together with confidence-diversity regularization, FF-TRUST improves robustness under noisy supervision. Experiments on five public datasets demonstrate consistent state-of-the-art performance under diverse symmetric and asymmetric noise settings.	arXiv:2604.10009v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Automatic sleep staging is a multimodal learning problem involving heterogeneous physiological signals such as EEG and EOG, which often suffer from domain shifts across institutions, devices, and populations. In practice, these data are also affected by noisy annotations, yet label-noise-robust multi-source domain generalization remains underexplored. We present the first benchmark for Noisy Labels in Multi-Source Domain-Generalized Sleep Staging (NL-DGSS) and show that existing noisy-label learning methods degrade substantially when domain shifts and label noise coexist. To address this challenge, we propose FF-TRUST, a domain-invariant multimodal sleep staging framework with Joint Time-Frequency Early Learning Regularization (JTF-ELR). By jointly exploiting temporal and spectral consistency together with confidence-diversity regularization, FF-TRUST improves robustness under noisy supervision. Experiments on five public datasets demonstrate consistent state-of-the-art performance under diverse symmetric and asymmetric noise settings.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.049074+00	\N	\N	8ad7e5354b9051434a6909e2220a3b9edacb7a85a997a8d1a6d30f1621dc91ee	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.049507+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.049524+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1e3af6a5-2c9f-4da8-a8f4-1177b6cbe081	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11927v1	Language processing in the autistic brain: A meta-analysis of neuroimaging studies across modalities and age	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11927	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11927	Hanlin Wu, Bei Xiao, Qingxi He	arXiv:2611.11927v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Language is central to social communication, which is altered in autism, and language abilities vary widely among autistic individuals. How the autistic brain processes language is central to understanding autistic communication. Yet neuroimaging studies report inconsistent findings on how autistic brain activity is altered during language processing. We synthesized 45 fMRI and PET studies (753 autistic and 777 non-autistic participants) with seed-based d mapping with permutation of subject images (SDM-PSI). We estimated autistic and non-autistic activation and their difference with meta-regressions adjusted for each study's language modality and mean age. Autistic and non-autistic brains activated a similar left-lateralized language network. Autistic brains showed reduced activity throughout the left perisylvian cortex, from the inferior frontal gyrus to the mid superior temporal gyrus and the posterior superior temporal sulcus, and increased activity in the right temporal pole. Spoken language showed a larger autistic reduction than written language in the left mid superior temporal gyrus. Adults, but not children and adolescents, showed increased autistic activity in the right superior temporal gyrus and cuneus. Autistic language processing thus relies on the typical left-lateralized network but shows reduced leftward lateralization.	arXiv:2611.11927v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Language is central to social communication, which is altered in autism, and language abilities vary widely among autistic individuals. How the autistic brain processes language is central to understanding autistic communication. Yet neuroimaging studies report inconsistent findings on how autistic brain activity is altered during language processing. We synthesized 45 fMRI and PET studies (753 autistic and 777 non-autistic participants) with seed-based d mapping with permutation of subject images (SDM-PSI). We estimated autistic and non-autistic activation and their difference with meta-regressions adjusted for each study's language modality and mean age. Autistic and non-autistic brains activated a similar left-lateralized language network. Autistic brains showed reduced activity throughout the left perisylvian cortex, from the inferior frontal gyrus to the mid superior temporal gyrus and the posterior superior temporal sulcus, and increased activity in the right temporal pole. Spoken language showed a larger autistic reduction than written language in the left mid superior temporal gyrus. Adults, but not children and adolescents, showed increased autistic activity in the right superior temporal gyrus and cuneus. Autistic language processing thus relies on the typical left-lateralized network but shows reduced leftward lateralization.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.071287+00	\N	\N	f3f304805e617190631cadc42a575aa76af6b43cc4624aa60337ef0b7ebe1913	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.071567+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.071569+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
56fc22ab-79cd-4d79-bbfb-851d42f9e1b9	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.09122v1	Improving Large Audio-Language Model Embedding Baselines for Instruction-Aware Speech Retrieval	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09122	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09122	Chen-An Li, Cheng-Han Chiang, Hung-yi Lee	arXiv:2611.09122v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Speech retrieval finds relevant spoken documents given a spoken query. The spoken query alone may not reveal the retrieval goal, such as finding an answer or matching speaker identity. Instruction-aware speech retrieval expresses this goal through a natural-language text instruction. Large audio-language models (LALMs) effectively process speech and text, and our analysis shows that different retrieval requirements favor hidden states from different audio-encoder layers. We introduce IRIS-Embed, which adapts LALMs into embedding models through contrastive training and instruction-aware audio layer weighting. We construct about 900K training samples from real data and synthetic data, with instructions expressing requirements for one or more speech attributes. On INSPIRE, an instruction-aware speech retrieval benchmark, IRIS-Embed consistently outperforms the corresponding LALM baselines. The best model achieves 62.92% macro-average Recall@50, surpassing the strongest baseline at 45.15%. Controlled comparisons demonstrate instruction awareness and the benefits of audio layer weighting. On MAEB, a massive audio embedding benchmark, macro-average gains of 9.4-19.8 points over the corresponding baselines show generalization beyond speech retrieval.	arXiv:2611.09122v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Speech retrieval finds relevant spoken documents given a spoken query. The spoken query alone may not reveal the retrieval goal, such as finding an answer or matching speaker identity. Instruction-aware speech retrieval expresses this goal through a natural-language text instruction. Large audio-language models (LALMs) effectively process speech and text, and our analysis shows that different retrieval requirements favor hidden states from different audio-encoder layers. We introduce IRIS-Embed, which adapts LALMs into embedding models through contrastive training and instruction-aware audio layer weighting. We construct about 900K training samples from real data and synthetic data, with instructions expressing requirements for one or more speech attributes. On INSPIRE, an instruction-aware speech retrieval benchmark, IRIS-Embed consistently outperforms the corresponding LALM baselines. The best model achieves 62.92% macro-average Recall@50, surpassing the strongest baseline at 45.15%. Controlled comparisons demonstrate instruction awareness and the benefits of audio layer weighting. On MAEB, a massive audio embedding benchmark, macro-average gains of 9.4-19.8 points over the corresponding baselines show generalization beyond speech retrieval.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.948705+00	\N	\N	6a6f1e3b1cdbcf8cc0ddaf86fec310da3cb90724d523d705f3c4de2f487ac5ba	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.949132+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.949133+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
318be62d-9762-4e19-9e4b-eb82daa3f41e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.01729v4	Stable GFlowNets with TV Monitoring and Probabilistic Guarantees	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.01729	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.01729	Zengxiang Lei, Ananth Shreekumar, Jonathan Rosenthal, Ruoyu Song, Alvaro A. Cardenas, Daniel J. Fremont, Dongyan Xu, Satish Ukkusuri, Z. Berkay Celik	arXiv:2605.01729v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Generative Flow Networks (GFlowNets) sample diverse structured objects in proportion to reward and have been applied to molecular discovery and biological-sequence design, where finding multiple high-quality candidates is more useful than returning a single optimum. Despite their theoretical promise, practical training is often unstable, exhibiting severe loss spikes and mode collapse. To address this, we first assess the sensitivity of GFlowNet objectives, demonstrating that a small Total Variation (TV) distance between the learned and target distributions does not preclude an unbounded training loss. Motivated by this mismatch, we establish converse guarantees by deriving loss-to-TV bounds that certify global fidelity from bounded trajectory balance losses. Lastly, we propose Stable GFlowNets, which leverages our theory to stabilize training via adaptive reference flow and improves the trade-off among mode coverage, robustness, and certifiability. Code is available at: https://github.com/tjleizeng/StableGFlowNets.	arXiv:2605.01729v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Generative Flow Networks (GFlowNets) sample diverse structured objects in proportion to reward and have been applied to molecular discovery and biological-sequence design, where finding multiple high-quality candidates is more useful than returning a single optimum. Despite their theoretical promise, practical training is often unstable, exhibiting severe loss spikes and mode collapse. To address this, we first assess the sensitivity of GFlowNet objectives, demonstrating that a small Total Variation (TV) distance between the learned and target distributions does not preclude an unbounded training loss. Motivated by this mismatch, we establish converse guarantees by deriving loss-to-TV bounds that certify global fidelity from bounded trajectory balance losses. Lastly, we propose Stable GFlowNets, which leverages our theory to stabilize training via adaptive reference flow and improves the trade-off among mode coverage, robustness, and certifiability. Code is available at: https://github.com/tjleizeng/StableGFlowNets.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.073803+00	\N	\N	59f50283f5369ea3c875c7e0101c2b5c3d364245069a2a68ab50352a5b26d35a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.074145+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.074146+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
016a9db9-cafe-44e2-a744-ca8afaa433e8	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.05851v2	Language models lack systematicity in generating and updating hypotheses	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.05851	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.05851	Hua-Dong Xiong	arXiv:2605.05851v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to complex problems, from automated code debugging to science itself. Such problems require generating plausible hypotheses from sparse, noisy, and ambiguous data and updating them as more information arrives. Yet how closely LLMs approach optimal inductive reasoning remains unclear. We study this question in the classic number game, in which a learner infers a concept from a few ambiguous positive integers: $\\{2, 16, 4, 8\\}$, for example, is explained both by ``powers of 2'' and by ``numbers up to 20.'' We probe the concepts LLMs hypothesize from the observed examples and use their behavioral patterns to understand how they generate and update hypotheses. We measure the LLM posterior over hypotheses in three tasks (prediction, evaluation, and generation) and compare LLM behavior with a Bayesian model and with humans. LLMs favor narrower hypotheses than humans and show a robust evaluation--generation gap: in evaluation they select hypotheses more consistent with the observed examples, whereas in generation they propose narrower hypotheses that are more often rules. They also update poorly: as examples accumulate, their accuracy declines while their confidence grows. Thinking reduces, and can even reverse, the gap; it also broadens the hypotheses LLMs favor, raising accuracy while lowering confidence. Whereas prior studies have shown that LLMs are good hypothesis proposers, our results and further comparisons with the Bayesian model show that LLM inductive reasoning lacks coherence across the three tasks, suggesting that LLMs do not maintain a stable representation shared across tasks that make the same demand. Our results highlight a limitation of LLMs in inductive inference, particularly in settings such as science, where hypotheses must go beyond the data.	arXiv:2605.05851v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to complex problems, from automated code debugging to science itself. Such problems require generating plausible hypotheses from sparse, noisy, and ambiguous data and updating them as more information arrives. Yet how closely LLMs approach optimal inductive reasoning remains unclear. We study this question in the classic number game, in which a learner infers a concept from a few ambiguous positive integers: $\\{2, 16, 4, 8\\}$, for example, is explained both by ``powers of 2'' and by ``numbers up to 20.'' We probe the concepts LLMs hypothesize from the observed examples and use their behavioral patterns to understand how they generate and update hypotheses. We measure the LLM posterior over hypotheses in three tasks (prediction, evaluation, and generation) and compare LLM behavior with a Bayesian model and with humans. LLMs favor narrower hypotheses than humans and show a robust evaluation--generation gap: in evaluation they select hypotheses more consistent with the observed examples, whereas in generation they propose narrower hypotheses that are more often rules. They also update poorly: as examples accumulate, their accuracy declines while their confidence grows. Thinking reduces, and can even reverse, the gap; it also broadens the hypotheses LLMs favor, raising accuracy while lowering confidence. Whereas prior studies have shown that LLMs are good hypothesis proposers, our results and further comparisons with the Bayesian model show that LLM inductive reasoning lacks coherence across the three tasks, suggesting that LLMs do not maintain a stable representation shared across tasks that make the same demand. Our results highlight a limitation of LLMs in inductive inference, particularly in settings such as science, where hypotheses must go beyond the data.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.083484+00	\N	\N	2570c06a78ade3d2bd1f7ed78f2f4cfd5288c4b9001291a96e311caaa8a41dca	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.083898+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.0839+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
eaf79e5a-7dcc-46d6-ad14-2bba0f157161	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.06081v2	Fast Gauss-Newton for Multiclass Cross-Entropy	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06081	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06081	Mikalai Korbit, Mario Zanon	arXiv:2605.06081v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: In multiclass softmax cross-entropy, the full generalized Gauss-Newton (GGN) curvature couples all output logits through the softmax covariance, making curvature-vector products harder to scale as the number of classes grows. We show that the standard multiclass GGN can be decomposed exactly into a true-vs-rest term and a positive semidefinite within-competitor covariance term. Fast Gauss-Newton (FGN) retains the first term and drops the second, yielding a positive semidefinite under-approximation of the multiclass GGN that is exact for binary classification. The derivation uses an exact true-vs-rest scalar-margin representation of softmax cross-entropy: the loss and gradient are unchanged, and the approximation enters only at the curvature level. Exploiting the FGN curvature structure, the damped update can be written as an equivalent whitened row-space system with one row per mini-batch example. We solve this system matrix-free by conjugate gradient using Jacobian-vector and vector-Jacobian products of the scalar margin map. Targeted mechanism experiments show how the dropped covariance grows with competitor dispersion and how damping can reduce its effect on the resulting steps. In frozen-feature head optimization and end-to-end CIFAR-100 training, FGN achieves similar test accuracy to a full GGN baseline with faster updates.	arXiv:2605.06081v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: In multiclass softmax cross-entropy, the full generalized Gauss-Newton (GGN) curvature couples all output logits through the softmax covariance, making curvature-vector products harder to scale as the number of classes grows. We show that the standard multiclass GGN can be decomposed exactly into a true-vs-rest term and a positive semidefinite within-competitor covariance term. Fast Gauss-Newton (FGN) retains the first term and drops the second, yielding a positive semidefinite under-approximation of the multiclass GGN that is exact for binary classification. The derivation uses an exact true-vs-rest scalar-margin representation of softmax cross-entropy: the loss and gradient are unchanged, and the approximation enters only at the curvature level. Exploiting the FGN curvature structure, the damped update can be written as an equivalent whitened row-space system with one row per mini-batch example. We solve this system matrix-free by conjugate gradient using Jacobian-vector and vector-Jacobian products of the scalar margin map. Targeted mechanism experiments show how the dropped covariance grows with competitor dispersion and how damping can reduce its effect on the resulting steps. In frozen-feature head optimization and end-to-end CIFAR-100 training, FGN achieves similar test accuracy to a full GGN baseline with faster updates.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.085891+00	\N	\N	79e2b0cb47e5fd3ed929e83b2df0c269d594b76c83019e7513453d2e9479d271	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.086207+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.086208+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cd254514-6fdb-411a-93d9-858ef86d8ecb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.06879v2	Better Protein Function Prediction by Modeling Survivorship Bias	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06879	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06879	Zhongmou Chao, Poompol Buathong, Ekaterina Selivanovitch, Susan Daniel, Peter I. Frazier	arXiv:2605.06879v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Predicting whether a protein sequence possesses a biochemical property of interest is important for understanding and designing protein functionality. Modern protein sequence databases provide valuable training data for functional prediction, but they exhibit survivorship bias: natural selection favors variants that support organismal survival while eliminating many others before they can be observed, complicating classifier training. This setting motivates positive-unlabeled (PU) learning, which treats observed functional sequences as positive and unobserved sequences as unlabeled rather than nonfunctional. However, existing protein PU methods do not account for cross-sequence heterogeneity in evolutionary accessibility: sequences one nucleotide mutation away from a common variant arise far more readily than distant ones. We introduce PULSE: PU Learning with Surveillance and Evolution, a framework that trains a probabilistic classifier for a target protein function using an evolution-aware observation likelihood. When considering functionality of proteins with high genomic sequence coverage, PULSE combines a probabilistic classifier that estimates whether an amino acid sequence performs the target function with an evolutionary model that estimates the likelihood that its encoding nucleotide sequences emerge and are detected based on mutational accessibility, parental prevalence, and surveillance e!ciency. By coupling these two components, PULSE treats the absence of an evolutionarily accessible sequence as stronger evidence of non-functionality than the absence of one unlikely to arise. Tested on eleven tasks focusing on multiple Spike protein properties spanning five SARS-CoV-2 variants, PULSE ranks first or second on all of them, and achieves the best overall performance among all methods, including standard PU learning, one-class classification methods, and protein language models.	arXiv:2605.06879v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Predicting whether a protein sequence possesses a biochemical property of interest is important for understanding and designing protein functionality. Modern protein sequence databases provide valuable training data for functional prediction, but they exhibit survivorship bias: natural selection favors variants that support organismal survival while eliminating many others before they can be observed, complicating classifier training. This setting motivates positive-unlabeled (PU) learning, which treats observed functional sequences as positive and unobserved sequences as unlabeled rather than nonfunctional. However, existing protein PU methods do not account for cross-sequence heterogeneity in evolutionary accessibility: sequences one nucleotide mutation away from a common variant arise far more readily than distant ones. We introduce PULSE: PU Learning with Surveillance and Evolution, a framework that trains a probabilistic classifier for a target protein function using an evolution-aware observation likelihood. When considering functionality of proteins with high genomic sequence coverage, PULSE combines a probabilistic classifier that estimates whether an amino acid sequence performs the target function with an evolutionary model that estimates the likelihood that its encoding nucleotide sequences emerge and are detected based on mutational accessibility, parental prevalence, and surveillance e!ciency. By coupling these two components, PULSE treats the absence of an evolutionarily accessible sequence as stronger evidence of non-functionality than the absence of one unlikely to arise. Tested on eleven tasks focusing on multiple Spike protein properties spanning five SARS-CoV-2 variants, PULSE ranks first or second on all of them, and achieves the best overall performance among all methods, including standard PU learning, one-class classification methods, and protein language models.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.094007+00	\N	\N	0afb32445d771537317e3fd874bf3f9a433e83c61233af0bb72c59ebc9b233a5	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.09439+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.094392+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cf9ed653-da67-4823-ac58-3cdad06eac82	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.08506v4	Learning Polyhedral Conformal Sets for Robust Optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08506	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08506	Shuyi Chen, Wenbin Zhou, Shixiang Zhu	arXiv:2605.08506v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Robust optimization (RO) provides a principled framework for decision-making under uncertainty, but its performance critically depends on the choice of the uncertainty set. While large sets ensure reliability, they often lead to overly conservative decisions, whereas small sets risk excluding the true outcome. Recent data-driven approaches, particularly conformal prediction, offer finite-sample validity guarantees but remain largely task-agnostic, ignoring the downstream decision structure. In this paper, we propose a decision-aware conformal framework that learns uncertainty sets tailored to robust optimization objectives. Our approach parameterizes a flexible family of polyhedral sets via data-driven hyperplanes and learns their geometry by directly minimizing the induced robust loss, while preserving statistical validity through conformal calibration. To correct for data-dependent selection, we incorporate a re-calibration step on an independent dataset to restore coverage. The resulting sets capture directional and anisotropic uncertainty aligned with the decision objective while remaining computationally tractable. We provide finite-sample coverage guarantees and bounds on the sub-optimality gap to an oracle decision. This work bridges the gap between statistical validity and decision optimality, providing a principled framework for data-driven robust optimization.	arXiv:2605.08506v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Robust optimization (RO) provides a principled framework for decision-making under uncertainty, but its performance critically depends on the choice of the uncertainty set. While large sets ensure reliability, they often lead to overly conservative decisions, whereas small sets risk excluding the true outcome. Recent data-driven approaches, particularly conformal prediction, offer finite-sample validity guarantees but remain largely task-agnostic, ignoring the downstream decision structure. In this paper, we propose a decision-aware conformal framework that learns uncertainty sets tailored to robust optimization objectives. Our approach parameterizes a flexible family of polyhedral sets via data-driven hyperplanes and learns their geometry by directly minimizing the induced robust loss, while preserving statistical validity through conformal calibration. To correct for data-dependent selection, we incorporate a re-calibration step on an independent dataset to restore coverage. The resulting sets capture directional and anisotropic uncertainty aligned with the decision objective while remaining computationally tractable. We provide finite-sample coverage guarantees and bounds on the sub-optimality gap to an oracle decision. This work bridges the gap between statistical validity and decision optimality, providing a principled framework for data-driven robust optimization.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.108493+00	\N	\N	cc290117da04f5cf5fe44056f4a3c43c1f9661e55802e87755f6c9d52e8c48c9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.108801+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.108802+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b0fe53a2-09fd-4111-bd93-3c117795bb38	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.10642v2	Composing diffusion priors with explicit physical context via generative Gibbs sampling	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10642	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10642	Weizhou Wang, Jonathan Weare, Aaron R. Dinner	arXiv:2605.10642v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Pretrained diffusion models provide powerful learned priors, but in scientific sampling the target distribution often depends on physical context that a single generative model can neither fully represent nor learn from scarce joint-system data. We introduce Generative Gibbs for Physics-Aware Sampling (GG-PA), a training-free framework that composes learned partial priors with explicit physical context through Gibbs sampling. We prove that this sampler is asymptotically exact as the diffusion time vanishes and remains exact at finite diffusion time for quadratic interactions. As a special case, the finite-time result corrects a finite-noise bias in plug-and-play split Gibbs samplers for linear inverse problems at no additional cost. We further introduce replica exchange over diffusion time to accelerate mixing. In experiments, a one-dimensional double-well prior recovers a context-induced distribution shift when coupled to an environment, and copies of such a prior composed on a lattice reproduce the emergent collective behavior of the $\\phi^4$ model. In atomistic peptide systems, GG-PA captures a context-induced distribution shift and the spontaneous formation of hydrogen-bonded dimers from two copies of a prior trained only on the monomer.	arXiv:2605.10642v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Pretrained diffusion models provide powerful learned priors, but in scientific sampling the target distribution often depends on physical context that a single generative model can neither fully represent nor learn from scarce joint-system data. We introduce Generative Gibbs for Physics-Aware Sampling (GG-PA), a training-free framework that composes learned partial priors with explicit physical context through Gibbs sampling. We prove that this sampler is asymptotically exact as the diffusion time vanishes and remains exact at finite diffusion time for quadratic interactions. As a special case, the finite-time result corrects a finite-noise bias in plug-and-play split Gibbs samplers for linear inverse problems at no additional cost. We further introduce replica exchange over diffusion time to accelerate mixing. In experiments, a one-dimensional double-well prior recovers a context-induced distribution shift when coupled to an environment, and copies of such a prior composed on a lattice reproduce the emergent collective behavior of the $\\phi^4$ model. In atomistic peptide systems, GG-PA captures a context-induced distribution shift and the spontaneous formation of hydrogen-bonded dimers from two copies of a prior trained only on the monomer.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.126258+00	\N	\N	868e36dff30a6a313b49db8b0d512aa9dc3e98370f093b63e318f0a32766e277	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.126585+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.126586+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1e51d926-bb3e-42e8-8d5b-77c6b7b31742	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.13810v2	DASHQuant: Provable Quantization with a Single Randomized Hadamard Transform	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13810	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13810	Ying Feng, Piotr Indyk, Michael Kapralov, Dmitry Krachun, Boris Prokhorov	arXiv:2605.13810v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Vector quantization via random projection followed by scalar quantization is a fundamental primitive in machine learning, with applications ranging from similarity search to federated learning and KV cache compression. While dense random rotations yield clean theoretical guarantees, they require $\\Theta(d^2)$ time. The randomized Hadamard transform $HD$ reduces this cost to $O(d \\log d)$, but its discrete structure complicates analysis and leads to weaker or purely empirical compression guarantees. In this work, we introduce DASHQuant (Dithered Accelerated Single-Hadamard Quantization), a provable variant of this approach. Specifically, the quantizer applies $HD$ to the input vector and adds a random dithering before quantizing, injecting additional randomness at negligible cost. We prove that this approach is unbiased and provides mean squared error bounds that asymptotically match those achievable with truly random rotation matrices. In particular, we prove that a dithered version of TurboQuant, and a special case of EDEN, achieves mean squared error $\\bigl(\\pi\\sqrt{3}/2 + o(1)\\bigr) \\cdot 4^{-b}$ at $b$ bits per coordinate, where the $o(1)$ term vanishes uniformly over all unit vectors and all dimensions as the number of quantization levels grows.	arXiv:2605.13810v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Vector quantization via random projection followed by scalar quantization is a fundamental primitive in machine learning, with applications ranging from similarity search to federated learning and KV cache compression. While dense random rotations yield clean theoretical guarantees, they require $\\Theta(d^2)$ time. The randomized Hadamard transform $HD$ reduces this cost to $O(d \\log d)$, but its discrete structure complicates analysis and leads to weaker or purely empirical compression guarantees. In this work, we introduce DASHQuant (Dithered Accelerated Single-Hadamard Quantization), a provable variant of this approach. Specifically, the quantizer applies $HD$ to the input vector and adds a random dithering before quantizing, injecting additional randomness at negligible cost. We prove that this approach is unbiased and provides mean squared error bounds that asymptotically match those achievable with truly random rotation matrices. In particular, we prove that a dithered version of TurboQuant, and a special case of EDEN, achieves mean squared error $\\bigl(\\pi\\sqrt{3}/2 + o(1)\\bigr) \\cdot 4^{-b}$ at $b$ bits per coordinate, where the $o(1)$ term vanishes uniformly over all unit vectors and all dimensions as the number of quantization levels grows.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.148545+00	\N	\N	448711a2e9dcaeb9ee5b7994f2cb5380797896735f69a1448569335a16a79b02	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.148874+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.148875+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
46cc690c-ee24-4fc7-924f-7e7c04cd5ce6	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/heimstaden	Heimstaden is changing residential real estate management with AI	https://openai.com/index/heimstaden	https://openai.com/index/heimstaden	\N	Discover how Heimstaden uses OpenAI’s ChatGPT Enterprise and Codex to transform property management, cut costs, and empower employees across Europe.	Discover how Heimstaden uses OpenAI’s ChatGPT Enterprise and Codex to transform property management, cut costs, and empower employees across Europe.	product_update	en	2026-11-03 08:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:41.909812+00	3	\N	726854c54e7ae9ec5793e402f54f09748353cf458b4e090ecb022bdb2f7476ae	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:41.91026+00	2026-11-10 14:42:46.359505+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
585efd39-c480-40ad-bc97-b81d7a697571	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11564v1	From Sound to Occupancy: Privacy-Aware Audio Sensing for Buildings	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11564	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11564	Myeongwon Chae, Seonghyuk Son	arXiv:2611.11564v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Occupancy-based controls regulate lighting and HVAC according to whether a space is in use. Passive infrared (PIR) sensors, the most common choice, respond to motion and can report an occupied room as vacant when its occupants remain still. Sound offers an alternative cue, since seated occupants type, turn pages and cough, but microphones raise privacy concerns. We therefore study a two-stage design: an on-device encoder maps each second of audio to a short embedding, and a server-side temporal model turns the stream of embeddings into an occupancy state. To our knowledge, no publicly available dataset pairs office audio with occupancy labels, so we evaluate the two stages separately on public data. The encoder is trained on synthetic scenes that mix empty-room recordings, room impulse responses, office sounds and read speech, and tested on unseen rooms, recordings and speakers for whether a second contains a sound made by a person. On 11 real office recordings (DCASE 2013) it reaches an AUC of 0.87, against 0.76 for log-mel statistics. Attacks on the transmitted embeddings recover at most 1% of the spoken words, against 94% from log-mel frames, but still identify the speaker among 40 in 69 to 77% of the utterances. Noise-floor normalization with adversarial training, an 8-dimensional embedding, or sending only class probabilities lowers this to 38 to 55% without hurting detection. On HPDmobile, which pairs home occupancy labels with an amplitude envelope of the audio, a causal temporal model pretrained with a hierarchical contrastive loss matches hand-crafted features (AUC 0.75) and does better when only one labelled day per home is available. These results make the two-stage design a practical basis for privacy-aware acoustic occupancy sensing, with speaker identity as the one leak still to close.	arXiv:2611.11564v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Occupancy-based controls regulate lighting and HVAC according to whether a space is in use. Passive infrared (PIR) sensors, the most common choice, respond to motion and can report an occupied room as vacant when its occupants remain still. Sound offers an alternative cue, since seated occupants type, turn pages and cough, but microphones raise privacy concerns. We therefore study a two-stage design: an on-device encoder maps each second of audio to a short embedding, and a server-side temporal model turns the stream of embeddings into an occupancy state. To our knowledge, no publicly available dataset pairs office audio with occupancy labels, so we evaluate the two stages separately on public data. The encoder is trained on synthetic scenes that mix empty-room recordings, room impulse responses, office sounds and read speech, and tested on unseen rooms, recordings and speakers for whether a second contains a sound made by a person. On 11 real office recordings (DCASE 2013) it reaches an AUC of 0.87, against 0.76 for log-mel statistics. Attacks on the transmitted embeddings recover at most 1% of the spoken words, against 94% from log-mel frames, but still identify the speaker among 40 in 69 to 77% of the utterances. Noise-floor normalization with adversarial training, an 8-dimensional embedding, or sending only class probabilities lowers this to 38 to 55% without hurting detection. On HPDmobile, which pairs home occupancy labels with an amplitude envelope of the audio, a causal temporal model pretrained with a hierarchical contrastive loss matches hand-crafted features (AUC 0.75) and does better when only one labelled day per home is available. These results make the two-stage design a practical basis for privacy-aware acoustic occupancy sensing, with speaker identity as the one leak still to close.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.580487+00	\N	\N	17a0998600c6726aee3d9c3a6f5622f45bf0649ce65d208fb85dc5d6fea771b8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.580896+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.580898+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
fffa942c-f5a9-466b-b552-b30ad9cb392d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.14343v2	Nearest-Neighbor Radii under Dependent Sampling	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14343	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14343	Yuanyuan Gao, Yilong Hou, Zhexiao Lin	arXiv:2605.14343v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Nearest-neighbor methods are fundamental to classical and modern machine learning, yet their geometric properties are typically analyzed under independent sampling. In this paper, we study the nearest-neighbor radii under dependent sampling. We consider strong mixing dependent observations and ask whether dependence changes the scale of nearest-neighbor neighborhoods. We establish distribution-free almost sure convergence under polynomial mixing and sharp non-asymptotic moment bounds under geometric mixing. The moment bounds depend on the local intrinsic dimension rather than the ambient dimension, making the results applicable to high-dimensional data supported on lower-dimensional manifolds. Synthetic experiments and real-world time-series benchmarks illustrate the theory, showing that nearest-neighbor geometry remains informative under dependent sampling.	arXiv:2605.14343v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Nearest-neighbor methods are fundamental to classical and modern machine learning, yet their geometric properties are typically analyzed under independent sampling. In this paper, we study the nearest-neighbor radii under dependent sampling. We consider strong mixing dependent observations and ask whether dependence changes the scale of nearest-neighbor neighborhoods. We establish distribution-free almost sure convergence under polynomial mixing and sharp non-asymptotic moment bounds under geometric mixing. The moment bounds depend on the local intrinsic dimension rather than the ambient dimension, making the results applicable to high-dimensional data supported on lower-dimensional manifolds. Synthetic experiments and real-world time-series benchmarks illustrate the theory, showing that nearest-neighbor geometry remains informative under dependent sampling.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.150873+00	\N	\N	077e4d1b099ccbb2bdf556ebf2f8b793b57b82b9781473de04d4b03b42cafde8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.15118+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.151181+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6f282de2-6b7c-487e-bcd4-20f8a3790954	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.15134v4	Training Agents to Fail Predictably: Fine-Tuning for Forecastable Tail Risk	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15134	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15134	Will Schwarzer, Scott Niekum	arXiv:2605.15134v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Before deployment, an AI system might be evaluated on thousands or even millions of inputs, but after deployment it may encounter billions, so its worst failures may not appear during evaluation. Jones et al. (2025) proposed forecasting how risky the riskiest deployment inputs will be by scoring how close the system comes to failure on each evaluation input and extrapolating from the highest scores. We show that the error of this forecast splits into a noise term, whose distribution is the same for every model up to scale, and terms that depend on the shape of the tail of the model's risk scores. These model-dependent terms can make the forecast under-predict badly, both in theory, whenever the evaluation set lacks a rare kind of input that deployment contains, and in practice: on real user prompts marked as toxic, the forecast under-predicted the probability that a response begins with an offensive word for all eleven language models we tested. Because the shape of this tail is a property of the model, we propose fine-tuning the model itself to make the forecast accurate, using the forecast error as the training loss. In a model organism of jailbreaking and in an RL gridworld with rare traps, our method reduced the held-out squared forecast error by factors of 14 to 60, lowered worst-case risk about as much as direct risk minimization (or more), and preserved the model's primary behavior. For settings in which the model should change as little as possible, we show that training with an alternative safety constraint and an added loss term improved the forecast with far smaller changes to the model. Our method also improved the forecast in RL from sampled returns, in language models of up to 8B parameters, and (with minimal-change training) on the toxic user prompts.	arXiv:2605.15134v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Before deployment, an AI system might be evaluated on thousands or even millions of inputs, but after deployment it may encounter billions, so its worst failures may not appear during evaluation. Jones et al. (2025) proposed forecasting how risky the riskiest deployment inputs will be by scoring how close the system comes to failure on each evaluation input and extrapolating from the highest scores. We show that the error of this forecast splits into a noise term, whose distribution is the same for every model up to scale, and terms that depend on the shape of the tail of the model's risk scores. These model-dependent terms can make the forecast under-predict badly, both in theory, whenever the evaluation set lacks a rare kind of input that deployment contains, and in practice: on real user prompts marked as toxic, the forecast under-predicted the probability that a response begins with an offensive word for all eleven language models we tested. Because the shape of this tail is a property of the model, we propose fine-tuning the model itself to make the forecast accurate, using the forecast error as the training loss. In a model organism of jailbreaking and in an RL gridworld with rare traps, our method reduced the held-out squared forecast error by factors of 14 to 60, lowered worst-case risk about as much as direct risk minimization (or more), and preserved the model's primary behavior. For settings in which the model should change as little as possible, we show that training with an alternative safety constraint and an added loss term improved the forecast with far smaller changes to the model. Our method also improved the forecast in RL from sampled returns, in language models of up to 8B parameters, and (with minimal-change training) on the toxic user prompts.	article	en	2026-11-10 0