8964a548-b668-455d-a853-718728fe051b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.13194v2	HARENA: A Living Benchmark for Sensor-Based Human Activity Recognition on Resource-Constrained Edge Devices	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13194	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13194	Maximilian Burzer, Tobias King, Till Riedel, Michael Beigl, Tobias R\\"oddiger	arXiv:2606.13194v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Sensor-based human activity recognition runs on edge devices, trading performance against latency, memory, and energy. Yet new architectures are usually compared with few, often outdated baselines on few datasets with study-specific data preparation and hardware, leaving open which perform best at what cost. We present HARENA, a living benchmark with a public leaderboard, to which new architectures and datasets can be added under one protocol. Its initial suite evaluates 16 neural architectures and three classical baselines on 29 datasets, measuring performance on unseen subjects and deployment cost on a smartphone, smartwatch, and microcontroller. CNN-HAR, the suite's oldest deep architecture, leads overall, but 8 architectures lie within 2.5 points of its mean test macro-F1, while their cost differs by orders of magnitude. On the microcontroller, Random Forest leads on latency and peak RAM, LITEWAY-Light on flash, and TinyHAR is most accurate within the real-time budget. Rankings shift with dataset and channel count, so general claims require many datasets, which a growing benchmark can provide. We find no sign of benchmark overfitting, but performance drops by a median of 15.0 points on unseen subjects, about three times the typical spread between architectures, making generalization and adaptation the most promising future directions.	arXiv:2606.13194v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Sensor-based human activity recognition runs on edge devices, trading performance against latency, memory, and energy. Yet new architectures are usually compared with few, often outdated baselines on few datasets with study-specific data preparation and hardware, leaving open which perform best at what cost. We present HARENA, a living benchmark with a public leaderboard, to which new architectures and datasets can be added under one protocol. Its initial suite evaluates 16 neural architectures and three classical baselines on 29 datasets, measuring performance on unseen subjects and deployment cost on a smartphone, smartwatch, and microcontroller. CNN-HAR, the suite's oldest deep architecture, leads overall, but 8 architectures lie within 2.5 points of its mean test macro-F1, while their cost differs by orders of magnitude. On the microcontroller, Random Forest leads on latency and peak RAM, LITEWAY-Light on flash, and TinyHAR is most accurate within the real-time budget. Rankings shift with dataset and channel count, so general claims require many datasets, which a growing benchmark can provide. We find no sign of benchmark overfitting, but performance drops by a median of 15.0 points on unseen subjects, about three times the typical spread between architectures, making generalization and adaptation the most promising future directions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.243028+00	\N	\N	475157c1cd42ccad5804f8b474f43c14566e3e2f0429f81efdc60f5dd54d9b22	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.243339+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.243341+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c74cee0c-3215-411a-8aee-fa3105e4e37a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.21023v2	Demystifying Numerical Instability in LLM Inference: Achieving Reproducible Inference for Mission-Critical Tasks with HEAL	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21023	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21023	Zhenting Zhu, Lucas Thai, Shan Yu, Yicheng Liu, Yifan Qiao, Chenxi Wang, Harry Xu, Junyi Shu	arXiv:2606.21023v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: As Large Language Models (LLMs) deploy into mission-critical domains (e.g., finance, medicine, and law), output reproducibility has become a strict system requirement. While practitioners use greedy decoding to eliminate algorithmic stochasticity, empirical deployments with 16-bit precisions still exhibit catastrophic output divergence across heterogeneous GPUs. Through SASS-level profiling, we reveal that this inconsistency is fundamentally driven by truncation errors introduced during downcasting at kernel boundaries. However, achieving reproducibility via a global FP32 pipeline incurs prohibitive system penalties: bypassing 16-bit hardware accelerators hurts compute efficiency, while upcasting the KV cache doubles memory overhead. To bridge this gap, we propose Hybrid Error ALleviation (HEAL), a targeted intervention that approximates FP32 precision while resolving hardware constraints through two targeted mechanisms. First, recognizing that floating-point formats underutilize their bit-width for Q, K, V tensors, HEAL applies INT16 quantization that preserves numerical stability without expanding the KV cache footprint. Second, HEAL synthesizes high-precision matrix multiplications via an algebraic error compensation strategy, executing entirely on high-throughput 16-bit Tensor Cores. To evaluate our approach practically, we introduce MCR-Bench, a benchmark targeting reproducibility in mission-critical tasks. HEAL achieves the same level of reproducibility on downstream tasks as the FP32 baseline while reducing the performance overhead by up to 6.0$\\times$.	arXiv:2606.21023v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: As Large Language Models (LLMs) deploy into mission-critical domains (e.g., finance, medicine, and law), output reproducibility has become a strict system requirement. While practitioners use greedy decoding to eliminate algorithmic stochasticity, empirical deployments with 16-bit precisions still exhibit catastrophic output divergence across heterogeneous GPUs. Through SASS-level profiling, we reveal that this inconsistency is fundamentally driven by truncation errors introduced during downcasting at kernel boundaries. However, achieving reproducibility via a global FP32 pipeline incurs prohibitive system penalties: bypassing 16-bit hardware accelerators hurts compute efficiency, while upcasting the KV cache doubles memory overhead. To bridge this gap, we propose Hybrid Error ALleviation (HEAL), a targeted intervention that approximates FP32 precision while resolving hardware constraints through two targeted mechanisms. First, recognizing that floating-point formats underutilize their bit-width for Q, K, V tensors, HEAL applies INT16 quantization that preserves numerical stability without expanding the KV cache footprint. Second, HEAL synthesizes high-precision matrix multiplications via an algebraic error compensation strategy, executing entirely on high-throughput 16-bit Tensor Cores. To evaluate our approach practically, we introduce MCR-Bench, a benchmark targeting reproducibility in mission-critical tasks. HEAL achieves the same level of reproducibility on downstream tasks as the FP32 baseline while reducing the performance overhead by up to 6.0$\\times$.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.257857+00	\N	\N	f6ac1d7a4aa20faaae0a6fa9632b99b34a7476cd90d3819db0a0acb724a5f1d8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.258184+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.258186+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
111d5cf4-9d52-4bf0-9fd4-acfa7755c79f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.01835v2	ReFrame: Silence-Aware Compute Reclamation for Full-Duplex Speech Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01835	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01835	Zheng Zhou, Guanyi Chen, Jing Wei, Haoyuan Liang, Jian Zhang, Haohuan Fu	arXiv:2611.01835v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Full-duplex speech models execute dense model inference for every audio frame throughout a live interaction. This uniform framewise schedule creates sustained GPU load across dialogue states whose computation has strong, reusable temporal structure. ReFrame formulates full-duplex inference as a silence-aware computation problem. Mutual Silence Speculation (MSS) operates when both participants are silent: it evaluates multi-frame Temporal continuations, verifies text and hidden-state stability, and applies batched Mimi audio-token correction. Listening Frame Folding (LFF) operates while the user speaks and the agent listens: it processes already-arrived user frames through parallel Temporal execution and frame-batched audio-token generation while accepting only the verified prefix. Frame-Budget Reinvestment (FBR) assigns the released frame intervals to resumable external retrieval and language-model workers, then injects timely references through Moshi's conditioning interface. We instantiate ReFrame on five full-duplex models spanning Moshi-family, phase-structured, and flattened autoregressive generation. Across the complete 1,225-conversation FDBench, ReFrame reduces measured GPU execution time by 18.80-31.01% and retains 96.92-100% non-PAD text agreement on five different models. On HaluEval tests, FBR increases the number of correct generated spoken answers from 86 to 205 under the same auxiliary-service budget. ReFrame turns recurring dialogue structure into lower inference cost and higher end-to-end service capability.	arXiv:2611.01835v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Full-duplex speech models execute dense model inference for every audio frame throughout a live interaction. This uniform framewise schedule creates sustained GPU load across dialogue states whose computation has strong, reusable temporal structure. ReFrame formulates full-duplex inference as a silence-aware computation problem. Mutual Silence Speculation (MSS) operates when both participants are silent: it evaluates multi-frame Temporal continuations, verifies text and hidden-state stability, and applies batched Mimi audio-token correction. Listening Frame Folding (LFF) operates while the user speaks and the agent listens: it processes already-arrived user frames through parallel Temporal execution and frame-batched audio-token generation while accepting only the verified prefix. Frame-Budget Reinvestment (FBR) assigns the released frame intervals to resumable external retrieval and language-model workers, then injects timely references through Moshi's conditioning interface. We instantiate ReFrame on five full-duplex models spanning Moshi-family, phase-structured, and flattened autoregressive generation. Across the complete 1,225-conversation FDBench, ReFrame reduces measured GPU execution time by 18.80-31.01% and retains 96.92-100% non-PAD text agreement on five different models. On HaluEval tests, FBR increases the number of correct generated spoken answers from 86 to 205 under the same auxiliary-service budget. ReFrame turns recurring dialogue structure into lower inference cost and higher end-to-end service capability.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.484488+00	\N	\N	54c45811a509bdf8f5f31f5620c038d3dee1a9bed9a7affc2840706699f273b1	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.48481+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.484812+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b578d83f-7b13-4ab6-95d8-9ca149aa9c8a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.27767v2	Difference of Convex Programming in the Wasserstein Space with Applications to MMD Optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27767	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27767	Cl\\'ement Bonet, Pierre-Cyril Aubin-Frankowski, Youssef Mroueh	arXiv:2606.27767v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Optimizing functionals over the space of probability measures is now ubiquitous in machine learning. A widely used approach is to perform the optimization directly over the Wasserstein space, but many objective functionals of practical interest are non-convex along Wasserstein geodesics, making the analysis of standard first-order methods challenging. In this work, we study a class of objectives over the Wasserstein space that admit a difference-of-convex (DC) decomposition and we lift the classical convex-concave procedure (CCCP) to this setting. Under smoothness and strong convexity assumptions on the convex components of the decomposition, we prove almost stationarity along the iterates of the resulting algorithm. Our main focus is on the Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) and the Energy Distance (ED) functionals, for which we develop explicit Wasserstein DC decompositions, and establish local convergence of the scheme under mild assumptions. Empirically, we show that well-chosen DC decompositions yield faster and more stable convergence than Wasserstein gradient descent on these MMD objectives.	arXiv:2606.27767v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Optimizing functionals over the space of probability measures is now ubiquitous in machine learning. A widely used approach is to perform the optimization directly over the Wasserstein space, but many objective functionals of practical interest are non-convex along Wasserstein geodesics, making the analysis of standard first-order methods challenging. In this work, we study a class of objectives over the Wasserstein space that admit a difference-of-convex (DC) decomposition and we lift the classical convex-concave procedure (CCCP) to this setting. Under smoothness and strong convexity assumptions on the convex components of the decomposition, we prove almost stationarity along the iterates of the resulting algorithm. Our main focus is on the Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) and the Energy Distance (ED) functionals, for which we develop explicit Wasserstein DC decompositions, and establish local convergence of the scheme under mild assumptions. Empirically, we show that well-chosen DC decompositions yield faster and more stable convergence than Wasserstein gradient descent on these MMD objectives.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.262691+00	\N	\N	dbd1c55a8ff99a6410110855427df7a0dc4e57a90c7f57134ab12cb2d8b89771	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.263015+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.263017+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
124d6aa3-76a7-4de4-80e5-97a269ea25d3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.27832v2	USAD: Uncertainty-aware Statistical Adversarial Detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27832	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27832	Zhijian Zhou, Xunye Tian, Jiacheng Zhang, Zesheng Ye, Yiyi Guo, Donghao Zhang, Liuhua Peng, Feng Liu	arXiv:2606.27832v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Statistical adversarial detection (SAD) treats detection as a two-sample test. Given a reference set of clean examples (CEs) and a batch of queries, potentially containing an unknown mixture of CEs and adversarial examples (AEs), SAD decides whether the query distribution drifts away from the CE distribution while controlling the false-alarm rate. Existing SAD-based methods mainly use maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) to measure the distributional discrepancy. However, MMD's distributional properties limit its ability to capture characteristic uncertainty patterns of AEs that are crucial for detection: AEs typically exhibit abnormal feature spread (i.e., global uncertainty) and instability under perturbations (i.e., local uncertainty). To close the gap, we propose Uncertainty-aware Statistical Adversarial Detection (USAD), which explicitly captures these uncertainty patterns with two new statistics: (1) Variance Discrepancy (VD), which measures the difference in feature spread between AEs and CEs to capture global uncertainty differences. (2) Perturbation-based Covariance Discrepancy (PCD), which compares feature covariance under Gaussian perturbations to capture local uncertainty differences. By aggregating VD and PCD, USAD achieves superior detection performances over baseline methods against various adversarial attacks, highlighting the importance of considering characteristic behaviors of AEs for effective SAD. Our code is available at: https://github.com/yeager20001118/USAD.	arXiv:2606.27832v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Statistical adversarial detection (SAD) treats detection as a two-sample test. Given a reference set of clean examples (CEs) and a batch of queries, potentially containing an unknown mixture of CEs and adversarial examples (AEs), SAD decides whether the query distribution drifts away from the CE distribution while controlling the false-alarm rate. Existing SAD-based methods mainly use maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) to measure the distributional discrepancy. However, MMD's distributional properties limit its ability to capture characteristic uncertainty patterns of AEs that are crucial for detection: AEs typically exhibit abnormal feature spread (i.e., global uncertainty) and instability under perturbations (i.e., local uncertainty). To close the gap, we propose Uncertainty-aware Statistical Adversarial Detection (USAD), which explicitly captures these uncertainty patterns with two new statistics: (1) Variance Discrepancy (VD), which measures the difference in feature spread between AEs and CEs to capture global uncertainty differences. (2) Perturbation-based Covariance Discrepancy (PCD), which compares feature covariance under Gaussian perturbations to capture local uncertainty differences. By aggregating VD and PCD, USAD achieves superior detection performances over baseline methods against various adversarial attacks, highlighting the importance of considering characteristic behaviors of AEs for effective SAD. Our code is available at: https://github.com/yeager20001118/USAD.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.265087+00	\N	\N	0574e556afee74e84f90560e05d3f764682ad2d725f570bc8cdeff01103b1e53	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.265402+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.265404+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c6ea0b84-1464-424c-ad78-476461fa0b5a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.30509v2	Muon learns balanced solutions in matrix factorization without slow saddle-to-saddle dynamics	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30509	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30509	Mark Rhee, Jamie Simon, Dhruva Karkada	arXiv:2606.30509v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Matrix factorization (i.e., problems of the form $\\min_{\\mathbf{P},\\mathbf{Q}} \\|\\mathbf{M}^\\star - \\mathbf{P}^\\top\\mathbf{Q}\\|_\\mathrm{F}^2$) is a minimal learning problem that exhibits both nonlinear parameter dynamics and representation learning. In this setting, we study how parameter trajectories under the Muon optimizer differ from those of gradient descent. We identify three main dynamical differences: (1) Muon avoids the slow saddle-to-saddle dynamics of GD from small initialization. Muon instead learns all the top singular vectors of $\\mathbf{M}^\\star$ at the same rate, and they saturate in order of increasing (rather than decreasing) singular value. (2) Muon does not diverge even when the learning rate exceeds the critical threshold set by the local loss sharpness. This frees the learning rate from the condition number of the problem, enabling rapid convergence via exponential learning rate annealing. (3) Once the weights are aligned with each other and the target, Muon flow conserves the matrix quantity $\\sqrt{\\mathbf{P}^\\top \\mathbf{P}}-\\sqrt{\\mathbf{Q}^\\top \\mathbf{Q}}$, while gradient flow is known to conserve the matrix $\\mathbf{P}^\\top\\mathbf{P} - \\mathbf{Q}^\\top\\mathbf{Q}$. Despite having distinct conserved quantities, both optimizers find the so-called balanced solution from vanishing initialization. When training from small random initialization, the weights spontaneously align early in training. We derive the alignment rates in simple settings and show that they predict the empirical alignment rates in general. Finally, we exploit structural properties of Muon to construct a learning rate schedule that achieves near-perfect alignment in only two optimization steps.	arXiv:2606.30509v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Matrix factorization (i.e., problems of the form $\\min_{\\mathbf{P},\\mathbf{Q}} \\|\\mathbf{M}^\\star - \\mathbf{P}^\\top\\mathbf{Q}\\|_\\mathrm{F}^2$) is a minimal learning problem that exhibits both nonlinear parameter dynamics and representation learning. In this setting, we study how parameter trajectories under the Muon optimizer differ from those of gradient descent. We identify three main dynamical differences: (1) Muon avoids the slow saddle-to-saddle dynamics of GD from small initialization. Muon instead learns all the top singular vectors of $\\mathbf{M}^\\star$ at the same rate, and they saturate in order of increasing (rather than decreasing) singular value. (2) Muon does not diverge even when the learning rate exceeds the critical threshold set by the local loss sharpness. This frees the learning rate from the condition number of the problem, enabling rapid convergence via exponential learning rate annealing. (3) Once the weights are aligned with each other and the target, Muon flow conserves the matrix quantity $\\sqrt{\\mathbf{P}^\\top \\mathbf{P}}-\\sqrt{\\mathbf{Q}^\\top \\mathbf{Q}}$, while gradient flow is known to conserve the matrix $\\mathbf{P}^\\top\\mathbf{P} - \\mathbf{Q}^\\top\\mathbf{Q}$. Despite having distinct conserved quantities, both optimizers find the so-called balanced solution from vanishing initialization. When training from small random initialization, the weights spontaneously align early in training. We derive the alignment rates in simple settings and show that they predict the empirical alignment rates in general. Finally, we exploit structural properties of Muon to construct a learning rate schedule that achieves near-perfect alignment in only two optimization steps.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.269926+00	\N	\N	0cda136adf70b9442f6907b167067731ecc6f1273bbebf6a99ffa721fc36f416	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.270248+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.27025+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
42d5dbcc-95ef-4102-8d45-99fba462f2b5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.30995v3	Multistage Defer Trees for Hybrid Interpretability: If at First You Can't Succeed, Tree Again	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30995	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30995	Zakk Heile, Hayden McTavish, Margo Seltzer, Cynthia Rudin	arXiv:2606.30995v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent work has shown that well-optimized individual decision trees can match complex black box models in some settings, primarily in noisy domains. For the remaining settings, however, complex ensembled compositions of trees often achieve higher accuracy at the cost of interpretability, leaving practitioners with difficult modeling decisions along an accuracy-interpretability tradeoff. Ideally, we would like to classify as much of the data as possible with one or a small number of trees, achieving interpretability for most samples while maintaining state-of-the-art accuracy. We introduce Multistage Defer Trees: a sequence of sparse decision trees that each make predictions for most samples, while deferring a small proportion to the next tree in the sequence or, ultimately, to a black box. We demonstrate that we can train this model class to match the performance of complex tree-based ensembles while routing most samples through only one or a small number of sparse decision trees. We discuss a range of techniques for training these models while maintaining simplicity. Our method expands the accuracy--interpretability frontier in settings where single-tree methods remain insufficient, demonstrating that even when complex models are necessary, they need not be fully opaque.	arXiv:2606.30995v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent work has shown that well-optimized individual decision trees can match complex black box models in some settings, primarily in noisy domains. For the remaining settings, however, complex ensembled compositions of trees often achieve higher accuracy at the cost of interpretability, leaving practitioners with difficult modeling decisions along an accuracy-interpretability tradeoff. Ideally, we would like to classify as much of the data as possible with one or a small number of trees, achieving interpretability for most samples while maintaining state-of-the-art accuracy. We introduce Multistage Defer Trees: a sequence of sparse decision trees that each make predictions for most samples, while deferring a small proportion to the next tree in the sequence or, ultimately, to a black box. We demonstrate that we can train this model class to match the performance of complex tree-based ensembles while routing most samples through only one or a small number of sparse decision trees. We discuss a range of techniques for training these models while maintaining simplicity. Our method expands the accuracy--interpretability frontier in settings where single-tree methods remain insufficient, demonstrating that even when complex models are necessary, they need not be fully opaque.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.272281+00	\N	\N	9f792c8673ae36836dfe87f3f248e5928bfbfeab766efd428d52462309fdc252	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.272618+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.27262+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b77caa22-1dfc-4097-a8ac-8486bfcb1960	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.12037v2	Clustered Randomized Smoothing for Stochastic Prediction Functions	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.12037	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.12037	Eduardo Figueiredo, Frederik Mathiesen, Julian Schumann, Jens Kober, Arkady Zgonnikov, Luca Laurenti	arXiv:2608.12037v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern stochastic predictors can model rich, multi-modal outcome distributions. However, this expressive power comes with challenges in ensuring robust predictions $-$ a critical requirement in safety-critical domains. Randomized smoothing is a leading technique for improving robustness, particularly against adversarial perturbations. Yet, in stochastic multi-modal regression settings, randomized smoothing often fails due to mode collapse, yielding averaged predictions that do not reflect the underlying distribution. To address this limitation, we propose clustered $\\alpha$-smoothing, a framework that (1) partitions noisy samples using an arbitrary clustering algorithm, (2) applies $\\alpha$-smoothing locally within each cluster, and (3) combines the resulting predictions into a mixture distribution. By interpreting the smoothing distribution as a mixture of $\\alpha$-smoothers, we derive a lower bound on the probability that the smoothed prediction lies within a union of compact regions corresponding to distinct modes. We empirically evaluate our framework on two benchmarks, demonstrating substantial improvements over state-of-the-art methods. In stochastic trajectory prediction on a driving simulator dataset, our approach achieves, on average, a $27\\%$ lower Wasserstein distance to the ground-truth distribution compared to $\\alpha$-smoothing. In quadrotor control, where modes correspond to distinct feasible paths to a target, our method reduces the collision rate by $90\\%$ relative to the state-of-the-art randomized smoothing.	arXiv:2608.12037v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern stochastic predictors can model rich, multi-modal outcome distributions. However, this expressive power comes with challenges in ensuring robust predictions $-$ a critical requirement in safety-critical domains. Randomized smoothing is a leading technique for improving robustness, particularly against adversarial perturbations. Yet, in stochastic multi-modal regression settings, randomized smoothing often fails due to mode collapse, yielding averaged predictions that do not reflect the underlying distribution. To address this limitation, we propose clustered $\\alpha$-smoothing, a framework that (1) partitions noisy samples using an arbitrary clustering algorithm, (2) applies $\\alpha$-smoothing locally within each cluster, and (3) combines the resulting predictions into a mixture distribution. By interpreting the smoothing distribution as a mixture of $\\alpha$-smoothers, we derive a lower bound on the probability that the smoothed prediction lies within a union of compact regions corresponding to distinct modes. We empirically evaluate our framework on two benchmarks, demonstrating substantial improvements over state-of-the-art methods. In stochastic trajectory prediction on a driving simulator dataset, our approach achieves, on average, a $27\\%$ lower Wasserstein distance to the ground-truth distribution compared to $\\alpha$-smoothing. In quadrotor control, where modes correspond to distinct feasible paths to a target, our method reduces the collision rate by $90\\%$ relative to the state-of-the-art randomized smoothing.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.320179+00	\N	\N	098b290ad07e26f798b08fc546dfc3d7c35431031b56d95a0b34fd51f3d7864d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.320545+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.320547+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8158377d-bc65-41d0-867e-5ef278b1891f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.12923v2	Annealing in variational inference mitigates mode collapse: A theoretical study on Gaussian mixtures	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12923	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12923	Luigi Fogliani, Bruno Loureiro, Marylou Gabri\\'e	arXiv:2602.12923v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Mode collapse, the failure to capture one or more modes when targeting a multimodal distribution, is a central challenge in modern variational inference. In this work, we provide a mathematical analysis of annealing based strategies for mitigating mode collapse in a tractable setting: learning a Gaussian mixture, where mode collapse is known to arise. Leveraging a low dimensional summary statistics description, we precisely characterize the interplay between the initial temperature and the annealing rate, and derive a sharp formula for the probability of mode collapse. Our analysis shows that an appropriately chosen annealing scheme can robustly prevent mode collapse. Finally, we present numerical evidence that these theoretical tradeoffs qualitatively extend to neural network based models, RealNVP normalizing flows, providing guidance for designing annealing strategies mitigating mode collapse in practical variational inference pipelines.	arXiv:2602.12923v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Mode collapse, the failure to capture one or more modes when targeting a multimodal distribution, is a central challenge in modern variational inference. In this work, we provide a mathematical analysis of annealing based strategies for mitigating mode collapse in a tractable setting: learning a Gaussian mixture, where mode collapse is known to arise. Leveraging a low dimensional summary statistics description, we precisely characterize the interplay between the initial temperature and the annealing rate, and derive a sharp formula for the probability of mode collapse. Our analysis shows that an appropriately chosen annealing scheme can robustly prevent mode collapse. Finally, we present numerical evidence that these theoretical tradeoffs qualitatively extend to neural network based models, RealNVP normalizing flows, providing guidance for designing annealing strategies mitigating mode collapse in practical variational inference pipelines.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.656617+00	\N	\N	9177aecf7c0ccb79799eb9c2f0fb703c0d23653176d9a63fe29dcdbff11e3793	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.656926+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.656928+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
839beb20-a4e2-4843-ab66-1b3a820493eb	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.14689v3	A Reproducibility Study of Partial Residual Ablations in Pre-LN Transformers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14689	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14689	Pratikkumar Babariya	arXiv:2608.14689v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Residual connections are a fundamental component of transformer architectures, yet the roles of the attention and feed-forward residual pathways remain poorly understood when considered independently. This paper presents a reproducibility study of partial residual ablations in Pre-LN GPT-style transformers trained at two scales (10M and 124M parameters). I compare four architectural configurations by selectively removing the attention residual connection, the feed-forward residual connection, or both. At 10M scale, a controlled 8-seed deterministic sweep shows a clear asymmetry: removing the attention residual (FFNOnly) reaches the No-Residual collapse regime (3.350 +/- 0.002), whereas removing the feed-forward residual (AttnOnly) remains well separated from it (1.580 +/- 0.003). A subsequent controlled 124M sweep establishes that the direction of this asymmetry persists under matched seeds: across five seeds each, AttnOnly reaches 6.037 +/- 0.355 versus 7.569 +/- 0.001 for FFNOnly, with no overlap between the observed ranges. AttnOnly nevertheless remains substantially degraded relative to FullResidual (4.577 +/- 0.012) and exhibits markedly greater seed sensitivity at 124M. During the investigation, I identified and corrected an experimental measurement confound in runtime gain scaling and retained an intermediate reproduction failure rather than excluding it. I propose cross-position routing through self-attention as a falsifiable hypothesis for the observed asymmetry; no clean causal test of this mechanism has yet been completed. To support reproducibility, I release the source code, experiment configurations, training logs, and experimental results, including intermediate non-reproducing runs.	arXiv:2608.14689v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Residual connections are a fundamental component of transformer architectures, yet the roles of the attention and feed-forward residual pathways remain poorly understood when considered independently. This paper presents a reproducibility study of partial residual ablations in Pre-LN GPT-style transformers trained at two scales (10M and 124M parameters). I compare four architectural configurations by selectively removing the attention residual connection, the feed-forward residual connection, or both. At 10M scale, a controlled 8-seed deterministic sweep shows a clear asymmetry: removing the attention residual (FFNOnly) reaches the No-Residual collapse regime (3.350 +/- 0.002), whereas removing the feed-forward residual (AttnOnly) remains well separated from it (1.580 +/- 0.003). A subsequent controlled 124M sweep establishes that the direction of this asymmetry persists under matched seeds: across five seeds each, AttnOnly reaches 6.037 +/- 0.355 versus 7.569 +/- 0.001 for FFNOnly, with no overlap between the observed ranges. AttnOnly nevertheless remains substantially degraded relative to FullResidual (4.577 +/- 0.012) and exhibits markedly greater seed sensitivity at 124M. During the investigation, I identified and corrected an experimental measurement confound in runtime gain scaling and retained an intermediate reproduction failure rather than excluding it. I propose cross-position routing through self-attention as a falsifiable hypothesis for the observed asymmetry; no clean causal test of this mechanism has yet been completed. To support reproducibility, I release the source code, experiment configurations, training logs, and experimental results, including intermediate non-reproducing runs.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.32283+00	\N	\N	6bee6a49522b4aeac84c97ab6c9b6b823d98709ab5af8a8866e843ad50b55f2b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.323161+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.323162+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9eb96b10-0f37-4be7-8362-f6942fdec123	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11866v1	DeFSM: Robust and Efficient Decentralization of Federated Submodular Maximization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11866	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11866	Duc A. Tran, Dung Truong, Duy Le	arXiv:2611.11866v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Submodular maximization seeks a small subset best representing a large dataset. In its federated setting, the server is a single point of failure and some clients may be Byzantine. Removing the server raises a trilemma of decentraliza- tion, efficiency, and security. We propose DeFSM, a Byzantine-robust and communication-efficient architecture that replaces the server with a lightweight smart contract and a pool of independent, consensus-free agents. Its aggregation rule, SafeGM, builds on two notions from combinatorial geometry: Tukey depth and safe points (the points lying in the convex hull of the good points wherever the bad points are). SafeGM makes the robust decision on 2D Gaussian random projections, which the contract can compute efficiently, and lifts it back as a convex combination of actual updates. Since lifting loses information, it uses the projected geometric median only when its depth certifies it, and otherwise a Tukey median. The share of bad updates is then bounded only by a count: how many there are, not what they contain. We prove, and confirm empirically, that this bound controls the solution quality. On CIFAR10 and PathMNIST, with up to 30% Byzantine clients and agents, DeFSM never collapses and retains on average about 91% of the quality of the ideal reference, the geometric median at a trusted central server. The geometric median decentralized in its best setting would retain about 96%, but the contract cannot compute it, and so each agent would have to download six times more data, or each client twice as much.	arXiv:2611.11866v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Submodular maximization seeks a small subset best representing a large dataset. In its federated setting, the server is a single point of failure and some clients may be Byzantine. Removing the server raises a trilemma of decentraliza- tion, efficiency, and security. We propose DeFSM, a Byzantine-robust and communication-efficient architecture that replaces the server with a lightweight smart contract and a pool of independent, consensus-free agents. Its aggregation rule, SafeGM, builds on two notions from combinatorial geometry: Tukey depth and safe points (the points lying in the convex hull of the good points wherever the bad points are). SafeGM makes the robust decision on 2D Gaussian random projections, which the contract can compute efficiently, and lifts it back as a convex combination of actual updates. Since lifting loses information, it uses the projected geometric median only when its depth certifies it, and otherwise a Tukey median. The share of bad updates is then bounded only by a count: how many there are, not what they contain. We prove, and confirm empirically, that this bound controls the solution quality. On CIFAR10 and PathMNIST, with up to 30% Byzantine clients and agents, DeFSM never collapses and retains on average about 91% of the quality of the ideal reference, the geometric median at a trusted central server. The geometric median decentralized in its best setting would retain about 96%, but the contract cannot compute it, and so each agent would have to download six times more data, or each client twice as much.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.643598+00	\N	\N	0beac49bb3757f0536fd00b65c75036af3faf87988902f4b0d21eee944ff4d25	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.643918+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.643919+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
097254fa-a2fb-4b6e-8d11-f1525859049f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.17162v2	OraclePhys: A Systematic Framework for LLM Fine-Tuning on Structural Mechanics	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17162	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17162	Mingyu Li, Guorui Song, Jing Lin, Haoqian Wang	arXiv:2608.17162v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: What fine-tuning teaches a model is usually diagnosed after the fact. We make it an experimental variable. OraclePhys has three parts. OraclePhys-Bench is a structural-mechanics benchmark whose finite-element oracle grades every answer and counterfactual edit. OraclePhys-30K holds seven answer forms over byte-identical structure descriptions. A controlled training study compares the forms and verifier roles. Two findings follow. First, within this task family the answer form of the label determines what fine-tuning teaches. Ranking supervision lifts held-out localization of the most-deforming story on taller structures from 0.25 to 0.86, a peak-value label yields at best a partial predictor, and a pass/fail label none. This ranking-versus-peak gap replicates on a second domain, a second model family and a paraphrased surface. Second, the gap between our standard SFT and GRPO runs is mostly a recipe effect. With learning rate and step budget matched, the two reach held-out accuracies within 0.03 of each other. At higher rates, GRPO falls below its start while SFT does not. Both also teach the model to apply edits given as instructions. The trained 8B matches a specialist and exceeds a frontier LLM. Both domains are linear, steady-state systems with synthetic text inputs.	arXiv:2608.17162v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: What fine-tuning teaches a model is usually diagnosed after the fact. We make it an experimental variable. OraclePhys has three parts. OraclePhys-Bench is a structural-mechanics benchmark whose finite-element oracle grades every answer and counterfactual edit. OraclePhys-30K holds seven answer forms over