98563d98-51cf-41f8-845e-b1c82d8a0e5b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.21810v2	MSM-Mem: A Universal Medical Structured Multimodal Memory Framework for Medical AI Agents	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21810	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21810	Md Asaduzzaman Jabin, Khoa Le, Lin Zhao, Tianming Liu	arXiv:2608.21810v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Clinical decision-making is inherently experience-driven: physicians progressively refine their reasoning by synthesizing patient history, multimodal observations, and prior diagnostic experiences across interactions. In contrast, current multimodal large language model (MLLM)-based medical AI agents largely operate as stateless inference systems, generating decisions independently for each interaction without retaining or internalizing experiential knowledge. This discrepancy limits their ability to progressively improve reasoning reliability through usage and adapt to longitudinal patient contexts in real-world clinical workflows. In this study, we propose Medical Structured Multimodal Memory (MSM-Mem), an agentic memory framework that enables medical AI agents to evolve through accumulated clinical experiences. MSM-Mem organizes heterogeneous clinical experiences into semantic, episodic, and visual memory and incrementally updates them during inference, allowing the agent to retrieve prior experiences to inform current reasoning and progressively refine decision-making over time. Evaluations on MoE-LLaVA backbones demonstrate consistent performance improve- ments with further gains observed through continued usage. In general, MSM-Mem offers a viable pathway toward medical AI agents capable of evolving their reasoning competence in a manner analogous to the way clinicians learn from practice over time.	arXiv:2608.21810v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Clinical decision-making is inherently experience-driven: physicians progressively refine their reasoning by synthesizing patient history, multimodal observations, and prior diagnostic experiences across interactions. In contrast, current multimodal large language model (MLLM)-based medical AI agents largely operate as stateless inference systems, generating decisions independently for each interaction without retaining or internalizing experiential knowledge. This discrepancy limits their ability to progressively improve reasoning reliability through usage and adapt to longitudinal patient contexts in real-world clinical workflows. In this study, we propose Medical Structured Multimodal Memory (MSM-Mem), an agentic memory framework that enables medical AI agents to evolve through accumulated clinical experiences. MSM-Mem organizes heterogeneous clinical experiences into semantic, episodic, and visual memory and incrementally updates them during inference, allowing the agent to retrieve prior experiences to inform current reasoning and progressively refine decision-making over time. Evaluations on MoE-LLaVA backbones demonstrate consistent performance improve- ments with further gains observed through continued usage. In general, MSM-Mem offers a viable pathway toward medical AI agents capable of evolving their reasoning competence in a manner analogous to the way clinicians learn from practice over time.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.335136+00	\N	\N	9d2b9e6967a3aac7ae76fbbb7bf6ad479747f1e3f83216e1c646a0751f8dd42e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.335487+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.335488+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6bbec670-b4ba-4d3e-b91a-4527f7fbded8	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.08740v2	PAC-Bayesian Bounds for Learning Partially Observed Stochastic Linear Time-Invariant State-Space Systems with Inputs and Sub-Gaussian Noise	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08740	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08740	Mihaly Petreczky, Mohamad Al Ahdab, John Leth	arXiv:2609.08740v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: In this paper we derive a Probably Approximately Correct (PAC)-Bayesian error bound for partially observed linear time-invariant (LTI) stochastic dynamical systems in state-space form with inputs and sub-Gaussian noise. Such bounds are widespread in machine learning, and they are useful for characterizing the predictive power of models learned from finitely many data points. The bound derived in this paper relates the expectation of prediction errors with the prediction error generated by the model on the data used for learning. In addition, we show that it can also be used to derive bounds for the parameter estimation error. In turn, this allows us to provide finite-sample error bounds for the prediction error and parameter estimation error for a wide class of system identification algorithms. Furthermore, as LTI systems are a sub-class of recurrent neural networks (RNNs), these error bounds could be a first step towards PAC-Bayesian bounds for RNNs.	arXiv:2609.08740v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: In this paper we derive a Probably Approximately Correct (PAC)-Bayesian error bound for partially observed linear time-invariant (LTI) stochastic dynamical systems in state-space form with inputs and sub-Gaussian noise. Such bounds are widespread in machine learning, and they are useful for characterizing the predictive power of models learned from finitely many data points. The bound derived in this paper relates the expectation of prediction errors with the prediction error generated by the model on the data used for learning. In addition, we show that it can also be used to derive bounds for the parameter estimation error. In turn, this allows us to provide finite-sample error bounds for the prediction error and parameter estimation error for a wide class of system identification algorithms. Furthermore, as LTI systems are a sub-class of recurrent neural networks (RNNs), these error bounds could be a first step towards PAC-Bayesian bounds for RNNs.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.355345+00	\N	\N	6c633b29b7d5c2d042e593c232f3c98dfc55c54a991319a6b3eb3f7244060492	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.35575+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.355752+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
7b8504e1-36c2-4d65-acaf-dbf103d55512	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.15825v3	Sharp Regret Bounds and a Task-Covariance Correction for Spectral Representation Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15825	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15825	Dier Tang, Jing Yee Tan, Guangyue Han	arXiv:2609.15825v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Spectral contrastive learning keeps the $k$ directions along which two views of the data depend most strongly, while downstream tasks may need other directions. Existing guarantees for spectral features assume that the distribution of tasks has no preferred direction. We show that this isotropy condition is the wrong criterion. With linear probing, the task distribution affects the average probing gain only through the matrix $B\\Lambda B^\\top$, where $B$ records the dependence between views and $\\Lambda$ is the task covariance: optimal features span its top-$k$ eigenspace, whereas spectral features span that of $BB^\\top$. Transfer therefore depends on alignment, and strongly uneven task preferences cost nothing when they favour the spectral directions. Otherwise the regret of spectral features is at most linear in the anisotropy of $\\Lambda$. No representation chosen without task information improves this rate when the leading spectrum is flat, and a spectral gap makes it quadratic. With labelled tasks, the same matrix gives a correction: Fisher discriminant directions for a frozen encoder, or a one-term change to the spectral contrastive loss. We bound the regret of the correction by the error in estimating $B\\Lambda B^\\top$, and compare this error with the misalignment of spectral features to decide when to correct. The analysis extends to finite samples, continuous variables and noisy views. Correction reduces synthetic held-out regret from $0.858$ to $0.003$ with $2000$ labelled tasks, and the decision rule gives the lowest regret among the tested rules at every bank size. On CIFAR-100, spectral features of frozen encoders keep most of the task signal on unseen classes, and where a bank of other classes would lower accuracy, the rule keeps them in every case.	arXiv:2609.15825v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Spectral contrastive learning keeps the $k$ directions along which two views of the data depend most strongly, while downstream tasks may need other directions. Existing guarantees for spectral features assume that the distribution of tasks has no preferred direction. We show that this isotropy condition is the wrong criterion. With linear probing, the task distribution affects the average probing gain only through the matrix $B\\Lambda B^\\top$, where $B$ records the dependence between views and $\\Lambda$ is the task covariance: optimal features span its top-$k$ eigenspace, whereas spectral features span that of $BB^\\top$. Transfer therefore depends on alignment, and strongly uneven task preferences cost nothing when they favour the spectral directions. Otherwise the regret of spectral features is at most linear in the anisotropy of $\\Lambda$. No representation chosen without task information improves this rate when the leading spectrum is flat, and a spectral gap makes it quadratic. With labelled tasks, the same matrix gives a correction: Fisher discriminant directions for a frozen encoder, or a one-term change to the spectral contrastive loss. We bound the regret of the correction by the error in estimating $B\\Lambda B^\\top$, and compare this error with the misalignment of spectral features to decide when to correct. The analysis extends to finite samples, continuous variables and noisy views. Correction reduces synthetic held-out regret from $0.858$ to $0.003$ with $2000$ labelled tasks, and the decision rule gives the lowest regret among the tested rules at every bank size. On CIFAR-100, spectral features of frozen encoders keep most of the task signal on unseen classes, and where a bank of other classes would lower accuracy, the rule keeps them in every case.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.357995+00	\N	\N	58cef58e96297d812dcfa0b1046a9c64f3fcf8451b71691c2430815d83059a0e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.358319+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.35832+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1de59e42-e72e-4835-b34c-5b9a0650e649	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.31351v2	Progressive Memory Transformer: Memory-Aware Attention for Time-Series	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31351	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31351	Tord Sture Stangeland, Andreas K\\"ohler, Steffen M{\\ae}land, Ad\\'in Ram\\'irez Rivera	arXiv:2609.31351v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Time-series carry structure simultaneously at multiple scales (fine-grained variation, mid-range motifs, and global properties) and downstream tasks operate at correspondingly different scales. Most existing self-supervised learning approaches supervise representations globally via instance-level contrastive losses and limited temporal neighborhood supervision, but do not explicitly exploit the structural hierarchy. We propose a learning framework that explicitly enforces a structural hierarchy across three scales independently: a local objective for token alignment, a mid-range objective for window-level motifs, and a global objective for sequence-level agreement. Realizing this framework requires the backbone to expose a representation at each scale; we introduce \\textbf{Progressive Memory Transformer} (PMT), which augments a transformer with writable, window-aligned memory that exposes the mid-range scale alongside the token and sequence-level representations conventional transformers already provide. Across eight UCR/UEA/UCI classification benchmarks, a cue-retention probe, and forecasting benchmarks, PMT learns representations that probe well at the global, mid-range, and local scales---strong low-label classification (1--5\\% labels), competitive forecasting performance across multiple horizons, and quantitative and qualitative evidence that memory states capture mid-range motifs.	arXiv:2609.31351v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Time-series carry structure simultaneously at multiple scales (fine-grained variation, mid-range motifs, and global properties) and downstream tasks operate at correspondingly different scales. Most existing self-supervised learning approaches supervise representations globally via instance-level contrastive losses and limited temporal neighborhood supervision, but do not explicitly exploit the structural hierarchy. We propose a learning framework that explicitly enforces a structural hierarchy across three scales independently: a local objective for token alignment, a mid-range objective for window-level motifs, and a global objective for sequence-level agreement. Realizing this framework requires the backbone to expose a representation at each scale; we introduce \\textbf{Progressive Memory Transformer} (PMT), which augments a transformer with writable, window-aligned memory that exposes the mid-range scale alongside the token and sequence-level representations conventional transformers already provide. Across eight UCR/UEA/UCI classification benchmarks, a cue-retention probe, and forecasting benchmarks, PMT learns representations that probe well at the global, mid-range, and local scales---strong low-label classification (1--5\\% labels), competitive forecasting performance across multiple horizons, and quantitative and qualitative evidence that memory states capture mid-range motifs.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.37956+00	\N	\N	beaf4601df03a78615662f46ad0b954131e2bf9f4e182785bd1d75ec9615f7d2	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.37987+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.379871+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6462e6d2-f3b9-478d-8d50-d789cc7c3127	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.34427v2	LLMs as Adaptive Meta-Solvers: Strategy-Diverse RL for Industrial-Scale Optimization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34427	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34427	Shihao Zhang, Weiting Liu, Siyu Shao, Yitian Chen, Jianfeng Feng, Dongdong Ge, Yinyu Ye	arXiv:2609.34427v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Scaling LLM-based optimization from textbook-scale instances to real-world, industrial tasks remains a critical open challenge. Existing approaches are predominantly evaluated on small, self-contained textual problems and often commit to a solver-integrated paradigm, limiting their ability to handle the scale and structural diversity of practical optimization workloads. In this work, we propose a practical framework for training open-source LLMs to tackle real-world, industrial-scale optimization. We first show empirically that solver-integrated reasoning, exact combinatorial algorithm, and heuristic search exhibit complementary strengths across different problem structures and scales. Motivated by this, we introduce Strategy-Diverse Reinforcement Learning (SDRL), which trains LLMs as adaptive optimization meta-solvers. SDRL leverages this complementarity through a correctness-gated hierarchical diversity reward that promotes robust exploration across varying strategies and within each strategy, effectively preventing premature strategy collapse. We further introduce a mixed-format training scheme that jointly supports both self-contained textual problems and file-grounded instances. Across comprehensive evaluations, our framework outperforms existing fine-tuned methods and frontier models including DeepSeek-V4-Pro and GPT-5.5, both on average across benchmarks and on industrial-scale optimization tasks. We release our code and training data to facilitate future research https://github.com/tokentideAI/LLMMetaSolver.	arXiv:2609.34427v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Scaling LLM-based optimization from textbook-scale instances to real-world, industrial tasks remains a critical open challenge. Existing approaches are predominantly evaluated on small, self-contained textual problems and often commit to a solver-integrated paradigm, limiting their ability to handle the scale and structural diversity of practical optimization workloads. In this work, we propose a practical framework for training open-source LLMs to tackle real-world, industrial-scale optimization. We first show empirically that solver-integrated reasoning, exact combinatorial algorithm, and heuristic search exhibit complementary strengths across different problem structures and scales. Motivated by this, we introduce Strategy-Diverse Reinforcement Learning (SDRL), which trains LLMs as adaptive optimization meta-solvers. SDRL leverages this complementarity through a correctness-gated hierarchical diversity reward that promotes robust exploration across varying strategies and within each strategy, effectively preventing premature strategy collapse. We further introduce a mixed-format training scheme that jointly supports both self-contained textual problems and file-grounded instances. Across comprehensive evaluations, our framework outperforms existing fine-tuned methods and frontier models including DeepSeek-V4-Pro and GPT-5.5, both on average across benchmarks and on industrial-scale optimization tasks. We release our code and training data to facilitate future research https://github.com/tokentideAI/LLMMetaSolver.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.381881+00	\N	\N	a14a604aae3528fd5103d9291f4c9b79af085ab6900d4cd378f3beb15ba9782a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.382202+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.382204+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bc67c372-a3a3-4d21-b2d7-ffed272e2aac	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.37161v2	The Vote Hides the Failure: Aggregation Choice and Noise Robustness in Heart Murmur Detection	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37161	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37161	Nicholaus Dismas Ladislaus, Olatunji Damilare Emmanuel, Samuel Chol Buol	arXiv:2609.37161v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Noise robustness in automated phonocardiogram (PCG) murmur detection, and how it is measured, remains underexamined despite growing interest in low-resource screening. We evaluate two independently reimplemented pipelines, Hierarchical Multi-Scale Convolutional Network (HMS-Net)--CNN, and Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM)--LSTM, under controlled, multi-severity noise with noise-augmented fine-tuning and held-out generalization testing. Under matched aggregation, the complete BiLSTM pipeline outperforms the complete HMS-Net pipeline across all conditions in accuracy and Weighted Accuracy. A stable aggregate accuracy score can misrepresent what individual predictions show: HMS-Net's native aggregation degrades under salt-and-pepper noise far less than majority-vote (MV) aggregation at the same severity, a gap reflecting window-level disagreement its native rule absorbs, while BiLSTM's MV accuracy rises after noise-augmented training even though its individual predictions do not improve. HMS-Net's training effect is significant under one accuracy metric but not another. Noise-robustness conclusions can depend as much on evaluation choices as on the models themselves.	arXiv:2609.37161v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Noise robustness in automated phonocardiogram (PCG) murmur detection, and how it is measured, remains underexamined despite growing interest in low-resource screening. We evaluate two independently reimplemented pipelines, Hierarchical Multi-Scale Convolutional Network (HMS-Net)--CNN, and Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM)--LSTM, under controlled, multi-severity noise with noise-augmented fine-tuning and held-out generalization testing. Under matched aggregation, the complete BiLSTM pipeline outperforms the complete HMS-Net pipeline across all conditions in accuracy and Weighted Accuracy. A stable aggregate accuracy score can misrepresent what individual predictions show: HMS-Net's native aggregation degrades under salt-and-pepper noise far less than majority-vote (MV) aggregation at the same severity, a gap reflecting window-level disagreement its native rule absorbs, while BiLSTM's MV accuracy rises after noise-augmented training even though its individual predictions do not improve. HMS-Net's training effect is significant under one accuracy metric but not another. Noise-robustness conclusions can depend as much on evaluation choices as on the models themselves.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.386819+00	\N	\N	af278066fb0c66e856a862c40cd7daea593ea4ab7b08b96df909e0f976f59e6b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.387344+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.387348+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b9f1e576-c864-4879-a156-b7ae407e141e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.00518v2	One-Step Generative Modeling via Training Dynamics Action	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00518	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00518	Zhangyong Liang, Ying Huang, Haibin Ling	arXiv:2610.00518v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: One-step generative models construct a static generator through iterative training-time transport. Existing transport objectives primarily assess distributional motion, although a neural generator needs to realize the requested sample displacements jointly through shared parameter updates. The training-time construction raises the question: \\emph{once training becomes the iterative process that constructs the final one-step map, what to optimize: the next distributional move, or the route by which the finite generator learns the final map?} To address the question, we introduce \\textbf{T}raining \\textbf{D}ynamics \\textbf{A}ction (\\textbf{TDAction}), which selects transport targets according to local shared-parameter realization cost while retaining a prescribed level of distributional progress. We formulate the cost as a soft-terminal control problem and derive a closed-form Batch Tangent Action-to-Go value that accounts for parameter effort and terminal mismatch. The criterion captures cross-sample interactions omitted by independent pairwise costs; under isotropic mobility, the criterion agrees with quadratic Euclidean assignment for deterministic balanced couplings. Randomized tangent probes provide a low-rank implementation that constructs shared detached targets without adding an inference-time trajectory. Controlled studies examine the relationship between generator geometry, transport selection, and realized local action. On ImageNet $256\\times256$, TDAction attains an FID below $1.1$ without distillation.	arXiv:2610.00518v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: One-step generative models construct a static generator through iterative training-time transport. Existing transport objectives primarily assess distributional motion, although a neural generator needs to realize the requested sample displacements jointly through shared parameter updates. The training-time construction raises the question: \\emph{once training becomes the iterative process that constructs the final one-step map, what to optimize: the next distributional move, or the route by which the finite generator learns the final map?} To address the question, we introduce \\textbf{T}raining \\textbf{D}ynamics \\textbf{A}ction (\\textbf{TDAction}), which selects transport targets according to local shared-parameter realization cost while retaining a prescribed level of distributional progress. We formulate the cost as a soft-terminal control problem and derive a closed-form Batch Tangent Action-to-Go value that accounts for parameter effort and terminal mismatch. The criterion captures cross-sample interactions omitted by independent pairwise costs; under isotropic mobility, the criterion agrees with quadratic Euclidean assignment for deterministic balanced couplings. Randomized tangent probes provide a low-rank implementation that constructs shared detached targets without adding an inference-time trajectory. Controlled studies examine the relationship between generator geometry, transport selection, and realized local action. On ImageNet $256\\times256$, TDAction attains an FID below $1.1$ without distillation.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.400235+00	\N	\N	b3c7e27ee6ed4977d9daafa8b1b1e2ae446d2dbe54fd3d110eefdd931b8dbbdd	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.400628+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.400629+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
be68beda-ba86-4557-8e18-ae24a606b283	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.02283v2	MLoRA:Continual Low-Rank Adaptation via Relative-Response Subspace Selection and Momentum Orthogonalization	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02283	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02283	Junkang Liu	arXiv:2610.02283v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) provides a parameter-efficient approach to continual learning, but its nominal rank can conceal a loss of effective adaptation capacity. We identify \\emph{spectral plasticity collapse}: during sequential adaptation, update energy becomes concentrated in a small subset of singular modes, leaving much of the available low-rank space underutilized. This exposes a limitation of interference avoidance alone: protecting historical representations does not ensure that the remaining adaptation capacity is responsive to new tasks or effectively utilized. To address this problem, we propose \\texttt{MLoRA}, which jointly controls capacity allocation and utilization. First, historical whitening identifies input directions with strong current-task response relative to accumulated historical response, yielding a task-adaptive basis that remains fixed during training. Second, approximate polar orthogonalization of momentum updates reduces spectral concentration within theselected space. An orthonormal basis connects these mechanisms by transferring the factor-update spectrum exactly tothe induced weight update. We establish a max--min characterization of exact subspace selection and derive cumulative spectral bounds under controlled cross-step anisotropy. Across five class-incremental benchmarks and eight incremental settings, \\texttt{MuLoRA} achieves the highest mean accuracy in 15 of 16 reported metrics.	arXiv:2610.02283v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) provides a parameter-efficient approach to continual learning, but its nominal rank can conceal a loss of effective adaptation capacity. We identify \\emph{spectral plasticity collapse}: during sequential adaptation, update energy becomes concentrated in a small subset of singular modes, leaving much of the available low-rank space underutilized. This exposes a limitation of interference avoidance alone: protecting historical representations does not ensure that the remaining adaptation capacity is responsive to new tasks or effectively utilized. To address this problem, we propose \\texttt{MLoRA}, which jointly controls capacity allocation and utilization. First, historical whitening identifies input directions with strong current-task response relative to accumulated historical response, yielding a task-adaptive basis that remains fixed during training. Second, approximate polar orthogonalization of momentum updates reduces spectral concentration within theselected space. An orthonormal basis connects these mechanisms by transferring the factor-update spectrum exactly tothe induced weight update. We establish a max--min characterization of exact subspace selection and derive cumulative spectral bounds under controlled cross-step anisotropy. Across five class-incremental benchmarks and eight incremental settings, \\texttt{MuLoRA} achieves the highest mean accuracy in 15 of 16 reported metrics.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.405336+00	\N	\N	e7d7cbe934f3615b9e698e44921ff46e48e2abfa00e5c2fe90e04c8d0148a212	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.405647+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.405649+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6ff6a029-5b85-4f30-a01f-4c34e2af36be	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.09471v5	When Should an In-Context Learner Expand Its Hypothesis Space?	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09471	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09471	Weihan Li, Xinlei Chen, Junhao Wu, Tianshi Zheng	arXiv:2610.09471v5 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Learning systems adapt quickly within a familiar family of models. The harder step is deciding, from a prediction failure, whether opening a richer family is worth its cost. We cast this as a costly sequential decision and introduce the Structural Revision Environment (SRE), which produces matched failures from noise, exceptions, change within the family or structure outside it, varies the price of expansion and the remaining horizon independently of the evidence, and admits an exact normative solution of the one-shot decision. The solution shows that revision is a value boundary, not an evidence threshold: except at the lowest prices, the richer family becomes the more probable explanation long before expanding becomes optimal. Small Transformers trained in SRE reproduce the expansion boundary from utility alone. Pretrained 7B and 8B language models fail at a definite point: their predictions carry a failure-sensitive signal that their decisions do not reflect. Told the gain of expanding, they weigh it against price and horizon only when they reason, and the one model that finishes its reasoning on the history alone still does not estimate that gain. Controlled post-training of the small Transformers moves the prior and the sharpness of predictions, and neither moves the criterion.	arXiv:2610.09471v5 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Learning systems adapt quickly within a familiar family of models. The harder step is deciding, from a prediction failure, whether opening a richer family is worth its cost. We cast this as a costly sequential decision and introduce the Structural Revision Environment (SRE), which produces matched failures from noise, exceptions, change within the family or structure outside it, varies the price of expansion and the remaining horizon independently of the evidence, and admits an exact normative solution of the one-shot decision. The solution shows that revision is a value boundary, not an evidence threshold: except at the lowest prices, the richer family becomes the more probable explanation long before expanding becomes optimal. Small Transformers trained in SRE reproduce the expansion boundary from utility alone. Pretrained 7B and 8B language models fail at a definite point: their predictions carry a failure-sensitive signal that their decisions do not reflect. Told the gain of expanding, they weigh it against price and horizon only when they reason, and the one model that finishes its reasoning on the history alone still does not estimate that gain. Controlled post-training of the small Transformers moves the prior and the sharpness of predictions, and neither moves the criterion.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.419685+00	\N	\N	a13995229154c8c7462d8b54be45f3b9e8c28ed9a0a26c3277724820b39d3322	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.420004+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.420006+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1cc3d7ba-c4c2-4c48-a488-be5467517d6d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.11288v2	Memorization and Malign Generalization in Conditional Diffusion Models with Random Features	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11288	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11288	Gwangho Kim, Sungyoon Lee	arXiv:2610.11288v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Conditional diffusion models generate diverse, novel, and high-quality samples under prescribed conditions. However, theoretical understanding of their memorization and generalization remains limited, while recent works have characterized these behaviors primarily in unconditional settings. In this work, we analyze a random-feature conditional score model in the high-dimensional proportional limit, deriving asymptotic expressions for training and test losses. By decomposing the test loss, we show that in the overparameterized regime, increasing model width improves prediction of the condition-dependent mean while reducing within-condition prediction variance, a phenomenon we term "malign generalization." We further show that in the infinite-width limit, the model predicts the conditional mean as accurately as ridge regression of the data on the condition, while its within-condition variance vanishes. Finally, analyzing the training loss reveals that more informative conditions lead to memorization of training samples at smaller widths. These theoretical findings are supported by experiments with U-Net architectures on realistic data.	arXiv:2610.11288v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Conditional diffusion models generate diverse, novel, and high-quality samples under prescribed conditions. However, theoretical understanding of their memorization and generalization remains limited, while recent works have characterized these behaviors primarily in unconditional settings. In this work, we analyze a random-feature conditional score model in the high-dimensional proportional limit, deriving asymptotic expressions for training and test losses. By decomposing the test loss, we show that in the overparameterized regime, increasing model width improves prediction of the condition-dependent mean while reducing within-condition prediction variance, a phenomenon we term "malign generalization." We further show that in the infinite-width limit, the model predicts the conditional mean as accurately as ridge regression of the data on the condition, while its within-condition variance vanishes. Finally, analyzing the training loss reveals that more informative conditions lead to memorization of training samples at smaller widths. These theoretical findings are supported by experiments with U-Net architectures on realistic data.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.4244+00	\N	\N	6986218123e9c1a82145fb43c17ad04ef10a2d0e18304cc34d48274462dc0cc9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.424749+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.424751+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cff96afa-ce8a-4ca4-a288-b5ae3563e30c	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.34045v2	Minimax Regret for Sobolev Kernel-Based Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34045	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34045	Haichen Hu, David Simchi-Levi	arXiv:2610.34045v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study the minimax regret rate for episodic kernel-based reinforcement learning, where the expected reward and transition density belong to a Sobolev reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS), with continuous state and action spaces, and adversarial initial states. For Sobolev smoothness parameter $\\nu>0$, state dimension $d_S$, and action dimension $d_A$, we prove that the minimax expected regret over $T$ episodes is $\\widetilde\\Theta(T^{\\max\\{e_A,e_B\\}})$, where $e_A=\\frac{\\nu+d_A}{2\\nu+d_A}$, $e_B=\\frac{\\nu+d_A+3d_S/2}{2\\nu+d_A+2d_S}$ with the other model parameters fixed. The two exponents reflect the distinct difficulties of action selection and learning across states. We introduce Sobolev Optimal Value Iteration (\\texttt{SOVI}), which attains this rate from observed rewards and transitions. For fixed smoothness and reference-kernel lengthscale, its regret bound has a prefactor polynomial in the state-action dimension $d=d_S+d_A$, horizon $H$, reward and transition RKHS norm bounds $B_r,B_p$, and the norm-comparison factor $\\Lambda$, up to logarithmic factors; $\\Lambda=1$ for normalized Mat\\'ern kernels. The algorithm separates initial action selection from continuation-value estimation and combines local polynomial approximation with the shared RKHS structure of the approximation errors. Our matching upper and lower bounds determine the optimal dependence on the number of episodes for Sobolev kernel-based reinforcement learning regret minimization. Thus, we partially address the open problem in Vakili(2024) under Sobolev kernel RL.	arXiv:2610.34045v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study the minimax regret rate for episodic kernel-based reinforcement learning, where the expected reward and transition density belong to a Sobolev reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS), with continuous state and action spaces, and adversarial initial states. For Sobolev smoothness parameter $\\nu>0$, state dimension $d_S$, and action dimension $d_A$, we prove that the minimax expected regret over $T$ episodes is $\\widetilde\\Theta(T^{\\max\\{e_A,e_B\\}})$, where $e_A=\\frac{\\nu+d_A}{2\\nu+d_A}$, $e_B=\\frac{\\nu+d_A+3d_S/2}{2\\nu+d_A+2d_S}$ with the other model parameters fixed. The two exponents reflect the distinct difficulties of action selection and learning across states. We introduce Sobolev Optimal Value Iteration (\\texttt{SOVI}), which attains this rate from observed rewards and transitions. For fixed smoothness and reference-kernel lengthscale, its regret bound has a prefactor polynomial in the state-action dimension $d=d_S+d_A$, horizon $H$, reward and transition RKHS norm bounds $B_r,B_p$, and the norm-comparison factor $\\Lambda$, up to logarithmic factors; $\\Lambda=1$ for normalized Mat\\'ern kernels. The algorithm separates initial action selection from continuation-value estimation and combines local polynomial approximation with the shared RKHS structure of the approximation errors. Our matching upper and lower bounds determine the optimal dependence on the number of episodes for Sobolev kernel-based reinforcement learning regret minimization. Thus, we partially address the open problem in Vakili(2024) under Sobolev kernel RL.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.454566+00	\N	\N	6857ec958d7a1d10115b0b2757b30b264e9a514b94bbb443952c7b1a9889a21c	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.454945+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.454946+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
65ef3b3f-82e2-48d9-82e2-837c3257f226	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.21743v2	Pairwise AUC Optimization Needs Corrective Power: A Unified View	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21743	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21743	Jia Chen, Zhiyong Yang, Shilong Bao, Qianqian Xu, Qingming Huang	arXiv:2610.21743v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Direct pairwise AUC-surrogate training is attractive because it trains toward the ranking metric used at test time, especially under class imbalance. This creates a direct alignment between the training objective and the evaluation metric, but such metric alignment alone does not guarantee that stochastic training remains corrective. We study this gap through Corrective Power: whether pairwise mini-batch updates produce stronger and more reliable improvements for more severe positive-negative ranking errors. Our main observation is that direct pairwise training can enter regions where severe errors remain common but no longer produce useful corrective motion. In such regions, even the mini-batch noise that would normally help SGD move along the steepest severe-error correction direction can become much weaker. In this paper, we seek a theoretical explanation for this by exploring the degenerate property of the U-statistics formed from the pairwise gradients therein. Our results provide a mechanistic explanation for several empirical patterns: (a) squared-hinge loss is often an effective AUC surrogate; (b) cross-entropy warm-up can help models that enter the AUC phase from a poor starting region; and (c) parameter-efficient fine-tuning from a pretrained model can reduce that need by starting closer to a favorable ranking geometry. Experiments on 8 CV tasks and 8 NLP tasks, using ResNet50, DenseNet121, DistilBERT-base, and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, support these predictions.	arXiv:2610.21743v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Direct pairwise AUC-surrogate training is attractive because it trains toward the ranking metric used at test time, especially under class imbalance. This creates a direct alignment between the training objective and the evaluation metric, but such metric alignment alone does not guarantee that stochastic training remains corrective. We study this gap through Corrective Power: whether pairwise mini-batch updates produce stronger and more reliable improvements for more severe positive-negative ranking errors. Our main observation is that direct pairwise training can enter regions where severe errors remain common but no longer produce useful corrective motion. In such regions, even the mini-batch noise that would normally help SGD move along the steepest severe-error correction direction can become much weaker. In this paper, we seek a theoretical explanation for this by exploring the degenerate property of the U-statistics formed from the pairwise gradients therein. Our results provide a mechanistic explanation for several empirical patterns: (a) squared-hinge loss is often an effective AUC surrogate; (b) cross-entropy warm-up can help models that enter the AUC phase from a poor starting region; and (c) parameter-efficient fine-tuning from a pretrained model can reduce that need by starting closer to a favorable ranking geometry. Experiments on 8 CV tasks and 8 NLP tasks, using ResNet50, DenseNet121, DistilBERT-base, and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, support these predictions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.439372+00	\N	\N	0a77f8409da6c2331af6470768c1a50b5b58e2063400ba1337c4c241d07f904f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.439692+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.439693+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
863b2cca-95e5-4d4f-ab98-16c09bd20c1a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.24775v2	Global Convergence in Deep Networks via the Second Law of Thermodynamics	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24775	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24775	Matan Tsipory, Ofir Gaash, Itamar Harel, Nathan Srebro, Daniel Soudry	arXiv:2610.24775v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Existing convergence analyses of finite-width overparameterized deep neural networks under standard parameterization often rely on parameters remaining close to their initialization and typically neglect the role of noise, despite empirical evidence that stochasticity can improve optimization and generalization. To bridge this gap, we study $L_2$-regularized continuous-time Langevin dynamics (CLD) with isotropic noise, for training feedforward networks with smooth activations. We introduce a change-of-measure inequality, inspired by the second law of thermodynamics, that enables control of key properties of the training dynamics under the time-evolving parameter distribution. Leveraging a sharpened lower bound on the minimum eigenvalue of the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) at initialization, we show that injected noise helps preserve NTK stability with high probability without confining the weights to a small neighborhood of their initialization. As a result, we derive a non-asymptotic upper bound on the expected training loss. Our analysis yields explicit conditions on width and noise temperature that guarantee convergence to a finite loss floor. Finally, we experimentally show the network Jacobian can change from initialization, departing the commonly analyzed lazy regime.	arXiv:2610.24775v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Existing convergence analyses of finite-width overparameterized deep neural networks under standard parameterization often rely on parameters remaining close to their initialization and typically neglect the role of noise, despite empirical evidence that stochasticity can improve optimization and generalization. To bridge this gap, we study $L_2$-regularized continuous-time Langevin dynamics (CLD) with isotropic noise, for training feedforward networks with smooth activations. We introduce a change-of-measure inequality, inspired by the second law of thermodynamics, that enables control of key properties of the training dynamics under the time-evolving parameter distribution. Leveraging a sharpened lower bound on the minimum eigenvalue of the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) at initialization, we show that injected noise helps preserve NTK stability with high probability without confining the weights to a small neighborhood of their initialization. As a result, we derive a non-asymptotic upper bound on the expected training loss. Our analysis yields explicit conditions on width and noise temperature that guarantee convergence to a finite loss floor. Finally, we experimentally show the network Jacobian can change from initialization, departing the commonly analyzed lazy regime.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.441988+00	\N	\N	30e474044c4f768e65f5f0cbdeb2dca7fe92b3d47e371ee159d8a3ecee3654dd	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.442365+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.442366+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ac3a7086-923f-4a3d-9c79-0cd4c9ed1a85	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.29391v2	Nominate-then-Adjudicate: LLM-Assisted One-Pass and Per-Instance MIP Solver Tuning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29391	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29391	Yifan Zhao, Qianyang Zhao, Xinyu Guan, Kai Wei, Yuming Deng	arXiv:2610.29391v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Parameter configurations control presolving, cut generation and branching strategies in mixed-integer programming (MIP) solvers, directly affecting solving efficiency. Effective configurations vary across instances, while searching for them online requires additional solver runs. We propose Nominate-then-Adjudicate: an empirical performance model (EPM) shortlists candidates from a fixed portfolio, and one large language model (LLM) call selects using static features, compressed formulation text and memory distilled from offline solver measurements. Text exposes distinctions omitted by the chosen aggregate features; selection requires no online candidate solves or foundation-model weight updates. Across sixteen synthetic families, adjudication improves on the EPM's top-ranked candidate (top-1), raising mean normalized performance score from 0.377 to 0.426 and the win rate against SCIP's default from 84.9% to 90.2%; all ten sequential batches show positive mean-score gains. On 400 Ecole instances, it outperforms the per-instance baseline ISAC+ in mean runtime-based score, with gains concentrated in facility location. Total solver time falls by 69.6% versus default, excluding LLM latency. Ablations support text and memory; a second LLM improves over top-1.The code is available at [this URL](https://github.com/cyberaz/nominate-then-adjudicate)	arXiv:2610.29391v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Parameter configurations control presolving, cut generation and branching strategies in mixed-integer programming (MIP) solvers, directly affecting solving efficiency. Effective configurations vary across instances, while searching for them online requires additional solver runs. We propose Nominate-then-Adjudicate: an empirical performance model (EPM) shortlists candidates from a fixed portfolio, and one large language model (LLM) call selects using static features, compressed formulation text and memory distilled from offline solver measurements. Text exposes distinctions omitted by the chosen aggregate features; selection requires no online candidate solves or foundation-model weight updates. Across sixteen synthetic families, adjudication improves on the EPM's top-ranked candidate (top-1), raising mean normalized performance score from 0.377 to 0.426 and the win rate against SCIP's default from 84.9% to 90.2%; all ten sequential batches show positive mean-score gains. On 400 Ecole instances, it outperforms the per-instance baseline ISAC+ in mean runtime-based score, with gains concentrated in facility location. Total solver time falls by 69.6% versus default, excluding LLM latency. Ablations support text and memory; a second LLM improves over top-1.The code is available at [this URL](https://github.com/cyberaz/nominate-then-adjudicate)	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.446589+00	\N	\N	95d6ce4be55c6b2fc85aacdb68cf591063c0de89564bb3c872e42709df3d809c	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.446953+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.446954+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0997b3cd-3c18-4a3f-ad5c-2fa71bbc0233	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.32740v2	Strongly Adaptive Online Learning with Time-Varying Movement Costs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32740	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32740	Andrew Jacobsen, Emmanuel Esposito, Hao Qiu, Mengxiao Zhang	arXiv:2610.32740v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study online convex optimization (OCO) with time-varying movement costs, where the learner incurs both a loss and a time-dependent penalty for changing its decisions. While previous work has established dynamic regret guarantees for this setting, obtaining strongly adaptive guarantees that hold simultaneously over arbitrary intervals remains an open challenge. We address this by developing a new algorithm which, for every interval $[a,b]\\subseteq[T]$ and arbitrary comparator sequence, achieves a data-dependent regret bound of $ \\widetilde{\\mathcal{O}}\\left( \\sqrt{(D^2+DP_{[a,b]})\\sum_{t \\in [a,b]}(\\|g_t\\|^2+\\lambda_t\\|g_t\\|)} \\right) $, where $D$ is the diameter of the decision domain, $P_{[a,b]}$ is the path length of the comparator sequence over $[a,b]$, $g_t$ is the gradient of the loss, and $\\lambda_t$ is the movement-cost coefficient at round~$t$. Key to our approach is a novel reduction to sleeping experts with movement costs, a previously unexplored variant that is of independent interest. As a result of our development, we further obtain strongly-adaptive dynamic regret guarantees under arbitrary delayed feedback with a refined dependence on interval-restricted cumulative delays. Our approach also naturally extends to OCO with time-varying memory, where we obtain strongly-adaptive guarantees which improve the best-known bounds.	arXiv:2610.32740v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study online convex optimization (OCO) with time-varying movement costs, where the learner incurs both a loss and a time-dependent penalty for changing its decisions. While previous work has established dynamic regret guarantees for this setting, obtaining strongly adaptive guarantees that hold simultaneously over arbitrary intervals remains an open challenge. We address this by developing a new algorithm which, for every interval $[a,b]\\subseteq[T]$ and arbitrary comparator sequence, achieves a data-dependent regret bound of $ \\widetilde{\\mathcal{O}}\\left( \\sqrt{(D^2+DP_{[a,b]})\\sum_{t \\in [a,b]}(\\|g_t\\|^2+\\lambda_t\\|g_t\\|)} \\right) $, where $D$ is the diameter of the decision domain, $P_{[a,b]}$ is the path length of the comparator sequence over $[a,b]$, $g_t$ is the gradient of the loss, and $\\lambda_t$ is the movement-cost coefficient at round~$t$. Key to our approach is a novel reduction to sleeping experts with movement costs, a previously unexplored variant that is of independent interest. As a result of our development, we further obtain strongly-adaptive dynamic regret guarantees under arbitrary delayed feedback with a refined dependence on interval-restricted cumulative delays. Our approach also naturally extends to OCO with time-varying memory, where we obtain strongly-adaptive guarantees which improve the best-known bounds.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.449148+00	\N	\N	73073075c71bc20e47c4612dce7e51e3942477edcd1cb88c8928ebb3e63d00d4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.449506+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.449507+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8c93cef7-2203-4446-91ba-8da6c3ebae92	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.00284v2	OCELOT: Planetary-Scale Species Distribution Modeling with Satellite Foundation Embeddings	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00284	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00284	Bart van Merri\\"enboer, Lauren Harrell, Keith Anderson, Burcu Karagol Ayan, Elise Kleeman, Tom Denton, Drew Purves	arXiv:2611.00284v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large-scale repositories of species observations now contain billions of occurrence records from around the world, but these sightings cluster heavily in human-accessible areas. Existing models use only a small fraction of these records and are not evaluated on extrapolating to remote, unvisited regions--where species distribution models can be used as geographic priors to help classify data from camera traps and acoustic recorders. We scale relative occurrence species distribution models by training a multimodal implicit neural representation network called OCELOT that fuses spatial coordinates with satellite foundation embeddings on two orders of magnitude more observations than previous work: 2 billion GBIF records across 100,000 taxa. Scaling to this regime requires co-designing the data pipeline, taxonomic representation, loss formulation, and model architecture: spatiotemporal thinning and sub-linear cell sampling correct for spatial redundancy and observer bias, while an additive taxonomic readout shares representations across species, genera, and families to improve predictions for rare taxa. To evaluate performance in remote areas, we benchmark geographic extrapolation using the NDCG metric on spatial block holdouts up to 600 km. Satellite foundation embeddings improve extrapolation by 51% over coordinate-only models and outperform classical bioclimatic rasters. The resulting model also matches or outperforms existing baselines on established interpolation benchmarks, demonstrating that optimizing for geographic extrapolation does not compromise in-distribution accuracy.	arXiv:2611.00284v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large-scale repositories of species observations now contain billions of occurrence records from around the world, but these sightings cluster heavily in human-accessible areas. Existing models use only a small fraction of these records and are not evaluated on extrapolating to remote, unvisited regions--where species distribution models can be used as geographic priors to help classify data from camera traps and acoustic recorders. We scale relative occurrence species distribution models by training a multimodal implicit neural representation network called OCELOT that fuses spatial coordinates with satellite foundation embeddings on two orders of magnitude more observations than previous work: 2 billion GBIF records across 100,000 taxa. Scaling to this regime requires co-designing the data pipeline, taxonomic representation, loss formulation, and model architecture: spatiotemporal thinning and sub-linear cell sampling correct for spatial redundancy and observer bias, while an additive taxonomic readout shares representations across species, genera, and families to improve predictions for rare taxa. To evaluate performance in remote areas, we benchmark geographic extrapolation using the NDCG metric on spatial block holdouts up to 600 km. Satellite foundation embeddings improve extrapolation by 51% over coordinate-only models and outperform classical bioclimatic rasters. The resulting model also matches or outperforms existing baselines on established interpolation benchmarks, demonstrating that optimizing for geographic extrapolation does not compromise in-distribution accuracy.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.469486+00	\N	\N	7f31b9e24dbbce599f0f7c99e65cde25fadaae16574ba55d3a62b45794cfeadd	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.469867+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.469868+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b02e1856-c4a3-4eda-8e7d-fdb31ded57bf	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.00403v2	MXCode: Vector Quantization for MX-Native Inference at Scale	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00403	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00403	Gaurav Kolhe, Cansu Demirkiran, Shubhi Tyagi, Sulaiman Sadiq, Boyao Wang, Richard Wang, Maximiliana Behnke, Shreyas Labhsetwar, Levi Melnick, Matthew Mattina, Partha Maji	arXiv:2611.00403v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Memory capacity and bandwidth are major bottlenecks in LLM inference, motivating weight compression beyond conventional 4-bit quantization. Vector quantization (VQ) enables approximately 2-bit weight storage but often relies on codebooks incompatible with native FP4 computation. We introduce MXCode (MX-Native Codebook Quantization), a framework bridging ultra-low-bit VQ compression with MicroXcaling (MX)-native FP4 computation. MXCode proposes three strategies: PostScale, which quantizes learned codebooks to MXFP4; PreScale, which extracts MX scales before codebook learning; and PreScale+, which further compresses these scales using VQ. These strategies explore tradeoffs among quantization quality, scale overhead, and codebook granularity. All reconstruct MX-native W4A4 operands without materializing intermediate high-precision weights. We evaluate MXCode on Qwen3 (235B), DeepSeek-R1 (671B), and GLM-5.2 (753B) using the Microsoft MX Emulation Library. MXCode achieves approximately 2 bits per weight with competitive, workload-dependent model quality. On GB300 hardware, experiments with Qwen3 (235B) show approximately 50% lower model-load memory than Native FP4 while maintaining near-Native-FP4 serving throughput. Under memory-constrained prefix reuse, P90 TTFT improves by up to 94.5%. These results demonstrate software-based reconstruction on existing FP4 hardware and inform the development of future MX-native inference systems.	arXiv:2611.00403v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Memory capacity and bandwidth are major bottlenecks in LLM inference, motivating weight compression beyond conventional 4-bit quantization. Vector quantization (VQ) enables approximately 2-bit weight storage but often relies on codebooks incompatible with native FP4 computation. We introduce MXCode (MX-Native Codebook Quantization), a framework bridging ultra-low-bit VQ compression with MicroXcaling (MX)-native FP4 computation. MXCode proposes three strategies: PostScale, which quantizes learned codebooks to MXFP4; PreScale, which extracts MX scales before codebook learning; and PreScale+, which further compresses these scales using VQ. These strategies explore tradeoffs among quantization quality, scale overhead, and codebook granularity. All reconstruct MX-native W4A4 operands without materializing intermediate high-precision weights. We evaluate MXCode on Qwen3 (235B), DeepSeek-R1 (671B), and GLM-5.2 (753B) using the Microsoft MX Emulation Library. MXCode achieves approximately 2 bits per weight with competitive, workload-dependent model quality. On GB300 hardware, experiments with Qwen3 (235B) show approximately 50% lower model-load memory than Native FP4 while maintaining near-Native-FP4 serving throughput. Under memory-constrained prefix reuse, P90 TTFT improves by up to 94.5%. These results demonstrate software-based reconstruction on existing FP4 hardware and inform the development of future MX-native inference systems.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.472197+00	\N	\N	d308c1f05d96f34d4d473db469a3d406384bf9c8e48dc1af8d0080f25d24c330	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.472525+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.472527+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2d599a1f-8fc2-41ba-b455-5057f510a211	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/ramp	How Ramp engineers accelerate code review with Codex	https://openai.com/index/ramp	https://openai.com/index/ramp	\N	How Ramp engineers use Codex with GPT-5.5 to review code and ship improvements, allowing them to get substantive feedback in minutes instead of hours.	How Ramp engineers use Codex with GPT-5.5 to review code and ship improvements, allowing them to get substantive feedback in minutes instead of hours.	product_update	en	2026-05-20 00:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.730544+00	3	\N	56c366cb03ea7a903a9f2a2866d40101da32203064a15bbe43f45eebc36376b3	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.731081+00	2026-11-10 14:23:58.950182+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
260fab1f-f7c8-49ab-bfac-e41ecdf29d4d	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.00920v2	Non-intrusive appliance state detection in unseen homes using classical machine learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00920	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00920	Zablon Ombiro	arXiv:2611.00920v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Non-intrusive load monitoring (NILM) infers, from the aggregate signal of a single smart meter, when each appliance in a home is ON or OFF, offering significant potential to enhance energy efficiency. Classical machine-learning detectors report F1 scores (the harmonic mean of precision and recall) from 0.53 to 0.98, but the causes of this spread are unseparated, so it is unclear which design choices matter. Real-world deployments, however, require detectors that work in houses never seen during training, whereas most studies test on houses used for training. To address this challenge, we propose a factorial analysis of variance design that separates the effects of classifier, feature set and class-imbalance strategy across five appliances (270 configurations), and a leave-house-out protocol with house-grouped model selection. Unlike studies that vary one factor at a time or pool houses, the approach attributes performance to each factor and measures it on unseen homes. Extensive experiments on REFIT, a public dataset of 20 UK households, show that the appliance explains 38 percent of F1 variance, the feature set 13 percent, the classifier 8 percent and the imbalance strategy 2 percent. Pooled testing overstates support-weighted F1 (0.548 versus 0.447 in unseen houses), class weighting gains grow from 32 percent to 69 percent, and feature engineering gives the same 52 percent gain in both protocols. House-grouped selection raises F1 for three of five appliances, by up to 0.110, and a convolutional neural network scores 0.450 (0.496 calibrated) against 0.447 for classical models. The findings guide appliance-level monitoring in smart-metering and energy-management systems.	arXiv:2611.00920v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Non-intrusive load monitoring (NILM) infers, from the aggregate signal of a single smart meter, when each appliance in a home is ON or OFF, offering significant potential to enhance energy efficiency. Classical machine-learning detectors report F1 scores (the harmonic mean of precision and recall) from 0.53 to 0.98, but the causes of this spread are unseparated, so it is unclear which design choices matter. Real-world deployments, however, require detectors that work in houses never seen during training, whereas most studies test on houses used for training. To address this challenge, we propose a factorial analysis of variance design that separates the effects of classifier, feature set and class-imbalance strategy across five appliances (270 configurations), and a leave-house-out protocol with house-grouped model selection. Unlike studies that vary one factor at a time or pool houses, the approach attributes performance to each factor and measures it on unseen homes. Extensive experiments on REFIT, a public dataset of 20 UK households, show that the appliance explains 38 percent of F1 variance, the feature set 13 percent, the classifier 8 percent and the imbalance strategy 2 percent. Pooled testing overstates support-weighted F1 (0.548 versus 0.447 in unseen houses), class weighting gains grow from 32 percent to 69 percent, and feature engineering gives the same 52 percent gain in both protocols. House-grouped selection raises F1 for three of five appliances, by up to 0.110, and a convolutional neural network scores 0.450 (0.496 calibrated) against 0.447 for classical models. The findings guide appliance-level monitoring in smart-metering and energy-management systems.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.474497+00	\N	\N	8c4e969f42a9369dfbc3ce7447453604395a9900f0e66f975c69dc86a1e25734	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.474872+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.474874+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
acb68d1d-c0a7-4d68-81fe-1347b6beeb5a	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.01077v2	Multi-Turn Semantic Caching for Low-Cost LLM Serving	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01077	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01077	Baran Atalar, Selly Beyene, Xutong Liu, Carlee Joe-Wong	arXiv:2611.01077v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Semantic caching lowers the cost of serving large language models (LLMs) by reusing stored responses for similar requests. In multi-turn conversations, whether a cached response is acceptable depends on both conversations' histories, which embedding similarity of requests and histories predicts poorly (AUC 0.62--0.74 on five workloads). We therefore learn the \\emph{reuse risk}, the probability that a cached response is unacceptable, from judge feedback, and choose a budgeted set of conversation states to cache by weighing the generation cost a reuse avoids against a price for unacceptable reuse. We propose CUCB-SC-MT, which learns a cache from logged conversations with a suboptimality guarantee, and CLCB-LS-MT, which learns online and has $\\tilde O(\\sqrt N)$ regret over $N$ requests when conversation states recur. On math-tutoring (SocraTeach) and movie-ticketing (Taskmaster-3) conversations, both achieve lower loss than prior semantic caching approaches: ContextCache, multi-turn variants of ElastiCache and vCache, and single-turn semantic caching at every cache budget. With 1,000 cache entries, CLCB-LS-MT avoids 35--41\\% of the generation cost through acceptable reuses, against at most 22--25\\% for the baselines, and lowers estimated serving latency by 19--35\\%.	arXiv:2611.01077v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Semantic caching lowers the cost of serving large language models (LLMs) by reusing stored responses for similar requests. In multi-turn conversations, whether a cached response is acceptable depends on both conversations' histories, which embedding similarity of requests and histories predicts poorly (AUC 0.62--0.74 on five workloads). We therefore learn the \\emph{reuse risk}, the probability that a cached response is unacceptable, from judge feedback, and choose a budgeted set of conversation states to cache by weighing the generation cost a reuse avoids against a price for unacceptable reuse. We propose CUCB-SC-MT, which learns a cache from logged conversations with a suboptimality guarantee, and CLCB-LS-MT, which learns online and has $\\tilde O(\\sqrt N)$ regret over $N$ requests when conversation states recur. On math-tutoring (SocraTeach) and movie-ticketing (Taskmaster-3) conversations, both achieve lower loss than prior semantic caching approaches: ContextCache, multi-turn variants of ElastiCache and vCache, and single-turn semantic caching at every cache budget. With 1,000 cache entries, CLCB-LS-MT avoids 35--41\\% of the generation cost through acceptable reuses, against at most 22--25\\% for the baselines, and lowers estimated serving latency by 19--35\\%.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.476885+00	\N	\N	7d4f84345a4b3984cdd56128ed9e329554c76746a72aa38e3a75cc492392a9d2	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.477194+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.477195+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1e2fcbb9-1feb-4be1-9ac0-b7db6b8bd307	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.01472v2	Causal Optimal-Transport Attention: Positional Priors and Length Extrapolation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01472	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01472	Ming Lian	arXiv:2611.01472v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Causal masking obstructs the column balancing used by optimal-transport (OT) attention. We study an unbalanced OT operator that uses only current and earlier rows and characterize its induced positional prior. For bounded scores and any fixed balancing strength below one, its fixed point assigns vanishing mass to every fixed window as context grows. With constant scores, the prior has an exact scale-invariant form. We classify positional encodings by whether they prevent this dilution. We also derive finite-length window-mass bounds for log-distance priors with exponents near one. Preregistered experiments use 25M- and 100M-parameter language models. The OT operator improves loss extrapolation over softmax, while exploratory comparisons favor an explicit distance bias at a fraction of the cost. Changing only the bias beyond the training length moves long-context loss in the predicted direction on all 30 tested checkpoints. During training, learned exponents approach one even when constrained above it. A margin chosen from prior calculations controls distant-mass growth in the tested setting.	arXiv:2611.01472v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Causal masking obstructs the column balancing used by optimal-transport (OT) attention. We study an unbalanced OT operator that uses only current and earlier rows and characterize its induced positional prior. For bounded scores and any fixed balancing strength below one, its fixed point assigns vanishing mass to every fixed window as context grows. With constant scores, the prior has an exact scale-invariant form. We classify positional encodings by whether they prevent this dilution. We also derive finite-length window-mass bounds for log-distance priors with exponents near one. Preregistered experiments use 25M- and 100M-parameter language models. The OT operator improves loss extrapolation over softmax, while exploratory comparisons favor an explicit distance bias at a fraction of the cost. Changing only the bias beyond the training length moves long-context loss in the predicted direction on all 30 tested checkpoints. During training, learned exponents approach one even when constrained above it. A margin chosen from prior calculations controls distant-mass growth in the tested setting.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.479273+00	\N	\N	e6c29af7ad836cbf906663c3446bb46e9e6b7a80405106780da4df29479f09a3	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.479606+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.479607+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6b6c77c9-b0b9-481a-b7a3-33ca5ab263ae	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.01666v2	Stand-In Effects and Pooling Amplification: Lessons from Biological World Models for Testing Action Effects	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01666	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01666	Bowen Xu, Boyu Chen, Jiaying Li	arXiv:2611.01666v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Whether an action-conditioned world model uses actions is usually checked by replacing or removing them and measuring lost accuracy. With biological experiments as the guiding case, we show that such diagnostics report action effects where there are none: under autocorrelated designs, future actions stand in for history the model does not see, and a better model learns the stand-in better. When the design law is known, a conditional randomization test written as an e-value is valid in finite samples whatever the model, provided the resampler conditions on the full state the design used; resampling from a simpler law reproduces the false effect. A second failure is specific to world models: when many sites share one action sequence (cells in a microscope field, pixels in a frame), the pooled score turns small model errors along the action contrast into overwhelming evidence against a true effect. In a pixel world, its median log e-value fell from $-17.3$ to $-6832$ as frames grew from 64 to 4096 pixels, while the oracle's rose. Our preregistered analysis of optogenetic ERK experiments failed this way; a tempered statistic, selected on synthetic data and registered before the data were obtained, then rejected the null on a never-opened field ($E\\approx1.1\\times10^{10}$). Tempering factors estimated from residuals can err by an order of magnitude, whereas a mixture over a fixed grid of factors needs no estimate and loses at most the logarithm of the grid size in log e-value relative to its best member. In a registered test on a diffusion world model for three Atari games released by others, the pooled test rejected in 0 of 96 units in which actions mattered and Kish and contrast tempering in 0 and 1, while the mixture rejected in 96; under an exact null, eight statistics made 0 rejections in 1,536 tests. Every analysis is preregistered, fixed in a dated protocol, or labelled exploratory.	arXiv:2611.01666v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Whether an action-conditioned world model uses actions is usually checked by replacing or removing them and measuring lost accuracy. With biological experiments as the guiding case, we show that such diagnostics report action effects where there are none: under autocorrelated designs, future actions stand in for history the model does not see, and a better model learns the stand-in better. When the design law is known, a conditional randomization test written as an e-value is valid in finite samples whatever the model, provided the resampler conditions on the full state the design used; resampling from a simpler law reproduces the false effect. A second failure is specific to world models: when many sites share one action sequence (cells in a microscope field, pixels in a frame), the pooled score turns small model errors along the action contrast into overwhelming evidence against a true effect. In a pixel world, its median log e-value fell from $-17.3$ to $-6832$ as frames grew from 64 to 4096 pixels, while the oracle's rose. Our preregistered analysis of optogenetic ERK experiments failed this way; a tempered statistic, selected on synthetic data and registered before the data were obtained, then rejected the null on a never-opened field ($E\\approx1.1\\times10^{10}$). Tempering factors estimated from residuals can err by an order of magnitude, whereas a mixture over a fixed grid of factors needs no estimate and loses at most the logarithm of the grid size in log e-value relative to its best member. In a registered test on a diffusion world model for three Atari games released by others, the pooled test rejected in 0 of 96 units in which actions mattered and Kish and contrast tempering in 0 and 1, while the mixture rejected in 96; under an exact null, eight statistics made 0 rejections in 1,536 tests. Every analysis is preregistered, fixed in a dated protocol, or labelled exploratory.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.48188+00	\N	\N	20a2319efb343c54c778fb84f13ca5adde1e1ddc35e25a6e712e0b0f1a4e04e4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.48221+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.482211+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ea629436-cecb-471d-b668-d167838612e5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.02267v2	Overcoming Demand Censoring through Randomization for Risk-Sensitive Newsvendor Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02267	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02267	Zhengchao Wang, Nam Ho-Nguyen	arXiv:2611.02267v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study online newsvendor learning with bounded demand, censored observations, and convex nonlinear shortage costs. Because stockouts can conceal the shortage magnitudes needed by gradient-based algorithms, we use randomized ordering to construct unbiased subgradient estimates. We characterize admissible randomization distributions within this framework and develop an online inventory policy that combines the resulting subgradient estimates with gradient-based updates. Our regret analysis quantifies the cost of randomization via the estimation variance and an additional loss term. We characterize the randomization distributions that minimize this objective. For piecewise-linear shortage costs, the optimal distribution is computed through a linear-time recursion; for costs with bounded curvature density, it is characterized by a scalar ordinary differential equation. The policy achieves expected regret of $O(T^{2/3})$ against the best fixed order in hindsight over $T$ periods. Under independent demand and suitable curvature of expected losses, the bound improves to $O(\\sqrt{T})$. Matching minimax lower bounds establish rate optimality over the corresponding classes with a fixed marginal-cost bound. Both rates extend to nonperishable inventory under uniformly positive conditional expected demand. Numerical experiments on synthetic and retail data show improvements over alternative policies using censored observations.	arXiv:2611.02267v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study online newsvendor learning with bounded demand, censored observations, and convex nonlinear shortage costs. Because stockouts can conceal the shortage magnitudes needed by gradient-based algorithms, we use randomized ordering to construct unbiased subgradient estimates. We characterize admissible randomization distributions within this framework and develop an online inventory policy that combines the resulting subgradient estimates with gradient-based updates. Our regret analysis quantifies the cost of randomization via the estimation variance and an additional loss term. We characterize the randomization distributions that minimize this objective. For piecewise-linear shortage costs, the optimal distribution is computed through a linear-time recursion; for costs with bounded curvature density, it is characterized by a scalar ordinary differential equation. The policy achieves expected regret of $O(T^{2/3})$ against the best fixed order in hindsight over $T$ periods. Under independent demand and suitable curvature of expected losses, the bound improves to $O(\\sqrt{T})$. Matching minimax lower bounds establish rate optimality over the corresponding classes with a fixed marginal-cost bound. Both rates extend to nonperishable inventory under uniformly positive conditional expected demand. Numerical experiments on synthetic and retail data show improvements over alternative policies using censored observations.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.487331+00	\N	\N	221a7408a504e395e5bf14d384857b39d8957bad232e6cddc0ec85c62a2d3f44	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.487738+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.48774+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6e69a825-8311-4214-b9a0-9e46e808be1e	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.03486v2	A Hybrid Visitor Prediction Framework using Interpretable Temporal features, Integrating IQR and Anomaly Detection in Lakes in Nebraska	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03486	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03486	Israt Zarin, Christopher Chizinski	arXiv:2611.03486v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: For effective resource management, infrastructure planning, and sustainable tourism, it is really important to predict accurate visitors. This paper estimates the daily visitation of Nebraska lakes using spatial, basic temporal, and interpretable temporal features. Moreover, we evaluate the base models with multiple metrics. Using the best three models, our method will ensemble hybrid models and choose the best hybrid technique. Further, we investigate the impact of lag features and how the models work with different outlier detection techniques such as Interquartile Range (IQR) and anomaly detection. The models are evaluated using regression metrics (MAE, MSE, RMSE, MAPE, and R2). Experiment results show that, after removing anomalies, MAE, MSE, RMSE, and MAPE are reduced. Adding lag features also shows some positive effects on the results. Moreover, anomaly detection techniques perform better than IQR in improving performance while preserving data. The reported anomaly-based cross-validation results favored the voting ensemble over the compared base models on the evaluated metrics. In anomaly-based cross-validation, VOTE improved prediction errors compared with MLP (256,128,64); improvements varied in the single-split comparison. Specifically, the hybrid model reduced MAE by 9.82%, MSE by 3.12%, RMSE by 1.76%, and MAPE by 6.77%. In the reported cross-validation comparison, the best anomaly-based hybrid model had lower MAE, MSE, RMSE, and MAPE than the best IQR-based hybrid model by 9.81%, 34.30%, 18.99%, and 0.66%, respectively.	arXiv:2611.03486v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: For effective resource management, infrastructure planning, and sustainable tourism, it is really important to predict accurate visitors. This paper estimates the daily visitation of Nebraska lakes using spatial, basic temporal, and interpretable temporal features. Moreover, we evaluate the base models with multiple metrics. Using the best three models, our method will ensemble hybrid models and choose the best hybrid technique. Further, we investigate the impact of lag features and how the models work with different outlier detection techniques such as Interquartile Range (IQR) and anomaly detection. The models are evaluated using regression metrics (MAE, MSE, RMSE, MAPE, and R2). Experiment results show that, after removing anomalies, MAE, MSE, RMSE, and MAPE are reduced. Adding lag features also shows some positive effects on the results. Moreover, anomaly detection techniques perform better than IQR in improving performance while preserving data. The reported anomaly-based cross-validation results favored the voting ensemble over the compared base models on the evaluated metrics. In anomaly-based cross-validation, VOTE improved prediction errors compared with MLP (256,128,64); improvements varied in the single-split comparison. Specifically, the hybrid model reduced MAE by 9.82%, MSE by 3.12%, RMSE by 1.76%, and MAPE by 6.77%. In the reported cross-validation comparison, the best anomaly-based hybrid model had lower MAE, MSE, RMSE, and MAPE than the best IQR-based hybrid model by 9.81%, 34.30%, 18.99%, and 0.66%, respectively.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.490096+00	\N	\N	4ee5d86fccbc0ba0b06feaa32b5dd731011b8d83e2f16fba75f5fe48b4cd9568	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.490435+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.490436+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
07d31870-7b4b-4a03-aa71-454768082768	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.04072v2	Finite-Sample Convergence in Networked Average Reward MARL: Decentralization Pitfalls and Entropy Remedies	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04072	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04072	Yizhou Zhang, Yashaswini Murthy, Laixi Shi, Adam Wierman	arXiv:2611.04072v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study the problem of average reward Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) where agents interact within a network. Each agent in the network conducts local updates with information within its $k$-hop neighborhood and collaboratively maximizes the overall reward of the entire network. We first provide impossibility results in achieving convergence to the global optimal joint policies in our setting. These impossibility results highlight the challenges of decentralized learning with local information in multi-agent systems, namely the issues of multiagency, where each agent independently optimizes its policy, and partial observability, where agents have limited visibility into the global state. Given these challenges which indicate that decentralized policy optimization can generally be certified only up to stationarity, we provide finite-sample convergence guarantees for a decentralized actor-critic algorithm with linear function approximation that converges to an approximate stationary point with small gradient norm. To overcome such impossibility results, we further show that adding fixed-state entropy regularization reshapes the objective landscape which leads to even stronger global convergence guarantees for our proposed actor-critic algorithm.	arXiv:2611.04072v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study the problem of average reward Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) where agents interact within a network. Each agent in the network conducts local updates with information within its $k$-hop neighborhood and collaboratively maximizes the overall reward of the entire network. We first provide impossibility results in achieving convergence to the global optimal joint policies in our setting. These impossibility results highlight the challenges of decentralized learning with local information in multi-agent systems, namely the issues of multiagency, where each agent independently optimizes its policy, and partial observability, where agents have limited visibility into the global state. Given these challenges which indicate that decentralized policy optimization can generally be certified only up to stationarity, we provide finite-sample convergence guarantees for a decentralized actor-critic algorithm with linear function approximation that converges to an approximate stationary point with small gradient norm. To overcome such impossibility results, we further show that adding fixed-state entropy regularization reshapes the objective landscape which leads to even stronger global convergence guarantees for our proposed actor-critic algorithm.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.49254+00	\N	\N	aab56a1c2fa144cf0906403b2ba7c8a2a8cb41f9b074c9d1c9c86737135f56ac	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.49285+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.492851+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3d177adc-9a02-4712-8d4e-c81e0f393108	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.04571v2	Ground-Wing: A Parametric 3D Wing-in-Ground-Effect Dataset for Benchmarking Neural Surrogate Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04571	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04571	Andrea Cenzato, Lukas Karch, Andre Luckow, Yoran Pichon, Francesco Pasqua, Abouzar Ghasemi, Rishikesh Ranade, Ira Shokar, Julia Kowalski	arXiv:2611.04571v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This paper presents a CFD dataset of a highly parametrized 3D wing operating in ground effect, designed for benchmarking neural surrogate models for aerodynamic prediction, using a wing shape and flow conditions typically encountered in motorsport applications. The dataset comprises 770 RANS simulations of 194 unique wing configurations, sampled across 27 airfoil profile variants and 4 distances from the ground, spanning variations in wing sweep, chord, taper, angle of attack, and tip twist. Six neural network architectures representing distinct paradigms --- convolutional (DoMINO), graph-based (X-MeshGraphNet), transformer-based (AB-UPT, GeoTransolver, GAOT), and JEPA-based (AeroJEPA) --- are evaluated on their ability to predict surface pressure coefficient and wall shear stress distributions, from which integral drag and lift coefficients are recovered. The dataset is intended to be used as a challenging benchmark for surrogate CFD models, due to the uniqueness of the ground effect being absent from other wing and airfoil datasets. The dataset is available for download at https://huggingface.co/datasets/andreacenzato/Ground-Wing	arXiv:2611.04571v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: This paper presents a CFD dataset of a highly parametrized 3D wing operating in ground effect, designed for benchmarking neural surrogate models for aerodynamic prediction, using a wing shape and flow conditions typically encountered in motorsport applications. The dataset comprises 770 RANS simulations of 194 unique wing configurations, sampled across 27 airfoil profile variants and 4 distances from the ground, spanning variations in wing sweep, chord, taper, angle of attack, and tip twist. Six neural network architectures representing distinct paradigms --- convolutional (DoMINO), graph-based (X-MeshGraphNet), transformer-based (AB-UPT, GeoTransolver, GAOT), and JEPA-based (AeroJEPA) --- are evaluated on their ability to predict surface pressure coefficient and wall shear stress distributions, from which integral drag and lift coefficients are recovered. The dataset is intended to be used as a challenging benchmark for surrogate CFD models, due to the uniqueness of the ground effect being absent from other wing and airfoil datasets. The dataset is available for download at https://huggingface.co/datasets/andreacenzato/Ground-Wing	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.494864+00	\N	\N	d5dc2861e25dc45e2f05f5f4ac48e715ed840a05b9a9198d268b93da3c67d04f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.495185+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.495186+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
df74419d-26c1-431e-9ddb-8d4aece0c8c1	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.04614v2	Policy Network Diffusion	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04614	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04614	Jiate Liu, Deheng Ye	arXiv:2611.04614v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Diffusion models have shown remarkable progress in AI-generated content, including images and videos. In this paper, we explore whether diffusion models can generate the parameters of policy networks in reinforcement learning (RL), and whether the generated policies exhibit generalization capabilities. To achieve this, we propose Diffusion-based Conditional Policy Network Parameter Generation (DPPG), which consists of a Transformer-based autoencoder and a task-conditioned diffusion model. The autoencoder achieves a high compression rate by patchifying network parameters, while the task-conditioned diffusion model generates corresponding parameters conditioned on different environments. Our experiments demonstrate that, with a single unified DPPG model, we can synthesize high-quality policies across a diverse set of tasks from different environment domains, including Classic Control, Atari games, and MuJoCo. Additionally, our method can generate networks with a larger number of parameters compared to previous approaches. Furthermore, we find that in previously unseen environments, DPPG can generate parameters that achieve faster convergence and reach competitive performance, showcasing the potential future applications of parameter generation via diffusion.	arXiv:2611.04614v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Diffusion models have shown remarkable progress in AI-generated content, including images and videos. In this paper, we explore whether diffusion models can generate the parameters of policy networks in reinforcement learning (RL), and whether the generated policies exhibit generalization capabilities. To achieve this, we propose Diffusion-based Conditional Policy Network Parameter Generation (DPPG), which consists of a Transformer-based autoencoder and a task-conditioned diffusion model. The autoencoder achieves a high compression rate by patchifying network parameters, while the task-conditioned diffusion model generates corresponding parameters conditioned on different environments. Our experiments demonstrate that, with a single unified DPPG model, we can synthesize high-quality policies across a diverse set of tasks from different environment domains, including Classic Control, Atari games, and MuJoCo. Additionally, our method can generate networks with a larger number of parameters compared to previous approaches. Furthermore, we find that in previously unseen environments, DPPG can generate parameters that achieve faster convergence and reach competitive performance, showcasing the potential future applications of parameter generation via diffusion.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.497429+00	\N	\N	37605f68319bd524a476801eccec033c7551055b1aa171342f683c9c56270a87	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.497764+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.497766+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
7fcbc993-4b90-490f-b11f-5a8002777ba3	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.06700v2	PINR-ESI: Physics-Informed Self-Supervised Implicit Neural Representation Framework for EEG Extended-Source Imaging	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06700	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06700	Zhiyong Zhou, Sheng Liao, Xiaolin Li, Youshen Xiao, Bin Dai, Surui Liu, Yakang Dai	arXiv:2611.06700v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: EEG extended-source imaging aims to localize active cortical regions and characterize their spatial extent, yet supervised inverse networks typically depend on synthetic EEG-source pairs and may be sensitive to synthetic-to-real mismatch. We propose a physics-informed self-supervised implicit neural representation (INR) framework for spatial source reconstruction without paired source labels or simulation-based supervised pretraining. The lead field matrix encodes the source-to-sensor volume-conduction model and constrains the INR through agreement between predicted and measured EEG. Cortical current density is modeled as a continuous coordinate-conditioned field. A classical inverse estimate provides warm initialization and spatial constraints on source activity, while patch-integrated projection maps the field to discrete source coefficients for forward modeling. Simulations across varying noise levels, source extents, and source counts showed consistently low spatial dispersion and good localization performance. Quantitative and qualitative evaluations using real EEG recordings from epilepsy patients and intracranial stimulation experiments further supported the feasibility of the framework for source imaging. These findings suggest the potential of physics-informed self-supervised INR for extended-source imaging.	arXiv:2611.06700v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: EEG extended-source imaging aims to localize active cortical regions and characterize their spatial extent, yet supervised inverse networks typically depend on synthetic EEG-source pairs and may be sensitive to synthetic-to-real mismatch. We propose a physics-informed self-supervised implicit neural representation (INR) framework for spatial source reconstruction without paired source labels or simulation-based supervised pretraining. The lead field matrix encodes the source-to-sensor volume-conduction model and constrains the INR through agreement between predicted and measured EEG. Cortical current density is modeled as a continuous coordinate-conditioned field. A classical inverse estimate provides warm initialization and spatial constraints on source activity, while patch-integrated projection maps the field to discrete source coefficients for forward modeling. Simulations across varying noise levels, source extents, and source counts showed consistently low spatial dispersion and good localization performance. Quantitative and qualitative evaluations using real EEG recordings from epilepsy patients and intracranial stimulation experiments further supported the feasibility of the framework for source imaging. These findings suggest the potential of physics-informed self-supervised INR for extended-source imaging.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.514479+00	\N	\N	8c11be6be1369c13e6a3926c65b8b67a9b7ebe35ae70339961d23e70781b1758	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.514848+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.514849+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2ebb4c56-5fbd-44dc-b909-633c4dc6b6f8	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/adventhealth	AdventHealth advances whole-person care with OpenAI	https://openai.com/index/adventhealth	https://openai.com/index/adventhealth	\N	AdventHealth is using ChatGPT for Healthcare to streamline workflows, reduce administrative burden, and return more time to patient care.	AdventHealth is using ChatGPT for Healthcare to streamline workflows, reduce administrative burden, and return more time to patient care.	product_update	en	2026-05-21 12:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.727577+00	3	\N	b1076a7155abc642e6b07bbf3698759c534b8a5e8ab7f4a0b3a38bc1ff50eb9f	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.728075+00	2026-11-10 14:24:06.347012+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e1e30748-c4d7-4e7b-9144-22b6ba3c88d4	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2211.10351v2	Deep learning for structural health monitoring: An application to heritage structures	https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10351	https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10351	Fabio Carrara, Fabrizio Falchi, Maria Girardi, Nicola Messina, Cristina Padovani, Daniele Pellegrini	arXiv:2211.10351v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Thanks to recent advancements in numerical methods, computer power, and monitoring technology, seismic ambient noise provides precious information about the structural behavior of old buildings. The measurement of the vibrations produced by anthropic and environmental sources and their use for dynamic identification and structural health monitoring of buildings initiated an emerging, cross-disciplinary field engaging seismologists, engineers, mathematicians, and computer scientists. In this work, we employ recent deep learning techniques for time-series forecasting to inspect and detect anomalies in the large dataset recorded during a long-term monitoring campaign conducted on the San Frediano bell tower in Lucca. We frame the problem as an unsupervised anomaly detection task and train a Temporal Fusion Transformer to learn the normal dynamics of the structure. We then detect the anomalies by looking at the differences between the predicted and observed frequencies.	arXiv:2211.10351v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Thanks to recent advancements in numerical methods, computer power, and monitoring technology, seismic ambient noise provides precious information about the structural behavior of old buildings. The measurement of the vibrations produced by anthropic and environmental sources and their use for dynamic identification and structural health monitoring of buildings initiated an emerging, cross-disciplinary field engaging seismologists, engineers, mathematicians, and computer scientists. In this work, we employ recent deep learning techniques for time-series forecasting to inspect and detect anomalies in the large dataset recorded during a long-term monitoring campaign conducted on the San Frediano bell tower in Lucca. We frame the problem as an unsupervised anomaly detection task and train a Temporal Fusion Transformer to learn the normal dynamics of the structure. We then detect the anomalies by looking at the differences between the predicted and observed frequencies.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.540291+00	\N	\N	4040763aaddb246c3ab07311201222bd989e46f04e0e30debe0498413bec1ef4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.540617+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.540619+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
dc8bf64f-31bc-4404-bd0c-adac16fc1c43	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.07998v2	Towards Multiscale Molecular Representation Learning through Geometry-Based Fragmentation for Polymer Property Prediction	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07998	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07998	Makoto Takamoto, Chaitanya Sampara, Viktor Zaverkin, Daniel O\\~noro-Rubio, Mathias Niepert	arXiv:2611.07998v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: It is well established that molecular substructures can contribute unequally to chemical and physical properties, with specific motifs or side chains often playing dominant roles. Fragment-based representations have therefore emerged as an effective strategy for incorporating chemically meaningful inductive biases, as exemplified by molecular fingerprints and fragment-based attention mechanisms. However, many existing fragmentation-based machine learning methods rely on predefined chemical rules, which may yield suboptimal fragmentation for previously unseen or chemically unfamiliar structures and often overlook the importance of multiscale molecular representations. To address these limitations, we formulate molecular fragmentation as a radius-constrained optimization problem inspired by the k-center formulation, where the number of clusters is not fixed a priori but emerges from the specified radius, and propose a geometry-based fragmentation method that partitions arbitrary molecular or material structures using largely geometric information, without relying on predefined chemical fragmentation rules. The method provides flexible control over fragment size, enabling the construction of multiscale fragment representations for scale-adaptive modeling. Building on this framework, we develop a geometric fragment-based attention mechanism that integrates multiscale structural information and incorporate it into a multimodal machine learning model combining p-SMILES representations with three-dimensional (3D) conformational information. Experiments on a benchmark polymer dataset show that incorporating 3D conformational information improves prediction accuracy for several properties over models based exclusively on p-SMILES representations. Explainable artificial intelligence analyses further provide insights into the physical relevance of the learned representations.	arXiv:2611.07998v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: It is well established that molecular substructures can contribute unequally to chemical and physical properties, with specific motifs or side chains often playing dominant roles. Fragment-based representations have therefore emerged as an effective strategy for incorporating chemically meaningful inductive biases, as exemplified by molecular fingerprints and fragment-based attention mechanisms. However, many existing fragmentation-based machine learning methods rely on predefined chemical rules, which may yield suboptimal fragmentation for previously unseen or chemically unfamiliar structures and often overlook the importance of multiscale molecular representations. To address these limitations, we formulate molecular fragmentation as a radius-constrained optimization problem inspired by the k-center formulation, where the number of clusters is not fixed a priori but emerges from the specified radius, and propose a geometry-based fragmentation method that partitions arbitrary molecular or material structures using largely geometric information, without relying on predefined chemical fragmentation rules. The method provides flexible control over fragment size, enabling the construction of multiscale fragment representations for scale-adaptive modeling. Building on this framework, we develop a geometric fragment-based attention mechanism that integrates multiscale structural information and incorporate it into a multimodal machine learning model combining p-SMILES representations with three-dimensional (3D) conformational information. Experiments on a benchmark polymer dataset show that incorporating 3D conformational information improves prediction accuracy for several properties over models based exclusively on p-SMILES representations. Explainable artificial intelligence analyses further provide insights into the physical relevance of the learned representations.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.529552+00	\N	\N	9954cb6a4f5fd089598e83123170ecd6292701b805bb513426fa48d5ff952ae2	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.529891+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.529892+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
fe5b2686-5610-4650-9d8d-e2e1206abbd6	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08136v2	How Far Can Untrained Neural Networks Go in Anomaly Detection? From Tables to Pixels	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08136	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08136	Hyojun Yi, Geunyoung Kim, Hogeon Seo, Seunghyoung Ryu	arXiv:2611.08136v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Anomaly detection (AD) is often framed as a representation-learning problem, where strong performance is expected to require learned features or AD-specific optimization. However, this view can overlook the intrinsic separability of anomalies from normal references, even in the absence of learned representations. We therefore investigate how far untrained neural networks can go in anomaly detection. To this end, we propose PRISM, a framework combining fixed random nonlinear features with normal-reference Mahalanobis scoring for tabular detection and dense visual localization. Across 47 tabular datasets in ADBench, PRISM-T achieves the highest mean AUROC (86.4%) and the second-highest mean AUPRC (65.9%) among the compared methods. On MVTec AD and VisA, covering 27 visual categories, PRISM-V achieves mean image-level and pixel-level AUROCs of 90.4% and 96.4%, respectively. Notably, both models achieve these results without neural weight optimization or pretrained representations. A theoretical analysis further characterizes how nonlinear transformations affect the mean and covariance contributions to the Mahalanobis score gap, while empirical analyses examine the resulting score separability. These findings establish PRISM as both a competitive anomaly detector and a prior-to-learning baseline for assessing the added value of learned representations in anomaly detection.	arXiv:2611.08136v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Anomaly detection (AD) is often framed as a representation-learning problem, where strong performance is expected to require learned features or AD-specific optimization. However, this view can overlook the intrinsic separability of anomalies from normal references, even in the absence of learned representations. We therefore investigate how far untrained neural networks can go in anomaly detection. To this end, we propose PRISM, a framework combining fixed random nonlinear features with normal-reference Mahalanobis scoring for tabular detection and dense visual localization. Across 47 tabular datasets in ADBench, PRISM-T achieves the highest mean AUROC (86.4%) and the second-highest mean AUPRC (65.9%) among the compared methods. On MVTec AD and VisA, covering 27 visual categories, PRISM-V achieves mean image-level and pixel-level AUROCs of 90.4% and 96.4%, respectively. Notably, both models achieve these results without neural weight optimization or pretrained representations. A theoretical analysis further characterizes how nonlinear transformations affect the mean and covariance contributions to the Mahalanobis score gap, while empirical analyses examine the resulting score separability. These findings establish PRISM as both a competitive anomaly detector and a prior-to-learning baseline for assessing the added value of learned representations in anomaly detection.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.532293+00	\N	\N	cffc9a0ed47806e5f1103ad43b257d00fa9b860b00c96b1cd797816355db75e6	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.532619+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.53262+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9460ea89-0ad8-4a55-b955-a7f350247753	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08420v2	On the limitations of molecular representations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08420	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08420	Acer Blake, Charlotte M. Deane, Fergus Imrie	arXiv:2611.08420v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Molecular representations such as fingerprints and learned embeddings play a central role in cheminformatics and small-molecule machine learning. They serve as the primary input for a diverse array of tasks, including clustering, dataset splitting, and virtual screening, yet their evaluation is often limited to downstream model performance on narrow benchmark tasks. We carried out a systematic investigation of the quality of molecular representations, finding that none of the state-of-the-art molecular embedding models capture graph structure, 3D shape, or ADMET endpoint similarity better than fingerprints or physicochemical descriptors. However, classical fingerprints also revealed their own material limitations. For example, we show that depending on the representation, between 225M and $\\sim$4B molecules have non-unique representations in the 10.8B Enamine REAL database, with implications for virtual screening. Our findings provide an insight into the current limitations of molecular representations, outlining future research directions for the development of more robust encodings of small molecules.	arXiv:2611.08420v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Molecular representations such as fingerprints and learned embeddings play a central role in cheminformatics and small-molecule machine learning. They serve as the primary input for a diverse array of tasks, including clustering, dataset splitting, and virtual screening, yet their evaluation is often limited to downstream model performance on narrow benchmark tasks. We carried out a systematic investigation of the quality of molecular representations, finding that none of the state-of-the-art molecular embedding models capture graph structure, 3D shape, or ADMET endpoint similarity better than fingerprints or physicochemical descriptors. However, classical fingerprints also revealed their own material limitations. For example, we show that depending on the representation, between 225M and $\\sim$4B molecules have non-unique representations in the 10.8B Enamine REAL database, with implications for virtual screening. Our findings provide an insight into the current limitations of molecular representations, outlining future research directions for the development of more robust encodings of small molecules.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.534578+00	\N	\N	08550b40ba66cd8a90bfba2215f202b604d98616e7e63b9aef3e2329736a2c02	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.534912+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.534914+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
df8d0b0f-45a1-4bb8-9469-482c00bc60fa	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.08487v2	User Embeddings are Superpositions of Interpretable Behavioral Modes	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08487	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08487	Jonathan Kozaczuk, Zheng Dong, Noyan Evirgen, Keivan Majidi, Eddie K. H. Ng, Chris Porter, Qi Zhao, Jia Geng, Changmao Li, Brandon R. Feng	arXiv:2611.08487v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: User embeddings are central components of modern recommendation, personalization, and behavioral modeling systems. Though these embeddings can compactly encode rich behavioral information, they are inherently dense and opaque, limiting interpretability. To address this gap, we argue that user embeddings can be understood as sparse superpositions of interpretable behavioral modes. This structure is directly predicted by generative models of user behavior for linear embeddings and extends approximately to deep-learning representations by the superposition hypothesis. Superimposed modes can naturally be recovered by sparse dictionary learning methods, such as sparse autoencoders (SAEs), and recovered modes can be automatically named and queried in natural language. We validate mode recovery against known ground truth on synthetic data, where SAEs recover modes better than classical sparse baselines. We then apply our methods to classical and transformer-based user embeddings from two real-world datasets. We also show how the corresponding natural-language labels enable zero-shot audience retrieval and characterization over embeddings never trained on text. The framework is post-hoc and model-agnostic, requiring no modification to the upstream embedding system.	arXiv:2611.08487v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: User embeddings are central components of modern recommendation, personalization, and behavioral modeling systems. Though these embeddings can compactly encode rich behavioral information, they are inherently dense and opaque, limiting interpretability. To address this gap, we argue that user embeddings can be understood as sparse superpositions of interpretable behavioral modes. This structure is directly predicted by generative models of user behavior for linear embeddings and extends approximately to deep-learning representations by the superposition hypothesis. Superimposed modes can naturally be recovered by sparse dictionary learning methods, such as sparse autoencoders (SAEs), and recovered modes can be automatically named and queried in natural language. We validate mode recovery against known ground truth on synthetic data, where SAEs recover modes better than classical sparse baselines. We then apply our methods to classical and transformer-based user embeddings from two real-world datasets. We also show how the corresponding natural-language labels enable zero-shot audience retrieval and characterization over embeddings never trained on text. The framework is post-hoc and model-agnostic, requiring no modification to the upstream embedding system.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.536993+00	\N	\N	618b4be515993b890dd18ca488ac622dd0cf8594e65fc1ba7f4527b3fd811254	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.537375+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.537377+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e7a3cad7-67a1-41c3-bf49-ea8cd526ecbf	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2401.07174v2	On the (In)Compatibility between Group Fairness and Individual Fairness	https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.07174	https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.07174	Shizhou Xu, Thomas Strohmer	arXiv:2401.07174v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study the compatibility between utility-optimized statistical parity and individual fairness. While individual fairness seeks to treat similar individuals similarly, optimal statistical parity aims to provide similar treatment to individuals who share relative similarity within their respective sensitive groups. Although both fairness perspectives are desirable, they often conflict in practical applications. Our goal in this work is to analyze the existence of this conflict and its potential solution. In particular, we establish sufficient (sharp) conditions for the compatibility between the optimal (post-processing) statistical parity $L^2$ learning and the ($K$-Lipschitz or $(\\epsilon,\\delta)$) individual fairness requirements. When a conflict arises, we first relax the optimal statistical parity solution to the Pareto frontier, representing the optimal trade-off between $L^2$ error and statistical disparity, and then examine its compatibility with individual fairness. Our analysis identifies regions along the Pareto frontier that satisfy individual fairness constraints. Additionally, we provide individual fairness guarantees for the composition of a trained model with the optimal post-processing step, enabling practitioners to assess the compatibility of post-processed models. This provides practitioners with a principled approach to achieving Pareto-optimal statistical parity while maintaining individual fairness constraints.	arXiv:2401.07174v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study the compatibility between utility-optimized statistical parity and individual fairness. While individual fairness seeks to treat similar individuals similarly, optimal statistical parity aims to provide similar treatment to individuals who share relative similarity within their respective sensitive groups. Although both fairness perspectives are desirable, they often conflict in practical applications. Our goal in this work is to analyze the existence of this conflict and its potential solution. In particular, we establish sufficient (sharp) conditions for the compatibility between the optimal (post-processing) statistical parity $L^2$ learning and the ($K$-Lipschitz or $(\\epsilon,\\delta)$) individual fairness requirements. When a conflict arises, we first relax the optimal statistical parity solution to the Pareto frontier, representing the optimal trade-off between $L^2$ error and statistical disparity, and then examine its compatibility with individual fairness. Our analysis identifies regions along the Pareto frontier that satisfy individual fairness constraints. Additionally, we provide individual fairness guarantees for the composition of a trained model with the optimal post-processing step, enabling practitioners to assess the compatibility of post-processed models. This provides practitioners with a principled approach to achieving Pareto-optimal statistical parity while maintaining individual fairness constraints.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.545518+00	\N	\N	b610205398c55f4873947f6bc1f6af6bd6dc8484ba953e3b6aa4c0fee64da0bd	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.545924+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.545926+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5c656a61-0e93-4d80-8985-9fc3128e66e0	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2410.02504v3	Dual Active Learning for Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback	https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02504	https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02504	Pangpang Liu, Chengchun Shi, Will Wei Sun	arXiv:2410.02504v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Aligning large language models (LLMs) with human preferences is critical to recent advances in generative artificial intelligence. Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) is widely applied to achieve this objective. A key step in RLHF is to learn the reward function from human feedback. However, human feedback is costly and time-consuming, making it essential to collect high-quality conversation data for human teachers to label. Additionally, different human teachers have different levels of expertise. It is thus critical to query the most appropriate teacher for their opinions. In this paper, we use reinforcement learning (RL) to formulate the alignment problem. Motivated by the idea of $D$-optimal design, we first propose a dual active reward learning algorithm for the simultaneous selection of conversations and teachers. Next, we apply pessimistic RL to solve the alignment problem, based on the learned reward estimator. Theoretically, we show that the reward estimator obtained through our proposed adaptive selection strategy achieves minimal generalized variance asymptotically, and prove that the sub-optimality of our pessimistic policy scales as $O(1/\\sqrt{T})$ with a given sample budget $T$. Through simulations and experiments on LLMs, including annotator-level preference data and downstream Best-of-$N$ response-selection evaluations, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our algorithm and its superiority over existing baselines.	arXiv:2410.02504v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Aligning large language models (LLMs) with human preferences is critical to recent advances in generative artificial intelligence. Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) is widely applied to achieve this objective. A key step in RLHF is to learn the reward function from human feedback. However, human feedback is costly and time-consuming, making it essential to collect high-quality conversation data for human teachers to label. Additionally, different human teachers have different levels of expertise. It is thus critical to query the most appropriate teacher for their opinions. In this paper, we use reinforcement learning (RL) to formulate the alignment problem. Motivated by the idea of $D$-optimal design, we first propose a dual active reward learning algorithm for the simultaneous selection of conversations and teachers. Next, we apply pessimistic RL to solve the alignment problem, based on the learned reward estimator. Theoretically, we show that the reward estimator obtained through our proposed adaptive selection strategy achieves minimal generalized variance asymptotically, and prove that the sub-optimality of our pessimistic policy scales as $O(1/\\sqrt{T})$ with a given sample budget $T$. Through simulations and experiments on LLMs, including annotator-level preference data and downstream Best-of-$N$ response-selection evaluations, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our algorithm and its superiority over existing baselines.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.548033+00	\N	\N	ac3f72fa645e6f047a84d9a46703c4c1ae9b9a4227ae4e03d7798fdb3869c37f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.548351+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.548352+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1e07d4c1-77d0-4047-9f50-8488b8538421	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2506.15018v3	Private Continual Counting of Unbounded Streams	https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15018	https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15018	Ben Jacobsen, Kassem Fawaz	arXiv:2506.15018v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study the problem of differentially private continual counting in the unbounded setting where the input size $n$ is not known in advance. Current state-of-the-art algorithms based on optimal instantiations of the matrix mechanism cannot be directly applied here because their privacy guarantees only hold when key parameters are tuned to $n$. Using the common `doubling trick' avoids knowledge of $n$ but leads to suboptimal and non-smooth error. We solve this problem by introducing novel matrix factorizations based on logarithmic perturbations of the function $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-z}}$ studied in prior works, which may be of independent interest. The resulting algorithm has smooth error, and for any $\\alpha > 0$ and $t\\leq n$, it is able to privately estimate the sum of the first $t$ data points with $O(\\log^{2}(t)(\\log\\log t)^{1+2\\alpha})$ variance. It requires $O(t)$ space and amortized $O(\\log t)$ time per round, compared to $O(\\log(n)\\log(t))$ variance, $O(n)$ space and $O(n \\log n)$ pre-processing time for the nearly-optimal bounded-input algorithm of Henzinger et al. Empirically, we find that our algorithm's performance is also comparable to theirs in absolute terms: with optimized parameters, our variance is less than $2\\times$ theirs for $t$ as large as $2^{32}$.	arXiv:2506.15018v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study the problem of differentially private continual counting in the unbounded setting where the input size $n$ is not known in advance. Current state-of-the-art algorithms based on optimal instantiations of the matrix mechanism cannot be directly applied here because their privacy guarantees only hold when key parameters are tuned to $n$. Using the common `doubling trick' avoids knowledge of $n$ but leads to suboptimal and non-smooth error. We solve this problem by introducing novel matrix factorizations based on logarithmic perturbations of the function $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-z}}$ studied in prior works, which may be of independent interest. The resulting algorithm has smooth error, and for any $\\alpha > 0$ and $t\\leq n$, it is able to privately estimate the sum of the first $t$ data points with $O(\\log^{2}(t)(\\log\\log t)^{1+2\\alpha})$ variance. It requires $O(t)$ space and amortized $O(\\log t)$ time per round, compared to $O(\\log(n)\\log(t))$ variance, $O(n)$ space and $O(n \\log n)$ pre-processing time for the nearly-optimal bounded-input algorithm of Henzinger et al. Empirically, we find that our algorithm's performance is also comparable to theirs in absolute terms: with optimized parameters, our variance is less than $2\\times$ theirs for $t$ as large as $2^{32}$.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.587629+00	\N	\N	59a355b8569ec504b6b518340baf5e30013f5c392fbf62b154014a0675c07148	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.587998+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.588+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8b482679-5a48-4763-a254-89fc4cdf520b	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2506.15643v4	Understanding Randomization in Greedy Model Search	https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15643	https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15643	Xin Chen, Jason M. Klusowski, Yan Shuo Tan, Chang Yu	arXiv:2506.15643v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study feature subsampling in greedy model search, a randomization mechanism motivated by random forests and other ensemble methods. Although recent theory draws an analogy between this randomization and ridge regularization, these results largely rely on base learners optimized via ordinary least squares (OLS). We therefore investigate the effects of feature subsampling on greedy forward selection, a tractable abstraction of the adaptive split search used by decision trees. Assuming an orthogonal design, we prove that, under suitable conditions, ensembling with a fixed feature subsampling size can reduce expected training error without increasing degrees of freedom, thereby reducing fixed design prediction error. We also show that both the training error and degrees of freedom need not be monotone in the subsampling rate, breaking the analogy with standard shrinkage methods like the lasso or ridge regression under orthogonality. Furthermore, we characterize the exact asymptotic behavior of the estimator, showing that it adaptively reweights OLS coefficients based on their rank, with weights that are bounded by explicit logistic functions.	arXiv:2506.15643v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We study feature subsampling in greedy model search, a randomization mechanism motivated by random forests and other ensemble methods. Although recent theory draws an analogy between this randomization and ridge regularization, these results largely rely on base learners optimized via ordinary least squares (OLS). We therefore investigate the effects of feature subsampling on greedy forward selection, a tractable abstraction of the adaptive split search used by decision trees. Assuming an orthogonal design, we prove that, under suitable conditions, ensembling with a fixed feature subsampling size can reduce expected training error without increasing degrees of freedom, thereby reducing fixed design prediction error. We also show that both the training error and degrees of freedom need not be monotone in the subsampling rate, breaking the analogy with standard shrinkage methods like the lasso or ridge regression under orthogonality. Furthermore, we characterize the exact asymptotic behavior of the estimator, showing that it adaptively reweights OLS coefficients based on their rank, with weights that are bounded by explicit logistic functions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.590173+00	\N	\N	cc252a2c3c327c342203a1b704ae91e47975dea6df623ff016e7100b496ca278	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.590496+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.590498+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4233d973-facb-46c4-89d4-ca203073c0d2	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2508.00110v3	funOCLUST: Clustering Functional Data with Outliers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00110	https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00110	Katharine M. Clark, Paul D. McNicholas	arXiv:2508.00110v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Functional data present unique challenges for clustering due to their infinite-dimensional nature and potential sensitivity to outliers. An extension of the OCLUST algorithm to the functional setting is proposed to address these issues. The approach leverages the OCLUST framework, creating a robust method to cluster curves and trim outliers. The methodology is evaluated on both simulated and real-world functional datasets, demonstrating strong performance in clustering and outlier identification.	arXiv:2508.00110v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Functional data present unique challenges for clustering due to their infinite-dimensional nature and potential sensitivity to outliers. An extension of the OCLUST algorithm to the functional setting is proposed to address these issues. The approach leverages the OCLUST framework, creating a robust method to cluster curves and trim outliers. The methodology is evaluated on both simulated and real-world functional datasets, demonstrating strong performance in clustering and outlier identification.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.597681+00	\N	\N	c7e8bbb5c583e10504c56e6857d76fae3a844d33d0e8d03722b168c6733af190	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.598115+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.598117+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8aca5e23-e34a-4c6c-90d1-fb583abdd863	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2509.16801v2	Sublinear Time Quantum Sensitivity Sampling	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16801	https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16801	Zhao Song, David P. Woodruff, Lichen Zhang	arXiv:2509.16801v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We present a unified quantum sensitivity sampling framework that streamlines coreset construction for many classical approximation problems, significantly speeding up clustering, regression, and low-rank approximation. We assume $n,d\\geq\\mathrm{poly}(k/\\epsilon)$ and, for regression, $n\\geq\\mathrm{poly}(d)$:\n  - $k$-median/$k$-means: For $n$ points in $\\mathbb{R}^d$, we build an $\\epsilon$-coreset of input points of size $\\tilde O(k^{4/3}\\epsilon^{-2})$ (resp. $\\tilde O(k^{3/2}\\epsilon^{-2})$) in time $\\tilde O(\\epsilon^{-1}n^{0.5}dk^{1.67})$ (resp. $\\tilde O(\\epsilon^{-1}n^{0.5}dk^{1.75})$). Our better $d$- and linear $1/\\epsilon$-dependence improve on [Xue, Chen, Li and Jiang, ICML'23] for $d\\geq\\Omega(k^{7/3})$ (resp. $\\Omega(k^{2.5})$).\n  - $\\ell_p$ regression: For $\\min_x\\|Ax-b\\|_p$, $A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$, we build an $\\epsilon$-coreset of size $\\tilde O_p(d^{\\max\\{1,p/2\\}}\\epsilon^{-2})$ in time $\\tilde O_p(n^{0.5}d^{1.5}\\epsilon^{-1})$ for $p\\leq 2$ and $\\tilde O_p(n^{0.5}d^{p/4+1}(\\epsilon^{-1}+d^{0.5}))$ for $p>2$, matching [Liu and Ji, ICML'26] for $p\\leq 2$ and improving on the prior best quantum sampling approach [Apers and Gribling, SICOMP'26] for all $p>2$.\n  - Low-rank approximation: We give the first quantum sublinear-time algorithm for Frobenius-norm rank-$k$ approximation of $A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times n}$ free of data-dependent parameters, running in $\\tilde O(n^{0.5}dk^{0.5}\\epsilon^{-0.5})$ time with factored output (plus $\\tilde O(nk^2\\epsilon^{-2})$ to materialize them). We also give sublinear algorithms for kernel and tensor variants.\n  - Lower bound: For generic sensitivity sampling with total sensitivity $S$, any quantum algorithm outputting an $\\epsilon$-coreset of size $o(\\sqrt{nS})$ needs $\\Omega(\\sqrt{nS})$ queries, so our query complexity is optimal up to $\\mathrm{poly}(\\epsilon^{-1},\\log n)$ and VC-dimension factors.	arXiv:2509.16801v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We present a unified quantum sensitivity sampling framework that streamlines coreset construction for many classical approximation problems, significantly speeding up clustering, regression, and low-rank approximation. We assume $n,d\\geq\\mathrm{poly}(k/\\epsilon)$ and, for regression, $n\\geq\\mathrm{poly}(d)$:\n  - $k$-median/$k$-means: For $n$ points in $\\mathbb{R}^d$, we build an $\\epsilon$-coreset of input points of size $\\tilde O(k^{4/3}\\epsilon^{-2})$ (resp. $\\tilde O(k^{3/2}\\epsilon^{-2})$) in time $\\tilde O(\\epsilon^{-1}n^{0.5}dk^{1.67})$ (resp. $\\tilde O(\\epsilon^{-1}n^{0.5}dk^{1.75})$). Our better $d$- and linear $1/\\epsilon$-dependence improve on [Xue, Chen, Li and Jiang, ICML'23] for $d\\geq\\Omega(k^{7/3})$ (resp. $\\Omega(k^{2.5})$).\n  - $\\ell_p$ regression: For $\\min_x\\|Ax-b\\|_p$, $A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$, we build an $\\epsilon$-coreset of size $\\tilde O_p(d^{\\max\\{1,p/2\\}}\\epsilon^{-2})$ in time $\\tilde O_p(n^{0.5}d^{1.5}\\epsilon^{-1})$ for $p\\leq 2$ and $\\tilde O_p(n^{0.5}d^{p/4+1}(\\epsilon^{-1}+d^{0.5}))$ for $p>2$, matching [Liu and Ji, ICML'26] for $p\\leq 2$ and improving on the prior best quantum sampling approach [Apers and Gribling, SICOMP'26] for all $p>2$.\n  - Low-rank approximation: We give the first quantum sublinear-time algorithm for Frobenius-norm rank-$k$ approximation of $A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times n}$ free of data-dependent parameters, running in $\\tilde O(n^{0.5}dk^{0.5}\\epsilon^{-0.5})$ time with factored output (plus $\\tilde O(nk^2\\epsilon^{-2})$ to materialize them). We also give sublinear algorithms for kernel and tensor variants.\n  - Lower bound: For generic sensitivity sam