107868a4-e858-4368-acb1-5392ea3885a5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.30212v2	Anchored Extra-Proximal Methods: Optimal Higher-Order Methods for Monotone Inclusion Problems	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30212	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30212	Ruichen Jiang, TaeHo Yoon	arXiv:2609.30212v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We characterize, up to logarithmic factors, the optimal higher-order oracle complexity of finding approximate solutions to composite monotone inclusion problems, formed by the sum of a smooth single-valued monotone operator and a maximal monotone set-valued operator. We introduce the Anchored Extra-Proximal (AEP) framework, which combines an anchored extrapolation step with an inexact anchored proximal update satisfying a relative-error condition. This framework recovers the composite Fast Extragradient method in the first-order setting and yields natural higher-order extensions through Taylor approximations of the smooth operator at the extrapolated point. For every $p\\geq 2$, assuming that the $(p-1)$th derivative of the single-valued operator is $L_p$-Lipschitz continuous and the initial distance to a solution is at most $R$, we combine AEP with a bisection line search to obtain a $p$th-order method that attains a tangent residual of at most $\\varepsilon$ in $\\widetilde{O}((L_p R^{p}/\\varepsilon)^{\\frac{2}{3p-1}})$ oracle calls. We complement this result with a worst-case lower bound of $\\Omega((L_p R^p/\\varepsilon)^{\\frac{2}{3p-1}})$ for every deterministic algorithm in the $p$th-order oracle model, without imposing any prescribed update structure. For the subclass of smooth nonexpansive fixed-point problems, we further establish a lower bound of $\\Omega(\\min\\{R/\\varepsilon, {(L_p R^{p}/\\varepsilon)}^{\\frac{2}{3p-1}}\\})$. By choosing between AEP and Halpern iteration, we match this lower bound up to logarithmic factors, thereby also settling the deterministic oracle complexity of smooth nonexpansive fixed-point problems.	arXiv:2609.30212v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We characterize, up to logarithmic factors, the optimal higher-order oracle complexity of finding approximate solutions to composite monotone inclusion problems, formed by the sum of a smooth single-valued monotone operator and a maximal monotone set-valued operator. We introduce the Anchored Extra-Proximal (AEP) framework, which combines an anchored extrapolation step with an inexact anchored proximal update satisfying a relative-error condition. This framework recovers the composite Fast Extragradient method in the first-order setting and yields natural higher-order extensions through Taylor approximations of the smooth operator at the extrapolated point. For every $p\\geq 2$, assuming that the $(p-1)$th derivative of the single-valued operator is $L_p$-Lipschitz continuous and the initial distance to a solution is at most $R$, we combine AEP with a bisection line search to obtain a $p$th-order method that attains a tangent residual of at most $\\varepsilon$ in $\\widetilde{O}((L_p R^{p}/\\varepsilon)^{\\frac{2}{3p-1}})$ oracle calls. We complement this result with a worst-case lower bound of $\\Omega((L_p R^p/\\varepsilon)^{\\frac{2}{3p-1}})$ for every deterministic algorithm in the $p$th-order oracle model, without imposing any prescribed update structure. For the subclass of smooth nonexpansive fixed-point problems, we further establish a lower bound of $\\Omega(\\min\\{R/\\varepsilon, {(L_p R^{p}/\\varepsilon)}^{\\frac{2}{3p-1}}\\})$. By choosing between AEP and Halpern iteration, we match this lower bound up to logarithmic factors, thereby also settling the deterministic oracle complexity of smooth nonexpansive fixed-point problems.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.948404+00	\N	\N	6e6a167fdcd5e08e4521c0992ad0c0675fd27ac8459063ff70c8aafa5d3c3f2a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.948732+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.948734+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a04bd431-c651-4871-9024-b927fde501fa	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.31470v2	Beyond Empirical Support: Structured Outlier Generation via Sinkhorn Optimal Transport	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31470	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31470	Haixiang Sun, Andrew L. Liu	arXiv:2609.31470v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Outliers are essential for evaluating and improving the robustness of machine learning systems, especially when future distributions may differ significantly from historical training data. In high-stakes applications, robustness often depends on rare cases that finite datasets fail to capture, making simple resampling or perturbation insufficient for stress scenario generation. Existing outlier synthesis methods typically rely on sparse neighborhoods, low support latent regions, or classifier boundary crossings, which can be heuristic, unstable, and tied to specific modalities or architectures. We therefore propose Sinkhorn Boundary Outlier Generation (SBOG), a structured framework for latent-space outlier generation that couples Sinkhorn optimal transport geometry with distributionally robust boundary modeling. The resulting Sinkhorn-induced support cost guides the sampler toward weakly supported boundary regions, while semantic constraints prevent uncontrolled drift from the intended context, yielding controlled deviations from the in-distribution reference measure rather than arbitrary sparse-region samples. Experiments on time series anomaly generation and image outlier synthesis show that our framework produces informative, semantically controlled outliers and improves downstream robustness evaluation across modalities, providing a foundation for stress scenario generation beyond empirical support.	arXiv:2609.31470v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Outliers are essential for evaluating and improving the robustness of machine learning systems, especially when future distributions may differ significantly from historical training data. In high-stakes applications, robustness often depends on rare cases that finite datasets fail to capture, making simple resampling or perturbation insufficient for stress scenario generation. Existing outlier synthesis methods typically rely on sparse neighborhoods, low support latent regions, or classifier boundary crossings, which can be heuristic, unstable, and tied to specific modalities or architectures. We therefore propose Sinkhorn Boundary Outlier Generation (SBOG), a structured framework for latent-space outlier generation that couples Sinkhorn optimal transport geometry with distributionally robust boundary modeling. The resulting Sinkhorn-induced support cost guides the sampler toward weakly supported boundary regions, while semantic constraints prevent uncontrolled drift from the intended context, yielding controlled deviations from the in-distribution reference measure rather than arbitrary sparse-region samples. Experiments on time series anomaly generation and image outlier synthesis show that our framework produces informative, semantically controlled outliers and improves downstream robustness evaluation across modalities, providing a foundation for stress scenario generation beyond empirical support.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.953282+00	\N	\N	969ac5ac638e1f7db798545e417a3d00a595c7b14f117f7a161214bef550c39d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.953625+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.953627+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4c575bc5-f3e8-4058-9670-445d240b50cc	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.33239v4	Schr\\"odinger--F\\"ollmer Actor--Critic: Diffusion Policy Improvement with Finite-Sample Analysis	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33239	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33239	Yuling Jiao, Lican Kang, Jerry Zhijian Yang, Jincheng Ying	arXiv:2609.33239v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Diffusion policies represent multimodal action distributions, but a $Q$-weighted update does not specify how to sample from the resulting target distribution. We propose Schr\\"odinger--F\\"ollmer Actor--Critic (SFAC), an offline-to-online reinforcement learning (RL) method for Kullback--Leibler (KL)-regularized policy improvement through conditional diffusion. A minimax Bellman critic estimates the action-value function, which defines an exponentially tilted target policy. A Doob $h$-transform expresses this update as a correction to the reference diffusion drift. The target drift is a weighted conditional mean of Brownian-bridge labels. We fit it by weighted regression using endpoints from the current policy and pointwise action-value evaluations. In the small-update regime, the KL-regularized update follows the natural policy-gradient direction, and the Doob correction represents the same local change in the space of diffusion drifts. Under suitable conditions, we derive finite-sample bounds that separate the errors from critic estimation, weighted neural drift regression, and diffusion discretization through a reverse-KL actor bound. The average-performance bound depends explicitly on the comparison coefficients over the policy iterations. Across six D4RL offline-to-online tasks, the final scores of the $Q$-guided bridge-regression implementation exceed its offline initialization on every task and Cal-QL on five tasks. We also compare with offline RL and online-only baselines.	arXiv:2609.33239v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Diffusion policies represent multimodal action