0bc4e34f-c972-471f-bc62-926949be7be1	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11416v1	Modeling individual and item differences in atypicality inferences with ACT-R	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11416	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11416	Guifu Liu, Vera Demberg	arXiv:2611.11416v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Humans engage in pragmatic reasoning when encountering mentions of highly predictable events. Such mentions may appear redundant, leading comprehenders to infer that the event is atypical, and accommodate this inference with an explanation for why it is atypical. However, these inferences are not drawn uniformly across individuals, and experimental items vary in inference rates due to differences in event typicality. We show that these patterns can be predicted with a resource-rational cognitive model in which comprehenders select interpretation strategies based on individual exploration tendencies and item-level predispositions. These parameters are estimated independently from participants' Raven's Matrices score, event typicality rating and language model based accommodation likelihood. Taken together with recent findings from reference games, our results motivate a new perspective on the mechanisms that support pragmatic inferences.	arXiv:2611.11416v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Humans engage in pragmatic reasoning when encountering mentions of highly predictable events. Such mentions may appear redundant, leading comprehenders to infer that the event is atypical, and accommodate this inference with an explanation for why it is atypical. However, these inferences are not drawn uniformly across individuals, and experimental items vary in inference rates due to differences in event typicality. We show that these patterns can be predicted with a resource-rational cognitive model in which comprehenders select interpretation strategies based on individual exploration tendencies and item-level predispositions. These parameters are estimated independently from participants' Raven's Matrices score, event typicality rating and language model based accommodation likelihood. Taken together with recent findings from reference games, our results motivate a new perspective on the mechanisms that support pragmatic inferences.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.829648+00	\N	\N	97754fdea20d5860a80b0a4c12c46a4998e7938d112c471bab5da62699fdd6c3	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.830031+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.830032+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5066532b-710d-4cc2-b426-9ae19ab0d1a3	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11471v1	Saidzoda-CA-EmbeddingGemma-300M: One Small Embedder for Tajik, Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11471	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11471	Tohir Saidzoda, Nekruz Maxudov, Bedil Karimov, Abubakr Abdullozoda, Amin Jalilov, Jonibek Kishvarov, Rahmonali Sharipov, Mariyamkhon Shodyeva	arXiv:2611.11471v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The small open embedder EmbeddingGemma-300M, used as released, finds the right passage first for only 51.7% to 60.8% of Belebele questions in Tajik, Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz. We present Saidzoda-CA-EmbeddingGemma-300M, one model for all four languages. It is our Tajik fine-tune of EmbeddingGemma-300M, trained for one more epoch on 279,904 pairs: questions in Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz that our own language model wrote about source passages, a Tajik refresher, and English and Russian replay. On Belebele, recall@1 rises by 14.4 to 26.0 points in each language, to between 75.2% and 77.7%. Against single-language models trained with the same settings, the one model stays within 2.8 points at recall@1 in Kazakh, Uzbek and Tajik, with overlapping recall@10 intervals, though it also had more training. It keeps the Tajik law recall@10 of its starting point (92.2 against 92.4) but loses 4.4 points of law recall@1. A model trained on one of these languages also improves the others on Belebele, but less than training on them. The model still trails the 560M XLM-RoBERTa embedders on Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz Belebele: the better of multilingual-e5-large-instruct and BGE-M3, used as released, is 9.4 to 10.2 points ahead at recall@1. Our scores of those released models agree with the published MTEB results within 2.4 points. Russian and English recall@1 fall by 3.0 and 4.2 points.	arXiv:2611.11471v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: The small open embedder EmbeddingGemma-300M, used as released, finds the right passage first for only 51.7% to 60.8% of Belebele questions in Tajik, Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz. We present Saidzoda-CA-EmbeddingGemma-300M, one model for all four languages. It is our Tajik fine-tune of EmbeddingGemma-300M, trained for one more epoch on 279,904 pairs: questions in Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz that our own language model wrote about source passages, a Tajik refresher, and English and Russian replay. On Belebele, recall@1 rises by 14.4 to 26.0 points in each language, to between 75.2% and 77.7%. Against single-language models trained with the same settings, the one model stays within 2.8 points at recall@1 in Kazakh, Uzbek and Tajik, with overlapping recall@10 intervals, though it also had more training. It keeps the Tajik law recall@10 of its starting point (92.2 against 92.4) but loses 4.4 points of law recall@1. A model trained on one of these languages also improves the others on Belebele, but less than training on them. The model still trails the 560M XLM-RoBERTa embedders on Kazakh, Uzbek and Kyrgyz Belebele: the better of multilingual-e5-large-instruct and BGE-M3, used as released, is 9.4 to 10.2 points ahead at recall@1. Our scores of those released models agree with the published MTEB results within 2.4 points. Russian and English recall@1 fall by 3.0 and 4.2 points.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.834338+00	\N	\N	71cff49736890e09b654faa16fc0430cadc04a3d0bc99f2ee8b9b38d3b695f01	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.834885+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.834887+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4400d28d-24d9-4406-b556-a3a2923a3d10	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/kuken	Kuken Industries searches 70 years of drawings in minutes	https://openai.com/index/kuken	https://openai.com/index/kuken	\N	Kuken Industries uses the OpenAI API to search 70 years of cooling tower drawings. Engineers can find the drawing for a repair in 4.5 minutes, down from nearly an hour.	Kuken Industries uses the OpenAI API to search 70 years of cooling tower drawings. Engineers can find the drawing for a repair in 4.5 minutes, down from nearly an hour.	product_update	en	2026-11-09 08:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:59.034359+00	3	\N	0464c95a496eecf20c56b7f3221064fa64fb9db0b52127640ae0c174fe22ab0f	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:59.03555+00	2026-11-10 16:30:16.204635+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
