688936ee-3b01-4d59-a0f2-8a48a7cde0b4	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12392v1	More Than Just Style: Benchmarking Personality Influence in Large Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12392	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12392	Yuhao Liu, Mengyu Wang, Xiao Chen, Lu Liu	arXiv:2611.12392v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In addition to the effects of explicit input prompts and instructions, large language model outputs are influenced by underlying behavioral preferences similar to human personality tendencies. These tendencies can affect how models interpret situations and make decisions. However, their influence is difficult to disentangle from the effects of model capabilities and instructions. Therefore, we introduce SPACE, a benchmark framework combining a controlled evaluation protocol with complementary datasets for personality verification, capability assessment, and hierarchical situational tasks. Controlled comparisons across guidance conditions help distinguish personality influence from measured capability changes and immediate instruction effects. Experimental results demonstrate that although prompting can shift generation style, models' internal personality tendencies are persistent and not readily overridden by opposing instructions. Furthermore, these underlying tendencies have an observable influence on LLM task performance, highlighting the need for greater attention to these characteristics and potential applications. Training code and SPACE benchmark specifications are available at https://github.com/Niklauseik/SPACE-training.	arXiv:2611.12392v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: In addition to the effects of explicit input prompts and instructions, large language model outputs are influenced by underlying behavioral preferences similar to human personality tendencies. These tendencies can affect how models interpret situations and make decisions. However, their influence is difficult to disentangle from the effects of model capabilities and instructions. Therefore, we introduce SPACE, a benchmark framework combining a controlled evaluation protocol with complementary datasets for personality verification, capability assessment, and hierarchical situational tasks. Controlled comparisons across guidance conditions help distinguish personality influence from measured capability changes and immediate instruction effects. Experimental results demonstrate that although prompting can shift generation style, models' internal personality tendencies are persistent and not readily overridden by opposing instructions. Furthermore, these underlying tendencies have an observable influence on LLM task performance, highlighting the need for greater attention to these characteristics and potential applications. Training code and SPACE benchmark specifications are available at https://github.com/Niklauseik/SPACE-training.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.904867+00	\N	\N	7fd85176eb1782c9dd2e1074be86ebbfbbe6dec0f4630a87706ade5ba9e935fd	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.905124+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.905125+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
26f8ab70-c63d-4fd5-ba9a-51053dffff00	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/codex-for-knowledge-work	Codex is becoming a productivity tool for everyone	https://openai.com/index/codex-for-knowledge-work	https://openai.com/index/codex-for-knowledge-work	\N	The Next Era of Knowledge Work report explores how Codex is transforming productivity through AI-powered research, data analysis, workflow automation, and content creation.	The Next Era of Knowledge Work report explores how Codex is transforming productivity through AI-powered research, data analysis, workflow automation, and content creation.	product_update	en	2026-06-02 02:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.679433+00	4	\N	5bc67574e440af882409a61339c684a79daf035fefccaebd3502ebd9e7448620	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.679893+00	2026-11-10 14:25:02.646375+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
79ac55e1-97bf-4601-be6f-4ee8fa7f6836	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12407v1	BDBBQ: A Human-AI Collaborative Benchmark for Bengali Social Bias in LLMs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12407	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12407	Provat Saha, Ashika Habib Khan, Tahseen Tanha, Hasin Ishrak, Muhammad Iqbal Hossain	arXiv:2611.12407v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Despite growing attention to social bias in large language models (LLMs), existing bias benchmarks provide limited coverage of culturally grounded evaluation in Bengali, particularly within Bangladesh's sociocultural context. To address this gap, we develop a bilingual extension of BanglaBBQ comprising 61,312 question-answering instances across 12 social categories in English and Bengali. The benchmark incorporates balanced answer permutations, contrasting question framings, and both ambiguous and disambiguated contexts to systematically examine model performance and stereotype-aligned bias across languages. We validate the dataset through structural checks, 220 statistical tests, and expert annotation of 150 instances by four annotators, achieving strong inter-annotator agreement (Fleiss' \\k{appa}=0.8349). We evaluate eight LLMs across 490,496 instances and identify five key findings. First, disambiguated contexts yield substantially greater accuracy improvements in Bengali (+36.59 percentage points) than in English (+17.71). Second, all evaluated models exhibit lower accuracy in Bengali, although Mistral-Nemo shows the smallest cross-lingual accuracy gap (5.12 percentage points). Third, stereotype-aligned bias exhibits language and model dependent variation, increasing in Bengali for Gemma 2 9B but decreasing for Mistral-Nemo. Fourth, higher accuracy does not necessarily correspond to lower stereotype-aligned bias. Finally, bias varies considerably across social categories, with Qwen 2.5 7B exhibiting stronger stereotype alignment in Bengali for Politics and Physical Appearance. These findings demonstrate that cross-lingual performance and social bias are distinct dimensions of LLM behavior and underscore the importance of culturally grounded, language-sensitive benchmarks for evaluating fairness beyond English-centric settings.	arXiv:2611.12407v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Despite growing attention to social bias in large language models (LLMs), existing bias benchmarks provide limited coverage of culturally grounded evaluation in Bengali, particularly within Bangladesh's sociocultural context. To address this gap, we develop a bilingual extension of BanglaBBQ comprising 61,312 question-answering instances across 12 social categories in English and Bengali. The benchmark incorporates balanced answer permutations, contrasting question framings, and both ambiguous and disambiguated contexts to systematically examine model performance and stereotype-aligned bias across languages. We validate the dataset through structural checks, 220 statistical tests, and expert annotation of 150 instances by four annotators, achieving strong inter-annotator agreement (Fleiss' \\k{appa}=0.8349). We evaluate eight LLMs across 490,496 instances and identify five key findings. First, disambiguated contexts yield substantially greater accuracy improvements in Bengali (+36.59 percentage points) than in English (+17.71). Second, all evaluated models exhibit lower accuracy in Bengali, although Mistral-Nemo shows the smallest cross-lingual accuracy gap (5.12 percentage points). Third, stereotype-aligned bias exhibits language and model dependent variation, increasing in Bengali for Gemma 2 9B but decreasing for Mistral-Nemo. Fourth, higher accuracy does not necessarily correspond to lower stereotype-aligned bias. Finally, bias varies considerably across social categories, with Qwen 2.5 7B exhibiting stronger stereotype alignment in Bengali for Politics and Physical Appearance. These findings demonstrate that cross-lingual performance and social bias are distinct dimensions of LLM behavior and underscore the importance of culturally grounded, language-sensitive benchmarks for evaluating fairness beyond English-centric settings.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.906515+00	\N	\N	55da30c982e9cf65d7f258a571d5f2df11366bded920286432e90f16b7effcd3	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.906815+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.906816+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0ce98d35-3bde-4a4e-b703-a40f05836439	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12420v1	Path-Level Structural and Semantic Alignment for Hierarchical Multi-Label Text Classification	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12420	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12420	Xindi Wang, Aifen Xiong, Baiyang Wang, Xuri Ge, Robert Mercer, Frank Rudzicz, Yifan Peng, Zhumin Chen, Hongjun Wang	arXiv:2611.12420v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Hierarchical multi-label text classification (HMTC) requires models to capture both structural dependencies among labels and the fine-grained semantics of labels at different hierarchy levels. Existing methods often model hierarchy through prediction constraints or representations of individual labels and local relations. Structural and semantic supervision, however, are usually formulated separately. We instead use complete root-to-node label paths as a shared unit for both. Our framework introduces two auxiliary training objectives while retaining a standard multi-label classifier at inference. PathSimNCE aligns document representations with hierarchical paths using hierarchy-aware contrastive targets. These targets provide graded supervision for structurally related paths while preserving explicit supervision for the ground-truth paths. The complementary semantic objective uses LLM-generated, document-conditioned descriptions of the same positive paths. It thereby provides context-specific label semantics beyond static label representations. Experiments on three benchmarks show consistent improvements: structural alignment is particularly beneficial in deeper hierarchies, while semantic alignment provides additional gains for fine-grained labels.	arXiv:2611.12420v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Hierarchical multi-label text classification (HMTC) requires models to capture both structural dependencies among labels and the fine-grained semantics of labels at different hierarchy levels. Existing methods often model hierarchy through prediction constraints or representations of individual labels and local relations. Structural and semantic supervision, however, are usually formulated separately. We instead use complete root-to-node label paths as a shared unit for both. Our framework introduces two auxiliary training objectives while retaining a standard multi-label classifier at inference. PathSimNCE aligns document representations with hierarchical paths using hierarchy-aware contrastive targets. These targets provide graded supervision for structurally related paths while preserving explicit supervision for the ground-truth paths. The complementary semantic objective uses LLM-generated, document-conditioned descriptions of the same positive paths. It thereby provides context-specific label semantics beyond static label representations. Experiments on three benchmarks show consistent improvements: structural alignment is particularly beneficial in deeper hierarchies, while semantic alignment provides additional gains for fine-grained labels.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.90818+00	\N	\N	5665261123b3dc12ea244e05c1a1e8cec38cdfe7b0e5f6fc119dbc58931ce74f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.908431+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.908432+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9748f3ab-9484-4374-b81e-ffe249bf33a4	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12429v1	Can Frontier Auto Researchers Reconstruct Human Research Decisions in AI?	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12429	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12429	Shengyun Si, Junjie Chu, Ye Leng, Yang Zhang	arXiv:2611.12429v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Scientific research has long been driven by substantial human effort, from reviewing prior work and identifying research gaps to designing specific solutions. Recent advances in harness-based agent systems have given rise to increasingly capable Auto Researchers, which can autonomously carry out these tasks and, increasingly, entire end-to-end research workflows. Yet despite their rapidly growing capabilities, how closely the research decisions and outputs produced by auto researchers align with those of human experts remains underexplored. We investigate this gap through the lens of research reconstruction and study: Given comparable research context, how similar are the research decisions and outputs produced by Auto Researchers to those of human researchers in AI research? To answer this question, we introduce AutoResearcher-Human Research Similarity (AutoHRS), a stage-wise evaluation framework for assessing how faithfully auto researchers reconstruct human research artifacts across different stages of the scientific workflow. Using 152 ICLR oral papers as our corpus, we generate 456 research trajectories spanning different stages of the research process. Applying AutoHRS to these trajectories, we find that current Auto Researchers frequently fail to recover key research decisions and their resulting artifacts across multiple stages of the research process.	arXiv:2611.12429v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Scientific research has long been driven by substantial human effort, from reviewing prior work and identifying research gaps to designing specific solutions. Recent advances in harness-based agent systems have given rise to increasingly capable Auto Researchers, which can autonomously carry out these tasks and, increasingly, entire end-to-end research workflows. Yet despite their rapidly growing capabilities, how closely the research decisions and outputs produced by auto researchers align with those of human experts remains underexplored. We investigate this gap through the lens of research reconstruction and study: Given comparable research context, how similar are the research decisions and outputs produced by Auto Researchers to those of human researchers in AI research? To answer this question, we introduce AutoResearcher-Human Research Similarity (AutoHRS), a stage-wise evaluation framework for assessing how faithfully auto researchers reconstruct human research artifacts across different stages of the scientific workflow. Using 152 ICLR oral papers as our corpus, we generate 456 research trajectories spanning different stages of the research process. Applying AutoHRS to these trajectories, we find that current Auto Researchers frequently fail to recover key research decisions and their resulting artifacts across multiple stages of the research process.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.909982+00	\N	\N	13d198b47165a3e8d0625c52d94ccef9577c3f61df616350787e2a01825b7a62	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.910255+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.910256+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5a2bb132-2a93-4cb0-b1e3-a0c4fb2d1af5	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2604.00449v3	Convergence of Byzantine-Resilient Gradient Tracking via Probabilistic Edge Dropout	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00449	https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00449	Amirhossein Dezhboro, Fateme Maleki, Arman Adibi, Erfan Amini, Jose E. Ramirez-Marquez	arXiv:2604.00449v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study distributed optimization over networks with Byzantine agents that may send arbitrary adversarial messages. We propose Gradient Tracking with Probabilistic Edge Dropout (GT-PD), which combines a universal self-centred projection that clips incoming messages to a radius-$\\tau$ ball around the receiver with a fully decentralized probabilistic dropout rule driven by a threshold-gated, dual-metric trust score in the decision and tracking channels. These identity-free defences bound adversarial perturbations while preserving doubly stochastic mixing. The projection preserves the honest network average exactly in every realization, even when honest messages are clipped. Under complete Byzantine isolation ($p_b=0$), GT-PD converges linearly to a neighbourhood of order $\\alpha\\sigma^2/(\\mu n_{\\mathcal H})$ plus higher-order network terms, matching centralized stochastic gradient descent to first order in the step size. We also give a radius computable from problem data that ensures honest messages remain unclipped with high probability. Under partial isolation ($p_b>0$), a leaky variant, GT-PD-L, controls accumulated tracking perturbations and converges linearly to a bounded neighbourhood governed by stochastic variance and the clipping-to-leak ratio, under explicit conditions on the step size, leak, and aggregate Byzantine mixing weight. With two-tier dropout and $p_h=1$, Byzantine isolation adds no variance to honest consensus dynamics. MNIST experiments under Sign Flip, ALIE, and Inner Product Manipulation attacks show that GT-PD-L outperforms coordinate-wise trimmed mean by up to 4.3 percentage points under stealth attacks.	arXiv:2604.00449v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We study distributed optimization over networks with Byzantine agents that may send arbitrary adversarial messages. We propose Gradient Tracking with Probabilistic Edge Dropout (GT-PD), which combines a universal self-centred projection that clips incoming messages to a radius-$\\tau$ ball around the receiver with a fully decentralized probabilistic dropout rule driven by a threshold-gated, dual-metric trust score in the decision and tracking channels. These identity-free defences bound adversarial perturbations while preserving doubly stochastic mixing. The projection preserves the honest network average exactly in every realization, even when honest messages are clipped. Under complete Byzantine isolation ($p_b=0$), GT-PD converges linearly to a neighbourhood of order $\\alpha\\sigma^2/(\\mu n_{\\mathcal H})$ plus higher-order network terms, matching centralized stochastic gradient descent to first order in the step size. We also give a radius computable from problem data that ensures honest messages remain unclipped with high probability. Under partial isolation ($p_b>0$), a leaky variant, GT-PD-L, controls accumulated tracking perturbations and converges linearly to a bounded neighbourhood governed by stochastic variance and the clipping-to-leak ratio, under explicit conditions on the step size, leak, and aggregate Byzantine mixing weight. With two-tier dropout and $p_h=1$, Byzantine isolation adds no variance to honest consensus dynamics. MNIST experiments under Sign Flip, ALIE, and Inner Product Manipulation attacks show that GT-PD-L outperforms coordinate-wise trimmed mean by up to 4.3 percentage points under stealth attacks.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.030136+00	\N	\N	4e1e2e5e54d350254f406aa38cb49e413faaba4666acd1ecffaab59def16dbfb	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.030443+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.030445+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
bf2ef8b1-f07c-49d2-8e96-34467a5cc68e	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12538v1	Language Defaults Before Content: A Causal Layer Map of Multilingual Generation	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12538	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12538	Shenbin Qian, Yves Scherrer	arXiv:2611.12538v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Multilingual generation requires large language models (LLMs) to determine both what to say and in what language to say it, yet these two commitments are difficult to disentangle from model outputs alone. We use translation as a controlled probe: for multi-parallel segments that a model translates well in one language direction but poorly in another, content is held fixed while language varies. Using activation patching, we write residual-stream states from successful runs into failing ones and compare content-matched states with content-independent controls, allowing us to trace content-specific and language-related signals across layers. Across three LLMs of different scales and architectures, we find a consistent three-stage pattern of multilingual generation. Intermediate layers first express a default English signal; content information becomes causally available one layer later; and the final target language is resolved only at later layers. The ordering of the English default before content also appears in a reading-comprehension task. Our sparse autoencoder analyses reveal a further dissociation: the intermediate language signal is only weakly represented by sparse features, whereas a later language distinction is clearly sparse, but resists intervention at the patched position. These results provide a mechanistic account of how language selection and content information emerge and interact during multilingual generation.	arXiv:2611.12538v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Multilingual generation requires large language models (LLMs) to determine both what to say and in what language to say it, yet these two commitments are difficult to disentangle from model outputs alone. We use translation as a controlled probe: for multi-parallel segments that a model translates well in one language direction but poorly in another, content is held fixed while language varies. Using activation patching, we write residual-stream states from successful runs into failing ones and compare content-matched states with content-independent controls, allowing us to trace content-specific and language-related signals across layers. Across three LLMs of different scales and architectures, we find a consistent three-stage pattern of multilingual generation. Intermediate layers first express a default English signal; content information becomes causally available one layer later; and the final target language is resolved only at later layers. The ordering of the English default before content also appears in a reading-comprehension task. Our sparse autoencoder analyses reveal a further dissociation: the intermediate language signal is only weakly represented by sparse features, whereas a later language distinction is clearly sparse, but resists intervention at the patched position. These results provide a mechanistic account of how language selection and content information emerge and interact during multilingual generation.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.91746+00	\N	\N	8227ef76d4c388827a12c1322a9066e45eaccce6ab91fa91883c62c85ddfe409	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.917729+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.91773+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
619afa7d-f539-463d-9174-d23ed08be922	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.22678v1	ViCLIP-OT: The First Foundation Vision-Language Model for Vietnamese Image-Text Retrieval with Optimal Transport	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22678	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22678	Quoc-Khang Tran, Minh-Thien Nguyen, Nguyen-Khang Pham	arXiv:2602.22678v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Image-text retrieval has become a fundamental component in intelligent multimedia systems; however, most existing vision-language models are optimized for highresource languages and remain suboptimal for low-resource settings such as Vietnamese. This work introduces ViCLIP-OT, a foundation vision-language model specifically designed for Vietnamese image-text retrieval. The proposed framework integrates CLIP-style contrastive learning with a Similarity-Graph Regularized Optimal Transport (SIGROT) loss to enhance global cross-modal consistency and mitigate modality gap issues. Extensive experiments on three Vietnamese benchmarks (UITOpenViIC, KTVIC, and Crossmodal-3600) demonstrate that ViCLIP-OT consistently outperforms CLIP and SigLIP baselines in both in-domain and zero-shot settings. On UIT-OpenViIC, the model achieves an average Recall@K of 67.34%, improving upon CLIP by 5.75 percentage points. In zero-shot evaluation on Crossmodal-3600, ViCLIPOT surpasses CLIP by 11.72 percentage points. Embedding-space analysis further confirms improved alignment and reduced modality gap. The results indicate that integrating SIGROT provides an effective and scalable strategy for cross-modal retrieval in low-resource languages, offering practical implications for intelligent multimedia retrieval systems in Vietnamese and other underrepresented linguistic contexts.	arXiv:2602.22678v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Image-text retrieval has become a fundamental component in intelligent multimedia systems; however, most existing vision-language models are optimized for highresource languages and remain suboptimal for low-resource settings such as Vietnamese. This work introduces ViCLIP-OT, a foundation vision-language model specifically designed for Vietnamese image-text retrieval. The proposed framework integrates CLIP-style contrastive learning with a Similarity-Graph Regularized Optimal Transport (SIGROT) loss to enhance global cross-modal consistency and mitigate modality gap issues. Extensive experiments on three Vietnamese benchmarks (UITOpenViIC, KTVIC, and Crossmodal-3600) demonstrate that ViCLIP-OT consistently outperforms CLIP and SigLIP baselines in both in-domain and zero-shot settings. On UIT-OpenViIC, the model achieves an average Recall@K of 67.34%, improving upon CLIP by 5.75 percentage points. In zero-shot evaluation on Crossmodal-3600, ViCLIPOT surpasses CLIP by 11.72 percentage points. Embedding-space analysis further confirms improved alignment and reduced modality gap. The results indicate that integrating SIGROT provides an effective and scalable strategy for cross-modal retrieval in low-resource languages, offering practical implications for intelligent multimedia retrieval systems in Vietnamese and other underrepresented linguistic contexts.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.946574+00	\N	\N	468a6e27aef7ee3ea91962a8215ec11dc965b1336fd68312842320acd9fa4cca	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.94698+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.946982+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6bb6a8c7-67db-4039-b186-689f08a342e0	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10659v1	Pandemonium: Longform Target-Speaker Speech Recognition with Cross-Speaker Interactions	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10659	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10659	Alexander Polok, Dominik Klement, Ivan Medennikov, Antoine Laurent, Petr P\\'alka, Samuele Cornell, Shinji Watanabe, Sanjeev Khudanpur, Jagadeesh Balam, Jan \\v{C}ernock\\'y, Luk\\'a\\v{s} Burget, Matthew Wiesner	arXiv:2611.10659v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Diarization-conditioned target-speaker ASR (TS-ASR), in particular Diarization-conditioned Whisper (DiCoW), is an accurate and data-efficient approach to multi-talker transcription. It has, however, three structural limitations: the model transcribes each speaker independently, operates on short (30 s) windows, and optimizes its diarization and TS-ASR components separately. We address the first two. We integrate the diarization conditioning into a longform Parakeet backbone (DiCoP) and add cross-speaker attention, which lets the model attend across the speaker dimension so that speakers are no longer decoded in isolation, and an STNO mask refiner that re-estimates the diarization-derived conditioning. As conversational data with genuine multi-speaker interaction is scarce, we scale training with a pseudo-labeling pipeline that mines 5.4k hours of in-the-wild multi-speaker audio from YODAS2. Combining these with synthetic and real data yields state-of-the-art results across several meeting benchmarks. We open-source the resulting models, DiCoP and Pandemonium, and the pseudo-labeled data.	arXiv:2611.10659v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Diarization-conditioned target-speaker ASR (TS-ASR), in particular Diarization-conditioned Whisper (DiCoW), is an accurate and data-efficient approach to multi-talker transcription. It has, however, three structural limitations: the model transcribes each speaker independently, operates on short (30 s) windows, and optimizes its diarization and TS-ASR components separately. We address the first two. We integrate the diarization conditioning into a longform Parakeet backbone (DiCoP) and add cross-speaker attention, which lets the model attend across the speaker dimension so that speakers are no longer decoded in isolation, and an STNO mask refiner that re-estimates the diarization-derived conditioning. As conversational data with genuine multi-speaker interaction is scarce, we scale training with a pseudo-labeling pipeline that mines 5.4k hours of in-the-wild multi-speaker audio from YODAS2. Combining these with synthetic and real data yields state-of-the-art results across several meeting benchmarks. We open-source the resulting models, DiCoP and Pandemonium, and the pseudo-labeled data.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.002738+00	\N	\N	5242598580899e200fd7813523353e443ffe9775b25ddadc102b3498cab4c059	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.003011+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.003012+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a31da296-76ce-4611-8336-35b98025ea3e	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11316v1	ReGKG-2025: A GKG-Compatible Reconstruction of the GDELT Global Knowledge Graph for the Missing Period of 14 June to 2 July 2025	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11316	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11316	Nikita A. Klimin (HSE University, Moscow, Russia)	arXiv:2611.11316v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: The June 2025 outage in the Global Database of Events, Language, and Tone (GDELT) collection pipeline left a gap in the Global Knowledge Graph (GKG), interrupting article-level news metadata used in downstream research. ReGKG-2025 is an archive-derived sample of 281,620 reconstructed article records covering all 1,665 missing 15-minute batches. Articles were recovered from Common Crawl and the Wayback Machine using source quotas estimated from surrounding native GKG data. Text was screened and normalized, non-English bodies were translated, and flagged records were manually reviewed. Eligible articles were converted to the 27-column GKG 2.1 layout. The versioned package includes record-level provenance, archive replay and WARC locators, source summaries, source-by-day calibration weights, and the complete manual decision journal. The sample reaches about 14% of modeled native volume and represents sources accounting for 84% of selected reference record mass. In an intact-week test, 41% of recovered articles matched native GKG records; field agreement on matched pairs is close to the processor baseline (tone MAE 1.69). The dataset supports retrospective content analysis and source-level aggregates; its tone and theme distributions differ from native GKG, and it does not support exhaustive counts. Data: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23964265.	arXiv:2611.11316v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: The June 2025 outage in the Global Database of Events, Language, and Tone (GDELT) collection pipeline left a gap in the Global Knowledge Graph (GKG), interrupting article-level news metadata used in downstream research. ReGKG-2025 is an archive-derived sample of 281,620 reconstructed article records covering all 1,665 missing 15-minute batches. Articles were recovered from Common Crawl and the Wayback Machine using source quotas estimated from surrounding native GKG data. Text was screened and normalized, non-English bodies were translated, and flagged records were manually reviewed. Eligible articles were converted to the 27-column GKG 2.1 layout. The versioned package includes record-level provenance, archive replay and WARC locators, source summaries, source-by-day calibration weights, and the complete manual decision journal. The sample reaches about 14% of modeled native volume and represents sources accounting for 84% of selected reference record mass. In an intact-week test, 41% of recovered articles matched native GKG records; field agreement on matched pairs is close to the processor baseline (tone MAE 1.69). The dataset supports retrospective content analysis and source-level aggregates; its tone and theme distributions differ from native GKG, and it does not support exhaustive counts. Data: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23964265.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.045993+00	\N	\N	42286a7511fe88a1e1e2a65e327b3cb16c71264e66d7b0c0ba655f4804be4c59	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.046261+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.046262+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
b0b2d63c-9821-4168-8041-5cd911ef4530	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11867v1	Competing Influence in LLM Populations: Budget Timing and Baseline Drift	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11867	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11867	Mehwish Nasim, Nicolas Fay	arXiv:2611.11867v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: When competing campaigns target a population of large language model (LLM) agents, observed opinion changes may reflect both campaign influence and changes that would occur without intervention. We study how the timing of campaign expenditure and the population's own evolution shape outcomes in a network of persona-conditioned LLM agents that exchange and revise opinions in natural language. A proponent and a challenger are tasked with generating opposing messages under equal delivery budgets, while a separate stance probe measures opinions without influencing the simulation. Across eight claims, we compare single-campaign and competitive conditions against matched no-campaign runs, and evaluate all nine combinations of early, sustained, and late spending on a reference topic. Baseline stance drift varies substantially across claims and, on some, accounts for a sizeable fraction of apparent campaign effects. After accounting for this drift, the direction of observed campaign-associated changes aligns more closely with the claim's epistemic standing than with the campaigner's assigned role. Uninfluenced populations also consistently lose stance diversity, demonstrating that directional opinion change and convergence are distinct phenomena. In networks with two opinion-segregated communities, campaigns primarily reduce differences between communities rather than diversity within them. LLM campaign agents do not always argue for their assigned positions, particularly when these conflict with established evidence. Finally, campaign outcomes depend on the opponent's expenditure schedule: unlike a matched numerical bounded-confidence population receiving interventions at the same times and locations, the LLM population exhibits a payoff structure with no pure-strategy equilibrium.	arXiv:2611.11867v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: When competing campaigns target a population of large language model (LLM) agents, observed opinion changes may reflect both campaign influence and changes that would occur without intervention. We study how the timing of campaign expenditure and the population's own evolution shape outcomes in a network of persona-conditioned LLM agents that exchange and revise opinions in natural language. A proponent and a challenger are tasked with generating opposing messages under equal delivery budgets, while a separate stance probe measures opinions without influencing the simulation. Across eight claims, we compare single-campaign and competitive conditions against matched no-campaign runs, and evaluate all nine combinations of early, sustained, and late spending on a reference topic. Baseline stance drift varies substantially across claims and, on some, accounts for a sizeable fraction of apparent campaign effects. After accounting for this drift, the direction of observed campaign-associated changes aligns more closely with the claim's epistemic standing than with the campaigner's assigned role. Uninfluenced populations also consistently lose stance diversity, demonstrating that directional opinion change and convergence are distinct phenomena. In networks with two opinion-segregated communities, campaigns primarily reduce differences between communities rather than diversity within them. LLM campaign agents do not always argue for their assigned positions, particularly when these conflict with established evidence. Finally, campaign outcomes depend on the opponent's expenditure schedule: unlike a matched numerical bounded-confidence population receiving interventions at the same times and locations, the LLM population exhibits a payoff structure with no pure-strategy equilibrium.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.065654+00	\N	\N	2e0493d918cf56d2ab5b1dd680e645c91391163e1afa49a142fe3de3fec870b4	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.065907+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.065908+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
9ff9dd47-09ee-4bcb-81ed-dd3935982b82	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2507.15778v3	Stabilizing Knowledge, Promoting Reasoning: Dual-Token Constraints for RLVR	https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15778	https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15778	Runze Liu, Jiakang Wang, Fuzheng Zhang, Xiu Li, Guorui Zhou, Kun Gai, Ling Pan	arXiv:2507.15778v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has become an effective post-training method for improving the reasoning abilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, existing methods mainly apply uniform optimization constraints across all tokens, ignoring their heterogeneous roles. Prior work shows that high-entropy tokens are closely tied to reasoning, while low-entropy tokens primarily encode factual knowledge, and recent approaches attempt to exploit this distinction by isolating token updates via masking or asynchronous training. We argue that retaining training signals across token types can lead to more effective learning. To address this, we propose Archer, an entropy-aware RLVR framework with dual-token constraints that preserves joint optimization while modulating update strength across token types. Our method introduces response-level entropy normalization for stable token classification and applies differentiated clipping ranges and KL regularization to encourage exploration on reasoning tokens while preserving knowledge tokens. Experiments on mathematical reasoning and code generation benchmarks show that Archer consistently outperforms strong baselines across multiple model scales, improving both pass@1 and pass@K performance. These results highlight the importance of respecting sequence-level dependencies when designing fine-grained RL optimization strategies for LLMs. The code is available at https://github.com/wizard-III/ArcherCodeR.	arXiv:2507.15778v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has become an effective post-training method for improving the reasoning abilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, existing methods mainly apply uniform optimization constraints across all tokens, ignoring their heterogeneous roles. Prior work shows that high-entropy tokens are closely tied to reasoning, while low-entropy tokens primarily encode factual knowledge, and recent approaches attempt to exploit this distinction by isolating token updates via masking or asynchronous training. We argue that retaining training signals across token types can lead to more effective learning. To address this, we propose Archer, an entropy-aware RLVR framework with dual-token constraints that preserves joint optimization while modulating update strength across token types. Our method introduces response-level entropy normalization for stable token classification and applies differentiated clipping ranges and KL regularization to encourage exploration on reasoning tokens while preserving knowledge tokens. Experiments on mathematical reasoning and code generation benchmarks show that Archer consistently outperforms strong baselines across multiple model scales, improving both pass@1 and pass@K performance. These results highlight the importance of respecting sequence-level dependencies when designing fine-grained RL optimization strategies for LLMs. The code is available at https://github.com/wizard-III/ArcherCodeR.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.119254+00	\N	\N	d194b22d5ae3bea150709d1271034c0c239a0ac6d08d147919da1641a602c70e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.119503+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.119504+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cbe4d034-3aa3-42d3-994a-eaedcb06e597	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.09870v4	Steer2Edit: From Activation Steering to Component-Level Editing	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09870	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09870	Chung-En Sun, Ge Yan, Zimo Wang, Tsui-Wei Weng	arXiv:2602.09870v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Steering methods influence Large Language Model behavior by identifying semantic directions in hidden representations, but are typically realized through inference-time activation interventions that apply a fixed, global modification to the model's internal states. While effective, such interventions often induce unfavorable attribute-utility trade-offs under strong control, as they ignore the fact that many behaviors are governed by a small and heterogeneous subset of model components. We propose Steer2Edit, a theoretically grounded, training-free framework that transforms steering vectors from inference-time control signals into diagnostic signals for component-level rank-1 weight editing. Instead of uniformly injecting a steering direction during generation, Steer2Edit selectively redistributes behavioral influence across individual attention heads and MLP neurons, yielding interpretable edits that preserve the standard forward pass and remain compatible with optimized parallel inference. Across safety alignment, hallucination mitigation, and reasoning efficiency, Steer2Edit consistently achieves more favorable attribute-utility trade-offs: at matched downstream performance, it improves safety by up to 17.2%, increases truthfulness by 9.8%, and reduces reasoning length by 12.2% on average. Overall, Steer2Edit provides a principled bridge between representation steering and weight editing by translating steering signals into interpretable, training-free parameter updates. Our code is available at https://github.com/Trustworthy-ML-Lab/Steer2Edit	arXiv:2602.09870v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Steering methods influence Large Language Model behavior by identifying semantic directions in hidden representations, but are typically realized through inference-time activation interventions that apply a fixed, global modification to the model's internal states. While effective, such interventions often induce unfavorable attribute-utility trade-offs under strong control, as they ignore the fact that many behaviors are governed by a small and heterogeneous subset of model components. We propose Steer2Edit, a theoretically grounded, training-free framework that transforms steering vectors from inference-time control signals into diagnostic signals for component-level rank-1 weight editing. Instead of uniformly injecting a steering direction during generation, Steer2Edit selectively redistributes behavioral influence across individual attention heads and MLP neurons, yielding interpretable edits that preserve the standard forward pass and remain compatible with optimized parallel inference. Across safety alignment, hallucination mitigation, and reasoning efficiency, Steer2Edit consistently achieves more favorable attribute-utility trade-offs: at matched downstream performance, it improves safety by up to 17.2%, increases truthfulness by 9.8%, and reduces reasoning length by 12.2% on average. Overall, Steer2Edit provides a principled bridge between representation steering and weight editing by translating steering signals into interpretable, training-free parameter updates. Our code is available at https://github.com/Trustworthy-ML-Lab/Steer2Edit	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.141471+00	\N	\N	fbb60f4881d1fbf8723fc3e52b8c56c90477c5808d1b05783453e8b2021c4663	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.141767+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.141768+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
dee901d5-3568-4cdf-bf20-67bbff4ca4ec	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.15721v2	Contexting as Recommendation: Evolutionary Collaborative Filtering for Context Engineering	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15721	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15721	Jiachen Zhu, Zhuoying Ou, Congmin Zheng, Yuxiang Chen, Zeyu Zheng, Rong Shan, Lingyu Yang, Lionel Z. Wang, Weiwen Liu, Yong Yu, Weinan Zhang, Jianghao Lin	arXiv:2605.15721v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large Language Models (LLMs) are highly sensitive to their input contexts, motivating the development of automated context engineering. However, existing methods predominantly treat this as a global search problem, seeking a single context strategy that maximizes average performance across a dataset. This restrictive assumption overlooks the fact that different inputs often require distinct guidance, leaving substantial instance-level performance gains untapped. In this paper, we propose a paradigm shift by formulating context engineering as a recommendation problem. We introduce \\textbf{Neural Collaborative Context Engineering (NCCE)}, a framework that transitions optimization from a static global search to dynamic, instance-wise routing. NCCE first bootstraps a diverse catalog of anchor contexts and then employs a novel \\textbf{Context-CF Co-Evolution} mechanism. This stage establishes a synergistic feedback loop: a lightweight Neural Collaborative Filtering (NCF) model learns instance-context preferences to guide the generation of specialized context variants, while the newly evaluated contexts continuously refine the NCF model's understanding of latent preferences. At inference time, the trained NCF model acts as a context router, dynamically assigning the most suitable context strategy to each unseen instance. Theoretical Proofs and comprehensive experiments demonstrate that by matching individual inputs with their optimal contexts, NCCE significantly improves task accuracy, highlighting the critical importance of personalization in LLM context engineering.	arXiv:2605.15721v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large Language Models (LLMs) are highly sensitive to their input contexts, motivating the development of automated context engineering. However, existing methods predominantly treat this as a global search problem, seeking a single context strategy that maximizes average performance across a dataset. This restrictive assumption overlooks the fact that different inputs often require distinct guidance, leaving substantial instance-level performance gains untapped. In this paper, we propose a paradigm shift by formulating context engineering as a recommendation problem. We introduce \\textbf{Neural Collaborative Context Engineering (NCCE)}, a framework that transitions optimization from a static global search to dynamic, instance-wise routing. NCCE first bootstraps a diverse catalog of anchor contexts and then employs a novel \\textbf{Context-CF Co-Evolution} mechanism. This stage establishes a synergistic feedback loop: a lightweight Neural Collaborative Filtering (NCF) model learns instance-context preferences to guide the generation of specialized context variants, while the newly evaluated contexts continuously refine the NCF model's understanding of latent preferences. At inference time, the trained NCF model acts as a context router, dynamically assigning the most suitable context strategy to each unseen instance. Theoretical Proofs and comprehensive experiments demonstrate that by matching individual inputs with their optimal contexts, NCCE significantly improves task accuracy, highlighting the critical importance of personalization in LLM context engineering.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.166465+00	\N	\N	7387aa1745cba3e83578733475e12ab8f3081c5bc7905a8f6fa2e11bbc6a3e17	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.166772+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.166773+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
2ca92acd-bf0c-4fd9-a115-e88d0cb2c9c7	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.30214v2	GRUFF: LLM Pronoun Fidelity, Reasoning, and Biases in German	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.30214	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.30214	Fabian Mewes, Anne Lauscher, Vagrant Gautam	arXiv:2605.30214v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Third-person singular pronouns have long been used to study stereotypical biases in language models and to test their abilities to reason about reference. More recently, the interplay between reasoning and bias has been investigated with the task of pronoun fidelity, which assesses models' abilities to correctly reuse a previously-specified pronoun for a discourse entity, independent of other potentially distracting discourse entities mentioned in between. However, such research focuses on English, a language with limited grammatical gender and almost no gender agreement. In this paper we contribute a novel, large-scale dataset, GRUFF, to measure pronoun fidelity in German, covering four different gender agreement systems in nouns, and four sets of pronouns. With this dataset, we show that LLMs display strong grammatical agreement for masculine and feminine entities in the absence of explicit context, but not for neopronouns xier and en. In the presence of distractors, however, models degrade on all pronoun sets. Finally, we show that occupational stereotypes in this context are poorly correlated across grammatical cases and most models, except ones with closely related architectures.	arXiv:2605.30214v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Third-person singular pronouns have long been used to study stereotypical biases in language models and to test their abilities to reason about reference. More recently, the interplay between reasoning and bias has been investigated with the task of pronoun fidelity, which assesses models' abilities to correctly reuse a previously-specified pronoun for a discourse entity, independent of other potentially distracting discourse entities mentioned in between. However, such research focuses on English, a language with limited grammatical gender and almost no gender agreement. In this paper we contribute a novel, large-scale dataset, GRUFF, to measure pronoun fidelity in German, covering four different gender agreement systems in nouns, and four sets of pronouns. With this dataset, we show that LLMs display strong grammatical agreement for masculine and feminine entities in the absence of explicit context, but not for neopronouns xier and en. In the presence of distractors, however, models degrade on all pronoun sets. Finally, we show that occupational stereotypes in this context are poorly correlated across grammatical cases and most models, except ones with closely related architectures.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.172466+00	\N	\N	6570ee22f644489c20486b92e9c132f3802845f93c280405d2fca5d067bba58d	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.172715+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.172717+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
e92e8509-ebab-4e44-bd97-8cbe0e72d7a4	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.03021v5	Hint-Guided Diversified Policy Optimization for LLM Reasoning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03021	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03021	Zhiyu Cao, Kaixin Wu, Mingjie Zhong, Peifeng Li, Can Ye, Qiaoming Zhu	arXiv:2606.03021v5 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent developments in Large Language Models (LLMs) have showcased impressive reasoning capabilities, with Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) being a promising enhancement strategy. However, existing reward mechanisms are constrained to the outcome-level correctness and lack explicit signals to guide the model to consider diverse solutions. In contrast, human problem solving typically involves evaluating multiple potential approaches and selecting the most reliable solution, a cognitive process that current RLVR frameworks do not explicitly incentivize. Inspired by this, we propose Hint-Guided Diversified Policy Optimization (HDPO), allowing the model to first list all potential candidate solution outlines as hints and then select the most reliable one for further reasoning. HDPO comprises two stages of Cold Start for Structured Reasoning and Hint-Guided Diversified Reinforcement Learning to incentivize the model to generate diverse and reliable solutions following the ``propose-select-think'' trajectory. Experimental results show that HDPO effectively boosts LLM reasoning and enhances the diversity of candidate solutions as well as the LLM's ability to identify reliable solutions.	arXiv:2606.03021v5 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent developments in Large Language Models (LLMs) have showcased impressive reasoning capabilities, with Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) being a promising enhancement strategy. However, existing reward mechanisms are constrained to the outcome-level correctness and lack explicit signals to guide the model to consider diverse solutions. In contrast, human problem solving typically involves evaluating multiple potential approaches and selecting the most reliable solution, a cognitive process that current RLVR frameworks do not explicitly incentivize. Inspired by this, we propose Hint-Guided Diversified Policy Optimization (HDPO), allowing the model to first list all potential candidate solution outlines as hints and then select the most reliable one for further reasoning. HDPO comprises two stages of Cold Start for Structured Reasoning and Hint-Guided Diversified Reinforcement Learning to incentivize the model to generate diverse and reliable solutions following the ``propose-select-think'' trajectory. Experimental results show that HDPO effectively boosts LLM reasoning and enhances the diversity of candidate solutions as well as the LLM's ability to identify reliable solutions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.178843+00	\N	\N	8945e1e5668a326bd88c98e2051709281985502f7bd3de8eb3962f7bc5b7162a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.179267+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.179268+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6d8b6a08-ae98-4bc4-b852-712fea8fe69f	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.08809v2	Tevatron-Elastic: A Unified Abstraction for Training Elastic Retrievers and Rerankers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08809	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08809	Yu Wang, Shengyao Zhuang, Xueguang Ma, Zongyu Wu, Jimmy Lin, Vivek Srikumar, Zhichao Xu	arXiv:2608.08809v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: A single model scale challenges the flexibility of a production retrieval system: some settings need it faster, others need a smaller index, and the right trade-off changes with the workload. In the context of information retrieval (IR), a transformer-based model can be made smaller in three ways --- using fewer layers, passing fewer tokens through the upper layers, or producing a shorter embedding --- and each way saves a different resource. These options have been studied in isolation, each as its own method with its own code and training setup, which makes them hard to combine or adapt to a new model. In this work, we present Tevatron-Elastic to bring all three under one unified abstraction: a single object names any size the model can run at, and a short schedule lists the sizes to train. Training then produces one checkpoint that serves all of those sizes, and at deployment the user has the liberty to pick any of them. The same abstraction covers both retrievers and rerankers and both encoder and decoder models, as it works through interfaces that Hugging Face transformers already expose; adding a new backbone can be a configuration change rather than a model rewrite. Prior methods --- Matryoshka embeddings, early exit, 2D Matryoshka (Starbucks), and layerwise token compression (LTC) --- become special cases of our unified abstraction. With Tevatron-Elastic, we also propose a new elastic algorithm Matryoshka~LTC (MLTC) to jointly train several token-compression ratios in one retriever checkpoint. To validate our framework, we train 20 checkpoints across three backbones and two tasks: the quality curves are smooth, full-point quality remains close to that of models trained for a single size, and a controlled study confirms the wall-clock speedups. We release the framework and all checkpoints to support elastic retrieval research.	arXiv:2608.08809v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: A single model scale challenges the flexibility of a production retrieval system: some settings need it faster, others need a smaller index, and the right trade-off changes with the workload. In the context of information retrieval (IR), a transformer-based model can be made smaller in three ways --- using fewer layers, passing fewer tokens through the upper layers, or producing a shorter embedding --- and each way saves a different resource. These options have been studied in isolation, each as its own method with its own code and training setup, which makes them hard to combine or adapt to a new model. In this work, we present Tevatron-Elastic to bring all three under one unified abstraction: a single object names any size the model can run at, and a short schedule lists the sizes to train. Training then produces one checkpoint that serves all of those sizes, and at deployment the user has the liberty to pick any of them. The same abstraction covers both retrievers and rerankers and both encoder and decoder models, as it works through interfaces that Hugging Face transformers already expose; adding a new backbone can be a configuration change rather than a model rewrite. Prior methods --- Matryoshka embeddings, early exit, 2D Matryoshka (Starbucks), and layerwise token compression (LTC) --- become special cases of our unified abstraction. With Tevatron-Elastic, we also propose a new elastic algorithm Matryoshka~LTC (MLTC) to jointly train several token-compression ratios in one retriever checkpoint. To validate our framework, we train 20 checkpoints across three backbones and two tasks: the quality curves are smooth, full-point quality remains close to that of models trained for a single size, and a controlled study confirms the wall-clock speedups. We release the framework and all checkpoints to support elastic retrieval research.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.208458+00	\N	\N	25d905c63b25f0d4e8322ba3800cc879efa9b4965c5726cba4219ed3f89de5b1	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.208767+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.208768+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3364cf8e-0cca-4961-8dbe-bf810cc02117	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.17583v2	Auditing Exposure to Harmful Content on TikTok using Multimodal Language Models: A Cross-National, Age-Stratified Study	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17583	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17583	Hamidreza Saffari, Francesco Pierri	arXiv:2608.17583v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Online video platforms can expose young users to harmful content, but independent audits remain difficult because video annotation is costly and moderation judgments vary across languages. We audit TikTok in France, Italy, and Sweden with sockpuppet accounts representing four age personas (13, 16, 19, 40), collecting 36,971 videos from passive For-You-page scrolling and active sessions that scroll, search for harm keywords, and scroll again. To scale annotation, we validate four multimodal LLMs against native-speaker labels on a 300-video reference set. Gemini 2.5 Flash with eight sampled frames plus text performs best (aggregate kappa = 0.42), at half the per-call cost of native-video upload, and we apply it to a 10% sample for approximately \\$50 in total API spend across both modalities. Keyword search returns 35-56% harmful content, a 1.5-7.5x increase over the scrolling baseline in ten of twelve country-age combinations; the spike is temporary and flattens the age differences observed in France and Sweden. Under passive scrolling, Italy has the highest harm rate at every age, with Italian age-19 reaching 48.6%. Overall, MLLM-based auditing offers a scalable approach for cross-national youth-safety audits, while provider safety filters (1.1% refusal rate) under-count the most explicit harms.	arXiv:2608.17583v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Online video platforms can expose young users to harmful content, but independent audits remain difficult because video annotation is costly and moderation judgments vary across languages. We audit TikTok in France, Italy, and Sweden with sockpuppet accounts representing four age personas (13, 16, 19, 40), collecting 36,971 videos from passive For-You-page scrolling and active sessions that scroll, search for harm keywords, and scroll again. To scale annotation, we validate four multimodal LLMs against native-speaker labels on a 300-video reference set. Gemini 2.5 Flash with eight sampled frames plus text performs best (aggregate kappa = 0.42), at half the per-call cost of native-video upload, and we apply it to a 10% sample for approximately \\$50 in total API spend across both modalities. Keyword search returns 35-56% harmful content, a 1.5-7.5x increase over the scrolling baseline in ten of twelve country-age combinations; the spike is temporary and flattens the age differences observed in France and Sweden. Under passive scrolling, Italy has the highest harm rate at every age, with Italian age-19 reaching 48.6%. Overall, MLLM-based auditing offers a scalable approach for cross-national youth-safety audits, while provider safety filters (1.1% refusal rate) under-count the most explicit harms.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.219091+00	\N	\N	bfb45a031589faae0cc726689a5a1188eeff08991d03cdbdecdba3471d90a426	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.219517+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.219519+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a7fbfc50-0318-4603-adc3-af24974f1ddc	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.01136v2	Spectral Graph Sparsification Preserves Representation Geometry in Graph Neural Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.01136	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.01136	Sanjukta Krishnagopal	arXiv:2605.01136v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Spectral graph sparsification is a classical tool for reducing graph complexity while preserving Laplacian quadratic forms. In graph neural networks (GNNs), sparsification is often used to accelerate computation while maintaining predictive performance. In this work, we study a complementary representation-level question: does sparsification preserve the geometry of learned embeddings?\n  For polynomial-filter GNNs, we prove that any $\\epsilon$-spectral sparsifier induces $O(\\epsilon)$ perturbations in polynomial graph filters, multilayer hidden representations, and their Gram matrices. These guarantees imply stability of squared pairwise distances, class means, and covariance structure in embedding space. We further establish finite-time training stability: under smoothness and boundedness assumptions, gradient descent on dense and sparsified graphs produces weight trajectories whose separation grows at most proportionally to the sparsification distortion.\n  Empirically, effective-resistance sparsification validates the predicted perturbation chain on synthetic graphs and preserves hidden representation geometry on real datasets. In our experiments, the gram matrix and training dynamics show low divergence even under substantial sparsification, consistent with the predicted stability under spectral sparsification. Hidden Gram preservation strongly predicts neighborhood preservation and class-centroid stability across FashionMNIST, Cora, and Paul15. Together, these results show that spectral sparsification preserves not only graph operators, but also the representation geometry that supports downstream use of GNN embeddings for interpretability.	arXiv:2605.01136v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Spectral graph sparsification is a classical tool for reducing graph complexity while preserving Laplacian quadratic forms. In graph neural networks (GNNs), sparsification is often used to accelerate computation while maintaining predictive performance. In this work, we study a complementary representation-level question: does sparsification preserve the geometry of learned embeddings?\n  For polynomial-filter GNNs, we prove that any $\\epsilon$-spectral sparsifier induces $O(\\epsilon)$ perturbations in polynomial graph filters, multilayer hidden representations, and their Gram matrices. These guarantees imply stability of squared pairwise distances, class means, and covariance structure in embedding space. We further establish finite-time training stability: under smoothness and boundedness assumptions, gradient descent on dense and sparsified graphs produces weight trajectories whose separation grows at most proportionally to the sparsification distortion.\n  Empirically, effective-resistance sparsification validates the predicted perturbation chain on synthetic graphs and preserves hidden representation geometry on real datasets. In our experiments, the gram matrix and training dynamics show low divergence even under substantial sparsification, consistent with the predicted stability under spectral sparsification. Hidden Gram preservation strongly predicts neighborhood preservation and class-centroid stability across FashionMNIST, Cora, and Paul15. Together, these results show that spectral sparsification preserves not only graph operators, but also the representation geometry that supports downstream use of GNN embeddings for interpretability.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.071303+00	\N	\N	f7396e5f56b9ef3843f04607360b550f157e021719807e3ffe1575e084a55406	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.071632+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.071633+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
8edb8951-5c1f-44a8-9299-a99800bbc3cd	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11558v1	InfraRSI: Harnessing Recursive Self-Improvement for Training Infrastructure	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11558	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11558	InfraRSI Team, Yuchuan Tian, Haocheng Mei, Ye Yuan, Chao Xu, Boxun Li, Guohao Dai, Kai Han, Allen Chen, Yunhe Wang, Yu Wang	arXiv:2611.11558v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: While Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI) accelerates isolated algorithm and kernel synthesis, distributed LLM training depends on tightly coupled launch settings, operator kernels, and communication schedules, where single edits often hit out-of-memory (OOM) boundaries, compiler conflicts, measurement jitter, or silent numerical drift. We present InfraRSI, the agentic harness that turns open-ended human commands into verified training optimizations via a five-stage workflow: init locks editable scope and a fixed task contract over workload, loss, and timing invariants to prevent reward hacking, doctor profiles baseline phases and calibrates cluster noise, run co-optimizes launch knobs and code patches before re-verifying top candidates against the baseline and logging findings to a cross-hardware experience bank, and report and apply audit source paths, track failed-trial costs, and commit promoted changes. Rather than stepping greedily from a single incumbent, InfraRSI initializes from diverse experience-seeded configurations and fits global execution-time and censored peak-memory models across all trials, including OOMs. Using these models, it prices per-setting memory costs, revises miscalibrated LLM priors from live runs, and pairs memory-hungry optimizations with memory savers and verified code patches. Across four single- and multi-node dense and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) LLM workloads, InfraRSI achieves a speedup of up to $2.75\\times$ and lifts mean Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from $17.4\\%$ to $30.0\\%$, outperforming existing tuners and coding agents while transferring experience across GPUs. As it improves training infrastructure efficiency, InfraRSI forms a two-level Recursive Self-Improvement loop---revising priors online within runs and distilling experience across clusters---to push foundation model scaling.	arXiv:2611.11558v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: While Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI) accelerates isolated algorithm and kernel synthesis, distributed LLM training depends on tightly coupled launch settings, operator kernels, and communication schedules, where single edits often hit out-of-memory (OOM) boundaries, compiler conflicts, measurement jitter, or silent numerical drift. We present InfraRSI, the agentic harness that turns open-ended human commands into verified training optimizations via a five-stage workflow: init locks editable scope and a fixed task contract over workload, loss, and timing invariants to prevent reward hacking, doctor profiles baseline phases and calibrates cluster noise, run co-optimizes launch knobs and code patches before re-verifying top candidates against the baseline and logging findings to a cross-hardware experience bank, and report and apply audit source paths, track failed-trial costs, and commit promoted changes. Rather than stepping greedily from a single incumbent, InfraRSI initializes from diverse experience-seeded configurations and fits global execution-time and censored peak-memory models across all trials, including OOMs. Using these models, it prices per-setting memory costs, revises miscalibrated LLM priors from live runs, and pairs memory-hungry optimizations with memory savers and verified code patches. Across four single- and multi-node dense and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) LLM workloads, InfraRSI achieves a speedup of up to $2.75\\times$ and lifts mean Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from $17.4\\%$ to $30.0\\%$, outperforming existing tuners and coding agents while transferring experience across GPUs. As it improves training infrastructure efficiency, InfraRSI forms a two-level Recursive Self-Improvement loop---revising priors online within runs and distilling experience across clusters---to push foundation model scaling.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:54.942667+00	\N	\N	51b037adf49aa414e021fe567475513c952163f5a08bc3ae8b3f9a785e397d5c	pending	2026-11-10 16:29:54.943082+00	2026-11-10 16:29:54.943084+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
075650ba-0be0-4c19-9a1c-a3d06ec164d6	110c8b74-5d9a-4479-9380-aab19486cc0c	\N	https://deepmind.google/blog/beyond-tree-cover-mapping-the-worlds-forest-types/	Beyond tree cover: mapping the world’s forest types	https://deepmind.google/blog/beyond-tree-cover-mapping-the-worlds-forest-types/	https://deepmind.google/blog/beyond-tree-cover-mapping-the-worlds-forest-types/	\N	A global map categorizing every 10-meter patch of land on Earth into distinct forest types.	A global map categorizing every 10-meter patch of land on Earth into distinct forest types.	research	en	2026-10-22 14:30:24+00	2026-11-09 17:12:35.109357+00	4	\N	6c4109f7ae4c8020661d5492160d38733c80496ed1d0aa16d3026ca60a04d4af	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:35.109965+00	2026-11-10 13:24:47.050454+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
97676484-7497-43ce-bd78-6c3bf445a3b4	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2608.22490v2	Who Pays More for Safety? Measuring the Disparate Cost of Safety Alignment across Languages	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22490	https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22490	Chanwoong Yoon, Jungsoo Park, Alan Ritter	arXiv:2608.22490v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Safety alignment helps models adhere to human values, but it often reduces response utility. We ask a critical but understudied question: Does safety alignment impose the cost equally across language groups? To answer this, we introduce a rigorous protocol to measure the utility loss imposed solely by safety alignment, which we term Safety Cost. Through direct pairwise comparisons between safety-aligned models and their unaligned counterparts, we find a systematic inequity: non-English users consistently bear a higher Safety Cost than English users. We further identify three underlying patterns. First, multiple languages lie in a double-penalty zone, experiencing both weaker safety protection and larger utility loss. Second, certain languages exhibit apparent utility gains that are in fact a consequence of safety filters failing to engage. Third, even high-resource languages pay a larger Safety Cost than English to reach the same level of safety. We show that these disparities arise from both explicit refusals and implicit qualitative differences across multiple dimensions. By accurately measuring the disparate effects of safety alignment, our findings expose a systematic disparity in current safety alignment practices.	arXiv:2608.22490v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Safety alignment helps models adhere to human values, but it often reduces response utility. We ask a critical but understudied question: Does safety alignment impose the cost equally across language groups? To answer this, we introduce a rigorous protocol to measure the utility loss imposed solely by safety alignment, which we term Safety Cost. Through direct pairwise comparisons between safety-aligned models and their unaligned counterparts, we find a systematic inequity: non-English users consistently bear a higher Safety Cost than English users. We further identify three underlying patterns. First, multiple languages lie in a double-penalty zone, experiencing both weaker safety protection and larger utility loss. Second, certain languages exhibit apparent utility gains that are in fact a consequence of safety filters failing to engage. Third, even high-resource languages pay a larger Safety Cost than English to reach the same level of safety. We show that these disparities arise from both explicit refusals and implicit qualitative differences across multiple dimensions. By accurately measuring the disparate effects of safety alignment, our findings expose a systematic disparity in current safety alignment practices.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.22123+00	\N	\N	1472abf861bbda2758e2c5000eb7c494e010528b81d1590fe927ec88dd5de3c5	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.221618+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.221621+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
43516582-3ec1-49f0-b40e-4d8f886daccb	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.30849v2	Enhancing Assessment of Self-Consistency in LLM Explanations using Perturbation Strength	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30849	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30849	Phuong Q. Le, Kemal Kurniawan, Jey Han Lau	arXiv:2609.30849v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Prior work has examined the self-consistency of LLM-generated explanations using surface-level perturbation methods. However, the strength of these perturbations is not explicitly measured and controlled. In this work, we propose an LLM-as-a-judge approach to measure perturbation strength in a unified manner across input and CoT perturbations. We then evaluate the self-consistency in explanations generated from various LLMs under controlled strength conditions, ensuring a fair comparison across perturbation types. Experiments show that our proposed LLM-based perturbation strength measure outperforms other embedding- and probability-based approaches and that input perturbations generally affect LLMs more strongly than CoT perturbations. Our work suggests that judgments about a model's self-consistency is fair only within the same perturbation type.	arXiv:2609.30849v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Prior work has examined the self-consistency of LLM-generated explanations using surface-level perturbation methods. However, the strength of these perturbations is not explicitly measured and controlled. In this work, we propose an LLM-as-a-judge approach to measure perturbation strength in a unified manner across input and CoT perturbations. We then evaluate the self-consistency in explanations generated from various LLMs under controlled strength conditions, ensuring a fair comparison across perturbation types. Experiments show that our proposed LLM-based perturbation strength measure outperforms other embedding- and probability-based approaches and that input perturbations generally affect LLMs more strongly than CoT perturbations. Our work suggests that judgments about a model's self-consistency is fair only within the same perturbation type.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.23672+00	\N	\N	d89169a72d4808904a2bbbc02587adb511972e959ecb82a0684870dbe123e2d9	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.237171+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.237174+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
0145e1f9-ce89-4326-81d1-382fae2c2629	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.30906v3	ToolSearcher: Optimizing Tool Selection at Scale via Reinforcement Learning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30906	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30906	Zhenlong Dai, Xujie Song, Zitong Wang, Tong Niu, Jian liu, Weiqiang Wang, Xiu Tang, Sai Wu, Chang Yao, Jingyuan Chen	arXiv:2609.30906v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language models (LLMs) excel at natural language processing but struggle to interact with external environments. Tool learning provides a promising way to extend LLMs into actionable agents, where tool selection is a critical prerequisite for successful tool use. Existing work often assumes a small or predefined set of tools, leaving large-scale tool selection underexplored. Real-world repositories contain a vast and diverse array of tools, making it difficult for LLMs to effectively search, distinguish, and compose tools under context-length constraints. We identify large-scale tool selection as a new challenge for agentic reinforcement learning, highlighting that existing RL methods for knowledge-based question answering are inadequate for selecting tools while considering compatibility. To address this challenge, we propose ToolSearcher, a novel RL framework for effective multi-turn search and fine-grained optimization in large-scale tool selection. Specifically, we introduce category-constrained tool discrimination to improve the model's ability to distinguish functionally similar tools, event-level search modeling to explicitly optimize the discovery of target tools during multi-turn search, and trajectory-aligned credit allocation to provide fine-grained reward signals for different stages of the search-selection process. Extensive experiments on large-scale tool selection benchmarks demonstrate that ToolSearcher consistently outperforms a set of strong baselines in challenging settings involving iterative search and complex tool composition.	arXiv:2609.30906v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language models (LLMs) excel at natural language processing but struggle to interact with external environments. Tool learning provides a promising way to extend LLMs into actionable agents, where tool selection is a critical prerequisite for successful tool use. Existing work often assumes a small or predefined set of tools, leaving large-scale tool selection underexplored. Real-world repositories contain a vast and diverse array of tools, making it difficult for LLMs to effectively search, distinguish, and compose tools under context-length constraints. We identify large-scale tool selection as a new challenge for agentic reinforcement learning, highlighting that existing RL methods for knowledge-based question answering are inadequate for selecting tools while considering compatibility. To address this challenge, we propose ToolSearcher, a novel RL framework for effective multi-turn search and fine-grained optimization in large-scale tool selection. Specifically, we introduce category-constrained tool discrimination to improve the model's ability to distinguish functionally similar tools, event-level search modeling to explicitly optimize the discovery of target tools during multi-turn search, and trajectory-aligned credit allocation to provide fine-grained reward signals for different stages of the search-selection process. Extensive experiments on large-scale tool selection benchmarks demonstrate that ToolSearcher consistently outperforms a set of strong baselines in challenging settings involving iterative search and complex tool composition.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.239148+00	\N	\N	ad8f0533321a931e5c024e1821d183b6553335f2a2c920e01391a1be5ad4da0a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.239478+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.23948+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
4cebf2ce-15b0-48cc-9d0c-9dea6eb59384	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.34660v3	Rewarding Novel Deductions: Solver-guided Process Supervision for Logical Reasoning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34660	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34660	Muhammad Asif Ali, Wenqing Wang, Huan Wang, Mohammad Raza	arXiv:2609.34660v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Logical reasoning remains a major challenge for large language models (LLMs), particularly on structured problems that require precise constraint tracking, consistency preservation, and multi-step deduction. This challenge is especially acute for small-scale LLMs, which are more prone to producing inconsistent, redundant, or brittle reasoning trajectories. Existing approaches for improving logical reasoning largely optimize for final-answer correctness, providing only weak supervision over the intermediate reasoning process. In this work, we propose SPRING: (Solver-guided Process Rewards for Novel LogIcal ReasoNing Step Generation). SPRING uses an SMT solver as a training-time verifier of intermediate reasoning steps to provide process-level supervision. It introduces the notion of a novel reasoning step, namely, a step that is logically valid, consistent with the evolving reasoning state, and not already implied by previously accepted non-contradictory deductions. Based on this solver-based assessment, it designs process rewards that encourage novel inferential progress while penalizing contradictory and uninformative reasoning steps. Evaluation across three logical reasoning benchmarks: ZebraLogic, AR-LSAT, and Knights and Knaves, and four LLMs shows that SPRING consistently outperforms base LLMs, outcome-only reward baselines, and Logic-LM. On ZebraLogic, SPRING improves puzzle accuracy by up to 49.71 and 15.43 points over the base LLM and strongest outcome-only baseline, respectively. On AR-LSAT, it improves overall accuracy by up to 64.43 and 11.39 percentage points over the base LLM and strongest outcome-only baseline, respectively. On Knights and Knaves, SPRING achieves up to 93.14 puzzle accuracy and 96.05 person accuracy.	arXiv:2609.34660v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Logical reasoning remains a major challenge for large language models (LLMs), particularly on structured problems that require precise constraint tracking, consistency preservation, and multi-step deduction. This challenge is especially acute for small-scale LLMs, which are more prone to producing inconsistent, redundant, or brittle reasoning trajectories. Existing approaches for improving logical reasoning largely optimize for final-answer correctness, providing only weak supervision over the intermediate reasoning process. In this work, we propose SPRING: (Solver-guided Process Rewards for Novel LogIcal ReasoNing Step Generation). SPRING uses an SMT solver as a training-time verifier of intermediate reasoning steps to provide process-level supervision. It introduces the notion of a novel reasoning step, namely, a step that is logically valid, consistent with the evolving reasoning state, and not already implied by previously accepted non-contradictory deductions. Based on this solver-based assessment, it designs process rewards that encourage novel inferential progress while penalizing contradictory and uninformative reasoning steps. Evaluation across three logical reasoning benchmarks: ZebraLogic, AR-LSAT, and Knights and Knaves, and four LLMs shows that SPRING consistently outperforms base LLMs, outcome-only reward baselines, and Logic-LM. On ZebraLogic, SPRING improves puzzle accuracy by up to 49.71 and 15.43 points over the base LLM and strongest outcome-only baseline, respectively. On AR-LSAT, it improves overall accuracy by up to 64.43 and 11.39 percentage points over the base LLM and strongest outcome-only baseline, respectively. On Knights and Knaves, SPRING achieves up to 93.14 puzzle accuracy and 96.05 person accuracy.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.241263+00	\N	\N	1a41e7cb9566f1e1466fa9630999ccf97617c1d1688f0db2c067992ffa8fc95b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.241652+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.241654+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
fd215b67-6197-473b-ae34-ba2340843135	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.35279v2	Measuring Collapse and Correction in Homogeneous-Panel LLM Debate	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35279	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35279	Xin Li, Mengbing Liu, Chau Yuen	arXiv:2609.35279v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Multi-agent large language model (LLM) debate is often evaluated by whether final answers improve, but a changed answer is not necessarily a better one: the same discussion can correct an initially wrong majority or collapse an initially correct one. Standard final-accuracy evaluations conflate these opposing transitions. We introduce an auditable protocol for homogeneous debate on multiple-choice questions (MCQs) that records each run as a transition ledger over collapse, correction, onset, and signed intervention utility. On 6,925 MMLU-Pro debates, the protocol records 253 collapses together with the corrections that offset them, and the correction side changes how interventions should be judged. Replay experiments reveal the central tradeoff: freezing every debate prevents 251 collapses but loses 804 corrections under equal weights, and a leave-one-model-out probe-gated freeze that is tuned for net utility never fires, so collapse prevention alone can recommend the wrong policy. A compact pre-debate 8-probe screen is a triage signal: its unadjusted family-level association with conditional-collapse risk is high (G=7, Spearman $\\rho$ = 0.893, exact two-sided p = 0.0123), but initial-majority accuracy is a close comparator ($\\rho$ = -0.821; family partial $\\rho$ = 0.767, p = 0.0877), so we do not treat it as calibrated or capability-adjusted prediction. Round-level traces localize many collapses to the first debate round, where early disagreement can precede both collapses and corrections. Reasoning-mode models, including one model at two reasoning-effort levels, also show both collapses and corrections. We release replayable schemas, coders, audits, cost cards, and zero-API rebuild scripts so future model rows can be compared under the same denominators and signed utility ledger.	arXiv:2609.35279v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Multi-agent large language model (LLM) debate is often evaluated by whether final answers improve, but a changed answer is not necessarily a better one: the same discussion can correct an initially wrong majority or collapse an initially correct one. Standard final-accuracy evaluations conflate these opposing transitions. We introduce an auditable protocol for homogeneous debate on multiple-choice questions (MCQs) that records each run as a transition ledger over collapse, correction, onset, and signed intervention utility. On 6,925 MMLU-Pro debates, the protocol records 253 collapses together with the corrections that offset them, and the correction side changes how interventions should be judged. Replay experiments reveal the central tradeoff: freezing every debate prevents 251 collapses but loses 804 corrections under equal weights, and a leave-one-model-out probe-gated freeze that is tuned for net utility never fires, so collapse prevention alone can recommend the wrong policy. A compact pre-debate 8-probe screen is a triage signal: its unadjusted family-level association with conditional-collapse risk is high (G=7, Spearman $\\rho$ = 0.893, exact two-sided p = 0.0123), but initial-majority accuracy is a close comparator ($\\rho$ = -0.821; family partial $\\rho$ = 0.767, p = 0.0877), so we do not treat it as calibrated or capability-adjusted prediction. Round-level traces localize many collapses to the first debate round, where early disagreement can precede both collapses and corrections. Reasoning-mode models, including one model at two reasoning-effort levels, also show both collapses and corrections. We release replayable schemas, coders, audits, cost cards, and zero-API rebuild scripts so future model rows can be compared under the same denominators and signed utility ledger.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.243441+00	\N	\N	c4cfd6adf1cf1429d27e7bd70cfd207948397a21429bd00313733c3cac92c808	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.243748+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.24375+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a5c1b4c7-ac1e-460b-aaa5-c60a69f7fdd5	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.24184v2	Multi-author style detection with stylistic embeddings	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24184	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24184	Man-Ling Sung, Chak Fai Li, Damianos Karakos, Fred Bernardin, Brian Ulicny	arXiv:2610.24184v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We investigate the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in multi-author style change detection. Using off-the-shelf LLMs or supervised fine-tuning solely within the target domain fails to yield effective features; for this reason, we propose cross-domain transfer learning with LLM-based embeddings fine-tuned for authorship verification. We fine-tune Mistral-7B embedding model and compare it with DeBERTa, used in the state-of-the-art (SOTA) systems in PAN-25 multi-author writing style analysis challenge. Results show that DeBERTa and Mistral excel in different user-generated text scenarios. We exploit this diversity by combining both embeddings, leading to more effective style change detection.	arXiv:2610.24184v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We investigate the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in multi-author style change detection. Using off-the-shelf LLMs or supervised fine-tuning solely within the target domain fails to yield effective features; for this reason, we propose cross-domain transfer learning with LLM-based embeddings fine-tuned for authorship verification. We fine-tune Mistral-7B embedding model and compare it with DeBERTa, used in the state-of-the-art (SOTA) systems in PAN-25 multi-author writing style analysis challenge. Results show that DeBERTa and Mistral excel in different user-generated text scenarios. We exploit this diversity by combining both embeddings, leading to more effective style change detection.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.254253+00	\N	\N	76eb20d58b0d8b7ec098adc52f23d1fb8b4690a92663bff0aae9083af10c60e1	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.254568+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.25457+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
6a06d433-f2d3-4e27-9d44-1c02eb16ec38	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.01370v2	XDecideBench: Evaluating Typed Decision Models and Confidence Policies Across Languages	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01370	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01370	Marek \\v{S}uppa, Jaroslav Kop\\v{c}an	arXiv:2611.01370v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Typed Decision Models such as Jev, Clef and pplx-decider return a probability for each answer an application provides. Applications use them in many languages, but are compared mostly in English. We introduce XDecideBench, a multilingual benchmark of nine suites in 122 languages. On our twelve-language suite, three open models fine-tuned for typed decisions beat their Qwen base models in every language. On new test data with local base models, their accuracy gain is larger in languages with less web text than in those with more. They fix a similar share of base-model errors in both, so the larger gain may reflect weaker base models. The best model on the twelve-language suite is best on only two other suites. The best English model on each suite, chosen in hindsight, is significantly beaten in about a quarter of suite and language combinations. On Belebele, Decision-1 is best in English but falls far behind Jev in Burmese. A confidence threshold fitted on English to allow 5% errors gives a higher error rate on XNLI, even in German and French. XDecideBench shows that results from one suite or from English are a poor guide to how a model and its threshold behave in other languages.	arXiv:2611.01370v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Typed Decision Models such as Jev, Clef and pplx-decider return a probability for each answer an application provides. Applications use them in many languages, but are compared mostly in English. We introduce XDecideBench, a multilingual benchmark of nine suites in 122 languages. On our twelve-language suite, three open models fine-tuned for typed decisions beat their Qwen base models in every language. On new test data with local base models, their accuracy gain is larger in languages with less web text than in those with more. They fix a similar share of base-model errors in both, so the larger gain may reflect weaker base models. The best model on the twelve-language suite is best on only two other suites. The best English model on each suite, chosen in hindsight, is significantly beaten in about a quarter of suite and language combinations. On Belebele, Decision-1 is best in English but falls far behind Jev in Burmese. A confidence threshold fitted on English to allow 5% errors gives a higher error rate on XNLI, even in German and French. XDecideBench shows that results from one suite or from English are a poor guide to how a model and its threshold behave in other languages.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.273617+00	\N	\N	64418416d423728acc9ac12a8ae52dd337eb75259a55e1e658db99facbfb6567	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.273918+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.273919+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
aba31f5a-4f13-4062-bec9-f0b1aae2ba67	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.03196v2	Dhara Bench: An Evidence-Oriented Retrieval Benchmark for Indian Knowledge Systems	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03196	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03196	Avinash Holenarasipur Nagendra, Anand Kaniyaar Sriranga Prasad, Kousik Sarathy Sridharan, Mohan Raghavan	arXiv:2611.03196v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Dhara Bench is a retrieval benchmark for Indian Knowledge Systems (IKS), with 325 questions in 12 categories and 60,121 text chunks from 15 source groups. The categories were developed based on 4,500 questions submitted to the Dhara application; the evaluation set combines corpus-aligned application queries with expert-contributed questions. The annotations comprise 7,578 question-chunk relevance links involving 6,847 distinct corpus chunks. For 34 composite and multi-hop questions, 401 conjugate groups specify alternative sets of passages that jointly support each question. Chunk identifiers and hierarchical references connect the judgments to their source texts. The paper describes corpus preparation, annotation, and validation, and reports a fixed BM25 baseline implemented with Pyserini with $k_1=0.9$ and $b=0.4$. Across all questions, BM25 achieves MRR@20 of 0.315, Recall@10 of 0.182, and nDCG@10 of 0.205. On the group-annotated subset, complete-evidence scores are CE_Recall@20 = 0.294 and CE_MRR@20 = 0.141. The category results expose particularly weak verse retrieval, while group scores reveal gaps between finding individual passages and completing their support. The corpus and evaluation code are available at https://github.com/Avinash2236/Dhara-Bench.	arXiv:2611.03196v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Dhara Bench is a retrieval benchmark for Indian Knowledge Systems (IKS), with 325 questions in 12 categories and 60,121 text chunks from 15 source groups. The categories were developed based on 4,500 questions submitted to the Dhara application; the evaluation set combines corpus-aligned application queries with expert-contributed questions. The annotations comprise 7,578 question-chunk relevance links involving 6,847 distinct corpus chunks. For 34 composite and multi-hop questions, 401 conjugate groups specify alternative sets of passages that jointly support each question. Chunk identifiers and hierarchical references connect the judgments to their source texts. The paper describes corpus preparation, annotation, and validation, and reports a fixed BM25 baseline implemented with Pyserini with $k_1=0.9$ and $b=0.4$. Across all questions, BM25 achieves MRR@20 of 0.315, Recall@10 of 0.182, and nDCG@10 of 0.205. On the group-annotated subset, complete-evidence scores are CE_Recall@20 = 0.294 and CE_MRR@20 = 0.141. The category results expose particularly weak verse retrieval, while group scores reveal gaps between finding individual passages and completing their support. The corpus and evaluation code are available at https://github.com/Avinash2236/Dhara-Bench.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.435172+00	\N	\N	833242152fdfe9988a5a77561e8741f770d46d7d959ba3a393f2e0abcdcc6da8	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.435562+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.435564+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
3abbd9a2-7fdb-403c-8c50-f9be94f3dbf3	f8782f3e-cbda-4809-b208-87367f54d3c8	\N	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/claude-code-settings-files-expose-thousands-of-secrets-on-github/	Claude Code is accidentally exposing secrets all over GitHub	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/claude-code-settings-files-expose-thousands-of-secrets-on-github/	https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/claude-code-settings-files-expose-thousands-of-secrets-on-github/	Kyle Orland	"Do not ask again" prompt leads to passwords, API keys, and more being shared.	Claude Code is leaking sensitive information on public GitHub instances thanks to a partial oversight in the way its settings files are handled. That's the finding of AI security researchers at Apiiro, who discovered thousands of secrets , including database passwords, API tokens, and root logins, amid Claude Code settings files embedded in GitHub repositories. The leak stems from the way Claude Code handles user approval for automated shell commands. When a user chooses the "Yes, and don't ask again" option to approve running a command, that choice gets stored as part of a rule in the local ".claude/settings.local.json" file on the user's computer. That rule is sometimes coded as a pattern, but the command itself is often written to that settings file as is, including any sensitive information that may be embedded within it. This isn't really a significant security issue if the settings file stays on the user's local machine (assuming that machine doesn't become compromised itself, of course). And Anthropic notes in its Claude Code documentation that this particular settings file gets added to the developer's global .gitignore list , which usually prevents it from being shared publicly. Read full article Comments	news	en	2026-11-09 17:24:26+00	2026-11-10 16:29:52.952675+00	4	\N	a2a7ca70e4ba10c503ab995f96c21f48b636f8dbc905e88d86b0f2b0251b4053	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:52.953049+00	2026-11-10 16:30:25.203335+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
677b0cd4-f1b1-4601-bb4c-e0fe13281bf4	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2510.23891v2	OpenMarkX: An Extensible Platform for Watermarking Open-Source Model and Agent Outputs	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23891	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23891	Jiaqi Xue, Yifei Zhao, Mansour Al Ghanim, Shangqian Gao, Ruimin Sun, Qian Lou, Mengxin Zheng	arXiv:2510.23891v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We introduce OpenMarkX, an extensible platform for watermarking open-source model and agent outputs under standard inference. It takes models, compatible methods, private keys, and evaluation settings, and produces watermarked weights, detection results, and quality and robustness reports. Its 16 text-watermark configurations are complemented by agent, vision-language, image, and physics-field workflows. Our proposed method, OpenMark, addresses learnability and robustness through joint model--policy learning and perturbation-aware training. On several open-source LLMs, OpenMark achieves 0.92 TPR at 1% FPR while reducing perplexity by 20.5% on average relative to the strongest prior method, and remains more detectable after such modifications, e.g., retaining 0.57 TPR at 5% FPR after 0.5-ratio model merging, compared with at most 0.34 for prior methods.	arXiv:2510.23891v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: We introduce OpenMarkX, an extensible platform for watermarking open-source model and agent outputs under standard inference. It takes models, compatible methods, private keys, and evaluation settings, and produces watermarked weights, detection results, and quality and robustness reports. Its 16 text-watermark configurations are complemented by agent, vision-language, image, and physics-field workflows. Our proposed method, OpenMark, addresses learnability and robustness through joint model--policy learning and perturbation-aware training. On several open-source LLMs, OpenMark achieves 0.92 TPR at 1% FPR while reducing perplexity by 20.5% on average relative to the strongest prior method, and remains more detectable after such modifications, e.g., retaining 0.57 TPR at 5% FPR after 0.5-ratio model merging, compared with at most 0.34 for prior methods.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:56.355806+00	4	\N	adee354d0d333f7205b421e4a551fef8e05061eda2b017d155a5af30a199354a	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:56.356127+00	2026-11-10 16:34:22.156737+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5c872727-798f-43e2-a924-3395291aa584	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.07524v3	Stagewise Reinforcement Learning and the Geometry of the Regret Landscape	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07524	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07524	Chris Elliott, Einar Urdshals, David Quarel, Matthew Farrugia-Roberts, Daniel Murfet	arXiv:2601.07524v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Singular learning theory characterizes Bayesian learning as an evolving tradeoff between accuracy and complexity, with transitions between qualitatively different solutions as sample size increases. We extend this theory to reinforcement learning, proving that the concentration of a generalized posterior over policies is governed by the local learning coefficient (LLC), an invariant of the geometry of the regret function. This theory predicts that deep reinforcement learning with SGD should proceed from simple policies with high regret to complex policies with low regret. We verify this prediction empirically in a gridworld environment exhibiting stagewise policy development: phase transitions over training manifest as "opposing staircases" where regret decreases sharply while the LLC increases.	arXiv:2601.07524v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Singular learning theory characterizes Bayesian learning as an evolving tradeoff between accuracy and complexity, with transitions between qualitatively different solutions as sample size increases. We extend this theory to reinforcement learning, proving that the concentration of a generalized posterior over policies is governed by the local learning coefficient (LLC), an invariant of the geometry of the regret function. This theory predicts that deep reinforcement learning with SGD should proceed from simple policies with high regret to complex policies with low regret. We verify this prediction empirically in a gridworld environment exhibiting stagewise policy development: phase transitions over training manifest as "opposing staircases" where regret decreases sharply while the LLC increases.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.886988+00	3	\N	d6fad06d9768d625c397f3a33261ad48a4ea6c9651ff3e93af5164d4c35893d6	processed	2026-11-10 16:30:02.887327+00	2026-11-10 16:34:29.136834+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
cb9c667a-531e-464a-88f0-fb68137ce0b0	ffdef1b3-f1c0-4fa1-a6e3-8c350077f091	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10679v1	RankShift: Evaluating Ranking Instability Across Benchmark Dimensions in Document Parsing	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10679	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10679	Halimat Afolabi, Daeyoung Lee	arXiv:2611.10679v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Document parsing benchmarks are often treated as stable measures of model quality, yet rankings may depend substantially on benchmark design choices. In this work, we present RankShift, a systematic study comparing OCR and vision-language models (VLMs) under controlled variation in dataset composition, matching strategy, and scoring methodology. Using OmniDocBench as a case study, we evaluate the ranking stability of 16 models across 3 dataset configurations, 6 matching strategies, and 3 scoring metrics. We quantify rank stability using pairwise rank correlation, top-k churn, and pairwise reversal rate. Our results show that the interaction between matching strategy and ground truth format is a substantial but understudied driver of instability: scoring against OmniDocBench's JSON annotations versus its Markdown rendering yields a mean Kendall's tau of approximately 0.33 under region-level matching strategies, with individual pairs as low as tau = 0.23, compared with tau = 0.77 when each page is scored as a single concatenated string. Dataset composition produces comparable instability across page-type strata (mean tau = 0.48) and layout-type strata (mean tau = 0.49), while the cross-dataset shift from OmniDocBench to Real5's Scanning reconstruction of the same pages is more stable (tau = 0.80). Metric choice is the most stable dimension, with mean tau of approximately 0.78 across NED, BLEU, and METEOR. These findings indicate that leaderboard position is not solely a property of model capability, motivating leaderboards that report not only headline scores but also the robustness of rankings across reasonable design choices.	arXiv: