0a72f4bb-159d-43f7-aca3-cd2dc189a229	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics	Sharing AI progress in mathematics	https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics	https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics	\N	OpenAI publishes new results on open problems in mathematics from an internal frontier model and shares Lean proof formalizations and research details on GitHub.	OpenAI publishes new results on open problems in mathematics from an internal frontier model and shares Lean proof formalizations and research details on GitHub.	research	en	2026-10-06 12:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.091117+00	4	\N	e169566049280ff8a5cf8e97512969a28e1ca1f510be9fd1878cf93fd106c9da	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.091477+00	2026-11-10 14:37:26.399907+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
aec29886-9918-4f68-a059-e7b07be4b0e4	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10322v1	Police Behavior Patterns Through Radio Analysis	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10322	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10322	Milo Z. Trujillo, Adam Perhala, Elaine Zou, Tyler Mckenzie, Juniper Lovato, Brooke Foucault Welles, Alyssa Hasegawa Smith	arXiv:2611.10322v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: In the United States, most police departments communicate using police radio, which allows for officer-to-officer and officer-to-dispatch communication without depending on cellular network or WiFi availability. While some departments use encrypted digital radio, many continue to transmit in the clear, and are legally monitored by public citizens with `police scanners.' Because police radio is intended for internal police use and represents real-time communication, it may offer valuable insight into patterns of law enforcement activities without the potential bias and filtering introduced by a press release after the fact. In this study, we collect and examine over 14 million radio transmissions representing over 100,000 hours of speech by officers in 34 cities across the United States from July 2025 through July 2026; to our knowledge the largest scale analysis of Broadcast Police Communication (BPC) to date. We highlight both typical patterns of police communication and categorize anomalous activity that represents incidents including protests and major weather events. We present this dataset as a starting point for an array of police communication studies, conduct our own preliminary analysis, and propose several further areas of inquiry.	arXiv:2611.10322v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: In the United States, most police departments communicate using police radio, which allows for officer-to-officer and officer-to-dispatch communication without depending on cellular network or WiFi availability. While some departments use encrypted digital radio, many continue to transmit in the clear, and are legally monitored by public citizens with `police scanners.' Because police radio is intended for internal police use and represents real-time communication, it may offer valuable insight into patterns of law enforcement activities without the potential bias and filtering introduced by a press release after the fact. In this study, we collect and examine over 14 million radio transmissions representing over 100,000 hours of speech by officers in 34 cities across the United States from July 2025 through July 2026; to our knowledge the largest scale analysis of Broadcast Police Communication (BPC) to date. We highlight both typical patterns of police communication and categorize anomalous activity that represents incidents including protests and major weather events. We present this dataset as a starting point for an array of police communication studies, conduct our own preliminary analysis, and propose several further areas of inquiry.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.986028+00	\N	\N	a2e4eaf4f9d6ca1453d08d26625c84a35caef4ac5638e0caee6ab9389ad8eec0	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.986392+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.986393+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
05e3856f-4456-4eec-8732-0235f22de34a	a030efb9-b99a-47ce-adca-332347828e88	\N	https://techcrunch.com/?p=3186034	OpenAI reportedly raises an additional $11 billion at a $1.25 trillion valuation	https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/06/openai-reportedly-raises-an-additional-11-billion-at-a-1-25-trillion-valuation/	https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/06/openai-reportedly-raises-an-additional-11-billion-at-a-1-25-trillion-valuation/	Lucas Ropek	OpenAI has reportedly raised an additional $11 billion as part of a funding round that hasn't closed yet but is expected to in the coming weeks.	OpenAI has reportedly raised an additional $11 billion as part of a funding round that hasn't closed yet but is expected to in the coming weeks.	news	en	2026-11-06 22:53:46+00	2026-11-09 17:12:39.775281+00	5	\N	82ce36468c12e2c0a218260b870d978ff9c959e45974ab7a8e044ba1ef8016e8	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:39.775684+00	2026-11-10 14:44:58.060243+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
f0aef726-84fb-4970-9116-a6af77af2d0b	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.10341v1	Collaboration Signatures: Tracking Long-Horizon Dynamics of Cross-Meeting Collaboration Networks	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10341	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10341	Shengqi Zhu, Sheshera Mysore, Bahareh Sarrafzadeh	arXiv:2611.10341v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Enterprise platforms have received increasing attention as a core component of social networks and the Web. However, existing work mostly relies on fragmented logs of small units of work, like emails and chats, and focuses on who connects with whom without sufficient account of collaboration content and dynamics. This work systematically explores tracking collaborations across sequences of meetings as a new approach to interpreting their long-horizon dynamics. Linking 45,426 meetings in a large multinational technology company based on full transcripts and 1.6 million extracted agenda items, we establish a cross-meeting collaboration network of 6,065 collaborations. The network recovers the natural shapes of workplace collaborations: meetings, as snapshots of ongoing collaboration, link into largely tree-shaped structures, while the work content forms nested directed acyclic graphs (DAGs). Further, we explore how these networks evolve over time, examining three established perspectives: task division, team composition, and temporal continuity. Each explains part of the dynamics but does not capture the distinctness of long-horizon workplace collaboration: e.g., work flows in a consistent direction between a few pairs of subjects, yet a collaboration's subject explains little of how its meetings are conducted. Instead, accumulating the agenda, interaction, and language of meetings along each tree yields a signature of the collaboration, a distinctive profile that picks out the collaboration of a held-out meeting at 4.5 times chance. The signature models collaboration better than team or task views, individually or combined, in next-meeting prediction and tree-level fitting. We discuss the implications of these cross-meeting dynamics for designing office applications and support tools, and call for next-generation Web systems that represent collaborations over their long horizon.	arXiv:2611.10341v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Enterprise platforms have received increasing attention as a core component of social networks and the Web. However, existing work mostly relies on fragmented logs of small units of work, like emails and chats, and focuses on who connects with whom without sufficient account of collaboration content and dynamics. This work systematically explores tracking collaborations across sequences of meetings as a new approach to interpreting their long-horizon dynamics. Linking 45,426 meetings in a large multinational technology company based on full transcripts and 1.6 million extracted agenda items, we establish a cross-meeting collaboration network of 6,065 collaborations. The network recovers the natural shapes of workplace collaborations: meetings, as snapshots of ongoing collaboration, link into largely tree-shaped structures, while the work content forms nested directed acyclic graphs (DAGs). Further, we explore how these networks evolve over time, examining three established perspectives: task division, team composition, and temporal continuity. Each explains part of the dynamics but does not capture the distinctness of long-horizon workplace collaboration: e.g., work flows in a consistent direction between a few pairs of subjects, yet a collaboration's subject explains little of how its meetings are conducted. Instead, accumulating the agenda, interaction, and language of meetings along each tree yields a signature of the collaboration, a distinctive profile that picks out the collaboration of a held-out meeting at 4.5 times chance. The signature models collaboration better than team or task views, individually or combined, in next-meeting prediction and tree-level fitting. We discuss the implications of these cross-meeting dynamics for designing office applications and support tools, and call for next-generation Web systems that represent collaborations over their long horizon.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.990761+00	\N	\N	1fcf5f299135773f735f28e44d75e10c3fe2b46f90061733dd692ed779d0a6f2	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.991108+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.99111+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
