72c9d8a4-b5cd-4311-98ad-bf8051d9ccbc	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.11592v1	LAION-Audio-XL: A Web-Scale Corpus and Scaling Studies for Contrastive Language-Audio Pretraining	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11592	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11592	Gijs Wijngaard, Felix F\\"orster, Ross Wightman, Marianna Nezhurina, Christoph Schuhmann, Jenia Jitsev, A. Sophia Koepke	arXiv:2611.11592v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Recent progress on contrastive audio-language models can be attributed mainly to the scale and quality of their training data. Larger training datasets expose models to a broader range of sounds and corresponding audio captions. To exploit this, we collect 1.48 billion audio samples from academic datasets, internet datasets, web collections, Common Crawl, and YouTube, and process them with a multi-stage curation pipeline. Furthermore, we generate short and long captions for samples in our corpus that lack them. We use our dataset to study the scaling behavior of contrastive audio-language models based on the NaFlex architecture for the audio encoder. Our experiments identify trade-offs between model size and training data for a given compute budget. Moreover, our resulting models, CLAP2, outperform prior audio-text models of comparable size on the MAEB and HEAR benchmarks. We release our dataset, captions, model checkpoints, and code.	arXiv:2611.11592v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Recent progress on contrastive audio-language models can be attributed mainly to the scale and quality of their training data. Larger training datasets expose models to a broader range of sounds and corresponding audio captions. To exploit this, we collect 1.48 billion audio samples from academic datasets, internet datasets, web collections, Common Crawl, and YouTube, and process them with a multi-stage curation pipeline. Furthermore, we generate short and long captions for samples in our corpus that lack them. We use our dataset to study the scaling behavior of contrastive audio-language models based on the NaFlex architecture for the audio encoder. Our experiments identify trade-offs between model size and training data for a given compute budget. Moreover, our resulting models, CLAP2, outperform prior audio-text models of comparable size on the MAEB and HEAR benchmarks. We release our dataset, captions, model checkpoints, and code.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.056195+00	\N	\N	f747989b2998b5209f6dbb851f5eb96f44230e7313e806a3f7b3352acc10ff85	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.056445+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.056446+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
