0772f022-5d4c-4b07-aad4-6fe0e5cbeed6	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/block-ai-powered-security	How Block is building AI-powered security with OpenAI technology	https://openai.com/index/block-ai-powered-security	https://openai.com/index/block-ai-powered-security	\N	Block is building AI-powered security with OpenAI. Its security team investigates threats faster and proposes patches in minutes.	Block is building AI-powered security with OpenAI. Its security team investigates threats faster and proposes patches in minutes.	news	en	2026-10-14 07:00:00+00	2026-11-09 17:12:42.060186+00	4	\N	da6bc6318bd430998e083b3474d37b78685dede34aff178ee85c6937c2e2f570	processed	2026-11-09 17:12:42.060656+00	2026-11-10 14:38:11.015152+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
69c75f12-cf24-4779-be4f-73901c4839bf	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.13481v2	A Hybrid Hierarchical 1D-CNN-BiLSTM Framework for Extractive Summarization of Biomedical and Clinical Text	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13481	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13481	Saad Bin Ather, Muhammad Saif, Ali Hassan Khan, Manzer Abbas, Hajra Waheed	arXiv:2609.13481v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language models have made abstractive summarization remarkably fluent, but fluency has come at a cost. Models trained to generate freely over long documents may hallucinate, producing summaries that read well but drift from what the source actually says. This risk is especially serious in biomedical and clinical text, where fabricated details can have serious consequences. We address this by removing free-form generation from the pipeline rather than trying to constrain it. Our Hybrid Hierarchical CNN-LSTM Summarizer reframes summarization as extractive sentence selection: a stacked, multi-kernel convolutional network progressively refines contextual representations, while a bidirectional LSTM models long-range dependencies across the document. A lightweight scoring head predicts sentence importance using binary cross-entropy supervision. For PubMed, a document-adaptive mean-plus-standard-deviation threshold with a Top-3 fallback determines extraction; clinical evaluations instead use a fixed top-30\\% sentence-selection policy. Because output sentences are copied verbatim, the framework prevents source-external generation, although omissions and contextual inaccuracies remain possible. Evaluated on PubMed and transferred without clinical fine-tuning to MIMIC-CXR and MIMIC-IV BHC, the model demonstrates competitive extractive summarization performance, with threshold and component ablations supporting adaptive extraction and the combined CNN--BiLSTM architecture. Clinical factuality analysis further highlights limitations in preserving important findings and negations. These results suggest that structural constraints offer a promising direction for source-grounded biomedical summarization, while emphasizing the importance of clinical completeness and contextual fidelity.	arXiv:2609.13481v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Large language models have made abstractive summarization remarkably fluent, but fluency has come at a cost. Models trained to generate freely over long documents may hallucinate, producing summaries that read well but drift from what the source actually says. This risk is especially serious in biomedical and clinical text, where fabricated details can have serious consequences. We address this by removing free-form generation from the pipeline rather than trying to constrain it. Our Hybrid Hierarchical CNN-LSTM Summarizer reframes summarization as extractive sentence selection: a stacked, multi-kernel convolutional network progressively refines contextual representations, while a bidirectional LSTM models long-range dependencies across the document. A lightweight scoring head predicts sentence importance using binary cross-entropy supervision. For PubMed, a document-adaptive mean-plus-standard-deviation threshold with a Top-3 fallback determines extraction; clinical evaluations instead use a fixed top-30\\% sentence-selection policy. Because output sentences are copied verbatim, the framework prevents source-external generation, although omissions and contextual inaccuracies remain possible. Evaluated on PubMed and transferred without clinical fine-tuning to MIMIC-CXR and MIMIC-IV BHC, the model demonstrates competitive extractive summarization performance, with threshold and component ablations supporting adaptive extraction and the combined CNN--BiLSTM architecture. Clinical factuality analysis further highlights limitations in preserving important findings and negations. These results suggest that structural constraints offer a promising direction for source-grounded biomedical summarization, while emphasizing the importance of clinical completeness and contextual fidelity.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.225681+00	\N	\N	b8257326da108f5a19e84ff9c6e9e3a19b4a5d6e6bd0256fe0b30975b7788a32	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.226+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.226002+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
803ce0c5-b504-4919-a225-aeba906fd367	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.26249v2	PACE-dLLM: Elastic Block Decoding via Confidence Cliff Estimation for Diffusion Language Models	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26249	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26249	Xiaocheng Lu, Shuhan Guo, Ziyue Ma, Jie Zhang, Jian Liu, Jingcai Guo, Haoxuan Che, Song Guo	arXiv:2609.26249v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Diffusion language models (dLLMs), such as LLaDA and Dream, have become competitive with autoregressive (AR) LLMs in generation quality while supporting native parallel decoding. A standard acceleration strategy is block-wise decoding, where each forward pass predicts a block of length B and commits high-confidence tokens. Confidence-threshold commitment adapts which tokens are accepted, but a fixed B still constrains the prediction scope. Existing horizon policies use manually chosen sizes, volatility statistics, or candidate-boundary scores. We instead use the model's own per-step confidence: in-window confidence typically follows a context-dependent cliff, providing a direct signal for adapting the look-ahead horizon. In this work, we propose PACE-dLLM, which refits this parametric cliff periodically in closed form, sets the next horizon at a shared confidence anchor, and retains confidence-threshold token commitment. Under a saturated-yield abstraction with iid, policy-independent monotone profiles, the oracle horizon is the smallest attaining maximal per-pass yield and improves the asymptotic forward-pass rate when fixed blocks undershoot it with positive probability. On four reasoning and code benchmarks across two open-source dLLM backbones, PACE-dLLM achieves the best average accuracy among the evaluated decoders on both backbones, with average throughput speedups of 5.23x on LLaDA and 3.06x on Dream (up to 8.52x on LLaDA MATH) over the unaccelerated semi-AR baseline, advancing the quality-throughput Pareto frontier.	arXiv:2609.26249v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Diffusion language models (dLLMs), such as LLaDA and Dream, have become competitive with autoregressive (AR) LLMs in generation quality while supporting native parallel decoding. A standard acceleration strategy is block-wise decoding, where each forward pass predicts a block of length B and commits high-confidence tokens. Confidence-threshold commitment adapts which tokens are accepted, but a fixed B still constrains the prediction scope. Existing horizon policies use manually chosen sizes, volatility statistics, or candidate-boundary scores. We instead use the model's own per-step confidence: in-window confidence typically follows a context-dependent cliff, providing a direct signal for adapting the look-ahead horizon. In this work, we propose PACE-dLLM, which refits this parametric cliff periodically in closed form, sets the next horizon at a shared confidence anchor, and retains confidence-threshold token commitment. Under a saturated-yield abstraction with iid, policy-independent monotone profiles, the oracle horizon is the smallest attaining maximal per-pass yield and improves the asymptotic forward-pass rate when fixed blocks undershoot it with positive probability. On four reasoning and code benchmarks across two open-source dLLM backbones, PACE-dLLM achieves the best average accuracy among the evaluated decoders on both backbones, with average throughput speedups of 5.23x on LLaDA and 3.06x on Dream (up to 8.52x on LLaDA MATH) over the unaccelerated semi-AR baseline, advancing the quality-throughput Pareto frontier.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.234633+00	\N	\N	c9968322fe2d12b105591e4714677a31439b990ece06a83fa49afcc3c72c6f47	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.234999+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.235001+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
