979fc80d-c461-4d69-bf8b-0b6dbf4cc3a6	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.37675v2	LEMON-ZEST: Evolution-Informed Tokenization for Efficient Protein Language Modeling	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37675	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37675	Biswajit Banerjee, Claudia Alvarez Carreno, Anton S. Petrov	arXiv:2609.37675v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Protein Language Models (PLMs) have made remarkable progress following scaling laws established in natural language processing across sequence- and structure-based tasks, yet the potential of tokenization remains underexploited. Unlike human language, proteins preserve structure despite extensive sequence variation, a property standard tokenization strategies fundamentally fail to capture. We introduce ZEST (Zoned Encoding of Sequence Traits), an evolution-informed vocabulary derived from conserved regions of multiple sequence alignments. ZEST allows embedding domain-level biological priors directly at the tokenization stage rather than learning them implicitly through scale. ZEST natively compresses sequences to an average token length of 3.3 residues, enabling our model to process $\\geq3380$ residues within a standard 1024-token context window. Building on this, we present LEMON (Layered Extraction of Molecular Ordering from Nature), a compact 200M-parameter sequence-based model for detection of remote homology between protein sequences trained on a single H100 GPU for one week. Despite its modest size, LEMON outperforms models of 600M to 3B parameters and is on-par with TM-Vec. Our results demonstrate that evolution-informed tokenization can substitute for massive parameter scaling, opening a new direction for efficient, biologically-grounded protein representation learning. All code and model weights are publicly available (at \\href{https://github.com/LDWLab/ZEST}{github.com/LDWLab/ZEST}) under the MIT license.	arXiv:2609.37675v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Protein Language Models (PLMs) have made remarkable progress following scaling laws established in natural language processing across sequence- and structure-based tasks, yet the potential of tokenization remains underexploited. Unlike human language, proteins preserve structure despite extensive sequence variation, a property standard tokenization strategies fundamentally fail to capture. We introduce ZEST (Zoned Encoding of Sequence Traits), an evolution-informed vocabulary derived from conserved regions of multiple sequence alignments. ZEST allows embedding domain-level biological priors directly at the tokenization stage rather than learning them implicitly through scale. ZEST natively compresses sequences to an average token length of 3.3 residues, enabling our model to process $\\geq3380$ residues within a standard 1024-token context window. Building on this, we present LEMON (Layered Extraction of Molecular Ordering from Nature), a compact 200M-parameter sequence-based model for detection of remote homology between protein sequences trained on a single H100 GPU for one week. Despite its modest size, LEMON outperforms models of 600M to 3B parameters and is on-par with TM-Vec. Our results demonstrate that evolution-informed tokenization can substitute for massive parameter scaling, opening a new direction for efficient, biologically-grounded protein representation learning. All code and model weights are publicly available (at \\href{https://github.com/LDWLab/ZEST}{github.com/LDWLab/ZEST}) under the MIT license.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.392814+00	\N	\N	951dd9cdc950599c741b8dbd91fccc0e26c280cc0ecc114eb5e543fdf2decd2f	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.393179+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.393181+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
