52d3a9e0-4e06-4eb6-9260-ff9bb9dc3a2f	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.12161v1	GPU-Accelerated Non-Interactive Encrypted Neural Network Training with Batched Diagonal Packing	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12161	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12161	Kentaro Mihara	arXiv:2611.12161v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Training a neural network under homomorphic encryption requires transposes, batch reductions, optimizer updates, and repeated refreshes in addition to forward matrix products. We combine rectangular diagonal packing, feature-major SIMD mini-batch lanes, baby-step giant-step evaluation, and compact optimizer state in an MIT-licensed Rust CKKS implementation. Its CUDA path keeps training and bootstrap ciphertext arithmetic on the GPU, while retaining host key generation and client operations. In a matched 100-update experiment on the main non-interactive MNIST model, device-resident bootstrapping reduces training time from 14,268.295 to 293.075 seconds, a $48.68\\times$ ratio against our CPU implementation, with exactly equal decoded parameters and predictions. Including setup and final evaluation gives a $40.67\\times$ ratio. A separate GPU run completes ten passes over 4,800 images in 2,967.095 seconds and achieves 90.35% test accuracy versus 90.41% for its plaintext twin. These are single-initialization results; exact CPU/GPU parameter agreement is verified at 100 updates, not for the full 1,000-update run. Earlier completed CPU runs support a larger-batch key-switch advantage and baseline-batch accuracy within the registered tolerance, but the baseline-batch normalized key-switch target is missed. The released ReBoot baseline does not meet the common security-estimator threshold, and its full-setting CPU time is unmeasured. We therefore distinguish measured acceleration of our implementation from conditional layout comparisons and do not claim unconditional superiority over ReBoot.	arXiv:2611.12161v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Training a neural network under homomorphic encryption requires transposes, batch reductions, optimizer updates, and repeated refreshes in addition to forward matrix products. We combine rectangular diagonal packing, feature-major SIMD mini-batch lanes, baby-step giant-step evaluation, and compact optimizer state in an MIT-licensed Rust CKKS implementation. Its CUDA path keeps training and bootstrap ciphertext arithmetic on the GPU, while retaining host key generation and client operations. In a matched 100-update experiment on the main non-interactive MNIST model, device-resident bootstrapping reduces training time from 14,268.295 to 293.075 seconds, a $48.68\\times$ ratio against our CPU implementation, with exactly equal decoded parameters and predictions. Including setup and final evaluation gives a $40.67\\times$ ratio. A separate GPU run completes ten passes over 4,800 images in 2,967.095 seconds and achieves 90.35% test accuracy versus 90.41% for its plaintext twin. These are single-initialization results; exact CPU/GPU parameter agreement is verified at 100 updates, not for the full 1,000-update run. Earlier completed CPU runs support a larger-batch key-switch advantage and baseline-batch accuracy within the registered tolerance, but the baseline-batch normalized key-switch target is missed. The released ReBoot baseline does not meet the common security-estimator threshold, and its full-setting CPU time is unmeasured. We therefore distinguish measured acceleration of our implementation from conditional layout comparisons and do not claim unconditional superiority over ReBoot.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.685749+00	\N	\N	536170859e281cb521871bb0cd543e231ff2a8527f2f80c10c1bad0c24e1d103	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.6861+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.686102+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
1d0ff098-e18a-4cbb-8195-d4a488d3f7b1	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2511.15856v4	GLOBE: Accurate Surrogates for Boundary-Driven PDEs via Domain-Inspired Architectures and Equivariance	https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15856	https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15856	Peter Sharpe	arXiv:2511.15856v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce GLOBE, a nonlinear neural surrogate for boundary-driven, homogeneous, weakly nonlinear PDEs that draws inductive bias from boundary-element methods and equivariant ML, representing solutions as superpositions of learnable Green's-function-like kernels evaluated from boundary faces to targets. The architecture is translation-, rotation-, and parity-equivariant; discretization-invariant; and units-invariant via rigorous nondimensionalization. On AirFRANS (steady incompressible RANS over NACA airfoils), a compact ~117k-parameter GLOBE model has lower mean-squared error than published values for the strongest reported ML surrogates on both the interpolation and low-data splits, in single-run comparisons under the released AirFRANS normalization. The model evaluates fields at arbitrary points, handles non-watertight meshes, and generalizes under Reynolds number and angle-of-attack distribution shifts. We further introduce a hierarchical Barnes-Hut acceleration that exploits the kernel's far-field decay to reduce evaluation cost from quadratic to near-linear in the number of boundary elements, with a tunable opening angle that recovers exact dense evaluation in the zero-angle limit. Quantitative validation focuses on 2D AirFRANS; on 3D DrivAerML cars, GLOBE predicts surface and volume fields on the full meshes, and we compare it with a matched Transolver baseline. Together, these results show that boundary-element-inspired inductive biases can substantially improve the accuracy of ML surrogates for boundary-driven PDEs while scaling to large 3D meshes.	arXiv:2511.15856v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: We introduce GLOBE, a nonlinear neural surrogate for boundary-driven, homogeneous, weakly nonlinear PDEs that draws inductive bias from boundary-element methods and equivariant ML, representing solutions as superpositions of learnable Green's-function-like kernels evaluated from boundary faces to targets. The architecture is translation-, rotation-, and parity-equivariant; discretization-invariant; and units-invariant via rigorous nondimensionalization. On AirFRANS (steady incompressible RANS over NACA airfoils), a compact ~117k-parameter GLOBE model has lower mean-squared error than published values for the strongest reported ML surrogates on both the interpolation and low-data splits, in single-run comparisons under the released AirFRANS normalization. The model evaluates fields at arbitrary points, handles non-watertight meshes, and generalizes under Reynolds number and angle-of-attack distribution shifts. We further introduce a hierarchical Barnes-Hut acceleration that exploits the kernel's far-field decay to reduce evaluation cost from quadratic to near-linear in the number of boundary elements, with a tunable opening angle that recovers exact dense evaluation in the zero-angle limit. Quantitative validation focuses on 2D AirFRANS; on 3D DrivAerML cars, GLOBE predicts surface and volume fields on the full meshes, and we compare it with a matched Transolver baseline. Together, these results show that boundary-element-inspired inductive biases can substantially improve the accuracy of ML surrogates for boundary-driven PDEs while scaling to large 3D meshes.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.867836+00	\N	\N	c47d267e398ea4f1070507f036b2d484387f42d3ace3935e31af184ca8747455	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.868163+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.868164+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
ec00b0c1-ba76-4b6e-93ab-60519f4cc437	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.31963v2	Signed-Permutation Coordinate Transport for RMSNorm Transformers	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31963	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31963	John Sweeney	arXiv:2606.31963v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern LLM workflows move coordinate-indexed objects across checkpoints: steering vectors, sparse autoencoders, top-$k$ neuron sets, attribution lists, LoRA updates, optimizer states, and merge alignments. This requires fixing the model's residual-stream gauge. We show that the native coordinate-preserving orthogonal gauge is architecture-dependent: LayerNorm residual charts have permutation gauge $S_d$ up to a global sign flip, while RMSNorm charts with generic gains have signed-permutation gauge $B_d = S_d \\ltimes \\{\\pm 1\\}^d$. Permutation-only alignment is thus symmetry-incomplete for RMSNorm models. We introduce sign-marginalized Hungarian matching and prove that with decorrelated coordinates, signed-correlation matching has a permutation-accuracy ceiling equal to the true gauge's positive-sign fraction up to $O(d 2^{-d})$, which sign-marginalized matching removes. Omitting signs induces expected squared error $2\\|\\Delta W\\|_F^2$ on weight updates and loses orientation on vectors and AdamW first moments, while RMSNorm gains and AdamW second moments permute without signs. Controlled tests confirm these laws: a Qwen2.5-1.5B SST-2 LoRA adapter retains 0.944 accuracy (from 0.945) under $B_d$ transport vs. 0.523 under $S_d$, and steering preserves effects to $<10^{-4}$ at zero raw top-10 overlap. Across natural checkpoints, inherited slots suffice for same-base descendants (96.2%-100% retention), whereas cross-generation pairs support sparse prompt-stable subsets with causally decisive negative signs ($+0.185$ vs. $-0.010$ at $8\\sigma$ on Qwen2/2.5-0.5B; $+0.189$ vs. $-0.021$ on 1.5B). Along fine-tuning trajectories, composing local $B_d$ gauges recovers 91.1% of cross-run Qwen2.5-1.5B coordinates at 1,500 steps vs. 60.3% for endpoint matching. Merge and interpretability audits confirm that coordinate names are reproducible only relative to an explicit $B_d$ gauge.	arXiv:2606.31963v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Modern LLM workflows move coordinate-indexed objects across checkpoints: steering vectors, sparse autoencoders, top-$k$ neuron sets, attribution lists, LoRA updates, optimizer states, and merge alignments. This requires fixing the model's residual-stream gauge. We show that the native coordinate-preserving orthogonal gauge is architecture-dependent: LayerNorm residual charts have permutation gauge $S_d$ up to a global sign flip, while RMSNorm charts with generic gains have signed-permutation gauge $B_d = S_d \\ltimes \\{\\pm 1\\}^d$. Permutation-only alignment is thus symmetry-incomplete for RMSNorm models. We introduce sign-marginalized Hungarian matching and prove that with decorrelated coordinates, signed-correlation matching has a permutation-accuracy ceiling equal to the true gauge's positive-sign fraction up to $O(d 2^{-d})$, which sign-marginalized matching removes. Omitting signs induces expected squared error $2\\|\\Delta W\\|_F^2$ on weight updates and loses orientation on vectors and AdamW first moments, while RMSNorm gains and AdamW second moments permute without signs. Controlled tests confirm these laws: a Qwen2.5-1.5B SST-2 LoRA adapter retains 0.944 accuracy (from 0.945) under $B_d$ transport vs. 0.523 under $S_d$, and steering preserves effects to $<10^{-4}$ at zero raw top-10 overlap. Across natural checkpoints, inherited slots suffice for same-base descendants (96.2%-100% retention), whereas cross-generation pairs support sparse prompt-stable subsets with causally decisive negative signs ($+0.185$ vs. $-0.010$ at $8\\sigma$ on Qwen2/2.5-0.5B; $+0.189$ vs. $-0.021$ on 1.5B). Along fine-tuning trajectories, composing local $B_d$ gauges recovers 91.1% of cross-run Qwen2.5-1.5B coordinates at 1,500 steps vs. 60.3% for endpoint matching. Merge and interpretability audits confirm that coordinate names are reproducible only relative to an explicit $B_d$ gauge.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.274735+00	\N	\N	4082bcd6c658567ad6c2405c784e4f355e8acf656f49a1c0cadf1f8ae0e47f3a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.275072+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.275074+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
