43b64c56-024c-4ec9-9d87-6df1cb8ae4e7	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2601.02915v3	ChemBART: Pre-training a Reaction Language Model for Comprehensive Synthesis Planning	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02915	https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02915	Kenan Li, Yijian Zhang, Jin Wang, Haipeng Gan, Zeying Sun, Xiaoguang Lei, Hao Dong	arXiv:2601.02915v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated transformative potential across diverse fields. However, in computer-aided synthesis planning (CASP), current LLM methodologies are often restricted to single-molecule representations, limiting their application to isolated, single-task scenarios such as precursor prediction. Addressing this gap, we present ChemBART, a BART-based language model that adopts a reaction-centric pre-training strategy. By leveraging a mask-filling pre-training task on holistic reaction expressions (Reactant > Reagent > Product), ChemBART effectively solves a variety of chemical problems, including precursor/reagent generation, temperature-yield regression, and molecular property classification. The reaction-centric pre-training further enables the integration of ChemBART with Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) for multi-step synthesis route design, where the model serves to optimize the policy and value functions within a reinforcement learning framework. Beyond single-molecule pre-trained LLMs that are constrained to specific applications, ChemBART unifies diverse chemical tasks to facilitate synthesis planning with comprehensive implementation information. Most significantly, the proposed multi-step synthesis routes and reaction conditions were validated in the wet lab, confirming the discovery of shorter synthetic pathways with a >20% yield improvement over established literature benchmarks. Our work highlights the potential of reaction-focused pre-training and showcases the broad utility of ChemBART in closing the loop between in silico prediction and wet-lab synthesis.	arXiv:2601.02915v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated transformative potential across diverse fields. However, in computer-aided synthesis planning (CASP), current LLM methodologies are often restricted to single-molecule representations, limiting their application to isolated, single-task scenarios such as precursor prediction. Addressing this gap, we present ChemBART, a BART-based language model that adopts a reaction-centric pre-training strategy. By leveraging a mask-filling pre-training task on holistic reaction expressions (Reactant > Reagent > Product), ChemBART effectively solves a variety of chemical problems, including precursor/reagent generation, temperature-yield regression, and molecular property classification. The reaction-centric pre-training further enables the integration of ChemBART with Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) for multi-step synthesis route design, where the model serves to optimize the policy and value functions within a reinforcement learning framework. Beyond single-molecule pre-trained LLMs that are constrained to specific applications, ChemBART unifies diverse chemical tasks to facilitate synthesis planning with comprehensive implementation information. Most significantly, the proposed multi-step synthesis routes and reaction conditions were validated in the wet lab, confirming the discovery of shorter synthetic pathways with a >20% yield improvement over established literature benchmarks. Our work highlights the potential of reaction-focused pre-training and showcases the broad utility of ChemBART in closing the loop between in silico prediction and wet-lab synthesis.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.882097+00	\N	\N	2c047d80ac270dc6fe6c0a72db3327abbc7cfea0ac16152fab8b00bbbfe1aa3b	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:02.882412+00	2026-11-10 16:30:02.882414+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
08513b90-4b6d-47af-a702-1430245877e7	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.18078v3	Beyond Embedding Transfer: Component Roles in Grokking Transfer and Stability	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18078	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18078	Zeyu Jia	arXiv:2609.18078v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Warm-start transfer accelerates delayed generalization (grokking) on algorithmic tasks, yet how internal blocks, residual-stream depth, group non-commutativity, and continual optimization govern transferred circuits remains unclear. Across ten scale-matched batteries ($>1{,}800$ trajectories) on abelian $\\mathbb{Z}_{113}$ ($L \\in \\{1,2,3\\}$) and non-abelian $S_5$, transferring internal blocks alongside boundary carriers lifts early accuracy by $5.46$ percentage points and cuts validation confirmation latency by $616.7 \\pm 129.0$ steps in 1-layer models ($p_{\\rm Holm}=0.004$). In 2-layer models, full transfer compresses trigger onset $8.1\\times$ ($+704.7 \\pm 84.3$ integral units), whereas omitting donor embeddings incurs a $-4{,}475.6 \\pm 76.8$-unit interface deficit. On ternary $S_5$ conjugation ($aba^{-1}$), two-token architectures stall in a unary conjugacy-class shortcut ($\\mathrm{Cl}(aba^{-1})=\\mathrm{Cl}(b)$, $34.48\\% \\pm 6.19\\%$ peak accuracy). Combining three-token causal routing ($[a,b,a]$) with Peter--Weyl width ($d_{\\rm model}=256 > \\sum_\\lambda d_\\lambda^2 = 120$) reaches $97.97\\% \\pm 1.01\\%$ ($80.08\\% \\pm 7.81\\%$ at equal $427\\text{k}$ parameters) with $3.2\\times$ faster onset than scratch. Under continued AdamW optimization, unshielded circuits relapse by $11.85\\%\\text{--}80.58\\%$ drawdown, and depth-$L$ stability follows a graded $(L-1)$-prefix pinning hierarchy ($75.99\\% \\to 31.26\\% \\to 0.32\\% \\to 0.04\\%$ drawdown at $L=3$). Finally, \\textbf{Closed-Loop Curvature-Anchored Consolidation} ($\\eta_k = \\eta_0(1 - A_{\\rm val}(k))$ with monotonic AMSGrad second moments) holds saturated drawdown to $0.00\\%\\text{--}0.12\\%$ and lifts pre-saturation terminal accuracy by $+1.67\\text{--}5.37$ points over fixed-budget micro-stepping ($87.06\\% \\pm 0.94\\%$ vs.\\ $81.69\\% \\pm 0.90\\%$ on $S_5$, $p_{\\rm Holm}=0.002$).	arXiv:2609.18078v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Warm-start transfer accelerates delayed generalization (grokking) on algorithmic tasks, yet how internal blocks, residual-stream depth, group non-commutativity, and continual optimization govern transferred circuits remains unclear. Across ten scale-matched batteries ($>1{,}800$ trajectories) on abelian $\\mathbb{Z}_{113}$ ($L \\in \\{1,2,3\\}$) and non-abelian $S_5$, transferring internal blocks alongside boundary carriers lifts early accuracy by $5.46$ percentage points and cuts validation confirmation latency by $616.7 \\pm 129.0$ steps in 1-layer models ($p_{\\rm Holm}=0.004$). In 2-layer models, full transfer compresses trigger onset $8.1\\times$ ($+704.7 \\pm 84.3$ integral units), whereas omitting donor embeddings incurs a $-4{,}475.6 \\pm 76.8$-unit interface deficit. On ternary $S_5$ conjugation ($aba^{-1}$), two-token architectures stall in a unary conjugacy-class shortcut ($\\mathrm{Cl}(aba^{-1})=\\mathrm{Cl}(b)$, $34.48\\% \\pm 6.19\\%$ peak accuracy). Combining three-token causal routing ($[a,b,a]$) with Peter--Weyl width ($d_{\\rm model}=256 > \\sum_\\lambda d_\\lambda^2 = 120$) reaches $97.97\\% \\pm 1.01\\%$ ($80.08\\% \\pm 7.81\\%$ at equal $427\\text{k}$ parameters) with $3.2\\times$ faster onset than scratch. Under continued AdamW optimization, unshielded circuits relapse by $11.85\\%\\text{--}80.58\\%$ drawdown, and depth-$L$ stability follows a graded $(L-1)$-prefix pinning hierarchy ($75.99\\% \\to 31.26\\% \\to 0.32\\% \\to 0.04\\%$ drawdown at $L=3$). Finally, \\textbf{Closed-Loop Curvature-Anchored Consolidation} ($\\eta_k = \\eta_0(1 - A_{\\rm val}(k))$ with monotonic AMSGrad second moments) holds saturated drawdown to $0.00\\%\\text{--}0.12\\%$ and lifts pre-saturation terminal accuracy by $+1.67\\text{--}5.37$ points over fixed-budget micro-stepping ($87.06\\% \\pm 0.94\\%$ vs.\\ $81.69\\% \\pm 0.90\\%$ on $S_5$, $p_{\\rm Holm}=0.002$).	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.360422+00	\N	\N	89ef3ba684d092bce9f0d54ba911b86ad5c92b9011bf0887caf2d5803040731e	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.360806+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.360808+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
a1f7f4b3-b4e1-4d19-b581-5475ce8aaeb4	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.01425v3	Why Does Train-Validation Separation Emerge? Update-Pressure Density Dynamics in Pretrained Backbones	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01425	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01425	Yuchen Li, Zongqi Fan, Ken-Tye Yong, Nguyen H. Tran	arXiv:2610.01425v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: During fine-tuning, training accuracy often continues to improve after validation accuracy levels off, widening the empirical gap. We propose a support-transfer account: early updates reuse broadly shared structure, while later fitting relies increasingly on narrower structure with less benefit for held-out examples. From fixed training probes, we normalize per-example gradient magnitudes and measure their pooled dispersion, $D_{\\mathrm{den}}$, and their probe-count-weighted within-class dispersion, $D_{\\mathrm{within}}^{\\mathrm{wt}}$. A local model gives conditions for dispersion and directional cross-entropy separation to grow together, separating uniform logit scaling; additional decision-boundary conditions characterize accuracy-gap growth. A controlled five-level hierarchy tests the effect of sample-private support. A 240-branch intervention matrix crosses training-progress anchors, batch sizes, private shares, and gradient-differentiation doses. The negative gap dose response is clearer at later training stages and is retained across the four batch sizes. A further 48 branches replicate its direction across additional seeds at a fixed stage and batch. Both normalized readouts are positively associated with the accuracy gap in all 90 language fine-tuning runs across three backbones and six tasks. The same direction holds in all 20 vision runs across four datasets. Together, the controlled hierarchy, targeted intervention, and real-model trajectories support a connection between evolving gradient differentiation and train-validation separation.	arXiv:2610.01425v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: During fine-tuning, training accuracy often continues to improve after validation accuracy levels off, widening the empirical gap. We propose a support-transfer account: early updates reuse broadly shared structure, while later fitting relies increasingly on narrower structure with less benefit for held-out examples. From fixed training probes, we normalize per-example gradient magnitudes and measure their pooled dispersion, $D_{\\mathrm{den}}$, and their probe-count-weighted within-class dispersion, $D_{\\mathrm{within}}^{\\mathrm{wt}}$. A local model gives conditions for dispersion and directional cross-entropy separation to grow together, separating uniform logit scaling; additional decision-boundary conditions characterize accuracy-gap growth. A controlled five-level hierarchy tests the effect of sample-private support. A 240-branch intervention matrix crosses training-progress anchors, batch sizes, private shares, and gradient-differentiation doses. The negative gap dose response is clearer at later training stages and is retained across the four batch sizes. A further 48 branches replicate its direction across additional seeds at a fixed stage and batch. Both normalized readouts are positively associated with the accuracy gap in all 90 language fine-tuning runs across three backbones and six tasks. The same direction holds in all 20 vision runs across four datasets. Together, the controlled hierarchy, targeted intervention, and real-model trajectories support a connection between evolving gradient differentiation and train-validation separation.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.402749+00	\N	\N	76694514fef282c81fa08b1820b020c6c32c39004ac68db1c62201e7efc9dccf	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.403104+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.403105+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
72d64aa5-998d-41d6-a321-6b8921c26b77	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2602.11042v3	Characterizing the Trainability of Instantaneous Quantum Polynomial Circuit Born Machines	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11042	https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11042	Kevin Shen, Susanne Pielawa, Vedran Dunjko, Hao Wang	arXiv:2602.11042v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Instantaneous Quantum Polynomial Circuit Born Machines (IQP-QCBMs) have been proposed as quantum generative models that combine a classically tractable training objective - based on the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) - with a potential quantum advantage motivated by sampling-complexity arguments. While recent works have explored this model across various application domains, fundamental questions remain: does the model suffer from exponentially vanishing loss gradients, known as the barren plateau problem - a pervasive obstacle in quantum machine learning - and how do regimes of trainability relate to regimes of possible quantum advantage? Here, we address both questions analytically. To study trainability, we derive closed-form expressions for the variances of the partial derivatives of the MMD loss function and establish general upper and lower bounds. We explicitly characterize how trainability depends on the generator set and the spectrum of the chosen kernel, identifying regimes in which low Hamming-weight kernels avoid exponential gradient suppression under structured topologies. Regarding potential quantum advantage, we reformulate the anti-concentration property in terms of the same generator-set quantities that govern trainability, enabling a unified analysis. We show that sparse IQP architectures can produce classically intractable output distributions while simultaneously remaining trainable, at least at lower-weight frequencies. Our analytical results corroborate the numerical observations of prior work and provide principled guidelines for designing scalable IQP-QCBM architectures.	arXiv:2602.11042v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Instantaneous Quantum Polynomial Circuit Born Machines (IQP-QCBMs) have been proposed as quantum generative models that combine a classically tractable training objective - based on the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) - with a potential quantum advantage motivated by sampling-complexity arguments. While recent works have explored this model across various application domains, fundamental questions remain: does the model suffer from exponentially vanishing loss gradients, known as the barren plateau problem - a pervasive obstacle in quantum machine learning - and how do regimes of trainability relate to regimes of possible quantum advantage? Here, we address both questions analytically. To study trainability, we derive closed-form expressions for the variances of the partial derivatives of the MMD loss function and establish general upper and lower bounds. We explicitly characterize how trainability depends on the generator set and the spectrum of the chosen kernel, identifying regimes in which low Hamming-weight kernels avoid exponential gradient suppression under structured topologies. Regarding potential quantum advantage, we reformulate the anti-concentration property in terms of the same generator-set quantities that govern trainability, enabling a unified analysis. We show that sparse IQP architectures can produce classically intractable output distributions while simultaneously remaining trainable, at least at lower-weight frequencies. Our analytical results corroborate the numerical observations of prior work and provide principled guidelines for designing scalable IQP-QCBM architectures.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.654149+00	\N	\N	6658f021b6702ddae0b0f1f7f4b84ebfb2909cdca6cd8d6e19021f9efc756783	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.654528+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.65453+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
911dc3f3-fbe9-4524-9f9e-8c5d071abf99	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2605.08811v2	Learning Theory of Transformers: Local-to-Global Approximation via Softmax Partition of Unity	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08811	https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08811	Zhongjie Shi, Wenjing Liao	arXiv:2605.08811v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: This paper investigates the learning theory of Transformer networks for regression tasks on the compact Euclidean domain $[0,1]^d$ and $d$-dimensional compact Riemannian manifolds. We propose a novel constructive approximation framework for Transformers that builds local approximations of the target function and aggregates them into a global approximation via softmax partition of unity (POU). This approach leverages the attention mechanism to achieve spatial localization through affine transformations of the input. The softmax activation plays a crucial role in aggregating local approximations to a global output. From an approximation perspective, we prove that a dense Transformer equipped with only two encoder blocks and standard single-hidden-layer point-wise feed-forward networks can achieve a uniform $\\varepsilon$-approximation error for $\\alpha$-H\\"older continuous functions with $\\alpha \\in (0,1]$ using $\\mathcal{O}(\\varepsilon^{-d/\\alpha})$ total parameters. Building upon this approximation guarantee, we establish a near minimax-optimal generalization error bound of order $\\mathcal{O}\\big(n^{-\\frac{2\\alpha}{2\\alpha+d}} \\log n\\big)$ for the empirical risk minimizer, where $n$ is the training data size. The Transformer architecture studied in this paper uses native softmax attention and sinusoidal positional encodings, with a fixed depth of two encoder blocks. Finally, numerical experiments reveal spatially localized, Gaussian-like attention weights that emerge during training, resembling our Softmax POU weights.	arXiv:2605.08811v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: This paper investigates the learning theory of Transformer networks for regression tasks on the compact Euclidean domain $[0,1]^d$ and $d$-dimensional compact Riemannian manifolds. We propose a novel constructive approximation framework for Transformers that builds local approximations of the target function and aggregates them into a global approximation via softmax partition of unity (POU). This approach leverages the attention mechanism to achieve spatial localization through affine transformations of the input. The softmax activation plays a crucial role in aggregating local approximations to a global output. From an approximation perspective, we prove that a dense Transformer equipped with only two encoder blocks and standard single-hidden-layer point-wise feed-forward networks can achieve a uniform $\\varepsilon$-approximation error for $\\alpha$-H\\"older continuous functions with $\\alpha \\in (0,1]$ using $\\mathcal{O}(\\varepsilon^{-d/\\alpha})$ total parameters. Building upon this approximation guarantee, we establish a near minimax-optimal generalization error bound of order $\\mathcal{O}\\big(n^{-\\frac{2\\alpha}{2\\alpha+d}} \\log n\\big)$ for the empirical risk minimizer, where $n$ is the training data size. The Transformer architecture studied in this paper uses native softmax attention and sinusoidal positional encodings, with a fixed depth of two encoder blocks. Finally, numerical experiments reveal spatially localized, Gaussian-like attention weights that emerge during training, resembling our Softmax POU weights.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.735139+00	\N	\N	8f753e3e13145a921894aa947a5dcad3fd08cdfa31a6bd325f15e570d9c3e22a	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.735441+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.735443+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
c7c5a360-446c-4f83-8c08-f8224cf977ea	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2606.03180v2	GLINT: Sparsely Gated Vision-Language Alignment for Fine-Grained Radiology Representations	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03180	https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03180	Jonggwon Park, Seongeun Lee, Junhyun Park, Hannah Yun, Hyunwoong Kim, Sohyun Jeong, Hyewon Kang, Byungmu Yoon, Kyoyun Choi	arXiv:2606.03180v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Vision-language models (VLMs) for radiology have emerged as a scalable paradigm by leveraging image-report pairs naturally produced in clinical workflows. However, this pairing reveals a mismatch in scale: each finding occupies only a small region of the image, yet supervision is provided only at the global image-report level. This poses a central challenge: prior approaches spread weight densely across all patches rather than concentrating on the sparse subset relevant to a given query. To address this, we present GLINT (Gated Language-Image alignmeNT), a framework that models this sparse correspondence. On the alignment side, we introduce Sparsely Gated Alignment, a novel architecture in which a sigmoid gate over a separate gate embedding space activates only the patches relevant to each textual query, yielding sparsity. On the representation side, we add Dense Feature Regularization, which anchors the trainable encoder's intermediate features to a frozen self-supervised learning (SSL) teacher, preserving the fine-grained patch features that the gate relies on. The same recipe applies to both 2D chest X-ray (CXR) and 3D chest computed tomography (CT), built with DINOv3 and V-JEPA 2.1, respectively. GLINT enables zero-shot classification, grounding, and segmentation from free-text queries, and to our knowledge is the first to demonstrate zero-shot segmentation on 3D CT volumes without mask supervision. Notably, the most pronounced gains arise on zero-shot grounding and segmentation, where sparse, query-specific localization is required, consistent with our design intent. In downstream evaluation, GLINT outperforms both SSL encoders and medical VLMs on classification, report generation, and segmentation. Code is available at https://github.com/deepnoid-ai/GLINT.	arXiv:2606.03180v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Vision-language models (VLMs) for radiology have emerged as a scalable paradigm by leveraging image-report pairs naturally produced in clinical workflows. However, this pairing reveals a mismatch in scale: each finding occupies only a small region of the image, yet supervision is provided only at the global image-report level. This poses a central challenge: prior approaches spread weight densely across all patches rather than concentrating on the sparse subset relevant to a given query. To address this, we present GLINT (Gated Language-Image alignmeNT), a framework that models this sparse correspondence. On the alignment side, we introduce Sparsely Gated Alignment, a novel architecture in which a sigmoid gate over a separate gate embedding space activates only the patches relevant to each textual query, yielding sparsity. On the representation side, we add Dense Feature Regularization, which anchors the trainable encoder's intermediate features to a frozen self-supervised learning (SSL) teacher, preserving the fine-grained patch features that the gate relies on. The same recipe applies to both 2D chest X-ray (CXR) and 3D chest computed tomography (CT), built with DINOv3 and V-JEPA 2.1, respectively. GLINT enables zero-shot classification, grounding, and segmentation from free-text queries, and to our knowledge is the first to demonstrate zero-shot segmentation on 3D CT volumes without mask supervision. Notably, the most pronounced gains arise on zero-shot grounding and segmentation, where sparse, query-specific localization is required, consistent with our design intent. In downstream evaluation, GLINT outperforms both SSL encoders and medical VLMs on classification, report generation, and segmentation. Code is available at https://github.com/deepnoid-ai/GLINT.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.813632+00	\N	\N	b08ae41f3c3737958fd6759dbe825a87bc3b713a65ab55eae32ab7715404d619	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:03.813986+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.813987+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
d0522877-d1ef-498c-af18-cfc133161f10	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2611.07502v2	Coverage Hides Churn: Auditing and Preserving Conformal Decisions as VLM Label Registries Grow	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07502	https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07502	Jai Kumar Sharma, Peeyush Tapadiya	arXiv:2611.07502v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Vision-language classifiers can learn new labels from text alone, so the label lists they serve keep growing. We show that this growth silently changes conformal decisions about old labels. Common softmax-based conformal scores compare each label with an average over all labels; adding labels changes that average, and with it the score of every old label, even when the model, the image, and the old calibration data stay the same. Recalibrating after the update keeps the coverage guarantee, yet across eleven settings it changes at least one old decision on 53-90% of new images. Coverage cannot reveal this, because it checks only the true label: in five controlled settings that keep the old calibration, so that only the normalizer changes, old-class coverage moves by at most 0.01 while 60-67% of images change. We propose a lifecycle audit that measures these changes alongside coverage and set size, and a simple fix, PrefixCP: each class keeps the label registry that existed when it was added. Its old decisions then never change, each class keeps exact class-conditional coverage under exchangeability, and one prefix scan computes all historical normalizers at once. PrefixCP recovers 40.9-58.5% of the set-size gap between never updating the reference and recalibrating, which gives smaller sets but changes old decisions.	arXiv:2611.07502v2 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Vision-language classifiers can learn new labels from text alone, so the label lists they serve keep growing. We show that this growth silently changes conformal decisions about old labels. Common softmax-based conformal scores compare each label with an average over all labels; adding labels changes that average, and with it the score of every old label, even when the model, the image, and the old calibration data stay the same. Recalibrating after the update keeps the coverage guarantee, yet across eleven settings it changes at least one old decision on 53-90% of new images. Coverage cannot reveal this, because it checks only the true label: in five controlled settings that keep the old calibration, so that only the normalizer changes, old-class coverage moves by at most 0.01 while 60-67% of images change. We propose a lifecycle audit that measures these changes alongside coverage and set size, and a simple fix, PrefixCP: each class keeps the label registry that existed when it was added. Its old decisions then never change, each class keeps exact class-conditional coverage under exchangeability, and one prefix scan computes all historical normalizers at once. PrefixCP recovers 40.9-58.5% of the set-size gap between never updating the reference and recalibrating, which gives smaller sets but changes old decisions.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.039273+00	\N	\N	5bb62344d913853aba90783123b90633cce5673e055a369d88d94e8512e25adf	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:04.039706+00	2026-11-10 16:30:04.039708+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
46634878-7df2-470e-b10d-dc20d78a96c6	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2510.04302v3	When Guessing is Rewarded: Rethinking Language Model Evaluation with Distributional Uncertainty Scoring	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04302	https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04302	Thomas F Burns	arXiv:2510.04302v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Standard language model evaluation assigns scores to single predicted answers, rewarding high-confidence responses regardless of how residual probability mass is distributed over alternative options. This creates a systematic pressure toward overconfident guessing: under accuracy-based schemes, a model maximises its expected score by always committing to an answer rather than abstaining, even when its uncertainty is high. While penalty-based approaches partially address this by raising the confidence threshold for strategic guessing, they still treat all sub-threshold responses identically, ignoring a fundamental distinction in how models can express uncertainty - for example between hedging toward incorrect answers versus hedging toward "I don't know" responses. This paper introduces a novel evaluation metric to solve this problem of not considering a model's entire probability distribution over answer choices. The metric naturally distinguishes between harmful overconfidence in wrong answers and uncertainty expressed through abstention, providing scores in an interpretable default range. Through theoretical analysis and illustrative examples, the metric is shown to offer a more nuanced and aligned evaluation paradigm that incentivises models to express genuine uncertainty rather than guessing. Adapting 12 existing evaluation benchmarks to the metric's variants and measuring performance on six language models shows that for half of the tested benchmarks scores are negative across all tested models, indicating significant tendencies towards hallucination.	arXiv:2510.04302v3 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Standard language model evaluation assigns scores to single predicted answers, rewarding high-confidence responses regardless of how residual probability mass is distributed over alternative options. This creates a systematic pressure toward overconfident guessing: under accuracy-based schemes, a model maximises its expected score by always committing to an answer rather than abstaining, even when its uncertainty is high. While penalty-based approaches partially address this by raising the confidence threshold for strategic guessing, they still treat all sub-threshold responses identically, ignoring a fundamental distinction in how models can express uncertainty - for example between hedging toward incorrect answers versus hedging toward "I don't know" responses. This paper introduces a novel evaluation metric to solve this problem of not considering a model's entire probability distribution over answer choices. The metric naturally distinguishes between harmful overconfidence in wrong answers and uncertainty expressed through abstention, providing scores in an interpretable default range. Through theoretical analysis and illustrative examples, the metric is shown to offer a more nuanced and aligned evaluation paradigm that incentivises models to express genuine uncertainty rather than guessing. Adapting 12 existing evaluation benchmarks to the metric's variants and measuring performance on six language models shows that for half of the tested benchmarks scores are negative across all tested models, indicating significant tendencies towards hallucination.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.122867+00	\N	\N	4c053e765253014091040fd6cc3469412ad7d2c83cbca4881ba3a114d2b9d487	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.123115+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.123116+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
5460ff8c-abd3-4bf0-8330-62098311528e	aa25b96e-953d-4a88-a929-27c2362afe1d	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2610.31620v2	Bilingual Language Models Capture Cross-Linguistic Influence in Second-Language Reading	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31620	https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31620	Abderrahmane Issam, Irene E. Winther, Stefan L. Frank	arXiv:2610.31620v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: While monolingual language model (LM) surprisal is widely established as a predictor of reading times, it cannot capture the non-native (L2) reading dynamics that are shaped by the reader's first language (L1). In this work, we investigate whether the cross-lingual transfer within bilingual models aligns with human L1-driven biases during L2 reading. We evaluate bilingual models on eye-tracking data of readers from 12 diverse L1 backgrounds reading English texts. To isolate L1-specific transfer, we compare bilingual models trained on an L1 that matches the readers' L1 background to both a monolingual English baseline and bilingual models trained on other L1s. We demonstrate that matching the readers' L1 improves the prediction of L2 reading times. This predictive advantage is concentrated in late-stage eye-tracking measures, modulated by L1-L2 script overlap, and peaks among intermediate-proficiency readers. These findings provide evidence that bilingual LMs capture aspects of cross-linguistic influence, and that such influence is present in L2 reading behavior.	arXiv:2610.31620v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: While monolingual language model (LM) surprisal is widely established as a predictor of reading times, it cannot capture the non-native (L2) reading dynamics that are shaped by the reader's first language (L1). In this work, we investigate whether the cross-lingual transfer within bilingual models aligns with human L1-driven biases during L2 reading. We evaluate bilingual models on eye-tracking data of readers from 12 diverse L1 backgrounds reading English texts. To isolate L1-specific transfer, we compare bilingual models trained on an L1 that matches the readers' L1 background to both a monolingual English baseline and bilingual models trained on other L1s. We demonstrate that matching the readers' L1 improves the prediction of L2 reading times. This predictive advantage is concentrated in late-stage eye-tracking measures, modulated by L1-L2 script overlap, and peaks among intermediate-proficiency readers. These findings provide evidence that bilingual LMs capture aspects of cross-linguistic influence, and that such influence is present in L2 reading behavior.	article	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.260532+00	\N	\N	bb9540372f2d4ce4be500a86770f2e5ce9d6021e034ae7f10a2e6bb995179339	pending	2026-11-10 16:30:05.261053+00	2026-11-10 16:30:05.261055+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
88fc7075-e6a9-44bf-a6a6-caf42c9ff2c3	d3342e2c-5c4d-4f89-a00d-09b38226d5e5	\N	https://openai.com/index/coderabbit-codex-daybreak	CodeRabbit scales agentic software development and ships 20x more	https://openai.com/index/coderabbit-codex-daybreak	https://openai.com/index/coderabbit-codex-daybreak	\N	CodeRabbit uses OpenAI models to govern and trust AI-generated code and Codex to ship 20 times more software with the same engineering team.	CodeRabbit uses OpenAI models to govern and trust AI-generated code and Codex to ship 20 times more software with the same engineering team.	product_update	en	2026-11-09 08:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:29:59.0313+00	4	\N	545e586d20a598c1c73e173601dcdd9c621730d6ca714497fcb48c0268f2b45d	processed	2026-11-10 16:29:59.031691+00	2026-11-10 16:30:12.767293+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
de280016-ad36-4577-9a8b-002c78d77491	928ad6da-fec1-474b-ae1a-3cf781cb4d3e	\N	oai:arXiv.org:2609.22212v3	A Channel-Boosted Multi-Agent System with Iterative Consultation for Document Sensitivity Classification	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22212	https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22212	Aleesha Zainab, Asifullah Khan, Muhammad Ahmed Khalid, Faheem Ullah Khan	arXiv:2609.22212v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Organizations in critical national infrastructure sectors must assess heterogeneous documents for sensitivity before routing or storage. Manual assessment is slow, inconsistent, and unscalable. Extending our prior leakage-controlled benchmark, BERT established the top single-encoder baseline (89.14% accuracy, 89.33% F1-score under 5-fold cross-validation on the Strategic 16K corpus). However, transformer baselines suffer from a structural limitation: fixed input length truncation discards evidence beyond the retained window-precisely where sensitive cables tend to be longest. We present Channel-Boosted MAS (CB-MAS) and instantiate it as IC-MAS (Iterative Consultation Multi-Agent System) to solve this without long-context computational costs. A Channel Critic Agent learns document-adaptive trust weights governing Gated Channel Boosting between two first-window encoders, while paired Consultation Agents iteratively exchange belief states to reconcile evidence from the beginning and end of long documents. IC-MAS holds computation constant regardless of document length by reconciling fixed windows in a compact representation space. Ablation studies show critic-controlled Channel Boosting provides the bulk of accuracy gains, while consultation recovers recall without precision collapse. Critic-Controlled Gated Channel Boosting with Max-Pool fusion and Blackboard Adaptive Consultation achieves 90.72% accuracy, 91.23% F1-score, 92.01% sensitive recall, and 90.46% sensitive precision, using about 54% less average computation than a fixed-round baseline. Gains over the single-encoder baseline are statistically significant (McNemar's test, p less than 0.000001; paired t-test). We include LIME/SHAP explainability, multi-agent evaluation, and an honest accounting of limitations.	arXiv:2609.22212v3 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Organizations in critical national infrastructure sectors must assess heterogeneous documents for sensitivity before routing or storage. Manual assessment is slow, inconsistent, and unscalable. Extending our prior leakage-controlled benchmark, BERT established the top single-encoder baseline (89.14% accuracy, 89.33% F1-score under 5-fold cross-validation on the Strategic 16K corpus). However, transformer baselines suffer from a structural limitation: fixed input length truncation discards evidence beyond the retained window-precisely where sensitive cables tend to be longest. We present Channel-Boosted MAS (CB-MAS) and instantiate it as IC-MAS (Iterative Consultation Multi-Agent System) to solve this without long-context computational costs. A Channel Critic Agent learns document-adaptive trust weights governing Gated Channel Boosting between two first-window encoders, while paired Consultation Agents iteratively exchange belief states to reconcile evidence from the beginning and end of long documents. IC-MAS holds computation constant regardless of document length by reconciling fixed windows in a compact representation space. Ablation studies show critic-controlled Channel Boosting provides the bulk of accuracy gains, while consultation recovers recall without precision collapse. Critic-Controlled Gated Channel Boosting with Max-Pool fusion and Blackboard Adaptive Consultation achieves 90.72% accuracy, 91.23% F1-score, 92.01% sensitive recall, and 90.46% sensitive precision, using about 54% less average computation than a fixed-round baseline. Gains over the single-encoder baseline are statistically significant (McNemar's test, p less than 0.000001; paired t-test). We include LIME/SHAP explainability, multi-agent evaluation, and an honest accounting of limitations.	research	en	2026-11-10 05:00:00+00	2026-11-10 16:30:03.935226+00	4	\N	040e91a9903197d8fc6f458ae13d3944d5210a3cc3933cadf0c4018b9f1bf5c7	processed	2026-11-10 16:30:03.935612+00	2026-11-10 16:36:37.893518+00	pending	\N	\N	pending	0	\N	\N
